\par
CHAPTER I\par
\par
home is the resort\par
Of love, of joy, of peace and plenty, where,\par
Supporting and supported, polish’d friends\par
And dear relations mingle into bliss.\par
                    THOMSON\par
\par
\par
On the pleasant banks of the Garonne, in the province of Gascony,\par
stood, in the year 1584, the château of Monsieur St. Aubert. From its\par
windows were seen the pastoral landscapes of Guienne and Gascony\par
stretching along the river, gay with luxuriant woods and vine, and\par
plantations of olives. To the south, the view was bounded by the\par
majestic Pyrenees, whose summits, veiled in clouds, or exhibiting awful\par
forms, seen, and lost again, as the partial vapours rolled along, were\par
sometimes barren, and gleamed through the blue tinge of air, and\par
sometimes frowned with forests of gloomy pine, that swept downward to\par
their base. These tremendous precipices were contrasted by the soft\par
green of the pastures and woods that hung upon their skirts; among\par
whose flocks, and herds, and simple cottages, the eye, after having\par
scaled the cliffs above, delighted to repose. To the north, and to the\par
east, the plains of Guienne and Languedoc were lost in the mist of\par
distance; on the west, Gascony was bounded by the waters of Biscay.\par
\par
M. St. Aubert loved to wander, with his wife and daughter, on the\par
margin of the Garonne, and to listen to the music that floated on its\par
waves. He had known life in other forms than those of pastoral\par
simplicity, having mingled in the gay and in the busy scenes of the\par
world; but the flattering portrait of mankind, which his heart had\par
delineated in early youth, his experience had too sorrowfully\par
corrected. Yet, amidst the changing visions of life, his principles\par
remained unshaken, his benevolence unchilled; and he retired from the\par
multitude “more in _pity_ than in anger,” to scenes of simple nature,\par
to the pure delights of literature, and to the exercise of domestic\par
virtues.\par
\par
He was a descendant from the younger branch of an illustrious family,\par
and it was designed, that the deficiency of his patrimonial wealth\par
should be supplied either by a splendid alliance in marriage, or by\par
success in the intrigues of public affairs. But St. Aubert had too nice\par
a sense of honour to fulfil the latter hope, and too small a portion of\par
ambition to sacrifice what he called happiness, to the attainment of\par
wealth. After the death of his father he married a very amiable woman,\par
his equal in birth, and not his superior in fortune. The late Monsieur\par
St. Aubert’s liberality, or extravagance, had so much involved his\par
affairs, that his son found it necessary to dispose of a part of the\par
family domain, and, some years after his marriage, he sold it to\par
Monsieur Quesnel, the brother of his wife, and retired to a small\par
estate in Gascony, where conjugal felicity, and parental duties,\par
divided his attention with the treasures of knowledge and the\par
illuminations of genius.\par
\par
To this spot he had been attached from his infancy. He had often made\par
excursions to it when a boy, and the impressions of delight given to\par
his mind by the homely kindness of the grey-headed peasant, to whom it\par
was intrusted, and whose fruit and cream never failed, had not been\par
obliterated by succeeding circumstances. The green pastures along which\par
he had so often bounded in the exultation of health, and youthful\par
freedom—the woods, under whose refreshing shade he had first indulged\par
that pensive melancholy, which afterwards made a strong feature of his\par
character—the wild walks of the mountains, the river, on whose waves he\par
had floated, and the distant plains, which seemed boundless as his\par
early hopes—were never after remembered by St. Aubert but with\par
enthusiasm and regret. At length he disengaged himself from the world,\par
and retired hither, to realise the wishes of many years.\par
\par
The building, as it then stood, was merely a summer cottage, rendered\par
interesting to a stranger by its neat simplicity, or the beauty of the\par
surrounding scene; and considerable additions were necessary to make it\par
a comfortable family residence. St. Aubert felt a kind of affection for\par
every part of the fabric, which he remembered in his youth, and would\par
not suffer a stone of it to be removed, so that the new building,\par
adapted to the style of the old one, formed with it only a simple and\par
elegant residence. The taste of Madame St. Aubert was conspicuous in\par
its internal finishing, where the same chaste simplicity was observable\par
in the furniture, and in the few ornaments of the apartments, that\par
characterised the manners of its inhabitants.\par
\par
The library occupied the west side of the château, and was enriched by\par
a collection of the best books in the ancient and modern languages.\par
This room opened upon a grove, which stood on the brow of a gentle\par
declivity, that fell towards the river, and the tall trees gave it a\par
melancholy and pleasing shade; while from the windows the eye caught,\par
beneath the spreading branches, the gay and luxuriant landscape\par
stretching to the west, and overlooked on the left by the bold\par
precipices of the Pyrenees. Adjoining the library was a green-house,\par
stored with scarce and beautiful plants; for one of the amusements of\par
St. Aubert was the study of botany, and among the neighbouring\par
mountains, which afforded a luxurious feast to the mind of the\par
naturalist, he often passed the day in the pursuit of his favourite\par
science. He was sometimes accompanied in these little excursions by\par
Madame St. Aubert, and frequently by his daughter; when, with a small\par
osier basket to receive plants, and another filled with cold\par
refreshments, such as the cabin of the shepherd did not afford, they\par
wandered away among the most romantic and magnificent scenes, nor\par
suffered the charms of Nature’s lowly children to abstract them from\par
the observance of her stupendous works. When weary of sauntering among\par
cliffs that seemed scarcely accessible but to the steps of the\par
enthusiast, and where no track appeared on the vegetation, but what the\par
foot of the izard had left; they would seek one of those green\par
recesses, which so beautifully adorn the bosom of these mountains,\par
where, under the shade of the lofty larch, or cedar, they enjoyed their\par
simple repast, made sweeter by the waters of the cool stream, that\par
crept along the turf, and by the breath of wild flowers and aromatic\par
plants, that fringed the rocks, and inlaid the grass.\par
\par
Adjoining the eastern side of the green-house, looking towards the\par
plains of Languedoc, was a room, which Emily called hers, and which\par
contained her books, her drawings, her musical instruments, with some\par
favourite birds and plants. Here she usually exercised herself in\par
elegant arts, cultivated only because they were congenial to her taste,\par
and in which native genius, assisted by the instructions of Monsieur\par
and Madame St. Aubert, made her an early proficient. The windows of\par
this room were particularly pleasant; they descended to the floor, and,\par
opening upon the little lawn that surrounded the house, the eye was led\par
between groves of almond, palm-trees, flowering-ash, and myrtle, to the\par
distant landscape, where the Garonne wandered.\par
\par
The peasants of this gay climate were often seen on an evening, when\par
the day’s labour was done, dancing in groups on the margin of the\par
river. Their sprightly melodies, _debonnaire_ steps, the fanciful\par
figure of their dances, with the tasteful and capricious manner in\par
which the girls adjusted their simple dress, gave a character to the\par
scene entirely French.\par
\par
The front of the château, which, having a southern aspect, opened upon\par
the grandeur of the mountains, was occupied on the ground floor by a\par
rustic hall, and two excellent sitting rooms. The first floor, for the\par
cottage had no second story, was laid out in bed-chambers, except one\par
apartment that opened to a balcony, and which was generally used for a\par
breakfast-room.\par
\par
In the surrounding ground, St. Aubert had made very tasteful\par
improvements; yet, such was his attachment to objects he had remembered\par
from his boyish days, that he had in some instances sacrificed taste to\par
sentiment. There were two old larches that shaded the building, and\par
interrupted the prospect; St. Aubert had sometimes declared that he\par
believed he should have been weak enough to have wept at their fall. In\par
addition to these larches he planted a little grove of beech, pine, and\par
mountain-ash. On a lofty terrace, formed by the swelling bank of the\par
river, rose a plantation of orange, lemon, and palm-trees, whose fruit,\par
in the coolness of evening, breathed delicious fragrance. With these\par
were mingled a few trees of other species. Here, under the ample shade\par
of a plane-tree, that spread its majestic canopy towards the river, St.\par
Aubert loved to sit in the fine evenings of summer, with his wife and\par
children, watching, beneath its foliage, the setting sun, the mild\par
splendour of its light fading from the distant landscape, till the\par
shadows of twilight melted its various features into one tint of sober\par
grey. Here, too, he loved to read, and to converse with Madame St.\par
Aubert; or to play with his children, resigning himself to the\par
influence of those sweet affections, which are ever attendant on\par
simplicity and nature. He has often said, while tears of pleasure\par
trembled in his eyes, that these were moments infinitely more\par
delightful than any passed amid the brilliant and tumultuous scenes\par
that are courted by the world. His heart was occupied; it had, what can\par
be so rarely said, no wish for a happiness beyond what it experienced.\par
The consciousness of acting right diffused a serenity over his manners,\par
which nothing else could impart to a man of moral perceptions like his,\par
and which refined his sense of every surrounding blessing.\par
\par
The deepest shade of twilight did not send him from his favourite\par
plane-tree. He loved the soothing hour, when the last tints of light\par
die away; when the stars, one by one, tremble through æther, and are\par
reflected on the dark mirror of the waters; that hour, which, of all\par
others, inspires the mind with pensive tenderness, and often elevates\par
it to sublime contemplation. When the moon shed her soft rays among the\par
foliage, he still lingered, and his pastoral supper of cream and fruits\par
was often spread beneath it. Then, on the stillness of night, came the\par
song of the nightingale, breathing sweetness, and awakening melancholy.\par
\par
The first interruptions to the happiness he had known since his\par
retirement, were occasioned by the death of his two sons. He lost them\par
at that age when infantine simplicity is so fascinating; and though, in\par
consideration of Madame St. Aubert’s distress, he restrained the\par
expression of his own, and endeavoured to bear it, as he meant, with\par
philosophy, he had, in truth, no philosophy that could render him calm\par
to such losses. One daughter was now his only surviving child; and,\par
while he watched the unfolding of her infant character, with anxious\par
fondness, he endeavoured, with unremitting effort, to counteract those\par
traits in her disposition, which might hereafter lead her from\par
happiness. She had discovered in her early years uncommon delicacy of\par
mind, warm affections, and ready benevolence; but with these was\par
observable a degree of susceptibility too exquisite to admit of lasting\par
peace. As she advanced in youth, this sensibility gave a pensive tone\par
to her spirits, and a softness to her manner, which added grace to\par
beauty, and rendered her a very interesting object to persons of a\par
congenial disposition. But St. Aubert had too much good sense to prefer\par
a charm to a virtue; and had penetration enough to see, that this charm\par
was too dangerous to its possessor to be allowed the character of a\par
blessing. He endeavoured, therefore, to strengthen her mind; to enure\par
her to habits of self-command; to teach her to reject the first impulse\par
of her feelings, and to look, with cool examination, upon the\par
disappointments he sometimes threw in her way. While he instructed her\par
to resist first impressions, and to acquire that steady dignity of\par
mind, that can alone counterbalance the passions, and bear us, as far\par
as is compatible with our nature, above the reach of circumstances, he\par
taught himself a lesson of fortitude; for he was often obliged to\par
witness, with seeming indifference, the tears and struggles which his\par
caution occasioned her.\par
\par
In person, Emily resembled her mother; having the same elegant symmetry\par
of form, the same delicacy of features, and the same blue eyes, full of\par
tender sweetness. But, lovely as was her person, it was the varied\par
expression of her countenance, as conversation awakened the nicer\par
emotions of her mind, that threw such a captivating grace around her:\par
\par
Those tend’rer tints, that shun the careless eye,\par
And, in the world’s contagious circle, die.\par
\par
\par
St. Aubert cultivated her understanding with the most scrupulous care.\par
He gave her a general view of the sciences, and an exact acquaintance\par
with every part of elegant literature. He taught her Latin and English,\par
chiefly that she might understand the sublimity of their best poets.\par
She discovered in her early years a taste for works of genius; and it\par
was St. Aubert’s principle, as well as his inclination, to promote\par
every innocent means of happiness. “A well-informed mind,” he would\par
say, “is the best security against the contagion of folly and of vice.\par
The vacant mind is ever on the watch for relief, and ready to plunge\par
into error, to escape from the languor of idleness. Store it with\par
ideas, teach it the pleasure of thinking; and the temptations of the\par
world without, will be counteracted by the gratifications derived from\par
the world within. Thought, and cultivation, are necessary equally to\par
the happiness of a country and a city life; in the first they prevent\par
the uneasy sensations of indolence, and afford a sublime pleasure in\par
the taste they create for the beautiful, and the grand; in the latter,\par
they make dissipation less an object of necessity, and consequently of\par
interest.”\par
\par
It was one of Emily’s earliest pleasures to ramble among the scenes of\par
nature; nor was it in the soft and glowing landscape that she most\par
delighted; she loved more the wild wood-walks, that skirted the\par
mountain; and still more the mountain’s stupendous recesses, where the\par
silence and grandeur of solitude impressed a sacred awe upon her heart,\par
and lifted her thoughts to the GOD OF HEAVEN AND EARTH. In scenes like\par
these she would often linger along, wrapt in a melancholy charm, till\par
the last gleam of day faded from the west; till the lonely sound of a\par
sheep-bell, or the distant bark of a watch-dog, were all that broke on\par
the stillness of the evening. Then, the gloom of the woods; the\par
trembling of their leaves, at intervals, in the breeze; the bat,\par
flitting on the twilight; the cottage-lights, now seen, and now\par
lost—were circumstances that awakened her mind into effort, and led to\par
enthusiasm and poetry.\par
\par
Her favourite walk was to a little fishing-house, belonging to St.\par
Aubert, in a woody glen, on the margin of a rivulet that descended from\par
the Pyrenees, and, after foaming among their rocks, wound its silent\par
way beneath the shades it reflected. Above the woods, that screened\par
this glen, rose the lofty summits of the Pyrenees, which often burst\par
boldly on the eye through the glades below. Sometimes the shattered\par
face of a rock only was seen, crowned with wild shrubs; or a shepherd’s\par
cabin seated on a cliff, overshadowed by dark cypress, or waving ash.\par
Emerging from the deep recesses of the woods, the glade opened to the\par
distant landscape, where the rich pastures and vine-covered slopes of\par
Gascony gradually declined to the plains; and there, on the winding\par
shores of the Garonne, groves, and hamlets, and villas—their outlines\par
softened by distance, melted from the eye into one rich harmonious\par
tint.\par
\par
This, too, was the favourite retreat of St. Aubert, to which he\par
frequently withdrew from the fervour of noon, with his wife, his\par
daughter, and his books; or came at the sweet evening hour to welcome\par
the silent dusk, or to listen for the music of the nightingale.\par
Sometimes, too, he brought music of his own, and awakened every fairy\par
echo with the tender accents of his oboe; and often have the tones of\par
Emily’s voice drawn sweetness from the waves, over which they trembled.\par
\par
It was in one of these excursions to this spot, that she observed the\par
following lines written with a pencil on a part of the wainscot:\par
\par
SONNET\par
\par
Go, pencil! faithful to thy master’s sighs!\par
Go—tell the Goddess of the fairy scene,\par
When next her light steps wind these wood-walks green,\par
Whence all his tears, his tender sorrows, rise;\par
Ah! paint her form, her soul-illumin’d eyes,\par
The sweet expression of her pensive face,\par
The light’ning smile, the animated grace—\par
The portrait well the lover’s voice supplies;\par
Speaks all his heart must feel, his tongue would say:\par
Yet ah! not all his heart must sadly feel!\par
How oft the flow’ret’s silken leaves conceal\par
The drug that steals the vital spark away!\par
And who that gazes on that angel-smile,\par
Would fear its charm, or think it could beguile!\par
\par
\par
These lines were not inscribed to any person; Emily therefore could not\par
apply them to herself, though she was undoubtedly the nymph of these\par
shades. Having glanced round the little circle of her acquaintance\par
without being detained by a suspicion as to whom they could be\par
addressed, she was compelled to rest in uncertainty; an uncertainty\par
which would have been more painful to an idle mind than it was to hers.\par
She had no leisure to suffer this circumstance, trifling at first, to\par
swell into importance by frequent remembrance. The little vanity it had\par
excited (for the incertitude which forbade her to presume upon having\par
inspired the sonnet, forbade her also to disbelieve it) passed away,\par
and the incident was dismissed from her thoughts amid her books, her\par
studies, and the exercise of social charities.\par
\par
Soon after this period, her anxiety was awakened by the indisposition\par
of her father, who was attacked with a fever; which, though not thought\par
to be of a dangerous kind, gave a severe shock to his constitution.\par
Madame St. Aubert and Emily attended him with unremitting care; but his\par
recovery was very slow, and, as he advanced towards health, Madame\par
seemed to decline.\par
\par
The first scene he visited, after he was well enough to take the air,\par
was his favourite fishing-house. A basket of provisions was sent\par
thither, with books, and Emily’s lute; for fishing-tackle he had no\par
use, for he never could find amusement in torturing or destroying.\par
\par
After employing himself, for about an hour, in botanizing, dinner was\par
served. It was a repast, to which gratitude, for being again permitted\par
to visit this spot, gave sweetness; and family happiness once more\par
smiled beneath these shades. Monsieur St. Aubert conversed with unusual\par
cheerfulness; every object delighted his senses. The refreshing\par
pleasure from the first view of nature, after the pain of illness, and\par
the confinement of a sick-chamber, is above the conceptions, as well as\par
the descriptions, of those in health. The green woods and pastures; the\par
flowery turf; the blue concave of the heavens; the balmy air; the\par
murmur of the limpid stream; and even the hum of every little insect of\par
the shade, seem to revivify the soul, and make mere existence bliss.\par
\par
Madame St. Aubert, reanimated by the cheerfulness and recovery of her\par
husband, was no longer sensible of the indisposition which had lately\par
oppressed her; and, as she sauntered along the wood-walks of this\par
romantic glen, and conversed with him, and with her daughter, she often\par
looked at them alternately with a degree of tenderness, that filled her\par
eyes with tears. St. Aubert observed this more than once, and gently\par
reproved her for the emotion; but she could only smile, clasp his hand,\par
and that of Emily, and weep the more. He felt the tender enthusiasm\par
stealing upon himself in a degree that became almost painful; his\par
features assumed a serious air, and he could not forbear secretly\par
sighing—“Perhaps I shall some time look back to these moments, as to\par
the summit of my happiness, with hopeless regret. But let me not misuse\par
them by useless anticipation; let me hope I shall not live to mourn the\par
loss of those who are dearer to me than life.”\par
\par
To relieve, or perhaps to indulge, the pensive temper of his mind, he\par
bade Emily fetch the lute she knew how to touch with such sweet pathos.\par
As she drew near the fishing-house, she was surprised to hear the tones\par
of the instrument, which were awakened by the hand of taste, and\par
uttered a plaintive air, whose exquisite melody engaged all her\par
attention. She listened in profound silence, afraid to move from the\par
spot, lest the sound of her steps should occasion her to lose a note of\par
the music, or should disturb the musician. Everything without the\par
building was still, and no person appeared. She continued to listen,\par
till timidity succeeded to surprise and delight; a timidity, increased\par
by a remembrance of the pencilled lines she had formerly seen, and she\par
hesitated whether to proceed, or to return.\par
\par
While she paused, the music ceased; and, after a momentary hesitation,\par
she recollected courage to advance to the fishing-house, which she\par
entered with faltering steps, and found unoccupied! Her lute lay on the\par
table; everything seemed undisturbed, and she began to believe it was\par
another instrument she had heard, till she remembered, that, when she\par
followed M. and Madame St. Aubert from this spot, her lute was left on\par
a window seat. She felt alarmed, yet knew not wherefore; the melancholy\par
gloom of evening, and the profound stillness of the place, interrupted\par
only by the light trembling of leaves, heightened her fanciful\par
apprehensions, and she was desirous of quitting the building, but\par
perceived herself grow faint, and sat down. As she tried to recover\par
herself, the pencilled lines on the wainscot met her eye; she started,\par
as if she had seen a stranger; but, endeavouring to conquer the tremor\par
of her spirits, rose, and went to the window. To the lines before\par
noticed she now perceived that others were added, in which her name\par
appeared.\par
\par
Though no longer suffered to doubt that they were addressed to herself,\par
she was as ignorant, as before, by whom they could be written. While\par
she mused, she thought she heard the sound of a step without the\par
building, and again alarmed, she caught up her lute, and hurried away.\par
Monsieur and Madame St. Aubert she found in a little path that wound\par
along the sides of the glen.\par
\par
Having reached a green summit, shadowed by palm-trees, and overlooking\par
the valleys and plains of Gascony, they seated themselves on the turf;\par
and while their eyes wandered over the glorious scene, and they inhaled\par
the sweet breath of flowers and herbs that enriched the grass, Emily\par
played and sung several of their favourite airs, with the delicacy of\par
expression in which she so much excelled.\par
\par
Music and conversation detained them in this enchanting spot, till the\par
sun’s last light slept upon the plains; till the white sails that\par
glided beneath the mountains, where the Garonne wandered, became dim,\par
and the gloom of evening stole over the landscape. It was a melancholy\par
but not unpleasing gloom. St. Aubert and his family rose, and left the\par
place with regret; alas! Madame St. Aubert knew not that she left it\par
for ever.\par
\par
When they reached the fishing-house she missed her bracelet, and\par
recollected that she had taken it from her arm after dinner, and had\par
left it on the table when she went to walk. After a long search, in\par
which Emily was very active, she was compelled to resign herself to the\par
loss of it. What made this bracelet valuable to her was a miniature of\par
her daughter to which it was attached, esteemed a striking resemblance,\par
and which had been painted only a few months before. When Emily was\par
convinced that the bracelet was really gone, she blushed, and became\par
thoughtful. That some stranger had been in the fishing-house, during\par
her absence, her lute, and the additional lines of a pencil, had\par
already informed her: from the purport of these lines it was not\par
unreasonable to believe, that the poet, the musician, and the thief\par
were the same person. But though the music she had heard, the written\par
lines she had seen, and the disappearance of the picture, formed a\par
combination of circumstances very remarkable, she was irresistibly\par
restrained from mentioning them; secretly determining, however, never\par
again to visit the fishing-house without Monsieur or Madame St. Aubert.\par
\par
They returned pensively to the château, Emily musing on the incident\par
which had just occurred; St. Aubert reflecting, with placid gratitude,\par
on the blessings he possessed; and Madame St. Aubert somewhat\par
disturbed, and perplexed, by the loss of her daughter’s picture. As\par
they drew near the house, they observed an unusual bustle about it; the\par
sound of voices was distinctly heard, servants and horses were seen\par
passing between the trees, and, at length, the wheels of a carriage\par
rolled along. Having come within view of the front of the château, a\par
landau, with smoking horses, appeared on the little lawn before it. St.\par
Aubert perceived the liveries of his brother-in-law, and in the parlour\par
he found Monsieur and Madame Quesnel already entered. They had left\par
Paris some days before, and were on the way to their estate, only ten\par
leagues distant from La Vallée, and which Monsieur Quesnel had\par
purchased several years before of St. Aubert. This gentleman was the\par
only brother of Madame St. Aubert; but the ties of relationship having\par
never been strengthened by congeniality of character, the intercourse\par
between them had not been frequent. M. Quesnel had lived altogether in\par
the world; his aim had been consequence; splendour was the object of\par
his taste; and his address and knowledge of character had carried him\par
forward to the attainment of almost all that he had courted. By a man\par
of such a disposition, it is not surprising that the virtues of St.\par
Aubert should be overlooked; or that his pure taste, simplicity, and\par
moderated wishes, were considered as marks of a weak intellect, and of\par
confined views. The marriage of his sister with St. Aubert had been\par
mortifying to his ambition, for he had designed that the matrimonial\par
connection she formed should assist him to attain the consequence which\par
he so much desired; and some offers were made her by persons whose rank\par
and fortune flattered his warmest hope. But his sister, who was then\par
addressed also by St. Aubert, perceived, or thought she perceived, that\par
happiness and splendour were not the same, and she did not hesitate to\par
forego the last for the attainment of the former. Whether Monsieur\par
Quesnel thought them the same, or not, he would readily have sacrificed\par
his sister’s peace to the gratification of his own ambition; and, on\par
her marriage with St. Aubert, expressed in private his contempt of her\par
spiritless conduct, and of the connection which it permitted. Madame\par
St. Aubert, though she concealed this insult from her husband, felt,\par
perhaps, for the first time, resentment lighted in her heart; and,\par
though a regard for her own dignity, united with considerations of\par
prudence, restrained her expression of this resentment, there was ever\par
after a mild reserve in her manner towards M. Quesnel, which he both\par
understood and felt.\par
\par
In his own marriage he did not follow his sister’s example. His lady\par
was an Italian, and an heiress by birth; and, by nature and education,\par
was a vain and frivolous woman.\par
\par
They now determined to pass the night with St. Aubert; and as the\par
château was not large enough to accommodate their servants, the latter\par
were dismissed to the neighbouring village. When the first compliments\par
were over, and the arrangements for the night made M. Quesnel began the\par
display of his intelligence and his connections; while St. Aubert, who\par
had been long enough in retirement to find these topics recommended by\par
their novelty, listened, with a degree of patience and attention, which\par
his guest mistook for the humility of wonder. The latter, indeed,\par
described the few festivities which the turbulence of that period\par
permitted to the court of Henry the Third, with a minuteness, that\par
somewhat recompensed for his ostentation; but, when he came to speak of\par
the character of the Duke de Joyeuse, of a secret treaty, which he knew\par
to be negotiating with the Porte, and of the light in which Henry of\par
Navarre was received, M. St. Aubert recollected enough of his former\par
experience to be assured, that his guest could be only of an inferior\par
class of politicians; and that, from the importance of the subjects\par
upon which he committed himself, he could not be of the rank to which\par
he pretended to belong. The opinions delivered by M. Quesnel, were such\par
as St. Aubert forebore to reply to, for he knew that his guest had\par
neither humanity to feel, nor discernment to perceive, what is just.\par
\par
Madame Quesnel, meanwhile, was expressing to Madame St. Aubert her\par
astonishment, that she could bear to pass her life in this remote\par
corner of the world, as she called it, and describing, from a wish,\par
probably, of exciting envy, the splendour of the balls, banquets, and\par
processions which had just been given by the court, in honour of the\par
nuptials of the Duke de Joyeuse with Margaretta of Lorrain, the sister\par
of the Queen. She described with equal minuteness the magnificence she\par
had seen, and that from which she had been excluded; while Emily’s\par
vivid fancy, as she listened with the ardent curiosity of youth,\par
heightened the scenes she heard of; and Madame St. Aubert, looking on\par
her family, felt, as a tear stole to her eye, that though splendour may\par
grace happiness, virtue only can bestow it.\par
\par
“It is now twelve years, St. Aubert,” said M. Quesnel, “since I\par
purchased your family estate.”—“Somewhere thereabout,” replied St.\par
Aubert, suppressing a sigh. “It is near five years since I have been\par
there,” resumed Quesnel; “for Paris and its neighbourhood is the only\par
place in the world to live in, and I am so immersed in politics, and\par
have so many affairs of moment on my hands, that I find it difficult to\par
steal away even for a month or two.” St. Aubert remaining silent, M.\par
Quesnel proceeded: “I have sometimes wondered how you, who have lived\par
in the capital, and have been accustomed to company, can exist\par
elsewhere;—especially in so remote a country as this, where you can\par
neither hear nor see anything, and can in short be scarcely conscious\par
of life.”\par
\par
“I live for my family and myself,” said St. Aubert; “I am now contented\par
to know only happiness;—formerly I knew life.”\par
\par
“I mean to expend thirty or forty thousand livres on improvements,”\par
said M. Quesnel, without seeming to notice the words of St. Aubert;\par
“for I design, next summer, to bring here my friends, the Duke de\par
Durefort and the Marquis Ramont, to pass a month or two with me.” To\par
St. Aubert’s enquiry, as to these intended improvements, he replied,\par
that he should take down the whole east wing of the château, and raise\par
upon the site a set of stables. “Then I shall build,” said he, “a\par
_salle à manger_, a _salon_, a _salle au commune_, and a number of\par
rooms for servants; for at present there is not accommodation for a\par
third part of my own people.”\par
\par
“It accommodated our father’s household,” said St. Aubert, grieved that\par
the old mansion was to be thus improved, “and that was not a small\par
one.”\par
\par
“Our notions are somewhat enlarged since those days,” said M.\par
Quesnel;—“what was then thought a decent style of living would not now\par
be endured.” Even the calm St. Aubert blushed at these words, but his\par
anger soon yielded to contempt. “The ground about the château is\par
encumbered with trees; I mean to cut some of them down.”\par
\par
“Cut down the trees too!” said St. Aubert.\par
\par
“Certainly. Why should I not? they interrupt my prospects. There is a\par
chesnut which spreads its branches before the whole south side of the\par
château, and which is so ancient that they tell me the hollow of its\par
trunk will hold a dozen men. Your enthusiasm will scarcely contend that\par
there can be either use, or beauty, in such a sapless old tree as\par
this.”\par
\par
“Good God!” exclaimed St. Aubert, “you surely will not destroy that\par
noble chesnut, which has flourished for centuries, the glory of the\par
estate! It was in its maturity when the present mansion was built. How\par
often, in my youth, have I climbed among its broad branches, and sat\par
embowered amidst a world of leaves, while the heavy shower has pattered\par
above, and not a rain drop reached me! How often I have sat with a book\par
in my hand, sometimes reading, and sometimes looking out between the\par
branches upon the wide landscape, and the setting sun, till twilight\par
came, and brought the birds home to their little nests among the\par
leaves! How often—but pardon me,” added St. Aubert, recollecting that\par
he was speaking to a man who could neither comprehend, nor allow his\par
feelings, “I am talking of times and feelings as old-fashioned as the\par
taste that would spare that venerable tree.”\par
\par
“It will certainly come down,” said M. Quesnel; “I believe I shall\par
plant some Lombardy poplars among the clumps of chesnut, that I shall\par
leave of the avenue; Madame Quesnel is partial to the poplar, and tells\par
me how much it adorns a villa of her uncle, not far from Venice.”\par
\par
“On the banks of the Brenta, indeed,” continued St. Aubert, “where its\par
spiry form is intermingled with the pine, and the cypress, and where it\par
plays over light and elegant porticos and colonnades, it,\par
unquestionably, adorns the scene; but among the giants of the forest,\par
and near a heavy gothic mansion—”\par
\par
“Well, my good sir,” said M. Quesnel, “I will not dispute with you. You\par
must return to Paris before our ideas can at all agree. But _à propos_\par
of Venice, I have some thoughts of going thither, next summer; events\par
may call me to take possession of that same villa, too, which they tell\par
me is the most charming that can be imagined. In that case I shall\par
leave the improvements I mention to another year, and I may, perhaps,\par
be tempted to stay some time in Italy.”\par
\par
Emily was somewhat surprised to hear him talk of being tempted to\par
remain abroad, after he had mentioned his presence to be so necessary\par
at Paris, that it was with difficulty he could steal away for a month\par
or two; but St. Aubert understood the self-importance of the man too\par
well to wonder at this trait; and the possibility, that these projected\par
improvements might be deferred, gave him a hope, that they might never\par
take place.\par
\par
Before they separated for the night, M. Quesnel desired to speak with\par
St. Aubert alone, and they retired to another room, where they remained\par
a considerable time. The subject of this conversation was not known;\par
but, whatever it might be, St. Aubert, when he returned to the\par
supper-room, seemed much disturbed, and a shade of sorrow sometimes\par
fell upon his features that alarmed Madame St. Aubert. When they were\par
alone she was tempted to enquire the occasion of it, but the delicacy\par
of mind, which had ever appeared in his conduct, restrained her: she\par
considered that, if St. Aubert wished her to be acquainted with the\par
subject of his concern, he would not wait on her enquiries.\par
\par
On the following day, before M. Quesnel departed, he had a second\par
conference with St. Aubert.\par
\par
The guests, after dining at the château, set out in the cool of the day\par
for Epourville, whither they gave him and Madame St. Aubert a pressing\par
invitation, prompted rather by the vanity of displaying their\par
splendour, than by a wish to make their friends happy.\par
\par
Emily returned, with delight, to the liberty which their presence had\par
restrained, to her books, her walks, and the rational conversation of\par
M. and Madame St. Aubert, who seemed to rejoice, no less, that they\par
were delivered from the shackles, which arrogance and frivolity had\par
imposed.\par
\par
Madame St. Aubert excused herself from sharing their usual evening\par
walk, complaining that she was not quite well, and St. Aubert and Emily\par
went out together.\par
\par
They chose a walk towards the mountains, intending to visit some old\par
pensioners of St. Aubert, which, from his very moderate income, he\par
contrived to support, though it is probable M. Quesnel, with his very\par
large one, could not have afforded this.\par
\par
After distributing to his pensioners their weekly stipends, listening\par
patiently to the complaints of some, redressing the grievances of\par
others, and softening the discontents of all, by the look of sympathy,\par
and the smile of benevolence, St. Aubert returned home through the\par
woods,\par
\par
where,\par
At fall of eve the fairy-people throng,\par
In various games and revelry to pass\par
The summer night, as village stories tell.\par
                    THOMSON\par
\par
\par
“The evening gloom of woods was always delightful to me,” said St.\par
Aubert, whose mind now experienced the sweet calm, which results from\par
the consciousness of having done a beneficent action, and which\par
disposes it to receive pleasure from every surrounding object. “I\par
remember that in my youth this gloom used to call forth to my fancy a\par
thousand fairy visions, and romantic images; and, I own, I am not yet\par
wholly insensible of that high enthusiasm, which wakes the poet’s\par
dream: I can linger, with solemn steps, under the deep shades, send\par
forward a transforming eye into the distant obscurity, and listen with\par
thrilling delight to the mystic murmuring of the woods.”\par
\par
“O my dear father,” said Emily, while a sudden tear started to her eye,\par
“how exactly you describe what I have felt so often, and which I\par
thought nobody had ever felt but myself! But hark! here comes the\par
sweeping sound over the wood-tops;—now it dies away;—how solemn the\par
stillness that succeeds! Now the breeze swells again. It is like the\par
voice of some supernatural being—the voice of the spirit of the woods,\par
that watches over them by night. Ah! what light is yonder? But it is\par
gone. And now it gleams again, near the root of that large chestnut:\par
look, sir!”\par
\par
“Are you such an admirer of nature,” said St. Aubert, “and so little\par
acquainted with her appearances as not to know that for the glow-worm?\par
But come,” added he gaily, “step a little further, and we shall see\par
fairies, perhaps; they are often companions. The glow-worm lends his\par
light, and they in return charm him with music, and the dance. Do you\par
see nothing tripping yonder?”\par
\par
Emily laughed. “Well, my dear sir,” said she, “since you allow of this\par
alliance, I may venture to own I have anticipated you; and almost dare\par
venture to repeat some verses I made one evening in these very woods.”\par
\par
“Nay,” replied St. Aubert, “dismiss the _almost_, and venture quite;\par
let us hear what vagaries fancy has been playing in your mind. If she\par
has given you one of her spells, you need not envy those of the\par
fairies.”\par
\par
“If it is strong enough to enchant your judgment, sir,” said Emily,\par
“while I disclose her images, I need _not_ envy them. The lines go in a\par
sort of tripping measure, which I thought might suit the subject well\par
enough, but I fear they are too irregular.”\par
\par
THE GLOW-WORM.\par
\par
How pleasant is the green-wood’s deep-matted shade\par
On a mid-summer’s eve, when the fresh rain is o’er;\par
When the yellow beams slope, and sparkle thro’ the glade,\par
And swiftly in the thin air the light swallows soar!\par
\par
But sweeter, sweeter still, when the sun sinks to rest,\par
And twilight comes on, with the fairies so gay\par
Tripping through the forest-walk, where flow’rs, unprest,\par
Bow not their tall heads beneath their frolic play.\par
\par
To music’s softest sounds they dance away the hour,\par
Till moonlight steals down among the trembling leaves,\par
And checquers all the ground, and guides them to the bow’r,\par
The long haunted bow’r, where the nightingale grieves.\par
\par
Then no more they dance, till her sad song is done,\par
But, silent as the night, to her mourning attend;\par
And often as her dying notes their pity have won,\par
They vow all her sacred haunts from mortals to defend.\par
\par
When, down among the mountains, sinks the ev’ning star,\par
And the changing moon forsakes this shadowy sphere,\par
How cheerless would they be, tho’ they fairies are,\par
If I, with my pale light, came not near!\par
\par
Yet cheerless tho’ they’d be, they’re ungrateful to my love!\par
For, often when the traveller’s benighted on his way,\par
And I glimmer in his path, and would guide him thro’ the grove,\par
They bind me in their magic spells to lead him far astray;\par
\par
And in the mire to leave him, till the stars are all burnt out,\par
While, in strange-looking shapes, they frisk about the ground,\par
And, afar in the woods, they raise a dismal shout,\par
Till I shrink into my cell again for terror of the sound!\par
\par
But, see where all the tiny elves come dancing in a ring,\par
With the merry, merry pipe, and the tabor, and the horn,\par
And the timbrel so clear, and the lute with dulcet string;\par
Then round about the oak they go till peeping of the morn.\par
\par
Down yonder glade two lovers steal, to shun the fairy-queen,\par
Who frowns upon their plighted vows, and jealous is of me,\par
That yester-eve I lighted them, along the dewy green,\par
To seek the purple flow’r, whose juice from all her spells can free.\par
\par
And now, to punish me, she keeps afar her jocund band,\par
With the merry, merry pipe, and the tabor, and the lute;\par
If I creep near yonder oak she will wave her fairy wand,\par
And to me the dance will cease, and the music all be mute.\par
\par
O! had I but that purple flow’r whose leaves her charms can foil,\par
And knew like fays to draw the juice, and throw it on the wind,\par
I’d be her slave no longer, nor the traveller beguile,\par
And help all faithful lovers, nor fear the fairy kind!\par
\par
But soon the _vapour of the woods_ will wander afar,\par
And the fickle moon will fade, and the stars disappear,\par
Then, cheerless will they be, tho’ they fairies are,\par
If I, with my pale light, come not near!\par
\par
\par
Whatever St. Aubert might think of the stanzas, he would not deny his\par
daughter the pleasure of believing that he approved them; and, having\par
given his commendation, he sunk into a reverie, and they walked on in\par
silence.\par
\par
A faint erroneous ray,\par
Glanc’d from th’ imperfect surfaces of things,\par
Flung half an image on the straining eye;\par
While waving woods, and villages, and streams,\par
And rocks, and mountain-tops, that long retain\par
The ascending gleam, are all one swimming scene,\par
Uncertain if beheld.\par
                    THOMSON.\par
\par
\par
St. Aubert continued silent till he reached the château, where his wife\par
had retired to her chamber. The languor and dejection, that had lately\par
oppressed her, and which the exertion called forth by the arrival of\par
her guests had suspended, now returned with increased effect. On the\par
following day, symptoms of fever appeared, and St. Aubert, having sent\par
for medical advice, learned, that her disorder was a fever of the same\par
nature as that, from which he had lately recovered. She had, indeed,\par
taken the infection, during her attendance upon him, and, her\par
constitution being too weak to throw out the disease immediately, it\par
had lurked in her veins, and occasioned the heavy languor of which she\par
had complained. St. Aubert, whose anxiety for his wife overcame every\par
other consideration, detained the physician in his house. He remembered\par
the feelings and the reflections that had called a momentary gloom upon\par
his mind, on the day when he had last visited the fishing-house, in\par
company with Madame St. Aubert, and he now admitted a presentiment,\par
that this illness would be a fatal one. But he effectually concealed\par
this from her, and from his daughter, whom he endeavoured to reanimate\par
with hopes that her constant assiduities would not be unavailing. The\par
physician, when asked by St. Aubert for his opinion of the disorder,\par
replied, that the event of it depended upon circumstances which he\par
could not ascertain. Madame St. Aubert seemed to have formed a more\par
decided one; but her eyes only gave hints of this. She frequently fixed\par
them upon her anxious friends with an expression of pity, and of\par
tenderness, as if she anticipated the sorrow that awaited them, and\par
that seemed to say, it was for their sakes only, for their sufferings,\par
that she regretted life. On the seventh day, the disorder was at its\par
crisis. The physician assumed a graver manner, which she observed, and\par
took occasion, when her family had once quitted the chamber, to tell\par
him, that she perceived her death was approaching. “Do not attempt to\par
deceive me,” said she, “I feel that I cannot long survive. I am\par
prepared for the event, I have long, I hope, been preparing for it.\par
Since I have not long to live, do not suffer a mistaken compassion to\par
induce you to flatter my family with false hopes. If you do, their\par
affliction will only be the heavier when it arrives: I will endeavour\par
to teach them resignation by my example.”\par
\par
The physician was affected; he promised to obey her, and told St.\par
Aubert, somewhat abruptly, that there was nothing to expect. The latter\par
was not philosopher enough to restrain his feelings when he received\par
this information; but a consideration of the increased affliction which\par
the observance of his grief would occasion his wife, enabled him, after\par
some time, to command himself in her presence. Emily was at first\par
overwhelmed with the intelligence; then, deluded by the strength of her\par
wishes, a hope sprung up in her mind that her mother would yet recover,\par
and to this she pertinaciously adhered almost to the last hour.\par
\par
The progress of this disorder was marked, on the side of Madame St.\par
Aubert, by patient suffering, and subjected wishes. The composure, with\par
which she awaited her death, could be derived only from the retrospect\par
of a life governed, as far as human frailty permits, by a consciousness\par
of being always in the presence of the Deity, and by the hope of a\par
higher world. But her piety could not entirely subdue the grief of\par
parting from those whom she so dearly loved. During these her last\par
hours, she conversed much with St. Aubert and Emily, on the prospect of\par
futurity, and on other religious topics. The resignation she expressed,\par
with the firm hope of meeting in a future world the friends she left in\par
this, and the effort which sometimes appeared to conceal her sorrow at\par
this temporary separation, frequently affected St. Aubert so much as to\par
oblige him to leave the room. Having indulged his tears a while, he\par
would dry them and return to the chamber with a countenance composed by\par
an endeavour which did but increase his grief.\par
\par
Never had Emily felt the importance of the lessons, which had taught\par
her to restrain her sensibility, so much as in these moments, and never\par
had she practised them with a triumph so complete. But when the last\par
was over, she sunk at once under the pressure of her sorrow, and then\par
perceived that it was hope, as well as fortitude, which had hitherto\par
supported her. St. Aubert was for a time too devoid of comfort himself\par
to bestow any on his daughter.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER II\par
\par
I could a tale unfold, whose lightest word\par
Would harrow up thy soul.\par
                    SHAKESPEARE\par
\par
\par
Madame St. Aubert was interred in the neighbouring village church; her\par
husband and daughter attended her to the grave, followed by a long\par
train of the peasantry, who were sincere mourners of this excellent\par
woman.\par
\par
On his return from the funeral, St. Aubert shut himself in his chamber.\par
When he came forth, it was with a serene countenance, though pale in\par
sorrow. He gave orders that his family should attend him. Emily only\par
was absent; who, overcome with the scene she had just witnessed, had\par
retired to her closet to weep alone. St. Aubert followed her thither:\par
he took her hand in silence, while she continued to weep; and it was\par
some moments before he could so far command his voice as to speak. It\par
trembled while he said, “My Emily, I am going to prayers with my\par
family; you will join us. We must ask support from above. Where else\par
ought we to seek it—where else can we find it?”\par
\par
Emily checked her tears, and followed her father to the parlour, where,\par
the servants being assembled, St. Aubert read, in a low and solemn\par
voice, the evening service, and added a prayer for the soul of the\par
departed. During this, his voice often faltered, his tears fell upon\par
the book, and at length he paused. But the sublime emotions of pure\par
devotion gradually elevated his views above this world, and finally\par
brought comfort to his heart.\par
\par
When the service was ended, and the servants were withdrawn, he\par
tenderly kissed Emily, and said, “I have endeavoured to teach you, from\par
your earliest youth, the duty of self-command; I have pointed out to\par
you the great importance of it through life, not only as it preserves\par
us in the various and dangerous temptations that call us from rectitude\par
and virtue, but as it limits the indulgences which are termed virtuous,\par
yet which, extended beyond a certain boundary, are vicious, for their\par
consequence is evil. All excess is vicious; even that sorrow, which is\par
amiable in its origin, becomes a selfish and unjust passion, if\par
indulged at the expence of our duties—by our duties I mean what we owe\par
to ourselves, as well as to others. The indulgence of excessive grief\par
enervates the mind, and almost incapacitates it for again partaking of\par
those various innocent enjoyments which a benevolent God designed to be\par
the sunshine of our lives. My dear Emily, recollect and practise the\par
precepts I have so often given you, and which your own experience has\par
so often shown you to be wise.\par
\par
“Your sorrow is useless. Do not receive this as merely a commonplace\par
remark, but let reason _therefore_ restrain sorrow. I would not\par
annihilate your feelings, my child, I would only teach you to command\par
them; for whatever may be the evils resulting from a too susceptible\par
heart, nothing can be hoped from an insensible one; that, on the other\par
hand, is all vice—vice, of which the deformity is not softened, or the\par
effect consoled for, by any semblance or possibility of good. You know\par
my sufferings, and are, therefore, convinced that mine are not the\par
light words which, on these occasions, are so often repeated to destroy\par
even the sources of honest emotion, or which merely display the selfish\par
ostentation of a false philosophy. I will show my Emily, that I can\par
practise what I advise. I have said thus much, because I cannot bear to\par
see you wasting in useless sorrow, for want of that resistance which is\par
due from mind; and I have not said it till now, because there is a\par
period when all reasoning must yield to nature; that is past: and\par
another, when excessive indulgence, having sunk into habit, weighs down\par
the elasticity of the spirits so as to render conquest nearly\par
impossible; this is to come. You, my Emily, will show that you are\par
willing to avoid it.”\par
\par
Emily smiled through her tears upon her father: “Dear sir,” said she,\par
and her voice trembled; she would have added, “I will show myself\par
worthy of being your daughter;” but a mingled emotion of gratitude,\par
affection, and grief overcame her. St. Aubert suffered her to weep\par
without interruption, and then began to talk on common topics.\par
\par
The first person who came to condole with St. Aubert was a M. Barreaux,\par
an austere and seemingly unfeeling man. A taste for botany had\par
introduced them to each other, for they had frequently met in their\par
wanderings among the mountains. M. Barreaux had retired from the world,\par
and almost from society, to live in a pleasant château, on the skirts\par
of the woods, near La Vallée. He also had been disappointed in his\par
opinion of mankind; but he did not, like St. Aubert, pity and mourn for\par
them; he felt more indignation at their vices, than compassion for\par
their weaknesses.\par
\par
St. Aubert was somewhat surprised to see him; for, though he had often\par
pressed him to come to the château, he had never till now accepted the\par
invitation; and now he came without ceremony or reserve, entering the\par
parlour as an old friend. The claims of misfortune appeared to have\par
softened down all the ruggedness and prejudices of his heart. St.\par
Aubert unhappy, seemed to be the sole idea that occupied his mind. It\par
was in manners, more than in words, that he appeared to sympathise with\par
his friends: he spoke little on the subject of their grief; but the\par
minute attention he gave them, and the modulated voice, and softened\par
look that accompanied it, came from his heart, and spoke to theirs.\par
\par
At this melancholy period St. Aubert was likewise visited by Madame\par
Cheron, his only surviving sister, who had been some years a widow, and\par
now resided on her own estate near Thoulouse. The intercourse between\par
them had not been very frequent. In her condolements, words were not\par
wanting; she understood not the magic of the look that speaks at once\par
to the soul, or the voice that sinks like balm to the heart: but she\par
assured St. Aubert that she sincerely sympathised with him, praised the\par
virtues of his late wife, and then offered what she considered to be\par
consolation. Emily wept unceasingly while she spoke; St. Aubert was\par
tranquil, listened to what she said in silence, and then turned the\par
discourse upon another subject.\par
\par
At parting she pressed him and her niece to make her an early visit.\par
“Change of place will amuse you,” said she, “and it is wrong to give\par
way to grief.” St. Aubert acknowledged the truth of these words of\par
course; but, at the same time, felt more reluctant than ever to quit\par
the spot which his past happiness had consecrated. The presence of his\par
wife had sanctified every surrounding scene, and, each day, as it\par
gradually softened the acuteness of his suffering, assisted the tender\par
enchantment that bound him to home.\par
\par
But there were calls which must be complied with, and of this kind was\par
the visit he paid to his brother-in-law M. Quesnel. An affair of an\par
interesting nature made it necessary that he should delay this visit no\par
longer, and, wishing to rouse Emily from her dejection, he took her\par
with him to Epourville.\par
\par
As the carriage entered upon the forest that adjoined his paternal\par
domain, his eyes once more caught, between the chesnut avenue, the\par
turreted corners of the château. He sighed to think of what had passed\par
since he was last there, and that it was now the property of a man who\par
neither revered nor valued it. At length he entered the avenue, whose\par
lofty trees had so often delighted him when a boy, and whose melancholy\par
shade was now so congenial with the tone of his spirits. Every feature\par
of the edifice, distinguished by an air of heavy grandeur, appeared\par
successively between the branches of the trees—the broad turret, the\par
arched gateway that led into the courts, the drawbridge, and the dry\par
fossé which surrounded the whole.\par
\par
The sound of carriage wheels brought a troop of servants to the great\par
gate, where St. Aubert alighted, and from which he led Emily into the\par
gothic hall, now no longer hung with the arms and ancient banners of\par
the family. These were displaced, and the oak wainscotting, and beams\par
that crossed the roof, were painted white. The large table, too, that\par
used to stretch along the upper end of the hall, where the master of\par
the mansion loved to display his hospitality, and whence the peal of\par
laughter, and the song of conviviality had so often resounded, was now\par
removed; even the benches that had surrounded the hall were no longer\par
there. The heavy walls were hung with frivolous ornaments, and\par
everything that appeared denoted the false taste and corrupted\par
sentiments of the present owner.\par
\par
St. Aubert followed a gay Parisian servant to a parlour, where sat\par
Mons. and Madame Quesnel, who received him with a stately politeness,\par
and, after a few formal words of condolement, seemed to have forgotten\par
that they ever had a sister.\par
\par
Emily felt tears swell into her eyes, and then resentment checked them.\par
St. Aubert, calm and deliberate, preserved his dignity without assuming\par
importance, and Quesnel was depressed by his presence without exactly\par
knowing wherefore.\par
\par
After some general conversation, St. Aubert requested to speak with him\par
alone; and Emily, being left with Madame Quesnel, soon learned that a\par
large party was invited to dine at the château, and was compelled to\par
hear that nothing which was past and irremediable ought to prevent the\par
festivity of the present hour.\par
\par
St. Aubert, when he was told that company were expected, felt a mixed\par
emotion of disgust and indignation against the insensibility of\par
Quesnel, which prompted him to return home immediately. But he was\par
informed, that Madame Cheron had been asked to meet him; and, when he\par
looked at Emily, and considered that a time might come when the enmity\par
of her uncle would be prejudicial to her, he determined not to incur it\par
himself, by conduct which would be resented as indecorous, by the very\par
persons who now showed so little sense of decorum.\par
\par
Among the visitors assembled at dinner were two Italian gentlemen, of\par
whom one was named Montoni, a distant relation of Madame Quesnel, a man\par
about forty, of an uncommonly handsome person, with features manly and\par
expressive, but whose countenance exhibited, upon the whole, more of\par
the haughtiness of command, and the quickness of discernment, than of\par
any other character.\par
\par
Signor Cavigni, his friend, appeared to be about thirty—inferior in\par
dignity, but equal to him in penetration of countenance, and superior\par
in insinuation of manner.\par
\par
Emily was shocked by the salutation with which Madame Cheron met her\par
father—“Dear brother,” said she, “I am concerned to see you look so\par
very ill; do, pray, have advice!” St. Aubert answered, with a\par
melancholy smile, that he felt himself much as usual; but Emily’s fears\par
made her now fancy that her father looked worse than he really did.\par
\par
Emily would have been amused by the new characters she saw, and the\par
varied conversation that passed during dinner, which was served in a\par
style of splendour she had seldom seen before, had her spirits been\par
less oppressed. Of the guests, Signor Montoni was lately come from\par
Italy, and he spoke of the commotions which at that period agitated the\par
country; talked of party differences with warmth, and then lamented the\par
probable consequences of the tumults. His friend spoke with equal\par
ardour, of the politics of his country; praised the government and\par
prosperity of Venice, and boasted of its decided superiority over all\par
the other Italian states. He then turned to the ladies, and talked with\par
the same eloquence, of Parisian fashions, the French opera, and French\par
manners; and on the latter subject he did not fail to mingle what is so\par
particularly agreeable to French taste. The flattery was not detected\par
by those to whom it was addressed, though its effect, in producing\par
submissive attention, did not escape his observation. When he could\par
disengage himself from the assiduities of the other ladies, he\par
sometimes addressed Emily: but she knew nothing of Parisian fashions,\par
or Parisian operas; and her modesty, simplicity, and correct manners\par
formed a decided contrast to those of her female companions.\par
\par
After dinner, St. Aubert stole from the room to view once more the old\par
chesnut which Quesnel talked of cutting down. As he stood under its\par
shade, and looked up among its branches, still luxuriant, and saw here\par
and there the blue sky trembling between them; the pursuits and events\par
of his early days crowded fast to his mind, with the figures and\par
characters of friends—long since gone from the earth; and he now felt\par
himself to be almost an insulated being, with nobody but his Emily for\par
his heart to turn to.\par
\par
He stood lost amid the scenes of years which fancy called up, till the\par
succession closed with the picture of his dying wife, and he started\par
away, to forget it, if possible, at the social board.\par
\par
St. Aubert ordered his carriage at an early hour, and Emily observed,\par
that he was more than usually silent and dejected on the way home; but\par
she considered this to be the effect of his visit to a place which\par
spoke so eloquently of former times, nor suspected that he had a cause\par
of grief which he concealed from her.\par
\par
On entering the château she felt more depressed than ever, for she more\par
than ever missed the presence of that dear parent, who, whenever she\par
had been from home, used to welcome her return with smiles and\par
fondness; now, all was silent and forsaken.\par
\par
But what reason and effort may fail to do, time effects. Week after\par
week passed away, and each, as it passed, stole something from the\par
harshness of her affliction, till it was mellowed to that tenderness\par
which the feeling heart cherishes as sacred. St. Aubert, on the\par
contrary, visibly declined in health; though Emily, who had been so\par
constantly with him, was almost the last person who observed it. His\par
constitution had never recovered from the late attack of the fever, and\par
the succeeding shock it received from Madame St. Aubert’s death had\par
produced its present infirmity. His physician now ordered him to\par
travel; for it was perceptible that sorrow had seized upon his nerves,\par
weakened as they had been by the preceding illness; and variety of\par
scene, it was probable, would, by amusing his mind, restore them to\par
their proper tone.\par
\par
For some days Emily was occupied in preparations to attend him; and he,\par
by endeavours to diminish his expences at home during the journey—a\par
purpose which determined him at length to dismiss his domestics. Emily\par
seldom opposed her father’s wishes by questions or remonstrances, or\par
she would now have asked why he did not take a servant, and have\par
represented that his infirm health made one almost necessary. But when,\par
on the eve of their departure, she found that he had dismissed Jacques,\par
Francis, and Mary, and detained only Theresa the old housekeeper, she\par
was extremely surprised, and ventured to ask his reason for having done\par
so. “To save expences, my dear,” he replied—“we are going on an\par
expensive excursion.”\par
\par
The physician had prescribed the air of Languedoc and Provence; and St.\par
Aubert determined, therefore, to travel leisurely along the shores of\par
the Mediterranean, towards Provence.\par
\par
They retired early to their chamber on the night before their\par
departure; but Emily had a few books and other things to collect, and\par
the clock had struck twelve before she had finished, or had remembered\par
that some of her drawing instruments, which she meant to take with her,\par
were in the parlour below. As she went to fetch these, she passed her\par
father’s room, and, perceiving the door half open, concluded that he\par
was in his study—for, since the death of Madame St. Aubert, it had been\par
frequently his custom to rise from his restless bed, and go thither to\par
compose his mind. When she was below stairs she looked into this room,\par
but without finding him; and as she returned to her chamber, she tapped\par
at his door, and receiving no answer, stepped softly in, to be certain\par
whether he was there.\par
\par
The room was dark, but a light glimmered through some panes of glass\par
that were placed in the upper part of a closet-door. Emily believed her\par
father to be in the closet, and, surprised that he was up at so late an\par
hour, apprehended he was unwell, and was going to enquire; but,\par
considering that her sudden appearance at this hour might alarm him,\par
she removed her light to the staircase, and then stepped softly to the\par
closet. On looking through the panes of glass, she saw him seated at a\par
small table, with papers before him, some of which he was reading with\par
deep attention and interest, during which he often wept and sobbed\par
aloud. Emily, who had come to the door to learn whether her father was\par
ill, was now detained there by a mixture of curiosity and tenderness.\par
She could not witness his sorrow, without being anxious to know the\par
subject of; and she therefore continued to observe him in silence,\par
concluding that those papers were letters of her late mother. Presently\par
he knelt down, and with a look so solemn as she had seldom seen him\par
assume, and which was mingled with a certain wild expression, that\par
partook more of horror than of any other character, he prayed silently\par
for a considerable time.\par
\par
When he rose, a ghastly paleness was on his countenance. Emily was\par
hastily retiring; but she saw him turn again to the papers, and she\par
stopped. He took from among them a small case, and from thence a\par
miniature picture. The rays of light fell strongly upon it, and she\par
perceived it to be that of a lady, but not of her mother.\par
\par
St. Aubert gazed earnestly and tenderly upon his portrait, put it to\par
his lips, and then to his heart, and sighed with a convulsive force.\par
Emily could scarcely believe what she saw to be real. She never knew\par
till now that he had a picture of any other lady than her mother, much\par
less that he had one which he evidently valued so highly; but having\par
looked repeatedly, to be certain that it was not the resemblance of\par
Madame St. Aubert, she became entirely convinced that it was designed\par
for that of some other person.\par
\par
At length St. Aubert returned the picture to its case; and Emily,\par
recollecting that she was intruding upon his private sorrows, softly\par
withdrew from the chamber.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER III\par
\par
O how canst thou renounce the boundless store\par
Of charms which nature to her vot’ry yields!\par
The warbling woodland, the resounding shore,\par
The pomp of groves, and garniture of fields;\par
All that the genial ray of morning gilds,\par
And all that echoes to the song of even;\par
All that the mountain’s shelt’ring bosom shields,\par
And all the dread magnificence of heaven;\par
O how canst thou renounce, and hope to be forgiven!\par
.....\par
These charms shall work thy soul’s eternal health,\par
And love, and gentleness, and joy, impart.\par
                    THE MINSTREL\par
\par
\par
St. Aubert, instead of taking the more direct road, that ran along the\par
feet of the Pyrenees to Languedoc, chose one that, winding over the\par
heights, afforded more extensive views and greater variety of romantic\par
scenery. He turned a little out of his way to take leave of M.\par
Barreaux, whom he found botanizing in the wood near his château, and\par
who, when he was told the purpose of St. Aubert’s visit, expressed a\par
degree of concern, such as his friend had thought it was scarcely\par
possible for him to feel on any similar occasion. They parted with\par
mutual regret.\par
\par
“If anything could have tempted me from my retirement,” said M.\par
Barreaux, “it would have been the pleasure of accompanying you on this\par
little tour. I do not often offer compliments; you may, therefore,\par
believe me, when I say, that I shall look for your return with\par
impatience.”\par
\par
The travellers proceeded on their journey. As they ascended the\par
heights, St. Aubert often looked back upon the château, in the plain\par
below; tender images crowded to his mind; his melancholy imagination\par
suggested that he should return no more; and though he checked this\par
wandering thought, still he continued to look, till the haziness of\par
distance blended his home with the general landscape, and St. Aubert\par
seemed to\par
\par
Drag at each remove a lengthening chain.\par
\par
\par
He and Emily continued sunk in musing silence for some leagues, from\par
which melancholy reverie Emily first awoke, and her young fancy, struck\par
with the grandeur of the objects around, gradually yielded to\par
delightful impressions. The road now descended into glens, confined by\par
stupendous walls of rock, grey and barren, except where shrubs fringed\par
their summits, or patches of meagre vegetation tinted their recesses,\par
in which the wild goat was frequently browsing. And now, the way led to\par
the lofty cliffs, from whence the landscape was seen extending in all\par
its magnificence.\par
\par
Emily could not restrain her transport as she looked over the pine\par
forests of the mountains upon the vast plains, that, enriched with\par
woods, towns, blushing vines, and plantations of almonds, palms, and\par
olives, stretched along, till their various colours melted in distance\par
into one harmonious hue, that seemed to unite earth with heaven.\par
Through the whole of this glorious scene the majestic Garonne wandered;\par
descending from its source among the Pyrenees, and winding its blue\par
waves towards the Bay of Biscay.\par
\par
The ruggedness of the unfrequented road often obliged the wanderers to\par
alight from their little carriage, but they thought themselves amply\par
repaid for this inconvenience by the grandeur of the scenes; and, while\par
the muleteer led his animals slowly over the broken ground, the\par
travellers had leisure to linger amid these solitudes, and to indulge\par
the sublime reflections, which soften, while they elevate, the heart,\par
and fill it with the certainty of a present God! Still the enjoyment of\par
St. Aubert was touched with that pensive melancholy, which gives to\par
every object a mellower tint, and breathes a sacred charm over all\par
around.\par
\par
They had provided against part of the evil to be encountered from a\par
want of convenient inns, by carrying a stock of provisions in the\par
carriage, so that they might take refreshment on any pleasant spot, in\par
the open air, and pass the nights wherever they should happen to meet\par
with a comfortable cottage. For the mind, also, they had provided, by a\par
work on botany, written by M. Barreaux, and by several of the Latin and\par
Italian poets; while Emily’s pencil enabled her to preserve some of\par
those combinations of forms, which charmed her at every step.\par
\par
The loneliness of the road, where, only now and then, a peasant was\par
seen driving his mule, or some mountaineer-children at play among the\par
rocks, heightened the effect of the scenery. St. Aubert was so much\par
struck with it, that he determined, if he could hear of a road, to\par
penetrate further among the mountains, and, bending his way rather more\par
to the south, to emerge into Rousillon, and coast the Mediterranean\par
along part of that country to Languedoc.\par
\par
Soon after mid-day, they reached the summit of one of those cliffs,\par
which, bright with the verdure of palm-trees, adorn, like gems, the\par
tremendous walls of the rocks, and which overlooked the greater part of\par
Gascony, and part of Languedoc. Here was shade, and the fresh water of\par
a spring, that, gliding among the turf, under the trees, thence\par
precipitated itself from rock to rock, till its dashing murmurs were\par
lost in the abyss, though its white foam was long seen amid the\par
darkness of the pines below.\par
\par
This was a spot well suited for rest, and the travellers alighted to\par
dine, while the mules were unharnessed to browse on the savoury herbs\par
that enriched this summit.\par
\par
It was some time before St. Aubert or Emily could withdraw their\par
attention from the surrounding objects, so as to partake of their\par
little repast. Seated in the shade of the palms, St. Aubert pointed out\par
to her observation the course of the rivers, the situation of great\par
towns, and the boundaries of provinces, which science, rather than the\par
eye, enabled him to describe. Notwithstanding this occupation, when he\par
had talked awhile he suddenly became silent, thoughtful, and tears\par
often swelled to his eyes, which Emily observed, and the sympathy of\par
her own heart told her their cause. The scene before them bore some\par
resemblance, though it was on a much grander scale, to a favourite one\par
of the late Madame St. Aubert, within view of the fishing-house. They\par
both observed this, and thought how delighted she would have been with\par
the present landscape, while they knew that her eyes must never, never\par
more open upon this world. St. Aubert remembered the last time of his\par
visiting that spot in company with her, and also the mournfully\par
presaging thoughts which had then arisen in his mind, and were now,\par
even thus soon, realised! The recollections subdued him, and he\par
abruptly rose from his seat, and walked away to where no eye could\par
observe his grief.\par
\par
When he returned, his countenance had recovered its usual serenity; he\par
took Emily’s hand, pressed it affectionately, without speaking, and\par
soon after called to the muleteer, who sat at a little distance,\par
concerning a road among the mountains towards Rousillon. Michael said,\par
there were several that way, but he did not know how far they extended,\par
or even whether they were passable; and St. Aubert, who did not intend\par
to travel after sunset, asked what village they could reach about that\par
time. The muleteer calculated that they could easily reach Mateau,\par
which was in their present road; but that, if they took a road that\par
sloped more to the south, towards Rousillon, there was a hamlet, which\par
he thought they could gain before the evening shut in.\par
\par
St. Aubert, after some hesitation, determined to take the latter\par
course, and Michael, having finished his meal, and harnessed his mules,\par
again set forward, but soon stopped; and St. Aubert saw him doing\par
homage to a cross, that stood on a rock impending over their way.\par
Having concluded his devotions, he smacked his whip in the air, and, in\par
spite of the rough road, and the pain of his poor mules, which he had\par
been lately lamenting, rattled, in a full gallop, along the edge of a\par
precipice, which it made the eye dizzy to look down. Emily was\par
terrified almost to fainting; and St. Aubert, apprehending still\par
greater danger from suddenly stopping the driver, was compelled to sit\par
quietly, and trust his fate to the strength and discretion of the\par
mules, who seemed to possess a greater portion of the latter quality\par
than their master; for they carried the travellers safely into the\par
valley, and there stopped upon the brink of the rivulet that watered\par
it.\par
\par
Leaving the splendour of extensive prospects, they now entered this\par
narrow valley screened by\par
\par
Rocks on rocks piled, as if by magic spell,\par
Here scorch’d by lightnings, there with ivy green.\par
\par
\par
The scene of barrenness was here and there interrupted by the spreading\par
branches of the larch and cedar, which threw their gloom over the\par
cliff, or athwart the torrent that rolled in the vale. No living\par
creature appeared, except the lizard, scrambling among the rocks, and\par
often hanging upon points so dangerous, that fancy shrunk from the view\par
of them. This was such a scene as _Salvator_ would have chosen, had he\par
then existed, for his canvas; St. Aubert, impressed by the romantic\par
character of the place, almost expected to see banditti start from\par
behind some projecting rock, and he kept his hand upon the arms with\par
which he always travelled.\par
\par
As they advanced, the valley opened; its savage features gradually\par
softened, and, towards evening, they were among heathy mountains,\par
stretched in far perspective, along which the solitary sheep-bell was\par
heard, and the voice of the shepherd calling his wandering flocks to\par
the nightly fold. His cabin, partly shadowed by the cork-tree and the\par
ilex, which St. Aubert observed to flourish in higher regions of the\par
air than any other trees, except the fir, was all the human habitation\par
that yet appeared. Along the bottom of this valley the most vivid\par
verdure was spread; and, in the little hollow recesses of the\par
mountains, under the shade of the oak and chestnut, herds of cattle\par
were grazing. Groups of them, too, were often seen reposing on the\par
banks of the rivulet, or laving their sides in the cool stream, and\par
sipping its wave.\par
\par
The sun was now setting upon the valley; its last light gleamed upon\par
the water, and heightened the rich yellow and purple tints of the heath\par
and broom, that overspread the mountains. St. Aubert enquired of\par
Michael the distance to the hamlet he had mentioned, but the man could\par
not with certainty tell; and Emily began to fear that he had mistaken\par
the road. Here was no human being to assist, or direct them; they had\par
left the shepherd and his cabin far behind, and the scene became so\par
obscured in twilight, that the eye could not follow the distant\par
perspective of the valley in search of a cottage, or a hamlet. A glow\par
of the horizon still marked the west, and this was of some little use\par
to the travellers. Michael seemed endeavouring to keep up his courage\par
by singing; his music, however, was not of a kind to disperse\par
melancholy; he sung, in a sort of chant, one of the most dismal ditties\par
his present auditors had ever heard, and St. Aubert at length\par
discovered it to be a vesper-hymn to his favourite saint.\par
\par
They travelled on, sunk in that thoughtful melancholy, with which\par
twilight and solitude impress the mind. Michael had now ended his\par
ditty, and nothing was heard but the drowsy murmur of the breeze among\par
the woods, and its light flutter, as it blew freshly into the carriage.\par
They were at length roused by the sound of fire-arms. St. Aubert called\par
to the muleteer to stop, and they listened. The noise was not repeated;\par
but presently they heard a rustling among the brakes. St. Aubert drew\par
forth a pistol, and ordered Michael to proceed as fast as possible; who\par
had not long obeyed, before a horn sounded, that made the mountains\par
ring. He looked again from the window, and then saw a young man spring\par
from the bushes into the road, followed by a couple of dogs. The\par
stranger was in a hunter’s dress. His gun was slung across his\par
shoulders, the hunter’s horn hung from his belt, and in his hand was a\par
small pike, which, as he held it, added to the manly grace of his\par
figure, and assisted the agility of his steps.\par
\par
After a moment’s hesitation, St. Aubert again stopped the carriage, and\par
waited till he came up, that they might enquire concerning the hamlet\par
they were in search of. The stranger informed him, that it was only\par
half a league distant, that he was going thither himself, and would\par
readily show the way. St. Aubert thanked him for the offer, and,\par
pleased with his chevalier-like air and open countenance, asked him to\par
take a seat in the carriage; which the stranger, with an\par
acknowledgment, declined, adding that he would keep pace with the\par
mules. “But I fear you will be wretchedly accommodated,” said he: “the\par
inhabitants of these mountains are a simple people, who are not only\par
without the luxuries of life, but almost destitute of what in other\par
places are held to be its necessaries.”\par
\par
“I perceive you are not one of its inhabitants, sir,” said St. Aubert.\par
\par
“No, sir, I am only a wanderer here.”\par
\par
The carriage drove on, and the increasing dusk made the travellers very\par
thankful that they had a guide; the frequent glens, too, that now\par
opened among the mountains, would likewise have added to their\par
perplexity. Emily, as she looked up one of these, saw something at a\par
great distance like a bright cloud in the air. “What light is yonder,\par
sir?” said she.\par
\par
St. Aubert looked, and perceived that it was the snowy summit of a\par
mountain, so much higher than any around it, that it still reflected\par
the sun’s rays, while those below lay in deep shade.\par
\par
At length, the village lights were seen to twinkle through the dusk,\par
and, soon after, some cottages were discovered in the valley, or rather\par
were seen by reflection in the stream, on whose margin they stood, and\par
which still gleamed with the evening light.\par
\par
The stranger now came up, and St. Aubert, on further enquiry, found not\par
only that there was no inn in the place, but not any sort of house of\par
public reception. The stranger, however, offered to walk on, and\par
enquire for a cottage to accommodate them; for which further civility\par
St. Aubert returned his thanks, and said, that, as the village was so\par
near, he would alight, and walk with him. Emily followed slowly in the\par
carriage.\par
\par
On the way, St. Aubert asked his companion what success he had had in\par
the chase. “Not much, sir,” he replied, “nor do I aim at it. I am\par
pleased with the country, and mean to saunter away a few weeks among\par
its scenes. My dogs I take with me more for companionship than for\par
game. This dress, too, gives me an ostensible business, and procures me\par
that respect from the people, which would, perhaps, be refused to a\par
lonely stranger, who had no visible motive for coming among them.”\par
\par
“I admire your taste,” said St. Aubert, “and, if I was a younger man,\par
should like to pass a few weeks in your way exceedingly. I, too, am a\par
wanderer, but neither my plan nor pursuits are exactly like yours—I go\par
in search of health, as much as of amusement.” St. Aubert sighed, and\par
paused; and then, seeming to recollect himself, he resumed: “If I can\par
hear of a tolerable road, that shall afford decent accommodation, it is\par
my intention to pass into Rousillon, and along the sea-shore to\par
Languedoc. You, sir, seem to be acquainted with the country, and can,\par
perhaps, give me information on the subject.”\par
\par
The stranger said, that what information he could give was entirely at\par
his service; and then mentioned a road rather more to the east, which\par
led to a town, whence it would be easy to proceed into Rousillon.\par
\par
They now arrived at the village, and commenced their search for a\par
cottage, that would afford a night’s lodging. In several, which they\par
entered, ignorance, poverty, and mirth seemed equally to prevail; and\par
the owners eyed St. Aubert with a mixture of curiosity and timidity.\par
Nothing like a bed could be found, and he had ceased to enquire for\par
one, when Emily joined him, who observed the languor of her father’s\par
countenance, and lamented, that he had taken a road so ill provided\par
with the comforts necessary for an invalid. Other cottages, which they\par
examined, seemed somewhat less savage than the former, consisting of\par
two rooms, if such they could be called; the first of these occupied by\par
mules and pigs, the second by the family, which generally consisted of\par
six or eight children, with their parents, who slept on beds of skins\par
and dried beech leaves, spread upon a mud floor. Here, light was\par
admitted, and smoke discharged, through an aperture in the roof; and\par
here the scent of spirits (for the travelling smugglers, who haunted\par
the Pyrenees, had made this rude people familiar with the use of\par
liquors) was generally perceptible enough. Emily turned from such\par
scenes, and looked at her father with anxious tenderness, which the\par
young stranger seemed to observe; for, drawing St. Aubert aside, he\par
made him an offer of his own bed. “It is a decent one,” said he, “when\par
compared with what we have just seen, yet such as in other\par
circumstances I should be ashamed to offer you.” St. Aubert\par
acknowledged how much he felt himself obliged by this kindness, but\par
refused to accept it, till the young stranger would take no denial. “Do\par
not give me the pain of knowing, sir,” said he, “that an invalid, like\par
you, lies on hard skins, while I sleep in a bed. Besides, sir, your\par
refusal wounds my pride; I must believe you think my offer unworthy\par
your acceptance. Let me show you the way. I have no doubt my landlady\par
can accommodate this young lady also.”\par
\par
St. Aubert at length consented, that, if this could be done, he would\par
accept his kindness, though he felt rather surprised, that the stranger\par
had proved himself so deficient in gallantry, as to administer to the\par
repose of an infirm man, rather than to that of a very lovely young\par
woman, for he had not once offered the room for Emily. But she thought\par
not of herself, and the animated smile she gave him, told how much she\par
felt herself obliged for the preference of her father.\par
\par
On their way, the stranger, whose name was Valancourt, stepped on first\par
to speak to his hostess, and she came out to welcome St. Aubert into a\par
cottage, much superior to any he had seen. This good woman seemed very\par
willing to accommodate the strangers, who were soon compelled to accept\par
the only two beds in the place. Eggs and milk were the only food the\par
cottage afforded; but against scarcity of provisions St. Aubert had\par
provided, and he requested Valancourt to stay, and partake with him of\par
less homely fare; an invitation, which was readily accepted, and they\par
passed an hour in intelligent conversation. St. Aubert was much pleased\par
with the manly frankness, simplicity, and keen susceptibility to the\par
grandeur of nature, which his new acquaintance discovered; and, indeed,\par
he had often been heard to say, that, without a certain simplicity of\par
heart, this taste could not exist in any strong degree.\par
\par
The conversation was interrupted by a violent uproar without, in which\par
the voice of the muleteer was heard above every other sound. Valancourt\par
started from his seat, and went to enquire the occasion; but the\par
dispute continued so long afterwards, that St. Aubert went himself, and\par
found Michael quarrelling with the hostess, because she had refused to\par
let his mules lie in a little room where he and three of her sons were\par
to pass the night. The place was wretched enough, but there was no\par
other for these people to sleep in; and, with somewhat more of delicacy\par
than was usual among the inhabitants of this wild tract of country, she\par
persisted in refusing to let the animals have the same _bed-chamber_\par
with her children. This was a tender point with the muleteer; his\par
honour was wounded when his mules were treated with disrespect, and he\par
would have received a blow, perhaps, with more meekness. He declared\par
that his beasts were as honest beasts, and as good beasts, as any in\par
the whole province; and that they had a right to be well treated\par
wherever they went. “They are as harmless as lambs,” said he, “if\par
people don’t affront them. I never knew them behave themselves amiss\par
above once or twice in my life, and then they had good reason for doing\par
so. Once, indeed, they kicked at a boy’s leg that lay asleep in the\par
stable, and broke it; but I told them they were out there, and by St.\par
Anthony! I believe they understood me, for they never did so again.”\par
\par
He concluded this eloquent harangue with protesting, that they should\par
share with him, go where he would.\par
\par
The dispute was at length settled by Valancourt, who drew the hostess\par
aside, and desired she would let the muleteer and his beasts have the\par
place in question to themselves, while her sons should have the bed of\par
skins designed for him, for that he would wrap himself in his cloak,\par
and sleep on the bench by the cottage door. But this she thought it her\par
duty to oppose, and she felt it to be her inclination to disappoint the\par
muleteer. Valancourt, however, was positive, and the tedious affair was\par
at length settled.\par
\par
It was late when St. Aubert and Emily retired to their rooms, and\par
Valancourt to his station at the door, which, at this mild season, he\par
preferred to a close cabin and a bed of skins. St. Aubert was somewhat\par
surprised to find in his room volumes of Homer, Horace, and Petrarch;\par
but the name of Valancourt, written in them, told him to whom they\par
belonged.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER IV\par
\par
In truth he was a strange and wayward wight,\par
Fond of each gentle, and each dreadful scene,\par
In darkness, and in storm he found delight;\par
Nor less than when on ocean-wave serene\par
The southern sun diffus’d his dazzling sheen.\par
Even sad vicissitude amus’d his soul;\par
And if a sigh would sometimes intervene,\par
And down his cheek a tear of pity roll,\par
A sigh, a tear, so sweet, he wish’d not to control.\par
                    THE MINSTREL\par
\par
\par
St. Aubert awoke at an early hour, refreshed by sleep, and desirous to\par
set forward. He invited the stranger to breakfast with him; and,\par
talking again of the road, Valancourt said, that, some months past, he\par
had travelled as far as Beaujeu, which was a town of some consequence\par
on the way to Rousillon. He recommended it to St. Aubert to take that\par
route, and the latter determined to do so.\par
\par
“The road from this hamlet,” said Valancourt, “and that to Beaujeu,\par
part at the distance of about a league and a half from hence; if you\par
will give me leave, I will direct your muleteer so far. I must wander\par
somewhere, and your company would make this a pleasanter ramble than\par
any other I could take.”\par
\par
St. Aubert thankfully accepted his offer, and they set out together,\par
the young stranger on foot, for he refused the invitation of St. Aubert\par
to take a seat in his little carriage.\par
\par
The road wound along the feet of the mountains through a pastoral\par
valley, bright with verdure, and varied with groves of dwarf oak, beech\par
and sycamore, under whose branches herds of cattle reposed. The\par
mountain-ash too, and the weeping birch, often threw their pendant\par
foliage over the steeps above, where the scanty soil scarcely concealed\par
their roots, and where their light branches waved to every breeze that\par
fluttered from the mountains.\par
\par
The travellers were frequently met at this early hour, for the sun had\par
not yet risen upon the valley, by shepherds driving immense flocks from\par
their folds to feed upon the hills. St. Aubert had set out thus early,\par
not only that he might enjoy the first appearance of sunrise, but that\par
he might inhale the first pure breath of morning, which above all\par
things is refreshing to the spirits of the invalid. In these regions it\par
was particularly so, where an abundance of wild flowers and aromatic\par
herbs breathed forth their essence on the air.\par
\par
The dawn, which softened the scenery with its peculiar grey tint, now\par
dispersed, and Emily watched the progress of the day, first trembling\par
on the tops of the highest cliffs, then touching them with splendid\par
light, while their sides and the vale below were still wrapt in dewy\par
mist. Meanwhile, the sullen grey of the eastern clouds began to blush,\par
then to redden, and then to glow with a thousand colours, till the\par
golden light darted over all the air, touched the lower points of the\par
mountain’s brow, and glanced in long sloping beams upon the valley and\par
its stream. All nature seemed to have awakened from death into life;\par
the spirit of St. Aubert was renovated. His heart was full; he wept,\par
and his thoughts ascended to the Great Creator.\par
\par
Emily wished to trip along the turf, so green and bright with dew, and\par
to taste the full delight of that liberty, which the izard seemed to\par
enjoy as he bounded along the brow of the cliffs; while Valancourt\par
often stopped to speak with the travellers, and with social feeling to\par
point out to them the peculiar objects of his admiration. St. Aubert\par
was pleased with him: “Here is the real ingenuousness and ardour of\par
youth,” said he to himself; “this young man has never been at Paris.”\par
\par
He was sorry when they came to the spot where the roads parted, and his\par
heart took a more affectionate leave of him than is usual after so\par
short an acquaintance. Valancourt talked long by the side of the\par
carriage; seemed more than once to be going, but still lingered, and\par
appeared to search anxiously for topics of conversation to account for\par
his delay. At length he took leave. As he went, St. Aubert observed him\par
look with an earnest and pensive eye at Emily, who bowed to him with a\par
countenance full of timid sweetness, while the carriage drove on. St.\par
Aubert, for whatever reason, soon after looked from the window, and saw\par
Valancourt standing upon the bank of the road, resting on his pike with\par
folded arms, and following the carriage with his eyes. He waved his\par
hand, and Valancourt, seeming to awake from his reverie, returned the\par
salute, and started away.\par
\par
The aspect of the country now began to change, and the travellers soon\par
found themselves among mountains covered from their base nearly to\par
their summits with forests of gloomy pine, except where a rock of\par
granite shot up from the vale, and lost its snowy top in the clouds.\par
The rivulet, which had hitherto accompanied them, now expanded into a\par
river; and, flowing deeply and silently along, reflected, as in a\par
mirror, the blackness of the impending shades. Sometimes a cliff was\par
seen lifting its bold head above the woods and the vapours, that\par
floated mid-way down the mountains; and sometimes a face of\par
perpendicular marble rose from the water’s edge, over which the larch\par
threw his gigantic arms, here scathed with lightning, and there\par
floating in luxuriant foliage.\par
\par
They continued to travel over a rough and unfrequented road, seeing now\par
and then at a distance the solitary shepherd, with his dog, stalking\par
along the valley, and hearing only the dashing of torrents, which the\par
woods concealed from the eye, the long sullen murmur of the breeze, as\par
it swept over the pines, or the notes of the eagle and the vulture,\par
which were seen towering round the beetling cliff.\par
\par
Often, as the carriage moved slowly over uneven ground, St. Aubert\par
alighted, and amused himself with examining the curious plants that\par
grew on the banks of the road, and with which these regions abound;\par
while Emily, wrapt in high enthusiasm, wandered away under the shades,\par
listening in deep silence to the lonely murmur of the woods.\par
\par
Neither village nor hamlet was seen for many leagues; the goat-herd’s\par
or the hunter’s cabin, perched among the cliffs of the rocks, were the\par
only human habitations that appeared.\par
\par
The travellers again took their dinner in the open air, on a pleasant\par
spot in the valley, under the spreading shade of cedars; and then set\par
forward towards Beaujeu.\par
\par
The road now began to descend, and, leaving the pine forests behind,\par
wound among rocky precipices. The evening twilight again fell over the\par
scene, and the travellers were ignorant how far they might yet be from\par
Beaujeu. St. Aubert, however, conjectured that the distance could not\par
be very great, and comforted himself with the prospect of travelling on\par
a more frequented road after reaching that town, where he designed to\par
pass the night. Mingled woods, and rocks, and heathy mountains were now\par
seen obscurely through the dusk; but soon even these imperfect images\par
faded in darkness. Michael proceeded with caution, for he could\par
scarcely distinguish the road; his mules, however, seemed to have more\par
sagacity, and their steps were sure.\par
\par
On turning the angle of a mountain, a light appeared at a distance,\par
that illumined the rocks, and the horizon to a great extent. It was\par
evidently a large fire, but whether accidental, or otherwise, there\par
were no means of knowing. St. Aubert thought it was probably kindled by\par
some of the numerous banditti, that infested the Pyrenees, and he\par
became watchful and anxious to know whether the road passed near this\par
fire. He had arms with him, which, on an emergency, might afford some\par
protection, though certainly a very unequal one, against a band of\par
robbers, so desperate too as those usually were who haunted these wild\par
regions. While many reflections rose upon his mind, he heard a voice\par
shouting from the road behind, and ordering the muleteer to stop. St.\par
Aubert bade him proceed as fast as possible; but either Michael, or his\par
mules were obstinate, for they did not quit the old pace. Horses’ feet\par
were now heard; a man rode up to the carriage, still ordering the\par
driver to stop; and St. Aubert, who could no longer doubt his purpose,\par
was with difficulty able to prepare a pistol for his defence, when his\par
hand was upon the door of the chaise. The man staggered on his horse,\par
the report of the pistol was followed by a groan, and St. Aubert’s\par
horror may be imagined, when in the next instant he thought he heard\par
the faint voice of Valancourt. He now himself bade the muleteer stop;\par
and, pronouncing the name of Valancourt, was answered in a voice, that\par
no longer suffered him to doubt. St. Aubert, who instantly alighted and\par
went to his assistance, found him still sitting on his horse, but\par
bleeding profusely, and appearing to be in great pain, though he\par
endeavoured to soften the terror of St. Aubert by assurances that he\par
was not materially hurt, the wound being only in his arm. St. Aubert,\par
with the muleteer, assisted him to dismount, and he sat down on the\par
bank of the road, where St. Aubert tried to bind up his arm, but his\par
hands trembled so excessively that he could not accomplish it; and,\par
Michael being now gone in pursuit of the horse, which, on being\par
disengaged from his rider, had galloped off, he called Emily to his\par
assistance. Receiving no answer, he went to the carriage, and found her\par
sunk on the seat in a fainting fit. Between the distress of this\par
circumstance and that of leaving Valancourt bleeding, he scarcely knew\par
what he did; he endeavoured, however, to raise her, and called to\par
Michael to fetch water from the rivulet that flowed by the road, but\par
Michael was gone beyond the reach of his voice. Valancourt, who heard\par
these calls, and also the repeated name of Emily, instantly understood\par
the subject of his distress; and, almost forgetting his own condition,\par
he hastened to her relief. She was reviving when he reached the\par
carriage; and then, understanding that anxiety for him had occasioned\par
her indisposition, he assured her, in a voice that trembled, but not\par
from anguish, that his wound was of no consequence. While he said this\par
St. Aubert turned round, and perceiving that he was still bleeding, the\par
subject of his alarm changed again, and he hastily formed some\par
handkerchiefs into a bandage. This stopped the effusion of the blood;\par
but St. Aubert, dreading the consequence of the wound, enquired\par
repeatedly how far they were from Beaujeu; when, learning that it was\par
at two leagues’ distance, his distress increased, since he knew not how\par
Valancourt, in his present state, would bear the motion of the\par
carriage, and perceived that he was already faint from loss of blood.\par
When he mentioned the subject of his anxiety, Valancourt entreated that\par
he would not suffer himself to be thus alarmed on his account, for that\par
he had no doubt he should be able to support himself very well; and\par
then he talked of the accident as a slight one. The muleteer being now\par
returned with Valancourt’s horse, assisted him into the chaise; and, as\par
Emily was now revived, they moved slowly on towards Beaujeu.\par
\par
St. Aubert, when he had recovered from the terror occasioned him by\par
this accident, expressed surprise on seeing Valancourt, who explained\par
his unexpected appearance by saying, “You, sir, renewed my taste for\par
society; when you had left the hamlet, it did indeed appear a solitude.\par
I determined, therefore, since my object was merely amusement, to\par
change the scene; and I took this road, because I knew it led through a\par
more romantic tract of mountains than the spot I have left. Besides,”\par
added he, hesitating for an instant, “I will own, and why should I not?\par
that I had some hope of overtaking you.”\par
\par
“And I have made you a very unexpected return for the compliment,” said\par
St. Aubert, who lamented again the rashness which had produced the\par
accident, and explained the cause of his late alarm. But Valancourt\par
seemed anxious only to remove from the minds of his companions every\par
unpleasant feeling relative to himself; and, for that purpose, still\par
struggled against a sense of pain, and tried to converse with gaiety.\par
Emily meanwhile was silent, except when Valancourt particularly\par
addressed her, and there was at those times a tremulous tone in his\par
voice that spoke much.\par
\par
They were now so near the fire, which had long flamed at a distance on\par
the blackness of night, that it gleamed upon the road, and they could\par
distinguish figures moving about the blaze. The way winding still\par
nearer, they perceived in the valley one of those numerous bands of\par
gipsies, which at that period particularly haunted the wilds of the\par
Pyrenees, and lived partly by plundering the traveller. Emily looked\par
with some degree of terror on the savage countenances of these people,\par
shown by the fire, which heightened the romantic effects of the\par
scenery, as it threw a red dusky gleam upon the rocks and on the\par
foliage of the trees, leaving heavy masses of shade and regions of\par
obscurity, which the eye feared to penetrate.\par
\par
They were preparing their supper; a large pot stood by the fire, over\par
which several figures were busy. The blaze discovered a rude kind of\par
tent, round which many children and dogs were playing, and the whole\par
formed a picture highly grotesque. The travellers saw plainly their\par
danger. Valancourt was silent, but laid his hand on one of St. Aubert’s\par
pistols; St. Aubert drew forth another, and Michael was ordered to\par
proceed as fast as possible. They passed the place, however, without\par
being attacked; the rovers being probably unprepared for the\par
opportunity, and too busy about their supper to feel much interest, at\par
the moment, in anything besides.\par
\par
After a league and a half more, passed in darkness, the travellers\par
arrived at Beaujeu, and drove up to the only inn the place afforded;\par
which, though superior to any they had seen since they entered the\par
mountains, was bad enough.\par
\par
The surgeon of the town was immediately sent for, if a surgeon he could\par
be called, who prescribed for horses as well as for men, and shaved\par
faces at least as dexterously as he set bones. After examining\par
Valancourt’s arm, and perceiving that the bullet had passed through the\par
flesh without touching the bone, he dressed it, and left him with a\par
solemn prescription of quiet, which his patient was not inclined to\par
obey. The delight of ease had now succeeded to pain; for ease may be\par
allowed to assume a positive quality when contrasted with anguish; and,\par
his spirits thus reanimated, he wished to partake of the conversation\par
of St. Aubert and Emily, who, released from so many apprehensions, were\par
uncommonly cheerful. Late as it was, however, St. Aubert was obliged to\par
go out with the landlord to buy meat for supper; and Emily, who, during\par
this interval, had been absent as long as she could, upon excuses of\par
looking to their accommodation, which she found rather better than she\par
expected, was compelled to return, and converse with Valancourt alone.\par
They talked of the character of the scenes they had passed, of the\par
natural history of the country, of poetry, and of St. Aubert; a subject\par
on which Emily always spoke and listened to with peculiar pleasure.\par
\par
The travellers passed an agreeable evening; but St. Aubert was fatigued\par
with his journey; and, as Valancourt seemed again sensible of pain,\par
they separated soon after supper.\par
\par
In the morning St. Aubert found that Valancourt had passed a restless\par
night; that he was feverish, and his wound very painful. The surgeon,\par
when he dressed it, advised him to remain quietly at Beaujeu; advice\par
which was too reasonable to be rejected. St. Aubert, however, had no\par
favourable opinion of this practitioner, and was anxious to commit\par
Valancourt into more skilful hands; but learning, upon enquiry, that\par
there was no town within several leagues which seemed more likely to\par
afford better advice, he altered the plan of his journey, and\par
determined to await the recovery of Valancourt, who, with somewhat more\par
ceremony than sincerity, made many objections to this delay.\par
\par
By order of his surgeon, Valancourt did not go out of the house that\par
day; but St. Aubert and Emily surveyed with delight the environs of the\par
town, situated at the feet of the Pyrenean Alps, that rose, some in\par
abrupt precipices, and others swelling with woods of cedar, fir, and\par
cypress, which stretched nearly to their highest summits. The cheerful\par
green of the beech and mountain-ash was sometimes seen, like a gleam of\par
light, amidst the dark verdure of the forest; and sometimes a torrent\par
poured its sparkling flood, high among the woods.\par
\par
Valancourt’s indisposition detained the travellers at Beaujeu several\par
days, during which interval St. Aubert had observed his disposition and\par
his talents with the philosophic inquiry so natural to him. He saw a\par
frank and generous nature, full of ardour, highly susceptible of\par
whatever is grand and beautiful, but impetuous, wild, and somewhat\par
romantic. Valancourt had known little of the world. His perceptions\par
were clear, and his feelings just; his indignation of an unworthy, or\par
his admiration of a generous action, were expressed in terms of equal\par
vehemence. St. Aubert sometimes smiled at his warmth, but seldom\par
checked it, and often repeated to himself, “This young man has never\par
been at Paris.” A sigh sometimes followed this silent ejaculation. He\par
determined not to leave Valancourt till he should be perfectly\par
recovered; and, as he was now well enough to travel, though not able to\par
manage his horse, St. Aubert invited him to accompany him for a few\par
days in the carriage. This he the more readily did, since he had\par
discovered that Valancourt was of a family of the same name in Gascony,\par
with whose respectability he was well acquainted. The latter accepted\par
the offer with great pleasure, and they again set forward among these\par
romantic wilds about Rousillon.\par
\par
They travelled leisurely; stopping wherever a scene uncommonly grand\par
appeared; frequently alighting to walk to an eminence, whither the\par
mules could not go, from which the prospect opened in greater\par
magnificence; and often sauntering over hillocks covered with lavender,\par
wild thyme, juniper, and tamarisc; and under the shades of woods,\par
between those boles they caught the long mountain-vista, sublime beyond\par
anything that Emily had ever imagined.\par
\par
St. Aubert sometimes amused himself with botanizing, while Valancourt\par
and Emily strolled on; he pointing out to her notice the objects that\par
particularly charmed him, and reciting beautiful passages from such of\par
the Latin and Italian poets as he had heard her admire. In the pauses\par
of conversation, when he thought himself not observed, he frequently\par
fixed his eyes pensively on her countenance, which expressed with so\par
much animation the taste and energy of her mind; and when he spoke\par
again, there was a peculiar tenderness in the tone of his voice, that\par
defeated any attempt to conceal his sentiments. By degrees these silent\par
pauses became more frequent; till Emily, only, betrayed an anxiety to\par
interrupt them; and she; who had been hitherto reserved, would now talk\par
again, and again, of the woods and the valleys and the mountains, to\par
avoid the danger of sympathy and silence.\par
\par
From Beaujeu the road had constantly ascended, conducting the\par
travellers into the higher regions of the air, where immense glaciers\par
exhibited their frozen horrors, and eternal snow whitened the summits\par
of the mountains. They often paused to contemplate these stupendous\par
scenes, and, seated on some wild cliff, where only the ilex or the\par
larch could flourish, looked over dark forests of fir, and precipices\par
where human foot had never wandered, into the glen—so deep, that the\par
thunder of the torrent, which was seen to foam along the bottom, was\par
scarcely heard to murmur. Over these crags rose others of stupendous\par
height, and fantastic shape; some shooting into cones; others impending\par
far over their base, in huge masses of granite, along whose broken\par
ridges was often lodged a weight of snow, that, trembling even to the\par
vibration of a sound, threatened to bear destruction in its course to\par
the vale. Around, on every side, far as the eye could penetrate, were\par
seen only forms of grandeur—the long perspective of mountain-tops,\par
tinged with ethereal blue, or white with snow; valleys of ice, and\par
forests of gloomy fir. The serenity and clearness of the air in these\par
high regions were particularly delightful to the travellers; it seemed\par
to inspire them with a finer spirit, and diffused an indescribable\par
complacency over their minds. They had no words to express the sublime\par
emotions they felt. A solemn expression characterised the feelings of\par
St. Aubert; tears often came to his eyes, and he frequently walked away\par
from his companions. Valancourt now and then spoke, to point to Emily’s\par
notice some feature of the scene. The thinness of the atmosphere,\par
through which every object came so distinctly to the eye, surprised and\par
deluded her; who could scarcely believe that objects, which appeared so\par
near, were, in reality, so distant. The deep silence of these solitudes\par
was broken only at intervals by the scream of the vultures, seen\par
cowering round some cliff below, or by the cry of the eagle sailing\par
high in the air; except when the travellers listened to the hollow\par
thunder that sometimes muttered at their feet. While, above, the deep\par
blue of the heavens was unobscured by the lightest cloud, half way down\par
the mountains, long billows of vapour were frequently seen rolling, now\par
wholly excluding the country below, and now opening, and partially\par
revealing its features. Emily delighted to observe the grandeur of\par
these clouds as they changed in shape and tints, and to watch their\par
various effect on the lower world, whose features, partly veiled, were\par
continually assuming new forms of sublimity.\par
\par
After traversing these regions for many leagues, they began to descend\par
towards Rousillon, and features of beauty then mingled with the scene.\par
Yet the travellers did not look back without some regret to the sublime\par
objects they had quitted; though the eye, fatigued with the extension\par
of its powers, was glad to repose on the verdure of woods and pastures,\par
that now hung on the margin of the river below; to view again the\par
humble cottage shaded by cedars, the playful group of\par
mountaineer-children, and the flowery nooks that appeared among the\par
hills.\par
\par
As they descended, they saw at a distance, on the right, one of the\par
grand passes of the Pyrenees into Spain, gleaming with its battlements\par
and towers to the splendour of the setting rays, yellow tops of woods\par
colouring the steeps below, while far above aspired the snowy points of\par
the mountains, still reflecting a rosy hue.\par
\par
St. Aubert began to look out for the little town he had been directed\par
to by the people of Beaujeu, and where he meant to pass the night; but\par
no habitation yet appeared. Of its distance Valancourt could not assist\par
him to judge, for he had never been so far along this chain of Alps\par
before. There was, however, a road to guide them; and there could be\par
little doubt that it was the right one; for, since they had left\par
Beaujeu, there had been no variety of tracks to perplex or mislead.\par
\par
The sun now gave his last light, and St. Aubert bade the muleteer\par
proceed with all possible dispatch. He found, indeed, the lassitude of\par
illness return upon him, after a day of uncommon fatigue, both of body\par
and mind, and he longed for repose. His anxiety was not soothed by\par
observing a numerous train, consisting of men, horses, and loaded\par
mules, winding down the steeps of an opposite mountain, appearing and\par
disappearing at intervals among the woods, so that its numbers could\par
not be judged of. Something bright, like arms, glanced in the setting\par
ray, and the military dress was distinguishable upon the men who were\par
in the van, and on others scattered among the troop that followed. As\par
these wound into the vale, the rear of the party emerged from the\par
woods, and exhibited a band of soldiers. St. Aubert’s apprehensions now\par
subsided; he had no doubt that the train before him consisted of\par
smugglers, who, in conveying prohibited goods over the Pyrenees, had\par
been encountered, and conquered by a party of troops.\par
\par
The travellers had lingered so long among the sublimer scenes of these\par
mountains, that they found themselves entirely mistaken in their\par
calculation that they could reach Montigny at sunset; but, as they\par
wound along the valley, the saw, on a rude Alpine bridge, that united\par
two lofty crags of the glen, a group of mountaineer-children, amusing\par
themselves with dropping pebbles into a torrent below, and watching the\par
stones plunge into the water, that threw up its white spray high in the\par
air as it received them, and returned a sullen sound, which the echoes\par
of the mountains prolonged. Under the bridge was seen a perspective of\par
the valley, with its cataract descending among the rocks, and a cottage\par
on a cliff, overshadowed with pines. It appeared, that they could not\par
be far from some small town. St. Aubert bade the muleteer stop, and\par
then called to the children to enquire if he was near Montigny; but the\par
distance, and the roaring of the waters, would not suffer his voice to\par
be heard; and the crags, adjoining the bridge, were of such tremendous\par
height and steepness, that to have climbed either would have been\par
scarcely practicable to a person unacquainted with the ascent. St.\par
Aubert, therefore, did not waste more moments in delay. They continued\par
to travel long after twilight had obscured the road, which was so\par
broken, that, now thinking it safer to walk than to ride, they all\par
alighted. The moon was rising, but her light was yet too feeble to\par
assist them. While they stepped carefully on, they heard the\par
vesper-bell of a convent. The twilight would not permit them to\par
distinguish anything like a building, but the sounds seemed to come\par
from some woods, that overhung an acclivity to the right. Valancourt\par
proposed to go in search of this convent. “If they will not accommodate\par
us with a night’s lodging,” said he, “they may certainly inform us how\par
far we are from Montigny, and direct us towards it.” He was bounding\par
forward, without waiting St. Aubert’s reply, when the latter stopped\par
him. “I am very weary,” said St. Aubert, “and wish for nothing so much\par
as for immediate rest. We will all go to the convent; your good looks\par
would defeat our purpose; but when they see mine and Emily’s exhausted\par
countenances, they will scarcely deny us repose.”\par
\par
As he said this, he took Emily’s arm within his, and, telling Michael\par
to wait awhile in the road with the carriage, they began to ascend\par
towards the woods, guided by the bell of the convent. His steps were\par
feeble, and Valancourt offered him his arm, which he accepted. The moon\par
now threw a faint light over their path, and, soon after, enabled them\par
to distinguish some towers rising above the tops of the woods. Still\par
following the note of the bell, they entered the shade of those woods,\par
lighted only by the moonbeams, that glided down between the leaves, and\par
threw a tremulous uncertain gleam upon the steep track they were\par
winding. The gloom and the silence that prevailed, except when the bell\par
returned upon the air, together with the wildness of the surrounding\par
scene, struck Emily with a degree of fear, which, however, the voice\par
and conversation of Valancourt somewhat repressed. When they had been\par
some time ascending, St. Aubert complained of weariness, and they\par
stopped to rest upon a little green summit, where the trees opened, and\par
admitted the moonlight. He sat down upon the turf, between Emily and\par
Valancourt. The bell had now ceased, and the deep repose of the scene\par
was undisturbed by any sound, for the low dull murmur of some distant\par
torrents might be said to sooth, rather than to interrupt, the silence.\par
\par
Before them, extended the valley they had quitted; its rocks, and woods\par
to the left, just silvered by the rays, formed a contrast to the deep\par
shadow, that involved the opposite cliffs, whose fringed summits only\par
were tipped with light; while the distant perspective of the valley was\par
lost in the yellow mist of moonlight. The travellers sat for some time\par
wrapt in the complacency which such scenes inspire.\par
\par
“These scenes,” said Valancourt, at length, “soften the heart, like the\par
notes of sweet music, and inspire that delicious melancholy which no\par
person, who had felt it once, would resign for the gayest pleasures.\par
They waken our best and purest feelings, disposing us to benevolence,\par
pity, and friendship. Those whom I love—I always seem to love more in\par
such an hour as this.” His voice trembled, and he paused.\par
\par
St. Aubert was silent; Emily perceived a warm tear fall upon the hand\par
he held; she knew the object of his thoughts; hers too had, for some\par
time, been occupied by the remembrance of her mother. He seemed by an\par
effort to rouse himself. “Yes,” said he, with a half-suppressed sigh,\par
“the memory of those we love—of times for ever past! in such an hour as\par
this steals upon the mind, like a strain of distant music in the\par
stillness of night;—all tender and harmonious as this landscape,\par
sleeping in the mellow moonlight.” After the pause of a moment, St.\par
Aubert added, “I have always fancied, that I thought with more\par
clearness, and precision, at such an hour than at any other, and that\par
heart must be insensible in a great degree, that does not soften to its\par
influence. But many such there are.”\par
\par
Valancourt sighed.\par
\par
“Are there, indeed, many such?” said Emily.\par
\par
“A few years hence, my Emily,” replied St. Aubert, “and you may smile\par
at the recollection of that question—if you do not weep to it. But\par
come, I am somewhat refreshed, let us proceed.”\par
\par
Having emerged from the woods, they saw, upon a turfy hillock above,\par
the convent of which they were in search. A high wall, that surrounded\par
it, led them to an ancient gate, at which they knocked; and the poor\par
monk, who opened it, conducted them into a small adjoining room, where\par
he desired they would wait while he informed the superior of their\par
request. In this interval, several friars came in separately to look at\par
them; and at length the first monk returned, and they followed him to a\par
room, where the superior was sitting in an arm-chair, with a large\par
folio volume, printed in black letter, open on a desk before him. He\par
received them with courtesy, though he did not rise from his seat; and,\par
having asked them a few questions, granted their request. After a short\par
conversation, formal and solemn on the part of the superior, they\par
withdrew to the apartment where they were to sup, and Valancourt, whom\par
one of the inferior friars civilly desired to accompany, went to seek\par
Michael and his mules. They had not descended half way down the cliffs,\par
before they heard the voice of the muleteer echoing far and wide.\par
Sometimes he called on St. Aubert, and sometimes on Valancourt; who\par
having, at length, convinced him that he had nothing to fear either for\par
himself, or his master; and having disposed of him, for the night, in a\par
cottage on the skirts of the woods, returned to sup with his friends,\par
on such sober fare as the monks thought it prudent to set before them.\par
While St. Aubert was too much indisposed to share it, Emily, in her\par
anxiety for her father, forgot herself; and Valancourt, silent and\par
thoughtful, yet never inattentive to them, appeared particularly\par
solicitous to accommodate and relieve St. Aubert, who often observed,\par
while his daughter was pressing him to eat, or adjusting the pillow she\par
had placed in the back of his arm-chair, that Valancourt fixed on her a\par
look of pensive tenderness, which he was not displeased to understand.\par
\par
They separated at an early hour, and retired to their respective\par
apartments. Emily was shown to hers by a nun of the convent, whom she\par
was glad to dismiss, for her heart was melancholy, and her attention so\par
much abstracted, that conversation with a stranger was painful. She\par
thought her father daily declining, and attributed his present fatigue\par
more to the feeble state of his frame, than to the difficulty of the\par
journey. A train of gloomy ideas haunted her mind, till she fell\par
asleep.\par
\par
In about two hours after, she was awakened by the chiming of a bell,\par
and then heard quick steps pass along the gallery, into which her\par
chamber opened. She was so little accustomed to the manners of a\par
convent, as to be alarmed by this circumstance; her fears, ever alive\par
for her father, suggested that he was very ill, and she rose in haste\par
to go to him. Having paused, however, to let the persons in the gallery\par
pass before she opened her door, her thoughts, in the mean time,\par
recovered from the confusion of sleep, and she understood that the bell\par
was the call of the monks to prayers. It had now ceased, and, all being\par
again still, she forbore to go to St. Aubert’s room. Her mind was not\par
disposed for immediate sleep, and the moonlight, that shone into her\par
chamber, invited her to open the casement, and look out upon the\par
country.\par
\par
It was a still and beautiful night, the sky was unobscured by any\par
cloud, and scarce a leaf of the woods beneath trembled in the air. As\par
she listened, the midnight hymn of the monks rose softly from a chapel,\par
that stood on one of the lower cliffs, a holy strain, that seemed to\par
ascend through the silence of night to heaven, and her thoughts\par
ascended with it. From the consideration of His works, her mind arose\par
to the adoration of the Deity, in His goodness and power; wherever she\par
turned her view, whether on the sleeping earth, or to the vast regions\par
of space, glowing with worlds beyond the reach of human thought, the\par
sublimity of God, and the majesty of His presence appeared. Her eyes\par
were filled with tears of awful love and admiration; and she felt that\par
pure devotion, superior to all the distinctions of human system, which\par
lifts the soul above this world, and seems to expand it into a nobler\par
nature; such devotion as can, perhaps, only be experienced, when the\par
mind, rescued, for a moment, from the humbleness of earthly\par
considerations, aspires to contemplate His power in the sublimity of\par
His works, and His goodness in the infinity of His blessings.\par
\par
Is it not now the hour,\par
The holy hour, when to the cloudless height\par
Of yon starred concave climbs the full-orbed moon,\par
And to this nether world in solemn stillness,\par
Gives sign, that, to the list’ning ear of Heaven\par
Religion’s voice should plead?  The very babe\par
Knows this, and, chance awak’d, his little hands\par
Lifts to the gods, and on his innocent couch\par
Calls down a blessing.\par
                    CARACTACUS\par
\par
\par
The midnight chant of the monks soon after dropped into silence; but\par
Emily remained at the casement, watching the setting moon, and the\par
valley sinking into deep shade, and willing to prolong her present\par
state of mind. At length she retired to her mattress, and sunk into\par
tranquil slumber.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER V\par
\par
While in the rosy vale\par
Love breath’d his infant sighs, from anguish free.\par
                    THOMSON\par
\par
\par
St. Aubert, sufficiently restored by a night’s repose to pursue his\par
journey, set out in the morning, with his family and Valancourt, for\par
Rousillon, which he hoped to reach before night-fall. The scenes,\par
through which they now passed, were as wild and romantic, as any they\par
had yet observed, with this difference, that beauty, every now and\par
then, softened the landscape into smiles. Little woody recesses\par
appeared among the mountains, covered with bright verdure and flowers;\par
or a pastoral valley opened its grassy bosom in the shade of the\par
cliffs, with flocks and herds loitering along the banks of a rivulet,\par
that refreshed it with perpetual green. St. Aubert could not repent the\par
having taken this fatiguing road, though he was this day, also,\par
frequently obliged to alight, to walk along the rugged precipice, and\par
to climb the steep and flinty mountain. The wonderful sublimity and\par
variety of the prospects repaid him for all this, and the enthusiasm,\par
with which they were viewed by his young companions, heightened his\par
own, and awakened a remembrance of all the delightful emotions of his\par
early days, when the sublime charms of nature were first unveiled to\par
him. He found great pleasure in conversing with Valancourt, and in\par
listening to his ingenuous remarks. The fire and simplicity of his\par
manners seemed to render him a characteristic figure in the scenes\par
around them; and St. Aubert discovered in his sentiments the justness\par
and the dignity of an elevated mind, unbiased by intercourse with the\par
world. He perceived, that his opinions were formed, rather than\par
imbibed; were more the result of thought, than of learning. Of the\par
world he seemed to know nothing; for he believed well of all mankind,\par
and this opinion gave him the reflected image of his own heart.\par
\par
St. Aubert, as he sometimes lingered to examine the wild plants in his\par
path, often looked forward with pleasure to Emily and Valancourt, as\par
they strolled on together; he, with a countenance of animated delight,\par
pointing to her attention some grand feature of the scene; and she,\par
listening and observing with a look of tender seriousness, that spoke\par
the elevation of her mind. They appeared like two lovers who had never\par
strayed beyond these their native mountains; whose situation had\par
secluded them from the frivolities of common life, whose ideas were\par
simple and grand, like the landscapes among which they moved, and who\par
knew no other happiness, than in the union of pure and affectionate\par
hearts. St. Aubert smiled, and sighed at the romantic picture of\par
felicity his fancy drew; and sighed again to think, that nature and\par
simplicity were so little known to the world, as that their pleasures\par
were thought romantic.\par
\par
“The world,” said he, pursuing this train of thought, “ridicules a\par
passion which it seldom feels; its scenes, and its interests, distract\par
the mind, deprave the taste, corrupt the heart, and love cannot exist\par
in a heart that has lost the meek dignity of innocence. Virtue and\par
taste are nearly the same, for virtue is little more than active taste,\par
and the most delicate affections of each combine in real love. How then\par
are we to look for love in great cities, where selfishness,\par
dissipation, and insincerity supply the place of tenderness, simplicity\par
and truth?”\par
\par
It was near noon, when the travellers, having arrived at a piece of\par
steep and dangerous road, alighted to walk. The road wound up an\par
ascent, that was clothed with wood, and, instead of following the\par
carriage, they entered the refreshing shade. A dewy coolness was\par
diffused upon the air, which, with the bright verdure of turf, that\par
grew under the trees, the mingled fragrance of flowers and of balm,\par
thyme, and lavender, that enriched it, and the grandeur of the pines,\par
beech, and chestnuts, that overshadowed them, rendered this a most\par
delicious retreat. Sometimes, the thick foliage excluded all view of\par
the country; at others, it admitted some partial catches of the distant\par
scenery, which gave hints to the imagination to picture landscapes more\par
interesting, more impressive, than any that had been presented to the\par
eye. The wanderers often lingered to indulge in these reveries of\par
fancy.\par
\par
The pauses of silence, such as had formerly interrupted the\par
conversations of Valancourt and Emily, were more frequent today than\par
ever. Valancourt often dropped suddenly from the most animating\par
vivacity into fits of deep musing, and there was, sometimes, an\par
unaffected melancholy in his smile, which Emily could not avoid\par
understanding, for her heart was interested in the sentiment it spoke.\par
\par
St. Aubert was refreshed by the shades, and they continued to saunter\par
under them, following, as nearly as they could guess, the direction of\par
the road, till they perceived that they had totally lost it. They had\par
continued near the brow of the precipice, allured by the scenery it\par
exhibited, while the road wound far away over the cliff above.\par
Valancourt called loudly to Michael, but heard no voice, except his\par
own, echoing among the rocks, and his various efforts to regain the\par
road were equally unsuccessful. While they were thus circumstanced,\par
they perceived a shepherd’s cabin, between the boles of the trees at\par
some distance, and Valancourt bounded on first to ask assistance. When\par
he reached it, he saw only two little children, at play, on the turf\par
before the door. He looked into the hut, but no person was there, and\par
the eldest of the boys told him that their father was with his flocks,\par
and their mother was gone down into the vale, but would be back\par
presently. As he stood, considering what was further to be done, on a\par
sudden he heard Michael’s voice roaring forth most manfully among the\par
cliffs above, till he made their echoes ring. Valancourt immediately\par
answered the call, and endeavoured to make his way through the thicket\par
that clothed the steeps, following the direction of the sound. After\par
much struggle over brambles and precipices, he reached Michael, and at\par
length prevailed with him to be silent, and to listen to him. The road\par
was at a considerable distance from the spot where St. Aubert and Emily\par
were; the carriage could not easily return to the entrance of the wood,\par
and, since it would be very fatiguing for St. Aubert to climb the long\par
and steep road to the place where it now stood, Valancourt was anxious\par
to find a more easy ascent, by the way he had himself passed.\par
\par
Meanwhile St. Aubert and Emily approached the cottage, and rested\par
themselves on a rustic bench, fastened between two pines, which\par
overshadowed it, till Valancourt, whose steps they had observed, should\par
return.\par
\par
The eldest of the children desisted from his play, and stood still to\par
observe the strangers, while the younger continued his little gambols,\par
and teased his brother to join in them. St. Aubert looked with pleasure\par
upon this picture of infantine simplicity, till it brought to his\par
remembrance his own boys, whom he had lost about the age of these, and\par
their lamented mother; and he sunk into a thoughtfulness, which Emily\par
observing, she immediately began to sing one of those simple and lively\par
airs he was so fond of, and which she knew how to give with the most\par
captivating sweetness. St. Aubert smiled on her through his tears, took\par
her hand and pressed it affectionately, and then tried to dissipate the\par
melancholy reflections that lingered in his mind.\par
\par
While she sung, Valancourt approached, who was unwilling to interrupt\par
her, and paused at a little distance to listen. When she had concluded,\par
he joined the party, and told them, that he had found Michael, as well\par
as a way, by which he thought they could ascend the cliff to the\par
carriage. He pointed to the woody steeps above, which St. Aubert\par
surveyed with an anxious eye. He was already wearied by his walk, and\par
this ascent was formidable to him. He thought, however, it would be\par
less toilsome than the long and broken road, and he determined to\par
attempt it; but Emily, ever watchful of his ease, proposing that he\par
should rest, and dine before they proceeded further, Valancourt went to\par
the carriage for the refreshments deposited there.\par
\par
On his return, he proposed removing a little higher up the mountain, to\par
where the woods opened upon a grand and extensive prospect; and thither\par
they were preparing to go, when they saw a young woman join the\par
children, and caress and weep over them.\par
\par
The travellers, interested by her distress, stopped to observe her. She\par
took the youngest of the children in her arms, and, perceiving the\par
strangers, hastily dried her tears, and proceeded to the cottage. St.\par
Aubert, on enquiring the occasion of her sorrow, learned that her\par
husband, who was a shepherd, and lived here in the summer months to\par
watch over the flocks he led to feed upon these mountains, had lost, on\par
the preceding night, his little all. A gang of gipsies, who had for\par
some time infested the neighbourhood, had driven away several of his\par
master’s sheep. “Jacques,” added the shepherd’s wife, “had saved a\par
little money, and had bought a few sheep with it, and now they must go\par
to his master for those that are stolen; and what is worse than all,\par
his master, when he comes to know how it is, will trust him no longer\par
with the care of his flocks, for he is a hard man! and then what is to\par
become of our children!”\par
\par
The innocent countenance of the woman, and the simplicity of her manner\par
in relating her grievance, inclined St. Aubert to believe her story;\par
and Valancourt, convinced that it was true, asked eagerly what was the\par
value of the stolen sheep; on hearing which he turned away with a look\par
of disappointment. St. Aubert put some money into her hand, Emily too\par
gave something from her little purse, and they walked towards the\par
cliff; but Valancourt lingered behind, and spoke to the shepherd’s\par
wife, who was now weeping with gratitude and surprise. He enquired how\par
much money was yet wanting to replace the stolen sheep, and found, that\par
it was a sum very little short of all he had about him. He was\par
perplexed and distressed. “This sum then,” said he to himself, “would\par
make this poor family completely happy—it is in my power to give it—to\par
make them completely happy! But what is to become of me?—how shall I\par
contrive to reach home with the little money that will remain?” For a\par
moment he stood, unwilling to forego the luxury of raising a family\par
from ruin to happiness, yet considering the difficulties of pursuing\par
his journey with so small a sum as would be left.\par
\par
While he was in this state of perplexity, the shepherd himself\par
appeared: his children ran to meet him; he took one of them in his\par
arms, and, with the other clinging to his coat, came forward with a\par
loitering step. His forlorn and melancholy look determined Valancourt\par
at once; he threw down all the money he had, except a very few louis,\par
and bounded away after St. Aubert and Emily, who were proceeding slowly\par
up the steep. Valancourt had seldom felt his heart so light as at this\par
moment; his gay spirits danced with pleasure; every object around him\par
appeared more interesting, or beautiful, than before. St. Aubert\par
observed the uncommon vivacity of his countenance: “What has pleased\par
you so much?” said he. “O what a lovely day,” replied Valancourt, “how\par
brightly the sun shines, how pure is this air, what enchanting\par
scenery!” “It is indeed enchanting,” said St. Aubert, whom early\par
experience had taught to understand the nature of Valancourt’s present\par
feelings. “What pity that the wealthy, who can command such sunshine,\par
should ever pass their days in gloom—in the cold shade of selfishness!\par
For you, my young friend, may the sun always shine as brightly as at\par
this moment; may your own conduct always give you the sunshine of\par
benevolence and reason united!”\par
\par
Valancourt, highly flattered by this compliment, could make no reply\par
but by a smile of gratitude.\par
\par
They continued to wind under the woods, between the grassy knolls of\par
the mountain, and, as they reached the shady summit, which he had\par
pointed out, the whole party burst into an exclamation. Behind the spot\par
where they stood, the rock rose perpendicularly in a massy wall to a\par
considerable height, and then branched out into overhanging crags.\par
Their grey tints were well contrasted by the bright hues of the plants\par
and wild flowers, that grew in their fractured sides, and were deepened\par
by the gloom of the pines and cedars, that waved above. The steeps\par
below, over which the eye passed abruptly to the valley, were fringed\par
with thickets of alpine shrubs; and, lower still, appeared the tufted\par
tops of the chesnut woods, that clothed their base, among which peeped\par
forth the shepherd’s cottage, just left by the travellers, with its\par
bluish smoke curling high in the air. On every side appeared the\par
majestic summits of the Pyrenees, some exhibiting tremendous crags of\par
marble, whose appearance was changing every instant, as the varying\par
lights fell upon their surface; others, still higher, displaying only\par
snowy points, while their lower steeps were covered almost invariably\par
with forests of pine, larch, and oak, that stretched down to the vale.\par
This was one of the narrow valleys, that open from the Pyrenees into\par
the country of Rousillon, and whose green pastures, and cultivated\par
beauty, form a decided and wonderful contrast to the romantic grandeur\par
that environs it. Through a vista of the mountains appeared the\par
lowlands of Rousillon, tinted with the blue haze of distance, as they\par
united with the waters of the Mediterranean; where, on a promontory,\par
which marked the boundary of the shore, stood a lonely beacon, over\par
which were seen circling flights of sea-fowl. Beyond, appeared, now and\par
then, a stealing sail, white with the sunbeam, and whose progress was\par
perceivable by its approach to the light-house. Sometimes, too, was\par
seen a sail so distant, that it served only to mark the line of\par
separation between the sky and the waves.\par
\par
On the other side of the valley, immediately opposite to the spot where\par
the travellers rested, a rocky pass opened toward Gascony. Here no sign\par
of cultivation appeared. The rocks of granite, that screened the glen,\par
rose abruptly from their base, and stretched their barren points to the\par
clouds, unvaried with woods, and uncheered even by a hunter’s cabin.\par
Sometimes, indeed, a gigantic larch threw its long shade over the\par
precipice, and here and there a cliff reared on its brow a monumental\par
cross, to tell the traveller the fate of him who had ventured thither\par
before. This spot seemed the very haunt of banditti; and Emily, as she\par
looked down upon it, almost expected to see them stealing out from some\par
hollow cave to look for their prey. Soon after an object not less\par
terrific struck her,—a gibbet standing on a point of rock near the\par
entrance of the pass, and immediately over one of the crosses she had\par
before observed. These were hieroglyphics that told a plain and\par
dreadful story. She forbore to point it out to St. Aubert, but it threw\par
a gloom over her spirits, and made her anxious to hasten forward, that\par
they might with certainty reach Rousillon before night-fall. It was\par
necessary, however, that St. Aubert should take some refreshment, and,\par
seating themselves on the short dry turf, they opened the basket of\par
provisions, while\par
\par
by breezy murmurs cool’d,\par
Broad o’er _their_ heads the verdant cedars wave,\par
And high palmetos lift their graceful shade.\par
———_they_ draw\par
Ethereal soul, there drink reviving gales\par
Profusely breathing from the piney groves,\par
And vales of fragrance; there at a distance hear\par
The roaring floods, and cataracts.\par
                    THOMSON\par
\par
\par
St. Aubert was revived by rest, and by the serene air of this summit;\par
and Valancourt was so charmed with all around, and with the\par
conversation of his companions, that he seemed to have forgotten he had\par
any further to go. Having concluded their simple repast, they gave a\par
long farewell look to the scene, and again began to ascend. St. Aubert\par
rejoiced when he reached the carriage, which Emily entered with him;\par
but Valancourt, willing to take a more extensive view of the enchanting\par
country, into which they were about to descend, than he could do from a\par
carriage, loosened his dogs, and once more bounded with them along the\par
banks of the road. He often quitted it for points that promised a wider\par
prospect, and the slow pace, at which the mules travelled, allowed him\par
to overtake them with ease. Whenever a scene of uncommon magnificence\par
appeared, he hastened to inform St. Aubert, who, though he was too much\par
tired to walk himself, sometimes made the chaise wait, while Emily went\par
to the neighbouring cliff.\par
\par
It was evening when they descended the lower alps, that bind Rousillon,\par
and form a majestic barrier round that charming country, leaving it\par
open only on the east to the Mediterranean. The gay tints of\par
cultivation once more beautified the landscape; for the lowlands were\par
coloured with the richest hues, which a luxuriant climate, and an\par
industrious people can awaken into life. Groves of orange and lemon\par
perfumed the air, their ripe fruit glowing among the foliage; while,\par
sloping to the plains, extensive vineyards spread their treasures.\par
Beyond these, woods and pastures, and mingled towns and hamlets\par
stretched towards the sea, on whose bright surface gleamed many a\par
distant sail; while, over the whole scene, was diffused the purple glow\par
of evening. This landscape with the surrounding alps did, indeed,\par
present a perfect picture of the lovely and the sublime, of “beauty\par
sleeping in the lap of horror.”\par
\par
The travellers, having reached the plains, proceeded, between hedges of\par
flowering myrtle and pomegranate, to the town of Arles, where they\par
proposed to rest for the night. They met with simple, but neat\par
accommodation, and would have passed a happy evening, after the toils\par
and the delights of this day, had not the approaching separation thrown\par
a gloom over their spirit. It was St. Aubert’s plan to proceed, on the\par
morrow, to the borders of the Mediterranean, and travel along its\par
shores into Languedoc; and Valancourt, since he was now nearly\par
recovered, and had no longer a pretence for continuing with his new\par
friends, resolved to leave them here. St. Aubert, who was much pleased\par
with him, invited him to go further, but did not repeat the invitation,\par
and Valancourt had resolution enough to forego the temptation of\par
accepting it, that he might prove himself not unworthy of the favour.\par
On the following morning, therefore, they were to part, St. Aubert to\par
pursue his way to Languedoc, and Valancourt to explore new scenes among\par
the mountains, on his return home. During this evening he was often\par
silent and thoughtful; St. Aubert’s manner towards him was\par
affectionate, though grave, and Emily was serious, though she made\par
frequent efforts to appear cheerful. After one of the most melancholy\par
evenings they had yet passed together, they separated for the night.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER VI\par
\par
I care not, Fortune! what you me deny;\par
You cannot rob me of free nature’s grace;\par
You cannot shut the windows of the sky,\par
Through which Aurora shows her brightening face;\par
You cannot bar my constant feet to trace\par
The woods and lawns, by living stream, at eve:\par
Let health my nerves and finer fibres brace,\par
And I their toys to the great children leave:\par
Of fancy, reason, virtue, nought can me bereave.\par
                    THOMSON\par
\par
\par
In the morning, Valancourt breakfasted with St. Aubert and Emily,\par
neither of whom seemed much refreshed by sleep. The languor of illness\par
still hung over St. Aubert, and to Emily’s fears his disorder appeared\par
to be increasing fast upon him. She watched his looks with anxious\par
affection, and their expression was always faithfully reflected in her\par
own.\par
\par
At the commencement of their acquaintance, Valancourt had made known\par
his name and family. St. Aubert was not a stranger to either, for the\par
family estates, which were now in the possession of an elder brother of\par
Valancourt, were little more than twenty miles distant from La Vallée,\par
and he had sometimes met the elder Valancourt on visits in the\par
neighbourhood. This knowledge had made him more willingly receive his\par
present companion; for, though his countenance and manners would have\par
won him the acquaintance of St. Aubert, who was very apt to trust to\par
the intelligence of his own eyes, with respect to countenances, he\par
would not have accepted these, as sufficient introductions to that of\par
his daughter.\par
\par
The breakfast was almost as silent as the supper of the preceding\par
night; but their musing was at length interrupted by the sound of the\par
carriage wheels, which were to bear away St. Aubert and Emily.\par
Valancourt started from his chair, and went to the window; it was\par
indeed the carriage, and he returned to his seat without speaking. The\par
moment was now come when they must part. St. Aubert told Valancourt,\par
that he hoped he would never pass La Vallée without favouring him with\par
a visit; and Valancourt, eagerly thanking him, assured him that he\par
never would; as he said which he looked timidly at Emily, who tried to\par
smile away the seriousness of her spirits. They passed a few minutes in\par
interesting conversation, and St. Aubert then led the way to the\par
carriage, Emily and Valancourt following in silence. The latter\par
lingered at the door several minutes after they were seated, and none\par
of the party seemed to have courage enough to say—Farewell. At length,\par
St. Aubert pronounced the melancholy word, which Emily passed to\par
Valancourt, who returned it, with a dejected smile, and the carriage\par
drove on.\par
\par
The travellers remained, for some time, in a state of tranquil\par
pensiveness, which is not unpleasing. St. Aubert interrupted it by\par
observing, “This is a very promising young man; it is many years since\par
I have been so much pleased with any person, on so short an\par
acquaintance. He brings back to my memory the days of my youth, when\par
every scene was new and delightful!” St. Aubert sighed, and sunk again\par
into a reverie; and, as Emily looked back upon the road they had\par
passed, Valancourt was seen, at the door of the little inn, following\par
them with his eyes. Her perceived her, and waved his hand; and she\par
returned the adieu, till the winding road shut her from his sight.\par
\par
“I remember when I was about his age,” resumed St. Aubert, “and I\par
thought, and felt exactly as he does. The world was opening upon me\par
then, now—it is closing.”\par
\par
“My dear sir, do not think so gloomily,” said Emily in a trembling\par
voice, “I hope you have many, many years to live—for your own sake—for\par
_my_ sake.”\par
\par
“Ah, my Emily!” replied St. Aubert, “for thy sake! Well—I hope it is\par
so.” He wiped away a tear, that was stealing down his cheek, threw a\par
smile upon his countenance, and said in a cheering voice, “there is\par
something in the ardour and ingenuousness of youth, which is\par
particularly pleasing to the contemplation of an old man, if his\par
feelings have not been entirely corroded by the world. It is cheering\par
and reviving, like the view of spring to a sick person; his mind\par
catches somewhat of the spirit of the season, and his eyes are lighted\par
up with a transient sunshine. Valancourt is this spring to me.”\par
\par
Emily, who pressed her father’s hand affectionately, had never before\par
listened with so much pleasure to the praises he bestowed; no, not even\par
when he had bestowed them on herself.\par
\par
They travelled on, among vineyards, woods, and pastures, delighted with\par
the romantic beauty of the landscape, which was bounded, on one side,\par
by the grandeur of the Pyrenees, and, on the other, by the ocean; and,\par
soon after noon, they reached the town of Colioure, situated on the\par
Mediterranean. Here they dined, and rested till towards the cool of\par
day, when they pursued their way along the shores—those enchanting\par
shores!—which extend to Languedoc. Emily gazed with enthusiasm on the\par
vastness of the sea, its surface varying, as the lights and shadows\par
fell, and on its woody banks, mellowed with autumnal tints.\par
\par
St. Aubert was impatient to reach Perpignan, where he expected letters\par
from M. Quesnel; and it was the expectation of these letters, that had\par
induced him to leave Colioure, for his feeble frame had required\par
immediate rest. After travelling a few miles, he fell asleep; and\par
Emily, who had put two or three books into the carriage, on leaving La\par
Vallée, had now the leisure for looking into them. She sought for one,\par
in which Valancourt had been reading the day before, and hoped for the\par
pleasure of retracing a page, over which the eyes of a beloved friend\par
had lately passed, of dwelling on the passages, which he had admired,\par
and of permitting them to speak to her in the language of his own mind,\par
and to bring himself to her presence. On searching for the book, she\par
could find it nowhere, but in its stead perceived a volume of\par
Petrarch’s poems, that had belonged to Valancourt, whose name was\par
written in it, and from which he had frequently read passages to her,\par
with all the pathetic expression, that characterised the feelings of\par
the author. She hesitated in believing, what would have been\par
sufficiently apparent to almost any other person, that he had purposely\par
left this book, instead of the one she had lost, and that love had\par
prompted the exchange; but, having opened it with impatient pleasure,\par
and observed the lines of his pencil drawn along the various passages\par
he had read aloud, and under others more descriptive of delicate\par
tenderness than he had dared to trust his voice with, the conviction\par
came, at length, to her mind. For some moments she was conscious only\par
of being beloved; then, a recollection of all the variations of tone\par
and countenance, with which he had recited these sonnets, and of the\par
soul, which spoke in their expression, pressed to her memory, and she\par
wept over the memorial of his affection.\par
\par
They arrived at Perpignan soon after sunset, where St. Aubert found, as\par
he had expected, letters from M. Quesnel, the contents of which so\par
evidently and grievously affected him, that Emily was alarmed, and\par
pressed him, as far as her delicacy would permit, to disclose the\par
occasion of his concern; but he answered her only by tears, and\par
immediately began to talk on other topics. Emily, though she forbore to\par
press the one most interesting to her, was greatly affected by her\par
father’s manner, and passed a night of sleepless solicitude.\par
\par
In the morning they pursued their journey along the coast towards\par
Leucate, another town on the Mediterranean, situated on the borders of\par
Languedoc and Rousillon. On the way, Emily renewed the subject of the\par
preceding night, and appeared so deeply affected by St. Aubert’s\par
silence and dejection, that he relaxed from his reserve. “I was\par
unwilling, my dear Emily,” said he, “to throw a cloud over the pleasure\par
you receive from these scenes, and meant, therefore, to conceal, for\par
the present, some circumstances, with which, however, you must at\par
length have been made acquainted. But your anxiety has defeated my\par
purpose; you suffer as much from this, perhaps, as you will do from a\par
knowledge of the facts I have to relate. M. Quesnel’s visit proved an\par
unhappy one to me; he came to tell me part of the news he has now\par
confirmed. You may have heard me mention a M. Motteville, of Paris, but\par
you did not know that the chief of my personal property was invested in\par
his hands. I had great confidence in him, and I am yet willing to\par
believe, that he is not wholly unworthy of my esteem. A variety of\par
circumstances have concurred to ruin him, and—I am ruined with him.”\par
\par
St. Aubert paused to conceal his emotion.\par
\par
“The letters I have just received from M. Quesnel,” resumed he,\par
struggling to speak with firmness, “enclosed others from Motteville,\par
which confirmed all I dreaded.”\par
\par
“Must we then quit La Vallée?” said Emily, after a long pause of\par
silence. “That is yet uncertain,” replied St. Aubert, “it will depend\par
upon the compromise Motteville is able to make with his creditors. My\par
income, you know, was never large, and now it will be reduced to little\par
indeed! It is for you, Emily, for you, my child, that I am most\par
afflicted.” His last words faltered; Emily smiled tenderly upon him\par
through her tears, and then, endeavouring to overcome her emotion, “My\par
dear father,” said she, “do not grieve for me, or for yourself; we may\par
yet be happy;—if La Vallée remains for us, we must be happy. We will\par
retain only one servant, and you shall scarcely perceive the change in\par
your income. Be comforted, my dear sir; we shall not feel the want of\par
those luxuries, which others value so highly, since we never had a\par
taste for them; and poverty cannot deprive us of many consolations. It\par
cannot rob us of the affection we have for each other, or degrade us in\par
our own opinion, or in that of any person, whose opinion we ought to\par
value.”\par
\par
St. Aubert concealed his face with his handkerchief, and was unable to\par
speak; but Emily continued to urge to her father the truths, which\par
himself had impressed upon her mind.\par
\par
“Besides, my dear sir, poverty cannot deprive us of intellectual\par
delights. It cannot deprive you of the comfort of affording me examples\par
of fortitude and benevolence; nor me of the delight of consoling a\par
beloved parent. It cannot deaden our taste for the grand, and the\par
beautiful, or deny us the means of indulging it; for the scenes of\par
nature—those sublime spectacles, so infinitely superior to all\par
artificial luxuries! are open for the enjoyment of the poor, as well as\par
of the rich. Of what, then, have we to complain, so long as we are not\par
in want of necessaries? Pleasures, such as wealth cannot buy, will\par
still be ours. We retain, then, the sublime luxuries of nature, and\par
lose only the frivolous ones of art.”\par
\par
St. Aubert could not reply: he caught Emily to his bosom, their tears\par
flowed together, but—they were not tears of sorrow. After this language\par
of the heart, all other would have been feeble, and they remained\par
silent for some time. Then, St. Aubert conversed as before; for, if his\par
mind had not recovered its natural tranquillity, it at least assumed\par
the appearance of it.\par
\par
They reached the romantic town of Leucate early in the day, but St.\par
Aubert was weary, and they determined to pass the night there. In the\par
evening, he exerted himself so far as to walk with his daughter to view\par
the environs that overlook the lake of Leucate, the Mediterranean, part\par
of Rousillon, with the Pyrenees, and a wide extent of the luxuriant\par
province of Languedoc, now blushing with the ripened vintage, which the\par
peasants were beginning to gather. St. Aubert and Emily saw the busy\par
groups, caught the joyous song, that was wafted on the breeze, and\par
anticipated, with apparent pleasure, their next day’s journey over this\par
gay region. He designed, however, still to wind along the sea-shore. To\par
return home immediately was partly his wish, but from this he was\par
withheld by a desire to lengthen the pleasure, which the journey gave\par
his daughter, and to try the effect of the sea air on his own disorder.\par
\par
On the following day, therefore, they recommenced their journey through\par
Languedoc, winding the shores of the Mediterranean; the Pyrenees still\par
forming the magnificent back-ground of their prospects, while on their\par
right was the ocean, and, on their left, wide extended plains melting\par
into the blue horizon. St. Aubert was pleased, and conversed much with\par
Emily, yet his cheerfulness was sometimes artificial, and sometimes a\par
shade of melancholy would steal upon his countenance, and betray him.\par
This was soon chased away by Emily’s smile; who smiled, however, with\par
an aching heart, for she saw that his misfortunes preyed upon his mind,\par
and upon his enfeebled frame.\par
\par
It was evening when they reached a small village of Upper Languedoc,\par
where they meant to pass the night, but the place could not afford them\par
beds; for here, too, it was the time of the vintage, and they were\par
obliged to proceed to the next post. The languor of illness and of\par
fatigue, which returned upon St. Aubert, required immediate repose, and\par
the evening was now far advanced; but from necessity there was no\par
appeal, and he ordered Michael to proceed.\par
\par
The rich plains of Languedoc, which exhibited all the glories of the\par
vintage, with the gaieties of a French festival, no longer awakened St.\par
Aubert to pleasure, whose condition formed a mournful contrast to the\par
hilarity and youthful beauty which surrounded him. As his languid eyes\par
moved over the scene, he considered, that they would soon, perhaps, be\par
closed for ever on this world. “Those distant and sublime mountains,”\par
said he secretly, as he gazed on a chain of the Pyrenees that stretched\par
towards the west, “these luxuriant plains, this blue vault, the\par
cheerful light of day, will be shut from my eyes! The song of the\par
peasant, the cheering voice of man—will no longer sound for me!”\par
\par
The intelligent eyes of Emily seemed to read what passed in the mind of\par
her father, and she fixed them on his face, with an expression of such\par
tender pity, as recalled his thoughts from every desultory object of\par
regret, and he remembered only, that he must leave his daughter without\par
protection. This reflection changed regret to agony; he sighed deeply,\par
and remained silent, while she seemed to understand that sigh, for she\par
pressed his hand affectionately, and then turned to the window to\par
conceal her tears. The sun now threw a last yellow gleam on the waves\par
of the Mediterranean, and the gloom of twilight spread fast over the\par
scene, till only a melancholy ray appeared on the western horizon,\par
marking the point where the sun had set amid the vapours of an autumnal\par
evening. A cool breeze now came from the shore, and Emily let down the\par
glass; but the air, which was refreshing to health, was as chilling to\par
sickness, and St. Aubert desired, that the window might be drawn up.\par
Increasing illness made him now more anxious than ever to finish the\par
day’s journey, and he stopped the muleteer to enquire how far they had\par
yet to go to the next post. He replied, “Nine miles.” “I feel I am\par
unable to proceed much further,” said St. Aubert; “enquire, as you go,\par
if there is any house on the road that would accommodate us for the\par
night.” He sunk back in the carriage, and Michael, cracking his whip in\par
the air, set off, and continued on the full gallop, till St. Aubert,\par
almost fainting, called to him to stop. Emily looked anxiously from the\par
window, and saw a peasant walking at some little distance on the road,\par
for whom they waited, till he came up, when he was asked, if there was\par
any house in the neighbourhood that accommodated travellers. He\par
replied, that he knew of none. “There is a château, indeed, among those\par
woods on the right,” added he, “but I believe it receives nobody, and I\par
cannot show you the way, for I am almost a stranger here.” St. Aubert\par
was going to ask him some further question concerning the château, but\par
the man abruptly passed on. After some consideration, he ordered\par
Michael to proceed slowly to the woods. Every moment now deepened the\par
twilight, and increased the difficulty of finding the road. Another\par
peasant soon after passed. “Which is the way to the château in the\par
woods?” cried Michael.\par
\par
“The château in the woods!” exclaimed the peasant—“Do you mean that\par
with the turret, yonder?”\par
\par
“I don’t know as for the turret, as you call it,” said Michael, “I mean\par
that white piece of a building, that we see at a distance there, among\par
the trees.”\par
\par
“Yes, that is the turret; why, who are you, that you are going\par
thither?” said the man with surprise.\par
\par
St. Aubert, on hearing this odd question, and observing the peculiar\par
tone in which it was delivered, looked out from the carriage. “We are\par
travellers,” said he, “who are in search of a house of accommodation\par
for the night; is there any hereabout?”\par
\par
“None, Monsieur, unless you have a mind to try your luck yonder,”\par
replied the peasant, pointing to the woods, “but I would not advise you\par
to go there.”\par
\par
“To whom does the château belong?”\par
\par
“I scarcely know myself, Monsieur.”\par
\par
“It is uninhabited, then?”—“No, not uninhabited; the steward and\par
housekeeper are there, I believe.”\par
\par
On hearing this, St. Aubert determined to proceed to the château, and\par
risk the refusal of being accommodated for the night; he therefore\par
desired the countryman would show Michael the way, and bade him expect\par
reward for his trouble. The man was for a moment silent, and then said,\par
that he was going on other business, but that the road could not be\par
missed, if they went up an avenue to the right, to which he pointed.\par
St. Aubert was going to speak, but the peasant wished him good night,\par
and walked on.\par
\par
The carriage now moved towards the avenue, which was guarded by a gate,\par
and Michael having dismounted to open it, they entered between rows of\par
ancient oak and chesnut, whose intermingled branches formed a lofty\par
arch above. There was something so gloomy and desolate in the\par
appearance of this avenue, and its lonely silence, that Emily almost\par
shuddered as she passed along; and, recollecting the manner in which\par
the peasant had mentioned the château, she gave a mysterious meaning to\par
his words, such as she had not suspected when he uttered them. These\par
apprehensions, however, she tried to check, considering that they were\par
probably the effect of a melancholy imagination, which her father’s\par
situation, and a consideration of her own circumstances, had made\par
sensible to every impression.\par
\par
They passed slowly on, for they were now almost in darkness, which,\par
together with the unevenness of the ground, and the frequent roots of\par
old trees, that shot up above the soil, made it necessary to proceed\par
with caution. On a sudden Michael stopped the carriage; and, as St.\par
Aubert looked from the window to enquire the cause, he perceived a\par
figure at some distance moving up the avenue. The dusk would not permit\par
him to distinguish what it was, but he bade Michael go on.\par
\par
“This seems a wild place,” said Michael; “there is no house hereabout,\par
don’t your honour think we had better turn back?”\par
\par
“Go a little farther, and if we see no house then, we will return to\par
the road,” replied St. Aubert.\par
\par
Michael proceeded with reluctance, and the extreme slowness of his pace\par
made St. Aubert look again from the window to hasten him, when again he\par
saw the same figure. He was somewhat startled: probably the gloominess\par
of the spot made him more liable to alarm than usual; however this\par
might be, he now stopped Michael, and bade him call to the person in\par
the avenue.\par
\par
“Please your honour, he may be a robber,” said Michael. “It does not\par
please me,” replied St. Aubert, who could not forbear smiling at the\par
simplicity of his phrase, “and we will, therefore, return to the road,\par
for I see no probability of meeting here with what we seek.”\par
\par
Michael turned about immediately, and was retracing his way with\par
alacrity, when a voice was heard from among the trees on the left. It\par
was not the voice of command, or distress, but a deep hollow tone,\par
which seemed to be scarcely human. The man whipped his mules till they\par
went as fast as possible, regardless of the darkness, the broken\par
ground, and the necks of the whole party, nor once stopped till he\par
reached the gate, which opened from the avenue into the high-road,\par
where he went into a more moderate pace.\par
\par
“I am very ill,” said St. Aubert, taking his daughter’s hand. “You are\par
worse, then, sir!” said Emily, extremely alarmed by his manner, “you\par
are worse, and here is no assistance. Good God! what is to be done!” He\par
leaned his head on her shoulder, while she endeavoured to support him\par
with her arm, and Michael was again ordered to stop. When the rattling\par
of the wheels had ceased, music was heard on their air; it was to Emily\par
the voice of Hope. “Oh! we are near some human habitation!” said she,\par
“help may soon be had.”\par
\par
She listened anxiously; the sounds were distant, and seemed to come\par
from a remote part of the woods that bordered the road; and, as she\par
looked towards the spot whence they issued, she perceived in the faint\par
moonlight something like a château. It was difficult, however, to reach\par
this; St. Aubert was now too ill to bear the motion of the carriage;\par
Michael could not quit his mules; and Emily, who still supported her\par
father, feared to leave him, and also feared to venture alone to such a\par
distance, she knew not whither, or to whom. Something, however, it was\par
necessary to determine upon immediately; St. Aubert, therefore, told\par
Michael to proceed slowly; but they had not gone far, when he fainted,\par
and the carriage was again stopped. He lay quite senseless.—“My dear,\par
dear father!” cried Emily in great agony, who began to fear that he was\par
dying, “speak, if it is only one word to let me hear the sound of your\par
voice!” But no voice spoke in reply. In the agony of terror she bade\par
Michael bring water from the rivulet, that flowed along the road; and,\par
having received some in the man’s hat, with trembling hands she\par
sprinkled it over her father’s face, which, as the moon’s rays now fell\par
upon it, seemed to bear the impression of death. Every emotion of\par
selfish fear now gave way to a stronger influence, and, committing St.\par
Aubert to the care of Michael, who refused to go far from his mules,\par
she stepped from the carriage in search of the château she had seen at\par
a distance. It was a still moonlight night, and the music, which yet\par
sounded on the air, directed her steps from the high road, up a shadowy\par
lane, that led to the woods. Her mind was for some time so entirely\par
occupied by anxiety and terror for her father, that she felt none for\par
herself, till the deepening gloom of the overhanging foliage, which now\par
wholly excluded the moonlight, and the wildness of the place, recalled\par
her to a sense of her adventurous situation. The music had ceased, and\par
she had no guide but chance. For a moment she paused in terrified\par
perplexity, till a sense of her father’s condition again overcoming\par
every consideration for herself, she proceeded. The lane terminated in\par
the woods, but she looked round in vain for a house, or a human being,\par
and as vainly listened for a sound to guide her. She hurried on,\par
however, not knowing whither, avoiding the recesses of the woods, and\par
endeavouring to keep along their margin, till a rude kind of avenue,\par
which opened upon a moonlight spot, arrested her attention. The\par
wildness of this avenue brought to her recollection the one leading to\par
the turreted château, and she was inclined to believe, that this was a\par
part of the same domain, and probably led to the same point. While she\par
hesitated, whether to follow it or not, a sound of many voices in loud\par
merriment burst upon her ear. It seemed not the laugh of cheerfulness,\par
but of riot, and she stood appalled. While she paused, she heard a\par
distant voice, calling from the way she had come, and not doubting but\par
it was that of Michael, her first impulse was to hasten back; but a\par
second thought changed her purpose; she believed that nothing less than\par
the last extremity could have prevailed with Michael to quit his mules,\par
and fearing that her father was now dying, she rushed forward, with a\par
feeble hope of obtaining assistance from the people in the woods. Her\par
heart beat with fearful expectation, as she drew near the spot whence\par
the voices issued, and she often startled when her steps disturbed the\par
fallen leaves. The sounds led her towards the moonlight glade she had\par
before noticed; at a little distance from which she stopped, and saw,\par
between the boles of the trees, a small circular level of green turf,\par
surrounded by the woods, on which appeared a group of figures. On\par
drawing nearer, she distinguished these, by their dress, to be\par
peasants, and perceived several cottages scattered round the edge of\par
the woods, which waved loftily over this spot. While she gazed, and\par
endeavoured to overcome the apprehensions that withheld her steps,\par
several peasant girls came out of a cottage; music instantly struck up,\par
and the dance began. It was the joyous music of the vintage—the same\par
she had before heard upon the air. Her heart, occupied with terror for\par
her father, could not feel the contrast, which this gay scene offered\par
to her own distress; she stepped hastily forward towards a group of\par
elder peasants, who were seated at the door of a cottage, and, having\par
explained her situation, entreated their assistance. Several of them\par
rose with alacrity, and, offering any service in their power, followed\par
Emily, who seemed to move on the wind, as fast as they could towards\par
the road.\par
\par
When she reached the carriage she found St. Aubert restored to\par
animation. On the recovery of his senses, having heard from Michael\par
whither his daughter was gone, anxiety for her overcame every regard\par
for himself, and he had sent him in search of her. He was, however,\par
still languid, and, perceiving himself unable to travel much farther,\par
he renewed his enquiries for an inn, and concerning the château in the\par
woods. “The château cannot accommodate you, sir,” said a venerable\par
peasant who had followed Emily from the woods, “it is scarcely\par
inhabited; but, if you will do me the honour to visit my cottage, you\par
shall be welcome to the best bed it affords.”\par
\par
St. Aubert was himself a Frenchman; he therefore was not surprised at\par
French courtesy; but, ill as he was, he felt the value of the offer\par
enhanced by the manner which accompanied it. He had too much delicacy\par
to apologise, or to appear to hesitate about availing himself of the\par
peasant’s hospitality, but immediately accepted it with the same\par
frankness with which it was offered.\par
\par
The carriage again moved slowly on; Michael following the peasants up\par
the lane, which Emily had just quitted, till they came to the moonlight\par
glade. St. Aubert’s spirits were so far restored by the courtesy of his\par
host, and the near prospect of repose, that he looked with a sweet\par
complacency upon the moonlight scene, surrounded by the shadowy woods,\par
through which, here and there, an opening admitted the streaming\par
splendour, discovering a cottage, or a sparkling rivulet. He listened,\par
with no painful emotion, to the merry notes of the guitar and\par
tamborine; and, though tears came to his eyes, when he saw the\par
_debonnaire_ dance of the peasants, they were not merely tears of\par
mournful regret. With Emily it was otherwise; immediate terror for her\par
father had now subsided into a gentle melancholy, which every note of\par
joy, by awakening comparison, served to heighten.\par
\par
The dance ceased on the approach of the carriage, which was a\par
phenomenon in these sequestered woods, and the peasantry flocked round\par
it with eager curiosity. On learning that it brought a sick stranger,\par
several girls ran across the turf, and returned with wine and baskets\par
of grapes, which they presented to the travellers, each with kind\par
contention pressing for a preference. At length, the carriage stopped\par
at a neat cottage, and his venerable conductor, having assisted St.\par
Aubert to alight, led him and Emily to a small inner room, illuminated\par
only by moonbeams, which the open casement admitted. St. Aubert,\par
rejoicing in rest, seated himself in an arm-chair, and his senses were\par
refreshed by the cool and balmy air, that lightly waved the embowering\par
honeysuckles, and wafted their sweet breath into the apartment. His\par
host, who was called La Voisin, quitted the room, but soon returned\par
with fruits, cream, and all the pastoral luxury his cottage afforded;\par
having set down which, with a smile of unfeigned welcome, he retired\par
behind the chair of his guest. St. Aubert insisted on his taking a seat\par
at the table, and, when the fruit had allayed the fever of his palate,\par
and he found himself somewhat revived, he began to converse with his\par
host, who communicated several particulars concerning himself and his\par
family, which were interesting, because they were spoken from the\par
heart, and delineated a picture of the sweet courtesies of family\par
kindness. Emily sat by her father, holding his hand, and, while she\par
listened to the old man, her heart swelled with the affectionate\par
sympathy he described, and her tears fell to the mournful\par
consideration, that death would probably soon deprive her of the\par
dearest blessing she then possessed. The soft moonlight of an autumnal\par
evening, and the distant music, which now sounded a plaintive strain,\par
aided the melancholy of her mind. The old man continued to talk of his\par
family, and St. Aubert remained silent. “I have only one daughter\par
living,” said La Voisin, “but she is happily married, and is everything\par
to me. When I lost my wife,” he added with a sigh, “I came to live with\par
Agnes, and her family; she has several children, who are all dancing on\par
the green yonder, as merry as grasshoppers—and long may they be so! I\par
hope to die among them, monsieur. I am old now, and cannot expect to\par
live long, but there is some comfort in dying surrounded by one’s\par
children.”\par
\par
“My good friend,” said St. Aubert, while his voice trembled, “I hope\par
you will long live surrounded by them.”\par
\par
“Ah, sir! at my age I must not expect that!” replied the old man, and\par
he paused: “I can scarcely wish it,” he resumed, “for I trust that\par
whenever I die I shall go to heaven, where my poor wife is gone before\par
me. I can sometimes almost fancy I see her of a still moonlight night,\par
walking among these shades she loved so well. Do you believe, monsieur,\par
that we shall be permitted to revisit the earth, after we have quitted\par
the body?”\par
\par
Emily could no longer stifle the anguish of her heart; her tears fell\par
fast upon her father’s hand, which she yet held. He made an effort to\par
speak, and at length said in a low voice, “I hope we shall be permitted\par
to look down on those we have left on the earth, but I can only hope\par
it. Futurity is much veiled from our eyes, and faith and hope are our\par
only guides concerning it. We are not enjoined to believe, that\par
disembodied spirits watch over the friends they have loved, but we may\par
innocently hope it. It is a hope which I will never resign,” continued\par
he, while he wiped the tears from his daughter’s eyes, “it will sweeten\par
the bitter moments of death!” Tears fell slowly on his cheeks; La\par
Voisin wept too, and there was a pause of silence. Then, La Voisin,\par
renewing the subject, said, “But you believe, sir, that we shall meet\par
in another world the relations we have loved in this; I must believe\par
this.” “Then do believe it,” replied St. Aubert, “severe, indeed, would\par
be the pangs of separation, if we believed it to be eternal. Look up,\par
my dear Emily, we shall meet again!” He lifted his eyes towards heaven,\par
and a gleam of moonlight, which fell upon his countenance, discovered\par
peace and resignation, stealing on the lines of sorrow.\par
\par
La Voisin felt that he had pursued the subject too far, and he dropped\par
it, saying, “We are in darkness, I forgot to bring a light.”\par
\par
“No,” said St. Aubert, “this is a light I love. Sit down, my good\par
friend. Emily, my love, I find myself better than I have been all day;\par
this air refreshes me. I can enjoy this tranquil hour, and that music,\par
which floats so sweetly at a distance. Let me see you smile. Who\par
touches that guitar so tastefully? are there two instruments, or is it\par
an echo I hear?”\par
\par
“It is an echo, monsieur, I fancy. That guitar is often heard at night,\par
when all is still, but nobody knows who touches it, and it is sometimes\par
accompanied by a voice so sweet, and so sad, one would almost think the\par
woods were haunted.” “They certainly are haunted,” said St. Aubert with\par
a smile, “but I believe it is by mortals.” “I have sometimes heard it\par
at midnight, when I could not sleep,” rejoined La Voisin, not seeming\par
to notice this remark, “almost under my window, and I never heard any\par
music like it. It has often made me think of my poor wife till I cried.\par
I have sometimes got up to the window to look if I could see anybody,\par
but as soon as I opened the casement all was hushed, and nobody to be\par
seen; and I have listened, and listened till I have been so timorous,\par
that even the trembling of the leaves in the breeze has made me start.\par
They say it often comes to warn people of their death, but I have heard\par
it these many years, and outlived the warning.”\par
\par
Emily, though she smiled at the mention of this ridiculous\par
superstition, could not, in the present tone of her spirits, wholly\par
resist its contagion.\par
\par
“Well, but, my good friend,” said St. Aubert, “has nobody had courage\par
to follow the sounds? If they had, they would probably have discovered\par
who is the musician.” “Yes, sir, they have followed them some way into\par
the woods, but the music has still retreated, and seemed as distant as\par
ever, and the people have at last been afraid of being led into harm,\par
and would go no further. It is very seldom that I have heard these\par
sounds so early in the evening. They usually come about midnight, when\par
that bright planet, which is rising above the turret yonder, sets below\par
the woods on the left.”\par
\par
“What turret?” asked St. Aubert with quickness, “I see none.”\par
\par
“Your pardon, monsieur, you do see one indeed, for the moon shines full\par
upon it;—up the avenue yonder, a long way off; the château it belongs\par
to is hid among the trees.”\par
\par
“Yes, my dear sir,” said Emily, pointing, “don’t you see something\par
glitter above the dark woods? It is a fane, I fancy, which the rays\par
fall upon.”\par
\par
“O yes, I see what you mean; and who does the château belong to?”\par
\par
“The Marquis de Villeroi was its owner,” replied La Voisin,\par
emphatically.\par
\par
“Ah!” said St. Aubert, with a deep sigh, “are we then so near\par
Le-Blanc!” He appeared much agitated.\par
\par
“It used to be the Marquis’s favourite residence,” resumed La Voisin,\par
“but he took a dislike to the place, and has not been there for many\par
years. We have heard lately that he is dead, and that it is fallen into\par
other hands.” St. Aubert, who had sat in deep musing, was roused by the\par
last words. “Dead!” he exclaimed, “Good God! when did he die?”\par
\par
“He is reported to have died about five weeks since,” replied La\par
Voisin. “Did you know the Marquis, sir?”\par
\par
“This is very extraordinary!” said St. Aubert without attending to the\par
question. “Why is it so, my dear sir?” said Emily, in a voice of timid\par
curiosity. He made no reply, but sunk again into a reverie; and in a\par
few moments, when he seemed to have recovered himself, asked who had\par
succeeded to the estates. “I have forgot his title, monsieur,” said La\par
Voisin; “but my lord resides at Paris chiefly; I hear no talk of his\par
coming hither.”\par
\par
“The château is shut up then, still?”\par
\par
“Why, little better, sir; the old housekeeper, and her husband the\par
steward, have the care of it, but they live generally in a cottage hard\par
by.”\par
\par
“The château is spacious, I suppose,” said Emily, “and must be desolate\par
for the residence of only two persons.”\par
\par
“Desolate enough, mademoiselle,” replied La Voisin, “I would not pass\par
one night in the château, for the value of the whole domain.”\par
\par
“What is that?” said St. Aubert, roused again from thoughtfulness. As\par
his host repeated his last sentence, a groan escaped from St. Aubert,\par
and then, as if anxious to prevent it from being noticed, he hastily\par
asked La Voisin how long he had lived in this neighbourhood. “Almost\par
from my childhood, sir,” replied his host.\par
\par
“You remember the late marchioness, then?” said St. Aubert in an\par
altered voice.\par
\par
“Ah, monsieur!—that I do well. There are many besides me who remember\par
her.”\par
\par
“Yes—” said St. Aubert, “and I am one of those.”\par
\par
“Alas, sir! you remember, then, a most beautiful and excellent lady.\par
She deserved a better fate.”\par
\par
Tears stood in St. Aubert’s eyes; “Enough,” said he, in a voice almost\par
stifled by the violence of his emotions,—“it is enough, my friend.”\par
\par
Emily, though extremely surprised by her father’s manner, forbore to\par
express her feelings by any question. La Voisin began to apologise, but\par
St. Aubert interrupted him; “Apology is quite unnecessary,” said he,\par
“let us change the topic. You were speaking of the music we just now\par
heard.”\par
\par
“I was, monsieur—but hark!—it comes again; listen to that voice!” They\par
were all silent;\par
\par
At last a soft and solemn-breathing sound\par
Rose, like a stream of rich distilled perfumes,\par
And stole upon the air, that even Silence\par
Was took ere she was ’ware, and wished she might\par
Deny her nature, and be never more\par
Still, to be so displaced.\par
                    MILTON.\par
\par
\par
In a few moments the voice died into air, and the instrument, which had\par
been heard before, sounded in low symphony. St. Aubert now observed,\par
that it produced a tone much more full and melodious than that of a\par
guitar, and still more melancholy and soft than the lute. They\par
continued to listen, but the sounds returned no more. “This is\par
strange!” said St. Aubert, at length interrupting the silence. “Very\par
strange!” said Emily. “It is so,” rejoined La Voisin, and they were\par
again silent.\par
\par
After a long pause, “It is now about eighteen years since I first heard\par
that music,” said La Voisin; “I remember it was on a fine summer’s\par
night, much like this, but later, that I was walking in the woods, and\par
alone. I remember, too, that my spirits were very low, for one of my\par
boys was ill, and we feared we should lose him. I had been watching at\par
his bedside all the evening while his mother slept; for she had sat up\par
with him the night before. I had been watching, and went out for a\par
little fresh air, the day had been very sultry. As I walked under the\par
shades and mused, I heard music at a distance, and thought it was\par
Claude playing upon his flute, as he often did of a fine evening, at\par
the cottage door. But, when I came to a place where the trees opened,\par
(I shall never forget it!) and stood looking up at the north-lights,\par
which shot up the heaven to a great height, I heard all of a sudden\par
such sounds!—they came so as I cannot describe. It was like the music\par
of angels, and I looked up again almost expecting to see them in the\par
sky. When I came home, I told what I had heard, but they laughed at me,\par
and said it must be some of the shepherds playing on their pipes, and I\par
could not persuade them to the contrary. A few nights after, however,\par
my wife herself heard the same sounds, and was as much surprised as I\par
was, and Father Denis frightened her sadly by saying, that it was music\par
come to warn her of her child’s death, and that music often came to\par
houses where there was a dying person.”\par
\par
Emily, on hearing this, shrunk with a superstitious dread entirely new\par
to her, and could scarcely conceal her agitation from St. Aubert.\par
\par
“But the boy lived, monsieur, in spite of Father Denis.”\par
\par
“Father Denis!” said St. Aubert, who had listened to ‘narrative old\par
age’ with patient attention, “are we near a convent, then?”\par
\par
“Yes, sir; the convent of St. Clair stands at no great distance, on the\par
sea shore yonder.”\par
\par
“Ah!” said St. Aubert, as if struck with some sudden remembrance, “the\par
convent of St. Clair!” Emily observed the clouds of grief, mingled with\par
a faint expression of horror, gathering on his brow; his countenance\par
became fixed, and, touched as it now was by the silver whiteness of the\par
moonlight, he resembled one of those marble statues of a monument,\par
which seem to bend, in hopeless sorrow, over the ashes of the dead,\par
shown\par
\par
by the blunted light\par
That the dim moon through painted casements lends.\par
                    THE EMIGRANTS.\par
\par
\par
“But, my dear sir,” said Emily, anxious to dissipate his thoughts, “you\par
forget that repose is necessary to you. If our kind host will give me\par
leave, I will prepare your bed, for I know how you like it to be made.”\par
St. Aubert, recollecting himself, and smiling affectionately, desired\par
she would not add to her fatigue by that attention; and La Voisin,\par
whose consideration for his guest had been suspended by the interests\par
which his own narrative had recalled, now started from his seat, and,\par
apologising for not having called Agnes from the green, hurried out of\par
the room.\par
\par
In a few moments he returned with his daughter, a young woman of\par
pleasing countenance, and Emily learned from her, what she had not\par
before suspected, that, for their accommodation, it was necessary part\par
of La Voisin’s family should leave their beds; she lamented this\par
circumstance, but Agnes, by her reply, fully proved that she inherited,\par
at least, a share of her father’s courteous hospitality. It was\par
settled, that some of her children and Michael should sleep in the\par
neighbouring cottage.\par
\par
“If I am better, tomorrow, my dear,” said St. Aubert when Emily\par
returned to him, “I mean to set out at an early hour, that we may rest,\par
during the heat of the day, and will travel towards home. In the\par
present state of my health and spirits I cannot look on a longer\par
journey with pleasure, and I am also very anxious to reach La Vallée.”\par
Emily, though she also desired to return, was grieved at her father’s\par
sudden wish to do so, which she thought indicated a greater degree of\par
indisposition than he would acknowledge. St. Aubert now retired to\par
rest, and Emily to her little chamber, but not to immediate repose. Her\par
thoughts returned to the late conversation, concerning the state of\par
departed spirits; a subject, at this time, particularly affecting to\par
her, when she had every reason to believe that her dear father would\par
ere long be numbered with them. She leaned pensively on the little open\par
casement, and in deep thought fixed her eyes on the heaven, whose blue\par
unclouded concave was studded thick with stars, the worlds, perhaps, of\par
spirits, unsphered of mortal mould. As her eyes wandered along the\par
boundless æther, her thoughts rose, as before, towards the sublimity of\par
the Deity, and to the contemplation of futurity. No busy note of this\par
world interrupted the course of her mind; the merry dance had ceased,\par
and every cottager had retired to his home. The still air seemed\par
scarcely to breathe upon the woods, and, now and then, the distant\par
sound of a solitary sheep-bell, or of a closing casement, was all that\par
broke on silence. At length, even this hint of human being was heard no\par
more. Elevated and enwrapt, while her eyes were often wet with tears of\par
sublime devotion and solemn awe, she continued at the casement, till\par
the gloom of midnight hung over the earth, and the planet, which La\par
Voisin had pointed out, sunk below the woods. She then recollected what\par
he had said concerning this planet, and the mysterious music; and, as\par
she lingered at the window, half hoping and half fearing that it would\par
return, her mind was led to the remembrance of the extreme emotion her\par
father had shown on mention of the Marquis La Villeroi’s death, and of\par
the fate of the Marchioness, and she felt strongly interested\par
concerning the remote cause of this emotion. Her surprise and curiosity\par
were indeed the greater, because she did not recollect ever to have\par
heard him mention the name of Villeroi.\par
\par
No music, however, stole on the silence of the night, and Emily,\par
perceiving the lateness of the hour, returned to a scene of fatigue,\par
remembered that she was to rise early in the morning, and withdrew from\par
the window to repose.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER VII\par
\par
Let those deplore their doom,\par
Whose hope still grovels in this dark sojourn.\par
But lofty souls can look beyond the tomb,\par
Can smile at fate, and wonder how they mourn.\par
Shall Spring to these sad scenes no more return?\par
Is yonder wave the sun’s eternal bed?—\par
Soon shall the orient with new lustre burn,\par
And Spring shall soon her vital influence shed,\par
Again attune the grove, again adorn the mead!\par
                    BEATTIE\par
\par
\par
Emily, called, as she had requested, at an early hour, awoke, little\par
refreshed by sleep, for uneasy dreams had pursued her, and marred the\par
kindest blessing of the unhappy. But, when she opened her casement,\par
looked out upon the woods, bright with the morning sun, and inspired\par
the pure air, her mind was soothed. The scene was filled with that\par
cheering freshness, which seems to breathe the very spirit of health,\par
and she heard only sweet and _picturesque_ sounds, if such an\par
expression may be allowed—the matin-bell of a distant convent, the\par
faint murmur of the sea-waves, the song of birds, and the far-off low\par
of cattle, which she saw coming slowly on between the trunks of trees.\par
Struck with the circumstances of imagery around her, she indulged the\par
pensive tranquillity which they inspired; and while she leaned on her\par
window, waiting till St. Aubert should descend to breakfast, her ideas\par
arranged themselves in the following lines:\par
\par
THE FIRST HOUR OF MORNING\par
\par
How sweet to wind the forest’s tangled shade,\par
    When early twilight, from the eastern bound,\par
Dawns on the sleeping landscape in the glade,\par
    And fades as morning spreads her blush around!\par
\par
When ev’ry infant flower, that wept in night,\par
    Lifts its chill head soft glowing with a tear,\par
Expands its tender blossom to the light,\par
    And gives its incense to the genial air.\par
\par
How fresh the breeze that wafts the rich perfume,\par
    And swells the melody of waking birds;\par
The hum of bees, beneath the verdant gloom,\par
    And woodman’s song, and low of distant herds!\par
\par
Then, doubtful gleams the mountain’s hoary head,\par
    Seen through the parting foliage from afar;\par
And, farther still, the ocean’s misty bed,\par
    With flitting sails, that partial sunbeams share.\par
\par
But, vain the sylvan shade—the breath of May,\par
    The voice of music floating on the gale,\par
And forms, that beam through morning’s dewy veil,\par
    If health no longer bid the heart be gay!\par
\par
O balmy hour! ’tis thine her wealth to give,\par
Here spread her blush, and bid the parent live!\par
\par
\par
Emily now heard persons moving below in the cottage, and presently the\par
voice of Michael, who was talking to his mules, as he led them forth\par
from a hut adjoining. As she left her room, St. Aubert, who was now\par
risen, met her at the door, apparently as little restored by sleep as\par
herself. She led him down stairs to the little parlour, in which they\par
had supped on the preceding night, where they found a neat breakfast\par
set out, while the host and his daughter waited to bid them\par
good-morrow.\par
\par
“I envy you this cottage, my good friends,” said St. Aubert, as he met\par
them, “it is so pleasant, so quiet, and so neat; and this air, that one\par
breathes—if anything could restore lost health, it would surely be this\par
air.”\par
\par
La Voisin bowed gratefully, and replied, with the gallantry of a\par
Frenchman, “Our cottage may be envied, sir, since you and Mademoiselle\par
have honoured it with your presence.” St. Aubert gave him a friendly\par
smile for his compliment, and sat down to a table, spread with cream,\par
fruit, new cheese, butter, and coffee. Emily, who had observed her\par
father with attention and thought he looked very ill, endeavoured to\par
persuade him to defer travelling till the afternoon; but he seemed very\par
anxious to be at home, and his anxiety he expressed repeatedly, and\par
with an earnestness that was unusual with him. He now said, he found\par
himself as well as he had been of late, and that he could bear\par
travelling better in the cool hour of the morning, than at any other\par
time. But, while he was talking with his venerable host, and thanking\par
him for his kind attentions, Emily observed his countenance change,\par
and, before she could reach him, he fell back in his chair. In a few\par
moments he recovered from the sudden faintness that had come over him,\par
but felt so ill, that he perceived himself unable to set out, and,\par
having remained a little while, struggling against the pressure of\par
indisposition, he begged he might be helped up stairs to bed. This\par
request renewed all the terror which Emily had suffered on the\par
preceding evening; but, though scarcely able to support herself, under\par
the sudden shock it gave her, she tried to conceal her apprehensions\par
from St. Aubert, and gave her trembling arm to assist him to the door\par
of his chamber.\par
\par
When he was once more in bed, he desired that Emily, who was then\par
weeping in her own room, might be called; and, as she came, he waved\par
his hand for every other person to quit the apartment. When they were\par
alone, he held out his hand to her, and fixed his eyes upon her\par
countenance, with an expression so full of tenderness and grief, that\par
all her fortitude forsook her, and she burst into an agony of tears.\par
St. Aubert seemed struggling to acquire firmness, but was still unable\par
to speak; he could only press her hand, and check the tears that stood\par
trembling in his eyes. At length he commanded his voice, “My dear\par
child,” said he, trying to smile through his anguish, “my dear\par
Emily!”—and paused again. He raised his eyes to heaven, as if in\par
prayer, and then, in a firmer tone, and with a look, in which the\par
tenderness of the father was dignified by the pious solemnity of the\par
saint, he said, “My dear child, I would soften the painful truth I have\par
to tell you, but I find myself quite unequal to the art. Alas! I would,\par
at this moment, conceal it from you, but that it would be most cruel to\par
deceive you. It cannot be long before we must part; let us talk of it,\par
that our thoughts and our prayers may prepare us to bear it.” His voice\par
faltered, while Emily, still weeping, pressed his hand close to her\par
heart, which swelled with a convulsive sigh, but she could not look up.\par
\par
“Let me not waste these moments,” said St. Aubert, recovering himself,\par
“I have much to say. There is a circumstance of solemn consequence,\par
which I have to mention, and a solemn promise to obtain from you; when\par
this is done I shall be easier. You have observed, my dear, how anxious\par
I am to reach home, but know not all my reasons for this. Listen to\par
what I am going to say.—Yet stay—before I say more give me this\par
promise, a promise made to your dying father!”—St. Aubert was\par
interrupted; Emily, struck by his last words, as if for the first time,\par
with a conviction of his immediate danger, raised her head; her tears\par
stopped, and, gazing at him for a moment with an expression of\par
unutterable anguish, a slight convulsion seized her, and she sunk\par
senseless in her chair. St. Aubert’s cries brought La Voisin and his\par
daughter to the room, and they administered every means in their power\par
to restore her, but, for a considerable time, without effect. When she\par
recovered, St. Aubert was so exhausted by the scene he had witnessed,\par
that it was many minutes before he had strength to speak; he was,\par
however, somewhat revived by a cordial, which Emily gave him; and,\par
being again alone with her, he exerted himself to tranquilize her\par
spirits, and to offer her all the comfort of which her situation\par
admitted. She threw herself into his arms, wept on his neck, and grief\par
made her so insensible to all he said, that he ceased to offer the\par
alleviations, which he himself could not, at this moment, feel, and\par
mingled his silent tears with hers. Recalled, at length, to a sense of\par
duty, she tried to spare her father from a farther view of her\par
suffering; and, quitting his embrace, dried her tears, and said\par
something, which she meant for consolation. “My dear Emily,” replied\par
St. Aubert, “my dear child, we must look up with humble confidence to\par
that Being, who has protected and comforted us in every danger, and in\par
every affliction we have known; to whose eye every moment of our lives\par
has been exposed; he will not, he does not, forsake us now; I feel his\par
consolations in my heart. I shall leave you, my child, still in his\par
care; and, though I depart from this world, I shall be still in his\par
presence. Nay, weep not again, my Emily. In death there is nothing new,\par
or surprising, since we all know, that we are born to die; and nothing\par
terrible to those, who can confide in an all-powerful God. Had my life\par
been spared now, after a very few years, in the course of nature, I\par
must have resigned it; old age, with all its train of infirmity, its\par
privations and its sorrows, would have been mine; and then, at last,\par
death would have come, and called forth the tears you now shed. Rather,\par
my child, rejoice, that I am saved from such suffering, and that I am\par
permitted to die with a mind unimpaired, and sensible of the comforts\par
of faith and resignation.” St. Aubert paused, fatigued with speaking.\par
Emily again endeavoured to assume an air of composure; and, in replying\par
to what he had said, tried to sooth him with a belief, that he had not\par
spoken in vain.\par
\par
When he had reposed a while, he resumed the conversation. “Let me\par
return,” said he, “to a subject, which is very near my heart. I said I\par
had a solemn promise to receive from you; let me receive it now, before\par
I explain the chief circumstance which it concerns; there are others,\par
of which your peace requires that you should rest in ignorance.\par
Promise, then, that you will perform exactly what I shall enjoin.”\par
\par
Emily, awed by the earnest solemnity of his manner, dried her tears,\par
that had begun again to flow, in spite of her efforts to suppress them;\par
and, looking eloquently at St. Aubert, bound herself to do whatever he\par
should require by a vow, at which she shuddered, yet knew not why.\par
\par
He proceeded: “I know you too well, my Emily, to believe, that you\par
would break any promise, much less one thus solemnly given; your\par
assurance gives me peace, and the observance of it is of the utmost\par
importance to your tranquillity. Hear, then, what I am going to tell\par
you. The closet, which adjoins my chamber at La Vallée, has a sliding\par
board in the floor. You will know it by a remarkable knot in the wood,\par
and by its being the next board, except one, to the wainscot, which\par
fronts the door. At the distance of about a yard from that end, nearer\par
the window, you will perceive a line across it, as if the plank had\par
been joined;—the way to open it is this:—Press your foot upon the line;\par
the end of the board will then sink, and you may slide it with ease\par
beneath the other. Below, you will see a hollow place.” St. Aubert\par
paused for breath, and Emily sat fixed in deep attention. “Do you\par
understand these directions, my dear?” said he. Emily, though scarcely\par
able to speak, assured him that she did.\par
\par
“When you return home, then,” he added with a deep sigh—\par
\par
At the mention of her return home, all the melancholy circumstances,\par
that must attend this return, rushed upon her fancy; she burst into\par
convulsive grief, and St. Aubert himself, affected beyond the\par
resistance of the fortitude which he had, at first, summoned, wept with\par
her. After some moments, he composed himself. “My dear child,” said he,\par
“be comforted. When I am gone, you will not be forsaken—I leave you\par
only in the more immediate care of that Providence, which has never yet\par
forsaken me. Do not afflict me with this excess of grief; rather teach\par
me by your example to bear my own.” He stopped again, and Emily, the\par
more she endeavoured to restrain her emotion, found it the less\par
possible to do so.\par
\par
St. Aubert, who now spoke with pain, resumed the subject. “That closet,\par
my dear,—when you return home, go to it; and, beneath the board I have\par
described, you will find a packet of written papers. Attend to me now,\par
for the promise you have given particularly relates to what I shall\par
direct. These papers you must burn—and, solemnly I command you,\par
_without examining them_.”\par
\par
Emily’s surprise, for a moment, overcame her grief, and she ventured to\par
ask, why this must be? St. Aubert replied, that, if it had been right\par
for him to explain his reasons, her late promise would have been\par
unnecessarily exacted. “It is sufficient for you, my love, to have a\par
deep sense of the importance of observing me in this instance.” St.\par
Aubert proceeded. “Under that board you will also find about two\par
hundred louis d’ors, wrapped in a silk purse; indeed, it was to secure\par
whatever money might be in the château, that this secret place was\par
contrived, at a time when the province was over-run by troops of men,\par
who took advantage of the tumults, and became plunderers.\par
\par
“But I have yet another promise to receive from you, which is—that you\par
will never, whatever may be your future circumstances, _sell_ the\par
château.” St. Aubert even enjoined her, whenever she might marry, to\par
make it an article in the contract, that the château should always be\par
hers. He then gave her a more minute account of his present\par
circumstances than he had yet done, adding, “The two hundred louis,\par
with what money you will now find in my purse, is all the ready money I\par
have to leave you. I have told you how I am circumstanced with M.\par
Motteville, at Paris. Ah, my child! I leave you poor—but not\par
destitute,” he added, after a long pause. Emily could make no reply to\par
anything he now said, but knelt at the bedside, with her face upon the\par
quilt, weeping over the hand she held there.\par
\par
After this conversation, the mind of St. Aubert appeared to be much\par
more at ease; but, exhausted by the effort of speaking, he sunk into a\par
kind of doze, and Emily continued to watch and weep beside him, till a\par
gentle tap at the chamber-door roused her. It was La Voisin, come to\par
say, that a confessor from the neighbouring convent was below, ready to\par
attend St. Aubert. Emily would not suffer her father to be disturbed,\par
but desired, that the priest might not leave the cottage. When St.\par
Aubert awoke from this doze, his senses were confused, and it was some\par
moments before he recovered them sufficiently to know, that it was\par
Emily who sat beside him. He then moved his lips, and stretched forth\par
his hand to her; as she received which, she sunk back in her chair,\par
overcome by the impression of death on his countenance. In a few\par
minutes he recovered his voice, and Emily then asked, if he wished to\par
see the confessor; he replied, that he did; and, when the holy father\par
appeared, she withdrew. They remained alone together above half an\par
hour; when Emily was called in, she found St. Aubert more agitated than\par
when she had left him, and she gazed, with a slight degree of\par
resentment, at the friar, as the cause of this; who, however, looked\par
mildly and mournfully at her, and turned away. St. Aubert, in a\par
tremulous voice, said, he wished her to join in prayer with him, and\par
asked if La Voisin would do so too. The old man and his daughter came;\par
they both wept, and knelt with Emily round the bed, while the holy\par
father read in a solemn voice the service for the dying. St. Aubert lay\par
with a serene countenance, and seemed to join fervently in the\par
devotion, while tears often stole from beneath his closed eyelids, and\par
Emily’s sobs more than once interrupted the service.\par
\par
When it was concluded, and extreme unction had been administered, the\par
friar withdrew. St. Aubert then made a sign for La Voisin to come\par
nearer. He gave him his hand, and was, for a moment, silent. At length,\par
he said, in a trembling voice, “My good friend, our acquaintance has\par
been short, but long enough to give you an opportunity of showing me\par
much kind attention. I cannot doubt, that you will extend this kindness\par
to my daughter, when I am gone; she will have need of it. I entrust her\par
to your care during the few days she will remain here. I need say no\par
more—you know the feelings of a father, for you have children; mine\par
would be, indeed, severe if I had less confidence in you.” He paused.\par
La Voisin assured him, and his tears bore testimony to his sincerity,\par
that he would do all he could to soften her affliction, and that, if\par
St. Aubert wished it, he would even attend her into Gascony; an offer\par
so pleasing to St. Aubert, that he had scarcely words to acknowledge\par
his sense of the old man’s kindness, or to tell him, that he accepted\par
it. The scene, that followed between St. Aubert and Emily, affected La\par
Voisin so much, that he quitted the chamber, and she was again left\par
alone with her father, whose spirits seemed fainting fast, but neither\par
his senses, nor his voice, yet failed him; and, at intervals, he\par
employed much of these last awful moments in advising his daughter, as\par
to her future conduct. Perhaps, he never had thought more justly, or\par
expressed himself more clearly, than he did now.\par
\par
“Above all, my dear Emily,” said he, “do not indulge in the pride of\par
fine feeling, the romantic error of amiable minds. Those, who really\par
possess sensibility, ought early to be taught, that it is a dangerous\par
quality, which is continually extracting the excess of misery, or\par
delight, from every surrounding circumstance. And, since, in our\par
passage through this world, painful circumstances occur more frequently\par
than pleasing ones, and since our sense of evil is, I fear, more acute\par
than our sense of good, we become the victims of our feelings, unless\par
we can in some degree command them. I know you will say, (for you are\par
young, my Emily) I know you will say, that you are contented sometimes\par
to suffer, rather than to give up your refined sense of happiness, at\par
others; but, when your mind has been long harassed by vicissitude, you\par
will be content to rest, and you will then recover from your delusion.\par
You will perceive, that the phantom of happiness is exchanged for the\par
substance; for happiness arises in a state of peace, not of tumult. It\par
is of a temperate and uniform nature, and can no more exist in a heart,\par
that is continually alive to minute circumstances, than in one that is\par
dead to feeling. You see, my dear, that, though I would guard you\par
against the dangers of sensibility, I am not an advocate for apathy. At\par
your age I should have said _that_ is a vice more hateful than all the\par
errors of sensibility, and I say so still. I call it a _vice_, because\par
it leads to positive evil; in this, however, it does no more than an\par
ill-governed sensibility, which, by such a rule, might also be called a\par
vice; but the evil of the former is of more general consequence. I have\par
exhausted myself,” said St. Aubert, feebly, “and have wearied you, my\par
Emily; but, on a subject so important to your future comfort, I am\par
anxious to be perfectly understood.”\par
\par
Emily assured him, that his advice was most precious to her, and that\par
she would never forget it, or cease from endeavouring to profit by it.\par
St. Aubert smiled affectionately and sorrowfully upon her. “I repeat\par
it,” said he, “I would not teach you to become insensible, if I could;\par
I would only warn you of the evils of susceptibility, and point out how\par
you may avoid them. Beware, my love, I conjure you, of that\par
self-delusion, which has been fatal to the peace of so many persons;\par
beware of priding yourself on the gracefulness of sensibility; if you\par
yield to this vanity, your happiness is lost for ever. Always remember\par
how much more valuable is the strength of fortitude, than the grace of\par
sensibility. Do not, however, confound fortitude with apathy; apathy\par
cannot know the virtue. Remember, too, that one act of beneficence, one\par
act of real usefulness, is worth all the abstract sentiment in the\par
world. Sentiment is a disgrace, instead of an ornament, unless it lead\par
us to good actions. The miser, who thinks himself respectable, merely\par
because he possesses wealth, and thus mistakes the means of doing good,\par
for the actual accomplishment of it, is not more blameable than the man\par
of sentiment, without active virtue. You may have observed persons, who\par
delight so much in this sort of sensibility to sentiment, which\par
excludes that to the calls of any practical virtue, that they turn from\par
the distressed, and, because their sufferings are painful to be\par
contemplated, do not endeavour to relieve them. How despicable is that\par
humanity, which can be contented to pity, where it might assuage!”\par
\par
St. Aubert, some time after, spoke of Madame Cheron, his sister. “Let\par
me inform you of a circumstance, that nearly affects your welfare,” he\par
added. “We have, you know, had little intercourse for some years, but,\par
as she is now your only female relation, I have thought it proper to\par
consign you to her care, as you will see in my will, till you are of\par
age, and to recommend you to her protection afterwards. She is not\par
exactly the person, to whom I would have committed my Emily, but I had\par
no alternative, and I believe her to be upon the whole—a good kind of\par
woman. I need not recommend it to your prudence, my love, to endeavour\par
to conciliate her kindness; you will do this for his sake, who has\par
often wished to do so for yours.”\par
\par
Emily assured him, that, whatever he requested she would religiously\par
perform to the utmost of her ability. “Alas!” added she, in a voice\par
interrupted by sighs, “that will soon be all which remains for me; it\par
will be almost my only consolation to fulfil your wishes.”\par
\par
St. Aubert looked up silently in her face, as if would have spoken, but\par
his spirit sunk a while, and his eyes became heavy and dull. She felt\par
that look at her heart. “My dear father!” she exclaimed; and then,\par
checking herself, pressed his hand closer, and hid her face with her\par
handkerchief. Her tears were concealed, but St. Aubert heard her\par
convulsive sobs. His spirits returned. “O my child!” said he, faintly,\par
“let my consolations be yours. I die in peace; for I know, that I am\par
about to return to the bosom of my Father, who will still be your\par
Father, when I am gone. Always trust in him, my love, and he will\par
support you in these moments, as he supports me.”\par
\par
Emily could only listen, and weep; but the extreme composure of his\par
manner, and the faith and hope he expressed, somewhat soothed her\par
anguish. Yet, whenever she looked upon his emaciated countenance, and\par
saw the lines of death beginning to prevail over it—saw his sunk eyes,\par
still bent on her, and their heavy lids pressing to a close, there was\par
a pang in her heart, such as defied expression, though it required\par
filial virtue, like hers, to forbear the attempt.\par
\par
He desired once more to bless her; “Where are you, my dear?” said he,\par
as he stretched forth his hands. Emily had turned to the window, that\par
he might not perceive her anguish; she now understood, that his sight\par
had failed him. When he had given her his blessing, and it seemed to be\par
the last effort of expiring life, he sunk back on his pillow. She\par
kissed his forehead; the damps of death had settled there, and,\par
forgetting her fortitude for a moment, her tears mingled with them. St.\par
Aubert lifted up his eyes; the spirit of a father returned to them, but\par
it quickly vanished, and he spoke no more.\par
\par
St. Aubert lingered till about three o’clock in the afternoon, and,\par
thus gradually sinking into death, he expired without a struggle, or a\par
sigh.\par
\par
Emily was led from the chamber by La Voisin and his daughter, who did\par
what they could to comfort her. The old man sat and wept with her.\par
Agnes was more erroneously officious.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER VIII\par
\par
O’er him, whose doom thy virtues grieve,\par
Aerial forms shall sit at eve,\par
and bend the pensive head.\par
                    COLLINS\par
\par
\par
The monk, who had before appeared, returned in the evening to offer\par
consolation to Emily, and brought a kind message from the lady abbess,\par
inviting her to the convent. Emily, though she did not accept the\par
offer, returned an answer expressive of her gratitude. The holy\par
conversation of the friar, whose mild benevolence of manners bore some\par
resemblance to those of St. Aubert, soothed the violence of her grief,\par
and lifted her heart to the Being, who, extending through all place and\par
all eternity, looks on the events of this little world as on the\par
shadows of a moment, and beholds equally, and in the same instant, the\par
soul that has passed the gates of death, and that, which still lingers\par
in the body. “In the sight of God,” said Emily, “my dear father now\par
exists, as truly as he yesterday existed to me; it is to me only that\par
he is dead; to God and to himself he yet lives!”\par
\par
The good monk left her more tranquil than she had been since St. Aubert\par
died; and, before she retired to her little cabin for the night, she\par
trusted herself so far as to visit the corpse. Silent, and without\par
weeping, she stood by its side. The features, placid and serene, told\par
the nature of the last sensations, that had lingered in the now\par
deserted frame. For a moment she turned away, in horror of the\par
stillness in which death had fixed that countenance, never till now\par
seen otherwise than animated; then gazed on it with a mixture of doubt\par
and awful astonishment. Her reason could scarcely overcome an\par
involuntary and unaccountable expectation of seeing that beloved\par
countenance still susceptible. She continued to gaze wildly; took up\par
the cold hand; spoke; still gazed, and then burst into a transport of\par
grief. La Voisin, hearing her sobs, came into the room to lead her\par
away, but she heard nothing, and only begged that he would leave her.\par
\par
Again alone, she indulged her tears, and, when the gloom of evening\par
obscured the chamber, and almost veiled from her eyes the object of her\par
distress, she still hung over the body; till her spirits, at length,\par
were exhausted, and she became tranquil. La Voisin again knocked at the\par
door, and entreated that she would come to the common apartment. Before\par
she went, she kissed the lips of St. Aubert, as she was wont to do when\par
she bade him good night. Again she kissed them; her heart felt as if it\par
would break, a few tears of agony started to her eyes, she looked up to\par
heaven, then at St. Aubert, and left the room.\par
\par
Retired to her lonely cabin, her melancholy thoughts still hovered\par
round the body of her deceased parent; and, when she sunk into a kind\par
of slumber, the images of her waking mind still haunted her fancy. She\par
thought she saw her father approaching her with a benign countenance;\par
then, smiling mournfully and pointing upwards, his lips moved, but,\par
instead of words, she heard sweet music borne on the distant air, and\par
presently saw his features glow with the mild rapture of a superior\par
being. The strain seemed to swell louder, and she awoke. The vision was\par
gone, but music yet came to her ear in strains such as angels might\par
breathe. She doubted, listened, raised herself in the bed, and again\par
listened. It was music, and not an illusion of her imagination. After a\par
solemn steady harmony, it paused; then rose again, in mournful\par
sweetness, and then died, in a cadence, that seemed to bear away the\par
listening soul to heaven. She instantly remembered the music of the\par
preceding night, with the strange circumstances, related by La Voisin,\par
and the affecting conversation it had led to, concerning the state of\par
departed spirits. All that St. Aubert had said, on that subject, now\par
pressed upon her heart, and overwhelmed it. What a change in a few\par
hours! He, who then could only conjecture, was now made acquainted with\par
truth; was himself become one of the departed! As she listened, she was\par
chilled with superstitious awe, her tears stopped; and she rose, and\par
went to the window. All without was obscured in shade; but Emily,\par
turning her eyes from the massy darkness of the woods, whose waving\par
outline appeared on the horizon, saw, on the left, that effulgent\par
planet, which the old man had pointed out, setting over the woods. She\par
remembered what he had said concerning it, and, the music now coming at\par
intervals on the air, she unclosed the casement to listen to the\par
strains, that soon gradually sunk to a greater distance, and tried to\par
discover whence they came. The obscurity prevented her from\par
distinguishing any object on the green platform below; and the sounds\par
became fainter and fainter, till they softened into silence. She\par
listened, but they returned no more. Soon after, she observed the\par
planet trembling between the fringed tops of the woods, and, in the\par
next moment, sink behind them. Chilled with a melancholy awe, she\par
retired once more to her bed, and, at length, forgot for a while her\par
sorrows in sleep.\par
\par
On the following morning, she was visited by a sister of the convent,\par
who came, with kind offices and a second invitation from the lady\par
abbess; and Emily, though she could not forsake the cottage, while the\par
remains of her father were in it, consented, however painful such a\par
visit must be, in the present state of her spirits, to pay her respects\par
to the abbess, in the evening.\par
\par
About an hour before sunset, La Voisin showed her the way through the\par
woods to the convent, which stood in a small bay of the Mediterranean,\par
crowned by a woody amphitheatre; and Emily, had she been less unhappy,\par
would have admired the extensive sea view, that appeared from the green\par
slope, in front of the edifice, and the rich shores, hung with woods\par
and pastures, that extended on either hand. But her thoughts were now\par
occupied by one sad idea, and the features of nature were to her\par
colourless and without form. The bell for vespers struck, as she passed\par
the ancient gate of the convent, and seemed the funereal note for St.\par
Aubert. Little incidents affect a mind, enervated by sorrow; Emily\par
struggled against the sickening faintness, that came over her, and was\par
led into the presence of the abbess, who received her with an air of\par
maternal tenderness; an air of such gentle solicitude and\par
consideration, as touched her with an instantaneous gratitude; her eyes\par
were filled with tears, and the words she would have spoken faltered on\par
her lips. The abbess led her to a seat, and sat down beside her, still\par
holding her hand and regarding her in silence, as Emily dried her tears\par
and attempted to speak. “Be composed, my daughter,” said the abbess in\par
a soothing voice, “do not speak yet; I know all you would say. Your\par
spirits must be soothed. We are going to prayers;—will you attend our\par
evening service? It is comfortable, my child, to look up in our\par
afflictions to a father, who sees and pities us, and who chastens in\par
his mercy.”\par
\par
Emily’s tears flowed again, but a thousand sweet emotions mingled with\par
them. The abbess suffered her to weep without interruption, and watched\par
over her with a look of benignity, that might have characterised the\par
countenance of a guardian angel. Emily, when she became tranquil, was\par
encouraged to speak without reserve, and to mention the motive, that\par
made her unwilling to quit the cottage, which the abbess did not oppose\par
even by a hint; but praised the filial piety of her conduct, and added\par
a hope, that she would pass a few days at the convent, before she\par
returned to La Vallée. “You must allow yourself a little time to\par
recover from your first shock, my daughter, before you encounter a\par
second; I will not affect to conceal from you how much I know your\par
heart must suffer, on returning to the scene of your former happiness.\par
Here, you will have all, that quiet and sympathy and religion can give,\par
to restore your spirits. But come,” added she, observing the tears\par
swell in Emily’s eyes, “we will go to the chapel.”\par
\par
Emily followed to the parlour, where the nuns were assembled, to whom\par
the abbess committed her, saying, “This is a daughter, for whom I have\par
much esteem; be sisters to her.”\par
\par
They passed on in a train to the chapel, where the solemn devotion,\par
with which the service was performed, elevated her mind, and brought to\par
it the comforts of faith and resignation.\par
\par
Twilight came on, before the abbess’s kindness would suffer Emily to\par
depart, when she left the convent, with a heart much lighter than she\par
had entered it, and was reconducted by La Voisin through the woods, the\par
pensive gloom of which was in unison with the temper of her mind; and\par
she pursued the little wild path, in musing silence, till her guide\par
suddenly stopped, looked round, and then struck out of the path into\par
the high grass, saying he had mistaken the road. He now walked on\par
quickly, and Emily, proceeding with difficulty over the obscured and\par
uneven ground, was left at some distance, till her voice arrested him,\par
who seemed unwilling to stop, and still hurried on. “If you are in\par
doubt about the way,” said Emily, “had we not better enquire it at the\par
château yonder, between the trees?”\par
\par
“No,” replied La Voisin, “there is no occasion. When we reach that\par
brook, ma’amselle, (you see the light upon the water there, beyond the\par
woods) when we reach that brook, we shall be at home presently. I don’t\par
know how I happened to mistake the path; I seldom come this way after\par
sunset.”\par
\par
“It is solitary enough,” said Emily, “but you have no banditti here.”\par
\par
“No, ma’amselle—no banditti.”\par
\par
“What are you afraid of then, my good friend? you are not\par
superstitious?” “No, not superstitious; but, to tell you the truth,\par
lady, nobody likes to go near that château, after dusk.” “By whom is it\par
inhabited,” said Emily, “that it is so formidable?” “Why, ma’amselle,\par
it is scarcely inhabited, for our lord the Marquis, and the lord of all\par
these find woods, too, is dead. He had not once been in it, for these\par
many years, and his people, who have the care of it, live in a cottage\par
close by.” Emily now understood this to be the château, which La Voisin\par
had formerly pointed out, as having belonged to the Marquis Villeroi,\par
on the mention of which her father had appeared so much affected.\par
\par
“Ah! it is a desolate place now,” continued La Voisin, “and such a\par
grand, fine place, as I remember it!” Emily enquired what had\par
occasioned this lamentable change; but the old man was silent, and\par
Emily, whose interest was awakened by the fear he had expressed, and\par
above all by a recollection of her father’s agitation, repeated the\par
question, and added, “If you are neither afraid of the inhabitants, my\par
good friend, nor are superstitious, how happens it, that you dread to\par
pass near that château in the dark?”\par
\par
“Perhaps, then, I am a little superstitious, ma’amselle; and, if you\par
knew what I do, you might be so too. Strange things have happened\par
there. Monsieur, your good father, appeared to have known the late\par
Marchioness.” “Pray inform me what did happen?” said Emily, with much\par
emotion.\par
\par
“Alas! ma’amselle,” answered La Voisin, “enquire no further; it is not\par
for me to lay open the domestic secrets of my lord.”—Emily, surprised\par
by the old man’s words, and his manner of delivering them, forbore to\par
repeat her question; a nearer interest, the remembrance of St. Aubert,\par
occupied her thoughts, and she was led to recollect the music she heard\par
on the preceding night, which she mentioned to La Voisin. “You were not\par
alone, ma’amselle, in this,” he replied, “I heard it too; but I have so\par
often heard it, at the same hour, that I was scarcely surprised.”\par
\par
“You doubtless believe this music to have some connection with the\par
château,” said Emily suddenly, “and are, therefore, superstitious.” “It\par
may be so, ma’amselle, but there are other circumstances, belonging to\par
that château, which I remember, and sadly too.” A heavy sigh followed:\par
but Emily’s delicacy restrained the curiosity these words revived, and\par
she enquired no further.\par
\par
On reaching the cottage, all the violence of her grief returned; it\par
seemed as if she had escaped its heavy pressure only while she was\par
removed from the object of it. She passed immediately to the chamber,\par
where the remains of her father were laid, and yielded to all the\par
anguish of hopeless grief. La Voisin, at length, persuaded her to leave\par
the room, and she returned to her own, where, exhausted by the\par
sufferings of the day, she soon fell into deep sleep, and awoke\par
considerably refreshed.\par
\par
When the dreadful hour arrived, in which the remains of St. Aubert were\par
to be taken from her for ever, she went alone to the chamber to look\par
upon his countenance yet once again, and La Voisin, who had waited\par
patiently below stairs, till her despair should subside, with the\par
respect due to grief, forbore to interrupt the indulgence of it, till\par
surprise, at the length of her stay, and then apprehension overcame his\par
delicacy, and he went to lead her from the chamber. Having tapped\par
gently at the door, without receiving an answer, he listened\par
attentively, but all was still; no sigh, no sob of anguish was heard.\par
Yet more alarmed by this silence, he opened the door, and found Emily\par
lying senseless across the foot of the bed, near which stood the\par
coffin. His calls procured assistance, and she was carried to her room,\par
where proper applications, at length, restored her.\par
\par
During her state of insensibility, La Voisin had given directions for\par
the coffin to be closed, and he succeeded in persuading Emily to\par
forbear revisiting the chamber. She, indeed, felt herself unequal to\par
this, and also perceived the necessity of sparing her spirits, and\par
recollecting fortitude sufficient to bear her through the approaching\par
scene. St. Aubert had given a particular injunction, that his remains\par
should be interred in the church of the convent of St. Clair, and, in\par
mentioning the north chancel, near the ancient tomb of the Villerois,\par
had pointed out the exact spot, where he wished to be laid. The\par
superior had granted this place for the interment, and thither,\par
therefore, the sad procession now moved, which was met, at the gates,\par
by the venerable priest, followed by a train of friars. Every person,\par
who heard the solemn chant of the anthem, and the peal of the organ,\par
that struck up, when the body entered the church, and saw also the\par
feeble steps, and the assumed tranquillity of Emily, gave her\par
involuntary tears. She shed none, but walked, her face partly shaded by\par
a thin black veil, between two persons, who supported her, preceded by\par
the abbess, and followed by nuns, whose plaintive voices mellowed the\par
swelling harmony of the dirge. When the procession came to the grave\par
the music ceased. Emily drew the veil entirely over her face, and, in a\par
momentary pause, between the anthem and the rest of the service, her\par
sobs were distinctly audible. The holy father began the service, and\par
Emily again commanded her feelings, till the coffin was let down, and\par
she heard the earth rattle on its lid. Then, as she shuddered, a groan\par
burst from her heart, and she leaned for support on the person who\par
stood next to her. In a few moments she recovered; and, when she heard\par
those affecting and sublime words: “His body is buried in peace, and\par
his soul returns to Him that gave it,” her anguish softened into tears.\par
\par
The abbess led her from the church into her own parlour, and there\par
administered all the consolations, that religion and gentle sympathy\par
can give. Emily struggled against the pressure of grief; but the\par
abbess, observing her attentively, ordered a bed to be prepared, and\par
recommended her to retire to repose. She also kindly claimed her\par
promise to remain a few days at the convent; and Emily, who had no wish\par
to return to the cottage, the scene of all her sufferings, had leisure,\par
now that no immediate care pressed upon her attention, to feel the\par
indisposition, which disabled her from immediately travelling.\par
\par
Meanwhile, the maternal kindness of the abbess, and the gentle\par
attentions of the nuns did all, that was possible, towards soothing her\par
spirits and restoring her health. But the latter was too deeply\par
wounded, through the medium of her mind, to be quickly revived. She\par
lingered for some weeks at the convent, under the influence of a slow\par
fever, wishing to return home, yet unable to go thither; often even\par
reluctant to leave the spot where her father’s relics were deposited,\par
and sometimes soothing herself with the consideration, that, if she\par
died here, her remains would repose beside those of St. Aubert. In the\par
meanwhile, she sent letters to Madame Cheron and to the old\par
housekeeper, informing them of the sad event, that had taken place, and\par
of her own situation. From her aunt she received an answer, abounding\par
more in common-place condolement, than in traits of real sorrow, which\par
assured her, that a servant should be sent to conduct her to La Vallée,\par
for that her own time was so much occupied by company, that she had no\par
leisure to undertake so long a journey. However Emily might prefer La\par
Vallée to Thoulouse, she could not be insensible to the indecorous and\par
unkind conduct of her aunt, in suffering her to return thither, where\par
she had no longer a relation to console and protect her; a conduct,\par
which was the more culpable, since St. Aubert had appointed Madame\par
Cheron the guardian of his orphan daughter.\par
\par
Madame Cheron’s servant made the attendance of the good La Voisin\par
unnecessary; and Emily, who felt sensibly her obligations to him, for\par
all his kind attention to her late father, as well as to herself, was\par
glad to spare him a long, and what, at his time of life, must have been\par
a troublesome journey.\par
\par
During her stay at the convent, the peace and sanctity that reigned\par
within, the tranquil beauty of the scenery without, and the delicate\par
attentions of the abbess and the nuns, were circumstances so soothing\par
to her mind, that they almost tempted her to leave a world, where she\par
had lost her dearest friends, and devote herself to the cloister, in a\par
spot, rendered sacred to her by containing the tomb of St. Aubert. The\par
pensive enthusiasm, too, so natural to her temper, had spread a\par
beautiful illusion over the sanctified retirement of a nun, that almost\par
hid from her view the selfishness of its security. But the touches,\par
which a melancholy fancy, slightly tinctured with superstition, gave to\par
the monastic scene, began to fade, as her spirits revived, and brought\par
once more to her heart an image, which had only transiently been\par
banished thence. By this she was silently awakened to hope and comfort\par
and sweet affections; visions of happiness gleamed faintly at a\par
distance, and, though she knew them to be illusions, she could not\par
resolve to shut them out for ever. It was the remembrance of\par
Valancourt, of his taste, his genius, and of the countenance which\par
glowed with both, that, perhaps, alone determined her to return to the\par
world. The grandeur and sublimity of the scenes, amidst which they had\par
first met, had fascinated her fancy, and had imperceptibly contributed\par
to render Valancourt more interesting by seeming to communicate to him\par
somewhat of their own character. The esteem, too, which St. Aubert had\par
repeatedly expressed for him, sanctioned this kindness; but, though his\par
countenance and manner had continually expressed his admiration of her,\par
he had not otherwise declared it; and even the hope of seeing him again\par
was so distant, that she was scarcely conscious of it, still less that\par
it influenced her conduct on this occasion.\par
\par
It was several days after the arrival of Madame Cheron’s servant before\par
Emily was sufficiently recovered to undertake the journey to La Vallée.\par
On the evening preceding her departure, she went to the cottage to take\par
leave of La Voisin and his family, and to make them a return for their\par
kindness. The old man she found sitting on a bench at his door, between\par
his daughter, and his son-in-law, who was just returned from his daily\par
labour, and who was playing upon a pipe, that, in tone, resembled an\par
oboe. A flask of wine stood beside the old man, and, before him, a\par
small table with fruit and bread, round which stood several of his\par
grandsons, fine rosy children, who were taking their supper, as their\par
mother distributed it. On the edge of the little green, that spread\par
before the cottage, were cattle and a few sheep reposing under the\par
trees. The landscape was touched with the mellow light of the evening\par
sun, whose long slanting beams played through a vista of the woods, and\par
lighted up the distant turrets of the château. She paused a moment,\par
before she emerged from the shade, to gaze upon the happy group before\par
her—on the complacency and ease of healthy age, depictured on the\par
countenance of La Voisin; the maternal tenderness of Agnes, as she\par
looked upon her children, and the innocency of infantine pleasures,\par
reflected in their smiles. Emily looked again at the venerable old man,\par
and at the cottage; the memory of her father rose with full force upon\par
her mind, and she hastily stepped forward, afraid to trust herself with\par
a longer pause. She took an affectionate and affecting leave of La\par
Voisin and his family; he seemed to love her as his daughter, and shed\par
tears; Emily shed many. She avoided going into the cottage, since she\par
knew it would revive emotions, such as she could not now endure.\par
\par
One painful scene yet awaited her, for she determined to visit again\par
her father’s grave; and that she might not be interrupted, or observed\par
in the indulgence of her melancholy tenderness, she deferred her visit,\par
till every inhabitant of the convent, except the nun who promised to\par
bring her the key of the church, should be retired to rest. Emily\par
remained in her chamber, till she heard the convent bell strike twelve,\par
when the nun came, as she had appointed, with the key of a private\par
door, that opened into the church, and they descended together the\par
narrow winding staircase, that led thither. The nun offered to\par
accompany Emily to the grave, adding, “It is melancholy to go alone at\par
this hour;” but the former, thanking her for the consideration, could\par
not consent to have any witness of her sorrow; and the sister, having\par
unlocked the door, gave her the lamp. “You will remember, sister,” said\par
she, “that in the east aisle, which you must pass, is a newly opened\par
grave; hold the light to the ground, that you may not stumble over the\par
loose earth.” Emily, thanking her again, took the lamp, and, stepping\par
into the church, sister Mariette departed. But Emily paused a moment at\par
the door; a sudden fear came over her, and she returned to the foot of\par
the staircase, where, as she heard the steps of the nun ascending, and,\par
while she held up the lamp, saw her black veil waving over the spiral\par
balusters, she was tempted to call her back. While she hesitated, the\par
veil disappeared, and, in the next moment, ashamed of her fears, she\par
returned to the church. The cold air of the aisles chilled her, and\par
their deep silence and extent, feebly shone upon by the moonlight, that\par
streamed through a distant gothic window, would at any other time have\par
awed her into superstition; now, grief occupied all her attention. She\par
scarcely heard the whispering echoes of her own steps, or thought of\par
the open grave, till she found herself almost on its brink. A friar of\par
the convent had been buried there on the preceding evening, and, as she\par
had sat alone in her chamber at twilight, she heard, at distance, the\par
monks chanting the requiem for his soul. This brought freshly to her\par
memory the circumstances of her father’s death; and, as the voices,\par
mingling with a low querulous peal of the organ, swelled faintly,\par
gloomy and affecting visions had arisen upon her mind. Now she\par
remembered them, and, turning aside to avoid the broken ground, these\par
recollections made her pass on with quicker steps to the grave of St.\par
Aubert, when in the moonlight, that fell athwart a remote part of the\par
aisle, she thought she saw a shadow gliding between the pillars. She\par
stopped to listen, and, not hearing any footstep, believed that her\par
fancy had deceived her, and, no longer apprehensive of being observed,\par
proceeded. St. Aubert was buried beneath a plain marble, bearing little\par
more than his name and the date of his birth and death, near the foot\par
of the stately monument of the Villerois. Emily remained at his grave,\par
till a chime, that called the monks to early prayers, warned her to\par
retire; then, she wept over it a last farewell, and forced herself from\par
the spot. After this hour of melancholy indulgence, she was refreshed\par
by a deeper sleep, than she had experienced for a long time, and, on\par
awakening, her mind was more tranquil and resigned, than it had been\par
since St. Aubert’s death.\par
\par
But, when the moment of her departure from the convent arrived, all her\par
grief returned; the memory of the dead, and the kindness of the living\par
attached her to the place; and for the sacred spot, where her father’s\par
remains were interred, she seemed to feel all those tender affections\par
which we conceive for home. The abbess repeated many kind assurances of\par
regard at their parting, and pressed her to return, if ever she should\par
find her condition elsewhere unpleasant; many of the nuns also\par
expressed unaffected regret at her departure, and Emily left the\par
convent with many tears, and followed by sincere wishes for her\par
happiness.\par
\par
She had travelled several leagues, before the scenes of the country,\par
through which she passed, had power to rouse her for a moment from the\par
deep melancholy, into which she was sunk, and, when they did, it was\par
only to remind her, that, on her last view of them, St. Aubert was at\par
her side, and to call up to her remembrance the remarks he had\par
delivered on similar scenery. Thus, without any particular occurrence,\par
passed the day in languor and dejection. She slept that night in a town\par
on the skirts of Languedoc, and, on the following morning, entered\par
Gascony.\par
\par
Towards the close of this day, Emily came within view of the plains in\par
the neighbourhood of La Vallée, and the well-known objects of former\par
times began to press upon her notice, and with them recollections, that\par
awakened all her tenderness and grief. Often, while she looked through\par
her tears upon the wild grandeur of the Pyrenees, now varied with the\par
rich lights and shadows of evening, she remembered, that, when last she\par
saw them, her father partook with her of the pleasure they inspired.\par
Suddenly some scene, which he had particularly pointed out to her,\par
would present itself, and the sick languor of despair would steal upon\par
her heart. “There!” she would exclaim, “there are the very cliffs,\par
there the wood of pines, which he looked at with such delight, as we\par
passed this road together for the last time. There, too, under the crag\par
of that mountain, is the cottage, peeping from among the cedars, which\par
he bade me remember, and copy with my pencil. O my father, shall I\par
never see you more!”\par
\par
As she drew near the château, these melancholy memorials of past times\par
multiplied. At length, the château itself appeared, amid the glowing\par
beauty of St. Aubert’s favourite landscape. This was an object, which\par
called for fortitude, not for tears; Emily dried hers, and prepared to\par
meet with calmness the trying moment of her return to that home, where\par
there was no longer a parent to welcome her. “Yes,” said she, “let me\par
not forget the lessons he has taught me! How often he has pointed out\par
the necessity of resisting even virtuous sorrow; how often we have\par
admired together the greatness of a mind, that can at once suffer and\par
reason! O my father! if you are permitted to look down upon your child,\par
it will please you to see, that she remembers, and endeavours to\par
practise, the precepts you have given her.”\par
\par
A turn on the road now allowed a nearer view of the château, the\par
chimneys, tipped with light, rising from behind St. Aubert’s favourite\par
oaks, whose foliage partly concealed the lower part of the building.\par
Emily could not suppress a heavy sigh. “This, too, was his favourite\par
hour,” said she, as she gazed upon the long evening shadows, stretched\par
athwart the landscape. “How deep the repose, how lovely the scene!\par
lovely and tranquil as in former days!”\par
\par
Again she resisted the pressure of sorrow, till her ear caught the gay\par
melody of the dance, which she had so often listened to, as she walked\par
with St. Aubert, on the margin of the Garonne, when all her fortitude\par
forsook her, and she continued to weep, till the carriage stopped at\par
the little gate, that opened upon what was now her own territory. She\par
raised her eyes on the sudden stopping of the carriage, and saw her\par
father’s old housekeeper coming to open the gate. Manchon also came\par
running, and barking before her; and when his young mistress alighted,\par
fawned, and played round her, gasping with joy.\par
\par
“Dear ma’amselle!” said Theresa, and paused, and looked as if she would\par
have offered something of condolement to Emily, whose tears now\par
prevented reply. The dog still fawned and ran round her, and then flew\par
towards the carriage, with a short quick bark. “Ah, ma’amselle!—my poor\par
master!” said Theresa, whose feelings were more awakened than her\par
delicacy, “Manchon’s gone to look for him.” Emily sobbed aloud; and, on\par
looking towards the carriage, which still stood with the door open, saw\par
the animal spring into it, and instantly leap out, and then with his\par
nose on the ground run round the horses.\par
\par
“Don’t cry so, ma’amselle,” said Theresa, “it breaks my heart to see\par
you.” The dog now came running to Emily, then returned to the carriage,\par
and then back again to her, whining and discontented. “Poor rogue!”\par
said Theresa, “thou hast lost thy master, thou mayst well cry! But\par
come, my dear young lady, be comforted. What shall I get to refresh\par
you?” Emily gave her hand to the old servant, and tried to restrain her\par
grief, while she made some kind enquiries concerning her health. But\par
she still lingered in the walk which led to the château, for within was\par
no person to meet her with the kiss of affection; her own heart no\par
longer palpitated with impatient joy to meet again the well-known\par
smile, and she dreaded to see objects, which would recall the full\par
remembrance of her former happiness. She moved slowly towards the door,\par
paused, went on, and paused again. How silent, how forsaken, how\par
forlorn did the château appear! Trembling to enter it, yet blaming\par
herself for delaying what she could not avoid, she, at length, passed\par
into the hall; crossed it with a hurried step, as if afraid to look\par
round, and opened the door of that room, which she was wont to call her\par
own. The gloom of evening gave solemnity to its silent and deserted\par
air. The chairs, the tables, every article of furniture, so familiar to\par
her in happier times, spoke eloquently to her heart. She seated\par
herself, without immediately observing it, in a window, which opened\par
upon the garden, and where St. Aubert had often sat with her, watching\par
the sun retire from the rich and extensive prospect, that appeared\par
beyond the groves.\par
\par
Having indulged her tears for some time, she became more composed; and,\par
when Theresa, after seeing the baggage deposited in her lady’s room,\par
again appeared, she had so far recovered her spirits, as to be able to\par
converse with her.\par
\par
“I have made up the green bed for you, ma’amselle,” said Theresa, as\par
she set the coffee upon the table. “I thought you would like it better\par
than your own now; but I little thought this day month, that you would\par
come back alone. A-well-a-day! the news almost broke my heart, when it\par
did come. Who would have believed, that my poor master, when he went\par
from home, would never return again!” Emily hid her face with her\par
handkerchief, and waved her hand.\par
\par
“Do taste the coffee,” said Theresa. “My dear young lady, be\par
comforted—we must all die. My dear master is a saint above.” Emily took\par
the handkerchief from her face, and raised her eyes full of tears\par
towards heaven; soon after she dried them, and, in a calm, but\par
tremulous voice, began to enquire concerning some of her late father’s\par
pensioners.\par
\par
“Alas-a-day!” said Theresa, as she poured out the coffee, and handed it\par
to her mistress, “all that could come, have been here every day to\par
enquire after you and my master.” She then proceeded to tell, that some\par
were dead whom they had left well; and others, who were ill, had\par
recovered. “And see, ma’amselle,” added Theresa, “there is old Mary\par
coming up the garden now; she has looked every day these three years as\par
if she would die, yet she is alive still. She has seen the chaise at\par
the door, and knows you are come home.”\par
\par
The sight of this poor old woman would have been too much for Emily,\par
and she begged Theresa would go and tell her, that she was too ill to\par
see any person that night. “Tomorrow I shall be better, perhaps; but\par
give her this token of my remembrance.”\par
\par
Emily sat for some time, given up to sorrow. Not an object, on which\par
her eye glanced, but awakened some remembrance, that led immediately to\par
the subject of her grief. Her favourite plants, which St. Aubert had\par
taught her to nurse; the little drawings, that adorned the room, which\par
his taste had instructed her to execute; the books, that he had\par
selected for her use, and which they had read together; her musical\par
instruments, whose sounds he loved so well, and which he sometimes\par
awakened himself—every object gave new force to sorrow. At length, she\par
roused herself from this melancholy indulgence, and, summoning all her\par
resolution, stepped forward to go into those forlorn rooms, which,\par
though she dreaded to enter, she knew would yet more powerfully affect\par
her, if she delayed to visit them.\par
\par
Having passed through the green-house, her courage for a moment forsook\par
her, when she opened the door of the library; and, perhaps, the shade,\par
which evening and the foliage of the trees near the windows threw\par
across the room, heightened the solemnity of her feelings on entering\par
that apartment, where everything spoke of her father. There was an arm\par
chair, in which he used to sit; she shrunk when she observed it, for\par
she had so often seen him seated there, and the idea of him rose so\par
distinctly to her mind, that she almost fancied she saw him before her.\par
But she checked the illusions of a distempered imagination, though she\par
could not subdue a certain degree of awe, which now mingled with her\par
emotions. She walked slowly to the chair, and seated herself in it;\par
there was a reading-desk before it, on which lay a book open, as it had\par
been left by her father. It was some moments before she recovered\par
courage enough to examine it; and, when she looked at the open page,\par
she immediately recollected, that St. Aubert, on the evening before his\par
departure from the château, had read to her some passages from this his\par
favourite author. The circumstance now affected her extremely; she\par
looked at the page, wept, and looked again. To her the book appeared\par
sacred and invaluable, and she would not have moved it, or closed the\par
page, which he had left open, for the treasures of the Indies. Still\par
she sat before the desk, and could not resolve to quit it, though the\par
increasing gloom, and the profound silence of the apartment, revived a\par
degree of painful awe. Her thoughts dwelt on the probable state of\par
departed spirits, and she remembered the affecting conversation, which\par
had passed between St. Aubert and La Voisin, on the night preceding his\par
death.\par
\par
As she mused she saw the door slowly open, and a rustling sound in a\par
remote part of the room startled her. Through the dusk she thought she\par
perceived something move. The subject she had been considering, and the\par
present tone of her spirits, which made her imagination respond to\par
every impression of her senses, gave her a sudden terror of something\par
supernatural. She sat for a moment motionless, and then, her dissipated\par
reason returning, “What should I fear?” said she. “If the spirits of\par
those we love ever return to us, it is in kindness.”\par
\par
The silence, which again reigned, made her ashamed of her late fears,\par
and she believed, that her imagination had deluded her, or that she had\par
heard one of those unaccountable noises, which sometimes occur in old\par
houses. The same sound, however, returned; and, distinguishing\par
something moving towards her, and in the next instant press beside her\par
into the chair, she shrieked; but her fleeting senses were instantly\par
recalled, on perceiving that it was Manchon who sat by her, and who now\par
licked her hands affectionately.\par
\par
Perceiving her spirits unequal to the task she had assigned herself of\par
visiting the deserted rooms of the château this night, when she left\par
the library, she walked into the garden, and down to the terrace, that\par
overhung the river. The sun was now set; but, under the dark branches\par
of the almond trees, was seen the saffron glow of the west, spreading\par
beyond the twilight of middle air. The bat flitted silently by; and,\par
now and then, the mourning note of the nightingale was heard. The\par
circumstances of the hour brought to her recollection some lines, which\par
she had once heard St. Aubert recite on this very spot, and she had now\par
a melancholy pleasure in repeating them.\par
\par
SONNET\par
\par
Now the bat circles on the breeze of eve,\par
    That creeps, in shudd’ring fits, along the wave,\par
    And trembles ’mid the woods, and through the cave\par
Whose lonely sighs the wanderer deceive;\par
\par
For oft, when melancholy charms his mind,\par
    He thinks the Spirit of the rock he hears,\par
    Nor listens, but with sweetly-thrilling fears,\par
To the low, mystic murmurs of the wind!\par
\par
Now the bat circles, and the twilight-dew\par
    Falls silent round, and, o’er the mountain-cliff,\par
    The gleaming wave, and far-discover’d skiff,\par
Spreads the grey veil of soft, harmonious hue.\par
\par
So falls o’er Grief the dew of pity’s tear\par
Dimming her lonely visions of despair.\par
\par
\par
Emily, wandering on, came to St. Aubert’s favourite plane-tree, where\par
so often, at this hour, they had sat beneath the shade together, and\par
with her dear mother so often had conversed on the subject of a future\par
state. How often, too, had her father expressed the comfort he derived\par
from believing, that they should meet in another world! Emily, overcome\par
by these recollections, left the plane-tree, and, as she leaned\par
pensively on the wall of the terrace, she observed a group of peasants\par
dancing gaily on the banks of the Garonne, which spread in broad\par
expanse below, and reflected the evening light. What a contrast they\par
formed to the desolate, unhappy Emily! They were gay and _debonnaire_,\par
as they were wont to be when she, too, was gay—when St. Aubert used to\par
listen to their merry music, with a countenance beaming pleasure and\par
benevolence. Emily, having looked for a moment on this sprightly band,\par
turned away, unable to bear the remembrances it excited; but where,\par
alas! could she turn, and not meet new objects to give acuteness to\par
grief?\par
\par
As she walked slowly towards the house, she was met by Theresa. “Dear\par
ma’amselle,” said she, “I have been seeking you up and down this half\par
hour, and was afraid some accident had happened to you. How can you\par
like to wander about so in this night air! Do come into the house.\par
Think what my poor master would have said, if he could see you. I am\par
sure, when my dear lady died, no gentleman could take it more to heart\par
than he did, yet you know he seldom shed a tear.”\par
\par
“Pray, Theresa, cease,” said Emily, wishing to interrupt this\par
ill-judged, but well-meaning harangue; Theresa’s loquacity, however,\par
was not to be silenced so easily. “And when you used to grieve so,” she\par
added, “he often told you how wrong it was—for that my mistress was\par
happy. And, if she was happy, I am sure he is so too; for the prayers\par
of the poor, they say, reach heaven.” During this speech, Emily had\par
walked silently into the château, and Theresa lighted her across the\par
hall into the common sitting parlour, where she had laid the cloth,\par
with one solitary knife and fork, for supper. Emily was in the room\par
before she perceived that it was not her own apartment, but she checked\par
the emotion which inclined her to leave it, and seated herself quietly\par
by the little supper table. Her father’s hat hung upon the opposite\par
wall; while she gazed at it, a faintness came over her. Theresa looked\par
at her, and then at the object, on which her eyes were settled, and\par
went to remove it; but Emily waved her hand—“No,” said she, “let it\par
remain. I am going to my chamber.” “Nay, ma’amselle, supper is ready.”\par
“I cannot take it,” replied Emily, “I will go to my room, and try to\par
sleep. Tomorrow I shall be better.”\par
\par
“This is poor doings!” said Theresa. “Dear lady! do take some food! I\par
have dressed a pheasant, and a fine one it is. Old Monsieur Barreaux\par
sent it this morning, for I saw him yesterday, and told him you were\par
coming. And I know nobody that seemed more concerned, when he heard the\par
sad news, then he.”\par
\par
“Did he?” said Emily, in a tender voice, while she felt her poor heart\par
warmed for a moment by a ray of sympathy.\par
\par
At length, her spirits were entirely overcome, and she retired to her\par
room.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER IX\par
\par
Can Music’s voice, can Beauty’s eye,\par
Can Painting’s glowing hand supply\par
A charm so suited to my mind,\par
As blows this hollow gust of wind?\par
As drops this little weeping rill,\par
Soft tinkling down the moss-grown hill;\par
While, through the west, where sinks the crimson day,\par
Meek Twilight slowly sails, and waves her banners grey?\par
                    MASON\par
\par
\par
Emily, some time after her return to La Vallée, received letters from\par
her aunt, Madame Cheron, in which, after some common-place condolement\par
and advice, she invited her to Thoulouse, and added, that, as her late\par
brother had entrusted Emily’s _education_ to her, she should consider\par
herself bound to overlook her conduct. Emily, at this time, wished only\par
to remain at La Vallée, in the scenes of her early happiness, now\par
rendered infinitely dear to her, as the late residence of those, whom\par
she had lost for ever, where she could weep unobserved, retrace their\par
steps, and remember each minute particular of their manners. But she\par
was equally anxious to avoid the displeasure of Madame Cheron.\par
\par
Though her affection would not suffer her to question, even a moment,\par
the propriety of St. Aubert’s conduct in appointing Madame Cheron for\par
her guardian, she was sensible, that this step had made her happiness\par
depend, in a great degree, on the humour of her aunt. In her reply, she\par
begged permission to remain, at present, at La Vallée, mentioning the\par
extreme dejection of her spirits, and the necessity she felt for quiet\par
and retirement to restore them. These she knew were not to be found at\par
Madame Cheron’s, whose inclinations led her into a life of dissipation,\par
which her ample fortune encouraged; and, having given her answer, she\par
felt somewhat more at ease.\par
\par
In the first days of her affliction, she was visited by Monsieur\par
Barreaux, a sincere mourner for St. Aubert. “I may well lament my\par
friend,” said he, “for I shall never meet with his resemblance. If I\par
could have found such a man in what is called society, I should not\par
have left it.”\par
\par
M. Barreaux’s admiration of her father endeared him extremely to Emily,\par
whose heart found almost its first relief in conversing of her parents,\par
with a man, whom she so much revered, and who, though with such an\par
ungracious appearance, possessed to much goodness of heart and delicacy\par
of mind.\par
\par
Several weeks passed away in quiet retirement, and Emily’s affliction\par
began to soften into melancholy. She could bear to read the books she\par
had before read with her father; to sit in his chair in the library—to\par
watch the flowers his hand had planted—to awaken the tones of that\par
instrument his fingers had pressed, and sometimes even to play his\par
favourite air.\par
\par
When her mind had recovered from the first shock of affliction,\par
perceiving the danger of yielding to indolence, and that activity alone\par
could restore its tone, she scrupulously endeavoured to pass all her\par
hours in employment. And it was now that she understood the full value\par
of the education she had received from St. Aubert, for in cultivating\par
her understanding he had secured her an asylum from indolence, without\par
recourse to dissipation, and rich and varied amusement and information,\par
independent of the society, from which her situation secluded her. Nor\par
were the good effects of this education confined to selfish advantages,\par
since, St. Aubert having nourished every amiable quality of her heart,\par
it now expanded in benevolence to all around her, and taught her, when\par
she could not remove the misfortunes of others, at least to soften them\par
by sympathy and tenderness;—a benevolence that taught her to feel for\par
all, that could suffer.\par
\par
Madame Cheron returned no answer to Emily’s letter, who began to hope,\par
that she should be permitted to remain some time longer in her\par
retirement, and her mind had now so far recovered its strength, that\par
she ventured to view the scenes, which most powerfully recalled the\par
images of past times. Among these was the fishing-house; and, to\par
indulge still more the affectionate melancholy of the visit, she took\par
thither her lute, that she might again hear there the tones, to which\par
St. Aubert and her mother had so often delighted to listen. She went\par
alone, and at that still hour of the evening which is so soothing to\par
fancy and to grief. The last time she had been here she was in company\par
with Monsieur and Madame St. Aubert, a few days preceding that, on\par
which the latter was seized with a fatal illness. Now, when Emily again\par
entered the woods, that surrounded the building, they awakened so\par
forcibly the memory of former times, that her resolution yielded for a\par
moment to excess of grief. She stopped, leaned for support against a\par
tree, and wept for some minutes, before she had recovered herself\par
sufficiently to proceed. The little path, that led to the building, was\par
overgrown with grass and the flowers which St. Aubert had scattered\par
carelessly along the border were almost choked with weeds—the tall\par
thistle—the fox-glove, and the nettle. She often paused to look on the\par
desolate spot, now so silent and forsaken, and when, with a trembling\par
hand, she opened the door of the fishing-house, “Ah!” said she,\par
“everything—everything remains as when I left it last—left it with\par
those who never must return!” She went to a window, that overhung the\par
rivulet, and, leaning over it, with her eyes fixed on the current, was\par
soon lost in melancholy reverie. The lute she had brought lay forgotten\par
beside her; the mournful sighing of the breeze, as it waved the high\par
pines above, and its softer whispers among the osiers, that bowed upon\par
the banks below, was a kind of music more in unison with her feelings.\par
It did not vibrate on the chords of unhappy memory, but was soothing to\par
the heart as the voice of Pity. She continued to muse, unconscious of\par
the gloom of evening, and that the sun’s last light trembled on the\par
heights above, and would probably have remained so much longer, if a\par
sudden footstep, without the building, had not alarmed her attention,\par
and first made her recollect that she was unprotected. In the next\par
moment, a door opened, and a stranger appeared, who stopped on\par
perceiving Emily, and then began to apologise for his intrusion. But\par
Emily, at the sound of his voice, lost her fear in a stronger emotion:\par
its tones were familiar to her ear, and, though she could not readily\par
distinguish through the dusk the features of the person who spoke, she\par
felt a remembrance too strong to be distrusted.\par
\par
He repeated his apology, and Emily then said something in reply, when\par
the stranger eagerly advancing, exclaimed, “Good God! can it be—surely\par
I am not mistaken—ma’amselle St. Aubert?—is it not?”\par
\par
“It is indeed,” said Emily, who was confirmed in her first conjecture,\par
for she now distinguished the countenance of Valancourt, lighted up\par
with still more than its usual animation. A thousand painful\par
recollections crowded to her mind, and the effort, which she made to\par
support herself, only served to increase her agitation. Valancourt,\par
meanwhile, having enquired anxiously after her health, and expressed\par
his hopes, that M. St. Aubert had found benefit from travelling,\par
learned from the flood of tears, which she could no longer repress, the\par
fatal truth. He led her to a seat, and sat down by her, while Emily\par
continued to weep, and Valancourt to hold the hand, which she was\par
unconscious he had taken, till it was wet with the tears, which grief\par
for St. Aubert and sympathy for herself had called forth.\par
\par
“I feel,” said he at length, “I feel how insufficient all attempt at\par
consolation must be on this subject. I can only mourn with you, for I\par
cannot doubt the source of your tears. Would to God I were mistaken!”\par
\par
Emily could still answer only by tears, till she rose, and begged they\par
might leave the melancholy spot, when Valancourt, though he saw her\par
feebleness, could not offer to detain her, but took her arm within his,\par
and led her from the fishing-house. They walked silently through the\par
woods, Valancourt anxious to know, yet fearing to ask any particulars\par
concerning St. Aubert; and Emily too much distressed to converse. After\par
some time, however, she acquired fortitude enough to speak of her\par
father, and to give a brief account of the manner of his death; during\par
which recital Valancourt’s countenance betrayed strong emotion, and,\par
when he heard that St. Aubert had died on the road, and that Emily had\par
been left among strangers, he pressed her hand between his, and\par
involuntarily exclaimed, “Why was I not there!” but in the next moment\par
recollected himself, for he immediately returned to the mention of her\par
father; till, perceiving that her spirits were exhausted, he gradually\par
changed the subject, and spoke of himself. Emily thus learned that,\par
after they had parted, he had wandered, for some time, along the shores\par
of the Mediterranean, and had then returned through Languedoc into\par
Gascony, which was his native province, and where he usually resided.\par
\par
When he had concluded his little narrative, he sunk into a silence,\par
which Emily was not disposed to interrupt, and it continued, till they\par
reached the gate of the château, when he stopped, as if he had known\par
this to be the limit of his walk. Here, saying, that it was his\par
intention to return to Estuvière on the following day, he asked her if\par
she would permit him to take leave of her in the morning; and Emily,\par
perceiving that she could not reject an ordinary civility, without\par
expressing by her refusal an expectation of something more, was\par
compelled to answer, that she should be at home.\par
\par
She passed a melancholy evening, during which the retrospect of all\par
that had happened, since she had seen Valancourt, would rise to her\par
imagination; and the scene of her father’s death appeared in tints as\par
fresh, as if it had passed on the preceding day. She remembered\par
particularly the earnest and solemn manner, in which he had required\par
her to destroy the manuscript papers, and, awakening from the lethargy,\par
in which sorrow had held her, she was shocked to think she had not yet\par
obeyed him, and determined, that another day should not reproach her\par
with the neglect.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER X\par
\par
Can such things be,\par
And overcome us like a summer’s cloud,\par
Without our special wonder?\par
                    MACBETH\par
\par
\par
On the next morning, Emily ordered a fire to be lighted in the stove of\par
the chamber, where St. Aubert used to sleep; and, as soon as she had\par
breakfasted, went thither to burn the papers. Having fastened the door\par
to prevent interruption, she opened the closet where they were\par
concealed, as she entered which, she felt an emotion of unusual awe,\par
and stood for some moments surveying it, trembling, and almost afraid\par
to remove the board. There was a great chair in one corner of the\par
closet, and, opposite to it, stood the table, at which she had seen her\par
father sit, on the evening that preceded his departure, looking over,\par
with so much emotion, what she believed to be these very papers.\par
\par
The solitary life, which Emily had led of late, and the melancholy\par
subjects, on which she had suffered her thoughts to dwell, had rendered\par
her at times sensible to the ‘thick-coming fancies’ of a mind greatly\par
enervated. It was lamentable, that her excellent understanding should\par
have yielded, even for a moment, to the reveries of superstition, or\par
rather to those starts of imagination, which deceive the senses into\par
what can be called nothing less than momentary madness. Instances of\par
this temporary failure of mind had more than once occurred since her\par
return home; particularly when, wandering through this lonely mansion\par
in the evening twilight, she had been alarmed by appearances, which\par
would have been unseen in her more cheerful days. To this infirm state\par
of her nerves may be attributed what she imagined, when, her eyes\par
glancing a second time on the arm-chair, which stood in an obscure part\par
of the closet, the countenance of her dead father appeared there. Emily\par
stood fixed for a moment to the floor, after which she left the closet.\par
Her spirits, however, soon returned; she reproached herself with the\par
weakness of thus suffering interruption in an act of serious\par
importance, and again opened the door. By the directions which St.\par
Aubert had given her, she readily found the board he had described in\par
an opposite corner of the closet, near the window; she distinguished\par
also the line he had mentioned, and, pressing it as he had bade her, it\par
slid down, and disclosed the bundle of papers, together with some\par
scattered ones, and the purse of louis. With a trembling hand she\par
removed them, replaced the board, paused a moment, and was rising from\par
the floor, when, on looking up, there appeared to her alarmed fancy the\par
same countenance in the chair. The illusion, another instance of the\par
unhappy effect which solitude and grief had gradually produced upon her\par
mind, subdued her spirits; she rushed forward into the chamber, and\par
sunk almost senseless into a chair. Returning reason soon overcame the\par
dreadful, but pitiable attack of imagination, and she turned to the\par
papers, though still with so little recollection, that her eyes\par
involuntarily settled on the writing of some loose sheets, which lay\par
open; and she was unconscious, that she was transgressing her father’s\par
strict injunction, till a sentence of dreadful import awakened her\par
attention and her memory together. She hastily put the papers from her;\par
but the words, which had roused equally her curiosity and terror, she\par
could not dismiss from her thoughts. So powerfully had they affected\par
her, that she even could not resolve to destroy the papers immediately;\par
and the more she dwelt on the circumstance, the more it inflamed her\par
imagination. Urged by the most forcible, and apparently the most\par
necessary, curiosity to enquire farther, concerning the terrible and\par
mysterious subject, to which she had seen an allusion, she began to\par
lament her promise to destroy the papers. For a moment, she even\par
doubted, whether it could justly be obeyed, in contradiction to such\par
reasons as there appeared to be for further information. But the\par
delusion was momentary.\par
\par
“I have given a solemn promise,” said she, “to observe a solemn\par
injunction, and it is not my business to argue, but to obey. Let me\par
hasten to remove the temptation, that would destroy my innocence, and\par
embitter my life with the consciousness of irremediable guilt, while I\par
have strength to reject it.”\par
\par
Thus reanimated with a sense of her duty, she completed the triumph of\par
her integrity over temptation, more forcible than any she had ever\par
known, and consigned the papers to the flames. Her eyes watched them as\par
they slowly consumed, she shuddered at the recollection of the sentence\par
she had just seen, and at the certainty, that the only opportunity of\par
explaining it was then passing away for ever.\par
\par
It was long after this, that she recollected the purse; and as she was\par
depositing it, unopened, in a cabinet, perceiving that it contained\par
something of a size larger than coin, she examined it. “His hand\par
deposited them here,” said she, as she kissed some pieces of the coin,\par
and wetted them with her tears, “his hand—which is now dust!” At the\par
bottom of the purse was a small packet, having taken out which, and\par
unfolded paper after paper, she found to be an ivory case, containing\par
the miniature of a—lady! She started—“The same,” said she, “my father\par
wept over!” On examining the countenance she could recollect no person\par
that it resembled. It was of uncommon beauty, and was characterised by\par
an expression of sweetness, shaded with sorrow, and tempered by\par
resignation.\par
\par
St. Aubert had given no directions concerning this picture, nor had\par
even named it; she, therefore, thought herself justified in preserving\par
it. More than once remembering his manner, when he had spoken of the\par
Marchioness of Villeroi, she felt inclined to believe that this was her\par
resemblance; yet there appeared no reason why he should have preserved\par
a picture of that lady, or, having preserved it, why he should lament\par
over it in a manner so striking and affecting as she had witnessed on\par
the night preceding his departure.\par
\par
Emily still gazed on the countenance, examining its features, but she\par
knew not where to detect the charm that captivated her attention, and\par
inspired sentiments of such love and pity. Dark brown hair played\par
carelessly along the open forehead; the nose was rather inclined to\par
aquiline; the lips spoke in a smile, but it was a melancholy one; the\par
eyes were blue, and were directed upwards with an expression of\par
peculiar meekness, while the soft cloud of the brow spoke of the fine\par
sensibility of the temper.\par
\par
Emily was roused from the musing mood into which the picture had thrown\par
her, by the closing of the garden gate; and, on turning her eyes to the\par
window, she saw Valancourt coming towards the château. Her spirits\par
agitated by the subjects that had lately occupied her mind, she felt\par
unprepared to see him, and remained a few moments in the chamber to\par
recover herself.\par
\par
When she met him in the parlour, she was struck with the change that\par
appeared in his air and countenance since they had parted in Rousillon,\par
which twilight and the distress she suffered on the preceding evening\par
had prevented her from observing. But dejection and languor\par
disappeared, for a moment, in the smile that now enlightened his\par
countenance, on perceiving her. “You see,” said he, “I have availed\par
myself of the permission with which you honoured me—of bidding _you_\par
farewell, whom I had the happiness of meeting only yesterday.”\par
\par
Emily smiled faintly, and, anxious to say something, asked if he had\par
been long in Gascony. “A few days only,” replied Valancourt, while a\par
blush passed over his cheek. “I engaged in a long ramble after I had\par
the misfortune of parting with the friends who had made my wanderings\par
among the Pyrenees so delightful.”\par
\par
A tear came to Emily’s eye, as Valancourt said this, which he observed;\par
and, anxious to draw off her attention from the remembrance that had\par
occasioned it, as well as shocked at his own thoughtlessness, he began\par
to speak on other subjects, expressing his admiration of the château,\par
and its prospects. Emily, who felt somewhat embarrassed how to support\par
a conversation, was glad of such an opportunity to continue it on\par
indifferent topics. They walked down to the terrace, where Valancourt\par
was charmed with the river scenery, and the views over the opposite\par
shores of Guienne.\par
\par
As he leaned on the wall of the terrace, watching the rapid current of\par
the Garonne, “I was a few weeks ago,” said he, “at the source of this\par
noble river; I had not then the happiness of knowing you, or I should\par
have regretted your absence—it was a scene so exactly suited to your\par
taste. It rises in a part of the Pyrenees, still wilder and more\par
sublime, I think, than any we passed in the way to Rousillon.” He then\par
described its fall among the precipices of the mountains, where its\par
waters, augmented by the streams that descend from the snowy summits\par
around, rush into the Vallée d’Aran, between whose romantic heights it\par
foams along, pursuing its way to the north west till it emerges upon\par
the plains of Languedoc. Then, washing the walls of Thoulouse, and\par
turning again to the north west, it assumes a milder character, as it\par
fertilizes the pastures of Gascony and Guienne, in its progress to the\par
Bay of Biscay.\par
\par
Emily and Valancourt talked of the scenes they had passed among the\par
Pyrenean Alps; as he spoke of which there was often a tremulous\par
tenderness in his voice, and sometimes he expatiated on them with all\par
the fire of genius, sometimes would appear scarcely conscious of the\par
topic, though he continued to speak. This subject recalled forcibly to\par
Emily the idea of her father, whose image appeared in every landscape,\par
which Valancourt particularized, whose remarks dwelt upon her memory,\par
and whose enthusiasm still glowed in her heart. Her silence, at length,\par
reminded Valancourt how nearly his conversation approached to the\par
occasion of her grief, and he changed the subject, though for one\par
scarcely less affecting to Emily. When he admired the grandeur of the\par
plane-tree, that spread its wide branches over the terrace, and under\par
whose shade they now sat, she remembered how often she had sat thus\par
with St. Aubert, and heard him express the same admiration.\par
\par
“This was a favourite tree with my dear father,” said she; “he used to\par
love to sit under its foliage with his family about him, in the fine\par
evenings of summer.”\par
\par
Valancourt understood her feelings, and was silent; had she raised her\par
eyes from the ground she would have seen tears in his. He rose, and\par
leaned on the wall of the terrace, from which, in a few moments, he\par
returned to his seat, then rose again, and appeared to be greatly\par
agitated; while Emily found her spirits so much depressed, that several\par
of her attempts to renew the conversation were ineffectual. Valancourt\par
again sat down, but was still silent, and trembled. At length he said,\par
with a hesitating voice, “This lovely scene!—I am going to leave—to\par
leave you—perhaps for ever! These moments may never return; I cannot\par
resolve to neglect, though I scarcely dare to avail myself of them. Let\par
me, however, without offending the delicacy of your sorrow, venture to\par
declare the admiration I must always feel of your goodness—O! that at\par
some future period I might be permitted to call it love!”\par
\par
Emily’s emotion would not suffer her to reply; and Valancourt, who now\par
ventured to look up, observing her countenance change, expected to see\par
her faint, and made an involuntary effort to support her, which\par
recalled Emily to a sense of her situation, and to an exertion of her\par
spirits. Valancourt did not appear to notice her indisposition, but,\par
when he spoke again, his voice told the tenderest love. “I will not\par
presume,” he added, “to intrude this subject longer upon your attention\par
at this time, but I may, perhaps, be permitted to mention, that these\par
parting moments would lose much of their bitterness if I might be\par
allowed to hope the declaration I have made would not exclude me from\par
your presence in future.”\par
\par
Emily made another effort to overcome the confusion of her thoughts,\par
and to speak. She feared to trust the preference her heart acknowledged\par
towards Valancourt, and to give him any encouragement for hope, on so\par
short an acquaintance. For though in this narrow period she had\par
observed much that was admirable in his taste and disposition, and\par
though these observations had been sanctioned by the opinion of her\par
father, they were not sufficient testimonies of his general worth to\par
determine her upon a subject so infinitely important to her future\par
happiness as that, which now solicited her attention. Yet, though the\par
thought of dismissing Valancourt was so very painful to her, that she\par
could scarcely endure to pause upon it, the consciousness of this made\par
her fear the partiality of her judgment, and hesitate still more to\par
encourage that suit, for which her own heart too tenderly pleaded. The\par
family of Valancourt, if not his circumstances, had been known to her\par
father, and known to be unexceptionable. Of his circumstances,\par
Valancourt himself hinted as far as delicacy would permit, when he said\par
he had at present little else to offer but a heart, that adored her. He\par
had solicited only for a distant hope, and she could not resolve to\par
forbid, though she scarcely dared to permit it; at length, she acquired\par
courage to say, that she must think herself honoured by the good\par
opinion of any person, whom her father had esteemed.\par
\par
“And was I, then, thought worthy of his esteem?” said Valancourt, in a\par
voice trembling with anxiety; then checking himself, he added, “But\par
pardon the question; I scarcely know what I say. If I might dare to\par
hope, that you think me not unworthy such honour, and might be\par
permitted sometimes to enquire after your health, I should now leave\par
you with comparative tranquillity.”\par
\par
Emily, after a moment’s silence, said, “I will be ingenuous with you,\par
for I know you will understand, and allow for my situation; you will\par
consider it as a proof of my—my esteem that I am so. Though I live here\par
in what was my father’s house, I live here alone. I have, alas! no\par
longer a parent—a parent, whose presence might sanction your visits. It\par
is unnecessary for me to point out the impropriety of my receiving\par
them.”\par
\par
“Nor will I affect to be insensible of this,” replied Valancourt,\par
adding mournfully—“but what is to console me for my candour? I distress\par
you, and would now leave the subject, if I might carry with me a hope\par
of being some time permitted to renew it, of being allowed to make\par
myself known to your family.”\par
\par
Emily was again confused, and again hesitated what to reply; she felt\par
most acutely the difficulty—the forlornness of her situation, which did\par
not allow her a single relative, or friend, to whom she could turn for\par
even a look, that might support and guide her in the present\par
embarrassing circumstances. Madame Cheron, who was her only relative,\par
and ought to have been this friend, was either occupied by her own\par
amusements, or so resentful of the reluctance her niece had shown to\par
quit La Vallée, that she seemed totally to have abandoned her.\par
\par
“Ah! I see,” said Valancourt, after a long pause, during which Emily\par
had begun, and left unfinished two or three sentences, “I see that I\par
have nothing to hope; my fears were too just, you think me unworthy of\par
your esteem. That fatal journey! which I considered as the happiest\par
period of my life—those delightful days were to embitter all my future\par
ones. How often I have looked back to them with hope and fear—yet never\par
till this moment could I prevail with myself to regret their enchanting\par
influence.”\par
\par
His voice faltered, and he abruptly quitted his seat and walked on the\par
terrace. There was an expression of despair on his countenance, that\par
affected Emily. The pleadings of her heart overcame, in some degree,\par
her extreme timidity, and, when he resumed his seat, she said, in an\par
accent that betrayed her tenderness, “You do both yourself and me\par
injustice when you say I think you unworthy of my esteem; I will\par
acknowledge that you have long possessed it, and—and—”\par
\par
Valancourt waited impatiently for the conclusion of the sentence, but\par
the words died on her lips. Her eyes, however, reflected all the\par
emotions of her heart. Valancourt passed, in an instant, from the\par
impatience of despair, to that of joy and tenderness. “O Emily!” he\par
exclaimed, “my own Emily—teach me to sustain this moment! Let me seal\par
it as the most sacred of my life!”\par
\par
He pressed her hand to his lips, it was cold and trembling; and,\par
raising her eyes, he saw the paleness of her countenance. Tears came to\par
her relief, and Valancourt watched in anxious silence over her. In a\par
few moments, she recovered herself, and smiling faintly through her\par
tears, said, “Can you excuse this weakness? My spirits have not yet, I\par
believe, recovered from the shock they lately received.”\par
\par
“I cannot excuse myself,” said Valancourt, “but I will forbear to renew\par
the subject, which may have contributed to agitate them, now that I can\par
leave you with the sweet certainty of possessing your esteem.”\par
\par
Then, forgetting his resolution, he again spoke of himself. “You know\par
not,” said he, “the many anxious hours I have passed near you lately,\par
when you believed me, if indeed you honoured me with a thought, far\par
away. I have wandered, near the château, in the still hours of the\par
night, when no eye could observe me. It was delightful to know I was so\par
near you, and there was something particularly soothing in the thought,\par
that I watched round your habitation, while you slept. These grounds\par
are not entirely new to me. Once I ventured within the fence, and spent\par
one of the happiest, and yet most melancholy hours of my life in\par
walking under what I believed to be your window.”\par
\par
Emily enquired how long Valancourt had been in the neighbourhood.\par
“Several days,” he replied. “It was my design to avail myself of the\par
permission M. St. Aubert had given me. I scarcely know how to account\par
for it; but, though I anxiously wished to do this, my resolution always\par
failed, when the moment approached, and I constantly deferred my visit.\par
I lodged in a village at some distance, and wandered with my dogs,\par
among the scenes of this charming country, wishing continually to meet\par
you, yet not daring to visit you.”\par
\par
Having thus continued to converse, without perceiving the flight of\par
time, Valancourt, at length, seemed to recollect himself. “I must go,”\par
said he mournfully, “but it is with the hope of seeing you again, of\par
being permitted to pay my respects to your family; let me hear this\par
hope confirmed by your voice.” “My family will be happy to see any\par
friend of my dear father,” said Emily. Valancourt kissed her hand, and\par
still lingered, unable to depart, while Emily sat silently, with her\par
eyes bent on the ground; and Valancourt, as he gazed on her, considered\par
that it would soon be impossible for him to recall, even to his memory,\par
the exact resemblance of the beautiful countenance he then beheld; at\par
this moment a hasty footstep approached from behind the plane-tree,\par
and, turning her eyes, Emily saw Madame Cheron. She felt a blush steal\par
upon her cheek, and her frame trembled with the emotion of her mind;\par
but she instantly rose to meet her visitor. “So, niece!” said Madame\par
Cheron, casting a look of surprise and enquiry on Valancourt, “so\par
niece, how do you do? But I need not ask, your looks tell me you have\par
already recovered your loss.”\par
\par
“My looks do me injustice then, Madame, my loss I know can never be\par
recovered.”\par
\par
“Well—well! I will not argue with you; I see you have exactly your\par
father’s disposition; and let me tell you it would have been much\par
happier for him, poor man! if it had been a different one.”\par
\par
A look of dignified displeasure, with which Emily regarded Madame\par
Cheron, while she spoke, would have touched almost any other heart; she\par
made no other reply, but introduced Valancourt, who could scarcely\par
stifle the resentment he felt, and whose bow Madame Cheron returned\par
with a slight curtsy, and a look of supercilious examination. After a\par
few moments he took leave of Emily, in a manner, that hastily expressed\par
his pain both at his own departure, and at leaving her to the society\par
of Madame Cheron.\par
\par
“Who is that young man?” said her aunt, in an accent which equally\par
implied inquisitiveness and censure. “Some idle admirer of yours I\par
suppose; but I believed niece you had a greater sense of propriety,\par
than to have received the visits of any young man in your present\par
unfriended situation. Let me tell you the world will observe those\par
things, and it will talk, aye and very freely too.”\par
\par
Emily, extremely shocked at this coarse speech, attempted to interrupt\par
it; but Madame Cheron would proceed, with all the self-importance of a\par
person, to whom power is new.\par
\par
“It is very necessary you should be under the eye of some person more\par
able to guide you than yourself. I, indeed, have not much leisure for\par
such a task; however, since your poor father made it his last request,\par
that I should overlook your conduct—I must even take you under my care.\par
But this let me tell you niece, that, unless you will determine to be\par
very conformable to my direction, I shall not trouble myself longer\par
about you.”\par
\par
Emily made no attempt to interrupt Madame Cheron a second time, grief\par
and the pride of conscious innocence kept her silent, till her aunt\par
said, “I am now come to take you with me to Thoulouse; I am sorry to\par
find, that your poor father died, after all, in such indifferent\par
circumstances; however, I shall take you home with me. Ah! poor man, he\par
was always more generous than provident, or he would not have left his\par
daughter dependent on his relations.”\par
\par
“Nor has he done so, I hope, madam,” said Emily calmly, “nor did his\par
pecuniary misfortunes arise from that noble generosity, which always\par
distinguished him. The affairs of M. de Motteville may, I trust, yet be\par
settled without deeply injuring his creditors, and in the meantime I\par
should be very happy to remain at La Vallée.”\par
\par
“No doubt you would,” replied Madame Cheron, with a smile of irony,\par
“and I shall no doubt consent to this, since I see how necessary\par
tranquillity and retirement are to restore your spirits. I did not\par
think you capable of so much duplicity, niece; when you pleaded this\par
excuse for remaining here, I foolishly believed it to be a just one,\par
nor expected to have found with you so agreeable a companion as this M.\par
La Val—, I forget his name.”\par
\par
Emily could no longer endure these cruel indignities. “It was a just\par
one, madam,” said she; “and now, indeed, I feel more than ever the\par
value of the retirement I then solicited; and, if the purport of your\par
visit is only to add insult to the sorrows of your brother’s child, she\par
could well have spared it.”\par
\par
“I see that I have undertaken a very troublesome task,” said Madame\par
Cheron, colouring highly. “I am sure, madam,” said Emily mildly, and\par
endeavouring to restrain her tears, “I am sure my father did not mean\par
it should be such. I have the happiness to reflect, that my conduct\par
under his eye was such as he often delighted to approve. It would be\par
very painful to me to disobey the sister of such a parent, and, if you\par
believe the task will really be so troublesome, I must lament, that it\par
is yours.”\par
\par
“Well! niece, fine speaking signifies little. I am willing, in\par
consideration of my poor brother, to overlook the impropriety of your\par
late conduct, and to try what your future will be.”\par
\par
Emily interrupted her, to beg she would explain what was the\par
impropriety she alluded to.\par
\par
“What impropriety! why that of receiving the visits of a lover unknown\par
to your family,” replied Madame Cheron, not considering the impropriety\par
of which she had herself been guilty, in exposing her niece to the\par
possibility of conduct so erroneous.\par
\par
A faint blush passed over Emily’s countenance; pride and anxiety\par
struggled in her breast; and, till she recollected, that appearances\par
did, in some degree, justify her aunt’s suspicions, she could not\par
resolve to humble herself so far as to enter into the defence of a\par
conduct, which had been so innocent and undesigning on her part. She\par
mentioned the manner of Valancourt’s introduction to her father; the\par
circumstances of his receiving the pistol-shot, and of their afterwards\par
travelling together; with the accidental way, in which she had met him,\par
on the preceding evening. She owned he had declared a partiality for\par
her, and that he had asked permission to address her family.\par
\par
“And who is this young adventurer, pray?” said Madame Cheron, “and what\par
are his pretensions?” “These he must himself explain, madam,” replied\par
Emily. “Of his family my father was not ignorant, and I believe it is\par
unexceptionable.” She then proceeded to mention what she knew\par
concerning it.\par
\par
“Oh, then, this it seems is a younger brother,” exclaimed her aunt,\par
“and of course a beggar. A very fine tale indeed! And so my brother\par
took a fancy to this young man after only a few days acquaintance!—but\par
that was so like him! In his youth he was always taking these likes and\par
dislikes, when no other person saw any reason for them at all; nay,\par
indeed, I have often thought the people he disapproved were much more\par
agreeable than those he admired;—but there is no accounting for tastes.\par
He was always so much influenced by people’s countenances; now I, for\par
my part, have no notion of this, it is all ridiculous enthusiasm. What\par
has a man’s face to do with his character? Can a man of good character\par
help having a disagreeable face?”—which last sentence Madame Cheron\par
delivered with the decisive air of a person who congratulates herself\par
on having made a grand discovery, and believes the question to be\par
unanswerably settled.\par
\par
Emily, desirous of concluding the conversation, enquired if her aunt\par
would accept some refreshment, and Madame Cheron accompanied her to the\par
château, but without desisting from a topic, which she discussed with\par
so much complacency to herself, and severity to her niece.\par
\par
“I am sorry to perceive, niece,” said she, in allusion to somewhat that\par
Emily had said, concerning physiognomy, “that you have a great many of\par
your father’s prejudices, and among them those sudden predilections for\par
people from their looks. I can perceive, that you imagine yourself to\par
be violently in love with this young adventurer, after an acquaintance\par
of only a few days. There was something, too, so charmingly romantic in\par
the manner of your meeting!”\par
\par
Emily checked the tears, that trembled in her eyes, while she said,\par
“When my conduct shall deserve this severity, madam, you will do well\par
to exercise it; till then justice, if not tenderness, should surely\par
restrain it. I have never willingly offended you; now I have lost my\par
parents, you are the only person to whom I can look for kindness. Let\par
me not lament more than ever the loss of such parents.” The last words\par
were almost stifled by her emotions, and she burst into tears.\par
Remembering the delicacy and the tenderness of St. Aubert, the happy,\par
happy days she had passed in these scenes, and contrasting them with\par
the coarse and unfeeling behaviour of Madame Cheron, and from the\par
future hours of mortification she must submit to in her presence—a\par
degree of grief seized her, that almost reached despair. Madame Cheron,\par
more offended by the reproof which Emily’s words conveyed, than touched\par
by the sorrow they expressed, said nothing, that might soften her\par
grief; but, notwithstanding an apparent reluctance to receive her\par
niece, she desired her company. The love of sway was her ruling\par
passion, and she knew it would be highly gratified by taking into her\par
house a young orphan, who had no appeal from her decisions, and on whom\par
she could exercise without control the capricious humour of the moment.\par
\par
On entering the château, Madame Cheron expressed a desire, that she\par
would put up what she thought necessary to take to Thoulouse, as she\par
meant to set off immediately. Emily now tried to persuade her to defer\par
the journey, at least till the next day, and, at length, with much\par
difficulty, prevailed.\par
\par
The day passed in the exercise of petty tyranny on the part of Madame\par
Cheron, and in mournful regret and melancholy anticipation on that of\par
Emily, who, when her aunt retired to her apartment for the night, went\par
to take leave of every other room in this her dear native home, which\par
she was now quitting for she knew not how long, and for a world, to\par
which she was wholly a stranger. She could not conquer a presentiment,\par
which frequently occurred to her, this night—that she should never more\par
return to La Vallée. Having passed a considerable time in what had been\par
her father’s study, having selected some of his favourite authors, to\par
put up with her clothes, and shed many tears, as she wiped the dust\par
from their covers, she seated herself in his chair before the reading\par
desk, and sat lost in melancholy reflection, till Theresa opened the\par
door to examine, as was her custom before she went to bed, it was all\par
safe. She started, on observing her young lady, who bade her come in,\par
and then gave her some directions for keeping the château in readiness\par
for her reception at all times.\par
\par
“Alas-a-day! that you should leave it!” said Theresa, “I think you\par
would be happier here than where you are going, if one may judge.”\par
Emily made no reply to this remark; the sorrow Theresa proceeded to\par
express at her departure affected her, but she found some comfort in\par
the simple affection of this poor old servant, to whom she gave such\par
directions as might best conduce to her comfort during her own absence.\par
\par
Having dismissed Theresa to bed, Emily wandered through every lonely\par
apartment of the château, lingering long in what had been her father’s\par
bedroom, indulging melancholy, yet not unpleasing, emotions, and,\par
having often returned within the door to take another look at it, she\par
withdrew to her own chamber. From her window she gazed upon the garden\par
below, shown faintly by the moon, rising over the tops of the\par
palm-trees, and, at length, the calm beauty of the night increased a\par
desire of indulging the mournful sweetness of bidding farewell to the\par
beloved shades of her childhood, till she was tempted to descend.\par
Throwing over her the light veil, in which she usually walked, she\par
silently passed into the garden, and, hastening towards the distant\par
groves, was glad to breathe once more the air of liberty, and to sigh\par
unobserved. The deep repose of the scene, the rich scents, that floated\par
on the breeze, the grandeur of the wide horizon and of the clear blue\par
arch, soothed and gradually elevated her mind to that sublime\par
complacency, which renders the vexations of this world so insignificant\par
and mean in our eyes, that we wonder they have had power for a moment\par
to disturb us. Emily forgot Madame Cheron and all the circumstances of\par
her conduct, while her thoughts ascended to the contemplation of those\par
unnumbered worlds, that lie scattered in the depths of æther, thousands\par
of them hid from human eyes, and almost beyond the flight of human\par
fancy. As her imagination soared through the regions of space, and\par
aspired to that Great First Cause, which pervades and governs all\par
being, the idea of her father scarcely ever left her; but it was a\par
pleasing idea, since she resigned him to God in the full confidence of\par
a pure and holy faith. She pursued her way through the groves to the\par
terrace, often pausing as memory awakened the pang of affection, and as\par
reason anticipated the exile, into which she was going.\par
\par
And now the moon was high over the woods, touching their summits with\par
yellow light, and darting between the foliage long level beams; while\par
on the rapid Garonne below the trembling radiance was faintly obscured\par
by the lightest vapour. Emily long watched the playing lustre, listened\par
to the soothing murmur of the current, and the yet lighter sounds of\par
the air, as it stirred, at intervals, the lofty palm-trees. “How\par
delightful is the sweet breath of these groves,” said she. “This lovely\par
scene!—how often shall I remember and regret it, when I am far away.\par
Alas! what events may occur before I see it again! O, peaceful, happy\par
shades!—scenes of my infant delights, of parental tenderness now lost\par
for ever!—why must I leave ye!—In your retreats I should still find\par
safety and repose. Sweet hours of my childhood—I am now to leave even\par
your last memorials! No objects, that would revive your impressions,\par
will remain for me!”\par
\par
Then drying her tears and looking up, her thoughts rose again to the\par
sublime subject she had contemplated; the same divine complacency stole\par
over her heart, and, hushing its throbs, inspired hope and confidence\par
and resignation to the will of the Deity, whose works filled her mind\par
with adoration.\par
\par
Emily gazed long on the plane-tree, and then seated herself, for the\par
last time, on the bench under its shade, where she had so often sat\par
with her parents, and where, only a few hours before, she had conversed\par
with Valancourt, at the remembrance of whom, thus revived, a mingled\par
sensation of esteem, tenderness and anxiety rose in her breast. With\par
this remembrance occurred a recollection of his late confession—that he\par
had often wandered near her habitation in the night, having even passed\par
the boundary of the garden, and it immediately occurred to her, that he\par
might be at this moment in the grounds. The fear of meeting him,\par
particularly after the declaration he had made, and of incurring a\par
censure, which her aunt might so reasonably bestow, if it was known,\par
that she was met by her lover, at this hour, made her instantly leave\par
her beloved plane-tree, and walk towards the château. She cast an\par
anxious eye around, and often stopped for a moment to examine the\par
shadowy scene before she ventured to proceed, but she passed on without\par
perceiving any person, till, having reached a clump of almond trees,\par
not far from the house, she rested to take a retrospect of the garden,\par
and to sigh forth another adieu. As her eyes wandered over the\par
landscape she thought she perceived a person emerge from the groves,\par
and pass slowly along a moonlight alley that led between them; but the\par
distance, and the imperfect light would not suffer her to judge with\par
any degree of certainty whether this was fancy or reality. She\par
continued to gaze for some time on the spot, till on the dead stillness\par
of the air she heard a sudden sound, and in the next instant fancied\par
she distinguished footsteps near her. Wasting not another moment in\par
conjecture, she hurried to the château, and, having reached it, retired\par
to her chamber, where, as she closed her window she looked upon the\par
garden, and then again thought she distinguished a figure, gliding\par
between the almond trees she had just left. She immediately withdrew\par
from the casement, and, though much agitated, sought in sleep the\par
refreshment of a short oblivion.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XI\par
\par
I leave that flowery path for eye\par
Of childhood, where I sported many a day,\par
Warbling and sauntering carelessly along;\par
Where every face was innocent and gay,\par
Each vale romantic, tuneful every tongue—\par
Sweet, wild, and artless, all.\par
                    THE MINSTREL\par
\par
\par
At an early hour, the carriage, which was to take Emily and Madame\par
Cheron to Thoulouse, appeared at the door of the château, and Madame\par
was already in the breakfast-room, when her niece entered it. The\par
repast was silent and melancholy on the part of Emily; and Madame\par
Cheron, whose vanity was piqued on observing her dejection, reproved\par
her in a manner that did not contribute to remove it. It was with much\par
reluctance, that Emily’s request to take with her the dog, which had\par
been a favourite of her father, was granted. Her aunt, impatient to be\par
gone, ordered the carriage to draw up; and, while she passed to the\par
hall door, Emily gave another look into the library, and another\par
farewell glance over the garden, and then followed. Old Theresa stood\par
at the door to take leave of her young lady. “God for ever keep you,\par
ma’amselle!” said she, while Emily gave her hand in silence, and could\par
answer only with a pressure of her hand, and a forced smile.\par
\par
At the gate, which led out of the grounds, several of her father’s\par
pensioners were assembled to bid her farewell, to whom she would have\par
spoken, if her aunt would have suffered the driver to stop; and, having\par
distributed to them almost all the money she had about her, she sunk\par
back in the carriage, yielding to the melancholy of her heart. Soon\par
after, she caught, between the steep banks of the road, another view of\par
the château, peeping from among the high trees, and surrounded by green\par
slopes and tufted groves, the Garonne winding its way beneath their\par
shades, sometimes lost among the vineyards, and then rising in greater\par
majesty in the distant pastures. The towering precipices of the\par
Pyrenees, that rose to the south, gave Emily a thousand interesting\par
recollections of her late journey; and these objects of her former\par
enthusiastic admiration, now excited only sorrow and regret. Having\par
gazed on the château and its lovely scenery, till the banks again\par
closed upon them, her mind became too much occupied by mournful\par
reflections, to permit her to attend to the conversation, which Madame\par
Cheron had begun on some trivial topic, so that they soon travelled in\par
profound silence.\par
\par
Valancourt, meanwhile, was returned to Estuvière, his heart occupied\par
with the image of Emily; sometimes indulging in reveries of future\par
happiness, but more frequently shrinking with dread of the opposition\par
he might encounter from her family. He was the younger son of an\par
ancient family of Gascony; and, having lost his parents at an early\par
period of his life, the care of his education and of his small portion\par
had devolved to his brother, the Count de Duvarney, his senior by\par
nearly twenty years. Valancourt had been educated in all the\par
accomplishments of his age, and had an ardour of spirit, and a certain\par
grandeur of mind, that gave him particular excellence in the exercises\par
then thought heroic. His little fortune had been diminished by the\par
necessary expences of his education; but M. La Valancourt, the elder,\par
seemed to think that his genius and accomplishments would amply supply\par
the deficiency of his inheritance. They offered flattering hopes of\par
promotion in the military profession, in those times almost the only\par
one in which a gentleman could engage without incurring a stain on his\par
name; and La Valancourt was of course enrolled in the army. The general\par
genius of his mind was but little understood by his brother. That\par
ardour for whatever is great and good in the moral world, as well as in\par
the natural one, displayed itself in his infant years; and the strong\par
indignation, which he felt and expressed at a criminal, or a mean\par
action, sometimes drew upon him the displeasure of his tutor; who\par
reprobated it under the general term of violence of temper; and who,\par
when haranguing on the virtues of mildness and moderation, seemed to\par
forget the gentleness and compassion, which always appeared in his\par
pupil towards objects of misfortune.\par
\par
He had now obtained leave of absence from his regiment when he made the\par
excursion into the Pyrenees, which was the means of introducing him to\par
St. Aubert; and, as this permission was nearly expired, he was the more\par
anxious to declare himself to Emily’s family, from whom he reasonably\par
apprehended opposition, since his fortune, though, with a moderate\par
addition from hers, it would be sufficient to support them, would not\par
satisfy the views, either of vanity, or ambition. Valancourt was not\par
without the latter, but he saw golden visions of promotion in the army;\par
and believed, that with Emily he could, in the mean time, be delighted\par
to live within the limits of his humble income. His thoughts were now\par
occupied in considering the means of making himself known to her\par
family, to whom, however, he had yet no address, for he was entirely\par
ignorant of Emily’s precipitate departure from La Vallée, of whom he\par
hoped to obtain it.\par
\par
Meanwhile, the travellers pursued their journey; Emily making frequent\par
efforts to appear cheerful, and too often relapsing into silence and\par
dejection. Madame Cheron, attributing her melancholy solely to the\par
circumstance of her being removed to a distance from her lover, and\par
believing, that the sorrow, which her niece still expressed for the\par
loss of St. Aubert, proceeded partly from an affectation of\par
sensibility, endeavoured to make it appear ridiculous to her, that such\par
deep regret should continue to be felt so long after the period usually\par
allowed for grief.\par
\par
At length, these unpleasant lectures were interrupted by the arrival of\par
the travellers at Thoulouse; and Emily, who had not been there for many\par
years, and had only a very faint recollection of it, was surprised at\par
the ostentatious style exhibited in her aunt’s house and furniture; the\par
more so, perhaps, because it was so totally different from the modest\par
elegance, to which she had been accustomed. She followed Madame Cheron\par
through a large hall, where several servants in rich liveries appeared,\par
to a kind of saloon, fitted up with more show than taste; and her aunt,\par
complaining of fatigue, ordered supper immediately. “I am glad to find\par
myself in my own house again,” said she, throwing herself on a large\par
settee, “and to have my own people about me. I detest travelling;\par
though, indeed, I ought to like it, for what I see abroad always makes\par
me delighted to return to my own château. What makes you so silent,\par
child?—What is it that disturbs you now?”\par
\par
Emily suppressed a starting tear, and tried to smile away the\par
expression of an oppressed heart; she was thinking of _her_ home, and\par
felt too sensibly the arrogance and ostentatious vanity of Madame\par
Cheron’s conversation. “Can this be my father’s sister!” said she to\par
herself; and then the conviction that she was so, warming her heart\par
with something like kindness towards her, she felt anxious to soften\par
the harsh impression her mind had received of her aunt’s character, and\par
to show a willingness to oblige her. The effort did not entirely fail;\par
she listened with apparent cheerfulness, while Madame Cheron expatiated\par
on the splendour of her house, told of the numerous parties she\par
entertained, and what she should expect of Emily, whose diffidence\par
assumed the air of a reserve, which her aunt, believing it to be that\par
of pride and ignorance united, now took occasion to reprehend. She knew\par
nothing of the conduct of a mind, that fears to trust its own powers;\par
which, possessing a nice judgment, and inclining to believe, that every\par
other person perceives still more critically, fears to commit itself to\par
censure, and seeks shelter in the obscurity of silence. Emily had\par
frequently blushed at the fearless manners, which she had seen admired,\par
and the brilliant nothings, which she had heard applauded; yet this\par
applause, so far from encouraging her to imitate the conduct that had\par
won it, rather made her shrink into the reserve, that would protect her\par
from such absurdity.\par
\par
Madame Cheron looked on her niece’s diffidence with a feeling very near\par
to contempt, and endeavoured to overcome it by reproof, rather than to\par
encourage it by gentleness.\par
\par
The entrance of supper somewhat interrupted the complacent discourse of\par
Madame Cheron and the painful considerations, which it had forced upon\par
Emily. When the repast, which was rendered ostentatious by the\par
attendance of a great number of servants, and by a profusion of plate,\par
was over, Madame Cheron retired to her chamber, and a female servant\par
came to show Emily to hers. Having passed up a large staircase, and\par
through several galleries, they came to a flight of back stairs, which\par
led into a short passage in a remote part of the château, and there the\par
servant opened the door of a small chamber, which she said was\par
Ma’amselle Emily’s, who, once more alone, indulged the tears she had\par
long tried to restrain.\par
\par
Those, who know, from experience, how much the heart becomes attached\par
even to inanimate objects, to which it has been long accustomed, how\par
unwillingly it resigns them; how with the sensations of an old friend\par
it meets them, after temporary absence, will understand the forlornness\par
of Emily’s feelings, of Emily shut out from the only home she had known\par
from her infancy, and thrown upon a scene, and among persons,\par
disagreeable for more qualities than their novelty. Her father’s\par
favourite dog, now in the chamber, thus seemed to acquire the character\par
and importance of a friend; and, as the animal fawned over her when she\par
wept, and licked her hands, “Ah, poor Manchon!” said she, “I have\par
nobody now to love me—but you!” and she wept the more. After some time,\par
her thoughts returning to her father’s injunctions, she remembered how\par
often he had blamed her for indulging useless sorrow; how often he had\par
pointed out to her the necessity of fortitude and patience, assuring\par
her, that the faculties of the mind strengthen by exertion, till they\par
finally unnerve affliction, and triumph over it. These recollections\par
dried her tears, gradually soothed her spirits, and inspired her with\par
the sweet emulation of practising precepts, which her father had so\par
frequently inculcated.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XII\par
\par
Some pow’r impart the spear and shield,\par
At which the wizard passions fly,\par
By which the giant follies die!\par
                    COLLINS\par
\par
\par
Madame Cheron’s house stood at a little distance from the city of\par
Thoulouse, and was surrounded by extensive gardens, in which Emily, who\par
had risen early, amused herself with wandering before breakfast. From a\par
terrace, that extended along the highest part of them, was a wide view\par
over Languedoc. On the distant horizon to the south, she discovered the\par
wild summits of the Pyrenees, and her fancy immediately painted the\par
green pastures of Gascony at their feet. Her heart pointed to her\par
peaceful home—to the neighbourhood where Valancourt was—where St.\par
Aubert had been; and her imagination, piercing the veil of distance,\par
brought that home to her eyes in all its interesting and romantic\par
beauty. She experienced an inexpressible pleasure in believing, that\par
she beheld the country around it, though no feature could be\par
distinguished, except the retiring chain of the Pyrenees; and,\par
inattentive to the scene immediately before her, and to the flight of\par
time, she continued to lean on the window of a pavilion, that\par
terminated the terrace, with her eyes fixed on Gascony, and her mind\par
occupied with the interesting ideas which the view of it awakened, till\par
a servant came to tell her breakfast was ready. Her thoughts thus\par
recalled to the surrounding objects, the straight walks, square\par
parterres, and artificial fountains of the garden, could not fail, as\par
she passed through it, to appear the worse, opposed to the negligent\par
graces, and natural beauties of the grounds of La Vallée, upon which\par
her recollection had been so intensely employed.\par
\par
“Whither have you been rambling so early?” said Madame Cheron, as her\par
niece entered the breakfast-room. “I don’t approve of these solitary\par
walks.” And Emily was surprised, when, having informed her aunt, that\par
she had been no further than the gardens, she understood these to be\par
included in the reproof. “I desire you will not walk there again at so\par
early an hour unattended,” said Madame Cheron; “my gardens are very\par
extensive; and a young woman, who can make assignations by moonlight at\par
La Vallée, is not to be trusted to her own inclinations elsewhere.”\par
\par
Emily, extremely surprised and shocked, had scarcely power to beg an\par
explanation of these words, and, when she did, her aunt absolutely\par
refused to give it, though, by her severe looks, and half sentences,\par
she appeared anxious to impress Emily with a belief, that she was well\par
informed of some degrading circumstances of her conduct. Conscious\par
innocence could not prevent a blush from stealing over Emily’s cheek;\par
she trembled, and looked confusedly under the bold eye of Madame\par
Cheron, who blushed also; but hers was the blush of triumph, such as\par
sometimes stains the countenance of a person, congratulating himself on\par
the penetration which had taught him to suspect another, and who loses\par
both pity for the supposed criminal, and indignation of his guilt, in\par
the gratification of his own vanity.\par
\par
Emily, not doubting that her aunt’s mistake arose from the having\par
observed her ramble in the garden on the night preceding her departure\par
from La Vallée, now mentioned the motive of it, at which Madame Cheron\par
smiled contemptuously, refusing either to accept this explanation, or\par
to give her reasons for refusing it; and, soon after, she concluded the\par
subject by saying, “I never trust people’s assertions, I always judge\par
of them by their actions; but I am willing to try what will be your\par
behaviour in future.”\par
\par
Emily, less surprised by her aunt’s moderation and mysterious silence,\par
than by the accusation she had received, deeply considered the latter,\par
and scarcely doubted, that it was Valancourt whom she had seen at night\par
in the gardens of La Vallée, and that he had been observed there by\par
Madame Cheron; who now passing from one painful topic only to revive\par
another almost equally so, spoke of the situation of her niece’s\par
property, in the hands of M. Motteville. While she thus talked with\par
ostentatious pity of Emily’s misfortunes, she failed not to inculcate\par
the duties of humility and gratitude, or to render Emily fully sensible\par
of every cruel mortification, who soon perceived, that she was to be\par
considered as a dependant, not only by her aunt, but by her aunt’s\par
servants.\par
\par
She was now informed, that a large party were expected to dinner, on\par
which account Madame Cheron repeated the lesson of the preceding night,\par
concerning her conduct in company, and Emily wished that she might have\par
courage enough to practise it. Her aunt then proceeded to examine the\par
simplicity of her dress, adding, that she expected to see her attired\par
with gaiety and taste; after which she condescended to show Emily the\par
splendour of her château, and to point out the particular beauty, or\par
elegance, which she thought distinguished each of her numerous suites\par
of apartments. She then withdrew to her toilet, the throne of her\par
homage, and Emily to her chamber, to unpack her books, and to try to\par
charm her mind by reading, till the hour of dressing.\par
\par
When the company arrived, Emily entered the saloon with an air of\par
timidity, which all her efforts could not overcome, and which was\par
increased by the consciousness of Madame Cheron’s severe observation.\par
Her mourning dress, the mild dejection of her beautiful countenance,\par
and the retiring diffidence of her manner, rendered her a very\par
interesting object to many of the company; among whom she distinguished\par
Signor Montoni, and his friend Cavigni, the late visitors at M.\par
Quesnel’s, who now seemed to converse with Madame Cheron with the\par
familiarity of old acquaintance, and she to attend to them with\par
particular pleasure.\par
\par
This Signor Montoni had an air of conscious superiority, animated by\par
spirit, and strengthened by talents, to which every person seemed\par
involuntarily to yield. The quickness of his perceptions was strikingly\par
expressed on his countenance, yet that countenance could submit\par
implicitly to occasion; and, more than once in this day, the triumph of\par
art over nature might have been discerned in it. His visage was long,\par
and rather narrow, yet he was called handsome; and it was, perhaps, the\par
spirit and vigour of his soul, sparkling through his features, that\par
triumphed for him. Emily felt admiration, but not the admiration that\par
leads to esteem; for it was mixed with a degree of fear she knew not\par
exactly wherefore.\par
\par
Cavigni was gay and insinuating as formerly; and, though he paid almost\par
incessant attention to Madame Cheron, he found some opportunities of\par
conversing with Emily, to whom he directed, at first, the sallies of\par
his wit, but now and then assumed an air of tenderness, which she\par
observed, and shrunk from. Though she replied but little, the\par
gentleness and sweetness of her manners encouraged him to talk, and she\par
felt relieved when a young lady of the party, who spoke incessantly,\par
obtruded herself on his notice. This lady, who possessed all the\par
sprightliness of a Frenchwoman, with all her coquetry, affected to\par
understand every subject, or rather there was no affectation in the\par
case; for, never looking beyond the limits of her own ignorance, she\par
believed she had nothing to learn. She attracted notice from all;\par
amused some, disgusted others for a moment, and was then forgotten.\par
\par
This day passed without any material occurrence; and Emily, though\par
amused by the characters she had seen, was glad when she could retire\par
to the recollections, which had acquired with her the character of\par
duties.\par
\par
A fortnight passed in a round of dissipation and company, and Emily,\par
who attended Madame Cheron in all her visits, was sometimes\par
entertained, but oftener wearied. She was struck by the apparent\par
talents and knowledge displayed in the various conversations she\par
listened to, and it was long before she discovered, that the talents\par
were for the most part those of imposture, and the knowledge nothing\par
more than was necessary to assist them. But what deceived her most, was\par
the air of constant gaiety and good spirits, displayed by every\par
visitor, and which she supposed to arise from content as constant, and\par
from benevolence as ready. At length, from the over-acting of some,\par
less accomplished than the others, she could perceive, that, though\par
contentment and benevolence are the only sure sources of cheerfulness,\par
the immoderate and feverish animation, usually exhibited in large\par
parties, results partly from an insensibility to the cares, which\par
benevolence must sometimes derive from the sufferings of others, and\par
partly from a desire to display the appearance of that prosperity,\par
which they know will command submission and attention to themselves.\par
\par
Emily’s pleasantest hours were passed in the pavilion of the terrace,\par
to which she retired, when she could steal from observation, with a\par
book to overcome, or a lute to indulge, her melancholy. There, as she\par
sat with her eyes fixed on the far-distant Pyrenees, and her thoughts\par
on Valancourt and the beloved scenes of Gascony, she would play the\par
sweet and melancholy songs of her native province—the popular songs she\par
had listened to from her childhood.\par
\par
One evening, having excused herself from accompanying her aunt abroad,\par
she thus withdrew to the pavilion, with books and her lute. It was the\par
mild and beautiful evening of a sultry day, and the windows, which\par
fronted the west, opened upon all the glory of a setting sun. Its rays\par
illuminated, with strong splendour, the cliffs of the Pyrenees, and\par
touched their snowy tops with a roseate hue, that remained, long after\par
the sun had sunk below the horizon, and the shades of twilight had\par
stolen over the landscape. Emily touched her lute with that fine\par
melancholy expression, which came from her heart. The pensive hour and\par
the scene, the evening light on the Garonne, that flowed at no great\par
distance, and whose waves, as they passed towards La Vallée, she often\par
viewed with a sigh—these united circumstances disposed her mind to\par
tenderness, and her thoughts were with Valancourt, of whom she had\par
heard nothing since her arrival at Thoulouse, and now that she was\par
removed from him, and in uncertainty, she perceived all the interest he\par
held in her heart. Before she saw Valancourt she had never met a mind\par
and taste so accordant with her own, and, though Madame Cheron told her\par
much of the arts of dissimulation, and that the elegance and propriety\par
of thought, which she so much admired in her lover, were assumed for\par
the purpose of pleasing her, she could scarcely doubt their truth. This\par
possibility, however, faint as it was, was sufficient to harass her\par
mind with anxiety, and she found, that few conditions are more painful\par
than that of uncertainty, as to the merit of a beloved object; an\par
uncertainty, which she would not have suffered, had her confidence in\par
her own opinions been greater.\par
\par
She was awakened from her musing by the sound of horses’ feet along a\par
road, that wound under the windows of the pavilion, and a gentleman\par
passed on horseback, whose resemblance to Valancourt, in air and\par
figure, for the twilight did not permit a view of his features,\par
immediately struck her. She retired hastily from the lattice, fearing\par
to be seen, yet wishing to observe further, while the stranger passed\par
on without looking up, and, when she returned to the lattice, she saw\par
him faintly through the twilight, winding under the high trees, that\par
led to Thoulouse. This little incident so much disturbed her spirits,\par
that the temple and its scenery were no longer interesting to her, and,\par
after walking a while on the terrace, she returned to the château.\par
\par
Madame Cheron, whether she had seen a rival admired, had lost at play,\par
or had witnessed an entertainment more splendid than her own, was\par
returned from her visit with a temper more than usually discomposed;\par
and Emily was glad, when the hour arrived, in which she could retire to\par
the solitude of her own apartment.\par
\par
On the following morning, she was summoned to Madame Cheron, whose\par
countenance was inflamed with resentment, and, as Emily advanced, she\par
held out a letter to her.\par
\par
“Do you know this hand?” said she, in a severe tone, and with a look\par
that was intended to search her heart, while Emily examined the letter\par
attentively, and assured her, that she did not.\par
\par
“Do not provoke me,” said her aunt; “you do know it, confess the truth\par
immediately. I insist upon your confessing the truth instantly.”\par
\par
Emily was silent, and turned to leave the room, but Madame called her\par
back. “O you are guilty, then,” said she, “you do know the hand.” “If\par
you were before in doubt of this, madam,” replied Emily calmly, “why\par
did you accuse me of having told a falsehood.” Madame Cheron did not\par
blush; but her niece did, a moment after, when she heard the name of\par
Valancourt. It was not, however, with the consciousness of deserving\par
reproof, for, if she ever had seen his hand-writing, the present\par
characters did not bring it to her recollection.\par
\par
“It is useless to deny it,” said Madame Cheron, “I see in your\par
countenance, that you are no stranger to this letter; and, I dare say,\par
you have received many such from this impertinent young man, without my\par
knowledge, in my own house.”\par
\par
Emily, shocked at the indelicacy of this accusation, still more than by\par
the vulgarity of the former, instantly forgot the pride, that had\par
imposed silence, and endeavoured to vindicate herself from the\par
aspersion, but Madame Cheron was not to be convinced.\par
\par
“I cannot suppose,” she resumed, “that this young man would have taken\par
the liberty of writing to me, if you had not encouraged him to do so,\par
and I must now”\par
\par
“You will allow me to remind you, madam,” said Emily timidly, “of some\par
particulars of a conversation we had at La Vallée. I then told you\par
truly, that I had only not forbade Monsieur Valancourt from addressing\par
my family.”\par
\par
“I will not be interrupted,” said Madame Cheron, interrupting her\par
niece, “I was going to say—I—I—have forgot what I was going to say. But\par
how happened it that you did not forbid him?” Emily was silent. “How\par
happened it that you encouraged him to trouble me with this letter?—A\par
young man that nobody knows;—an utter stranger in the place,—a young\par
adventurer, no doubt, who is looking out for a good fortune. However,\par
on that point he has mistaken his aim.”\par
\par
“His family was known to my father,” said Emily modestly, and without\par
appearing to be sensible of the last sentence.\par
\par
“O! that is no recommendation at all,” replied her aunt, with her usual\par
readiness upon this topic; “he took such strange fancies to people! He\par
was always judging persons by their countenances, and was continually\par
deceived.”\par
\par
“Yet it was but now, madam, that you judged me guilty by my\par
countenance,” said Emily, with a design of reproving Madame Cheron, to\par
which she was induced by this disrespectful mention of her father.\par
\par
“I called you here,” resumed her aunt, colouring, “to tell you, that I\par
will not be disturbed in my own house by any letters, or visits from\par
young men, who may take a fancy to flatter you. This M. de Valantine—I\par
think you call him, has the impertinence to beg I will permit him to\par
pay his respects to me! I shall send him a proper answer. And for you,\par
Emily, I repeat it once for all—if you are not contented to conform to\par
my directions, and to my way of live, I shall give up the task of\par
overlooking your conduct—I shall no longer trouble myself with your\par
education, but shall send you to board in a convent.”\par
\par
“Dear madam,” said Emily, bursting into tears, and overcome by the rude\par
suspicions her aunt had expressed, “how have I deserved these\par
reproofs?” She could say no more; and so very fearful was she of acting\par
with any degree of impropriety in the affair itself, that, at the\par
present moment, Madame Cheron might perhaps have prevailed with her to\par
bind herself by a promise to renounce Valancourt for ever. Her mind,\par
weakened by her terrors, would no longer suffer her to view him as she\par
had formerly done; she feared the error of her own judgment, not that\par
of Madame Cheron, and feared also, that, in her former conversation\par
with him, at La Vallée, she had not conducted herself with sufficient\par
reserve. She knew, that she did not deserve the coarse suspicions,\par
which her aunt had thrown out, but a thousand scruples rose to torment\par
her, such as would never have disturbed the peace of Madame Cheron.\par
Thus rendered anxious to avoid every opportunity of erring, and willing\par
to submit to any restrictions, that her aunt should think proper, she\par
expressed an obedience, to which Madame Cheron did not give much\par
confidence, and which she seemed to consider as the consequence of\par
either fear, or artifice.\par
\par
“Well, then,” said she, “promise me that you will neither see this\par
young man, nor write to him without my consent.” “Dear madam,” replied\par
Emily, “can you suppose I would do either, unknown to you!” “I don’t\par
know what to suppose; there is no knowing how young women will act. It\par
is difficult to place any confidence in them, for they have seldom\par
sense enough to wish for the respect of the world.”\par
\par
“Alas, madam!” said Emily, “I am anxious for my own respect; my father\par
taught me the value of that; he said if I deserved my own esteem, that\par
the world would follow of course.”\par
\par
“My brother was a good kind of a man,” replied Madame Cheron, “but he\par
did not know the world. I am sure I have always felt a proper respect\par
for myself, yet—” she stopped, but she might have added, that the world\par
had not always shown respect to her, and this without impeaching its\par
judgment.\par
\par
“Well!” resumed Madame Cheron, “you have not given me the promise,\par
though, that I demand.”\par
\par
Emily readily gave it, and, being then suffered to withdraw, she walked\par
in the garden; tried to compose her spirits, and, at length, arrived at\par
her favourite pavilion at the end of the terrace, where, seating\par
herself at one of the embowered windows, that opened upon a balcony,\par
the stillness and seclusion of the scene allowed her to recollect her\par
thoughts, and to arrange them so as to form a clearer judgment of her\par
former conduct. She endeavoured to review with exactness all the\par
particulars of her conversation with Valancourt at La Vallée, had the\par
satisfaction to observe nothing, that could alarm her delicate pride,\par
and thus to be confirmed in the self-esteem, which was so necessary to\par
her peace. Her mind then became tranquil, and she saw Valancourt\par
amiable and intelligent, as he had formerly appeared, and Madame Cheron\par
neither the one, nor the other. The remembrance of her lover, however,\par
brought with it many very painful emotions, for it by no means\par
reconciled her to the thought of resigning him; and, Madame Cheron\par
having already shown how highly she disapproved of the attachment, she\par
foresaw much suffering from the opposition of interests; yet with all\par
this was mingled a degree of delight, which, in spite of reason,\par
partook of hope. She determined, however, that no consideration should\par
induce her to permit a clandestine correspondence, and to observe in\par
her conversation with Valancourt, should they ever meet again, the same\par
nicety of reserve, which had hitherto marked her conduct. As she\par
repeated the words—“should we ever meet again!” she shrunk as if this\par
was a circumstance, which had never before occurred to her, and tears\par
came to her eyes, which she hastily dried, for she heard footsteps\par
approaching, and then the door of the pavilion open, and, on turning,\par
she saw—Valancourt.\par
\par
An emotion of mingled pleasure, surprise and apprehension pressed so\par
suddenly upon her heart as almost to overcome her spirits; the colour\par
left her cheeks, then returned brighter than before, and she was for a\par
moment unable to speak, or to rise from her chair. His countenance was\par
the mirror, in which she saw her own emotions reflected, and it roused\par
her to self-command. The joy, which had animated his features, when he\par
entered the pavilion, was suddenly repressed, as, approaching, he\par
perceived her agitation, and, in a tremulous voice, enquired after her\par
health. Recovered from her first surprise, she answered him with a\par
tempered smile; but a variety of opposite emotions still assailed her\par
heart, and struggled to subdue the mild dignity of her manner. It was\par
difficult to tell which predominated—the joy of seeing Valancourt, or\par
the terror of her aunt’s displeasure, when she should hear of this\par
meeting. After some short and embarrassed conversation, she led him\par
into the gardens, and enquired if he had seen Madame Cheron. “No,” said\par
he, “I have not yet seen her, for they told me she was engaged, and as\par
soon as I learned that you were in the gardens, I came hither.” He\par
paused a moment, in great agitation, and then added, “May I venture to\par
tell you the purport of my visit, without incurring your displeasure,\par
and to hope, that you will not accuse me of precipitation in now\par
availing myself of the permission you once gave me of addressing your\par
family?” Emily, who knew not what to reply, was spared from further\par
perplexity, and was sensible only of fear, when on raising her eyes,\par
she saw Madame Cheron turn into the avenue. As the consciousness of\par
innocence returned, this fear was so far dissipated as to permit her to\par
appear tranquil, and, instead of avoiding her aunt, she advanced with\par
Valancourt to meet her. The look of haughty and impatient displeasure,\par
with which Madame Cheron regarded them, made Emily shrink, who\par
understood from a single glance, that this meeting was believed to have\par
been more than accidental: having mentioned Valancourt’s name, she\par
became again too much agitated to remain with them, and returned into\par
the château; where she awaited long, in a state of trembling anxiety,\par
the conclusion of the conference. She knew not how to account for\par
Valancourt’s visit to her aunt, before he had received the permission\par
he solicited, since she was ignorant of a circumstance, which would\par
have rendered the request useless, even if Madame Cheron had been\par
inclined to grant it. Valancourt, in the agitation of his spirits, had\par
forgotten to date his letter, so that it was impossible for Madame\par
Cheron to return an answer; and, when he recollected this circumstance,\par
he was, perhaps, not so sorry for the omission as glad of the excuse it\par
allowed him for waiting on her before she could send a refusal.\par
\par
Madame Cheron had a long conversation with Valancourt, and, when she\par
returned to the château, her countenance expressed ill-humour, but not\par
the degree of severity, which Emily had apprehended. “I have dismissed\par
this young man, at last,” said she, “and I hope my house will never\par
again be disturbed with similar visits. He assures me, that your\par
interview was not preconcerted.”\par
\par
“Dear madam!” said Emily in extreme emotion, “you surely did not ask\par
him the question!” “Most certainly I did; you could not suppose I\par
should be so imprudent as to neglect it.”\par
\par
“Good God!” exclaimed Emily, “what an opinion must he form of me, since\par
you, Madam, could express a suspicion of such ill conduct!”\par
\par
“It is of very little consequence what opinion he may form of you,”\par
replied her aunt, “for I have put an end to the affair; but I believe\par
he will not form a worse opinion of me for my prudent conduct. I let\par
him see, that I was not to be trifled with, and that I had more\par
delicacy, than to permit any clandestine correspondence to be carried\par
on in my house.”\par
\par
Emily had frequently heard Madame Cheron use the word delicacy, but she\par
was now more than usually perplexed to understand how she meant to\par
apply it in this instance, in which her whole conduct appeared to merit\par
the very reverse of the term.\par
\par
“It was very inconsiderate of my brother,” resumed Madame Cheron, “to\par
leave the trouble of overlooking your conduct to me; I wish you were\par
well settled in life. But if I find, that I am to be further troubled\par
with such visitors as this M. Valancourt, I shall place you in a\par
convent at once;—so remember the alternative. This young man has the\par
impertinence to own to me,—he owns it! that his fortune is very small,\par
and that he is chiefly dependent on an elder brother and on the\par
profession he has chosen! He should have concealed these circumstances,\par
at least, if he expected to succeed with me. Had he the presumption to\par
suppose I would marry my niece to a person such as he describes\par
himself!”\par
\par
Emily dried her tears when she heard of the candid confession of\par
Valancourt; and, though the circumstances it discovered were afflicting\par
to her hopes, his artless conduct gave her a degree of pleasure, that\par
overcame every other emotion. But she was compelled, even thus early in\par
life, to observe, that good sense and noble integrity are not always\par
sufficient to cope with folly and narrow cunning; and her heart was\par
pure enough to allow her, even at this trying moment, to look with more\par
pride on the defeat of the former, than with mortification on the\par
conquests of the latter.\par
\par
Madame Cheron pursued her triumph. “He has also thought proper to tell\par
me, that he will receive his dismission from no person but yourself;\par
this favour, however, I have absolutely refused him. He shall learn,\par
that it is quite sufficient, that I disapprove him. And I take this\par
opportunity of repeating,—that if you concert any means of interview\par
unknown to me, you shall leave my house immediately.”\par
\par
“How little do you know me, madam, that you should think such an\par
injunction necessary!” said Emily, trying to suppress her emotion, “how\par
little of the dear parents, who educated me!”\par
\par
Madame Cheron now went to dress for an engagement, which she had made\par
for the evening; and Emily, who would gladly have been excused from\par
attending her aunt, did not ask to remain at home lest her request\par
should be attributed to an improper motive. When she retired to her own\par
room, the little fortitude, which had supported her in the presence of\par
her relation, forsook her; she remembered only that Valancourt, whose\par
character appeared more amiable from every circumstance, that unfolded\par
it, was banished from her presence, perhaps, for ever, and she passed\par
the time in weeping, which, according to her aunt’s direction, she\par
ought to have employed in dressing. This important duty was, however,\par
quickly dispatched; though, when she joined Madame Cheron at table, her\par
eyes betrayed, that she had been in tears, and drew upon her a severe\par
reproof.\par
\par
Her efforts to appear cheerful did not entirely fail when she joined\par
the company at the house of Madame Clairval, an elderly widow lady, who\par
had lately come to reside at Thoulouse, on an estate of her late\par
husband. She had lived many years at Paris in a splendid style; had\par
naturally a gay temper, and, since her residence at Thoulouse, had\par
given some of the most magnificent entertainments, that had been seen\par
in that neighbourhood.\par
\par
These excited not only the envy, but the trifling ambition of Madame\par
Cheron, who, since she could not rival the splendour of her\par
festivities, was desirous of being ranked in the number of her most\par
intimate friends. For this purpose she paid her the most obsequious\par
attention, and made a point of being disengaged, whenever she received\par
an invitation from Madame Clairval, of whom she talked, wherever she\par
went, and derived much self-consequence from impressing a belief on her\par
general acquaintance, that they were on the most familiar footing.\par
\par
The entertainments of this evening consisted of a ball and supper; it\par
was a fancy ball, and the company danced in groups in the gardens,\par
which were very extensive. The high and luxuriant trees, under which\par
the groups assembled, were illuminated with a profusion of lamps,\par
disposed with taste and fancy. The gay and various dresses of the\par
company, some of whom were seated on the turf, conversing at their\par
ease, observing the _cotillons_, taking refreshments, and sometimes\par
touching sportively a guitar; the gallant manners of the gentlemen, the\par
exquisitely capricious air of the ladies; the light fantastic steps of\par
their dances; the musicians, with the lute, the hautboy, and the tabor,\par
seated at the foot of an elm, and the sylvan scenery of woods around\par
were circumstances, that unitedly formed a characteristic and striking\par
picture of French festivity. Emily surveyed the gaiety of the scene\par
with a melancholy kind of pleasure, and her emotion may be imagined\par
when, as she stood with her aunt, looking at one of the groups, she\par
perceived Valancourt; saw him dancing with a young and beautiful lady,\par
saw him conversing with her with a mixture of attention and\par
familiarity, such as she had seldom observed in his manner. She turned\par
hastily from the scene, and attempted to draw away Madame Cheron, who\par
was conversing with Signor Cavigni, and neither perceived Valancourt,\par
nor was willing to be interrupted. A faintness suddenly came over\par
Emily, and, unable to support herself, she sat down on a turf bank\par
beneath the trees, where several other persons were seated. One of\par
these, observing the extreme paleness of her countenance, enquired if\par
she was ill, and begged she would allow him to fetch her a glass of\par
water, for which politeness she thanked him, but did not accept it. Her\par
apprehension lest Valancourt should observe her emotion made her\par
anxious to overcome it, and she succeeded so far as to recompose her\par
countenance. Madame Cheron was still conversing with Cavigni; and the\par
Count Bauvillers, who had addressed Emily, made some observations upon\par
the scene, to which she answered almost unconsciously, for her mind was\par
still occupied with the idea of Valancourt, to whom it was with extreme\par
uneasiness that she remained so near. Some remarks, however, which the\par
Count made upon the dance obliged her to turn her eyes towards it, and,\par
at that moment, Valancourt’s met hers. Her colour faded again, she\par
felt, that she was relapsing into faintness, and instantly averted her\par
looks, but not before she had observed the altered countenance of\par
Valancourt, on perceiving her. She would have left the spot\par
immediately, had she not been conscious, that this conduct would have\par
shown him more obviously the interest he held in her heart; and, having\par
tried to attend to the Count’s conversation, and to join in it, she, at\par
length, recovered her spirits. But, when he made some observation on\par
Valancourt’s partner, the fear of showing that she was interested in\par
the remark, would have betrayed it to him, had not the Count, while he\par
spoke, looked towards the person of whom he was speaking. “The lady,”\par
said he, “dancing with that young Chevalier, who appears to be\par
accomplished in everything, but in dancing, is ranked among the\par
beauties of Thoulouse. She is handsome, and her fortune will be very\par
large. I hope she will make a better choice in a partner for life than\par
she has done in a partner for the dance, for I observe he has just put\par
the set into great confusion; he does nothing but commit blunders. I am\par
surprised, that, with his air and figure, he has not taken more care to\par
accomplish himself in dancing.”\par
\par
Emily, whose heart trembled at every word, that was now uttered,\par
endeavoured to turn the conversation from Valancourt, by enquiring the\par
name of the lady, with whom he danced; but, before the Count could\par
reply, the dance concluded, and Emily, perceiving that Valancourt was\par
coming towards her, rose and joined Madame Cheron.\par
\par
“Here is the Chevalier Valancourt, madam,” said she in a whisper, “pray\par
let us go.” Her aunt immediately moved on, but not before Valancourt\par
had reached them, who bowed lowly to Madame Cheron, and with an earnest\par
and dejected look to Emily, with whom, notwithstanding all her effort,\par
an air of more than common reserve prevailed. The presence of Madame\par
Cheron prevented Valancourt from remaining, and he passed on with a\par
countenance, whose melancholy reproached her for having increased it.\par
Emily was called from the musing fit, into which she had fallen, by the\par
Count Bauvillers, who was known to her aunt.\par
\par
“I have your pardon to beg, ma’amselle,” said he, “for a rudeness,\par
which you will readily believe was quite unintentional. I did not know,\par
that the Chevalier was your acquaintance, when I so freely criticised\par
his dancing.” Emily blushed and smiled, and Madame Cheron spared her\par
the difficulty of replying. “If you mean the person, who has just\par
passed us,” said she, “I can assure you he is no acquaintance of either\par
mine, or ma’amselle St. Aubert’s: I know nothing of him.”\par
\par
“O! that is the Chevalier Valancourt,” said Cavigni carelessly, and\par
looking back. “You know him then?” said Madame Cheron. “I am not\par
acquainted with him,” replied Cavigni. “You don’t know, then, the\par
reason I have to call him impertinent;—he has had the presumption to\par
admire my niece!”\par
\par
“If every man deserves the title of impertinent, who admires ma’amselle\par
St. Aubert,” replied Cavigni, “I fear there are a great many\par
impertinents, and I am willing to acknowledge myself one of the\par
number.”\par
\par
“O Signor!” said Madame Cheron, with an affected smile, “I perceive you\par
have learnt the art of complimenting, since you came into France. But\par
it is cruel to compliment children, since they mistake flattery for\par
truth.”\par
\par
Cavigni turned away his face for a moment, and then said with a studied\par
air, “Whom then are we to compliment, madam? for it would be absurd to\par
compliment a woman of refined understanding; _she_ is above all\par
praise.” As he finished the sentence he gave Emily a sly look, and the\par
smile, that had lurked in his eye, stole forth. She perfectly\par
understood it, and blushed for Madame Cheron, who replied, “You are\par
perfectly right, signor, no woman of understanding can endure\par
compliment.”\par
\par
“I have heard Signor Montoni say,” rejoined Cavigni, “that he never\par
knew but one woman who deserved it.”\par
\par
“Well!” exclaimed Madame Cheron, with a short laugh, and a smile of\par
unutterable complacency, “and who could she be?”\par
\par
“O!” replied Cavigni, “it is impossible to mistake her, for certainly\par
there is not more than one woman in the world, who has both the merit\par
to deserve compliment and the wit to refuse it. Most women reverse the\par
case entirely.” He looked again at Emily, who blushed deeper than\par
before for her aunt, and turned from him with displeasure.\par
\par
“Well, signor!” said Madame Cheron, “I protest you are a Frenchman; I\par
never heard a foreigner say anything half so gallant as that!”\par
\par
“True, madam,” said the Count, who had been some time silent, and with\par
a low bow, “but the gallantry of the compliment had been utterly lost,\par
but for the ingenuity that discovered the application.”\par
\par
Madame Cheron did not perceive the meaning of this too satirical\par
sentence, and she, therefore, escaped the pain, which Emily felt on her\par
account. “O! here comes Signor Montoni himself,” said her aunt, “I\par
protest I will tell him all the fine things you have been saying to\par
me.” The Signor, however, passed at this moment into another walk.\par
“Pray, who is it, that has so much engaged your friend this evening?”\par
asked Madame Cheron, with an air of chagrin, “I have not seen him\par
once.”\par
\par
“He had a very particular engagement with the Marquis La Rivière,”\par
replied Cavigni, “which has detained him, I perceive, till this moment,\par
or he would have done himself the honour of paying his respects to you,\par
madam, sooner, as he commissioned me to say. But, I know not how it\par
is—your conversation is so fascinating—that it can charm even memory, I\par
think, or I should certainly have delivered my friend’s apology\par
before.”\par
\par
“The apology, sir, would have been more satisfactory from himself,”\par
said Madame Cheron, whose vanity was more mortified by Montoni’s\par
neglect, than flattered by Cavigni’s compliment. Her manner, at this\par
moment, and Cavigni’s late conversation, now awakened a suspicion in\par
Emily’s mind, which, notwithstanding that some recollections served to\par
confirm it, appeared preposterous. She thought she perceived, that\par
Montoni was paying serious addresses to her aunt, and that she not only\par
accepted them, but was jealously watchful of any appearance of neglect\par
on his part.—That Madame Cheron at her years should elect a second\par
husband was ridiculous, though her vanity made it not impossible; but\par
that Montoni, with his discernment, his figure, and pretensions, should\par
make a choice of Madame Cheron—appeared most wonderful. Her thoughts,\par
however, did not dwell long on the subject; nearer interests pressed\par
upon them; Valancourt, rejected of her aunt, and Valancourt dancing\par
with a gay and beautiful partner, alternately tormented her mind. As\par
she passed along the gardens she looked timidly forward, half fearing\par
and half hoping that he might appear in the crowd; and the\par
disappointment she felt on not seeing him, told her, that she had hoped\par
more than she had feared.\par
\par
Montoni soon after joined the party. He muttered over some short speech\par
about regret for having been so long detained elsewhere, when he knew\par
he should have the pleasure of seeing Madame Cheron here; and she,\par
receiving the apology with the air of a pettish girl, addressed herself\par
entirely to Cavigni, who looked archly at Montoni, as if he would have\par
said, “I will not triumph over you too much; I will have the goodness\par
to bear my honours meekly; but look sharp, Signor, or I shall certainly\par
run away with your prize.”\par
\par
The supper was served in different pavilions in the gardens, as well as\par
in one large saloon of the château, and with more of taste, than either\par
of splendour, or even of plenty. Madame Cheron and her party supped\par
with Madame Clairval in the saloon, and Emily, with difficulty,\par
disguised her emotion, when she saw Valancourt placed at the same table\par
with herself. There, Madame Cheron having surveyed him with high\par
displeasure, said to some person who sat next to her, “Pray, who _is_\par
that young man?” “It is the Chevalier Valancourt,” was the answer.\par
“Yes, I am not ignorant of his name, but who is this Chevalier\par
Valancourt that thus intrudes himself at this table?” The attention of\par
the person, to whom she spoke, was called off before she received a\par
second reply. The table, at which they sat, was very long, and,\par
Valancourt being seated, with his partner, near the bottom, and Emily\par
near the top, the distance between them may account for his not\par
immediately perceiving her. She avoided looking to that end of the\par
table, but whenever her eyes happened to glance towards it, she\par
observed him conversing with his beautiful companion, and the\par
observation did not contribute to restore her peace, any more than the\par
accounts she heard of the fortune and accomplishments of this same\par
lady.\par
\par
Madame Cheron, to whom these remarks were sometimes addressed, because\par
they supported topics for trivial conversation, seemed indefatigable in\par
her attempts to depreciate Valancourt, towards whom she felt all the\par
petty resentment of a narrow pride. “I admire the lady,” said she, “but\par
I must condemn her choice of a partner.” “Oh, the Chevalier Valancourt\par
is one of the most accomplished young men we have,” replied the lady,\par
to whom this remark was addressed: “it is whispered, that Mademoiselle\par
d’Emery, and her large fortune, are to be his.”\par
\par
“Impossible!” exclaimed Madame Cheron, reddening with vexation, “it is\par
impossible that she can be so destitute of taste; he has so little the\par
air of a person of condition, that, if I did not see him at the table\par
of Madame Clairval, I should never have suspected him to be one. I have\par
besides particular reasons for believing the report to be erroneous.”\par
\par
“I cannot doubt the truth of it,” replied the lady gravely, disgusted\par
by the abrupt contradiction she had received, concerning her opinion of\par
Valancourt’s merit. “You will, perhaps, doubt it,” said Madame Cheron,\par
“when I assure you, that it was only this morning that I rejected his\par
suit.”\par
\par
This was said without any intention of imposing the meaning it\par
conveyed, but simply from a habit of considering herself to be the most\par
important person in every affair that concerned her niece, and because\par
literally _she_ had rejected Valancourt. “Your reasons are indeed such\par
as cannot be doubted,” replied the lady, with an ironical smile. “Any\par
more than the discernment of the Chevalier Valancourt,” added Cavigni,\par
who stood by the chair of Madame Cheron, and had heard her arrogate to\par
herself, as he thought, a distinction which had been paid to her niece.\par
“His discernment _may_ be justly questioned, Signor,” said Madame\par
Cheron, who was not flattered by what she understood to be an encomium\par
on Emily.\par
\par
“Alas!” exclaimed Cavigni, surveying Madame Cheron with affected\par
ecstasy, “how vain is that assertion, while that face—that shape—that\par
air—combine to refute it! Unhappy Valancourt! his discernment has been\par
his destruction.”\par
\par
Emily looked surprised and embarrassed; the lady, who had lately\par
spoken, astonished, and Madame Cheron, who, though she did not\par
perfectly understand this speech, was very ready to believe herself\par
complimented by it, said smilingly, “O Signor! you are very gallant;\par
but those, who hear you vindicate the Chevalier’s discernment, will\par
suppose that I am the object of it.”\par
\par
“They cannot doubt it,” replied Cavigni, bowing low.\par
\par
“And would not that be very mortifying, Signor?”\par
\par
“Unquestionably it would,” said Cavigni.\par
\par
“I cannot endure the thought,” said Madame Cheron.\par
\par
“It is not to be endured,” replied Cavigni.\par
\par
“What can be done to prevent so humiliating a mistake?” rejoined Madame\par
Cheron.\par
\par
“Alas! I cannot assist you,” replied Cavigni, with a deliberating air.\par
“Your only chance of refuting the calumny, and of making people\par
understand what you wish them to believe, is to persist in your first\par
assertion; for, when they are told of the Chevalier’s want of\par
discernment, it is possible they may suppose he never presumed to\par
distress you with his admiration.—But then again—that diffidence, which\par
renders you so insensible to your own perfections—they will consider\par
this, and Valancourt’s taste will not be doubted, though you arraign\par
it. In short, they will, in spite of your endeavours, continue to\par
believe, what might very naturally have occurred to them without any\par
hint of mine—that the Chevalier has taste enough to admire a beautiful\par
woman.”\par
\par
“All this is very distressing!” said Madame Cheron, with a profound\par
sigh.\par
\par
“May I be allowed to ask what is so distressing?” said Madame Clairval,\par
who was struck with the rueful countenance and doleful accent, with\par
which this was delivered.\par
\par
“It is a delicate subject,” replied Madame Cheron, “a very mortifying\par
one to me.” “I am concerned to hear it,” said Madame Clairval, “I hope\par
nothing has occurred, this evening, particularly to distress you?”\par
“Alas, yes! within this half hour; and I know not where the report may\par
end;—my pride was never so shocked before, but I assure you the report\par
is totally void of foundation.” “Good God!” exclaimed Madame Clairval,\par
“what can be done? Can you point out any way, by which I can assist, or\par
console you?”\par
\par
“The only way, by which you can do either,” replied Madame Cheron, “is\par
to contradict the report wherever you go.”\par
\par
“Well! but pray inform me what I am to contradict.”\par
\par
“It is so very humiliating, that I know not how to mention it,”\par
continued Madame Cheron, “but you shall judge. Do you observe that\par
young man seated near the bottom of the table, who is conversing with\par
Mademoiselle d’Emery?” “Yes, I perceive whom you mean.” “You observe\par
how little he has the air of a person of condition; I was saying just\par
now, that I should not have thought him a gentleman, if I had not seen\par
him at this table.” “Well! but the report,” said Madame Clairval, “let\par
me understand the subject of your distress.” “Ah! the subject of my\par
distress,” replied Madame Cheron; “this person, whom nobody knows—(I\par
beg pardon, madam, I did not consider what I said)—this impertinent\par
young man, having had the presumption to address my niece, has, I fear,\par
given rise to a report, that he had declared himself my admirer. Now\par
only consider how very mortifying such a report must be! You, I know,\par
will feel for my situation. A woman of my condition!—think how\par
degrading even the rumour of such an alliance must be.”\par
\par
“Degrading indeed, my poor friend!” said Madame Clairval. “You may rely\par
upon it I will contradict the report wherever I go;” as she said which,\par
she turned her attention upon another part of the company; and Cavigni,\par
who had hitherto appeared a grave spectator of the scene, now fearing\par
he should be unable to smother the laugh, that convulsed him, walked\par
abruptly away.\par
\par
“I perceive you do not know,” said the lady who sat near Madame Cheron,\par
“that the gentleman you have been speaking of is Madame Clairval’s\par
nephew!” “Impossible!” exclaimed Madame Cheron, who now began to\par
perceive, that she had been totally mistaken in her judgment of\par
Valancourt, and to praise him aloud with as much servility, as she had\par
before censured him with frivolous malignity.\par
\par
Emily, who, during the greater part of this conversation, had been so\par
absorbed in thought as to be spared the pain of hearing it, was now\par
extremely surprised by her aunt’s praise of Valancourt, with whose\par
relationship to Madame Clairval she was unacquainted; but she was not\par
sorry when Madame Cheron, who, though she now tried to appear\par
unconcerned, was really much embarrassed, prepared to withdraw\par
immediately after supper. Montoni then came to hand Madame Cheron to\par
her carriage, and Cavigni, with an arch solemnity of countenance,\par
followed with Emily, who, as she wished them good night, and drew up\par
the glass, saw Valancourt among the crowd at the gates. Before the\par
carriage drove off, he disappeared. Madame Cheron forbore to mention\par
him to Emily, and, as soon as they reached the château, they separated\par
for the night.\par
\par
On the following morning, as Emily sat at breakfast with her aunt, a\par
letter was brought to her, of which she knew the handwriting upon the\par
cover; and, as she received it with a trembling hand, Madame Cheron\par
hastily enquired from whom it came. Emily, with her leave, broke the\par
seal, and, observing the signature of Valancourt, gave it unread to her\par
aunt, who received it with impatience; and, as she looked it over,\par
Emily endeavoured to read on her countenance its contents. Having\par
returned the letter to her niece, whose eyes asked if she might examine\par
it, “Yes, read it, child,” said Madame Cheron, in a manner less severe\par
than she had expected, and Emily had, perhaps, never before so\par
willingly obeyed her aunt. In this letter Valancourt said little of the\par
interview of the preceding day, but concluded with declaring, that he\par
would accept his dismission from Emily only, and with entreating, that\par
she would allow him to wait upon her, on the approaching evening. When\par
she read this, she was astonished at the moderation of Madame Cheron,\par
and looked at her with timid expectation, as she said sorrowfully—“What\par
am I to say, madam?”\par
\par
“Why—we must see the young man, I believe,” replied her aunt, “and hear\par
what he has further to say for himself. You may tell him he may come.”\par
Emily dared scarcely credit what she heard. “Yet, stay,” added Madame\par
Cheron, “I will tell him so myself.” She called for pen and ink; Emily\par
still not daring to trust the emotions she felt, and almost sinking\par
beneath them. Her surprise would have been less had she overheard, on\par
the preceding evening, what Madame Cheron had not forgotten—that\par
Valancourt was the nephew of Madame Clairval.\par
\par
What were the particulars of her aunt’s note Emily did not learn, but\par
the result was a visit from Valancourt in the evening, whom Madame\par
Cheron received alone, and they had a long conversation before Emily\par
was called down. When she entered the room, her aunt was conversing\par
with complacency, and she saw the eyes of Valancourt, as he impatiently\par
rose, animated with hope.\par
\par
“We have been talking over this affair,” said Madame Cheron, “the\par
chevalier has been telling me, that the late Monsieur Clairval was the\par
brother of the Countess de Duvarney, his mother. I only wish he had\par
mentioned his relationship to Madame Clairval before; I certainly\par
should have considered that circumstance as a sufficient introduction\par
to my house.” Valancourt bowed, and was going to address Emily, but her\par
aunt prevented him. “I have, therefore, consented that you shall\par
receive his visits; and, though I will not bind myself by any promise,\par
or say, that I shall consider him as my nephew, yet I shall permit the\par
intercourse, and shall look forward to any further connection as an\par
event, which may possibly take place in a course of years, provided the\par
chevalier rises in his profession, or any circumstance occurs, which\par
may make it prudent for him to take a wife. But Mons. Valancourt will\par
observe, and you too, Emily, that, till that happens, I positively\par
forbid any thoughts of marrying.”\par
\par
Emily’s countenance, during this coarse speech, varied every instant,\par
and, towards its conclusion, her distress had so much increased, that\par
she was on the point of leaving the room. Valancourt, meanwhile,\par
scarcely less embarrassed, did not dare to look at her, for whom he was\par
thus distressed; but, when Madame Cheron was silent, he said,\par
“Flattering, madam, as your approbation is to me—highly as I am\par
honoured by it—I have yet so much to fear, that I scarcely dare to\par
hope.” “Pray, sir, explain yourself,” said Madame Cheron; an unexpected\par
requisition, which embarrassed Valancourt again, and almost overcame\par
him with confusion, at circumstances, on which, had he been only a\par
spectator of the scene, he would have smiled.\par
\par
“Till I receive Mademoiselle St. Aubert’s permission to accept your\par
indulgence,” said he, falteringly—“till she allows me to hope—”\par
\par
“O! is that all?” interrupted Madame Cheron. “Well, I will take upon me\par
to answer for her. But at the same time, sir, give me leave to observe\par
to you, that I am her guardian, and that I expect, in every instance,\par
that my will is hers.”\par
\par
As she said this, she rose and quitted the room, leaving Emily and\par
Valancourt in a state of mutual embarrassment; and, when Valancourt’s\par
hopes enabled him to overcome his fears, and to address her with the\par
zeal and sincerity so natural to him, it was a considerable time before\par
she was sufficiently recovered to hear with distinctness his\par
solicitations and inquiries.\par
\par
The conduct of Madame Cheron in this affair had been entirely governed\par
by selfish vanity. Valancourt, in his first interview, had with great\par
candour laid open to her the true state of his present circumstances,\par
and his future expectancies, and she, with more prudence than humanity,\par
had absolutely and abruptly rejected his suit. She wished her niece to\par
marry ambitiously, not because she desired to see her in possession of\par
the happiness, which rank and wealth are usually believed to bestow,\par
but because she desired to partake the importance, which such an\par
alliance would give. When, therefore, she discovered that Valancourt\par
was the nephew of a person of so much consequence as Madame Clairval,\par
she became anxious for the connection, since the prospect it afforded\par
of future fortune and distinction for Emily, promised the exaltation\par
she coveted for herself. Her calculations concerning fortune in this\par
alliance were guided rather by her wishes, than by any hint of\par
Valancourt, or strong appearance of probability; and, when she rested\par
her expectation on the wealth of Madame Clairval, she seemed totally to\par
have forgotten, that the latter had a daughter. Valancourt, however,\par
had not forgotten this circumstance, and the consideration of it had\par
made him so modest in his expectations from Madame Clairval, that he\par
had not even named the relationship in his first conversation with\par
Madame Cheron. But, whatever might be the future fortune of Emily, the\par
present distinction, which the connection would afford for herself, was\par
certain, since the splendour of Madame Clairval’s establishment was\par
such as to excite the general envy and partial imitation of the\par
neighbourhood. Thus had she consented to involve her niece in an\par
engagement, to which she saw only a distant and uncertain conclusion,\par
with as little consideration of her happiness, as when she had so\par
precipitately forbade it: for though she herself possessed the means of\par
rendering this union not only certain, but prudent, yet to do so was no\par
part of her present intention.\par
\par
From this period Valancourt made frequent visits to Madame Cheron, and\par
Emily passed in his society the happiest hours she had known since the\par
death of her father. They were both too much engaged by the present\par
moments to give serious consideration to the future. They loved and\par
were beloved, and saw not, that the very attachment, which formed the\par
delight of their present days, might possibly occasion the sufferings\par
of years. Meanwhile, Madame Cheron’s intercourse with Madame Clairval\par
became more frequent than before, and her vanity was already gratified\par
by the opportunity of proclaiming, wherever she went, the attachment\par
that subsisted between their nephew and niece.\par
\par
Montoni was now also become a daily guest at the château, and Emily was\par
compelled to observe, that he really was a suitor, and a favoured\par
suitor, to her aunt.\par
\par
Thus passed the winter months, not only in peace, but in happiness, to\par
Valancourt and Emily; the station of his regiment being so near\par
Thoulouse, as to allow this frequent intercourse. The pavilion on the\par
terrace was the favourite scene of their interviews, and there Emily,\par
with Madame Cheron, would work, while Valancourt read aloud works of\par
genius and taste, listened to her enthusiasm, expressed his own, and\par
caught new opportunities of observing, that their minds were formed to\par
constitute the happiness of each other, the same taste, the same noble\par
and benevolent sentiments animating each.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XIII\par
\par
As when a shepherd of the Hebrid-Isles,\par
Placed far amid the melancholy main,\par
(Whether it be lone fancy him beguiles,\par
Or that aerial beings sometimes deign\par
To stand embodied to our senses plain)\par
Sees on the naked hill, or valley low,\par
The whilst in ocean Phœbus dips his wain,\par
A vast assembly moving to and fro,\par
Then all at once in air dissolves the wondrous show.\par
                    CASTLE OF INDOLENCE\par
\par
\par
Madame Cheron’s avarice at length yielded to her vanity. Some very\par
splendid entertainments, which Madame Clairval had given, and the\par
general adulation, which was paid her, made the former more anxious\par
than before to secure an alliance, that would so much exalt her in her\par
own opinion and in that of the world. She proposed terms for the\par
immediate marriage of her niece, and offered to give Emily a dower,\par
provided Madame Clairval observed equal terms, on the part of her\par
nephew. Madame Clairval listened to the proposal, and, considering that\par
Emily was the apparent heiress of her aunt’s wealth, accepted it.\par
Meanwhile, Emily knew nothing of the transaction, till Madame Cheron\par
informed her, that she must make preparation for the nuptials, which\par
would be celebrated without further delay; then, astonished and wholly\par
unable to account for this sudden conclusion, which Valancourt had not\par
solicited (for he was ignorant of what had passed between the elder\par
ladies, and had not dared to hope such good fortune), she decisively\par
objected to it. Madame Cheron, however, quite as jealous of\par
contradiction now, as she had been formerly, contended for a speedy\par
marriage with as much vehemence as she had formerly opposed whatever\par
had the most remote possibility of leading to it; and Emily’s scruples\par
disappeared, when she again saw Valancourt, who was now informed of the\par
happiness, designed for him, and came to claim a promise of it from\par
herself.\par
\par
While preparations were making for these nuptials, Montoni became the\par
acknowledged lover of Madame Cheron; and, though Madame Clairval was\par
much displeased, when she heard of the approaching connection, and was\par
willing to prevent that of Valancourt with Emily, her conscience told\par
her, that she had no right thus to trifle with their peace, and Madame\par
Clairval, though a woman of fashion, was far less advanced than her\par
friend in the art of deriving satisfaction from distinction and\par
admiration, rather than from conscience.\par
\par
Emily observed with concern the ascendancy, which Montoni had acquired\par
over Madame Cheron, as well as the increasing frequency of his visits;\par
and her own opinion of this Italian was confirmed by that of\par
Valancourt, who had always expressed a dislike of him. As she was, one\par
morning, sitting at work in the pavilion, enjoying the pleasant\par
freshness of spring, whose colours were now spread upon the landscape,\par
and listening to Valancourt, who was reading, but who often laid aside\par
the book to converse, she received a summons to attend Madame Cheron\par
immediately, and had scarcely entered the dressing-room, when she\par
observed with surprise the dejection of her aunt’s countenance, and the\par
contrasted gaiety of her dress. “So, niece!”—said Madame, and she\par
stopped under some degree of embarrassment.—“I sent for you—I—I wished\par
to see you; I have news to tell you. From this hour you must consider\par
the Signor Montoni as your uncle—we were married this morning.”\par
\par
Astonished—not so much at the marriage, as at the secrecy with which it\par
had been concluded, and the agitation with which it was announced,\par
Emily, at length, attributed the privacy to the wish of Montoni, rather\par
than of her aunt. His wife, however, intended, that the contrary should\par
be believed, and therefore added, “you see I wished to avoid a bustle;\par
but now the ceremony is over I shall do so no longer; and I wish to\par
announce to my servants that they must receive the Signor Montoni for\par
their master.” Emily made a feeble attempt to congratulate her on these\par
apparently imprudent nuptials. “I shall now celebrate my marriage with\par
some splendour,” continued Madame Montoni, “and to save time I shall\par
avail myself of the preparation that has been made for yours, which\par
will, of course, be delayed a little while. Such of your wedding\par
clothes as are ready I shall expect you will appear in, to do honour to\par
this festival. I also wish you to inform Monsieur Valancourt, that I\par
have changed my name, and he will acquaint Madame Clairval. In a few\par
days I shall give a grand entertainment, at which I shall request their\par
presence.”\par
\par
Emily was so lost in surprise and various thought, that she made Madame\par
Montoni scarcely any reply, but, at her desire, she returned to inform\par
Valancourt of what had passed. Surprise was not his predominant emotion\par
on hearing of these hasty nuptials; and, when he learned, that they\par
were to be the means of delaying his own, and that the very ornaments\par
of the château, which had been prepared to grace the nuptial day of his\par
Emily, were to be degraded to the celebration of Madame Montoni’s,\par
grief and indignation agitated him alternately. He could conceal\par
neither from the observation of Emily, whose efforts to abstract him\par
from these serious emotions, and to laugh at the apprehensive\par
considerations, that assailed him, were ineffectual; and, when, at\par
length, he took leave, there was an earnest tenderness in his manner,\par
that extremely affected her; she even shed tears, when he disappeared\par
at the end of the terrace, yet knew not exactly why she should do so.\par
\par
Montoni now took possession of the château, and the command of its\par
inhabitants, with the ease of a man, who had long considered it to be\par
his own. His friend Cavigni, who had been extremely serviceable, in\par
having paid Madame Cheron the attention and flattery, which she\par
required, but from which Montoni too often revolted, had apartments\par
assigned to him, and received from the domestics an equal degree of\par
obedience with the master of the mansion.\par
\par
Within a few days, Madame Montoni, as she had promised, gave a\par
magnificent entertainment to a very numerous company, among whom was\par
Valancourt; but at which Madame Clairval excused herself from\par
attending. There was a concert, ball and supper. Valancourt was, of\par
course, Emily’s partner, and though, when he gave a look to the\par
decorations of the apartments, he could not but remember, that they\par
were designed for other festivities, than those they now contributed to\par
celebrate, he endeavoured to check his concern by considering, that a\par
little while only would elapse before they would be given to their\par
original destination. During this evening, Madame Montoni danced,\par
laughed and talked incessantly; while Montoni, silent, reserved and\par
somewhat haughty, seemed weary of the parade, and of the frivolous\par
company it had drawn together.\par
\par
This was the first and the last entertainment, given in celebration of\par
their nuptials. Montoni, though the severity of his temper and the\par
gloominess of his pride prevented him from enjoying such festivities,\par
was extremely willing to promote them. It was seldom, that he could\par
meet in any company a man of more address, and still seldomer one of\par
more understanding, than himself; the balance of advantage in such\par
parties, or in the connections, which might arise from them, must,\par
therefore, be on his side; and, knowing, as he did, the selfish\par
purposes, for which they are generally frequented, he had no objection\par
to measure his talents of dissimulation with those of any other\par
competitor for distinction and plunder. But his wife, who, when her own\par
interest was immediately concerned, had sometimes more discernment than\par
vanity, acquired a consciousness of her inferiority to other women, in\par
personal attractions, which, uniting with the jealousy natural to the\par
discovery, counteracted his readiness for mingling with all the parties\par
Thoulouse could afford. Till she had, as she supposed, the affections\par
of a husband to lose, she had no motive for discovering the unwelcome\par
truth, and it had never obtruded itself upon her; but, now that it\par
influenced her policy, she opposed her husband’s inclination for\par
company, with the more eagerness, because she believed him to be really\par
as well received in the female society of the place, as, during his\par
addresses to her, he had affected to be.\par
\par
A few weeks only had elapsed, since the marriage, when Madame Montoni\par
informed Emily, that the Signor intended to return to Italy, as soon as\par
the necessary preparation could be made for so long a journey. “We\par
shall go to Venice,” said she, “where the Signor has a fine mansion,\par
and from thence to his estate in Tuscany. Why do you look so grave,\par
child?—You, who are so fond of a romantic country and fine views, will\par
doubtless be delighted with this journey.”\par
\par
“Am I then to be of the party, madam?” said Emily, with extreme\par
surprise and emotion. “Most certainly,” replied her aunt, “how could\par
you imagine we should leave you behind? But I see you are thinking of\par
the Chevalier; he is not yet, I believe, informed of the journey, but\par
he very soon will be so. Signor Montoni is gone to acquaint Madame\par
Clairval of our journey, and to say, that the proposed connection\par
between the families must from this time be thought of no more.”\par
\par
The unfeeling manner, in which Madame Montoni thus informed her niece,\par
that she must be separated, perhaps for ever, from the man, with whom\par
she was on the point of being united for life, added to the dismay,\par
which she must otherwise have suffered at such intelligence. When she\par
could speak, she asked the cause of the sudden change in Madame’s\par
sentiments towards Valancourt, but the only reply she could obtain was,\par
that the Signor had forbade the connection, considering it to be\par
greatly inferior to what Emily might reasonably expect.\par
\par
“I now leave the affair entirely to the Signor,” added Madame Montoni,\par
“but I must say, that M. Valancourt never was a favourite with me, and\par
I was overpersuaded, or I should not have given my consent to the\par
connection. I was weak enough—I am so foolish sometimes!—to suffer\par
other people’s uneasiness to affect me, and so my better judgment\par
yielded to your affliction. But the Signor has very properly pointed\par
out the folly of this, and he shall not have to reprove me a second\par
time. I am determined, that you shall submit to those, who know how to\par
guide you better than yourself—I am determined, that you shall be\par
conformable.”\par
\par
Emily would have been astonished at the assertions of this eloquent\par
speech, had not her mind been so overwhelmed by the sudden shock it had\par
received, that she scarcely heard a word of what was latterly addressed\par
to her. Whatever were the weaknesses of Madame Montoni, she might have\par
avoided to accuse herself with those of compassion and tenderness to\par
the feelings of others, and especially to those of Emily. It was the\par
same ambition, that lately prevailed upon her to solicit an alliance\par
with Madame Clairval’s family, which induced her to withdraw from it,\par
now that her marriage with Montoni had exalted her self-consequence,\par
and, with it, her views for her niece.\par
\par
Emily was, at this time, too much affected to employ either\par
remonstrance, or entreaty on this topic; and when, at length, she\par
attempted the latter, her emotion overcame her speech, and she retired\par
to her apartment, to think, if in the present state of her mind to\par
think was possible, upon this sudden and overwhelming subject. It was\par
very long, before her spirits were sufficiently composed to permit the\par
reflection, which, when it came, was dark and even terrible. She saw,\par
that Montoni sought to aggrandise himself in his disposal of her, and\par
it occurred, that his friend Cavigni was the person, for whom he was\par
interested. The prospect of going to Italy was still rendered darker,\par
when she considered the tumultuous situation of that country, then torn\par
by civil commotion, where every petty state was at war with its\par
neighbour, and even every castle liable to the attack of an invader.\par
She considered the person, to whose immediate guidance she would be\par
committed, and the vast distance, that was to separate her from\par
Valancourt, and, at the recollection of him, every other image vanished\par
from her mind, and every thought was again obscured by grief.\par
\par
In this perturbed state she passed some hours, and, when she was\par
summoned to dinner, she entreated permission to remain in her own\par
apartment; but Madame Montoni was alone, and the request was refused.\par
Emily and her aunt said little during the repast; the one occupied by\par
her griefs, the other engrossed by the disappointment, which the\par
unexpected absence of Montoni occasioned; for not only was her vanity\par
piqued by the neglect, but her jealousy alarmed by what she considered\par
as a mysterious engagement. When the cloth was drawn and they were\par
alone, Emily renewed the mention of Valancourt; but her aunt, neither\par
softened to pity, nor awakened to remorse, became enraged, that her\par
will should be opposed, and the authority of Montoni questioned, though\par
this was done by Emily with her usual gentleness, who, after a long,\par
and torturing conversation, retired in tears.\par
\par
As she crossed the hall, a person entered it by the great door, whom,\par
as her eyes hastily glanced that way, she imagined to be Montoni, and\par
she was passing on with quicker steps, when she heard the well-known\par
voice of Valancourt.\par
\par
“Emily, O! my Emily!” cried he in a tone faltering with impatience,\par
while she turned, and, as he advanced, was alarmed at the expression of\par
his countenance and the eager desperation of his air. “In tears, Emily!\par
I would speak with you,” said he, “I have much to say; conduct me to\par
where we may converse. But you tremble—you are ill! Let me lead you to\par
a seat.”\par
\par
He observed the open door of an apartment, and hastily took her hand to\par
lead her thither; but she attempted to withdraw it, and said, with a\par
languid smile, “I am better already; if you wish to see my aunt she is\par
in the dining-parlour.” “I must speak with _you_, my Emily,” replied\par
Valancourt, “Good God! is it already come to this? Are you indeed so\par
willing to resign me? But this is an improper place—I am overheard. Let\par
me entreat your attention, if only for a few minutes.”—“When you have\par
seen my aunt,” said Emily. “I was wretched enough when I came hither,”\par
exclaimed Valancourt, “do not increase my misery by this coldness—this\par
cruel refusal.”\par
\par
The despondency, with which he spoke this, affected her almost to\par
tears, but she persisted in refusing to hear him, till he had conversed\par
with Madame Montoni. “Where is her husband, where, then, is Montoni?”\par
said Valancourt, in an altered tone: “it is he, to whom I must speak.”\par
\par
Emily, terrified for the consequence of the indignation, that flashed\par
in his eyes, tremblingly assured him, that Montoni was not at home, and\par
entreated he would endeavour to moderate his resentment. At the\par
tremulous accents of her voice, his eyes softened instantly from\par
wildness into tenderness. “You are ill, Emily,” said he, “they will\par
destroy us both! Forgive me, that I dared to doubt your affection.”\par
\par
Emily no longer opposed him, as he led her into an adjoining parlour;\par
the manner, in which he had named Montoni, had so much alarmed her for\par
his own safety, that she was now only anxious to prevent the\par
consequences of his just resentment. He listened to her entreaties,\par
with attention, but replied to them only with looks of despondency and\par
tenderness, concealing, as much as possible, the sentiments he felt\par
towards Montoni, that he might soothe the apprehensions, which\par
distressed her. But she saw the veil he had spread over his resentment,\par
and, his assumed tranquillity only alarming her more, she urged, at\par
length, the impolicy of forcing an interview with Montoni, and of\par
taking any measure, which might render their separation irremediable.\par
Valancourt yielded to these remonstrances, and her affecting entreaties\par
drew from him a promise, that, however Montoni might persist in his\par
design of disuniting them, he would not seek to redress his wrongs by\par
violence. “For my sake,” said Emily, “let the consideration of what I\par
should suffer deter you from such a mode of revenge!” “For your sake,\par
Emily,” replied Valancourt, his eyes filling with tears of tenderness\par
and grief, while he gazed upon her. “Yes—yes—I shall subdue myself.\par
But, though I have given you my solemn promise to do this, do not\par
expect, that I can tamely submit to the authority of Montoni; if I\par
could, I should be unworthy of you. Yet, O Emily! how long may he\par
condemn me to live without you,—how long may it be before you return to\par
France!”\par
\par
Emily endeavoured to sooth him with assurances of her unalterable\par
affection, and by representing, that, in little more than a year, she\par
should be her own mistress, as far as related to her aunt, from whose\par
guardianship her age would then release her; assurances, which gave\par
little consolation to Valancourt, who considered, that she would then\par
be in Italy and in the power of those, whose dominion over her would\par
not cease with their rights; but he affected to be consoled by them.\par
Emily, comforted by the promise she had obtained, and by his apparent\par
composure, was about to leave him, when her aunt entered the room. She\par
threw a glance of sharp reproof upon her niece, who immediately\par
withdrew, and of haughty displeasure upon Valancourt.\par
\par
“This is not the conduct I should have expected from you, sir;” said\par
she, “I did not expect to see you in my house, after you had been\par
informed, that your visits were no longer agreeable, much less, that\par
you would seek a clandestine interview with my niece, and that she\par
would grant one.”\par
\par
Valancourt, perceiving it necessary to vindicate Emily from such a\par
design, explained, that the purpose of his own visit had been to\par
request an interview with Montoni, and he then entered upon the subject\par
of it, with the tempered spirit which the sex, rather than the\par
respectability, of Madame Montoni, demanded.\par
\par
His expostulations were answered with severe rebuke; she lamented\par
again, that her prudence had ever yielded to what she termed\par
compassion, and added, that she was so sensible of the folly of her\par
former consent, that, to prevent the possibility of a repetition, she\par
had committed the affair entirely to the conduct of Signor Montoni.\par
\par
The feeling eloquence of Valancourt, however, at length, made her\par
sensible in some measure of her unworthy conduct, and she became\par
susceptible to shame, but not remorse: she hated Valancourt, who\par
awakened her to this painful sensation, and, in proportion as she grew\par
dissatisfied with herself, her abhorrence of him increased. This was\par
also the more inveterate, because his tempered words and manner were\par
such as, without accusing her, compelled her to accuse herself, and\par
neither left her a hope, that the odious portrait was the caricature of\par
his prejudice, or afforded her an excuse for expressing the violent\par
resentment, with which she contemplated it. At length, her anger rose\par
to such a height, that Valancourt was compelled to leave the house\par
abruptly, lest he should forfeit his own esteem by an intemperate\par
reply. He was then convinced, that from Madame Montoni he had nothing\par
to hope, for what of either pity, or justice could be expected from a\par
person, who could feel the pain of guilt, without the humility of\par
repentance?\par
\par
To Montoni he looked with equal despondency, since it was nearly\par
evident, that this plan of separation originated with him, and it was\par
not probable, that he would relinquish his own views to entreaties, or\par
remonstrances, which he must have foreseen and have been prepared to\par
resist. Yet, remembering his promise to Emily, and more solicitous,\par
concerning his love, than jealous of his consequence, Valancourt was\par
careful to do nothing that might unnecessarily irritate Montoni, he\par
wrote to him, therefore, not to demand an interview, but to solicit\par
one, and, having done this, he endeavoured to wait with calmness his\par
reply.\par
\par
Madame Clairval was passive in the affair. When she gave her\par
approbation to Valancourt’s marriage, it was in the belief, that Emily\par
would be the heiress of Madame Montoni’s fortune; and, though, upon the\par
nuptials of the latter, when she perceived the fallacy of this\par
expectation, her conscience had withheld her from adopting any measure\par
to prevent the union, her benevolence was not sufficiently active to\par
impel her towards any step, that might now promote it. She was, on the\par
contrary, secretly pleased, that Valancourt was released from an\par
engagement, which she considered to be as inferior, in point of\par
fortune, to his merit, as his alliance was thought by Montoni to be\par
humiliating to the beauty of Emily; and, though her pride was wounded\par
by this rejection of a member of her family, she disdained to show\par
resentment otherwise, than by silence.\par
\par
Montoni, in his reply to Valancourt, said, that as an interview could\par
neither remove the objections of the one, nor overcome the wishes of\par
the other, it would serve only to produce useless altercation between\par
them. He, therefore, thought proper to refuse it.\par
\par
In consideration of the policy, suggested by Emily, and of his promise\par
to her, Valancourt restrained the impulse, that urged him to the house\par
of Montoni, to demand what had been denied to his entreaties. He only\par
repeated his solicitations to see him; seconding them with all the\par
arguments his situation could suggest. Thus several days passed, in\par
remonstrance, on one side, and inflexible denial, on the other; for,\par
whether it was fear, or shame, or the hatred, which results from both,\par
that made Montoni shun the man he had injured, he was peremptory in his\par
refusal, and was neither softened to pity by the agony, which\par
Valancourt’s letters portrayed, nor awakened to a repentance of his own\par
injustice by the strong remonstrances he employed. At length,\par
Valancourt’s letters were returned unopened, and then, in the first\par
moments of passionate despair, he forgot every promise to Emily, except\par
the solemn one, which bound him to avoid violence, and hastened to\par
Montoni’s château, determined to see him by whatever other means might\par
be necessary. Montoni was denied, and Valancourt, when he afterwards\par
enquired for Madame, and Ma’amselle St. Aubert, was absolutely refused\par
admittance by the servants. Not choosing to submit himself to a contest\par
with these, he, at length, departed, and, returning home in a state of\par
mind approaching to frenzy, wrote to Emily of what had passed,\par
expressed without restraint all the agony of his heart, and entreated,\par
that, since he must not otherwise hope to see her immediately, she\par
would allow him an interview unknown to Montoni. Soon after he had\par
dispatched this, his passions becoming more temperate, he was sensible\par
of the error he had committed in having given Emily a new subject of\par
distress in the strong mention of his own suffering, and would have\par
given half the world, had it been his, to recover the letter. Emily,\par
however, was spared the pain she must have received from it by the\par
suspicious policy of Madame Montoni, who had ordered, that all letters,\par
addressed to her niece, should be delivered to herself, and who, after\par
having perused this and indulged the expressions of resentment, which\par
Valancourt’s mention of Montoni provoked, had consigned it to the\par
flames.\par
\par
Montoni, meanwhile, every day more impatient to leave France, gave\par
repeated orders for dispatch to the servants employed in preparations\par
for the journey, and to the persons, with whom he was transacting some\par
particular business. He preserved a steady silence to the letters in\par
which Valancourt, despairing of greater good, and having subdued the\par
passion, that had transgressed against his policy, solicited only the\par
indulgence of being allowed to bid Emily farewell. But when Valancourt\par
learned that she was really to set out in a very few days, and that it\par
was designed he should see her no more, forgetting every consideration\par
of prudence, he dared, in a second letter to Emily, to propose a\par
clandestine marriage. This also was transmitted to Madame Montoni, and\par
the last day of Emily’s stay at Thoulouse arrived, without affording\par
Valancourt even a line to sooth his sufferings, or a hope, that he\par
should be allowed a parting interview.\par
\par
During this period of torturing suspense to Valancourt, Emily was sunk\par
into that kind of stupor, with which sudden and irremediable misfortune\par
sometimes overwhelms the mind. Loving him with the tenderest affection,\par
and having long been accustomed to consider him as the friend and\par
companion of all her future days, she had no ideas of happiness, that\par
were not connected with him. What, then, must have been her suffering,\par
when thus suddenly they were to be separated, perhaps, for ever,\par
certainly to be thrown into distant parts of the world, where they\par
could scarcely hear of each other’s existence; and all this in\par
obedience to the will of a stranger, for such as Montoni, and of a\par
person, who had but lately been anxious to hasten their nuptials! It\par
was in vain, that she endeavoured to subdue her grief, and resign\par
herself to an event, which she could not avoid. The silence of\par
Valancourt afflicted more than it surprised her, since she attributed\par
it to its just occasion; but, when the day, preceding that, on which\par
she was to quit Thoulouse, arrived, and she had heard no mention of his\par
being permitted to take leave of her, grief overcame every\par
consideration, that had made her reluctant to speak of him, and she\par
enquired of Madame Montoni, whether this consolation had been refused.\par
Her aunt informed her that it had, adding, that, after the provocation\par
she had herself received from Valancourt, in their last interview, and\par
the persecution, which the Signor had suffered from his letters, no\par
entreaties should avail to procure it.\par
\par
“If the chevalier expected this favour from us,” said she, “he should\par
have conducted himself in a very different manner; he should have\par
waited patiently, till he knew whether we were disposed to grant it,\par
and not have come and reproved me, because I did not think proper to\par
bestow my niece upon him, and then have persisted in troubling the\par
Signor, because he did not think proper to enter into any dispute about\par
so childish an affair. His behaviour throughout has been extremely\par
presumptuous and impertinent, and I desire, that I may never hear his\par
name repeated, and that you will get the better of those foolish\par
sorrows and whims, and look like other people, and not appear with that\par
dismal countenance, as if you were ready to cry. For, though you say\par
nothing, you cannot conceal your grief from my penetration. I can see\par
you are ready to cry at this moment, though I am reproving you for it;\par
aye, even now, in spite of my commands.”\par
\par
Emily, having turned away to hide her tears, quitted the room to\par
indulge them, and the day was passed in an intensity of anguish, such\par
as she had, perhaps, never known before. When she withdrew to her\par
chamber for the night, she remained in the chair where she had placed\par
herself, on entering the room, absorbed in her grief, till long after\par
every member of the family, except herself, was retired to rest. She\par
could not divest herself of a belief, that she had parted with\par
Valancourt to meet no more; a belief, which did not arise merely from\par
foreseen circumstances, for, though the length of the journey she was\par
about to commence, the uncertainty as to the period of her return,\par
together with the prohibitions she had received, seemed to justify it,\par
she yielded also to an impression, which she mistook for a\par
presentiment, that she was going from Valancourt for ever. How dreadful\par
to her imagination, too, was the distance that would separate them—the\par
Alps, those tremendous barriers! would rise, and whole countries extend\par
between the regions where each must exist! To live in adjoining\par
provinces, to live even in the same country, though without seeing him,\par
was comparative happiness to the conviction of this dreadful length of\par
distance.\par
\par
Her mind was, at length, so much agitated by the consideration of her\par
state, and the belief, that she had seen Valancourt for the last time,\par
that she suddenly became very faint, and, looking round the chamber for\par
something, that might revive her, she observed the casements, and had\par
just strength to throw one open, near which she seated herself. The air\par
recalled her spirits, and the still moonlight, that fell upon the elms\par
of a long avenue, fronting the window, somewhat soothed them, and\par
determined her to try whether exercise and the open air would not\par
relieve the intense pain that bound her temples. In the château all was\par
still; and, passing down the great staircase into the hall, from whence\par
a passage led immediately to the garden, she softly and unheard, as she\par
thought, unlocked the door, and entered the avenue. Emily passed on\par
with steps now hurried, and now faltering, as, deceived by the shadows\par
among the trees, she fancied she saw some person move in the distant\par
perspective, and feared, that it was a spy of Madame Montoni. Her\par
desire, however, to revisit the pavilion, where she had passed so many\par
happy hours with Valancourt, and had admired with him the extensive\par
prospect over Languedoc and her native Gascony, overcame her\par
apprehension of being observed, and she moved on towards the terrace,\par
which, running along the upper garden, commanded the whole of the lower\par
one, and communicated with it by a flight of marble steps, that\par
terminated the avenue.\par
\par
Having reached these steps, she paused a moment to look round, for her\par
distance from the château now increased the fear, which the stillness\par
and obscurity of the hour had awakened. But, perceiving nothing that\par
could justify it, she ascended to the terrace, where the moonlight\par
showed the long broad walk, with the pavilion at its extremity, while\par
the rays silvered the foliage of the high trees and shrubs, that\par
bordered it on the right, and the tufted summits of those, that rose to\par
a level with the balustrade on the left, from the garden below. Her\par
distance from the château again alarming her, she paused to listen; the\par
night was so calm, that no sound could have escaped her, but she heard\par
only the plaintive sweetness of the nightingale, with the light shiver\par
of the leaves, and she pursued her way towards the pavilion, having\par
reached which, its obscurity did not prevent the emotion, that a fuller\par
view of its well-known scene would have excited. The lattices were\par
thrown back, and showed beyond their embowered arch the moonlight\par
landscape, shadowy and soft; its groves, and plains extending gradually\par
and indistinctly to the eye, its distant mountains catching a stronger\par
gleam, and the nearer river reflecting the moon, and trembling to her\par
rays.\par
\par
Emily, as she approached the lattice, was sensible of the features of\par
this scene only as they served to bring Valancourt more immediately to\par
her fancy. “Ah!” said she, with a heavy sigh, as she threw herself into\par
a chair by the window, “how often have we sat together in this\par
spot—often have looked upon that landscape! Never, never more shall we\par
view it together—never—never more, perhaps, shall we look upon each\par
other!”\par
\par
Her tears were suddenly stopped by terror—a voice spoke near her in the\par
pavilion; she shrieked—it spoke again, and she distinguished the\par
well-known tones of Valancourt. It was indeed Valancourt who supported\par
her in his arms! For some moments their emotion would not suffer either\par
to speak. “Emily,” said Valancourt at length, as he pressed her hand in\par
his. “Emily!” and he was again silent, but the accent, in which he had\par
pronounced her name, expressed all his tenderness and sorrow.\par
\par
“O my Emily!” he resumed, after a long pause, “I do then see you once\par
again, and hear again the sound of that voice! I have haunted this\par
place—these gardens, for many—many nights, with a faint, very faint\par
hope of seeing you. This was the only chance that remained to me, and\par
thank Heaven! it has at length succeeded—I am not condemned to absolute\par
despair!”\par
\par
Emily said something, she scarcely knew what, expressive of her\par
unalterable affection, and endeavoured to calm the agitation of his\par
mind; but Valancourt could for some time only utter incoherent\par
expressions of his emotions; and, when he was somewhat more composed,\par
he said, “I came hither, soon after sunset, and have been watching in\par
the gardens, and in this pavilion ever since; for, though I had now\par
given up all hope of seeing you, I could not resolve to tear myself\par
from a place so near to you, and should probably have lingered about\par
the château till morning dawned. O how heavily the moments have passed,\par
yet with what various emotion have they been marked, as I sometimes\par
thought I heard footsteps, and fancied you were approaching, and then\par
again—perceived only a dead and dreary silence! But, when you opened\par
the door of the pavilion, and the darkness prevented my distinguishing\par
with certainty, whether it was my love—my heart beat so strongly with\par
hopes and fears, that I could not speak. The instant I heard the\par
plaintive accents of your voice, my doubts vanished, but not my fears,\par
till you spoke of me; then, losing the apprehension of alarming you in\par
the excess of my emotion, I could no longer be silent. O Emily! these\par
are moments, in which joy and grief struggle so powerfully for\par
pre-eminence, that the heart can scarcely support the contest!”\par
\par
Emily’s heart acknowledged the truth of this assertion, but the joy she\par
felt on thus meeting Valancourt, at the very moment when she was\par
lamenting, that they must probably meet no more, soon melted into\par
grief, as reflection stole over her thoughts, and imagination prompted\par
visions of the future. She struggled to recover the calm dignity of\par
mind, which was necessary to support her through this last interview,\par
and which Valancourt found it utterly impossible to attain, for the\par
transports of his joy changed abruptly into those of suffering, and he\par
expressed in the most impassioned language his horror of this\par
separation, and his despair of their ever meeting again. Emily wept\par
silently as she listened to him, and then, trying to command her own\par
distress, and to sooth his, she suggested every circumstance that could\par
lead to hope. But the energy of his fears led him instantly to detect\par
the friendly fallacies, which she endeavoured to impose on herself and\par
him, and also to conjure up illusions too powerful for his reason.\par
\par
“You are going from me,” said he, “to a distant country, O how\par
distant!—to new society, new friends, new admirers, with people too,\par
who will try to make you forget me, and to promote new connections! How\par
can I know this, and not know, that you will never return for me—never\par
can be mine.” His voice was stifled by sighs.\par
\par
“You believe, then,” said Emily, “that the pangs I suffer proceed from\par
a trivial and temporary interest; you believe—”\par
\par
“Suffer!” interrupted Valancourt, “suffer for me! O Emily—how sweet—how\par
bitter are those words; what comfort, what anguish do they give! I\par
ought not to doubt the steadiness of your affection, yet such is the\par
inconsistency of real love, that it is always awake to suspicion,\par
however unreasonable; always requiring new assurances from the object\par
of its interest, and thus it is, that I always feel revived, as by a\par
new conviction, when your words tell me I am dear to you; and, wanting\par
these, I relapse into doubt, and too often into despondency.” Then\par
seeming to recollect himself, he exclaimed, “But what a wretch am I,\par
thus to torture you, and in these moments, too! I, who ought to support\par
and comfort you!”\par
\par
This reflection overcame Valancourt with tenderness, but, relapsing\par
into despondency, he again felt only for himself, and lamented again\par
this cruel separation, in a voice and words so impassioned, that Emily\par
could no longer struggle to repress her own grief, or to sooth his.\par
Valancourt, between these emotions of love and pity, lost the power,\par
and almost the wish, of repressing his agitation; and, in the intervals\par
of convulsive sobs, he, at one moment, kissed away her tears, then told\par
her cruelly, that possibly she might never again weep for him, and then\par
tried to speak more calmly, but only exclaimed, “O Emily—my heart will\par
break!—I cannot—cannot leave you! Now—I gaze upon that countenance, now\par
I hold you in my arms! a little while, and all this will appear a\par
dream. I shall look, and cannot see you; shall try to recollect your\par
features—and the impression will be fled from my imagination;—to hear\par
the tones of your voice, and even memory will be silent!—I cannot,\par
cannot leave you! Why should we confide the happiness of our whole\par
lives to the will of people, who have no right to interrupt, and,\par
except in giving you to me, have no power to promote it? O Emily!\par
venture to trust your own heart, venture to be mine for ever!” His\par
voice trembled, and he was silent; Emily continued to weep, and was\par
silent also, when Valancourt proceeded to propose an immediate\par
marriage, and that at an early hour on the following morning, she\par
should quit Madame Montoni’s house, and be conducted by him to the\par
church of the Augustines, where a friar should await to unite them.\par
\par
The silence, with which she listened to a proposal, dictated by love\par
and despair, and enforced at a moment, when it seemed scarcely possible\par
for her to oppose it;—when her heart was softened by the sorrows of a\par
separation, that might be eternal, and her reason obscured by the\par
illusions of love and terror, encouraged him to hope, that it would not\par
be rejected. “Speak, my Emily!” said Valancourt eagerly, “let me hear\par
your voice, let me hear you confirm my fate.” she spoke not; her cheek\par
was cold, and her senses seemed to fail her, but she did not faint. To\par
Valancourt’s terrified imagination she appeared to be dying; he called\par
upon her name, rose to go to the château for assistance, and then,\par
recollecting her situation, feared to go, or to leave her for a moment.\par
\par
After a few minutes, she drew a deep sigh, and began to revive. The\par
conflict she had suffered, between love and the duty she at present\par
owed to her father’s sister; her repugnance to a clandestine marriage,\par
her fear of emerging on the world with embarrassments, such as might\par
ultimately involve the object of her affection in misery and\par
repentance;—all this various interest was too powerful for a mind,\par
already enervated by sorrow, and her reason had suffered a transient\par
suspension. But duty, and good sense, however hard the conflict, at\par
length, triumphed over affection and mournful presentiment; above all,\par
she dreaded to involve Valancourt in obscurity and vain regret, which\par
she saw, or thought she saw, must be the too certain consequence of a\par
marriage in their present circumstances; and she acted, perhaps, with\par
somewhat more than female fortitude, when she resolved to endure a\par
present, rather than provoke a distant misfortune.\par
\par
With a candour, that proved how truly she esteemed and loved him, and\par
which endeared her to him, if possible, more than ever, she told\par
Valancourt all her reasons for rejecting his proposals. Those, which\par
influenced her concerning his future welfare, he instantly refuted, or\par
rather contradicted; but they awakened tender considerations for her,\par
which the frenzy of passion and despair had concealed before, and love,\par
which had but lately prompted him to propose a clandestine and\par
immediate marriage, now induced him to renounce it. The triumph was\par
almost too much for his heart; for Emily’s sake, he endeavoured to\par
stifle his grief, but the swelling anguish would not be restrained. “O\par
Emily!” said he, “I must leave you—I _must_ leave you, and I know it is\par
for ever!”\par
\par
Convulsive sobs again interrupted his words, and they wept together in\par
silence, till Emily, recollecting the danger of being discovered, and\par
the impropriety of prolonging an interview, which might subject her to\par
censure, summoned all her fortitude to utter a last farewell.\par
\par
“Stay!” said Valancourt, “I conjure you stay, for I have much to tell\par
you. The agitation of my mind has hitherto suffered me to speak only on\par
the subject that occupied it;—I have forborne to mention a doubt of\par
much importance, partly, lest it should appear as if I told it with an\par
ungenerous view of alarming you into a compliance with my late\par
proposal.”\par
\par
Emily, much agitated, did not leave Valancourt, but she led him from\par
the pavilion, and, as they walked upon the terrace, he proceeded as\par
follows:\par
\par
“This Montoni: I have heard some strange hints concerning him. Are you\par
certain he is of Madame Quesnel’s family, and that his fortune is what\par
it appears to be?”\par
\par
“I have no reason to doubt either,” replied Emily, in a voice of alarm.\par
“Of the first, indeed, I cannot doubt, but I have no certain means of\par
judging of the latter, and I entreat you will tell me all you have\par
heard.”\par
\par
“That I certainly will, but it is very imperfect, and unsatisfactory\par
information. I gathered it by accident from an Italian, who was\par
speaking to another person of this Montoni. They were talking of his\par
marriage; the Italian said, that if he was the person he meant, he was\par
not likely to make Madame Cheron happy. He proceeded to speak of him in\par
general terms of dislike, and then gave some particular hints,\par
concerning his character, that excited my curiosity, and I ventured to\par
ask him a few questions. He was reserved in his replies, but, after\par
hesitating for some time, he owned, that he had understood abroad, that\par
Montoni was a man of desperate fortune and character. He said something\par
of a castle of Montoni’s, situated among the Apennines, and of some\par
strange circumstances, that might be mentioned, as to his former mode\par
of life. I pressed him to inform me further, but I believe the strong\par
interest I felt was visible in my manner, and alarmed him; for no\par
entreaties could prevail with him to give any explanation of the\par
circumstances he had alluded to, or to mention anything further\par
concerning Montoni. I observed to him, that, if Montoni was possessed\par
of a castle in the Apennines, it appeared from such a circumstance,\par
that he was of some family, and also seemed to contradict the report,\par
that he was a man of entirely broken fortunes. He shook his head, and\par
looked as if he could have said a great deal, but made no reply.\par
\par
“A hope of learning something more satisfactory, or more positive,\par
detained me in his company a considerable time, and I renewed the\par
subject repeatedly, but the Italian wrapped himself up in reserve,\par
said—that what he had mentioned he had caught only from a floating\par
report, and that reports frequently arose from personal malice, and\par
were very little to be depended upon. I forbore to press the subject\par
farther, since it was obvious that he was alarmed for the consequence\par
of what he had already said, and I was compelled to remain in\par
uncertainty on a point where suspense is almost intolerable. Think,\par
Emily, what I must suffer to see you depart for a foreign country,\par
committed to the power of a man of such doubtful character as is this\par
Montoni! But I will not alarm you unnecessarily;—it is possible, as the\par
Italian said, at first, that this is not the Montoni he alluded to.\par
Yet, Emily, consider well before you resolve to commit yourself to him.\par
O! I must not trust myself to speak—or I shall renounce all the\par
motives, which so lately influenced me to resign the hope of your\par
becoming mine immediately.”\par
\par
Valancourt walked upon the terrace with hurried steps, while Emily\par
remained leaning on the balustrade in deep thought. The information she\par
had just received excited, perhaps, more alarm than it could justify,\par
and raised once more the conflict of contrasted interests. She had\par
never liked Montoni. The fire and keenness of his eye, its proud\par
exultation, its bold fierceness, its sullen watchfulness, as occasion,\par
and even slight occasion, had called forth the latent soul, she had\par
often observed with emotion; while from the usual expression of his\par
countenance she had always shrunk. From such observations she was the\par
more inclined to believe, that it was this Montoni, of whom the Italian\par
had uttered his suspicious hints. The thought of being solely in his\par
power, in a foreign land, was terrifying to her, but it was not by\par
terror alone that she was urged to an immediate marriage with\par
Valancourt. The tenderest love had already pleaded his cause, but had\par
been unable to overcome her opinion, as to her duty, her disinterested\par
considerations for Valancourt, and the delicacy, which made her revolt\par
from a clandestine union. It was not to be expected, that a vague\par
terror would be more powerful, than the united influence of love and\par
grief. But it recalled all their energy, and rendered a second conquest\par
necessary.\par
\par
With Valancourt, whose imagination was now awake to the suggestion of\par
every passion; whose apprehensions for Emily had acquired strength by\par
the mere mention of them, and became every instant more powerful, as\par
his mind brooded over them—with Valancourt no second conquest was\par
attainable. He thought he saw in the clearest light, and love assisted\par
the fear, that this journey to Italy would involve Emily in misery; he\par
determined, therefore, to persevere in opposing it, and in conjuring\par
her to bestow upon him the title of her lawful protector.\par
\par
“Emily!” said he, with solemn earnestness, “this is no time for\par
scrupulous distinctions, for weighing the dubious and comparatively\par
trifling circumstances, that may affect our future comfort. I now see,\par
much more clearly than before, the train of serious dangers you are\par
going to encounter with a man of Montoni’s character. Those dark hints\par
of the Italian spoke much, but not more than the idea I have of\par
Montoni’s disposition, as exhibited even in his countenance. I think I\par
see at this moment all that could have been hinted, written there. He\par
is the Italian, whom I fear, and I conjure you for your own sake, as\par
well as for mine, to prevent the evils I shudder to foresee. O Emily!\par
let my tenderness, my arms withhold you from them—give me the right to\par
defend you!”\par
\par
Emily only sighed, while Valancourt proceeded to remonstrate and to\par
entreat with all the energy that love and apprehension could inspire.\par
But, as his imagination magnified to her the possible evils she was\par
going to meet, the mists of her own fancy began to dissipate, and\par
allowed her to distinguish the exaggerated images, which imposed on his\par
reason. She considered, that there was no proof of Montoni being the\par
person, whom the stranger had meant; that, even if he was so, the\par
Italian had noticed his character and broken fortunes merely from\par
report; and that, though the countenance of Montoni seemed to give\par
probability to a part of the rumour, it was not by such circumstances\par
that an implicit belief of it could be justified. These considerations\par
would probably not have arisen so distinctly to her mind, at this time,\par
had not the terrors of Valancourt presented to her such obvious\par
exaggerations of her danger, as incited her to distrust the fallacies\par
of passion. But, while she endeavoured in the gentlest manner to\par
convince him of his error, she plunged him into a new one. His voice\par
and countenance changed to an expression of dark despair. “Emily!” said\par
he, “this, this moment is the bitterest that is yet come to me. You do\par
not—cannot love me!—It would be impossible for you to reason thus\par
coolly, thus deliberately, if you did. I, _I_ am torn with anguish at\par
the prospect of our separation, and of the evils that may await you in\par
consequence of it; I would encounter any hazards to prevent it—to save\par
you. No! Emily, no!—you cannot love me.”\par
\par
“We have now little time to waste in exclamation, or assertion,” said\par
Emily, endeavouring to conceal her emotion: “if you are yet to learn\par
how dear you are, and ever must be, to my heart, no assurances of mine\par
can give you conviction.”\par
\par
The last words faltered on her lips, and her tears flowed fast. These\par
words and tears brought, once more, and with instantaneous force,\par
conviction of her love to Valancourt. He could only exclaim, “Emily!\par
Emily!” and weep over the hand he pressed to his lips; but she, after\par
some moments, again roused herself from the indulgence of sorrow, and\par
said, “I must leave you; it is late, and my absence from the château\par
may be discovered. Think of me—love me—when I am far away; the belief\par
of this will be my comfort!”\par
\par
“Think of you!—love you!” exclaimed Valancourt.\par
\par
“Try to moderate these transports,” said Emily, “for my sake, try.”\par
\par
“For your sake!”\par
\par
“Yes, for my sake,” replied Emily, in a tremulous voice, “I cannot\par
leave you thus!”\par
\par
“Then do not leave me!” said Valancourt, with quickness. “Why should we\par
part, or part for longer than till tomorrow?”\par
\par
“I am, indeed I am, unequal to these moments,” replied Emily, “you tear\par
my heart, but I never can consent to this hasty, imprudent proposal!”\par
\par
“If we could command our time, my Emily, it should not be thus hasty;\par
we must submit to circumstances.”\par
\par
“We must indeed! I have already told you all my heart—my spirits are\par
gone. You allowed the force of my objections, till your tenderness\par
called up vague terrors, which have given us both unnecessary anguish.\par
Spare me! do not oblige me to repeat the reasons I have already urged.”\par
\par
“Spare you!” cried Valancourt, “I am a wretch—a very wretch, that have\par
felt only for myself!—I! who ought to have shown the fortitude of a\par
man, who ought to have supported you, I have increased your sufferings\par
by the conduct of a child! Forgive me, Emily! think of the distraction\par
of my mind now that I am about to part with all that is dear to me—and\par
forgive me! When you are gone, I shall recollect with bitter remorse\par
what I have made you suffer, and shall wish in vain that I could see\par
you, if only for a moment, that I might sooth your grief.”\par
\par
Tears again interrupted his voice, and Emily wept with him. “I will\par
show myself more worthy of your love,” said Valancourt, at length; “I\par
will not prolong these moments. My Emily—my own Emily! never forget me!\par
God knows when we shall meet again! I resign you to his care.—O God!—O\par
God!—protect and bless her!”\par
\par
He pressed her hand to his heart. Emily sunk almost lifeless on his\par
bosom, and neither wept, nor spoke. Valancourt, now commanding his own\par
distress, tried to comfort and reassure her, but she appeared totally\par
unaffected by what he said, and a sigh, which she uttered, now and\par
then, was all that proved she had not fainted.\par
\par
He supported her slowly towards the château, weeping and speaking to\par
her; but she answered only in sighs, till, having reached the gate,\par
that terminated the avenue, she seemed to have recovered her\par
consciousness, and, looking round, perceived how near they were to the\par
château. “We must part here,” said she, stopping, “Why prolong these\par
moments? Teach me the fortitude I have forgot.”\par
\par
Valancourt struggled to assume a composed air. “Farewell, my love!”\par
said he, in a voice of solemn tenderness—“trust me we shall meet\par
again—meet for each other—meet to part no more!” His voice faltered,\par
but, recovering it, he proceeded in a firmer tone. “You know not what I\par
shall suffer, till I hear from you; I shall omit no opportunity of\par
conveying to you my letters, yet I tremble to think how few may occur.\par
And trust me, love, for your dear sake, I will try to bear this absence\par
with fortitude. O how little I have shown tonight!”\par
\par
“Farewell!” said Emily faintly. “When you are gone, I shall think of\par
many things I would have said to you.” “And I of many—many!” said\par
Valancourt; “I never left you yet, that I did not immediately remember\par
some question, or some entreaty, or some circumstance, concerning my\par
love, that I earnestly wished to mention, and feel wretched because I\par
could not. O Emily! this countenance, on which I now gaze—will, in a\par
moment, be gone from my eyes, and not all the efforts of fancy will be\par
able to recall it with exactness. O! what an infinite difference\par
between this moment and the next!—_now_, I am in your presence, can\par
behold you! _then_, all will be a dreary blank—and I shall be a\par
wanderer, exiled from my only home!”\par
\par
Valancourt again pressed her to his heart, and held her there in\par
silence, weeping. Tears once again calmed her oppressed mind. They\par
again bade each other farewell, lingered a moment, and then parted.\par
Valancourt seemed to force himself from the spot; he passed hastily up\par
the avenue, and Emily, as she moved slowly towards the château, heard\par
his distant steps. She listened to the sounds, as they sunk fainter and\par
fainter, till the melancholy stillness of night alone remained; and\par
then hurried to her chamber, to seek repose, which, alas! was fled from\par
her wretchedness.\par
\par
\par
\par
VOLUME 2\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER I\par
\par
Where’er I roam, whatever realms I see,\par
My heart untravell’d still shall turn to thee.\par
                    GOLDSMITH\par
\par
\par
The carriages were at the gates at an early hour; the bustle of the\par
domestics, passing to and fro in the galleries, awakened Emily from\par
harassing slumbers: her unquiet mind had, during the night, presented\par
her with terrific images and obscure circumstances, concerning her\par
affection and her future life. She now endeavoured to chase away the\par
impressions they had left on her fancy; but from imaginary evils she\par
awoke to the consciousness of real ones. Recollecting that she had\par
parted with Valancourt, perhaps for ever, her heart sickened as memory\par
revived. But she tried to dismiss the dismal forebodings that crowded\par
on her mind, and to restrain the sorrow which she could not subdue;\par
efforts which diffused over the settled melancholy of her countenance\par
an expression of tempered resignation, as a thin veil, thrown over the\par
features of beauty, renders them more interesting by a partial\par
concealment. But Madame Montoni observed nothing in this countenance\par
except its usual paleness, which attracted her censure. She told her\par
niece, that she had been indulging in fanciful sorrows, and begged she\par
would have more regard for decorum, than to let the world see that she\par
could not renounce an improper attachment; at which Emily’s pale cheek\par
became flushed with crimson, but it was the blush of pride, and she\par
made no answer. Soon after, Montoni entered the breakfast room, spoke\par
little, and seemed impatient to be gone.\par
\par
The windows of this room opened upon the garden. As Emily passed them,\par
she saw the spot where she had parted with Valancourt on the preceding\par
night: the remembrance pressed heavily on her heart, and she turned\par
hastily away from the object that had awakened it.\par
\par
The baggage being at length adjusted, the travellers entered their\par
carriages, and Emily would have left the château without one sigh of\par
regret, had it not been situated in the neighbourhood of Valancourt’s\par
residence.\par
\par
From a little eminence she looked back upon Thoulouse, and the far-seen\par
plains of Gascony, beyond which the broken summits of the Pyrenees\par
appeared on the distant horizon, lighted up by a morning sun. “Dear\par
pleasant mountains!” said she to herself, “how long may it be ere I see\par
ye again, and how much may happen to make me miserable in the interval!\par
Oh, could I now be certain, that I should ever return to ye, and find\par
that Valancourt still lived for me, I should go in peace! He will still\par
gaze on ye, gaze when I am far away!”\par
\par
The trees, that impended over the high banks of the road and formed a\par
line of perspective with the distant country, now threatened to exclude\par
the view of them; but the bluish mountains still appeared beyond the\par
dark foliage, and Emily continued to lean from the coach window, till\par
at length the closing branches shut them from her sight.\par
\par
Another object soon caught her attention. She had scarcely looked at a\par
person who walked along the bank, with his hat, in which was the\par
military feather, drawn over his eyes, before, at the sound of wheels,\par
he suddenly turned, and she perceived that it was Valancourt himself,\par
who waved his hand, sprung into the road, and through the window of the\par
carriage put a letter into her hand. He endeavoured to smile through\par
the despair that overspread his countenance as she passed on. The\par
remembrance of that smile seemed impressed on Emily’s mind for ever.\par
She leaned from the window, and saw him on a knoll of the broken bank,\par
leaning against the high trees that waved over him, and pursuing the\par
carriage with his eyes. He waved his hand, and she continued to gaze\par
till distance confused his figure, and at length another turn of the\par
road entirely separated him from her sight.\par
\par
Having stopped to take up Signor Cavigni at a château on the road, the\par
travellers, of whom Emily was disrespectfully seated with Madame\par
Montoni’s woman in a second carriage, pursued their way over the plains\par
of Languedoc. The presence of this servant restrained Emily from\par
reading Valancourt’s letter, for she did not choose to expose the\par
emotions it might occasion to the observation of any person. Yet such\par
was her wish to read this his last communication, that her trembling\par
hand was every moment on the point of breaking the seal.\par
\par
At length they reached the village, where they staid only to change\par
horses, without alighting, and it was not till they stopped to dine,\par
that Emily had an opportunity of reading the letter. Though she had\par
never doubted the sincerity of Valancourt’s affection, the fresh\par
assurances she now received of it revived her spirits; she wept over\par
his letter in tenderness, laid it by to be referred to when they should\par
be particularly depressed, and then thought of him with much less\par
anguish than she had done since they parted. Among some other requests,\par
which were interesting to her, because expressive of his tenderness,\par
and because a compliance with them seemed to annihilate for a while the\par
pain of absence, he entreated she would always think of him at sunset.\par
“You will then meet me in thought,” said he; “I shall constantly watch\par
the sunset, and I shall be happy in the belief, that your eyes are\par
fixed upon the same object with mine, and that our minds are\par
conversing. You know not, Emily, the comfort I promise myself from\par
these moments; but I trust you will experience it.”\par
\par
It is unnecessary to say with what emotion Emily, on this evening,\par
watched the declining sun, over a long extent of plains, on which she\par
saw it set without interruption, and sink towards the province which\par
Valancourt inhabited. After this hour her mind became far more tranquil\par
and resigned, than it had been since the marriage of Montoni and her\par
aunt.\par
\par
During several days the travellers journeyed over the plains of\par
Languedoc; and then entering Dauphiny, and winding for some time among\par
the mountains of that romantic province, they quitted their carriages\par
and began to ascend the Alps. And here such scenes of sublimity opened\par
upon them as no colours of language must dare to paint! Emily’s mind\par
was even so much engaged with new and wonderful images, that they\par
sometimes banished the idea of Valancourt, though they more frequently\par
revived it. These brought to her recollection the prospects among the\par
Pyrenees, which they had admired together, and had believed nothing\par
could excel in grandeur. How often did she wish to express to him the\par
new emotions which this astonishing scenery awakened, and that he could\par
partake of them! Sometimes too she endeavoured to anticipate his\par
remarks, and almost imagined him present. She seemed to have arisen\par
into another world, and to have left every trifling thought, every\par
trifling sentiment, in that below; those only of grandeur and sublimity\par
now dilated her mind, and elevated the affections of her heart.\par
\par
With what emotions of sublimity, softened by tenderness, did she meet\par
Valancourt in thought, at the customary hour of sunset, when, wandering\par
among the Alps, she watched the glorious orb sink amid their summits,\par
his last tints die away on their snowy points, and a solemn obscurity\par
steal over the scene! And when the last gleam had faded, she turned her\par
eyes from the west with somewhat of the melancholy regret that is\par
experienced after the departure of a beloved friend; while these lonely\par
feelings were heightened by the spreading gloom, and by the low sounds,\par
heard only when darkness confines attention, which make the general\par
stillness more impressive—leaves shook by the air, the last sigh of the\par
breeze that lingers after sunset, or the murmur of distant streams.\par
\par
During the first days of this journey among the Alps, the scenery\par
exhibited a wonderful mixture of solitude and inhabitation, of\par
cultivation and barrenness. On the edge of tremendous precipices, and\par
within the hollow of the cliffs, below which the clouds often floated,\par
were seen villages, spires, and convent towers; while green pastures\par
and vineyards spread their hues at the feet of perpendicular rocks of\par
marble, or of granite, whose points, tufted with alpine shrubs, or\par
exhibiting only massy crags, rose above each other, till they\par
terminated in the snowtopped mountain, whence the torrent fell, that\par
thundered along the valley.\par
\par
The snow was not yet melted on the summit of Mount Cenis, over which\par
the travellers passed; but Emily, as she looked upon its clear lake and\par
extended plain, surrounded by broken cliffs, saw, in imagination, the\par
verdant beauty it would exhibit when the snows should be gone, and the\par
shepherds, leading up the midsummer flocks from Piedmont, to pasture on\par
its flowery summit, should add Arcadian figures to Arcadian landscape.\par
\par
As she descended on the Italian side, the precipices became still more\par
tremendous, and the prospects still more wild and majestic, over which\par
the shifting lights threw all the pomp of colouring. Emily delighted to\par
observe the snowy tops of the mountains under the passing influence of\par
the day, blushing with morning, glowing with the brightness of noon, or\par
just tinted with the purple evening. The haunt of man could now only be\par
discovered by the simple hut of the shepherd and the hunter, or by the\par
rough pine bridge thrown across the torrent, to assist the latter in\par
his chase of the chamois over crags where, but for this vestige of man,\par
it would have been believed only the chamois or the wolf dared to\par
venture. As Emily gazed upon one of these perilous bridges, with the\par
cataract foaming beneath it, some images came to her mind, which she\par
afterwards combined in the following\par
\par
STORIED SONNET\par
\par
The weary traveller, who all night long\par
Has climb’d among the Alps’ tremendous steeps,\par
Skirting the pathless precipice, where throng\par
Wild forms of danger; as he onward creeps\par
If, chance, his anxious eye at distance sees\par
The mountain-shepherd’s solitary home,\par
Peeping from forth the moon-illumin’d trees,\par
What sudden transports to his bosom come!\par
But, if between some hideous chasm yawn,\par
Where the cleft pine a doubtful bridge displays,\par
In dreadful silence, on the brink, forlorn\par
He stands, and views in the faint rays\par
Far, far below, the torrent’s rising surge,\par
And listens to the wild impetuous roar;\par
Still eyes the depth, still shudders on the verge,\par
Fears to return, nor dares to venture o’er.\par
Desperate, at length the tottering plank he tries,\par
His weak steps slide, he shrieks, he sinks—he dies!\par
\par
\par
Emily, often as she travelled among the clouds, watched in silent awe\par
their billowy surges rolling below; sometimes, wholly closing upon the\par
scene, they appeared like a world of chaos, and, at others, spreading\par
thinly, they opened and admitted partial catches of the landscape—the\par
torrent, whose astounding roar had never failed, tumbling down the\par
rocky chasm, huge cliffs white with snow, or the dark summits of the\par
pine forests, that stretched mid-way down the mountains. But who may\par
describe her rapture, when, having passed through a sea of vapour, she\par
caught a first view of Italy; when, from the ridge of one of those\par
tremendous precipices that hang upon Mount Cenis and guard the entrance\par
of that enchanting country, she looked down through the lower clouds,\par
and, as they floated away, saw the grassy vales of Piedmont at her\par
feet, and, beyond, the plains of Lombardy extending to the farthest\par
distance, at which appeared, on the faint horizon, the doubtful towers\par
of Turin?\par
\par
The solitary grandeur of the objects that immediately surrounded her,\par
the mountain-region towering above, the deep precipices that fell\par
beneath, the waving blackness of the forests of pine and oak, which\par
skirted their feet, or hung within their recesses, the headlong\par
torrents that, dashing among their cliffs, sometimes appeared like a\par
cloud of mist, at others like a sheet of ice—these were features which\par
received a higher character of sublimity from the reposing beauty of\par
the Italian landscape below, stretching to the wide horizon, where the\par
same melting blue tint seemed to unite earth and sky.\par
\par
Madame Montoni only shuddered as she looked down precipices near whose\par
edge the chairmen trotted lightly and swiftly, almost, as the chamois\par
bounded, and from which Emily too recoiled; but with her fears were\par
mingled such various emotions of delight, such admiration,\par
astonishment, and awe, as she had never experienced before.\par
\par
Meanwhile the carriers, having come to a landing-place, stopped to\par
rest, and the travellers being seated on the point of a cliff, Montoni\par
and Cavigni renewed a dispute concerning Hannibal’s passage over the\par
Alps, Montoni contending that he entered Italy by way of Mount Cenis,\par
and Cavigni, that he passed over Mount St. Bernard. The subject brought\par
to Emily’s imagination the disasters he had suffered in this bold and\par
perilous adventure. She saw his vast armies winding among the defiles,\par
and over the tremendous cliffs of the mountains, which at night were\par
lighted up by his fires, or by the torches which he caused to be\par
carried when he pursued his indefatigable march. In the eye of fancy,\par
she perceived the gleam of arms through the duskiness of night, the\par
glitter of spears and helmets, and the banners floating dimly on the\par
twilight; while now and then the blast of a distant trumpet echoed\par
along the defile, and the signal was answered by a momentary clash of\par
arms. She looked with horror upon the mountaineers, perched on the\par
higher cliffs, assailing the troops below with broken fragments of the\par
mountain; on soldiers and elephants tumbling headlong down the lower\par
precipices; and, as she listened to the rebounding rocks, that followed\par
their fall, the terrors of fancy yielded to those of reality, and she\par
shuddered to behold herself on the dizzy height, whence she had\par
pictured the descent of others.\par
\par
Madame Montoni, meantime, as she looked upon Italy, was contemplating\par
in imagination the splendour of palaces and the grandeur of castles,\par
such as she believed she was going to be mistress of at Venice and in\par
the Apennine, and she became, in idea, little less than a princess.\par
Being no longer under the alarms which had deterred her from giving\par
entertainments to the beauties of Thoulouse, whom Montoni had mentioned\par
with more _eclât_ to his own vanity than credit to their discretion, or\par
regard to truth, she determined to give concerts, though she had\par
neither ear nor taste for music; _conversazione_, though she had no\par
talents for conversation; and to outvie, if possible, in the gaieties\par
of her parties and the magnificence of her liveries, all the noblesse\par
of Venice. This blissful reverie was somewhat obscured, when she\par
recollected the Signor, her husband, who, though he was not averse to\par
the profit which sometimes results from such parties, had always shown\par
a contempt of the frivolous parade that sometimes attends them; till\par
she considered that his pride might be gratified by displaying, among\par
his own friends, in his native city, the wealth which he had neglected\par
in France; and she courted again the splendid illusions that had\par
charmed her before.\par
\par
The travellers, as they descended, gradually, exchanged the region of\par
winter for the genial warmth and beauty of spring. The sky began to\par
assume that serene and beautiful tint peculiar to the climate of Italy;\par
patches of young verdure, fragrant shrubs and flowers looked gaily\par
among the rocks, often fringing their rugged brows, or hanging in tufts\par
from their broken sides; and the buds of the oak and mountain ash were\par
expanding into foliage. Descending lower, the orange and the myrtle,\par
every now and then, appeared in some sunny nook, with their yellow\par
blossoms peeping from among the dark green of their leaves, and\par
mingling with the scarlet flowers of the pomegranate and the paler ones\par
of the arbutus, that ran mantling to the crags above; while, lower\par
still, spread the pastures of Piedmont, where early flocks were\par
cropping the luxuriant herbage of spring.\par
\par
The river Doria, which, rising on the summit of Mount Cenis, had dashed\par
for many leagues over the precipices that bordered the road, now began\par
to assume a less impetuous, though scarcely less romantic character, as\par
it approached the green valleys of Piedmont, into which the travellers\par
descended with the evening sun; and Emily found herself once more amid\par
the tranquil beauty of pastoral scenery; among flocks and herds, and\par
slopes tufted with woods of lively verdure and with beautiful shrubs,\par
such as she had often seen waving luxuriantly over the alps above. The\par
verdure of the pasturage, now varied with the hues of early flowers,\par
among which were yellow ranunculuses and pansey violets of delicious\par
fragrance, she had never seen excelled.—Emily almost wished to become a\par
peasant of Piedmont, to inhabit one of the pleasant embowered cottages\par
which she saw peeping beneath the cliffs, and to pass her careless\par
hours among these romantic landscapes. To the hours, the months, she\par
was to pass under the dominion of Montoni, she looked with\par
apprehension; while those which were departed she remembered with\par
regret and sorrow.\par
\par
In the present scenes her fancy often gave her the figure of\par
Valancourt, whom she saw on a point of the cliffs, gazing with awe and\par
admiration on the imagery around him; or wandering pensively along the\par
vale below, frequently pausing to look back upon the scenery, and then,\par
his countenance glowing with the poet’s fire, pursuing his way to some\par
overhanging heights. When she again considered the time and the\par
distance that were to separate them, that every step she now took\par
lengthened this distance, her heart sunk, and the surrounding landscape\par
charmed her no more.\par
\par
The travellers, passing Novalesa, reached, after the evening had\par
closed, the small and ancient town of Susa, which had formerly guarded\par
this pass of the Alps into Piedmont. The heights which command it had,\par
since the invention of artillery, rendered its fortifications useless;\par
but these romantic heights, seen by moonlight, with the town below,\par
surrounded by its walls and watchtowers, and partially illumined,\par
exhibited an interesting picture to Emily. Here they rested for the\par
night at an inn, which had little accommodation to boast of; but the\par
travellers brought with them the hunger that gives delicious flavour to\par
the coarsest viands, and the weariness that ensures repose; and here\par
Emily first caught a strain of Italian music, on Italian ground. As she\par
sat after supper at a little window, that opened upon the country,\par
observing an effect of the moonlight on the broken surface of the\par
mountains, and remembering that on such a night as this she once had\par
sat with her father and Valancourt, resting upon a cliff of the\par
Pyrenees, she heard from below the long-drawn notes of a violin, of\par
such tone and delicacy of expression, as harmonised exactly with the\par
tender emotions she was indulging, and both charmed and surprised her.\par
Cavigni, who approached the window, smiled at her surprise. “This is\par
nothing extraordinary,” said he, “you will hear the same, perhaps, at\par
every inn on our way. It is one of our landlord’s family who plays, I\par
doubt not,” Emily, as she listened, thought he could be scarcely less\par
than a professor of music whom she heard; and the sweet and plaintive\par
strains soon lulled her into a reverie, from which she was very\par
unwillingly roused by the raillery of Cavigni, and by the voice of\par
Montoni, who gave orders to a servant to have the carriages ready at an\par
early hour on the following morning; and added, that he meant to dine\par
at Turin.\par
\par
Madame Montoni was exceedingly rejoiced to be once more on level\par
ground; and, after giving a long detail of the various terrors she had\par
suffered, which she forgot that she was describing to the companions of\par
her dangers, she added a hope, that she should soon be beyond the view\par
of these horrid mountains, “which all the world,” said she, “should not\par
tempt me to cross again.” Complaining of fatigue she soon retired to\par
rest, and Emily withdrew to her own room, when she understood from\par
Annette, her aunt’s woman, that Cavigni was nearly right in his\par
conjecture concerning the musician, who had awakened the violin with so\par
much taste, for that he was the son of a peasant inhabiting the\par
neighbouring valley. “He is going to the Carnival at Venice,” added\par
Annette, “for they say he has a fine hand at playing, and will get a\par
world of money; and the Carnival is just going to begin: but for my\par
part, I should like to live among these pleasant woods and hills,\par
better than in a town; and they say Ma’amselle, we shall see no woods,\par
or hills, or fields, at Venice, for that it is built in the very middle\par
of the sea.”\par
\par
Emily agreed with the talkative Annette, that this young man was making\par
a change for the worse, and could not forbear silently lamenting, that\par
he should be drawn from the innocence and beauty of these scenes, to\par
the corrupt ones of that voluptuous city.\par
\par
When she was alone, unable to sleep, the landscapes of her native home,\par
with Valancourt, and the circumstances of her departure, haunted her\par
fancy; she drew pictures of social happiness amidst the grand\par
simplicity of nature, such as she feared she had bade farewell to for\par
ever; and then, the idea of this young Piedmontese, thus ignorantly\par
sporting with his happiness, returned to her thoughts, and, glad to\par
escape a while from the pressure of nearer interests, she indulged her\par
fancy in composing the following lines.\par
\par
THE PIEDMONTESE\par
\par
Ah, merry swain, who laugh’d along the vales,\par
And with your gay pipe made the mountains ring,\par
Why leave your cot, your woods, and thymy gales,\par
And friends belov’d, for aught that wealth can bring?\par
He goes to wake o’er moonlight seas the string,\par
Venetian gold his untaught fancy hails!\par
Yet oft of home his simple carols sing,\par
And his steps pause, as the last Alp he scales.\par
Once more he turns to view his native scene—\par
Far, far below, as roll the clouds away,\par
He spies his cabin ’mid the pine-tops green,\par
The well-known woods, clear brook, and pastures gay;\par
And thinks of friends and parents left behind,\par
Of sylvan revels, dance, and festive song;\par
And hears the faint reed swelling in the wind;\par
And his sad sighs the distant notes prolong!\par
Thus went the swain, till mountain-shadows fell,\par
And dimm’d the landscape to his aching sight;\par
And must he leave the vales he loves so well!\par
Can foreign wealth, and shows, his heart delight?\par
No, happy vales! your wild rocks still shall hear\par
His pipe, light sounding on the morning breeze;\par
Still shall he lead the flocks to streamlet clear,\par
And watch at eve beneath the western trees.\par
Away, Venetian gold—your charm is o’er!\par
And now his swift step seeks the lowland bow’rs,\par
Where, through the leaves, his cottage light _once more_\par
Guides him to happy friends, and jocund hours.\par
Ah, merry swain! that laugh along the vales,\par
And with your gay pipe make the mountains ring,\par
Your cot, your woods, your thymy-scented gales—\par
And friends belov’d, more joy than wealth can bring!\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER II\par
\par
_Titania_.  If you will patiently dance in our round,\par
And see our moonlight revels, go with us.\par
                    MIDSUMMER NIGHT’S DREAM\par
\par
\par
Early on the following morning, the travellers set out for Turin. The\par
luxuriant plain, that extends from the feet of the Alps to that\par
magnificent city, was not then, as now, shaded by an avenue of trees\par
nine miles in length; but plantations of olives, mulberry and palms,\par
festooned with vines, mingled with the pastoral scenery, through with\par
the rapid Po, after its descent from the mountains, wandered to meet\par
the humble Doria at Turin. As they advanced towards this city, the\par
Alps, seen at some distance, began to appear in all their awful\par
sublimity; chain rising over chain in long succession, their higher\par
points darkened by the hovering clouds, sometimes hid, and at others\par
seen shooting up far above them; while their lower steeps, broken into\par
fantastic forms, were touched with blue and purplish tints, which, as\par
they changed in light and shade, seemed to open new scenes to the eye.\par
To the east stretched the plains of Lombardy, with the towers of Turin\par
rising at a distance; and beyond, the Apennines, bounding the horizon.\par
\par
The general magnificence of that city, with its vistas of churches and\par
palaces, branching from the grand square, each opening to a landscape\par
of the distant Alps or Apennines, was not only such as Emily had never\par
seen in France, but such as she had never imagined.\par
\par
Montoni, who had been often at Turin, and cared little about views of\par
any kind, did not comply with his wife’s request, that they might\par
survey some of the palaces; but staying only till the necessary\par
refreshments could be obtained, they set forward for Venice with all\par
possible rapidity. Montoni’s manner, during this journey, was grave,\par
and even haughty; and towards Madame Montoni he was more especially\par
reserved; but it was not the reserve of respect so much as of pride and\par
discontent. Of Emily he took little notice. With Cavigni his\par
conversations were commonly on political or military topics, such as\par
the convulsed state of their country rendered at this time particularly\par
interesting, Emily observed, that, at the mention of any daring\par
exploit, Montoni’s eyes lost their sullenness, and seemed\par
instantaneously to gleam with fire; yet they still retained somewhat of\par
a lurking cunning, and she sometimes thought that their fire partook\par
more of the glare of malice than the brightness of valour, though the\par
latter would well have harmonised with the high chivalric air of his\par
figure, in which Cavigni, with all his gay and gallant manners, was his\par
inferior.\par
\par
On entering the Milanese, the gentlemen exchanged their French hats for\par
the Italian cap of scarlet cloth, embroidered; and Emily was somewhat\par
surprised to observe, that Montoni added to his the military plume,\par
while Cavigni retained only the feather: which was usually worn with\par
such caps: but she at length concluded, that Montoni assumed this\par
ensign of a soldier for convenience, as a means of passing with more\par
safety through a country over-run with parties of the military.\par
\par
Over the beautiful plains of this country the devastations of war were\par
frequently visible. Where the lands had not been suffered to lie\par
uncultivated, they were often tracked with the steps of the spoiler;\par
the vines were torn down from the branches that had supported them, the\par
olives trampled upon the ground, and even the groves of mulberry trees\par
had been hewn by the enemy to light fires that destroyed the hamlets\par
and villages of their owners. Emily turned her eyes with a sigh from\par
these painful vestiges of contention, to the Alps of the Grison, that\par
overlooked them to the north, whose awful solitudes seemed to offer to\par
persecuted man a secure asylum.\par
\par
The travellers frequently distinguished troops of soldiers moving at a\par
distance; and they experienced, at the little inns on the road, the\par
scarcity of provision and other inconveniences, which are a part of the\par
consequence of intestine war; but they had never reason to be much\par
alarmed for their immediate safety, and they passed on to Milan with\par
little interruption of any kind, where they staid not to survey the\par
grandeur of the city, or even to view its vast cathedral, which was\par
then building.\par
\par
Beyond Milan, the country wore the aspect of a ruder devastation; and\par
though everything seemed now quiet, the repose was like that of death,\par
spread over features, which retain the impression of the last\par
convulsions.\par
\par
It was not till they had passed the eastern limits of the Milanese,\par
that the travellers saw any troops since they had left Milan, when, as\par
the evening was drawing to a close, they descried what appeared to be\par
an army winding onward along the distant plains, whose spears and other\par
arms caught the last rays of the sun. As the column advanced through a\par
part of the road, contracted between two hillocks, some of the\par
commanders, on horseback, were distinguished on a small eminence,\par
pointing and making signals for the march; while several of the\par
officers were riding along the line directing its progress, according\par
to the signs communicated by those above; and others, separating from\par
the vanguard, which had emerged from the pass, were riding carelessly\par
along the plains at some distance to the right of the army.\par
\par
As they drew nearer, Montoni, distinguishing the feathers that waved in\par
their caps, and the banners and liveries of the bands that followed\par
them, thought he knew this to be the small army commanded by the famous\par
captain Utaldo, with whom, as well as with some of the other chiefs, he\par
was personally acquainted. He, therefore, gave orders that the\par
carriages should draw up by the side of the road, to await their\par
arrival, and give them the pass. A faint strain of martial music now\par
stole by, and, gradually strengthening as the troops approached, Emily\par
distinguished the drums and trumpets, with the clash of cymbals and of\par
arms, that were struck by a small party, in time to the march.\par
\par
Montoni being now certain that these were the bands of the victorious\par
Utaldo, leaned from the carriage window, and hailed their general by\par
waving his cap in the air; which compliment the chief returned by\par
raising his spear, and then letting it down again suddenly, while some\par
of his officers, who were riding at a distance from the troops, came up\par
to the carriage, and saluted Montoni as an old acquaintance. The\par
captain himself soon after arriving, his bands halted while he\par
conversed with Montoni, whom he appeared much rejoiced to see; and from\par
what he said, Emily understood that this was a victorious army,\par
returning into their own principality; while the numerous waggons, that\par
accompanied them, contained the rich spoils of the enemy, their own\par
wounded soldiers, and the prisoners they had taken in battle, who were\par
to be ransomed when the peace, then negociating between the\par
neighbouring states, should be ratified. The chiefs on the following\par
day were to separate, and each, taking his share of the spoil, was to\par
return with his own band to his castle. This was therefore to be an\par
evening of uncommon and general festivity, in commemoration of the\par
victory they had accomplished together, and of the farewell which the\par
commanders were about to take of each other.\par
\par
Emily, as these officers conversed with Montoni, observed with\par
admiration, tinctured with awe, their high martial air, mingled with\par
the haughtiness of the noblesse of those days, and heightened by the\par
gallantry of their dress, by the plumes towering on their caps, the\par
armorial coat, Persian sash, and ancient Spanish cloak. Utaldo, telling\par
Montoni that his army were going to encamp for the night near a village\par
at only a few miles distance, invited him to turn back and partake of\par
their festivity, assuring the ladies also, that they should be\par
pleasantly accommodated; but Montoni excused himself, adding, that it\par
was his design to reach Verona that evening; and, after some\par
conversation concerning the state of the country towards that city,\par
they parted.\par
\par
The travellers proceeded without any interruption; but it was some\par
hours after sunset before they arrived at Verona, whose beautiful\par
environs were therefore not seen by Emily till the following morning;\par
when, leaving that pleasant town at an early hour, they set off for\par
Padua, where they embarked on the Brenta for Venice. Here the scene was\par
entirely changed; no vestiges of war, such as had deformed the plains\par
of the Milanese, appeared; on the contrary, all was peace and elegance.\par
The verdant banks of the Brenta exhibited a continued landscape of\par
beauty, gaiety, and splendour. Emily gazed with admiration on the\par
villas of the Venetian noblesse, with their cool porticos and\par
colonnades, overhung with poplars and cypresses of majestic height and\par
lively verdure; on their rich orangeries, whose blossoms perfumed the\par
air, and on the luxuriant willows, that dipped their light leaves in\par
the wave, and sheltered from the sun the gay parties whose music came\par
at intervals on the breeze. The Carnival did, indeed, appear to extend\par
from Venice along the whole line of these enchanting shores; the river\par
was gay with boats passing to that city, exhibiting the fantastic\par
diversity of a masquerade in the dresses of the people within them;\par
and, towards evening, groups of dancers frequently were seen beneath\par
the trees.\par
\par
Cavigni, meanwhile, informed her of the names of the noblemen to whom\par
the several villas they passed belonged, adding light sketches of their\par
characters, such as served to amuse rather than to inform, exhibiting\par
his own wit instead of the delineation of truth. Emily was sometimes\par
diverted by his conversation; but his gaiety did not entertain Madame\par
Montoni, as it had formerly done; she was frequently grave, and Montoni\par
retained his usual reserve.\par
\par
Nothing could exceed Emily’s admiration on her first view of Venice,\par
with its islets, palaces, and towers rising out of the sea, whose clear\par
surface reflected the tremulous picture in all its colours. The sun,\par
sinking in the west, tinted the waves and the lofty mountains of\par
Friuli, which skirt the northern shores of the Adriatic, with a saffron\par
glow, while on the marble porticos and colonnades of St. Mark were\par
thrown the rich lights and shades of evening. As they glided on, the\par
grander features of this city appeared more distinctly: its terraces,\par
crowned with airy yet majestic fabrics, touched, as they now were, with\par
the splendour of the setting sun, appeared as if they had been called\par
up from the ocean by the wand of an enchanter, rather than reared by\par
mortal hands.\par
\par
The sun, soon after, sinking to the lower world, the shadow of the\par
earth stole gradually over the waves, and then up the towering sides of\par
the mountains of Friuli, till it extinguished even the last upward\par
beams that had lingered on their summits, and the melancholy purple of\par
evening drew over them, like a thin veil. How deep, how beautiful was\par
the tranquillity that wrapped the scene! All nature seemed to repose;\par
the finest emotions of the soul were alone awake. Emily’s eyes filled\par
with tears of admiration and sublime devotion, as she raised them over\par
the sleeping world to the vast heavens, and heard the notes of solemn\par
music, that stole over the waters from a distance. She listened in\par
still rapture, and no person of the party broke the charm by an\par
enquiry. The sounds seemed to grow on the air; for so smoothly did the\par
barge glide along, that its motion was not perceivable, and the fairy\par
city appeared approaching to welcome the strangers. They now\par
distinguished a female voice, accompanied by a few instruments, singing\par
a soft and mournful air; and its fine expression, as sometimes it\par
seemed pleading with the impassioned tenderness of love, and then\par
languishing into the cadence of hopeless grief, declared, that it\par
flowed from no feigned sensibility. Ah! thought Emily, as she sighed\par
and remembered Valancourt, those strains come from the heart!\par
\par
She looked round, with anxious enquiry; the deep twilight, that had\par
fallen over the scene, admitted only imperfect images to the eye, but,\par
at some distance on the sea, she thought she perceived a gondola: a\par
chorus of voices and instruments now swelled on the air—so sweet, so\par
solemn! it seemed like the hymn of angels descending through the\par
silence of night! Now it died away, and fancy almost beheld the holy\par
choir reascending towards heaven; then again it swelled with the\par
breeze, trembled awhile, and again died into silence. It brought to\par
Emily’s recollection some lines of her late father, and she repeated in\par
a low voice,\par
\par
Oft I hear,\par
Upon the silence of the midnight air,\par
Celestial voices swell in holy chorus\par
That bears the soul to heaven!\par
\par
\par
The deep stillness, that succeeded, was as expressive as the strain\par
that had just ceased. It was uninterrupted for several minutes, till a\par
general sigh seemed to release the company from their enchantment.\par
Emily, however, long indulged the pleasing sadness, that had stolen\par
upon her spirits; but the gay and busy scene that appeared, as the\par
barge approached St. Mark’s Place, at length roused her attention. The\par
rising moon, which threw a shadowy light upon the terraces, and\par
illumined the porticos and magnificent arcades that crowned them,\par
discovered the various company, whose light steps, soft guitars, and\par
softer voices, echoed through the colonnades.\par
\par
The music they heard before now passed Montoni’s barge, in one of the\par
gondolas, of which several were seen skimming along the moonlight sea,\par
full of gay parties, catching the cool breeze. Most of these had music,\par
made sweeter by the waves over which it floated, and by the measured\par
sound of oars, as they dashed the sparkling tide. Emily gazed, and\par
listened, and thought herself in a fairy scene; even Madame Montoni was\par
pleased; Montoni congratulated himself on his return to Venice, which\par
he called the first city in the world, and Cavigni was more gay and\par
animated than ever.\par
\par
The barge passed on to the grand canal, where Montoni’s mansion was\par
situated. And here, other forms of beauty and of grandeur, such as her\par
imagination had never painted, were unfolded to Emily in the palaces of\par
Sansovino and Palladio, as she glided along the waves. The air bore no\par
sounds, but those of sweetness, echoing along each margin of the canal,\par
and from gondolas on its surface, while groups of masks were seen\par
dancing on the moonlight terraces, and seemed almost to realise the\par
romance of fairyland.\par
\par
The barge stopped before the portico of a large house, from whence a\par
servant of Montoni crossed the terrace, and immediately the party\par
disembarked. From the portico they passed a noble hall to a staircase\par
of marble, which led to a saloon, fitted up in a style of magnificence\par
that surprised Emily. The walls and ceilings were adorned with\par
historical and allegorical paintings, in _fresco_; silver tripods,\par
depending from chains of the same metal, illumined the apartment, the\par
floor of which was covered with Indian mats painted in a variety of\par
colours and devices; the couches and drapery of the lattices were of\par
pale green silk, embroidered and fringed with green and gold. Balcony\par
lattices opened upon the grand canal, whence rose a confusion of voices\par
and of musical instruments, and the breeze that gave freshness to the\par
apartment. Emily, considering the gloomy temper of Montoni, looked upon\par
the splendid furniture of this house with surprise, and remembered the\par
report of his being a man of broken fortune, with astonishment. “Ah!”\par
said she to herself, “if Valancourt could but see this mansion, what\par
peace would it give him! He would then be convinced that the report was\par
groundless.”\par
\par
Madame Montoni seemed to assume the air of a princess; but Montoni was\par
restless and discontented, and did not even observe the civility of\par
bidding her welcome to her home.\par
\par
Soon after his arrival, he ordered his gondola, and, with Cavigni, went\par
out to mingle in the scenes of the evening. Madame then became serious\par
and thoughtful. Emily, who was charmed with everything she saw,\par
endeavoured to enliven her; but reflection had not, with Madame\par
Montoni, subdued caprice and ill-humour, and her answers discovered so\par
much of both, that Emily gave up the attempt of diverting her, and\par
withdrew to a lattice, to amuse herself with the scene without, so new\par
and so enchanting.\par
\par
The first object that attracted her notice was a group of dancers on\par
the terrace below, led by a guitar and some other instruments. The\par
girl, who struck the guitar, and another, who flourished a tambourine,\par
passed on in a dancing step, and with a light grace and gaiety of\par
heart, that would have subdued the goddess of spleen in her worst\par
humour. After these came a group of fantastic figures, some dressed as\par
gondolieri, others as minstrels, while others seemed to defy all\par
description. They sung in parts, their voices accompanied by a few soft\par
instruments. At a little distance from the portico they stopped, and\par
Emily distinguished the verses of Ariosto. They sung of the wars of the\par
Moors against Charlemagne, and then of the woes of Orlando: afterwards\par
the measure changed, and the melancholy sweetness of Petrarch\par
succeeded. The magic of his grief was assisted by all that Italian\par
music and Italian expression, heightened by the enchantments of\par
Venetian moonlight, could give.\par
\par
Emily, as she listened, caught the pensive enthusiasm; her tears flowed\par
silently, while her fancy bore her far away to France and to\par
Valancourt. Each succeeding sonnet, more full of charming sadness than\par
the last, seemed to bind the spell of melancholy: with extreme regret\par
she saw the musicians move on, and her attention followed the strain\par
till the last faint warble died in air. She then remained sunk in that\par
pensive tranquillity which soft music leaves on the mind—a state like\par
that produced by the view of a beautiful landscape by moonlight, or by\par
the recollection of scenes marked with the tenderness of friends lost\par
for ever, and with sorrows, which time has mellowed into mild regret.\par
Such scenes are indeed, to the mind, like “those faint traces which the\par
memory bears of music that is past.”\par
\par
Other sounds soon awakened her attention: it was the solemn harmony of\par
horns, that swelled from a distance; and, observing the gondolas\par
arrange themselves along the margin of the terraces, she threw on her\par
veil, and, stepping into the balcony, discerned, in the distant\par
perspective of the canal, something like a procession, floating on the\par
light surface of the water: as it approached, the horns and other\par
instruments mingled sweetly, and soon after the fabled deities of the\par
city seemed to have arisen from the ocean; for Neptune, with Venice\par
personified as his queen, came on the undulating waves, surrounded by\par
tritons and sea-nymphs. The fantastic splendour of this spectacle,\par
together with the grandeur of the surrounding palaces, appeared like\par
the vision of a poet suddenly embodied, and the fanciful images, which\par
it awakened in Emily’s mind, lingered there long after the procession\par
had passed away. She indulged herself in imagining what might be the\par
manners and delights of a sea-nymph, till she almost wished to throw\par
off the habit of mortality, and plunge into the green wave to\par
participate them.\par
\par
“How delightful,” said she, “to live amidst the coral bowers and\par
crystal caverns of the ocean, with my sister nymphs, and listen to the\par
sounding waters above, and to the soft shells of the tritons! and then,\par
after sunset, to skim on the surface of the waves round wild rocks and\par
along sequestered shores, where, perhaps, some pensive wanderer comes\par
to weep! Then would I soothe his sorrows with my sweet music, and offer\par
him from a shell some of the delicious fruit that hangs round Neptune’s\par
palace.”\par
\par
She was recalled from her reverie to a mere mortal supper, and could\par
not forbear smiling at the fancies she had been indulging, and at her\par
conviction of the serious displeasure, which Madame Montoni would have\par
expressed, could she have been made acquainted with them.\par
\par
After supper, her aunt sat late, but Montoni did not return, and she at\par
length retired to rest. If Emily had admired the magnificence of the\par
saloon, she was not less surprised, on observing the half-furnished and\par
forlorn appearance of the apartments she passed in the way to her\par
chamber, whither she went through long suites of noble rooms, that\par
seemed, from their desolate aspect, to have been unoccupied for many\par
years. On the walls of some were the faded remains of tapestry; from\par
others, painted in fresco, the damps had almost withdrawn both colours\par
and design. At length she reached her own chamber, spacious, desolate,\par
and lofty, like the rest, with high lattices that opened towards the\par
Adriatic. It brought gloomy images to her mind, but the view of the\par
Adriatic soon gave her others more airy, among which was that of the\par
sea-nymph, whose delights she had before amused herself with picturing;\par
and, anxious to escape from serious reflections, she now endeavoured to\par
throw her fanciful ideas into a train, and concluded the hour with\par
composing the following lines:\par
\par
THE SEA-NYMPH\par
\par
Down, down a thousand fathom deep,\par
Among the sounding seas I go;\par
Play round the foot of ev’ry steep\par
Whose cliffs above the ocean grow.\par
\par
There, within their secret cares,\par
I hear the mighty rivers roar;\par
And guide their streams through Neptune’s waves\par
To bless the green earth’s inmost shore:\par
\par
And bid the freshen’d waters glide,\par
For fern-crown’d nymphs of lake, or brook,\par
Through winding woods and pastures wide,\par
And many a wild, romantic nook.\par
\par
For this the nymphs, at fall of eave,\par
Oft dance upon the flow’ry banks,\par
And sing my name, and garlands weave\par
To bear beneath the wave their thanks.\par
\par
In coral bow’rs I love to lie,\par
And hear the surges roll above,\par
And through the waters view on high\par
The proud ships sail, and gay clouds move.\par
\par
And oft at midnight’s stillest hour,\par
When summer seas the vessel lave,\par
I love to prove my charmful pow’r\par
While floating on the moonlight wave.\par
\par
And when deep sleep the crew has bound,\par
And the sad lover musing leans\par
O’er the ship’s side, I breathe around\par
Such strains as speak no mortal means!\par
\par
O’er the dim waves his searching eye\par
Sees but the vessel’s lengthen’d shade;\par
Above—the moon and azure sky;\par
Entranc’d he hears, and half afraid!\par
\par
Sometimes, a single note I swell,\par
That, softly sweet, at distance dies;\par
Then wake the magic of my shell,\par
And choral voices round me rise!\par
\par
The trembling youth, charm’d by my strain,\par
Calls up the crew, who, silent, bend\par
O’er the high deck, but list in vain;\par
My song is hush’d, my wonders end!\par
\par
Within the mountain’s woody bay,\par
Where the tall bark at anchor rides,\par
At twilight hour, with tritons gay,\par
I dance upon the lapsing tides:\par
\par
And with my sister-nymphs I sport,\par
Till the broad sun looks o’er the floods;\par
Then, swift we seek our crystal court,\par
Deep in the wave, ’mid Neptune’s woods.\par
\par
In cool arcades and glassy halls\par
We pass the sultry hours of noon,\par
Beyond wherever sunbeam falls,\par
Weaving sea-flowers in gay festoon.\par
\par
The while we chant our ditties sweet\par
To some soft shell that warbles near;\par
Join’d by the murmuring currents, fleet,\par
That glide along our halls so clear.\par
\par
There, the pale pearl and sapphire blue,\par
And ruby red, and em’rald green,\par
Dart from the domes a changing hue,\par
And sparry columns deck the scene.\par
\par
When the dark storm scowls o’er the deep,\par
And long, long peals of thunder sound,\par
On some high cliff my watch I keep\par
O’er all the restless seas around:\par
\par
Till on the ridgy wave afar\par
Comes the lone vessel, labouring slow,\par
Spreading the white foam in the air,\par
With sail and top-mast bending low.\par
\par
Then, plunge I ’mid the ocean’s roar,\par
My way by quiv’ring lightnings shown,\par
To guide the bark to peaceful shore,\par
And hush the sailor’s fearful groan.\par
\par
And if too late I reach its side\par
To save it from the ’whelming surge,\par
I call my dolphins o’er the tide,\par
To bear the crew where isles emerge.\par
\par
Their mournful spirits soon I cheer,\par
While round the desert coast I go,\par
With warbled songs they faintly hear,\par
Oft as the stormy gust sinks low.\par
\par
My music leads to lofty groves,\par
That wild upon the sea-bank wave;\par
Where sweet fruits bloom, and fresh spring roves,\par
And closing boughs the tempest brave.\par
\par
Then, from the air spirits obey\par
My potent voice they love so well,\par
And, on the clouds, paint visions gay,\par
While strains more sweet at distance swell.\par
\par
And thus the lonely hours I cheat,\par
Soothing the ship-wreck’d sailor’s heart,\par
Till from the waves the storms retreat,\par
And o’er the east the day-beams dart.\par
\par
Neptune for this oft binds me fast\par
To rocks below, with coral chain,\par
Till all the tempest’s over-past,\par
And drowning seamen cry in vain.\par
\par
Whoe’er ye are that love my lay,\par
Come, when red sunset tints the wave,\par
To the still sands, where fairies play;\par
There, in cool seas, I love to lave.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER III\par
\par
He is a great observer, and he looks\par
Quite through the deeds of men:  he loves no plays,\par
....................he hears no music;\par
Seldom he smiles; and smiles in such a sort,\par
As if he mock’d himself, and scorn’d his spirit\par
that could be mov’d to smile at anything.\par
Such men as he be never at heart’s ease,\par
While they behold a greater than themselves.\par
                    JULIUS CÆSAR\par
\par
\par
Montoni and his companion did not return home, till many hours after\par
the dawn had blushed upon the Adriatic. The airy groups, which had\par
danced all night along the colonnade of St. Mark, dispersed before the\par
morning, like so many spirits. Montoni had been otherwise engaged; his\par
soul was little susceptible of light pleasures. He delighted in the\par
energies of the passions; the difficulties and tempests of life, which\par
wreck the happiness of others, roused and strengthened all the powers\par
of his mind, and afforded him the highest enjoyments, of which his\par
nature was capable. Without some object of strong interest, life was to\par
him little more than a sleep; and, when pursuits of real interest\par
failed, he substituted artificial ones, till habit changed their\par
nature, and they ceased to be unreal. Of this kind was the habit of\par
gaming, which he had adopted, first, for the purpose of relieving him\par
from the languor of inaction, but had since pursued with the ardour of\par
passion. In this occupation he had passed the night with Cavigni and a\par
party of young men, who had more money than rank, and more vice than\par
either. Montoni despised the greater part of these for the inferiority\par
of their talents, rather than for their vicious inclinations, and\par
associated with them only to make them the instruments of his purposes.\par
Among these, however, were some of superior abilities, and a few whom\par
Montoni admitted to his intimacy, but even towards these he still\par
preserved a decisive and haughty air, which, while it imposed\par
submission on weak and timid minds, roused the fierce hatred of strong\par
ones. He had, of course, many and bitter enemies; but the rancour of\par
their hatred proved the degree of his power; and, as power was his\par
chief aim, he gloried more in such hatred, than it was possible he\par
could in being esteemed. A feeling so tempered as that of esteem, he\par
despised, and would have despised himself also had he thought himself\par
capable of being flattered by it.\par
\par
Among the few whom he distinguished, were the Signors Bertolini,\par
Orsino, and Verezzi. The first was a man of gay temper, strong\par
passions, dissipated, and of unbounded extravagance, but generous,\par
brave, and unsuspicious. Orsino was reserved, and haughty; loving power\par
more than ostentation; of a cruel and suspicious temper; quick to feel\par
an injury, and relentless in avenging it; cunning and unsearchable in\par
contrivance, patient and indefatigable in the execution of his schemes.\par
He had a perfect command of feature and of his passions, of which he\par
had scarcely any, but pride, revenge and avarice; and, in the\par
gratification of these, few considerations had power to restrain him,\par
few obstacles to withstand the depth of his stratagems. This man was\par
the chief favourite of Montoni. Verezzi was a man of some talent, of\par
fiery imagination, and the slave of alternate passions. He was gay,\par
voluptuous, and daring; yet had neither perseverance nor true courage,\par
and was meanly selfish in all his aims. Quick to form schemes, and\par
sanguine in his hope of success, he was the first to undertake, and to\par
abandon, not only his own plans, but those adopted from other persons.\par
Proud and impetuous, he revolted against all subordination; yet those\par
who were acquainted with his character, and watched the turn of his\par
passions, could lead him like a child.\par
\par
Such were the friends whom Montoni introduced to his family and his\par
table, on the day after his arrival at Venice. There were also of the\par
party a Venetian nobleman, Count Morano, and a Signora Livona, whom\par
Montoni had introduced to his wife, as a lady of distinguished merit,\par
and who, having called in the morning to welcome her to Venice, had\par
been requested to be of the dinner party.\par
\par
Madame Montoni received with a very ill grace, the compliments of the\par
Signors. She disliked them, because they were the friends of her\par
husband; hated them, because she believed they had contributed to\par
detain him abroad till so late an hour of the preceding morning; and\par
envied them, since, conscious of her own want of influence, she was\par
convinced, that he preferred their society to her own. The rank of\par
Count Morano procured him that distinction which she refused to the\par
rest of the company. The haughty sullenness of her countenance and\par
manner, and the ostentatious extravagance of her dress, for she had not\par
yet adopted the Venetian habit, were strikingly contrasted by the\par
beauty, modesty, sweetness and simplicity of Emily, who observed, with\par
more attention than pleasure, the party around her. The beauty and\par
fascinating manners of Signora Livona, however, won her involuntary\par
regard; while the sweetness of her accents and her air of gentle\par
kindness awakened with Emily those pleasing affections, which so long\par
had slumbered.\par
\par
In the cool of the evening the party embarked in Montoni’s gondola, and\par
rowed out upon the sea. The red glow of sunset still touched the waves,\par
and lingered in the west, where the melancholy gleam seemed slowly\par
expiring, while the dark blue of the upper æther began to twinkle with\par
stars. Emily sat, given up to pensive and sweet emotions. The\par
smoothness of the water, over which she glided, its reflected images—a\par
new heaven and trembling stars below the waves, with shadowy outlines\par
of towers and porticos, conspired with the stillness of the hour,\par
interrupted only by the passing wave, or the notes of distant music, to\par
raise those emotions to enthusiasm. As she listened to the measured\par
sound of the oars, and to the remote warblings that came in the breeze,\par
her softened mind returned to the memory of St. Aubert and to\par
Valancourt, and tears stole to her eyes. The rays of the moon,\par
strengthening as the shadows deepened, soon after threw a silvery gleam\par
upon her countenance, which was partly shaded by a thin black veil, and\par
touched it with inimitable softness. Hers was the _contour_ of a\par
Madona, with the sensibility of a Magdalen; and the pensive uplifted\par
eye, with the tear that glittered on her cheek, confirmed the\par
expression of the character.\par
\par
The last strain of distant music now died in air, for the gondola was\par
far upon the waves, and the party determined to have music of their\par
own. The Count Morano, who sat next to Emily, and who had been\par
observing her for some time in silence, snatched up a lute, and struck\par
the chords with the finger of harmony herself, while his voice, a fine\par
tenor, accompanied them in a rondeau full of tender sadness. To him,\par
indeed, might have been applied that beautiful exhortation of an\par
English poet, had it then existed:\par
\par
Strike up, my master,\par
But touch the strings with a religious softness!\par
Teach sounds to languish through the night’s dull ear\par
Till Melancholy starts from off her couch,\par
And Carelessness grows concert to attention!\par
\par
\par
With such powers of expression the Count sung the following\par
\par
RONDEAU\par
\par
Soft as yon silver ray, that sleeps\par
Upon the ocean’s trembling tide;\par
Soft as the air, that lightly sweeps\par
Yon sad, that swells in stately pride:\par
\par
Soft as the surge’s stealing note,\par
That dies along the distant shores,\par
Or warbled strain, that sinks remote—\par
So soft the sigh my bosom pours!\par
\par
True as the wave to Cynthia’s ray,\par
True as the vessel to the breeze,\par
True as the soul to music’s sway,\par
Or music to Venetian seas:\par
\par
Soft as yon silver beams, that sleep\par
Upon the ocean’s trembling breast;\par
So soft, so true, fond Love shall weep,\par
So soft, so true, with _thee_ shall rest.\par
\par
\par
The cadence with which he returned from the last stanza to a repetition\par
of the first; the fine modulation in which his voice stole upon the\par
first line, and the pathetic energy with which it pronounced the last,\par
were such as only exquisite taste could give. When he had concluded, he\par
gave the lute with a sigh to Emily, who, to avoid any appearance of\par
affectation, immediately began to play. She sung a melancholy little\par
air, one of the popular songs of her native province, with a simplicity\par
and pathos that made it enchanting. But its well-known melody brought\par
so forcibly to her fancy the scenes and the persons, among which she\par
had often heard it, that her spirits were overcome, her voice trembled\par
and ceased—and the strings of the lute were struck with a disordered\par
hand; till, ashamed of the emotion she had betrayed, she suddenly\par
passed on to a song so gay and airy, that the steps of the dance seemed\par
almost to echo to the notes. _Bravissimo!_ burst instantly from the\par
lips of her delighted auditors, and she was compelled to repeat the\par
air. Among the compliments that followed, those of the Count were not\par
the least audible, and they had not concluded, when Emily gave the\par
instrument to Signora Livona, whose voice accompanied it with true\par
Italian taste.\par
\par
Afterwards, the Count, Emily, Cavigni, and the Signora, sung\par
_canzonettes_, accompanied by a couple of lutes and a few other\par
instruments. Sometimes the instruments suddenly ceased, and the voices\par
dropped from the full swell of harmony into a low chant; then, after a\par
deep pause, they rose by degrees, the instruments one by one striking\par
up, till the loud and full chorus soared again to heaven!\par
\par
Meanwhile, Montoni, who was weary of this harmony, was considering how\par
he might disengage himself from his party, or withdraw with such of it\par
as would be willing to play, to a Casino. In a pause of the music, he\par
proposed returning to shore, a proposal which Orsino eagerly seconded,\par
but which the Count and the other gentlemen as warmly opposed.\par
\par
Montoni still meditated how he might excuse himself from longer\par
attendance upon the Count, for to him only he thought excuse necessary,\par
and how he might get to land, till the gondolieri of an empty boat,\par
returning to Venice, hailed his people. Without troubling himself\par
longer about an excuse, he seized this opportunity of going thither,\par
and, committing the ladies to the care of his friends, departed with\par
Orsino, while Emily, for the first time, saw him go with regret; for\par
she considered his presence a protection, though she knew not what she\par
should fear. He landed at St. Mark’s, and, hurrying to a Casino, was\par
soon lost amidst a crowd of gamesters.\par
\par
Meanwhile, the Count having secretly dispatched a servant in Montoni’s\par
boat, for his own gondola and musicians, Emily heard, without knowing\par
his project, the gay song of gondolieri approaching, as they sat on the\par
stern of the boat, and saw the tremulous gleam of the moonlight wave,\par
which their oars disturbed. Presently she heard the sound of\par
instruments, and then a full symphony swelled on the air, and, the\par
boats meeting, the gondolieri hailed each other. The Count then\par
explaining himself, the party removed into his gondola, which was\par
embellished with all that taste could bestow.\par
\par
While they partook of a collation of fruits and ice, the whole band,\par
following at a distance in the other boat, played the most sweet and\par
enchanting strains, and the Count, who had again seated himself by\par
Emily, paid her unremitted attention, and sometimes, in a low but\par
impassioned voice, uttered compliments which she could not\par
misunderstand. To avoid them she conversed with Signora Livona, and her\par
manner to the Count assumed a mild reserve, which, though dignified,\par
was too gentle to repress his assiduities: he could see, hear, speak to\par
no person, but Emily while Cavigni observed him now and then, with a\par
look of displeasure, and Emily, with one of uneasiness. She now wished\par
for nothing so much as to return to Venice, but it was near midnight\par
before the gondolas approached St. Mark’s Place, where the voice of\par
gaiety and song was loud. The busy hum of mingling sounds was heard at\par
a considerable distance on the water, and, had not a bright moonlight\par
discovered the city, with its terraces and towers, a stranger would\par
almost have credited the fabled wonders of Neptune’s court, and\par
believed, that the tumult arose from beneath the waves.\par
\par
They landed at St. Mark’s, where the gaiety of the colonnades and the\par
beauty of the night, made Madame Montoni willingly submit to the\par
Count’s solicitations to join the promenade, and afterwards to take a\par
supper with the rest of the party, at his Casino. If anything could\par
have dissipated Emily’s uneasiness, it would have been the grandeur,\par
gaiety, and novelty of the surrounding scene, adorned with Palladio’s\par
palaces, and busy with parties of masqueraders.\par
\par
At length they withdrew to the Casino, which was fitted up with\par
infinite taste, and where a splendid banquet was prepared; but here\par
Emily’s reserve made the Count perceive, that it was necessary for his\par
interest to win the favour of Madame Montoni, which, from the\par
condescension she had already shown to him, appeared to be an\par
achievement of no great difficulty. He transferred, therefore, part of\par
his attention from Emily to her aunt, who felt too much flattered by\par
the distinction even to disguise her emotion; and before the party\par
broke up, he had entirely engaged the esteem of Madame Montoni.\par
Whenever he addressed her, her ungracious countenance relaxed into\par
smiles, and to whatever he proposed she assented. He invited her, with\par
the rest of the party, to take coffee, in his box at the opera, on the\par
following evening, and Emily heard the invitation accepted, with strong\par
anxiety, concerning the means of excusing herself from attending Madame\par
Montoni thither.\par
\par
It was very late before their gondola was ordered, and Emily’s surprise\par
was extreme, when, on quitting the Casino, she beheld the broad sun\par
rising out of the Adriatic, while St. Mark’s Place was yet crowded with\par
company. Sleep had long weighed heavily on her eyes, but now the fresh\par
sea-breeze revived her, and she would have quitted the scene with\par
regret, had not the Count been present, performing the duty, which he\par
had imposed upon himself, of escorting them home. There they heard that\par
Montoni was not yet returned; and his wife, retiring in displeasure to\par
her apartment, at length released Emily from the fatigue of further\par
attendance.\par
\par
Montoni came home late in the morning, in a very ill humour, having\par
lost considerably at play, and, before he withdrew to rest, had a\par
private conference with Cavigni, whose manner, on the following day,\par
seemed to tell, that the subject of it had not been pleasing to him.\par
\par
In the evening, Madame Montoni, who, during the day, had observed a\par
sullen silence towards her husband, received visits from some Venetian\par
ladies, with whose sweet manners Emily was particularly charmed. They\par
had an air of ease and kindness towards the strangers, as if they had\par
been their familiar friends for years; and their conversation was by\par
turns tender, sentimental and gay. Madame, though she had no taste for\par
such conversation, and whose coarseness and selfishness sometimes\par
exhibited a ludicrous contrast to their excessive refinement, could not\par
remain wholly insensible to the captivations of their manner.\par
\par
In a pause of conversation, a lady who was called Signora Herminia took\par
up a lute, and began to play and sing, with as much easy gaiety, as if\par
she had been alone. Her voice was uncommonly rich in tone, and various\par
in expression; yet she appeared to be entirely unconscious of its\par
powers, and meant nothing less than to display them. She sung from the\par
gaiety of her heart, as she sat with her veil half thrown back, holding\par
gracefully the lute, under the spreading foliage and flowers of some\par
plants, that rose from baskets, and interlaced one of the lattices of\par
the saloon. Emily, retiring a little from the company, sketched her\par
figure, with the miniature scenery around her, and drew a very\par
interesting picture, which, though it would not, perhaps, have borne\par
criticism, had spirit and taste enough to awaken both the fancy and the\par
heart. When she had finished it, she presented it to the beautiful\par
original, who was delighted with the offering, as well as the sentiment\par
it conveyed, and assured Emily, with a smile of captivating sweetness,\par
that she should preserve it as a pledge of her friendship.\par
\par
In the evening Cavigni joined the ladies, but Montoni had other\par
engagements; and they embarked in the gondola for St. Mark’s, where the\par
same gay company seemed to flutter as on the preceding night. The cool\par
breeze, the glassy sea, the gentle sound of its waves, and the sweeter\par
murmur of distant music; the lofty porticos and arcades, and the happy\par
groups that sauntered beneath them; these, with every feature and\par
circumstance of the scene, united to charm Emily, no longer teased by\par
the officious attentions of Count Morano. But, as she looked upon the\par
moonlight sea, undulating along the walls of St. Mark, and, lingering\par
for a moment over those walls, caught the sweet and melancholy song of\par
some gondolier as he sat in his boat below, waiting for his master, her\par
softened mind returned to the memory of her home, of her friends, and\par
of all that was dear in her native country.\par
\par
After walking some time, they sat down at the door of a Casino, and,\par
while Cavigni was accommodating them with coffee and ice, were joined\par
by Count Morano. He sought Emily with a look of impatient delight, who,\par
remembering all the attention he had shown her on the preceding\par
evening, was compelled, as before, to shrink from his assiduities into\par
a timid reserve, except when she conversed with Signora Herminia and\par
the other ladies of her party.\par
\par
It was near midnight before they withdrew to the opera, where Emily was\par
not so charmed but that, when she remembered the scene she had just\par
quitted, she felt how infinitely inferior all the splendour of art is\par
to the sublimity of nature. Her heart was not now affected, tears of\par
admiration did not start to her eyes, as when she viewed the vast\par
expanse of ocean, the grandeur of the heavens, and listened to the\par
rolling waters, and to the faint music that, at intervals, mingled with\par
their roar. Remembering these, the scene before her faded into\par
insignificance.\par
\par
Of the evening, which passed on without any particular incident, she\par
wished the conclusion, that she might escape from the attentions of the\par
Count; and, as opposite qualities frequently attract each other in our\par
thoughts, thus Emily, when she looked on Count Morano, remembered\par
Valancourt, and a sigh sometimes followed the recollection.\par
\par
Several weeks passed in the course of customary visits, during which\par
nothing remarkable occurred. Emily was amused by the manners and scenes\par
that surrounded her, so different from those of France, but where Count\par
Morano, too frequently for her comfort, contrived to introduce himself.\par
His manner, figure and accomplishments, which were generally admired,\par
Emily would, perhaps, have admired also, had her heart been disengaged\par
from Valancourt, and had the Count forborne to persecute her with\par
officious attentions, during which she observed some traits in his\par
character, that prejudiced her against whatever might otherwise be good\par
in it.\par
\par
Soon after his arrival at Venice, Montoni received a packet from M.\par
Quesnel, in which the latter mentioned the death of his wife’s uncle,\par
at his villa on the Brenta; and that, in consequence of this event, he\par
should hasten to take possession of that estate and of other effects\par
bequeathed to him. This uncle was the brother of Madame Quesnel’s late\par
mother; Montoni was related to her by the father’s side, and though he\par
could have had neither claim nor expectation concerning these\par
possessions, he could scarcely conceal the envy which M. Quesnel’s\par
letter excited.\par
\par
Emily had observed with concern, that, since they left France, Montoni\par
had not even affected kindness towards her aunt, and that, after\par
treating her, at first, with neglect, he now met her with uniform\par
ill-humour and reserve. She had never supposed, that her aunt’s foibles\par
could have escaped the discernment of Montoni, or that her mind or\par
figure were of a kind to deserve his attention. Her surprise,\par
therefore, at this match, had been extreme; but since he had made the\par
choice, she did not suspect that he would so openly have discovered his\par
contempt of it. But Montoni, who had been allured by the seeming wealth\par
of Madame Cheron, was now severely disappointed by her comparative\par
poverty, and highly exasperated by the deceit she had employed to\par
conceal it, till concealment was no longer necessary. He had been\par
deceived in an affair, wherein he meant to be the deceiver; out-witted\par
by the superior cunning of a woman, whose understanding he despised,\par
and to whom he had sacrificed his pride and his liberty, without saving\par
himself from the ruin, which had impended over his head. Madame Montoni\par
had contrived to have the greatest part of what she really did possess,\par
settled upon herself: what remained, though it was totally inadequate\par
both to her husband’s expectations, and to his necessities, he had\par
converted into money, and brought with him to Venice, that he might a\par
little longer delude society, and make a last effort to regain the\par
fortunes he had lost.\par
\par
The hints which had been thrown out to Valancourt, concerning Montoni’s\par
character and condition, were too true; but it was now left to time and\par
occasion, to unfold the circumstances, both of what had, and of what\par
had not been hinted, and to time and occasion we commit them.\par
\par
Madame Montoni was not of a nature to bear injuries with meekness, or\par
to resent them with dignity: her exasperated pride displayed itself in\par
all the violence and acrimony of a little, or at least of an\par
ill-regulated mind. She would not acknowledge, even to herself, that\par
she had in any degree provoked contempt by her duplicity, but weakly\par
persisted in believing, that she alone was to be pitied, and Montoni\par
alone to be censured; for, as her mind had naturally little perception\par
of moral obligation, she seldom understood its force but when it\par
happened to be violated towards herself: her vanity had already been\par
severely shocked by a discovery of Montoni’s contempt; it remained to\par
be farther reproved by a discovery of his circumstances. His mansion at\par
Venice, though its furniture discovered a part of the truth to\par
unprejudiced persons, told nothing to those who were blinded by a\par
resolution to believe whatever they wished. Madame Montoni still\par
thought herself little less than a princess, possessing a palace at\par
Venice, and a castle among the Apennines. To the castle di Udolpho,\par
indeed, Montoni sometimes talked of going for a few weeks to examine\par
into its condition, and to receive some rents; for it appeared that he\par
had not been there for two years, and that, during this period, it had\par
been inhabited only by an old servant, whom he called his steward.\par
\par
Emily listened to the mention of this journey with pleasure, for she\par
not only expected from it new ideas, but a release from the persevering\par
assiduities of Count Morano. In the country, too, she would have\par
leisure to think of Valancourt, and to indulge the melancholy, which\par
his image, and a recollection of the scenes of La Vallée, always\par
blessed with the memory of her parents, awakened. The ideal scenes were\par
dearer, and more soothing to her heart, than all the splendour of gay\par
assemblies; they were a kind of talisman that expelled the poison of\par
temporary evils, and supported her hopes of happy days: they appeared\par
like a beautiful landscape, lighted up by a gleam of sunshine, and seen\par
through a perspective of dark and rugged rocks.\par
\par
But Count Morano did not long confine himself to silent assiduities; he\par
declared his passion to Emily, and made proposals to Montoni, who\par
encouraged, though Emily rejected, him: with Montoni for his friend,\par
and an abundance of vanity to delude him, he did not despair of\par
success. Emily was astonished and highly disgusted at his perseverance,\par
after she had explained her sentiments with a frankness that would not\par
allow him to misunderstand them.\par
\par
He now passed the greater part of his time at Montoni’s, dining there\par
almost daily, and attending Madame and Emily wherever they went; and\par
all this, notwithstanding the uniform reserve of Emily, whose aunt\par
seemed as anxious as Montoni to promote this marriage; and would never\par
dispense with her attendance at any assembly where the Count proposed\par
to be present.\par
\par
Montoni now said nothing of his intended journey, of which Emily waited\par
impatiently to hear; and he was seldom at home but when the Count, or\par
Signor Orsino, was there, for between himself and Cavigni a coolness\par
seemed to subsist, though the latter remained in his house. With\par
Orsino, Montoni was frequently closeted for hours together, and,\par
whatever might be the business, upon which they consulted, it appeared\par
to be of consequence, since Montoni often sacrificed to it his\par
favourite passion for play, and remained at home the whole night. There\par
was somewhat of privacy, too, in the manner of Orsino’s visits, which\par
had never before occurred, and which excited not only surprise, but\par
some degree of alarm in Emily’s mind, who had unwillingly discovered\par
much of his character when he had most endeavoured to disguise it.\par
After these visits, Montoni was often more thoughtful than usual;\par
sometimes the deep workings of his mind entirely abstracted him from\par
surrounding objects, and threw a gloom over his visage that rendered it\par
terrible; at others, his eyes seemed almost to flash fire, and all the\par
energies of his soul appeared to be roused for some great enterprise.\par
Emily observed these written characters of his thoughts with deep\par
interest, and not without some degree of awe, when she considered that\par
she was entirely in his power; but forbore even to hint her fears, or\par
her observations, to Madame Montoni, who discerned nothing in her\par
husband, at these times, but his usual sternness.\par
\par
A second letter from M. Quesnel announced the arrival of himself and\par
his lady at the Villa Miarenti; stated several circumstances of his\par
good fortune, respecting the affair that had brought him into Italy;\par
and concluded with an earnest request to see Montoni, his wife and\par
niece, at his new estate.\par
\par
Emily received, about the same period, a much more interesting letter,\par
and which soothed for a while every anxiety of her heart. Valancourt,\par
hoping she might be still at Venice, had trusted a letter to the\par
ordinary post, that told her of his health, and of his unceasing and\par
anxious affection. He had lingered at Thoulouse for some time after her\par
departure, that he might indulge the melancholy pleasure of wandering\par
through the scenes where he had been accustomed to behold her, and had\par
thence gone to his brother’s château, which was in the neighbourhood of\par
La Vallée. Having mentioned this, he added, “If the duty of attending\par
my regiment did not require my departure, I know not when I should have\par
resolution enough to quit the neighbourhood of a place which is\par
endeared by the remembrance of you. The vicinity to La Vallée has alone\par
detained me thus long at Estuvière: I frequently ride thither early in\par
the morning, that I may wander, at leisure, through the day, among\par
scenes, which were once your home, where I have been accustomed to see\par
you, and to hear you converse. I have renewed my acquaintance with the\par
good old Theresa, who rejoiced to see me, that she might talk of you: I\par
need not say how much this circumstance attached me to her, or how\par
eagerly I listened to her upon her favourite subject. You will guess\par
the motive that first induced me to make myself known to Theresa: it\par
was, indeed, no other than that of gaining admittance into the château\par
and gardens, which my Emily had so lately inhabited: here, then, I\par
wander, and meet your image under every shade: but chiefly I love to\par
sit beneath the spreading branches of your favourite plane, where once,\par
Emily, we sat together; where I first ventured to tell you, that I\par
loved. O Emily! the remembrance of those moments overcomes me—I sit\par
lost in reverie—I endeavour to see you dimly through my tears, in all\par
the heaven of peace and innocence, such as you then appeared to me; to\par
hear again the accents of that voice, which then thrilled my heart with\par
tenderness and hope. I lean on the wall of the terrace, where we\par
together watched the rapid current of the Garonne below, while I\par
described the wild scenery about its source, but thought only of you. O\par
Emily! are these moments passed for ever—will they never more return?”\par
\par
In another part of his letter he wrote thus. “You see my letter is\par
dated on many different days, and, if you look back to the first, you\par
will perceive, that I began to write soon after your departure from\par
France. To write was, indeed, the only employment that withdrew me from\par
my own melancholy, and rendered your absence supportable, or rather, it\par
seemed to destroy absence; for, when I was conversing with you on\par
paper, and telling you every sentiment and affection of my heart, you\par
almost appeared to be present. This employment has been from time to\par
time my chief consolation, and I have deferred sending off my packet,\par
merely for the comfort of prolonging it, though it was certain, that\par
what I had written, was written to no purpose till you received it.\par
Whenever my mind has been more than usually depressed I have come to\par
pour forth its sorrows to you, and have always found consolation; and,\par
when any little occurrence has interested my heart, and given a gleam\par
of joy to my spirits, I have hastened to communicate it to you, and\par
have received reflected satisfaction. Thus, my letter is a kind of\par
picture of my life and of my thoughts for the last month, and thus,\par
though it has been deeply interesting to me, while I wrote it, and I\par
dare hope will, for the same reason, be not indifferent to you, yet to\par
other readers it would seem to abound only in frivolities. Thus it is\par
always, when we attempt to describe the finer movements of the heart,\par
for they are too fine to be discerned, they can only be experienced,\par
and are therefore passed over by the indifferent observer, while the\par
interested one feels, that all description is imperfect and\par
unnecessary, except as it may prove the sincerity of the writer, and\par
sooth his own sufferings. You will pardon all this egotism—for I am a\par
lover.”\par
\par
“I have just heard of a circumstance, which entirely destroys all my\par
fairy paradise of ideal delight, and which will reconcile me to the\par
necessity of returning to my regiment, for I must no longer wander\par
beneath the beloved shades, where I have been accustomed to meet you in\par
thought.—La Vallée is let! I have reason to believe this is without\par
your knowledge, from what Theresa told me this morning, and, therefore,\par
I mention the circumstance. She shed tears, while she related, that she\par
was going to leave the service of her dear mistress, and the château\par
where she had lived so many happy years; and all this, added she,\par
without even a letter from Mademoiselle to soften the news; but it is\par
all Mons. Quesnel’s doings, and I dare say she does not even know what\par
is going forward.”\par
\par
“Theresa added, That she had received a letter from him, informing her\par
the château was let, and that, as her services would no longer be\par
required, she must quit the place, on that day week, when the new\par
tenant would arrive.”\par
\par
“Theresa had been surprised by a visit from M. Quesnel, some time\par
before the receipt of this letter, who was accompanied by a stranger\par
that viewed the premises with much curiosity.”\par
\par
Towards the conclusion of his letter, which is dated a week after this\par
sentence, Valancourt adds, “I have received a summons from my regiment,\par
and I join it without regret, since I am shut out from the scenes that\par
are so interesting to my heart. I rode to La Vallée this morning, and\par
heard that the new tenant was arrived, and that Theresa was gone. I\par
should not treat the subject thus familiarly if I did not believe you\par
to be uninformed of this disposal of your house; for your satisfaction\par
I have endeavoured to learn something of the character and fortune of\par
your tenant, but without success. He is a gentleman, they say, and this\par
is all I can hear. The place, as I wandered round the boundaries,\par
appeared more melancholy to my imagination, than I had ever seen it. I\par
wished earnestly to have got admittance, that I might have taken\par
another leave of your favourite plane-tree, and thought of you once\par
more beneath its shade: but I forbore to tempt the curiosity of\par
strangers: the fishing-house in the woods, however, was still open to\par
me; thither I went, and passed an hour, which I cannot even look back\par
upon without emotion. O Emily! surely we are not separated for\par
ever—surely we shall live for each other!”\par
\par
This letter brought many tears to Emily’s eyes; tears of tenderness and\par
satisfaction on learning that Valancourt was well, and that time and\par
absence had in no degree effaced her image from his heart. There were\par
passages in this letter which particularly affected her, such as those\par
describing his visits to La Vallée, and the sentiments of delicate\par
affection that its scenes had awakened. It was a considerable time\par
before her mind was sufficiently abstracted from Valancourt to feel the\par
force of his intelligence concerning La Vallée. That Mons. Quesnel\par
should let it, without even consulting her on the measure, both\par
surprised and shocked her, particularly as it proved the absolute\par
authority he thought himself entitled to exercise in her affairs. It is\par
true, he had proposed, before she left France, that the château should\par
be let, during her absence, and to the economical prudence of this she\par
had nothing to object; but the committing what had been her father’s\par
villa to the power and caprice of strangers, and the depriving herself\par
of a sure home, should any unhappy circumstances make her look back to\par
her home as an asylum, were considerations that made her, even then,\par
strongly oppose the measure. Her father, too, in his last hour, had\par
received from her a solemn promise never to dispose of La Vallée; and\par
this she considered as in some degree violated if she suffered the\par
place to be let. But it was now evident with how little respect M.\par
Quesnel had regarded these objections, and how insignificant he\par
considered every obstacle to pecuniary advantage. It appeared, also,\par
that he had not even condescended to inform Montoni of the step he had\par
taken, since no motive was evident for Montoni’s concealing the\par
circumstance from her, if it had been made known to him: this both\par
displeased and surprised her; but the chief subjects of her uneasiness\par
were—the temporary disposal of La Vallée, and the dismission of her\par
father’s old and faithful servant.—“Poor Theresa,” said Emily, “thou\par
hadst not saved much in thy servitude, for thou wast always tender\par
towards the poor, and believd’st thou shouldst die in the family, where\par
thy best years had been spent. Poor Theresa!—now thou art turned out in\par
thy old age to seek thy bread!”\par
\par
Emily wept bitterly as these thoughts passed over her mind, and she\par
determined to consider what could be done for Theresa, and to talk very\par
explicitly to M. Quesnel on the subject; but she much feared that his\par
cold heart could feel only for itself. She determined also to enquire\par
whether he had made any mention of her affairs, in his letter to\par
Montoni, who soon gave her the opportunity she sought, by desiring that\par
she would attend him in his study. She had little doubt, that the\par
interview was intended for the purpose of communicating to her a part\par
of M. Quesnel’s letter concerning the transactions at La Vallée, and\par
she obeyed him immediately. Montoni was alone.\par
\par
“I have just been writing to Mons. Quesnel,” said he when Emily\par
appeared, “in reply to the letter I received from him a few days ago,\par
and I wished to talk to you upon a subject that occupied part of it.”\par
\par
“I also wished to speak with you on this topic, sir,” said Emily.\par
\par
“It is a subject of some interest to you, undoubtedly,” rejoined\par
Montoni, “and I think you must see it in the light that I do; indeed it\par
will not bear any other. I trust you will agree with me, that any\par
objection founded on sentiment, as they call it, ought to yield to\par
circumstances of solid advantage.”\par
\par
“Granting this, sir,” replied Emily, modestly, “those of humanity ought\par
surely to be attended to. But I fear it is now too late to deliberate\par
upon this plan, and I must regret, that it is no longer in my power to\par
reject it.”\par
\par
“It is too late,” said Montoni; “but since it is so, I am pleased to\par
observe, that you submit to reason and necessity without indulging\par
useless complaint. I applaud this conduct exceedingly, the more,\par
perhaps, since it discovers a strength of mind seldom observable in\par
your sex. When you are older you will look back with gratitude to the\par
friends who assisted in rescuing you from the romantic illusions of\par
sentiment, and will perceive, that they are only the snares of\par
childhood, and should be vanquished the moment you escape from the\par
nursery. I have not closed my letter, and you may add a few lines to\par
inform your uncle of your acquiescence. You will soon see him, for it\par
is my intention to take you, with Madame Montoni, in a few days to\par
Miarenti, and you can then talk over the affair.”\par
\par
Emily wrote on the opposite page of the paper as follows:\par
\par
“It is now useless, sir, for me to remonstrate upon the circumstances\par
of which Signor Montoni informs me that he has written. I could have\par
wished, at least, that the affair had been concluded with less\par
precipitation, that I might have taught myself to subdue some\par
prejudices, as the Signor calls them, which still linger in my heart.\par
As it is, I submit. In point of prudence nothing certainly can be\par
objected; but, though I submit, I have yet much to say on some other\par
points of the subject, when I shall have the honour of seeing you. In\par
the meantime I entreat you will take care of Theresa, for the sake of,\par
            “Sir,\par
            “Your affectionate niece,\par
            “EMILY ST. AUBERT.”\par
\par
\par
Montoni smiled satirically at what Emily had written, but did not\par
object to it, and she withdrew to her own apartment, where she sat down\par
to begin a letter to Valancourt, in which she related the particulars\par
of her journey, and her arrival at Venice, described some of the most\par
striking scenes in the passage over the Alps; her emotions on her first\par
view of Italy; the manners and characters of the people around her, and\par
some few circumstances of Montoni’s conduct. But she avoided even\par
naming Count Morano, much more the declaration he had made, since she\par
well knew how tremblingly alive to fear is real love, how jealously\par
watchful of every circumstance that may affect its interest; and she\par
scrupulously avoided to give Valancourt even the slightest reason for\par
believing he had a rival.\par
\par
On the following day Count Morano dined again at Montoni’s. He was in\par
an uncommon flow of spirits, and Emily thought there was somewhat of\par
exultation in his manner of addressing her, which she had never\par
observed before. She endeavoured to repress this by more than her usual\par
reserve, but the cold civility of her air now seemed rather to\par
encourage than to depress him. He appeared watchful of an opportunity\par
of speaking with her alone, and more than once solicited this; but\par
Emily always replied, that she could hear nothing from him which he\par
would be unwilling to repeat before the whole company.\par
\par
In the evening, Madame Montoni and her party went out upon the sea, and\par
as the Count led Emily to his _zendaletto_, he carried her hand to his\par
lips, and thanked her for the condescension she had shown him. Emily,\par
in extreme surprise and displeasure, hastily withdrew her hand, and\par
concluded that he had spoken ironically; but, on reaching the steps of\par
the terrace, and observing by the livery, that it was the Count’s\par
_zendaletto_ which waited below, while the rest of the party, having\par
arranged themselves in the gondolas, were moving on, she determined not\par
to permit a separate conversation, and, wishing him a good evening,\par
returned to the portico. The Count followed to expostulate and entreat,\par
and Montoni, who then came out, rendered solicitation unnecessary, for,\par
without condescending to speak, he took her hand, and led her to the\par
_zendaletto_. Emily was not silent; she entreated Montoni, in a low\par
voice, to consider the impropriety of these circumstances, and that he\par
would spare her the mortification of submitting to them; he, however,\par
was inflexible.\par
\par
“This caprice is intolerable,” said he, “and shall not be indulged:\par
there is no impropriety in the case.”\par
\par
At this moment, Emily’s dislike of Count Morano rose to abhorrence.\par
That he should, with undaunted assurance, thus pursue her,\par
notwithstanding all she had expressed on the subject of his addresses,\par
and think, as it was evident he did, that her opinion of him was of no\par
consequence, so long as his pretensions were sanctioned by Montoni,\par
added indignation to the disgust which she had felt towards him. She\par
was somewhat relieved by observing that Montoni was to be of the party,\par
who seated himself on one side of her, while Morano placed himself on\par
the other. There was a pause for some moments as the gondolieri\par
prepared their oars, and Emily trembled from apprehension of the\par
discourse that might follow this silence. At length she collected\par
courage to break it herself, in the hope of preventing fine speeches\par
from Morano, and reproof from Montoni. To some trivial remark which she\par
made, the latter returned a short and disobliging reply; but Morano\par
immediately followed with a general observation, which he contrived to\par
end with a particular compliment, and, though Emily passed it without\par
even the notice of a smile, he was not discouraged.\par
\par
“I have been impatient,” said he, addressing Emily, “to express my\par
gratitude; to thank you for your goodness; but I must also thank Signor\par
Montoni, who has allowed me this opportunity of doing so.”\par
\par
Emily regarded the Count with a look of mingled astonishment and\par
displeasure.\par
\par
“Why,” continued he, “should you wish to diminish the delight of this\par
moment by that air of cruel reserve?—Why seek to throw me again into\par
the perplexities of doubt, by teaching your eyes to contradict the\par
kindness of your late declaration? You cannot doubt the sincerity, the\par
ardour of my passion; it is therefore unnecessary, charming Emily!\par
surely unnecessary, any longer to attempt a disguise of your\par
sentiments.”\par
\par
“If I ever had disguised them, sir,” said Emily, with recollected\par
spirit, “it would certainly be unnecessary any longer to do so. I had\par
hoped, sir, that you would have spared me any farther necessity of\par
alluding to them; but, since you do not grant this, hear me declare,\par
and for the last time, that your perseverance has deprived you even of\par
the esteem, which I was inclined to believe you merited.”\par
\par
“Astonishing!” exclaimed Montoni: “this is beyond even my expectation,\par
though I have hitherto done justice to the caprice of the sex! But you\par
will observe, Mademoiselle Emily, that I am no lover, though Count\par
Morano is, and that I will not be made the amusement of your capricious\par
moments. Here is the offer of an alliance, which would do honour to any\par
family; yours, you will recollect, is not noble; you long resisted my\par
remonstrances, but my honour is now engaged, and it shall not be\par
trifled with.—You shall adhere to the declaration, which you have made\par
me an agent to convey to the Count.”\par
\par
“I must certainly mistake you, sir,” said Emily; “my answers on the\par
subject have been uniform; it is unworthy of you to accuse me of\par
caprice. If you have condescended to be my agent, it is an honour I did\par
not solicit. I myself have constantly assured Count Morano, and you\par
also, sir, that I never can accept the honour he offers me, and I now\par
repeat the declaration.”\par
\par
The Count looked with an air of surprise and enquiry at Montoni, whose\par
countenance also was marked with surprise, but it was surprise mingled\par
with indignation.\par
\par
“Here is confidence, as well as caprice!” said the latter. “Will you\par
deny your own words, Madam?”\par
\par
“Such a question is unworthy of an answer, sir;” said Emily blushing;\par
“you will recollect yourself, and be sorry that you have asked it.”\par
\par
“Speak to the point,” rejoined Montoni, in a voice of increasing\par
vehemence. “Will you deny your own words; will you deny, that you\par
acknowledged, only a few hours ago, that it was too late to recede from\par
your engagements, and that you accepted the Count’s hand?”\par
\par
“I will deny all this, for no words of mine ever imported it.”\par
\par
“Astonishing! Will you deny what you wrote to Mons. Quesnel, your\par
uncle? If you do, your own hand will bear testimony against you. What\par
have you now to say?” continued Montoni, observing the silence and\par
confusion of Emily.\par
\par
“I now perceive, sir, that you are under a very great error, and that I\par
have been equally mistaken.”\par
\par
“No more duplicity, I entreat; be open and candid, if it be possible.”\par
\par
“I have always been so, sir; and can claim no merit in such conduct,\par
for I have had nothing to conceal.”\par
\par
“How is this, Signor?” cried Morano, with trembling emotion.\par
\par
“Suspend your judgment, Count,” replied Montoni, “the wiles of a female\par
heart are unsearchable. Now, Madame, your _explanation_.”\par
\par
“Excuse me, sir, if I withhold my explanation till you appear willing\par
to give me your confidence; assertion as present can only subject me to\par
insult.”\par
\par
“Your explanation, I entreat you!” said Morano.\par
\par
“Well, well,” rejoined Montoni, “I give you my confidence; let us hear\par
this explanation.”\par
\par
“Let me lead to it then, by asking a question.”\par
\par
“As many as you please,” said Montoni, contemptuously.\par
\par
“What, then, was the subject of your letter to Mons. Quesnel?”\par
\par
“The same that was the subject of your note to him, certainly. You did\par
well to stipulate for my confidence before you demanded that question.”\par
\par
“I must beg you will be more explicit, sir; what was that subject?”\par
\par
“What could it be, but the noble offer of Count Morano,” said Montoni.\par
\par
“Then, sir, we entirely misunderstood each other,” replied Emily.\par
\par
“We entirely misunderstood each other too, I suppose,” rejoined\par
Montoni, “in the conversation which preceded the writing of that note?\par
I must do you the justice to own, that you are very ingenious at this\par
same art of misunderstanding.”\par
\par
Emily tried to restrain the tears that came to her eyes, and to answer\par
with becoming firmness. “Allow me, sir, to explain myself fully, or to\par
be wholly silent.”\par
\par
“The explanation may now be dispensed with; it is anticipated. If Count\par
Morano still thinks one necessary, I will give him an honest one: you\par
have changed your intention since our last conversation; and, if he can\par
have patience and humility enough to wait till tomorrow, he will\par
probably find it changed again: but as I have neither the patience nor\par
the humility, which you expect from a lover, I warn you of the effect\par
of my displeasure!”\par
\par
“Montoni, you are too precipitate,” said the Count, who had listened to\par
this conversation in extreme agitation and impatience;—“Signora, I\par
entreat your own explanation of this affair!”\par
\par
“Signor Montoni has said justly,” replied Emily, “that all explanation\par
may now be dispensed with; after what has passed I cannot suffer myself\par
to give one. It is sufficient for me, and for you, sir, that I repeat\par
my late declaration; let me hope this is the last time it will be\par
necessary for me to repeat it—I never can accept the honour of your\par
alliance.”\par
\par
“Charming Emily!” exclaimed the Count in an impassioned tone, “let not\par
resentment make you unjust; let me not suffer for the offence of\par
Montoni!—Revoke—”\par
\par
“Offence!” interrupted Montoni—“Count, this language is ridiculous,\par
this submission is childish!—speak as becomes a man, not as the slave\par
of a pretty tyrant.”\par
\par
“You distract me, Signor; suffer me to plead my own cause; you have\par
already proved insufficient to it.”\par
\par
“All conversation on this subject, sir,” said Emily, “is worse than\par
useless, since it can bring only pain to each of us: if you would\par
oblige me, pursue it no farther.”\par
\par
“It is impossible, Madam, that I can thus easily resign the object of a\par
passion, which is the delight and torment of my life.—I must still\par
love—still pursue you with unremitting ardour;—when you shall be\par
convinced of the strength and constancy of my passion, your heart must\par
soften into pity and repentance.”\par
\par
“Is this generous, sir? is this manly? Can it either deserve or obtain\par
the esteem you solicit, thus to continue a persecution from which I\par
have no present means of escaping?”\par
\par
A gleam of moonlight that fell upon Morano’s countenance, revealed the\par
strong emotions of his soul; and, glancing on Montoni discovered the\par
dark resentment, which contrasted his features.\par
\par
“By Heaven this is too much!” suddenly exclaimed the Count; “Signor\par
Montoni, you treat me ill; it is from you that I shall look for\par
explanation.”\par
\par
“From me, sir! you shall have it;” muttered Montoni, “if your\par
discernment is indeed so far obscured by passion, as to make\par
explanation necessary. And for you, madam, you should learn, that a man\par
of honour is not to be trifled with, though you may, perhaps, with\par
impunity, treat a _boy_ like a puppet.”\par
\par
This sarcasm roused the pride of Morano, and the resentment which he\par
had felt at the indifference of Emily, being lost in indignation of the\par
insolence of Montoni, he determined to mortify him, by defending her.\par
\par
“This also,” said he, replying to Montoni’s last words, “this also,\par
shall not pass unnoticed. I bid you learn, sir, that you have a\par
stronger enemy than a woman to contend with: I will protect Signora St.\par
Aubert from your threatened resentment. You have misled me, and would\par
revenge your disappointed views upon the innocent.”\par
\par
“Misled you!” retorted Montoni with quickness, “is my conduct—my\par
word”—then pausing, while he seemed endeavouring to restrain the\par
resentment, that flashed in his eyes, in the next moment he added, in a\par
subdued voice, “Count Morano, this is a language, a sort of conduct to\par
which I am not accustomed: it is the conduct of a passionate boy—as\par
such, I pass it over in contempt.”\par
\par
“In contempt, Signor?”\par
\par
“The respect I owe myself,” rejoined Montoni, “requires, that I should\par
converse more largely with you upon some points of the subject in\par
dispute. Return with me to Venice, and I will condescend to convince\par
you of your error.”\par
\par
“Condescend, sir! but I will not condescend to be so conversed with.”\par
\par
Montoni smiled contemptuously; and Emily, now terrified for the\par
consequences of what she saw and heard, could no longer be silent. She\par
explained the whole subject upon which she had mistaken Montoni in the\par
morning, declaring, that she understood him to have consulted her\par
solely concerning the disposal of La Vallée, and concluding with\par
entreating, that he would write immediately to M. Quesnel, and rectify\par
the mistake.\par
\par
But Montoni either was, or affected to be, still incredulous; and Count\par
Morano was still entangled in perplexity. While she was speaking,\par
however, the attention of her auditors had been diverted from the\par
immediate occasion of their resentment, and their passion consequently\par
became less. Montoni desired the Count would order his servants to row\par
back to Venice, that he might have some private conversation with him;\par
and Morano, somewhat soothed by his softened voice and manner, and\par
eager to examine into the full extent of his difficulties, complied.\par
\par
Emily, comforted by this prospect of release, employed the present\par
moments in endeavouring, with conciliating care, to prevent any fatal\par
mischief between the persons who so lately had persecuted and insulted\par
her.\par
\par
Her spirits revived, when she heard once more the voice of song and\par
laughter, resounding from the grand canal, and at length entered again\par
between its stately piazzas. The _zendaletto_ stopped at Montoni’s\par
mansion, and the Count hastily led her into the hall, where Montoni\par
took his arm, and said something in a low voice, on which Morano kissed\par
the hand he held, notwithstanding Emily’s effort to disengage it, and,\par
wishing her a good evening, with an accent and look she could not\par
misunderstand, returned to his _zendaletto_ with Montoni.\par
\par
Emily, in her own apartment, considered with intense anxiety all the\par
unjust and tyrannical conduct of Montoni, the dauntless perseverance of\par
Morano, and her own desolate situation, removed from her friends and\par
country. She looked in vain to Valancourt, confined by his profession\par
to a distant kingdom, as her protector; but it gave her comfort to\par
know, that there was, at least, one person in the world, who would\par
sympathise in her afflictions, and whose wishes would fly eagerly to\par
release her. Yet she determined not to give him unavailing pain by\par
relating the reasons she had to regret the having rejected his better\par
judgment concerning Montoni; reasons, however, which could not induce\par
her to lament the delicacy and disinterested affection that had made\par
her reject his proposal for a clandestine marriage. The approaching\par
interview with her uncle she regarded with some degree of hope, for she\par
determined to represent to him the distresses of her situation, and to\par
entreat that he would allow her to return to France with him and Madame\par
Quesnel. Then, suddenly remembering that her beloved La Vallée, her\par
only home, was no longer at her command, her tears flowed anew, and she\par
feared that she had little pity to expect from a man who, like M.\par
Quesnel, could dispose of it without deigning to consult with her, and\par
could dismiss an aged and faithful servant, destitute of either support\par
or asylum. But, though it was certain, that she had herself no longer a\par
home in France, and few, very few friends there, she determined to\par
return, if possible, that she might be released from the power of\par
Montoni, whose particularly oppressive conduct towards herself, and\par
general character as to others, were justly terrible to her\par
imagination. She had no wish to reside with her uncle, M. Quesnel,\par
since his behaviour to her late father and to herself, had been\par
uniformly such as to convince her, that in flying to him she could only\par
obtain an exchange of oppressors; neither had she the slightest\par
intention of consenting to the proposal of Valancourt for an immediate\par
marriage, though this would give her a lawful and a generous protector,\par
for the chief reasons, which had formerly influenced her conduct, still\par
existed against it, while others, which seemed to justify the step,\par
would not be done away; and his interest, his fame were at all times\par
too dear to her, to suffer her to consent to a union, which, at this\par
early period of their lives, would probably defeat both. One sure, and\par
proper asylum, however, would still be open to her in France. She knew\par
that she could board in the convent, where she had formerly experienced\par
so much kindness, and which had an affecting and solemn claim upon her\par
heart, since it contained the remains of her late father. Here she\par
could remain in safety and tranquillity, till the term, for which La\par
Vallée might be let, should expire; or, till the arrangement of M.\par
Motteville’s affairs enabled her so far to estimate the remains of her\par
fortune, as to judge whether it would be prudent for her to reside\par
there.\par
\par
Concerning Montoni’s conduct with respect to his letters to M. Quesnel,\par
she had many doubts; however he might be at first mistaken on the\par
subject, she much suspected that he wilfully persevered in his error,\par
as a means of intimidating her into a compliance with his wishes of\par
uniting her to Count Morano. Whether this was or was not the fact, she\par
was extremely anxious to explain the affair to M. Quesnel, and looked\par
forward with a mixture of impatience, hope and fear, to her approaching\par
visit.\par
\par
On the following day, Madame Montoni, being alone with Emily,\par
introduced the mention of Count Morano, by expressing her surprise,\par
that she had not joined the party on the water the preceding evening,\par
and at her abrupt departure to Venice. Emily then related what had\par
passed, expressed her concern for the mutual mistake that had occurred\par
between Montoni and herself, and solicited her aunt’s kind offices in\par
urging him to give a decisive denial to the count’s further addresses;\par
but she soon perceived, that Madame Montoni had not been ignorant of\par
the late conversation, when she introduced the present.\par
\par
“You have no encouragement to expect from me,” said her aunt, “in these\par
notions. I have already given my opinion on the subject, and think\par
Signor Montoni right in enforcing, by any means, your consent. If young\par
persons will be blind to their interest, and obstinately oppose it,\par
why, the greatest blessings they can have are friends, who will oppose\par
their folly. Pray what pretensions of any kind do you think you have to\par
such a match as is now offered you?”\par
\par
“Not any whatever, Madam,” replied Emily, “and, therefore, at least,\par
suffer me to be happy in my humility.”\par
\par
“Nay, niece, it cannot be denied, that you have pride enough; my poor\par
brother, your father, had his share of pride too; though, let me add,\par
his fortune did not justify it.”\par
\par
Emily, somewhat embarrassed by the indignation, which this malevolent\par
allusion to her father excited, and by the difficulty of rendering her\par
answer as temperate as it should be reprehensive, hesitated for some\par
moments, in a confusion, which highly gratified her aunt. At length she\par
said, “My father’s pride, Madam, had a noble object—the happiness which\par
he knew could be derived only from goodness, knowledge and charity. As\par
it never consisted in his superiority, in point of fortune, to some\par
persons, it was not humbled by his inferiority, in that respect, to\par
others. He never disdained those, who were wretched by poverty and\par
misfortune; he did sometimes despise persons, who, with many\par
opportunities of happiness, rendered themselves miserable by vanity,\par
ignorance and cruelty. I shall think it my highest glory to emulate\par
such pride.”\par
\par
“I do not pretend to understand anything of these high-flown\par
sentiments, niece; you have all that glory to yourself: I would teach\par
you a little plain sense, and not have you so wise as to despise\par
happiness.”\par
\par
“That would indeed not be wisdom, but folly,” said Emily, “for wisdom\par
can boast no higher attainment than happiness; but you will allow,\par
Madam, that our ideas of happiness may differ. I cannot doubt, that you\par
wish me to be happy, but I must fear you are mistaken in the means of\par
making me so.”\par
\par
“I cannot boast of a learned education, niece, such as your father\par
thought proper to give you, and, therefore, do not pretend to\par
understand all these fine speeches about happiness. I must be contented\par
to understand only common sense, and happy would it have been for you\par
and your father, if that had been included in his education.”\par
\par
Emily was too much shocked by these reflections on her father’s memory,\par
to despise this speech as it deserved.\par
\par
Madame Montoni was about to speak, but Emily quitted the room, and\par
retired to her own, where the little spirit she had lately exerted\par
yielded to grief and vexation, and left her only to her tears. From\par
every review of her situation she could derive, indeed, only new\par
sorrow. To the discovery, which had just been forced upon her, of\par
Montoni’s unworthiness, she had now to add, that of the cruel vanity,\par
for the gratification of which her aunt was about to sacrifice her; of\par
the effrontery and cunning, with which, at the time that she meditated\par
the sacrifice, she boasted of her tenderness, or insulted her victim;\par
and of the venomous envy, which, as it did not scruple to attack her\par
father’s character, could scarcely be expected to withhold from her\par
own.\par
\par
During the few days that intervened between this conversation and the\par
departure for Miarenti, Montoni did not once address himself to Emily.\par
His looks sufficiently declared his resentment; but that he should\par
forbear to renew a mention of the subject of it, exceedingly surprised\par
her, who was no less astonished, that, during three days, Count Morano\par
neither visited Montoni, nor was named by him. Several conjectures\par
arose in her mind. Sometimes she feared that the dispute between them\par
had been revived, and had ended fatally to the Count. Sometimes she was\par
inclined to hope, that weariness, or disgust at her firm rejection of\par
his suit had induced him to relinquish it; and, at others, she\par
suspected that he had now recourse to stratagem, and forbore his\par
visits, and prevailed with Montoni to forbear the repetition of his\par
name, in the expectation that gratitude and generosity would prevail\par
with her to give him the consent, which he could not hope from love.\par
\par
Thus passed the time in vain conjecture, and alternate hopes and fears,\par
till the day arrived when Montoni was to set out for the villa of\par
Miarenti, which, like the preceding ones, neither brought the Count,\par
nor the mention of him.\par
\par
Montoni having determined not to leave Venice, till towards evening,\par
that he might avoid the heats, and catch the cool breezes of night,\par
embarked about an hour before sunset, with his family, in a barge, for\par
the Brenta. Emily sat alone near the stern of the vessel, and, as it\par
floated slowly on, watched the gay and lofty city lessening from her\par
view, till its palaces seemed to sink in the distant waves, while its\par
loftier towers and domes, illumined by the declining sun, appeared on\par
the horizon, like those far-seen clouds which, in more northern climes,\par
often linger on the western verge, and catch the last light of a\par
summer’s evening. Soon after, even these grew dim, and faded in\par
distance from her sight; but she still sat gazing on the vast scene of\par
cloudless sky, and mighty waters, and listening in pleasing awe to the\par
deep-sounding waves, while, as her eyes glanced over the Adriatic,\par
towards the opposite shores, which were, however, far beyond the reach\par
of sight, she thought of Greece, and, a thousand classical remembrances\par
stealing to her mind, she experienced that pensive luxury which is felt\par
on viewing the scenes of ancient story, and on comparing their present\par
state of silence and solitude with that of their former grandeur and\par
animation. The scenes of the Illiad illapsed in glowing colours to her\par
fancy—scenes, once the haunt of heroes—now lonely, and in ruins; but\par
which still shone, in the poet’s strain, in all their youthful\par
splendour.\par
\par
As her imagination painted with melancholy touches, the deserted plains\par
of Troy, such as they appeared in this after-day, she reanimated the\par
landscape with the following little story.\par
\par
STANZAS\par
\par
O’er Ilion’s plains, where once the warrior bled,\par
And once the poet rais’d his deathless strain,\par
O’er Ilion’s plains a weary driver led\par
His stately camels:  For the ruin’d fane\par
\par
Wide round the lonely scene his glance he threw,\par
For now the red cloud faded in the west,\par
And twilight o’er the silent landscape drew\par
Her deep’ning veil; eastward his course he prest:\par
\par
There, on the grey horizon’s glimm’ring bound,\par
Rose the proud columns of deserted Troy,\par
And wandering shepherds now a shelter found\par
Within those walls, where princes wont to joy.\par
\par
Beneath a lofty porch the driver pass’d,\par
Then, from his camels heav’d the heavy load;\par
Partook with them the simple, cool repast,\par
And in short vesper gave himself to God.\par
\par
From distant lands with merchandise he came,\par
His all of wealth his patient servants bore;\par
Oft deep-drawn sighs his anxious wish proclaim\par
To reach, again, his happy cottage door;\par
\par
For there, his wife, his little children, dwell;\par
Their smiles shall pay the toil of many an hour:\par
Ev’n now warm tears to expectation swell,\par
As fancy o’er his mind extends her pow’r.\par
\par
A death-like stillness reign’d, where once the song,\par
The song of heroes, wak’d the midnight air,\par
Save, when a solemn murmur roll’d along,\par
That seem’d to say—“for future worlds prepare.”\par
\par
For Time’s imperious voice was frequent heard\par
Shaking the marble temple to its fall,\par
(By hands he long had conquer’d, vainly rear’d),\par
And distant ruins answer’d to his call.\par
\par
While Hamet slept, his camels round him lay,\par
Beneath him, all his store of wealth was piled;\par
And here, his cruse and empty wallet lay,\par
And there, the flute that chear’d him in the wild.\par
\par
The robber Tartar on his slumber stole,\par
For o’er the waste, at eve, he watch’d his train;\par
Ah! who his thirst of plunder shall control?\par
Who calls on him for mercy—calls in vain!\par
\par
A poison’d poignard in his belt he wore,\par
A crescent sword depended at his side,\par
The deathful quiver at his back he bore,\par
And infants—at his very look had died!\par
\par
The moon’s cold beam athwart the temple fell,\par
And to his sleeping prey the Tartar led;\par
But soft!—a startled camel shook his bell,\par
Then stretch’d his limbs, and rear’d his drowsy head.\par
\par
Hamet awoke! the poignard glitter’d high!\par
Swift from his couch he sprung, and ’scap’d the blow;\par
When from an unknown hand the arrows fly,\par
That lay the ruffian, in his vengeance, low.\par
\par
He groan’d, he died! from forth a column’d gate\par
A fearful shepherd, pale and silent, crept,\par
Who, as he watch’d his folded flock star-late,\par
Had mark’d the robber steal where Hamet slept.\par
\par
He fear’d his own, and sav’d a stranger’s life!\par
Poor Hamet clasp’d him to his grateful heart;\par
Then, rous’d his camels for the dusty strife,\par
And, with the shepherd, hasten’d to depart.\par
\par
And now, aurora breathes her fresh’ning gale,\par
And faintly trembles on the eastern cloud;\par
And now, the sun, from under twilight’s veil,\par
Looks gaily forth, and melts her airy shroud.\par
\par
Wide o’er the level plains, his slanting beams\par
Dart their long lines on Ilion’s tower’d site;\par
The distant Hellespont with morning gleams,\par
And old Scamander winds his waves in light.\par
\par
All merry sound the camel bells, so gay,\par
And merry beats fond Hamet’s heart, for he,\par
E’er the dim evening steals upon the day,\par
His children, wife and happy home shall see.\par
\par
\par
As Emily approached the shores of Italy she began to discriminate the\par
rich features and varied colouring of the landscape—the purple hills,\par
groves of orange pine and cypress, shading magnificent villas, and\par
towns rising among vineyards and plantations. The noble Brenta, pouring\par
its broad waves into the sea, now appeared, and, when she reached its\par
mouth, the barge stopped, that the horses might be fastened which were\par
now to tow it up the stream. This done, Emily gave a last look to the\par
Adriatic, and to the dim sail,\par
\par
that from the sky-mix’d wave\par
Dawns on the sight,\par
\par
\par
and the barge slowly glided between the green and luxuriant slopes of\par
the river. The grandeur of the Palladian villas, that adorn these\par
shores, was considerably heightened by the setting rays, which threw\par
strong contrasts of light and shade upon the porticos and long arcades,\par
and beamed a mellow lustre upon the orangeries and the tall groves of\par
pine and cypress, that overhung the buildings. The scent of oranges, of\par
flowering myrtles, and other odoriferous plants was diffused upon the\par
air, and often, from these embowered retreats, a strain of music stole\par
on the calm, and “softened into silence.”\par
\par
The sun now sunk below the horizon, twilight fell over the landscape,\par
and Emily, wrapt in musing silence, continued to watch its features\par
gradually vanishing into obscurity. She remembered her many happy\par
evenings, when with St. Aubert she had observed the shades of twilight\par
steal over a scene as beautiful as this, from the gardens of La Vallée,\par
and a tear fell to the memory of her father. Her spirits were softened\par
into melancholy by the influence of the hour, by the low murmur of the\par
wave passing under the vessel, and the stillness of the air, that\par
trembled only at intervals with distant music:—why else should she, at\par
these moments, have looked on her attachment to Valancourt with\par
presages so very afflicting, since she had but lately received letters\par
from him, that had soothed for a while all her anxieties? It now seemed\par
to her oppressed mind, that she had taken leave of him for ever, and\par
that the countries, which separated them, would never more be retraced\par
by her. She looked upon Count Morano with horror, as in some degree the\par
cause of this; but apart from him, a conviction, if such that may be\par
called, which arises from no proof, and which she knew not how to\par
account for, seized her mind—that she should never see Valancourt\par
again. Though she knew, that neither Morano’s solicitations, nor\par
Montoni’s commands had lawful power to enforce her obedience, she\par
regarded both with a superstitious dread, that they would finally\par
prevail.\par
\par
Lost in this melancholy reverie, and shedding frequent tears, Emily was\par
at length roused by Montoni, and she followed him to the cabin, where\par
refreshments were spread, and her aunt was seated alone. The\par
countenance of Madame Montoni was inflamed with resentment, that\par
appeared to be the consequence of some conversation she had held with\par
her husband, who regarded her with a kind of sullen disdain, and both\par
preserved, for some time, a haughty silence. Montoni then spoke to\par
Emily of Mons. Quesnel: “You will not, I hope, persist in disclaiming\par
your knowledge of the subject of my letter to him?”\par
\par
“I had hoped, sir, that it was no longer necessary for me to disclaim\par
it,” said Emily, “I had hoped, from your silence, that you were\par
convinced of your error.”\par
\par
“You have hoped impossibilities then,” replied Montoni; “I might as\par
reasonably have expected to find sincerity and uniformity of conduct in\par
one of your sex, as you to convict me of error in this affair.”\par
\par
Emily blushed, and was silent; she now perceived too clearly, that she\par
had hoped an impossibility, for, where no mistake had been committed no\par
conviction could follow; and it was evident, that Montoni’s conduct had\par
not been the consequence of mistake, but of design.\par
\par
Anxious to escape from conversation, which was both afflicting and\par
humiliating to her, she soon returned to the deck, and resumed her\par
station near the stern, without apprehension of cold, for no vapour\par
rose from the water, and the air was dry and tranquil; here, at least,\par
the benevolence of nature allowed her the quiet which Montoni had\par
denied her elsewhere. It was now past midnight. The stars shed a kind\par
of twilight, that served to show the dark outline of the shores on\par
either hand, and the grey surface of the river; till the moon rose from\par
behind a high palm grove, and shed her mellow lustre over the scene.\par
The vessel glided smoothly on: amid the stillness of the hour Emily\par
heard, now and then, the solitary voice of the barge-men on the bank,\par
as they spoke to their horses; while, from a remote part of the vessel,\par
with melancholy song,\par
\par
The sailor sooth’d,\par
Beneath the trembling moon, the midnight wave.\par
\par
\par
Emily, meanwhile, anticipated her reception by Mons, and Madame\par
Quesnel; considered what she should say on the subject of La Vallée;\par
and then, to withhold her mind from more anxious topics, tried to amuse\par
herself by discriminating the faint-drawn features of the landscape,\par
reposing in the moonlight. While her fancy thus wandered, she saw, at a\par
distance, a building peeping between the moonlight trees, and, as the\par
barge approached, heard voices speaking, and soon distinguished the\par
lofty portico of a villa, overshadowed by groves of pine and sycamore,\par
which she recollected to be the same, that had formerly been pointed\par
out to her, as belonging to Madame Quesnel’s relative.\par
\par
The barge stopped at a flight of marble steps, which led up the bank to\par
a lawn. Lights appeared between some pillars beyond the portico.\par
Montoni sent forward his servant, and then disembarked with his family.\par
They found Mons. and Madame Quesnel, with a few friends, seated on\par
sofas in the portico, enjoying the cool breeze of the night, and eating\par
fruits and ices, while some of their servants at a little distance, on\par
the river’s bank, were performing a simple serenade. Emily was now\par
accustomed to the way of living in this warm country, and was not\par
surprised to find Mons. and Madame Quesnel in their portico, two hours\par
after midnight.\par
\par
The usual salutations being over, the company seated themselves in the\par
portico, and refreshments were brought them from the adjoining hall,\par
where a banquet was spread, and servants attended. When the bustle of\par
this meeting had subsided, and Emily had recovered from the little\par
flutter into which it had thrown her spirits, she was struck with the\par
singular beauty of the hall, so perfectly accommodated to the luxuries\par
of the season. It was of white marble, and the roof, rising into an\par
open cupola, was supported by columns of the same material. Two\par
opposite sides of the apartment, terminating in open porticos, admitted\par
to the hall a full view of the gardens, and of the river scenery; in\par
the centre a fountain continually refreshed the air, and seemed to\par
heighten the fragrance, that breathed from the surrounding orangeries,\par
while its dashing waters gave an agreeable and soothing sound. Etruscan\par
lamps, suspended from the pillars, diffused a brilliant light over the\par
interior part of the hall, leaving the remoter porticos to the softer\par
lustre of the moon.\par
\par
Mons. Quesnel talked apart to Montoni of his own affairs, in his usual\par
strain of self-importance; boasted of his new acquisitions, and then\par
affected to pity some disappointments, which Montoni had lately\par
sustained. Meanwhile, the latter, whose pride at least enabled him to\par
despise such vanity as this, and whose discernment at once detected\par
under this assumed pity, the frivolous malignity of Quesnel’s mind,\par
listened to him in contemptuous silence, till he named his niece, and\par
then they left the portico, and walked away into the gardens.\par
\par
Emily, however, still attended to Madame Quesnel, who spoke of France\par
(for even the name of her native country was dear to her) and she found\par
some pleasure in looking at a person, who had lately been in it. That\par
country, too, was inhabited by Valancourt, and she listened to the\par
mention of it, with a faint hope, that he also would be named. Madame\par
Quesnel, who, when she was in France, had talked with rapture of Italy,\par
now, that she was in Italy, talked with equal praise of France, and\par
endeavoured to excite the wonder and the envy of her auditors by\par
accounts of places, which they had not been happy enough to see. In\par
these descriptions she not only imposed upon them, but upon herself,\par
for she never thought a present pleasure equal to one, that was passed;\par
and thus the delicious climate, the fragrant orangeries and all the\par
luxuries, which surrounded her, slept unnoticed, while her fancy\par
wandered over the distant scenes of a northern country.\par
\par
Emily listened in vain for the name of Valancourt. Madame Montoni spoke\par
in her turn of the delights of Venice, and of the pleasure she expected\par
from visiting the fine castle of Montoni, on the Apennine; which latter\par
mention, at least, was merely a retaliating boast, for Emily well knew,\par
that her aunt had no taste for solitary grandeur, and, particularly,\par
for such as the castle of Udolpho promised. Thus the party continued to\par
converse, and, as far as civility would permit, to torture each other\par
by mutual boasts, while they reclined on sofas in the portico, and were\par
environed with delights both from nature and art, by which any honest\par
minds would have been tempered to benevolence, and happy imaginations\par
would have been soothed into enchantment.\par
\par
The dawn, soon after, trembled in the eastern horizon, and the light\par
tints of morning, gradually expanding, showed the beautifully declining\par
forms of the Italian mountains and the gleaming landscapes, stretched\par
at their feet. Then the sunbeams, shooting up from behind the hills,\par
spread over the scene that fine saffron tinge, which seems to impart\par
repose to all it touches. The landscape no longer gleamed; all its\par
glowing colours were revealed, except that its remoter features were\par
still softened and united in the mist of distance, whose sweet effect\par
was heightened to Emily by the dark verdure of the pines and cypresses,\par
that over-arched the foreground of the river.\par
\par
The market people, passing with their boats to Venice, now formed a\par
moving picture on the Brenta. Most of these had little painted awnings,\par
to shelter their owners from the sunbeams, which, together with the\par
piles of fruit and flowers, displayed beneath, and the tasteful\par
simplicity of the peasant girls, who watched the rural treasures,\par
rendered them gay and striking objects. The swift movement of the boats\par
down the current, the quick glance of oars in the water, and now and\par
then the passing chorus of peasants, who reclined under the sail of\par
their little bark, or the tones of some rustic instrument, played by a\par
girl, as she sat near her sylvan cargo, heightened the animation and\par
festivity of the scene.\par
\par
When Montoni and M. Quesnel had joined the ladies, the party left the\par
portico for the gardens, where the charming scenery soon withdrew\par
Emily’s thoughts from painful subjects. The majestic forms and rich\par
verdure of cypresses she had never seen so perfect before: groves of\par
cedar, lemon, and orange, the spiry clusters of the pine and poplar,\par
the luxuriant chesnut and oriental plane, threw all their pomp of shade\par
over these gardens; while bowers of flowering myrtle and other spicy\par
shrubs mingled their fragrance with that of flowers, whose vivid and\par
various colouring glowed with increased effect beneath the contrasted\par
umbrage of the groves. The air also was continually refreshed by\par
rivulets, which, with more taste than fashion, had been suffered to\par
wander among the green recesses.\par
\par
Emily often lingered behind the party, to contemplate the distant\par
landscape, that closed a vista, or that gleamed beneath the dark\par
foliage of the foreground;—the spiral summits of the mountains, touched\par
with a purple tint, broken and steep above, but shelving gradually to\par
their base; the open valley, marked by no formal lines of art; and the\par
tall groves of cypress, pine and poplar, sometimes embellished by a\par
ruined villa, whose broken columns appeared between the branches of a\par
pine, that seemed to droop over their fall.\par
\par
From other parts of the gardens, the character of the view was entirely\par
changed, and the fine solitary beauty of the landscape shifted for the\par
crowded features and varied colouring of inhabitation.\par
\par
The sun was now gaining fast upon the sky, and the party quitted the\par
gardens, and retired to repose.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER IV\par
\par
And poor Misfortune feels the lash of Vice.\par
                    THOMSON\par
\par
\par
Emily seized the first opportunity of conversing alone with Mons.\par
Quesnel, concerning La Vallée. His answers to her enquiries were\par
concise, and delivered with the air of a man, who is conscious of\par
possessing absolute power and impatient of hearing it questioned. He\par
declared, that the disposal of the place was a necessary measure; and\par
that she might consider herself indebted to his prudence for even the\par
small income that remained for her. “But, however,” added he, “when\par
this Venetian Count (I have forgot his name) marries you, your present\par
disagreeable state of dependence will cease. As a relation to you I\par
rejoice in the circumstance, which is so fortunate for you, and, I may\par
add, so unexpected by your friends.” For some moments Emily was chilled\par
into silence by this speech; and, when she attempted to undeceive him,\par
concerning the purport of the note she had inclosed in Montoni’s\par
letter, he appeared to have some private reason for disbelieving her\par
assertion, and, for a considerable time, persevered in accusing her of\par
capricious conduct. Being, at length, however, convinced that she\par
really disliked Morano and had positively rejected his suit, his\par
resentment was extravagant, and he expressed it in terms equally\par
pointed and inhuman; for, secretly flattered by the prospect of a\par
connection with a nobleman, whose title he had affected to forget, he\par
was incapable of feeling pity for whatever sufferings of his niece\par
might stand in the way of his ambition.\par
\par
Emily saw at once in his manner all the difficulties, that awaited her,\par
and, though no oppression could have power to make her renounce\par
Valancourt for Morano, her fortitude now trembled at an encounter with\par
the violent passions of her uncle.\par
\par
She opposed his turbulence and indignation only by the mild dignity of\par
a superior mind; but the gentle firmness of her conduct served to\par
exasperate still more his resentment, since it compelled him to feel\par
his own inferiority, and, when he left her, he declared, that, if she\par
persisted in her folly, both himself and Montoni would abandon her to\par
the contempt of the world.\par
\par
The calmness she had assumed in his presence failed Emily, when alone,\par
and she wept bitterly, and called frequently upon the name of her\par
departed father, whose advice to her from his death-bed she then\par
remembered. “Alas!” said she, “I do indeed perceive how much more\par
valuable is the strength of fortitude than the grace of sensibility,\par
and I will also endeavour to fulfil the promise I then made; I will not\par
indulge in unavailing lamentation, but will try to endure, with\par
firmness, the oppression I cannot elude.”\par
\par
Somewhat soothed by the consciousness of performing a part of St.\par
Aubert’s last request, and of endeavouring to pursue the conduct which\par
he would have approved, she overcame her tears, and, when the company\par
met at dinner, had recovered her usual serenity of countenance.\par
\par
In the cool of the evening, the ladies took the _fresco_ along the bank\par
of the Brenta in Madame Quesnel’s carriage. The state of Emily’s mind\par
was in melancholy contrast with the gay groups assembled beneath the\par
shades that overhung this enchanting stream. Some were dancing under\par
the trees, and others reclining on the grass, taking ices and coffee\par
and calmly enjoying the effect of a beautiful evening, on a luxuriant\par
landscape. Emily, when she looked at the snow-capped Apennines,\par
ascending in the distance, thought of Montoni’s castle, and suffered\par
some terror, lest he should convey her thither, for the purpose of\par
enforcing her obedience; but the thought vanished, when she considered,\par
that she was as much in his power at Venice as she could be elsewhere.\par
\par
It was moonlight before the party returned to the villa, where supper\par
was spread in the airy hall, which had so much enchanted Emily’s fancy,\par
on the preceding night. The ladies seated themselves in the portico,\par
till Mons. Quesnel, Montoni, and other gentlemen should join them at\par
table, and Emily endeavoured to resign herself to the tranquillity of\par
the hour. Presently, a barge stopped at the steps that led into the\par
gardens, and, soon after, she distinguished the voices of Montoni and\par
Quesnel, and then that of Morano, who, in the next moment, appeared.\par
His compliments she received in silence, and her cold air seemed at\par
first to discompose him; but he soon recovered his usual gaiety of\par
manner, though the officious kindness of M. and Madame Quesnel Emily\par
perceived disgusted him. Such a degree of attention she had scarcely\par
believed could be shown by M. Quesnel, for she had never before seen\par
him otherwise than in the presence of his inferiors or equals.\par
\par
When she could retire to her own apartment, her mind almost\par
involuntarily dwelt on the most probable means of prevailing with the\par
Count to withdraw his suit, and to her liberal mind none appeared more\par
probable, than that of acknowledging to him a prior attachment and\par
throwing herself upon his generosity for a release. When, however, on\par
the following day, he renewed his addresses, she shrunk from the\par
adoption of the plan she had formed. There was something so repugnant\par
to her just pride, in laying open the secret of her heart to such a man\par
as Morano, and in suing to him for compassion, that she impatiently\par
rejected this design and wondered, that she could have paused upon it\par
for a moment. The rejection of his suit she repeated in the most\par
decisive terms she could select, mingling with it a severe censure of\par
his conduct; but, though the Count appeared mortified by this, he\par
persevered in the most ardent professions of admiration, till he was\par
interrupted and Emily released by the presence of Madame Quesnel.\par
\par
During her stay at this pleasant villa, Emily was thus rendered\par
miserable by the assiduities of Morano, together with the cruelly\par
exerted authority of M. Quesnel and Montoni, who, with her aunt, seemed\par
now more resolutely determined upon this marriage than they had even\par
appeared to be at Venice. M. Quesnel, finding, that both argument and\par
menace were ineffectual in enforcing an immediate conclusion to it, at\par
length relinquished his endeavours, and trusted to the power of Montoni\par
and to the course of events at Venice. Emily, indeed, looked to Venice\par
with hope, for there she would be relieved in some measure from the\par
persecution of Morano, who would no longer be an inhabitant of the same\par
house with herself, and from that of Montoni, whose engagements would\par
not permit him to be continually at home. But amidst the pressure of\par
her own misfortunes, she did not forget those of poor Theresa, for whom\par
she pleaded with courageous tenderness to Quesnel, who promised, in\par
slight and general terms, that she should not be forgotten.\par
\par
Montoni, in a long conversation with M. Quesnel, arranged the plan to\par
be pursued respecting Emily, and M. Quesnel proposed to be at Venice,\par
as soon as he should be informed, that the nuptials were concluded.\par
\par
It was new to Emily to part with any person, with whom she was\par
connected, without feeling of regret; the moment, however, in which she\par
took leave of M. and Madame Quesnel, was, perhaps, the only\par
satisfactory one she had known in their presence.\par
\par
Morano returned in Montoni’s barge, and Emily, as she watched her\par
gradual approach to that magic city, saw at her side the only person,\par
who occasioned her to view it with less than perfect delight. They\par
arrived there about midnight, when Emily was released from the presence\par
of the Count, who, with Montoni, went to a Casino, and she was suffered\par
to retire to her own apartment.\par
\par
On the following day, Montoni, in a short conversation, which he held\par
with Emily, informed her, that he would no longer be _trifled_ with,\par
and that, since her marriage with the Count would be so highly\par
advantageous to her, that folly only could object to it, and folly of\par
such extent as was incapable of conviction, it should be celebrated\par
without further delay, and, if that was necessary, without her consent.\par
\par
Emily, who had hitherto tried remonstrance, had now recourse to\par
supplication, for distress prevented her from foreseeing, that, with a\par
man of Montoni’s disposition, supplication would be equally useless.\par
She afterwards enquired by what right he exerted this unlimited\par
authority over her, a question, which her better judgment would have\par
withheld her, in a calmer moment, from making, since it could avail her\par
nothing, and would afford Montoni another opportunity of triumphing\par
over her defenceless condition.\par
\par
“By what right!” cried Montoni, with a malicious smile, “by the right\par
of my will; if you can elude that, I will not inquire by what right you\par
do so. I now remind you, for the last time, that you are a stranger, in\par
a foreign country, and that it is your interest to make me your friend;\par
you know the means; if you compel me to become your enemy—I will\par
venture to tell you, that the punishment shall exceed your expectation.\par
You may know _I_ am not to be trifled with.”\par
\par
Emily continued, for some time after Montoni had left her, in a state\par
of despair, or rather stupefaction; a consciousness of misery was all\par
that remained in her mind. In this situation Madame Montoni found her,\par
at the sound of whose voice Emily looked up, and her aunt, somewhat\par
softened by the expression of despair, that fixed her countenance,\par
spoke in a manner more kind than she had ever yet done. Emily’s heart\par
was touched; she shed tears, and, after weeping for some time,\par
recovered sufficient composure to speak on the subject of her distress,\par
and to endeavour to interest Madame Montoni in her behalf. But, though\par
the compassion of her aunt had been surprised, her ambition was not to\par
be overcome, and her present object was to be the aunt of a Countess.\par
Emily’s efforts, therefore, were as unsuccessful as they had been with\par
Montoni, and she withdrew to her apartment to think and weep alone. How\par
often did she remember the parting scene with Valancourt, and wish,\par
that the Italian had mentioned Montoni’s character with less reserve!\par
When her mind, however, had recovered from the first shock of this\par
behaviour, she considered, that it would be impossible for him to\par
compel her alliance with Morano, if she persisted in refusing to repeat\par
any part of the marriage ceremony; and she persevered in her resolution\par
to await Montoni’s threatened vengeance rather than give herself for\par
life to a man, whom she must have despised for his present conduct, had\par
she never even loved Valancourt; yet she trembled at the revenge she\par
thus resolved to brave.\par
\par
An affair, however, soon after occurred, which somewhat called off\par
Montoni’s attention from Emily. The mysterious visits of Orsino were\par
renewed with more frequency since the return of the former to Venice.\par
There were others, also, besides Orsino, admitted to these midnight\par
councils, and among them Cavigni and Verezzi. Montoni became more\par
reserved and austere in his manner than ever; and Emily, if her own\par
interests had not made her regardless of his, might have perceived,\par
that something extraordinary was working in his mind.\par
\par
One night, on which a council was not held, Orsino came in great\par
agitation of spirits, and dispatched his confidential servant to\par
Montoni, who was at a Casino, desiring that he would return home\par
immediately; but charging the servant not to mention his name. Montoni\par
obeyed the summons, and, on meeting Orsino, was informed of the\par
circumstances, that occasioned his visit and his visible alarm, with a\par
part of which he was already acquainted.\par
\par
A Venetian nobleman, who had, on some late occasion, provoked the\par
hatred of Orsino, had been way-laid and poniarded by hired assassins:\par
and, as the murdered person was of the first connections, the Senate\par
had taken up the affair. One of the assassins was now apprehended, who\par
had confessed, that Orsino was his employer in the atrocious deed; and\par
the latter, informed of his danger, had now come to Montoni to consult\par
on the measures necessary to favour his escape. He knew, that, at this\par
time, the officers of the police were upon the watch for him, all over\par
the city; to leave it, at present, therefore, was impracticable, and\par
Montoni consented to secrete him for a few days till the vigilance of\par
justice should relax, and then to assist him in quitting Venice. He\par
knew the danger he himself incurred by permitting Orsino to remain in\par
his house, but such was the nature of his obligations to this man, that\par
he did not think it prudent to refuse him an asylum.\par
\par
Such was the person whom Montoni had admitted to his confidence, and\par
for whom he felt as much friendship as was compatible with his\par
character.\par
\par
While Orsino remained concealed in his house, Montoni was unwilling to\par
attract public observation by the nuptials of Count Morano; but this\par
obstacle was, in a few days, overcome by the departure of his criminal\par
visitor, and he then informed Emily, that her marriage was to be\par
celebrated on the following morning. To her repeated assurances, that\par
it should not take place, he replied only by a malignant smile; and,\par
telling her that the Count and a priest would be at his house, early in\par
the morning, he advised her no further to dare his resentment, by\par
opposition to his will and to her own interest. “I am now going out for\par
the evening,” said he, “remember, that I shall give your hand to Count\par
Morano in the morning.” Emily, having, ever since his late threats,\par
expected, that her trials would at length arrive to this crisis, was\par
less shocked by the declaration, that she otherwise would have been,\par
and she endeavoured to support herself by the belief, that the marriage\par
could not be valid, so long as she refused before the priest to repeat\par
any part of the ceremony. Yet, as the moment of trial approached, her\par
long-harassed spirits shrunk almost equally from the encounter of his\par
vengeance, and from the hand of Count Morano. She was not even\par
perfectly certain of the consequence of her steady refusal at the\par
altar, and she trembled, more than ever, at the power of Montoni, which\par
seemed unlimited as his will, for she saw, that he would not scruple to\par
transgress any law, if, by so doing, he could accomplish his project.\par
\par
While her mind was thus suffering and in a state little short of\par
distraction, she was informed that Morano asked permission to see her,\par
and the servant had scarcely departed with an excuse, before she\par
repented that she had sent one. In the next moment, reverting to her\par
former design, and determining to try, whether expostulation and\par
entreaty would not succeed, where a refusal and a just disdain had\par
failed, she recalled the servant, and, sending a different message,\par
prepared to go down to the Count.\par
\par
The dignity and assumed composure with which she met him, and the kind\par
of pensive resignation, that softened her countenance, were\par
circumstances not likely to induce him to relinquish her, serving, as\par
they did, to heighten a passion, which had already intoxicated his\par
judgment. He listened to all she said with an appearance of complacency\par
and of a wish to oblige her; but his resolution remained invariably the\par
same, and he endeavoured to win her admiration by every insinuating art\par
he so well knew how to practise. Being, at length, assured, that she\par
had nothing to hope from his justice, she repeated, in a solemn and\par
impressive manner, her absolute rejection of his suit, and quitted him\par
with an assurance, that her refusal would be effectually maintained\par
against every circumstance, that could be imagined for subduing it. A\par
just pride had restrained her tears in his presence, but now they\par
flowed from the fulness of her heart. She often called upon the name of\par
her late father, and often dwelt with unutterable anguish on the idea\par
of Valancourt.\par
\par
She did not go down to supper, but remained alone in her apartment,\par
sometimes yielding to the influence of grief and terror, and, at\par
others, endeavouring to fortify her mind against them, and to prepare\par
herself to meet, with composed courage, the scene of the following\par
morning, when all the stratagem of Morano and the violence of Montoni\par
would be united against her.\par
\par
The evening was far advanced, when Madame Montoni came to her chamber\par
with some bridal ornaments, which the Count had sent to Emily. She had,\par
this day, purposely avoided her niece; perhaps, because her usual\par
insensibility failed her, and she feared to trust herself with a view\par
of Emily’s distress; or possibly, though her conscience was seldom\par
audible, it now reproached her with her conduct to her brother’s orphan\par
child, whose happiness had been entrusted to her care by a dying\par
father.\par
\par
Emily could not look at these presents, and made a last, though almost\par
hopeless, effort to interest the compassion of Madame Montoni, who, if\par
she did feel any degree of pity, or remorse, successfully concealed it,\par
and reproached her niece with folly in being miserable, concerning a\par
marriage, which ought only to make her happy. “I am sure,” said she,\par
“if I was unmarried, and the Count had proposed to me, I should have\par
been flattered by the distinction: and if I should have been so, I am\par
sure, niece, you, who have no fortune, ought to feel yourself highly\par
honoured, and show a proper gratitude and humility towards the Count,\par
for his condescension. I am often surprised, I must own, to observe how\par
humbly he deports himself to you, notwithstanding the haughty airs you\par
give yourself; I wonder he has patience to humour you so: if I was he,\par
I know, I should often be ready to reprehend you, and make you know\par
yourself a little better. I would not have flattered you, I can tell\par
you, for it is this absurd flattery that makes you fancy yourself of so\par
much consequence, that you think nobody can deserve you, and I often\par
tell the Count so, for I have no patience to hear him pay you such\par
extravagant compliments, which you believe every word of!”\par
\par
“Your patience, madam, cannot suffer more cruelly on such occasions,\par
than my own,” said Emily.\par
\par
“O! that is all mere affectation,” rejoined her aunt. “I know that his\par
flattery delights you, and makes you so vain, that you think you may\par
have the whole world at your feet. But you are very much mistaken; I\par
can assure you, niece, you will not meet with many such suitors as the\par
Count: every other person would have turned upon his heel, and left you\par
to repent at your leisure, long ago.”\par
\par
“O that the Count had resembled every other person, then!” said Emily,\par
with a heavy sigh.\par
\par
“It is happy for you, that he does not,” rejoined Madame Montoni; “and\par
what I am now saying is from pure kindness. I am endeavouring to\par
convince you of your good fortune, and to persuade you to submit to\par
necessity with a good grace. It is nothing to me, you know, whether you\par
like this marriage or not, for it must be; what I say, therefore, is\par
from pure kindness. I wish to see you happy, and it is your own fault\par
if you are not so. I would ask you, now, seriously and calmly, what\par
kind of a match you can expect, since a Count cannot content your\par
ambition?”\par
\par
“I have no ambition whatever, madam,” replied Emily, “my only wish is\par
to remain in my present station.”\par
\par
“O! that is speaking quite from the purpose,” said her aunt, “I see you\par
are still thinking of Mons. Valancourt. Pray get rid of all those\par
fantastic notions about love, and this ridiculous pride, and be\par
something like a reasonable creature. But, however, this is nothing to\par
the purpose—for your marriage with the Count takes place tomorrow, you\par
know, whether you approve it or not. The Count will be trifled with no\par
longer.”\par
\par
Emily made no attempt to reply to this curious speech; she felt it\par
would be mean, and she knew it would be useless. Madame Montoni laid\par
the Count’s presents upon the table, on which Emily was leaning, and\par
then, desiring she would be ready early in the morning, bade her\par
good-night. “Good-night, madam,” said Emily, with a deep sigh, as the\par
door closed upon her aunt, and she was left once more to her own sad\par
reflections. For some time she sat so lost in thought, as to be wholly\par
unconscious where she was; at length, raising her head, and looking\par
round the room, its gloom and profound stillness awed her. She fixed\par
her eyes on the door, through which her aunt had disappeared, and\par
listened anxiously for some sound, that might relieve the deep\par
dejection of her spirits; but it was past midnight, and all the family\par
except the servant, who sat up for Montoni, had retired to bed. Her\par
mind, long harassed by distress, now yielded to imaginary terrors; she\par
trembled to look into the obscurity of her spacious chamber, and feared\par
she knew not what; a state of mind, which continued so long, that she\par
would have called up Annette, her aunt’s woman, had her fears permitted\par
her to rise from her chair, and to cross the apartment.\par
\par
These melancholy illusions at length began to disperse, and she retired\par
to her bed, not to sleep, for that was scarcely possible, but to try,\par
at least, to quiet her disturbed fancy, and to collect strength of\par
spirits sufficient to bear her through the scene of the approaching\par
morning.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER V\par
\par
Dark power! with shudd’ring, meek submitted thought\par
Be mine to read the visions old\par
Which thy awak’ning bards have told,\par
And, lest they meet my blasted view,\par
Hold each strange tale devoutly true.\par
                    COLLINS’ ODE TO FEAR\par
\par
\par
Emily was recalled from a kind of slumber, into which she had, at\par
length, sunk, by a quick knocking at her chamber door. She started up\par
in terror, for Montoni and Count Morano instantly came to her mind;\par
but, having listened in silence for some time, and recognising the\par
voice of Annette, she rose and opened the door. “What brings you hither\par
so early?” said Emily, trembling excessively. She was unable to support\par
herself, and sat down on the bed.\par
\par
“Dear ma’amselle!” said Annette, “do not look so pale. I am quite\par
frightened to see you. Here is a fine bustle below stairs, all the\par
servants running to and fro, and none of them fast enough! Here is a\par
bustle, indeed, all of a sudden, and nobody knows for what!”\par
\par
“Who is below besides them?” said Emily, “Annette, do not trifle with\par
me!”\par
\par
“Not for the world, ma’amselle, I would not trifle for the world; but\par
one cannot help making one’s remarks, and there is the Signor in such a\par
bustle, as I never saw him before; and he has sent me to tell you,\par
ma’am, to get ready immediately.”\par
\par
“Good God support me!” cried Emily, almost fainting, “Count Morano is\par
below, then!”\par
\par
“No, ma’amselle, he is not below that I know of,” replied Annette,\par
“only his _Excellenza_ sent me to desire you would get ready directly\par
to leave Venice, for that the gondolas would be at the steps of the\par
canal in a few minutes: but I must hurry back to my lady, who is just\par
at her wits’ end, and knows not which way to turn for haste.”\par
\par
“Explain, Annette, explain the meaning of all this before you go,” said\par
Emily, so overcome with surprise and timid hope, that she had scarcely\par
breath to speak.\par
\par
“Nay, ma’amselle, that is more than I can do. I only know that the\par
Signor is just come home in a very ill humour, that he has had us all\par
called out of our beds, and tells us we are all to leave Venice\par
immediately.”\par
\par
“Is Count Morano to go with the Signor?” said Emily, “and whither are\par
we going?”\par
\par
“I know neither, ma’am, for certain; but I heard Ludovico say something\par
about going, after we get to _Terra-firma_, to the signor’s castle\par
among some mountains, that he talked of.”\par
\par
“The Apennines!” said Emily, eagerly, “O! then I have little to hope!”\par
\par
“That is the very place, ma’am. But cheer up, and do not take it so\par
much to heart, and think what a little time you have to get ready in,\par
and how impatient the Signor is. Holy St. Mark! I hear the oars on the\par
canal; and now they come nearer, and now they are dashing at the steps\par
below; it is the gondola, sure enough.”\par
\par
Annette hastened from the room; and Emily prepared for this unexpected\par
flight, as fast as her trembling hands would permit, not perceiving,\par
that any change in her situation could possibly be for the worse. She\par
had scarcely thrown her books and clothes into her travelling trunk,\par
when, receiving a second summons, she went down to her aunt’s\par
dressing-room, where she found Montoni impatiently reproving his wife\par
for delay. He went out, soon after, to give some further orders to his\par
people, and Emily then enquired the occasion of this hasty journey; but\par
her aunt appeared to be as ignorant as herself, and to undertake the\par
journey with more reluctance.\par
\par
The family at length embarked, but neither Count Morano, nor Cavigni,\par
was of the party. Somewhat revived by observing this, Emily, when the\par
gondolieri dashed their oars in the water, and put off from the steps\par
of the portico, felt like a criminal, who receives a short reprieve.\par
Her heart beat yet lighter, when they emerged from the canal into the\par
ocean, and lighter still, when they skimmed past the walls of St. Mark,\par
without having stopped to take up Count Morano.\par
\par
The dawn now began to tint the horizon, and to break upon the shores of\par
the Adriatic. Emily did not venture to ask any questions of Montoni,\par
who sat, for some time, in gloomy silence, and then rolled himself up\par
in his cloak, as if to sleep, while Madame Montoni did the same; but\par
Emily, who could not sleep, undrew one of the little curtains of the\par
gondola, and looked out upon the sea. The rising dawn now enlightened\par
the mountain-tops of Friuli, but their lower sides, and the distant\par
waves, that rolled at their feet, were still in deep shadow. Emily,\par
sunk in tranquil melancholy, watched the strengthening light spreading\par
upon the ocean, showing successively Venice and her islets, and the\par
shores of Italy, along which boats, with their pointed latin sails,\par
began to move.\par
\par
The gondolieri were frequently hailed, at this early hour, by the\par
market-people, as they glided by towards Venice, and the lagune soon\par
displayed a gay scene of innumerable little barks, passing from\par
_Terra-firma_ with provisions. Emily gave a last look to that splendid\par
city, but her mind was then occupied by considering the probable\par
events, that awaited her, in the scenes, to which she was removing, and\par
with conjectures, concerning the motive of this sudden journey. It\par
appeared, upon calmer consideration, that Montoni was removing her to\par
his secluded castle, because he could there, with more probability of\par
success, attempt to terrify her into obedience; or, that, should its\par
gloomy and sequestered scenes fail of this effect, her forced marriage\par
with the Count could there be solemnized with the secrecy, which was\par
necessary to the honour of Montoni. The little spirit, which this\par
reprieve had recalled, now began to fail, and, when Emily reached the\par
shore, her mind had sunk into all its former depression.\par
\par
Montoni did not embark on the Brenta, but pursued his way in carriages\par
across the country, towards the Apennine; during which journey, his\par
manner to Emily was so particularly severe, that this alone would have\par
confirmed her late conjecture, had any such confirmation been\par
necessary. Her senses were now dead to the beautiful country, through\par
which she travelled. Sometimes she was compelled to smile at the\par
_naïveté_ of Annette, in her remarks on what she saw, and sometimes to\par
sigh, as a scene of peculiar beauty recalled Valancourt to her\par
thoughts, who was indeed seldom absent from them, and of whom she could\par
never hope to hear in the solitude, to which she was hastening.\par
\par
At length, the travellers began to ascend among the Apennines. The\par
immense pine-forests, which, at that period, overhung these mountains,\par
and between which the road wound, excluded all view but of the cliffs\par
aspiring above, except, that, now and then, an opening through the dark\par
woods allowed the eye a momentary glimpse of the country below. The\par
gloom of these shades, their solitary silence, except when the breeze\par
swept over their summits, the tremendous precipices of the mountains,\par
that came partially to the eye, each assisted to raise the solemnity of\par
Emily’s feelings into awe; she saw only images of gloomy grandeur, or\par
of dreadful sublimity, around her; other images, equally gloomy and\par
equally terrible, gleamed on her imagination. She was going she\par
scarcely knew whither, under the dominion of a person, from whose\par
arbitrary disposition she had already suffered so much, to marry,\par
perhaps, a man who possessed neither her affection, nor esteem; or to\par
endure, beyond the hope of succour, whatever punishment revenge, and\par
that Italian revenge, might dictate.—The more she considered what might\par
be the motive of the journey, the more she became convinced, that it\par
was for the purpose of concluding her nuptials with Count Morano, with\par
that secrecy, which her resolute resistance had made necessary to the\par
honour, if not to the safety, of Montoni. From the deep solitudes, into\par
which she was emerging, and from the gloomy castle, of which she had\par
heard some mysterious hints, her sick heart recoiled in despair, and\par
she experienced, that, though her mind was already occupied by peculiar\par
distress, it was still alive to the influence of new and local\par
circumstance; why else did she shudder at the idea of this desolate\par
castle?\par
\par
As the travellers still ascended among the pine forests, steep rose\par
over steep, the mountains seemed to multiply, as they went, and what\par
was the summit of one eminence proved to be only the base of another.\par
At length, they reached a little plain, where the drivers stopped to\par
rest the mules, whence a scene of such extent and magnificence opened\par
below, as drew even from Madame Montoni a note of admiration. Emily\par
lost, for a moment, her sorrows, in the immensity of nature. Beyond the\par
amphitheatre of mountains, that stretched below, whose tops appeared as\par
numerous almost, as the waves of the sea, and whose feet were concealed\par
by the forests—extended the _campagna_ of Italy, where cities and\par
rivers, and woods and all the glow of cultivation were mingled in gay\par
confusion. The Adriatic bounded the horizon, into which the Po and the\par
Brenta, after winding through the whole extent of the landscape, poured\par
their fruitful waves. Emily gazed long on the splendours of the world\par
she was quitting, of which the whole magnificence seemed thus given to\par
her sight only to increase her regret on leaving it; for her,\par
Valancourt alone was in that world; to him alone her heart turned, and\par
for him alone fell her bitter tears.\par
\par
From this sublime scene the travellers continued to ascend among the\par
pines, till they entered a narrow pass of the mountains, which shut out\par
every feature of the distant country, and, in its stead, exhibited only\par
tremendous crags, impending over the road, where no vestige of\par
humanity, or even of vegetation, appeared, except here and there the\par
trunk and scathed branches of an oak, that hung nearly headlong from\par
the rock, into which its strong roots had fastened. This pass, which\par
led into the heart of the Apennine, at length opened to day, and a\par
scene of mountains stretched in long perspective, as wild as any the\par
travellers had yet passed. Still vast pine-forests hung upon their\par
base, and crowned the ridgy precipice, that rose perpendicularly from\par
the vale, while, above, the rolling mists caught the sunbeams, and\par
touched their cliffs with all the magical colouring of light and shade.\par
The scene seemed perpetually changing, and its features to assume new\par
forms, as the winding road brought them to the eye in different\par
attitudes; while the shifting vapours, now partially concealing their\par
minuter beauties and now illuminating them with splendid tints,\par
assisted the illusions of the sight.\par
\par
Though the deep valleys between these mountains were, for the most\par
part, clothed with pines, sometimes an abrupt opening presented a\par
perspective of only barren rocks, with a cataract flashing from their\par
summit among broken cliffs, till its waters, reaching the bottom,\par
foamed along with unceasing fury; and sometimes pastoral scenes\par
exhibited their “green delights” in the narrow vales, smiling amid\par
surrounding horror. There herds and flocks of goats and sheep, browsing\par
under the shade of hanging woods, and the shepherd’s little cabin,\par
reared on the margin of a clear stream, presented a sweet picture of\par
repose.\par
\par
Wild and romantic as were these scenes, their character had far less of\par
the sublime, that had those of the Alps, which guard the entrance of\par
Italy. Emily was often elevated, but seldom felt those emotions of\par
indescribable awe which she had so continually experienced, in her\par
passage over the Alps.\par
\par
Towards the close of day, the road wound into a deep valley. Mountains,\par
whose shaggy steeps appeared to be inaccessible, almost surrounded it.\par
To the east, a vista opened, that exhibited the Apennines in their\par
darkest horrors; and the long perspective of retiring summits, rising\par
over each other, their ridges clothed with pines, exhibited a stronger\par
image of grandeur, than any that Emily had yet seen. The sun had just\par
sunk below the top of the mountains she was descending, whose long\par
shadow stretched athwart the valley, but his sloping rays, shooting\par
through an opening of the cliffs, touched with a yellow gleam the\par
summits of the forest, that hung upon the opposite steeps, and streamed\par
in full splendour upon the towers and battlements of a castle, that\par
spread its extensive ramparts along the brow of a precipice above. The\par
splendour of these illumined objects was heightened by the contrasted\par
shade, which involved the valley below.\par
\par
“There,” said Montoni, speaking for the first time in several hours,\par
“is Udolpho.”\par
\par
Emily gazed with melancholy awe upon the castle, which she understood\par
to be Montoni’s; for, though it was now lighted up by the setting sun,\par
the gothic greatness of its features, and its mouldering walls of dark\par
grey stone, rendered it a gloomy and sublime object. As she gazed, the\par
light died away on its walls, leaving a melancholy purple tint, which\par
spread deeper and deeper, as the thin vapour crept up the mountain,\par
while the battlements above were still tipped with splendour. From\par
those, too, the rays soon faded, and the whole edifice was invested\par
with the solemn duskiness of evening. Silent, lonely, and sublime, it\par
seemed to stand the sovereign of the scene, and to frown defiance on\par
all, who dared to invade its solitary reign. As the twilight deepened,\par
its features became more awful in obscurity, and Emily continued to\par
gaze, till its clustering towers were alone seen, rising over the tops\par
of the woods, beneath whose thick shade the carriages soon after began\par
to ascend.\par
\par
The extent and darkness of these tall woods awakened terrific images in\par
her mind, and she almost expected to see banditti start up from under\par
the trees. At length, the carriages emerged upon a heathy rock, and,\par
soon after, reached the castle gates, where the deep tone of the portal\par
bell, which was struck upon to give notice of their arrival, increased\par
the fearful emotions, that had assailed Emily. While they waited till\par
the servant within should come to open the gates, she anxiously\par
surveyed the edifice: but the gloom, that overspread it, allowed her to\par
distinguish little more than a part of its outline, with the massy\par
walls of the ramparts, and to know, that it was vast, ancient and\par
dreary. From the parts she saw, she judged of the heavy strength and\par
extent of the whole. The gateway before her, leading into the courts,\par
was of gigantic size, and was defended by two round towers, crowned by\par
overhanging turrets, embattled, where, instead of banners, now waved\par
long grass and wild plants, that had taken root among the mouldering\par
stones, and which seemed to sigh, as the breeze rolled past, over the\par
desolation around them. The towers were united by a curtain, pierced\par
and embattled also, below which appeared the pointed arch of a huge\par
portcullis, surmounting the gates: from these, the walls of the\par
ramparts extended to other towers, overlooking the precipice, whose\par
shattered outline, appearing on a gleam, that lingered in the west,\par
told of the ravages of war.—Beyond these all was lost in the obscurity\par
of evening.\par
\par
While Emily gazed with awe upon the scene, footsteps were heard within\par
the gates, and the undrawing of bolts; after which an ancient servant\par
of the castle appeared, forcing back the huge folds of the portal, to\par
admit his lord. As the carriage-wheels rolled heavily under the\par
portcullis, Emily’s heart sunk, and she seemed, as if she was going\par
into her prison; the gloomy court, into which she passed, served to\par
confirm the idea, and her imagination, ever awake to circumstance,\par
suggested even more terrors, than her reason could justify.\par
\par
Another gate delivered them into the second court, grass-grown, and\par
more wild than the first, where, as she surveyed through the twilight\par
its desolation—its lofty walls, overtopped with briony, moss and\par
nightshade, and the embattled towers that rose above,—long-suffering\par
and murder came to her thoughts. One of those instantaneous and\par
unaccountable convictions, which sometimes conquer even strong minds,\par
impressed her with its horror. The sentiment was not diminished, when\par
she entered an extensive gothic hall, obscured by the gloom of evening,\par
which a light, glimmering at a distance through a long perspective of\par
arches, only rendered more striking. As a servant brought the lamp\par
nearer partial gleams fell upon the pillars and the pointed arches,\par
forming a strong contrast with their shadows, that stretched along the\par
pavement and the walls.\par
\par
The sudden journey of Montoni had prevented his people from making any\par
other preparations for his reception, than could be had in the short\par
interval, since the arrival of the servant, who had been sent forward\par
from Venice; and this, in some measure, may account for the air of\par
extreme desolation, that everywhere appeared.\par
\par
The servant, who came to light Montoni, bowed in silence, and the\par
muscles of his countenance relaxed with no symptom of joy.—Montoni\par
noticed the salutation by a slight motion of his hand, and passed on,\par
while his lady, following, and looking round with a degree of surprise\par
and discontent, which she seemed fearful of expressing, and Emily,\par
surveying the extent and grandeur of the hall in timid wonder,\par
approached a marble staircase. The arches here opened to a lofty vault,\par
from the centre of which hung a tripod lamp, which a servant was\par
hastily lighting; and the rich fret-work of the roof, a corridor,\par
leading into several upper apartments, and a painted window, stretching\par
nearly from the pavement to the ceiling of the hall, became gradually\par
visible.\par
\par
Having crossed the foot of the staircase, and passed through an\par
ante-room, they entered a spacious apartment, whose walls, wainscoted\par
with black larch-wood, the growth of the neighbouring mountains, were\par
scarcely distinguishable from darkness itself. “Bring more light,” said\par
Montoni, as he entered. The servant, setting down his lamp, was\par
withdrawing to obey him, when Madame Montoni observing, that the\par
evening air of this mountainous region was cold, and that she should\par
like a fire, Montoni ordered that wood might be brought.\par
\par
While he paced the room with thoughtful steps, and Madame Montoni sat\par
silently on a couch, at the upper end of it, waiting till the servant\par
returned, Emily was observing the singular solemnity and desolation of\par
the apartment, viewed, as it now was, by the glimmer of the single\par
lamp, placed near a large Venetian mirror, that duskily reflected the\par
scene, with the tall figure of Montoni passing slowly along, his arms\par
folded, and his countenance shaded by the plume, that waved in his hat.\par
\par
From the contemplation of this scene, Emily’s mind proceeded to the\par
apprehension of what she might suffer in it, till the remembrance of\par
Valancourt, far, far distant! came to her heart, and softened it into\par
sorrow. A heavy sigh escaped her: but, trying to conceal her tears, she\par
walked away to one of the high windows, that opened upon the ramparts,\par
below which, spread the woods she had passed in her approach to the\par
castle. But the night-shade sat deeply on the mountains beyond, and\par
their indented outline alone could be faintly traced on the horizon,\par
where a red streak yet glimmered in the west. The valley between was\par
sunk in darkness.\par
\par
The scene within, upon which Emily turned on the opening of the door,\par
was scarcely less gloomy. The old servant, who had received them at the\par
gates, now entered, bending under a load of pine-branches, while two of\par
Montoni’s Venetian servants followed with lights.\par
\par
“Your _Excellenza_ is welcome to the castle,” said the old man, as he\par
raised himself from the hearth, where he had laid the wood: “it has\par
been a lonely place a long while; but you will excuse it, Signor,\par
knowing we had but short notice. It is near two years, come next feast\par
of St. Mark, since your _Excellenza_ was within these walls.”\par
\par
“You have a good memory, old Carlo,” said Montoni: “it is thereabout;\par
and how hast thou contrived to live so long?”\par
\par
“A-well-a-day, sir, with much ado; the cold winds, that blow through\par
the castle in winter, are almost too much for me; and I thought\par
sometimes of asking your _Excellenza_ to let me leave the mountains,\par
and go down into the lowlands. But I don’t know how it is—I am loth to\par
quit these old walls I have lived in so long.”\par
\par
“Well, how have you gone on in the castle, since I left it?” said\par
Montoni.\par
\par
“Why much as usual, Signor, only it wants a good deal of repairing.\par
There is the north tower—some of the battlements have tumbled down, and\par
had liked one day to have knocked my poor wife (God rest her soul!) on\par
the head. Your _Excellenza_ must know—”\par
\par
“Well, but the repairs,” interrupted Montoni.\par
\par
“Aye, the repairs,” said Carlo: “a part of the roof of the great hall\par
has fallen in, and all the winds from the mountains rushed through it\par
last winter, and whistled through the whole castle so, that there was\par
no keeping one’s self warm, be where one would. There, my wife and I\par
used to sit shivering over a great fire in one corner of the little\par
hall, ready to die with cold, and—”\par
\par
“But there are no more repairs wanted,” said Montoni, impatiently.\par
\par
“O Lord! Your _Excellenza_, yes—the wall of the rampart has tumbled\par
down in three places; then, the stairs, that lead to the west gallery,\par
have been a long time so bad, that it is dangerous to go up them; and\par
the passage leading to the great oak chamber, that overhangs the north\par
rampart—one night last winter I ventured to go there by myself, and\par
your _Excellenza_—”\par
\par
“Well, well, enough of this,” said Montoni, with quickness: “I will\par
talk more with thee tomorrow.”\par
\par
The fire was now lighted; Carlo swept the hearth, placed chairs, wiped\par
the dust from a large marble table that stood near it, and then left\par
the room.\par
\par
Montoni and his family drew round the fire. Madame Montoni made several\par
attempts at conversation, but his sullen answers repulsed her, while\par
Emily sat endeavouring to acquire courage enough to speak to him. At\par
length, in a tremulous voice, she said, “May I ask, sir, the motive of\par
this sudden journey?”—After a long pause, she recovered sufficient\par
courage to repeat the question.\par
\par
“It does not suit me to answer enquiries,” said Montoni, “nor does it\par
become you to make them; time may unfold them all: but I desire I may\par
be no further harassed, and I recommend it to you to retire to your\par
chamber, and to endeavour to adopt a more rational conduct, than that\par
of yielding to fancies, and to a sensibility, which, to call it by the\par
gentlest name, is only a weakness.”\par
\par
Emily rose to withdraw. “Good night, madam,” said she to her aunt, with\par
an assumed composure, that could not disguise her emotion.\par
\par
“Good night, my dear,” said Madame Montoni, in a tone of kindness,\par
which her niece had never before heard from her; and the unexpected\par
endearment brought tears to Emily’s eyes. She curtsied to Montoni, and\par
was retiring; “But you do not know the way to your chamber,” said her\par
aunt. Montoni called the servant, who waited in the ante-room, and bade\par
him send Madame Montoni’s woman, with whom, in a few minutes, Emily\par
withdrew.\par
\par
“Do you know which is my room?” said she to Annette, as they crossed\par
the hall.\par
\par
“Yes, I believe I do, ma’amselle; but this is such a strange rambling\par
place! I have been lost in it already: they call it the double chamber,\par
over the south rampart, and I went up this great staircase to it. My\par
lady’s room is at the other end of the castle.”\par
\par
Emily ascended the marble staircase, and came to the corridor, as they\par
passed through which, Annette resumed her chat—“What a wild lonely\par
place this is, ma’am! I shall be quite frightened to live in it. How\par
often, and often have I wished myself in France again! I little\par
thought, when I came with my lady to see the world, that I should ever\par
be shut up in such a place as this, or I would never have left my own\par
country! This way, ma’amselle, down this turning. I can almost believe\par
in giants again, and such like, for this is just like one of their\par
castles; and, some night or other, I suppose I shall see fairies too,\par
hopping about in that great old hall, that looks more like a church,\par
with its huge pillars, than anything else.”\par
\par
“Yes,” said Emily, smiling, and glad to escape from more serious\par
thought, “if we come to the corridor, about midnight, and look down\par
into the hall, we shall certainly see it illuminated with a thousand\par
lamps, and the fairies tripping in gay circles to the sound of\par
delicious music; for it is in such places as this, you know, that they\par
come to hold their revels. But I am afraid, Annette, you will not be\par
able to pay the necessary penance for such a sight: and, if once they\par
hear your voice, the whole scene will vanish in an instant.”\par
\par
“O! if you will bear me company, ma’amselle, I will come to the\par
corridor, this very night, and I promise you I will hold my tongue; it\par
shall not be my fault if the show vanishes.—But do you think they will\par
come?”\par
\par
“I cannot promise that with certainty, but I will venture to say, it\par
will not be your fault if the enchantment should vanish.”\par
\par
“Well, ma’amselle, that is saying more than I expected of you: but I am\par
not so much afraid of fairies, as of ghosts, and they say there are a\par
plentiful many of them about the castle: now I should be frightened to\par
death, if I should chance to see any of them. But hush! ma’amselle,\par
walk softly! I have thought, several times, something passed by me.”\par
\par
“Ridiculous!” said Emily, “you must not indulge such fancies.”\par
\par
“O ma’am! they are not fancies, for aught I know; Benedetto says these\par
dismal galleries and halls are fit for nothing but ghosts to live in;\par
and I verily believe, if I _live_ long in them I shall turn to one\par
myself!”\par
\par
“I hope,” said Emily, “you will not suffer Signor Montoni to hear of\par
these weak fears; they would highly displease him.”\par
\par
“What, you know then, ma’amselle, all about it!” rejoined Annette. “No,\par
no, I do know better than to do so; though, if the Signor can sleep\par
sound, nobody else in the castle has any right to lie awake, I am\par
sure.” Emily did not appear to notice this remark.\par
\par
“Down this passage, ma’amselle; this leads to a back staircase. O! if I\par
see anything, I shall be frightened out of my wits!”\par
\par
“That will scarcely be possible,” said Emily smiling, as she followed\par
the winding of the passage, which opened into another gallery: and then\par
Annette, perceiving that she had missed her way, while she had been so\par
eloquently haranguing on ghosts and fairies, wandered about through\par
other passages and galleries, till, at length, frightened by their\par
intricacies and desolation, she called aloud for assistance: but they\par
were beyond the hearing of the servants, who were on the other side of\par
the castle, and Emily now opened the door of a chamber on the left.\par
\par
“O! do not go in there, ma’amselle,” said Annette, “you will only lose\par
yourself further.”\par
\par
“Bring the light forward,” said Emily, “we may possibly find our way\par
through these rooms.”\par
\par
Annette stood at the door, in an attitude of hesitation, with the light\par
held up to show the chamber, but the feeble rays spread through not\par
half of it. “Why do you hesitate?” said Emily, “let me see whither this\par
room leads.”\par
\par
Annette advanced reluctantly. It opened into a suite of spacious and\par
ancient apartments, some of which were hung with tapestry, and others\par
wainscoted with cedar and black larch-wood. What furniture there was,\par
seemed to be almost as old as the rooms, and retained an appearance of\par
grandeur, though covered with dust, and dropping to pieces with the\par
damps, and with age.\par
\par
“How cold these rooms are, ma’amselle!” said Annette: “nobody has lived\par
in them for many, many years, they say. Do let us go.”\par
\par
“They may open upon the great staircase, perhaps,” said Emily, passing\par
on till she came to a chamber, hung with pictures, and took the light\par
to examine that of a soldier on horseback in a field of battle.—He was\par
darting his spear upon a man, who lay under the feet of the horse, and\par
who held up one hand in a supplicating attitude. The soldier, whose\par
beaver was up, regarded him with a look of vengeance, and the\par
countenance, with that expression, struck Emily as resembling Montoni.\par
She shuddered, and turned from it. Passing the light hastily over\par
several other pictures, she came to one concealed by a veil of black\par
silk. The singularity of the circumstance struck her, and she stopped\par
before it, wishing to remove the veil, and examine what could thus\par
carefully be concealed, but somewhat wanting courage. “Holy Virgin!\par
what can this mean?” exclaimed Annette. “This is surely the picture\par
they told me of at Venice.”\par
\par
“What picture?” said Emily. “Why a picture—a picture,” replied Annette,\par
hesitatingly—“but I never could make out exactly what it was about,\par
either.”\par
\par
“Remove the veil, Annette.”\par
\par
“What! I, ma’amselle!—I! not for the world!” Emily, turning round, saw\par
Annette’s countenance grow pale. “And pray, what have you heard of this\par
picture, to terrify you so, my good girl?” said she. “Nothing,\par
ma’amselle: I have heard nothing, only let us find our way out.”\par
\par
“Certainly: but I wish first to examine the picture; take the light,\par
Annette, while I lift the veil.” Annette took the light, and\par
immediately walked away with it, disregarding Emily’s call to stay,\par
who, not choosing to be left alone in the dark chamber, at length\par
followed her. “What is the reason of this, Annette?” said Emily, when\par
she overtook her, “what have you heard concerning that picture, which\par
makes you so unwilling to stay when I bid you?”\par
\par
“I don’t know what is the reason, ma’amselle,” replied Annette, “nor\par
anything about the picture, only I have heard there is something very\par
dreadful belonging to it—and that it has been covered up in black _ever\par
since_—and that nobody has looked at it for a great many years—and it\par
somehow has to do with the owner of this castle before Signor Montoni\par
came to the possession of it—and—”\par
\par
“Well, Annette,” said Emily, smiling, “I perceive it is as you say—that\par
you know nothing about the picture.”\par
\par
“No, nothing, indeed, ma’amselle, for they made me promise never to\par
tell:—but—”\par
\par
“Well,” rejoined Emily, who observed that she was struggling between\par
her inclination to reveal a secret, and her apprehension for the\par
consequence, “I will enquire no further—”\par
\par
“No, pray, ma’am, do not.”\par
\par
“Lest you should tell all,” interrupted Emily.\par
\par
Annette blushed, and Emily smiled, and they passed on to the extremity\par
of this suite of apartments, and found themselves, after some further\par
perplexity, once more at the top of the marble staircase, where Annette\par
left Emily, while she went to call one of the servants of the castle to\par
show them to the chamber, for which they had been seeking.\par
\par
While she was absent, Emily’s thoughts returned to the picture; an\par
unwillingness to tamper with the integrity of a servant, had checked\par
her enquiries on this subject, as well as concerning some alarming\par
hints, which Annette had dropped respecting Montoni; though her\par
curiosity was entirely awakened, and she had perceived, that her\par
questions might easily be answered. She was now, however, inclined to\par
go back to the apartment and examine the picture; but the loneliness of\par
the hour and of the place, with the melancholy silence that reigned\par
around her, conspired with a certain degree of awe, excited by the\par
mystery attending this picture, to prevent her. She determined,\par
however, when daylight should have reanimated her spirits, to go\par
thither and remove the veil. As she leaned from the corridor, over the\par
staircase, and her eyes wandered round, she again observed, with\par
wonder, the vast strength of the walls, now somewhat decayed, and the\par
pillars of solid marble, that rose from the hall, and supported the\par
roof.\par
\par
A servant now appeared with Annette, and conducted Emily to her\par
chamber, which was in a remote part of the castle, and at the very end\par
of the corridor, from whence the suite of apartments opened, through\par
which they had been wandering. The lonely aspect of her room made Emily\par
unwilling that Annette should leave her immediately, and the dampness\par
of it chilled her with more than fear. She begged Caterina, the servant\par
of the castle, to bring some wood and light a fire.\par
\par
“Aye, lady, it’s many a year since a fire was lighted here,” said\par
Caterina.\par
\par
“You need not tell us that, good woman,” said Annette; “every room in\par
the castle feels like a well. I wonder how you contrive to live here;\par
for my part, I wish myself at Venice again.” Emily waved her hand for\par
Caterina to fetch the wood.\par
\par
“I wonder, ma’am, why they call this the double chamber?” said Annette,\par
while Emily surveyed it in silence and saw that it was lofty and\par
spacious, like the others she had seen, and, like many of them, too,\par
had its walls lined with dark larch-wood. The bed and other furniture\par
was very ancient, and had an air of gloomy grandeur, like all that she\par
had seen in the castle. One of the high casements, which she opened,\par
overlooked a rampart, but the view beyond was hid in darkness.\par
\par
In the presence of Annette, Emily tried to support her spirits, and to\par
restrain the tears, which, every now and then, came to her eyes. She\par
wished much to enquire when Count Morano was expected at the castle,\par
but an unwillingness to ask unnecessary questions, and to mention\par
family concerns to a servant, withheld her. Meanwhile, Annette’s\par
thoughts were engaged upon another subject: she dearly loved the\par
marvellous, and had heard of a circumstance, connected with the castle,\par
that highly gratified this taste. Having been enjoined not to mention\par
it, her inclination to tell it was so strong, that she was every\par
instant on the point of speaking what she had heard. Such a strange\par
circumstance, too, and to be obliged to conceal it, was a severe\par
punishment; but she knew, that Montoni might impose one much severer,\par
and she feared to incur it by offending him.\par
\par
Caterina now brought the wood, and its bright blaze dispelled, for a\par
while, the gloom of the chamber. She told Annette, that her lady had\par
enquired for her, and Emily was once again left to her own sad\par
reflections. Her heart was not yet hardened against the stern manners\par
of Montoni, and she was nearly as much shocked now, as she had been\par
when she first witnessed them. The tenderness and affection, to which\par
she had been accustomed, till she lost her parents, had made her\par
particularly sensible to any degree of unkindness, and such a reverse\par
as this no apprehension had prepared her to support.\par
\par
To call off her attention from subjects, that pressed heavily on her\par
spirits, she rose and again examined her room and its furniture. As she\par
walked round it, she passed a door, that was not quite shut, and,\par
perceiving, that it was not the one, through which she entered, she\par
brought the light forward to discover whither it led. She opened it,\par
and, going forward, had nearly fallen down a steep, narrow staircase\par
that wound from it, between two stone walls. She wished to know to what\par
it led, and was the more anxious, since it communicated so immediately\par
with her apartment; but, in the present state of her spirits, she\par
wanted courage to venture into the darkness alone. Closing the door,\par
therefore, she endeavoured to fasten it, but, upon further examination,\par
perceived, that it had no bolts on the chamber side, though it had two\par
on the other. By placing a heavy chair against it, she in some measure\par
remedied the defect; yet she was still alarmed at the thought of\par
sleeping in this remote room alone, with a door opening she knew not\par
whither, and which could not be perfectly fastened on the inside.\par
Sometimes she wished to entreat of Madame Montoni, that Annette might\par
have leave to remain with her all night, but was deterred by an\par
apprehension of betraying what would be thought childish fears, and by\par
an unwillingness to increase the apt terrors of Annette.\par
\par
Her gloomy reflections were, soon after, interrupted by a footstep in\par
the corridor, and she was glad to see Annette enter with some supper,\par
sent by Madame Montoni. Having a table near the fire, she made the good\par
girl sit down and sup with her; and, when their little repast was over,\par
Annette, encouraged by her kindness and stirring the wood into a blaze,\par
drew her chair upon the hearth, nearer to Emily, and said—“Did you ever\par
hear, ma’amselle, of the strange accident, that made the Signor lord of\par
this castle?”\par
\par
“What wonderful story have you now to tell?” said Emily, concealing the\par
curiosity, occasioned by the mysterious hints she had formerly heard on\par
that subject.\par
\par
“I have heard all about it, ma’amselle,” said Annette, looking round\par
the chamber and drawing closer to Emily; “Benedetto told it me as we\par
travelled together: says he, ‘Annette, you don’t know about this castle\par
here, that we are going to?’ ‘No,’ says I, ‘Mr. Benedetto, pray what do\par
you know?’ But, ma’amselle, you can keep a secret, or I would not tell\par
it you for the world; for I promised never to tell, and they say, that\par
the Signor does not like to have it talked of.”\par
\par
“If you promised to keep this secret,” said Emily, “you do right not to\par
mention it.”\par
\par
Annette paused a moment, and then said, “O, but to you, ma’amselle, to\par
you I may tell it safely, I know.”\par
\par
Emily smiled, “I certainly shall keep it as faithful as yourself,\par
Annette.”\par
\par
Annette replied very gravely, that would do, and proceeded—“This\par
castle, you must know, ma’amselle, is very old, and very strong, and\par
has stood out many sieges as they say. Now it was not Signor Montoni’s\par
always, nor his father’s; no; but, by some law or other, it was to come\par
to the Signor, if the lady died unmarried.”\par
\par
“What lady?” said Emily.\par
\par
“I am not come to that yet,” replied Annette, “it is the lady I am\par
going to tell you about, ma’amselle: but, as I was saying, this lady\par
lived in the castle, and had everything very grand about her, as you\par
may suppose, ma’amselle. The Signor used often to come to see her, and\par
was in love with her, and offered to marry her; for, though he was\par
somehow related, that did not signify. But she was in love with\par
somebody else, and would not have him, which made him very angry, as\par
they say, and you know, ma’amselle, what an ill-looking gentleman he\par
is, when he is angry. Perhaps she saw him in a passion, and therefore\par
would not have him. But, as I was saying, she was very melancholy and\par
unhappy, and all that, for a long while, and—Holy Virgin! what noise is\par
that? did not you hear a sound, ma’amselle?”\par
\par
“It was only the wind,” said Emily, “but do come to the end of your\par
story.”\par
\par
“As I was saying—O, where was I?—as I was saying—she was very\par
melancholy and unhappy a long while, and used to walk about upon the\par
terrace, there, under the windows, by herself, and cry so! it would\par
have done your heart good to hear her. That is—I don’t mean good, but\par
it would have made you cry too, as they tell me.”\par
\par
“Well, but, Annette, do tell me the substance of your tale.”\par
\par
“All in good time, ma’am; all this I heard before at Venice, but what\par
is to come I never heard till today. This happened a great many years\par
ago, when Signor Montoni was quite a young man. The lady—they called\par
her Signora Laurentini, was very handsome, but she used to be in great\par
passions, too, sometimes, as well as the Signor. Finding he could not\par
make her listen to him—what does he do, but leave the castle, and never\par
comes near it for a long time! but it was all one to her; she was just\par
as unhappy whether he was here or not, till one evening, Holy St.\par
Peter! ma’amselle,” cried Annette, “look at that lamp, see how blue it\par
burns!” She looked fearfully round the chamber. “Ridiculous girl!” said\par
Emily, “why will you indulge those fancies? Pray let me hear the end of\par
your story, I am weary.”\par
\par
Annette still kept her eyes on the lamp, and proceeded in a lower\par
voice. “It was one evening, they say, at the latter end of the year, it\par
might be about the middle of September, I suppose, or the beginning of\par
October; nay, for that matter, it might be November, for that, too, is\par
the latter end of the year, but that I cannot say for certain, because\par
they did not tell me for certain themselves. However, it was at the\par
latter end of the year, this grand lady walked out of the castle into\par
the woods below, as she had often done before, all alone, only her maid\par
was with her. The wind blew cold, and strewed the leaves about, and\par
whistled dismally among those great old chesnut trees, that we passed,\par
ma’amselle, as we came to the castle—for Benedetto showed me the trees\par
as he was talking—the wind blew cold, and her woman would have\par
persuaded her to return: but all would not do, for she was fond of\par
walking in the woods, at evening time, and, if the leaves were falling\par
about her, so much the better.\par
\par
“Well, they saw her go down among the woods, but night came, and she\par
did not return: ten o’clock, eleven o’clock, twelve o’clock came, and\par
no lady! Well, the servants thought to be sure, some accident had\par
befallen her, and they went out to seek her. They searched all night\par
long, but could not find her, or any trace of her; and, from that day\par
to this, ma’amselle, she has never been heard of.”\par
\par
“Is this true, Annette?” said Emily, in much surprise.\par
\par
“True, ma’am!” said Annette, with a look of horror, “yes, it is true,\par
indeed. But they do say,” she added, lowering her voice, “they do say,\par
that the Signora has been seen, several times since, walking in the\par
woods and about the castle in the night: several of the old servants,\par
who remained here some time after, declare they saw her; and, since\par
then, she has been seen by some of the vassals, who have happened to be\par
in the castle, at night. Carlo, the old steward, could tell such\par
things, they say, if he would.”\par
\par
“How contradictory is this, Annette!” said Emily, “you say nothing has\par
been since known of her, and yet she has been seen!”\par
\par
“But all this was told me for a great secret,” rejoined Annette,\par
without noticing the remark, “and I am sure, ma’am, you would not hurt\par
either me or Benedetto, so much as to go and tell it again.” Emily\par
remained silent, and Annette repeated her last sentence.\par
\par
“You have nothing to fear from my indiscretion,” replied Emily, “and\par
let me advise you, my good Annette, be discreet yourself, and never\par
mention what you have just told me to any other person. Signor Montoni,\par
as you say, may be angry if he hears of it. But what inquiries were\par
made concerning the lady?”\par
\par
“O! a great deal, indeed, ma’amselle, for the Signor laid claim to the\par
castle directly, as being the next heir, and they said, that is, the\par
judges, or the senators, or somebody of that sort, said, he could not\par
take possession of it till so many years were gone by, and then, if,\par
after all, the lady could not be found, why she would be as good as\par
dead, and the castle would be his own; and so it is his own. But the\par
story went round, and many strange reports were spread, so very\par
strange, ma’amselle, that I shall not tell them.”\par
\par
“That is stranger still, Annette,” said Emily, smiling, and rousing\par
herself from her reverie. “But, when Signora Laurentini was afterwards\par
seen in the castle, did nobody speak to her?”\par
\par
“Speak—speak to her!” cried Annette, with a look of terror; “no, to be\par
sure.”\par
\par
“And why not?” rejoined Emily, willing to hear further.\par
\par
“Holy Mother! speak to a spirit!”\par
\par
“But what reason had they to conclude it was a spirit, unless they had\par
approached, and spoken to it?”\par
\par
“O ma’amselle, I cannot tell. How can you ask such shocking questions?\par
But nobody ever saw it come in, or go out of the castle; and it was in\par
one place now, and then the next minute in quite another part of the\par
castle; and then it never spoke, and, if it was alive, what should it\par
do in the castle if it never spoke? Several parts of the castle have\par
never been gone into since, they say, for that very reason.”\par
\par
“What, because it never spoke?” said Emily, trying to laugh away the\par
fears that began to steal upon her.\par
\par
“No, ma’amselle, no;” replied Annette, rather angrily “but because\par
something has been seen there. They say, too, there is an old chapel\par
adjoining the west side of the castle, where, any time at midnight, you\par
may hear such groans!—it makes one shudder to think of them!—and\par
strange sights have been seen there—”\par
\par
“Pr’ythee, Annette, no more of these silly tales,” said Emily.\par
\par
“Silly tales, ma’amselle! O, but I will tell you one story about this,\par
if you please, that Caterina told me. It was one cold winter’s night\par
that Caterina (she often came to the castle then, she says, to keep old\par
Carlo and his wife company, and so he recommended her afterwards to the\par
Signor, and she has lived here ever since) Caterina was sitting with\par
them in the little hall, says Carlo, ‘I wish we had some of those figs\par
to roast, that lie in the store-closet, but it is a long way off, and I\par
am loath to fetch them; do, Caterina,’ says he, ‘for you are young and\par
nimble, do bring us some, the fire is in nice trim for roasting them;\par
they lie,’ says he, ‘in such a corner of the store-room, at the end of\par
the north-gallery; here, take the lamp,’ says he, ‘and mind, as you go\par
up the great staircase, that the wind, through the roof, does not blow\par
it out.’ So, with that, Caterina took the lamp—Hush! ma’amselle, I\par
surely heard a noise!”\par
\par
Emily, whom Annette had now infected with her own terrors, listened\par
attentively; but everything was still, and Annette proceeded:\par
\par
“Caterina went to the north-gallery, that is the wide gallery we\par
passed, ma’am, before we came to the corridor, here. As she went with\par
the lamp in her hand, thinking of nothing at all—There, again!” cried\par
Annette suddenly—“I heard it again!—it was not fancy, ma’amselle!”\par
\par
“Hush!” said Emily, trembling. They listened, and, continuing to sit\par
quite still, Emily heard a low knocking against the wall. It came\par
repeatedly. Annette then screamed loudly, and the chamber door slowly\par
opened.—It was Caterina, come to tell Annette, that her lady wanted\par
her. Emily, though she now perceived who it was, could not immediately\par
overcome her terror; while Annette, half laughing, half crying, scolded\par
Caterina heartily for thus alarming them; and was also terrified lest\par
what she had told had been overheard.—Emily, whose mind was deeply\par
impressed by the chief circumstance of Annette’s relation, was\par
unwilling to be left alone, in the present state of her spirits; but,\par
to avoid offending Madame Montoni, and betraying her own weakness, she\par
struggled to overcome the illusions of fear, and dismissed Annette for\par
the night.\par
\par
When she was alone, her thoughts recurred to the strange history of\par
Signora Laurentini and then to her own strange situation, in the wild\par
and solitary mountains of a foreign country, in the castle, and the\par
power of a man, to whom, only a few preceding months, she was an entire\par
stranger; who had already exercised a usurped authority over her, and\par
whose character she now regarded, with a degree of terror, apparently\par
justified by the fears of others. She knew, that he had invention equal\par
to the conception and talents to the execution of any project, and she\par
greatly feared he had a heart too void of feeling to oppose the\par
perpetration of whatever his interest might suggest. She had long\par
observed the unhappiness of Madame Montoni, and had often been witness\par
to the stern and contemptuous behaviour she received from her husband.\par
To these circumstances, which conspired to give her just cause for\par
alarm, were now added those thousand nameless terrors, which exist only\par
in active imaginations, and which set reason and examination equally at\par
defiance.\par
\par
Emily remembered all that Valancourt had told her, on the eve of her\par
departure from Languedoc, respecting Montoni, and all that he had said\par
to dissuade her from venturing on the journey. His fears had often\par
since appeared to her prophetic—now they seemed confirmed. Her heart,\par
as it gave her back the image of Valancourt, mourned in vain regret,\par
but reason soon came with a consolation which, though feeble at first,\par
acquired vigour from reflection. She considered, that, whatever might\par
be her sufferings, she had withheld from involving him in misfortune,\par
and that, whatever her future sorrows could be, she was, at least, free\par
from self-reproach.\par
\par
Her melancholy was assisted by the hollow sighings of the wind along\par
the corridor and round the castle. The cheerful blaze of the wood had\par
long been extinguished, and she sat with her eyes fixed on the dying\par
embers, till a loud gust, that swept through the corridor, and shook\par
the doors and casements, alarmed her, for its violence had moved the\par
chair she had placed as a fastening, and the door, leading to the\par
private staircase stood half open. Her curiosity and her fears were\par
again awakened. She took the lamp to the top of the steps, and stood\par
hesitating whether to go down; but again the profound stillness and the\par
gloom of the place awed her, and, determining to enquire further, when\par
daylight might assist the search, she closed the door, and placed\par
against it a stronger guard.\par
\par
She now retired to her bed, leaving the lamp burning on the table; but\par
its gloomy light, instead of dispelling her fear, assisted it; for, by\par
its uncertain rays, she almost fancied she saw shapes flit past her\par
curtains and glide into the remote obscurity of her chamber.—The castle\par
clock struck one before she closed her eyes to sleep.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER VI\par
\par
I think it is the weakness of mine eyes,\par
That shapes this monstrous apparition.\par
It comes upon me!\par
                    JULIUS CÆSAR\par
\par
\par
Daylight dispelled from Emily’s mind the glooms of superstition, but\par
not those of apprehension. The Count Morano was the first image that\par
occurred to her waking thoughts, and then came a train of anticipated\par
evils, which she could neither conquer, nor avoid. She rose, and, to\par
relieve her mind from the busy ideas, that tormented it, compelled\par
herself to notice external objects. From her casement she looked out\par
upon the wild grandeur of the scene, closed nearly on all sides by\par
alpine steeps, whose tops, peeping over each other, faded from the eye\par
in misty hues, while the promontories below were dark with woods, that\par
swept down to their base, and stretched along the narrow valleys. The\par
rich pomp of these woods was particularly delightful to Emily; and she\par
viewed with astonishment the fortifications of the castle spreading\par
along a vast extent of rock, and now partly in decay, the grandeur of\par
the ramparts below, and the towers and battlements and various features\par
of the fabric above. From these her sight wandered over the cliffs and\par
woods into the valley, along which foamed a broad and rapid stream,\par
seen falling among the crags of an opposite mountain, now flashing in\par
the sunbeams, and now shadowed by over-arching pines, till it was\par
entirely concealed by their thick foliage. Again it burst from beneath\par
this darkness in one broad sheet of foam, and fell thundering into the\par
vale. Nearer, towards the west, opened the mountain-vista, which Emily\par
had viewed with such sublime emotion, on her approach to the castle: a\par
thin dusky vapour, that rose from the valley, overspread its features\par
with a sweet obscurity. As this ascended and caught the sunbeams, it\par
kindled into a crimson tint, and touched with exquisite beauty the\par
woods and cliffs, over which it passed to the summit of the mountains;\par
then, as the veil drew up, it was delightful to watch the gleaming\par
objects, that progressively disclosed themselves in the valley—the\par
green turf—dark woods—little rocky recesses—a few peasants’ huts—the\par
foaming stream—a herd of cattle, and various images of pastoral beauty.\par
Then, the pine-forests brightened, and then the broad breast of the\par
mountains, till, at length, the mist settled round their summit,\par
touching them with a ruddy glow. The features of the vista now appeared\par
distinctly, and the broad deep shadows, that fell from the lower\par
cliffs, gave strong effect to the streaming splendour above; while the\par
mountains, gradually sinking in the perspective, appeared to shelve\par
into the Adriatic sea, for such Emily imagined to be the gleam of\par
bluish light that terminated the view.\par
\par
Thus she endeavoured to amuse her fancy, and was not unsuccessful. The\par
breezy freshness of the morning, too, revived her. She raised her\par
thoughts in prayer, which she felt always most disposed to do, when\par
viewing the sublimity of nature, and her mind recovered its strength.\par
\par
When she turned from the casement, her eyes glanced upon the door she\par
had so carefully guarded, on the preceding night, and she now\par
determined to examine whither it led; but, on advancing to remove the\par
chairs, she perceived, that they were already moved a little way. Her\par
surprise cannot be easily imagined, when, in the next minute, she\par
perceived that the door was fastened.—She felt, as if she had seen an\par
apparition. The door of the corridor was locked as she had left it, but\par
this door, which could be secured only on the outside, must have been\par
bolted, during the night. She became seriously uneasy at the thought of\par
sleeping again in a chamber, thus liable to intrusion, so remote, too,\par
as it was from the family, and she determined to mention the\par
circumstance to Madame Montoni, and to request a change.\par
\par
After some perplexity she found her way into the great hall, and to the\par
room, which she had left, on the preceding night, where breakfast was\par
spread, and her aunt was alone, for Montoni had been walking over the\par
environs of the castle, examining the condition of its fortifications,\par
and talking for some time with Carlo. Emily observed that her aunt had\par
been weeping, and her heart softened towards her, with an affection,\par
that showed itself in her manner, rather than in words, while she\par
carefully avoided the appearance of having noticed, that she was\par
unhappy. She seized the opportunity of Montoni’s absence to mention the\par
circumstance of the door, to request that she might be allowed another\par
apartment, and to enquire again, concerning the occasion of their\par
sudden journey. On the first subject her aunt referred her to Montoni,\par
positively refusing to interfere in the affair; on the last, she\par
professed utter ignorance.\par
\par
Emily, then, with a wish of making her aunt more reconciled to her\par
situation, praised the grandeur of the castle and the surrounding\par
scenery, and endeavoured to soften every unpleasing circumstance\par
attending it. But, though misfortune had somewhat conquered the\par
asperities of Madame Montoni’s temper, and, by increasing her cares for\par
herself, had taught her to feel in some degree for others, the\par
capricious love of rule, which nature had planted and habit had\par
nourished in her heart, was not subdued. She could not now deny herself\par
the gratification of tyrannising over the innocent and helpless Emily,\par
by attempting to ridicule the taste she could not feel.\par
\par
Her satirical discourse was, however, interrupted by the entrance of\par
Montoni, and her countenance immediately assumed a mingled expression\par
of fear and resentment, while he seated himself at the breakfast-table,\par
as if unconscious of there being any person but himself in the room.\par
\par
Emily, as she observed him in silence, saw, that his countenance was\par
darker and sterner than usual. “O could I know,” said she to herself,\par
“what passes in that mind; could I know the thoughts, that are known\par
there, I should no longer be condemned to this torturing suspense!”\par
Their breakfast passed in silence, till Emily ventured to request, that\par
another apartment might be allotted to her, and related the\par
circumstance which made her wish it.\par
\par
“I have no time to attend to these idle whims,” said Montoni, “that\par
chamber was prepared for you, and you must rest contented with it. It\par
is not probable, that any person would take the trouble of going to\par
that remote staircase, for the purpose of fastening a door. If it was\par
not fastened, when you entered the chamber, the wind, perhaps, shook\par
the door and made the bolts slide. But I know not why I should\par
undertake to account for so trifling an occurrence.”\par
\par
This explanation was by no means satisfactory to Emily, who had\par
observed, that the bolts were rusted, and consequently could not be\par
thus easily moved; but she forbore to say so, and repeated her request.\par
\par
“If you will not release yourself from the slavery of these fears,”\par
said Montoni, sternly, “at least forbear to torment others by the\par
mention of them. Conquer such whims, and endeavour to strengthen your\par
mind. No existence is more contemptible than that, which is embittered\par
by fear.” As he said this, his eye glanced upon Madame Montoni, who\par
coloured highly, but was still silent. Emily, wounded and disappointed,\par
thought her fears were, in this instance, too reasonable to deserve\par
ridicule; but, perceiving, that, however they might oppress her, she\par
must endure them, she tried to withdraw her attention from the subject.\par
\par
Carlo soon after entered with some fruit:\par
\par
“Your _Excellenza_ is tired after your long ramble,” said he, as he set\par
the fruit upon the table; “but you have more to see after breakfast.\par
There is a place in the vaulted passage leading to—”\par
\par
Montoni frowned upon him, and waved his hand for him to leave the room.\par
Carlo stopped, looked down, and then added, as he advanced to the\par
breakfast-table, and took up the basket of fruit, “I made bold, your\par
_Excellenza_, to bring some cherries, here, for my honoured lady and my\par
young mistress. Will your ladyship taste them, madam?” said Carlo,\par
presenting the basket, “they are very fine ones, though I gathered them\par
myself, and from an old tree, that catches all the south sun; they are\par
as big as plums, your ladyship.”\par
\par
“Very well, old Carlo,” said Madame Montoni; “I am obliged to you.”\par
\par
“And the young Signora, too, she may like some of them,” rejoined\par
Carlo, turning with the basket to Emily, “it will do me good to see her\par
eat some.”\par
\par
“Thank you, Carlo,” said Emily, taking some cherries, and smiling\par
kindly.\par
\par
“Come, come,” said Montoni, impatiently, “enough of this. Leave the\par
room, but be in waiting. I shall want you presently.”\par
\par
Carlo obeyed, and Montoni, soon after, went out to examine further into\par
the state of the castle; while Emily remained with her aunt, patiently\par
enduring her ill humour, and endeavouring, with much sweetness, to\par
soothe her affliction, instead of resenting its effect.\par
\par
When Madame Montoni retired to her dressing-room, Emily endeavoured to\par
amuse herself by a view of the castle. Through a folding door she\par
passed from the great hall to the ramparts, which extended along the\par
brow of the precipice, round three sides of the edifice; the fourth was\par
guarded by the high walls of the courts, and by the gateway, through\par
which she had passed, on the preceding evening. The grandeur of the\par
broad ramparts, and the changing scenery they overlooked, excited her\par
high admiration; for the extent of the terraces allowed the features of\par
the country to be seen in such various points of view, that they\par
appeared to form new landscapes. She often paused to examine the gothic\par
magnificence of Udolpho, its proud irregularity, its lofty towers and\par
battlements, its high-arched casements, and its slender watch-towers,\par
perched upon the corners of turrets. Then she would lean on the wall of\par
the terrace, and, shuddering, measure with her eye the precipice below,\par
till the dark summits of the woods arrested it. Wherever she turned,\par
appeared mountain-tops, forests of pine and narrow glens, opening among\par
the Apennines and retiring from the sight into inaccessible regions.\par
\par
While she thus leaned, Montoni, followed by two men, appeared,\par
ascending a winding path, cut in the rock below. He stopped upon a\par
cliff, and, pointing to the ramparts, turned to his followers, and\par
talked with much eagerness of gesticulation.—Emily perceived, that one\par
of these men was Carlo; the other was in the dress of a peasant, and he\par
alone seemed to be receiving the directions of Montoni.\par
\par
She withdrew from the walls, and pursued her walk, till she heard at a\par
distance the sound of carriage wheels, and then the loud bell of the\par
portal, when it instantly occurred to her, that Count Morano was\par
arrived. As she hastily passed the folding doors from the terrace,\par
towards her own apartment, several persons entered the hall by an\par
opposite door. She saw them at the extremities of the arcades, and\par
immediately retreated; but the agitation of her spirits, and the extent\par
and duskiness of the hall, had prevented her from distinguishing the\par
persons of the strangers. Her fears, however, had but one object, and\par
they had called up that object to her fancy:—she believed that she had\par
seen Count Morano.\par
\par
When she thought that they had passed the hall, she ventured again to\par
the door, and proceeded, unobserved, to her room, where she remained,\par
agitated with apprehensions, and listening to every distant sound. At\par
length, hearing voices on the rampart, she hastened to her window, and\par
observed Montoni, with Signor Cavigni, walking below, conversing\par
earnestly, and often stopping and turning towards each other, at which\par
time their discourse seemed to be uncommonly interesting.\par
\par
Of the several persons who had appeared in the hall, here was Cavigni\par
alone: but Emily’s alarm was soon after heightened by the steps of some\par
one in the corridor, who, she apprehended, brought a message from the\par
Count. In the next moment, Annette appeared.\par
\par
“Ah! ma’amselle,” said she, “here is the Signor Cavigni arrived! I am\par
sure I rejoiced to see a christian person in this place; and then he is\par
so good natured too, he always takes so much notice of me!—And here is\par
also Signor Verezzi, and who do you think besides, ma’amselle?”\par
\par
“I cannot guess, Annette; tell me quickly.”\par
\par
“Nay, ma’am, do guess once.”\par
\par
“Well, then,” said Emily, with assumed composure, “it is—Count Morano,\par
I suppose.”\par
\par
“Holy Virgin!” cried Annette, “are you ill, ma’amselle? you are going\par
to faint! let me get some water.”\par
\par
Emily sunk into a chair. “Stay, Annette,” said she, feebly, “do not\par
leave me—I shall soon be better; open the casement.—The Count, you\par
say—he is come, then?”\par
\par
“Who, I!—the Count! No, ma’amselle, I did not say so.”\par
\par
“He is _not_ come then?” said Emily eagerly.\par
\par
“No, ma’amselle.”\par
\par
“You are sure of it?”\par
\par
“Lord bless me!” said Annette, “you recover very suddenly, ma’am! why,\par
I thought you were dying, just now.”\par
\par
“But the Count—you are sure, is not come?”\par
\par
“O yes, quite sure of that, ma’amselle. Why, I was looking out through\par
the grate in the north turret, when the carriages drove into the\par
courtyard, and I never expected to see such a goodly sight in this\par
dismal old castle! but here are masters and servants, too, enough to\par
make the place ring again. O! I was ready to leap through the rusty old\par
bars for joy!—O! who would ever have thought of seeing a christian face\par
in this huge dreary house? I could have kissed the very horses that\par
brought them.”\par
\par
“Well, Annette, well, I am better now.”\par
\par
“Yes, ma’amselle, I see you are. O! all the servants will lead merry\par
lives here, now; we shall have singing and dancing in the little hall,\par
for the Signor cannot hear us there—and droll stories—Ludovico’s come,\par
ma’am; yes, there is Ludovico come with them! You remember Ludovico,\par
ma’am—a tall, handsome young man—Signor Cavigni’s lacquey—who always\par
wears his cloak with such a grace, thrown round his left arm, and his\par
hat set on so smartly, all on one side, and—”\par
\par
“No,” said Emily, who was wearied by her loquacity.\par
\par
“What, ma’amselle, don’t you remember Ludovico—who rowed the\par
Cavaliero’s gondola, at the last regatta, and won the prize? And who\par
used to sing such sweet verses about Orlandos and about the\par
Black-a-moors, too; and Charly—Charly—magne, yes, that was the name,\par
all under my lattice, in the west portico, on the moonlight nights at\par
Venice? O! I have listened to him!—”\par
\par
“I fear, to thy peril, my good Annette,” said Emily; “for it seems his\par
verses have stolen thy heart. But let me advise you; if it is so, keep\par
the secret; never let him know it.”\par
\par
“Ah—ma’amselle!—how can one keep such a secret as that?”\par
\par
“Well, Annette, I am now so much better, that you may leave me.”\par
\par
“O, but, ma’amselle, I forgot to ask—how did you sleep in this dreary\par
old chamber last night?”—“As well as usual.”—“Did you hear no\par
noises?”—“None.”—“Nor see anything?”—“Nothing.”—“Well, that is\par
surprising!”—“Not in the least: and now tell me, why you ask these\par
questions.”\par
\par
“O, ma’amselle! I would not tell you for the world, nor all I have\par
heard about this chamber, either; it would frighten you so.”\par
\par
“If that is all, you have frightened me already, and may therefore tell\par
me what you know, without hurting your conscience.”\par
\par
“O Lord! they say the room is haunted, and has been so these many\par
years.”\par
\par
“It is by a ghost, then, who can draw bolts,” said Emily, endeavouring\par
to laugh away her apprehensions; “for I left the door open, last night,\par
and found it fastened this morning.”\par
\par
Annette turned pale, and said not a word.\par
\par
“Do you know whether any of the servants fastened this door in the\par
morning, before I rose?”\par
\par
“No, ma’am, that I will be bound they did not; but I don’t know: shall\par
I go and ask, ma’amselle?” said Annette, moving hastily towards the\par
corridor.\par
\par
“Stay, Annette, I have another question to ask; tell me what you have\par
heard concerning this room, and whither that staircase leads.”\par
\par
“I will go and ask it all directly, ma’am; besides, I am sure my lady\par
wants me. I cannot stay now, indeed, ma’am.”\par
\par
She hurried from the room, without waiting Emily’s reply, whose heart,\par
lightened by the certainty, that Morano was not arrived, allowed her to\par
smile at the superstitious terror, which had seized on Annette; for,\par
though she sometimes felt its influence herself, she could smile at it,\par
when apparent in other persons.\par
\par
Montoni having refused Emily another chamber, she determined to bear\par
with patience the evil she could not remove, and, in order to make the\par
room as comfortable as possible, unpacked her books, her sweet delight\par
in happier days, and her soothing resource in the hours of moderate\par
sorrow: but there were hours when even these failed of their effect;\par
when the genius, the taste, the enthusiasm of the sublimest writers\par
were felt no longer.\par
\par
Her little library being arranged on a high chest, part of the\par
furniture of the room, she took out her drawing utensils, and was\par
tranquil enough to be pleased with the thought of sketching the sublime\par
scenes, beheld from her windows; but she suddenly checked this\par
pleasure, remembering how often she had soothed herself by the\par
intention of obtaining amusement of this kind, and had been prevented\par
by some new circumstance of misfortune.\par
\par
“How can I suffer myself to be deluded by hope,” said she, “and,\par
because Count Morano is not yet arrived, feel a momentary happiness?\par
Alas! what is it to me, whether he is here today, or tomorrow, if he\par
comes at all?—and that he will come—it were weakness to doubt.”\par
\par
To withdraw her thoughts, however, from the subject of her misfortunes,\par
she attempted to read, but her attention wandered from the page, and,\par
at length, she threw aside the book, and determined to explore the\par
adjoining chambers of the castle. Her imagination was pleased with the\par
view of ancient grandeur, and an emotion of melancholy awe awakened all\par
its powers, as she walked through rooms, obscure and desolate, where no\par
footsteps had passed probably for many years, and remembered the\par
strange history of the former possessor of the edifice. This brought to\par
her recollection the veiled picture, which had attracted her curiosity,\par
on the preceding night, and she resolved to examine it. As she passed\par
through the chambers, that led to this, she found herself somewhat\par
agitated; its connection with the late lady of the castle, and the\par
conversation of Annette, together with the circumstance of the veil,\par
throwing a mystery over the subject, that excited a faint degree of\par
terror. But a terror of this nature, as it occupies and expands the\par
mind, and elevates it to high expectation, is purely sublime, and leads\par
us, by a kind of fascination, to seek even the object, from which we\par
appear to shrink.\par
\par
Emily passed on with faltering steps, and having paused a moment at the\par
door, before she attempted to open it, she then hastily entered the\par
chamber, and went towards the picture, which appeared to be enclosed in\par
a frame of uncommon size, that hung in a dark part of the room. She\par
paused again, and then, with a timid hand, lifted the veil; but\par
instantly let it fall—perceiving that what it had concealed was no\par
picture, and, before she could leave the chamber, she dropped senseless\par
on the floor.\par
\par
When she recovered her recollection, the remembrance of what she had\par
seen had nearly deprived her of it a second time. She had scarcely\par
strength to remove from the room, and regain her own; and, when arrived\par
there, wanted courage to remain alone. Horror occupied her mind, and\par
excluded, for a time, all sense of past, and dread of future\par
misfortune: she seated herself near the casement, because from thence\par
she heard voices, though distant, on the terrace, and might see people\par
pass, and these, trifling as they were, were reviving circumstances.\par
When her spirits had recovered their tone, she considered, whether she\par
should mention what she had seen to Madame Montoni, and various and\par
important motives urged her to do so, among which the least was the\par
hope of the relief, which an overburdened mind finds in speaking of the\par
subject of its interest. But she was aware of the terrible\par
consequences, which such a communication might lead to; and, dreading\par
the indiscretion of her aunt, at length, endeavoured to arm herself\par
with resolution to observe a profound silence on the subject. Montoni\par
and Verezzi soon after passed under the casement, speaking cheerfully,\par
and their voices revived her. Presently the Signors Bertolini and\par
Cavigni joined the party on the terrace, and Emily, supposing that\par
Madame Montoni was then alone, went to seek her; for the solitude of\par
her chamber, and its proximity to that where she had received so severe\par
a shock, again affected her spirit.\par
\par
She found her aunt in her dressing-room, preparing for dinner. Emily’s\par
pale and affrighted countenance alarmed even Madame Montoni; but she\par
had sufficient strength of mind to be silent on the subject, that still\par
made her shudder, and which was ready to burst from her lips. In her\par
aunt’s apartment she remained till they both descended to dinner. There\par
she met the gentlemen lately arrived, who had a kind of busy\par
seriousness in their looks, which was somewhat unusual with them, while\par
their thoughts seemed too much occupied by some deep interest, to\par
suffer them to bestow much attention either on Emily or Madame Montoni.\par
They spoke little, and Montoni less. Emily, as she now looked on him,\par
shuddered. The horror of the chamber rushed on her mind. Several times\par
the colour faded from her cheeks, and she feared, that illness would\par
betray her emotions, and compel her to leave the room; but the strength\par
of her resolution remedied the weakness of her frame; she obliged\par
herself to converse, and even tried to look cheerful.\par
\par
Montoni evidently laboured under some vexation, such as would probably\par
have agitated a weaker mind or a more susceptible heart, but which\par
appeared, from the sternness of his countenance, only to bend up his\par
faculties to energy and fortitude.\par
\par
It was a comfortless and silent meal. The gloom of the castle seemed to\par
have spread its contagion even over the gay countenance of Cavigni, and\par
with this gloom was mingled a fierceness such as she had seldom seen\par
him indicate. Count Morano was not named, and what conversation there\par
was, turned chiefly upon the wars which at that time agitated the\par
Italian states, the strength of the Venetian armies, and the characters\par
of their generals.\par
\par
After dinner, when the servants had withdrawn, Emily learned, that the\par
cavalier, who had drawn upon himself the vengeance of Orsino, had since\par
died of his wounds, and that strict search was still making for his\par
murderer. The intelligence seemed to disturb Montoni, who mused, and\par
then enquired, where Orsino had concealed himself. His guests, who all,\par
except Cavigni, were ignorant, that Montoni had himself assisted him to\par
escape from Venice, replied, that he had fled in the night with such\par
precipitation and secrecy, that his most intimate companions knew not\par
whither. Montoni blamed himself for having asked the question, for a\par
second thought convinced him, that a man of Orsino’s suspicious temper\par
was not likely to trust any of the persons present with the knowledge\par
of his asylum. He considered himself, however, as entitled to his\par
utmost confidence, and did not doubt, that he should soon hear of him.\par
\par
Emily retired with Madame Montoni, soon after the cloth was withdrawn,\par
and left the cavaliers to their secret councils, but not before the\par
significant frowns of Montoni had warned his wife to depart, who passed\par
from the hall to the ramparts, and walked, for some time, in silence,\par
which Emily did not interrupt, for her mind was also occupied by\par
interests of its own. It required all her resolution, to forbear\par
communicating to Madame Montoni the terrible subject, which still\par
thrilled her every nerve with horror; and sometimes she was on the\par
point of doing so, merely to obtain the relief of a moment; but she\par
knew how wholly she was in the power of Montoni, and, considering, that\par
the indiscretion of her aunt might prove fatal to them both, she\par
compelled herself to endure a present and an inferior evil, rather than\par
to tempt a future and a heavier one. A strange kind of presentiment\par
frequently, on this day, occurred to her;—it seemed as if her fate\par
rested here, and was by some invisible means connected with this\par
castle.\par
\par
“Let me not accelerate it,” said she to herself: “for whatever I may be\par
reserved, let me, at least, avoid self-reproach.”\par
\par
As she looked on the massy walls of the edifice, her melancholy spirits\par
represented it to be her prison; and she started as at a new\par
suggestion, when she considered how far distant she was from her native\par
country, from her little peaceful home, and from her only friend—how\par
remote was her hope of happiness, how feeble the expectation of again\par
seeing him! Yet the idea of Valancourt, and her confidence in his\par
faithful love, had hitherto been her only solace, and she struggled\par
hard to retain them. A few tears of agony started to her eyes, which\par
she turned aside to conceal.\par
\par
While she afterwards leaned on the wall of the rampart, some peasants,\par
at a little distance, were seen examining a breach, before which lay a\par
heap of stones, as if to repair it, and a rusty old cannon, that\par
appeared to have fallen from its station above. Madame Montoni stopped\par
to speak to the men, and enquired what they were going to do. “To\par
repair the fortifications, your ladyship,” said one of them; a labour\par
which she was somewhat surprised, that Montoni should think necessary,\par
particularly since he had never spoken of the castle, as of a place, at\par
which he meant to reside for any considerable time; but she passed on\par
towards a lofty arch, that led from the south to the east rampart, and\par
which adjoined the castle, on one side, while, on the other, it\par
supported a small watch-tower, that entirely commanded the deep valley\par
below. As she approached this arch, she saw, beyond it, winding along\par
the woody descent of a distant mountain, a long troop of horse and\par
foot, whom she knew to be soldiers, only by the glitter of their pikes\par
and other arms, for the distance did not allow her to discover the\par
colour of their liveries. As she gazed, the vanguard issued from the\par
woods into the valley, but the train still continued to pour over the\par
remote summit of the mountain, in endless succession; while, in the\par
front, the military uniform became distinguishable, and the commanders,\par
riding first, and seeming, by their gestures, to direct the march of\par
those that followed, at length, approached very near to the castle.\par
\par
Such a spectacle, in these solitary regions, both surprised and alarmed\par
Madame Montoni, and she hastened towards some peasants, who were\par
employed in raising bastions before the south rampart, where the rock\par
was less abrupt than elsewhere. These men could give no satisfactory\par
answers to her enquiries, but, being roused by them, gazed in stupid\par
astonishment upon the long cavalcade. Madame Montoni, then thinking it\par
necessary to communicate further the object of her alarm, sent Emily to\par
say, that she wished to speak to Montoni; an errand her niece did not\par
approve, for she dreaded his frowns, which she knew this message would\par
provoke; but she obeyed in silence.\par
\par
As she drew near the apartment, in which he sat with his guests, she\par
heard them in earnest and loud dispute, and she paused a moment,\par
trembling at the displeasure, which her sudden interruption would\par
occasion. In the next, their voices sunk all together; she then\par
ventured to open the door, and, while Montoni turned hastily and looked\par
at her, without speaking, she delivered her message.\par
\par
“Tell Madam Montoni I am engaged,” said he.\par
\par
Emily then thought it proper to mention the subject of her alarm.\par
Montoni and his companions rose instantly and went to the windows, but,\par
these not affording them a view of the troops, they at length proceeded\par
to the ramparts, where Cavigni conjectured it to be a legion of\par
_condottieri_, on their march towards Modena.\par
\par
One part of the cavalcade now extended along the valley, and another\par
wound among the mountains towards the north, while some troops still\par
lingered on the woody precipices, where the first had appeared, so that\par
the great length of the procession seemed to include a whole army.\par
While Montoni and his family watched its progress, they heard the sound\par
of trumpets and the clash of cymbals in the vale, and then others,\par
answering from the heights. Emily listened with emotion to the shrill\par
blast, that woke the echoes of the mountains, and Montoni explained the\par
signals, with which he appeared to be well acquainted, and which meant\par
nothing hostile. The uniforms of the troops, and the kind of arms they\par
bore, confirmed to him the conjecture of Cavigni, and he had the\par
satisfaction to see them pass by, without even stopping to gaze upon\par
his castle. He did not, however, leave the rampart, till the bases of\par
the mountains had shut them from his view, and the last murmur of the\par
trumpet floated away on the wind. Cavigni and Verezzi were inspirited\par
by this spectacle, which seemed to have roused all the fire of their\par
temper; Montoni turned into the castle in thoughtful silence.\par
\par
Emily’s mind had not yet sufficiently recovered from its late shock, to\par
endure the loneliness of her chamber, and she remained upon the\par
ramparts; for Madame Montoni had not invited her to her dressing-room,\par
whither she had gone evidently in low spirits, and Emily, from her late\par
experience, had lost all wish to explore the gloomy and mysterious\par
recesses of the castle. The ramparts, therefore, were almost her only\par
retreat, and here she lingered, till the grey haze of evening was again\par
spread over the scene.\par
\par
The cavaliers supped by themselves, and Madame Montoni remained in her\par
apartment, whither Emily went, before she retired to her own. She found\par
her aunt weeping, and in much agitation. The tenderness of Emily was\par
naturally so soothing, that it seldom failed to give comfort to the\par
drooping heart: but Madame Montoni’s was torn, and the softest accents\par
of Emily’s voice were lost upon it. With her usual delicacy, she did\par
not appear to observe her aunt’s distress, but it gave an involuntary\par
gentleness to her manners, and an air of solicitude to her countenance,\par
which Madame Montoni was vexed to perceive, who seemed to feel the pity\par
of her niece to be an insult to her pride, and dismissed her as soon as\par
she properly could. Emily did not venture to mention again the\par
reluctance she felt to her gloomy chamber, but she requested that\par
Annette might be permitted to remain with her till she retired to rest;\par
and the request was somewhat reluctantly granted. Annette, however, was\par
now with the servants, and Emily withdrew alone.\par
\par
With light and hasty steps she passed through the long galleries, while\par
the feeble glimmer of the lamp she carried only showed the gloom around\par
her, and the passing air threatened to extinguish it. The lonely\par
silence, that reigned in this part of the castle, awed her; now and\par
then, indeed, she heard a faint peal of laughter rise from a remote\par
part of the edifice, where the servants were assembled, but it was soon\par
lost, and a kind of breathless stillness remained. As she passed the\par
suite of rooms which she had visited in the morning, her eyes glanced\par
fearfully on the door, and she almost fancied she heard murmuring\par
sounds within, but she paused not a moment to enquire.\par
\par
Having reached her own apartment, where no blazing wood on the hearth\par
dissipated the gloom, she sat down with a book, to enliven her\par
attention, till Annette should come, and a fire could be kindled. She\par
continued to read till her light was nearly expired, but Annette did\par
not appear, and the solitude and obscurity of her chamber again\par
affected her spirits, the more, because of its nearness to the scene of\par
horror, that she had witnessed in the morning. Gloomy and fantastic\par
images came to her mind. She looked fearfully towards the door of the\par
staircase, and then, examining whether it was still fastened, found\par
that it was so. Unable to conquer the uneasiness she felt at the\par
prospect of sleeping again in this remote and insecure apartment, which\par
some person seemed to have entered during the preceding night, her\par
impatience to see Annette, whom she had bidden to enquire concerning\par
this circumstance, became extremely painful. She wished also to\par
question her, as to the object, which had excited so much horror in her\par
own mind, and which Annette on the preceding evening had appeared to be\par
in part acquainted with, though her words were very remote from the\par
truth, and it appeared plainly to Emily, that the girl had been\par
purposely misled by a false report: above all she was surprised, that\par
the door of the chamber, which contained it, should be left unguarded.\par
Such an instance of negligence almost surpassed belief. But her light\par
was now expiring; the faint flashes it threw upon the walls called up\par
all the terrors of fancy, and she rose to find her way to the habitable\par
part of the castle, before it was quite extinguished. As she opened the\par
chamber door, she heard remote voices, and, soon after, saw a light\par
issue upon the further end of the corridor, which Annette and another\par
servant approached. “I am glad you are come,” said Emily: “what has\par
detained you so long? Pray light me a fire immediately.”\par
\par
“My lady wanted me, ma’amselle,” replied Annette in some confusion; “I\par
will go and get the wood.”\par
\par
“No,” said Caterina, “that is my business,” and left the room\par
instantly, while Annette would have followed; but, being called back,\par
she began to talk very loud, and laugh, and seemed afraid to trust a\par
pause of silence.\par
\par
Caterina soon returned with the wood, and then, when the cheerful blaze\par
once more animated the room, and this servant had withdrawn, Emily\par
asked Annette, whether she had made the enquiry she bade her. “Yes,\par
ma’amselle,” said Annette, “but not a soul knows anything about the\par
matter: and old Carlo—I watched him well, for they say he knows strange\par
things—old Carlo looked so as I don’t know how to tell, and he asked me\par
again and again, if I was sure the door was ever unfastened. Lord, says\par
I—am I sure I am alive? And as for me, ma’am, I am all astounded, as\par
one may say, and would no more sleep in this chamber, than I would on\par
the great cannon at the end of the east rampart.”\par
\par
“And what objection have you to that cannon, more than to any of the\par
rest?” said Emily smiling: “the best would be rather a hard bed.”\par
\par
“Yes, ma’amselle, any of them would be hard enough for that matter; but\par
they do say, that something has been seen in the dead of night,\par
standing beside the great cannon, as if to guard it.”\par
\par
“Well! my good Annette, the people who tell such stories, are happy in\par
having you for an auditor, for I perceive you believe them all.”\par
\par
“Dear ma’amselle! I will show you the very cannon; you can see it from\par
these windows!”\par
\par
“Well,” said Emily, “but that does not prove, that an apparition guards\par
it.”\par
\par
“What! not if I show you the very cannon! Dear ma’am, you will believe\par
nothing.”\par
\par
“Nothing probably upon this subject, but what I see,” said\par
Emily.—“Well, ma’am, but you shall see it, if you will only step this\par
way to the casement.”—Emily could not forbear laughing, and Annette\par
looked surprised. Perceiving her extreme aptitude to credit the\par
marvellous, Emily forbore to mention the subject she had intended, lest\par
it should overcome her with idle terrors, and she began to speak on a\par
lively topic—the regattas of Venice.\par
\par
“Aye, ma’amselle, those rowing matches,” said Annette, “and the fine\par
moonlight nights, are all, that are worth seeing in Venice. To be sure\par
the moon is brighter than any I ever saw; and then to hear such sweet\par
music, too, as Ludovico has often and often sung under the lattice by\par
the west portico! Ma’amselle, it was Ludovico, that told me about that\par
picture, which you wanted so to look at last night, and—”\par
\par
“What picture?” said Emily, wishing Annette to explain herself.\par
\par
“O! that terrible picture with the black veil over it.”\par
\par
“You never saw it, then?” said Emily.\par
\par
“Who, I!—No, ma’amselle, I never did. But this morning,” continued\par
Annette, lowering her voice, and looking round the room, “this morning,\par
as it was broad daylight, do you know, ma’am, I took a strange fancy to\par
see it, as I had heard such odd hints about it, and I got as far as the\par
door, and should have opened it, if it had not been locked!”\par
\par
Emily, endeavouring to conceal the emotion this circumstance\par
occasioned, enquired at what hour she went to the chamber, and found,\par
that it was soon after herself had been there. She also asked further\par
questions, and the answers convinced her, that Annette, and probably\par
her informer, were ignorant of the terrible truth, though in Annette’s\par
account something very like the truth, now and then, mingled with the\par
falsehood. Emily now began to fear, that her visit to the chamber had\par
been observed, since the door had been closed, so immediately after her\par
departure; and dreaded lest this should draw upon her the vengeance of\par
Montoni. Her anxiety, also, was excited to know whence, and for what\par
purpose, the delusive report, which had been imposed upon Annette, had\par
originated, since Montoni could only have wished for silence and\par
secrecy; but she felt, that the subject was too terrible for this\par
lonely hour, and she compelled herself to leave it, to converse with\par
Annette, whose chat, simple as it was, she preferred to the stillness\par
of total solitude.\par
\par
Thus they sat, till near midnight, but not without many hints from\par
Annette, that she wished to go. The embers were now nearly burnt out;\par
and Emily heard, at a distance, the thundering sound of the hall doors,\par
as they were shut for the night. She, therefore, prepared for rest, but\par
was still unwilling that Annette should leave her. At this instant, the\par
great bell of the portal sounded. They listened in fearful expectation,\par
when, after a long pause of silence, it sounded again. Soon after, they\par
heard the noise of carriage wheels in the courtyard. Emily sunk almost\par
lifeless in her chair; “It is the Count,” said she.\par
\par
“What, at this time of night, ma’am!” said Annette: “no, my dear lady.\par
But, for that matter, it is a strange time of night for anybody to\par
come!”\par
\par
“Nay, pr’ythee, good Annette, stay not talking,” said Emily in a voice\par
of agony—“Go, pr’ythee, go, and see who it is.”\par
\par
Annette left the room, and carried with her the light, leaving Emily in\par
darkness, which a few moments before would have terrified her in this\par
room, but was now scarcely observed by her. She listened and waited, in\par
breathless expectation, and heard distant noises, but Annette did not\par
return. Her patience, at length, exhausted, she tried to find her way\par
to the corridor, but it was long before she could touch the door of the\par
chamber, and, when she had opened it, the total darkness without made\par
her fear to proceed. Voices were now heard, and Emily even thought she\par
distinguished those of Count Morano and Montoni. Soon after she heard\par
steps approaching, and then a ray of light streamed through the\par
darkness, and Annette appeared, whom Emily went to meet.\par
\par
“Yes, ma’amselle,” said she, “you were right, it is the Count sure\par
enough.”\par
\par
“It is he!” exclaimed Emily, lifting her eyes towards heaven and\par
supporting herself by Annette’s arm.\par
\par
“Good Lord! my dear lady, don’t be in such a _fluster_, and look so\par
pale, we shall soon hear more.”\par
\par
“We shall, indeed!” said Emily, moving as fast as she was able towards\par
her apartment. “I am not well; give me air.” Annette opened a casement,\par
and brought water. The faintness soon left Emily, but she desired\par
Annette would not go till she heard from Montoni.\par
\par
“Dear ma’amselle! he surely will not disturb you at this time of night;\par
why he must think you are asleep.”\par
\par
“Stay with me till I am so, then,” said Emily, who felt temporary\par
relief from this suggestion, which appeared probable enough, though her\par
fears had prevented its occurring to her. Annette, with secret\par
reluctance, consented to stay, and Emily was now composed enough to ask\par
her some questions; among others, whether she had seen the Count.\par
\par
“Yes, ma’am, I saw him alight, for I went from hence to the grate in\par
the north turret, that overlooks the inner courtyard, you know. There I\par
saw the Count’s carriage, and the Count in it, waiting at the great\par
door,—for the porter was just gone to bed—with several men on horseback\par
all by the light of the torches they carried.”\par
\par
Emily was compelled to smile. “When the door was opened, the Count said\par
something, that I could not make out, and then got out, and another\par
gentleman with him. I thought, to be sure, the Signor was gone to bed,\par
and I hastened away to my lady’s dressing-room, to see what I could\par
hear. But in the way I met Ludovico, and he told me that the Signor was\par
up, counselling with his master and the other Signors, in the room at\par
the end of the north gallery; and Ludovico held up his finger, and laid\par
it on his lips, as much as to say—There is more going on, than you\par
think of, Annette, but you must hold your tongue. And so I did hold my\par
tongue, ma’amselle, and came away to tell you directly.”\par
\par
Emily enquired who the cavalier was, that accompanied the Count, and\par
how Montoni received them; but Annette could not inform her.\par
\par
“Ludovico,” she added, “had just been to call Signor Montoni’s valet,\par
that he might tell him they were arrived, when I met him.”\par
\par
Emily sat musing, for some time, and then her anxiety was so much\par
increased, that she desired Annette would go to the servants’ hall,\par
where it was possible she might hear something of the Count’s\par
intention, respecting his stay at the castle.\par
\par
“Yes, ma’am,” said Annette with readiness; “but how am I to find the\par
way, if I leave the lamp with you?”\par
\par
Emily said she would light her, and they immediately quitted the\par
chamber. When they had reached the top of the great staircase, Emily\par
recollected, that she might be seen by the Count, and, to avoid the\par
great hall, Annette conducted her through some private passages to a\par
back staircase, which led directly to that of the servants.\par
\par
As she returned towards her chamber, Emily began to fear, that she\par
might again lose herself in the intricacies of the castle, and again be\par
shocked by some mysterious spectacle; and, though she was already\par
perplexed by the numerous turnings, she feared to open one of the many\par
doors that offered. While she stepped thoughtfully along, she fancied,\par
that she heard a low moaning at no great distance, and, having paused a\par
moment, she heard it again and distinctly. Several doors appeared on\par
the right hand of the passage. She advanced, and listened. When she\par
came to the second, she heard a voice, apparently in complaint, within,\par
to which she continued to listen, afraid to open the door, and\par
unwilling to leave it. Convulsive sobs followed, and then the piercing\par
accents of an agonizing spirit burst forth. Emily stood appalled, and\par
looked through the gloom, that surrounded her, in fearful expectation.\par
The lamentations continued. Pity now began to subdue terror; it was\par
possible she might administer comfort to the sufferer, at least, by\par
expressing sympathy, and she laid her hand on the door. While she\par
hesitated she thought she knew this voice, disguised as it was by tones\par
of grief. Having, therefore, set down the lamp in the passage, she\par
gently opened the door, within which all was dark, except that from an\par
inner apartment a partial light appeared; and she stepped softly on.\par
Before she reached it, the appearance of Madame Montoni, leaning on her\par
dressing-table, weeping, and with a handkerchief held to her eyes,\par
struck her, and she paused.\par
\par
Some person was seated in a chair by the fire, but who it was she could\par
not distinguish. He spoke, now and then, in a low voice, that did not\par
allow Emily to hear what was uttered, but she thought, that Madame\par
Montoni, at those times, wept the more, who was too much occupied by\par
her own distress, to observe Emily, while the latter, though anxious to\par
know what occasioned this, and who was the person admitted at so late\par
an hour to her aunt’s dressing-room, forbore to add to her sufferings\par
by surprising her, or to take advantage of her situation, by listening\par
to a private discourse. She, therefore, stepped softly back, and, after\par
some further difficulty, found the way to her own chamber, where nearer\par
interests, at length, excluded the surprise and concern she had felt,\par
respecting Madame Montoni.\par
\par
Annette, however, returned without satisfactory intelligence, for the\par
servants, among whom she had been, were either entirely ignorant, or\par
affected to be so, concerning the Count’s intended stay at the castle.\par
They could talk only of the steep and broken road they had just passed,\par
and of the numerous dangers they had escaped and express wonder how\par
their lord could choose to encounter all these, in the darkness of\par
night; for they scarcely allowed, that the torches had served for any\par
other purpose but that of showing the dreariness of the mountains.\par
Annette, finding she could gain no information, left them, making noisy\par
petitions, for more wood on the fire and more supper on the table.\par
\par
“And now, ma’amselle,” added she, “I am so sleepy!—I am sure, if you\par
were so sleepy, you would not desire me to sit up with you.”\par
\par
Emily, indeed, began to think it was cruel to wish it; she had also\par
waited so long, without receiving a summons from Montoni, that it\par
appeared he did not mean to disturb her, at this late hour, and she\par
determined to dismiss Annette. But, when she again looked round her\par
gloomy chamber, and recollected certain circumstances, fear seized her\par
spirits, and she hesitated.\par
\par
“And yet it were cruel of me to ask you to stay, till I am asleep,\par
Annette,” said she, “for I fear it will be very long before I forget\par
myself in sleep.”\par
\par
“I dare say it will be very long, ma’amselle,” said Annette.\par
\par
“But, before you go,” rejoined Emily, “let me ask you—Had Signor\par
Montoni left Count Morano, when you quitted the hall?”\par
\par
“O no, ma’am, they were alone together.”\par
\par
“Have you been in my aunt’s dressing-room, since you left me?”\par
\par
“No, ma’amselle, I called at the door as I passed, but it was fastened;\par
so I thought my lady was gone to bed.”\par
\par
“Who, then, was with your lady just now?” said Emily, forgetting, in\par
surprise, her usual prudence.\par
\par
“Nobody, I believe, ma’am,” replied Annette, “nobody has been with her,\par
I believe, since I left you.”\par
\par
Emily took no further notice of the subject, and, after some struggle\par
with imaginary fears, her good nature prevailed over them so far, that\par
she dismissed Annette for the night. She then sat, musing upon her own\par
circumstances and those of Madame Montoni, till her eye rested on the\par
miniature picture, which she had found, after her father’s death, among\par
the papers he had enjoined her to destroy. It was open upon the table,\par
before her, among some loose drawings, having, with them, been taken\par
out of a little box by Emily, some hours before. The sight of it called\par
up many interesting reflections, but the melancholy sweetness of the\par
countenance soothed the emotions, which these had occasioned. It was\par
the same style of countenance as that of her late father, and, while\par
she gazed on it with fondness on this account, she even fancied a\par
resemblance in the features. But this tranquillity was suddenly\par
interrupted, when she recollected the words in the manuscript, that had\par
been found with this picture, and which had formerly occasioned her so\par
much doubt and horror. At length, she roused herself from the deep\par
reverie, into which this remembrance had thrown her; but, when she rose\par
to undress, the silence and solitude, to which she was left, at this\par
midnight hour, for not even a distant sound was now heard, conspired\par
with the impression the subject she had been considering had given to\par
her mind, to appall her. Annette’s hints, too, concerning this chamber,\par
simple as they were, had not failed to affect her, since they followed\par
a circumstance of peculiar horror, which she herself had witnessed, and\par
since the scene of this was a chamber nearly adjoining her own.\par
\par
The door of the staircase was, perhaps, a subject of more reasonable\par
alarm, and she now began to apprehend, such was the aptitude of her\par
fears, that this staircase had some private communication with the\par
apartment, which she shuddered even to remember. Determined not to\par
undress, she lay down to sleep in her clothes, with her late father’s\par
dog, the faithful _Manchon_, at the foot of the bed, whom she\par
considered as a kind of guard.\par
\par
Thus circumstanced, she tried to banish reflection, but her busy fancy\par
would still hover over the subjects of her interest, and she heard the\par
clock of the castle strike two, before she closed her eyes.\par
\par
From the disturbed slumber, into which she then sunk, she was soon\par
awakened by a noise, which seemed to arise within her chamber; but the\par
silence, that prevailed, as she fearfully listened, inclined her to\par
believe, that she had been alarmed by such sounds as sometimes occur in\par
dreams, and she laid her head again upon the pillow.\par
\par
A return of the noise again disturbed her; it seemed to come from that\par
part of the room, which communicated with the private staircase, and\par
she instantly remembered the odd circumstance of the door having been\par
fastened, during the preceding night, by some unknown hand. Her late\par
alarming suspicion, concerning its communication, also occurred to her.\par
Her heart became faint with terror. Half raising herself from the bed,\par
and gently drawing aside the curtain, she looked towards the door of\par
the staircase, but the lamp, that burnt on the hearth, spread so feeble\par
a light through the apartment, that the remote parts of it were lost in\par
shadow. The noise, however, which, she was convinced, came from the\par
door, continued. It seemed like that made by the undrawing of rusty\par
bolts, and often ceased, and was then renewed more gently, as if the\par
hand, that occasioned it, was restrained by a fear of discovery.\par
\par
While Emily kept her eyes fixed on the spot, she saw the door move, and\par
then slowly open, and perceived something enter the room, but the\par
extreme duskiness prevented her distinguishing what it was. Almost\par
fainting with terror, she had yet sufficient command over herself, to\par
check the shriek, that was escaping from her lips, and, letting the\par
curtain drop from her hand, continued to observe in silence the motions\par
of the mysterious form she saw. It seemed to glide along the remote\par
obscurity of the apartment, then paused, and, as it approached the\par
hearth, she perceived, in the stronger light, what appeared to be a\par
human figure. Certain remembrances now struck upon her heart, and\par
almost subdued the feeble remains of her spirits; she continued,\par
however, to watch the figure, which remained for some time motionless,\par
but then, advancing slowly towards the bed, stood silently at the feet,\par
where the curtains, being a little open, allowed her still to see it;\par
terror, however, had now deprived her of the power of discrimination,\par
as well as of that of utterance.\par
\par
Having continued there a moment, the form retreated towards the hearth,\par
when it took the lamp, held it up, surveyed the chamber, for a few\par
moments, and then again advanced towards the bed. The light at that\par
instant awakening the dog, that had slept at Emily’s feet, he barked\par
loudly, and, jumping to the floor, flew at the stranger, who struck the\par
animal smartly with a sheathed sword, and, springing towards the bed,\par
Emily discovered—Count Morano!\par
\par
She gazed at him for a moment in speechless affright, while he,\par
throwing himself on his knee at the bedside, besought her to fear\par
nothing, and, having thrown down his sword, would have taken her hand,\par
when the faculties, that terror had suspended, suddenly returned, and\par
she sprung from the bed, in the dress, which surely a kind of prophetic\par
apprehension had prevented her, on this night, from throwing aside.\par
\par
Morano rose, followed her to the door, through which he had entered,\par
and caught her hand, as she reached the top of the staircase, but not\par
before she had discovered, by the gleam of a lamp, another man half-way\par
down the steps. She now screamed in despair, and, believing herself\par
given up by Montoni, saw, indeed, no possibility of escape.\par
\par
The Count, who still held her hand, led her back into the chamber.\par
\par
“Why all this terror?” said he, in a tremulous voice. “Hear me, Emily:\par
I come not to alarm you; no, by Heaven! I love you too well—too well\par
for my own peace.”\par
\par
Emily looked at him for a moment, in fearful doubt.\par
\par
“Then leave me, sir,” said she, “leave me instantly.”\par
\par
“Hear me, Emily,” resumed Morano, “hear me! I love, and am in\par
despair—yes—in despair. How can I gaze upon you, and know, that it is,\par
perhaps, for the last time, without suffering all the frenzy of\par
despair? But it shall not be so; you shall be mine, in spite of Montoni\par
and all his villany.”\par
\par
“In spite of Montoni!” cried Emily eagerly: “what is it I hear?”\par
\par
“You hear, that Montoni is a villain,” exclaimed Morano with\par
vehemence,—“a villain who would have sold you to my love!—Who—”\par
\par
“And is he less, who would have bought me?” said Emily, fixing on the\par
Count an eye of calm contempt. “Leave the room, sir, instantly,” she\par
continued in a voice, trembling between joy and fear, “or I will alarm\par
the family, and you may receive that from Signor Montoni’s vengeance,\par
which I have vainly supplicated from his pity.” But Emily knew, that\par
she was beyond the hearing of those, who might protect her.\par
\par
“You can never hope anything from his pity,” said Morano, “he has used\par
me infamously, and my vengeance shall pursue him. And for you, Emily,\par
for you, he has new plans more profitable than the last, no doubt.” The\par
gleam of hope, which the Count’s former speech had revived, was now\par
nearly extinguished by the latter; and, while Emily’s countenance\par
betrayed the emotions of her mind, he endeavoured to take advantage of\par
the discovery.\par
\par
“I lose time,” said he: “I came not to exclaim against Montoni; I came\par
to solicit, to plead—to Emily; to tell her all I suffer, to entreat her\par
to save me from despair, and herself from destruction. Emily! the\par
schemes of Montoni are insearchable, but, I warn you, they are\par
terrible; he has no principle, when interest, or ambition leads. Can I\par
love you, and abandon you to his power? Fly, then, fly from this gloomy\par
prison, with a lover, who adores you! I have bribed a servant of the\par
castle to open the gates, and, before tomorrow’s dawn, you shall be far\par
on the way to Venice.”\par
\par
Emily, overcome by the sudden shock she had received, at the moment,\par
too, when she had begun to hope for better days, now thought she saw\par
destruction surround her on every side. Unable to reply, and almost to\par
think, she threw herself into a chair, pale and breathless. That\par
Montoni had formerly sold her to Morano, was very probable; that he had\par
now withdrawn his consent to the marriage, was evident from the Count’s\par
present conduct; and it was nearly certain, that a scheme of stronger\par
interest only could have induced the selfish Montoni to forego a plan,\par
which he had hitherto so strenuously pursued. These reflections made\par
her tremble at the hints, which Morano had just given, which she no\par
longer hesitated to believe; and, while she shrunk from the new scenes\par
of misery and oppression, that might await her in the castle of\par
Udolpho, she was compelled to observe, that almost her only means of\par
escaping them was by submitting herself to the protection of this man,\par
with whom evils more certain and not less terrible appeared,—evils,\par
upon which she could not endure to pause for an instant.\par
\par
Her silence, though it was that of agony, encouraged the hopes of\par
Morano, who watched her countenance with impatience, took again the\par
resisting hand she had withdrawn, and, as he pressed it to his heart,\par
again conjured her to determine immediately. “Every moment we lose,\par
will make our departure more dangerous,” said he: “these few moments\par
lost may enable Montoni to overtake us.”\par
\par
“I beseech you, sir, be silent,” said Emily faintly: “I am indeed very\par
wretched, and wretched I must remain. Leave me—I command you, leave me\par
to my fate.”\par
\par
“Never!” cried the Count vehemently: “let me perish first! But forgive\par
my violence! the thought of losing you is madness. You cannot be\par
ignorant of Montoni’s character, you may be ignorant of his\par
schemes—nay, you must be so, or you would not hesitate between my love\par
and his power.”\par
\par
“Nor do I hesitate,” said Emily.\par
\par
“Let us go, then,” said Morano, eagerly kissing her hand, and rising,\par
“my carriage waits, below the castle walls.”\par
\par
“You mistake me, sir,” said Emily. “Allow me to thank you for the\par
interest you express in my welfare, and to decide by my own choice. I\par
shall remain under the protection of Signor Montoni.”\par
\par
“Under his protection!” exclaimed Morano, proudly, “his _protection!_\par
Emily, why will you suffer yourself to be thus deluded? I have already\par
told you what you have to expect from his _protection_.”\par
\par
“And pardon me, sir, if, in this instance, I doubt mere assertion, and,\par
to be convinced, require something approaching to proof.”\par
\par
“I have now neither the time, nor the means of adducing proof,” replied\par
the Count.\par
\par
“Nor have I, sir, the inclination to listen to it, if you had.”\par
\par
“But you trifle with my patience and my distress,” continued Morano.\par
“Is a marriage with a man, who adores you, so very terrible in your\par
eyes, that you would prefer to it all the misery, to which Montoni may\par
condemn you in this remote prison? Some wretch must have stolen those\par
affections, which ought to be mine, or you would not thus obstinately\par
persist in refusing an offer, that would place you beyond the reach of\par
oppression.” Morano walked about the room, with quick steps, and a\par
disturbed air.\par
\par
“This discourse, Count Morano, sufficiently proves, that my affections\par
ought not to be yours,” said Emily, mildly, “and this conduct, that I\par
should not be placed beyond the reach of oppression, so long as I\par
remained in your power. If you wish me to believe otherwise, cease to\par
oppress me any longer by your presence. If you refuse this, you will\par
compel me to expose you to the resentment of Signor Montoni.”\par
\par
“Yes, let him come,” cried Morano furiously, “and brave _my_\par
resentment! Let him dare to face once more the man he has so\par
courageously injured; danger shall teach him morality, and vengeance\par
justice—let him come, and receive my sword in his heart!”\par
\par
The vehemence, with which this was uttered, gave Emily new cause of\par
alarm, who arose from her chair, but her trembling frame refused to\par
support her, and she resumed her seat;—the words died on her lips, and,\par
when she looked wistfully towards the door of the corridor, which was\par
locked, she considered it was impossible for her to leave the\par
apartment, before Morano would be apprised of, and able to counteract,\par
her intention.\par
\par
Without observing her agitation, he continued to pace the room in the\par
utmost perturbation of spirits. His darkened countenance expressed all\par
the rage of jealousy and revenge; and a person, who had seen his\par
features under the smile of ineffable tenderness, which he so lately\par
assumed, would now scarcely have believed them to be the same.\par
\par
“Count Morano,” said Emily, at length recovering her voice, “calm, I\par
entreat you, these transports, and listen to reason, if you will not to\par
pity. You have equally misplaced your love, and your hatred.—I never\par
could have returned the affection, with which you honour me, and\par
certainly have never encouraged it; neither has Signor Montoni injured\par
you, for you must have known, that he had no right to dispose of my\par
hand, had he even possessed the power to do so. Leave, then, leave the\par
castle, while you may with safety. Spare yourself the dreadful\par
consequences of an unjust revenge, and the remorse of having prolonged\par
to me these moments of suffering.”\par
\par
“Is it for mine, or for Montoni’s safety, that you are thus alarmed?”\par
said Morano, coldly, and turning towards her with a look of acrimony.\par
\par
“For both,” replied Emily, in a trembling voice.\par
\par
“Unjust revenge!” cried the Count, resuming the abrupt tones of\par
passion. “Who, that looks upon that face, can imagine a punishment\par
adequate to the injury he would have done me? Yes, I will leave the\par
castle; but it shall not be alone. I have trifled too long. Since my\par
prayers and my sufferings cannot prevail, force shall. I have people in\par
waiting, who shall convey you to my carriage. Your voice will bring no\par
succour; it cannot be heard from this remote part of the castle;\par
submit, therefore, in silence, to go with me.”\par
\par
This was an unnecessary injunction, at present; for Emily was too\par
certain, that her call would avail her nothing; and terror had so\par
entirely disordered her thoughts, that she knew not how to plead to\par
Morano, but sat, mute and trembling, in her chair, till he advanced to\par
lift her from it, when she suddenly raised herself, and, with a\par
repulsive gesture, and a countenance of forced serenity, said, “Count\par
Morano! I am now in your power; but you will observe, that this is not\par
the conduct which can win the esteem you appear so solicitous to\par
obtain, and that you are preparing for yourself a load of remorse, in\par
the miseries of a friendless orphan, which can never leave you. Do you\par
believe your heart to be, indeed, so hardened, that you can look\par
without emotion on the suffering, to which you would condemn me?—”\par
\par
Emily was interrupted by the growling of the dog, who now came again\par
from the bed, and Morano looked towards the door of the staircase,\par
where no person appearing, he called aloud, “Cesario!”\par
\par
“Emily,” said the Count, “why will you reduce me to adopt this conduct?\par
How much more willingly would I persuade, than compel you to become my\par
wife! but, by Heaven! I will not leave you to be sold by Montoni. Yet a\par
thought glances across my mind, that brings madness with it. I know not\par
how to name it. It is preposterous—it cannot be.—Yet you tremble—you\par
grow pale! It is! it is so;—you—you—love Montoni!” cried Morano,\par
grasping Emily’s wrist, and stamping his foot on the floor.\par
\par
An involuntary air of surprise appeared on her countenance. “If you\par
have indeed believed so,” said she, “believe so still.”\par
\par
“That look, those words confirm it,” exclaimed Morano, furiously. “No,\par
no, no, Montoni had a richer prize in view, than gold. But he shall not\par
live to triumph over me!—This very instant—”\par
\par
He was interrupted by the loud barking of the dog.\par
\par
“Stay, Count Morano,” said Emily, terrified by his words, and by the\par
fury expressed in his eyes, “I will save you from this error.—Of all\par
men, Signor Montoni is not your rival; though, if I find all other\par
means of saving myself vain, I will try whether my voice may not arouse\par
his servants to my succour.”\par
\par
“Assertion,” replied Morano, “at such a moment, is not to be depended\par
upon. How could I suffer myself to doubt, even for an instant, that he\par
could see you, and not love?—But my first care shall be to convey you\par
from the castle. Cesario! ho,—Cesario!”\par
\par
A man now appeared at the door of the staircase, and other steps were\par
heard ascending. Emily uttered a loud shriek, as Morano hurried her\par
across the chamber, and, at the same moment, she heard a noise at the\par
door, that opened upon the corridor. The Count paused an instant, as if\par
his mind was suspended between love and the desire of vengeance; and,\par
in that instant, the door gave way, and Montoni, followed by the old\par
steward and several other persons, burst into the room.\par
\par
“Draw!” cried Montoni to the Count, who did not pause for a second\par
bidding, but, giving Emily into the hands of the people, that appeared\par
from the staircase, turned fiercely round. “This in thine heart,\par
villain!” said he, as he made a thrust at Montoni with his sword, who\par
parried the blow, and aimed another, while some of the persons, who had\par
followed him into the room, endeavoured to part the combatants, and\par
others rescued Emily from the hands of Morano’s servants.\par
\par
“Was it for this, Count Morano,” said Montoni, in a cool sarcastic tone\par
of voice, “that I received you under my roof, and permitted you, though\par
my declared enemy, to remain under it for the night? Was it, that you\par
might repay my hospitality with the treachery of a fiend, and rob me of\par
my niece?”\par
\par
“Who talks of treachery?” said Morano, in a tone of unrestrained\par
vehemence. “Let him that does, show an unblushing face of innocence.\par
Montoni, you are a villain! If there is treachery in this affair, look\par
to yourself as the author of it. _If_—do I say? _I_—whom you have\par
wronged with unexampled baseness, whom you have injured almost beyond\par
redress! But why do I use words?—Come on, coward, and receive justice\par
at my hands!”\par
\par
“Coward!” cried Montoni, bursting from the people who held him, and\par
rushing on the Count, when they both retreated into the corridor, where\par
the fight continued so desperately, that none of the spectators dared\par
approach them, Montoni swearing, that the first who interfered, should\par
fall by his sword.\par
\par
Jealousy and revenge lent all their fury to Morano, while the superior\par
skill and the temperance of Montoni enabled him to wound his adversary,\par
whom his servants now attempted to seize, but he would not be\par
restrained, and, regardless of his wound, continued to fight. He seemed\par
to be insensible both of pain and loss of blood, and alive only to the\par
energy of his passions. Montoni, on the contrary, persevered in the\par
combat, with a fierce, yet wary, valour; he received the point of\par
Morano’s sword on his arm, but, almost in the same instant, severely\par
wounded and disarmed him. The Count then fell back into the arms of his\par
servant, while Montoni held his sword over him, and bade him ask his\par
life. Morano, sinking under the anguish of his wound, had scarcely\par
replied by a gesture, and by a few words, feebly articulated, that he\par
would not—when he fainted; and Montoni was then going to have plunged\par
the sword into his breast, as he lay senseless, but his arm was\par
arrested by Cavigni. To the interruption he yielded without much\par
difficulty, but his complexion changed almost to blackness, as he\par
looked upon his fallen adversary, and ordered, that he should be\par
carried instantly from the castle.\par
\par
In the mean time, Emily, who had been withheld from leaving the chamber\par
during the affray, now came forward into the corridor, and pleaded a\par
cause of common humanity, with the feelings of the warmest benevolence,\par
when she entreated Montoni to allow Morano the assistance in the\par
castle, which his situation required. But Montoni, who had seldom\par
listened to pity, now seemed rapacious of vengeance, and, with a\par
monster’s cruelty, again ordered his defeated enemy to be taken from\par
the castle, in his present state, though there were only the woods, or\par
a solitary neighbouring cottage, to shelter him from the night.\par
\par
The Count’s servants having declared, that they would not move him till\par
he revived, Montoni’s stood inactive, Cavigni remonstrating, and Emily,\par
superior to Montoni’s menaces, giving water to Morano, and directing\par
the attendants to bind up his wound. At length, Montoni had leisure to\par
feel pain from his own hurt, and he withdrew to examine it.\par
\par
The Count, meanwhile, having slowly recovered, the first object he saw,\par
on raising his eyes, was Emily, bending over him with a countenance\par
strongly expressive of solicitude. He surveyed her with a look of\par
anguish.\par
\par
“I have deserved this,” said he, “but not from Montoni. It is from you,\par
Emily, that I have deserved punishment, yet I receive only pity!” He\par
paused, for he had spoken with difficulty. After a moment, he\par
proceeded. “I must resign you, but not to Montoni. Forgive me the\par
sufferings I have already occasioned you! But for _that_ villain—his\par
infamy shall not go unpunished. Carry me from this place,” said he to\par
his servants. “I am in no condition to travel: you must, therefore,\par
take me to the nearest cottage, for I will not pass the night under his\par
roof, although I may expire on the way from it.”\par
\par
Cesario proposed to go out, and enquire for a cottage, that might\par
receive his master, before he attempted to remove him: but Morano was\par
impatient to be gone; the anguish of his mind seemed to be even greater\par
than that of his wound, and he rejected, with disdain, the offer of\par
Cavigni to entreat Montoni, that he might be suffered to pass the night\par
in the castle. Cesario was now going to call up the carriage to the\par
great gate, but the Count forbade him. “I cannot bear the motion of a\par
carriage,” said he: “call some others of my people, that they may\par
assist in bearing me in their arms.”\par
\par
At length, however, Morano submitted to reason, and consented, that\par
Cesario should first prepare some cottage to receive him. Emily, now\par
that he had recovered his senses, was about to withdraw from the\par
corridor, when a message from Montoni commanded her to do so, and also\par
that the Count, if he was not already gone, should quit the castle\par
immediately. Indignation flashed from Morano’s eyes, and flushed his\par
cheeks.\par
\par
“Tell Montoni,” said he, “that I shall go when it suits my own\par
convenience; that I quit the castle, he dares to call his, as I would\par
the nest of a serpent, and that this is not the last he shall hear from\par
me. Tell him, I will not leave _another_ murder on his conscience, if I\par
can help it.”\par
\par
“Count Morano! do you know what you say?” said Cavigni.\par
\par
“Yes, Signor, I know well what I say, and he will understand well what\par
I mean. His conscience will assist his understanding, on this\par
occasion.”\par
\par
“Count Morano,” said Verezzi, who had hitherto silently observed him,\par
“dare again to insult my friend, and I will plunge this sword in your\par
body.”\par
\par
“It would be an action worthy the friend of a villain!” said Morano, as\par
the strong impulse of his indignation enabled him to raise himself from\par
the arms of his servants; but the energy was momentary, and he sunk\par
back, exhausted by the effort. Montoni’s people, meanwhile, held\par
Verezzi, who seemed inclined, even in this instant, to execute his\par
threat; and Cavigni, who was not so depraved as to abet the cowardly\par
malignity of Verezzi, endeavoured to withdraw him from the corridor;\par
and Emily, whom a compassionate interest had thus long detained, was\par
now quitting it in new terror, when the supplicating voice of Morano\par
arrested her, and, by a feeble gesture, he beckoned her to draw nearer.\par
She advanced with timid steps, but the fainting languor of his\par
countenance again awakened her pity, and overcame her terror.\par
\par
“I am going from hence for ever,” said he: “perhaps, I shall never see\par
you again. I would carry with me your forgiveness, Emily; nay more—I\par
would also carry your good wishes.”\par
\par
“You have my forgiveness, then,” said Emily, “and my sincere wishes for\par
your recovery.”\par
\par
“And only for my recovery?” said Morano, with a sigh. “For your general\par
welfare,” added Emily.\par
\par
“Perhaps I ought to be contented with this,” he resumed; “I certainly\par
have not deserved more; but I would ask you, Emily, sometimes to think\par
of me, and, forgetting my offence, to remember only the passion which\par
occasioned it. I would ask, alas! impossibilities: I would ask you to\par
love me! At this moment, when I am about to part with you, and that,\par
perhaps, for ever, I am scarcely myself. Emily—may you never know the\par
torture of a passion like mine! What do I say? O, that, for me, you\par
might be sensible of such a passion!”\par
\par
Emily looked impatient to be gone. “I entreat you, Count, to consult\par
your own safety,” said she, “and linger here no longer. I tremble for\par
the consequences of Signor Verezzi’s passion, and of Montoni’s\par
resentment, should he learn that you are still here.”\par
\par
Morano’s face was overspread with a momentary crimson, his eyes\par
sparkled, but he seemed endeavouring to conquer his emotion, and\par
replied in a calm voice, “Since you are interested for my safety, I\par
will regard it, and be gone. But, before I go, let me again hear you\par
say, that you wish me well,” said he, fixing on her an earnest and\par
mournful look.\par
\par
Emily repeated her assurances. He took her hand, which she scarcely\par
attempted to withdraw, and put it to his lips. “Farewell, Count\par
Morano!” said Emily; and she turned to go, when a second message\par
arrived from Montoni, and she again conjured Morano, as he valued his\par
life, to quit the castle immediately. He regarded her in silence, with\par
a look of fixed despair. But she had no time to enforce her\par
compassionate entreaties, and, not daring to disobey the second command\par
of Montoni, she left the corridor, to attend him.\par
\par
He was in the cedar parlour, that adjoined the great hall, laid upon a\par
couch, and suffering a degree of anguish from his wound, which few\par
persons could have disguised, as he did. His countenance, which was\par
stern, but calm, expressed the dark passion of revenge, but no symptom\par
of pain; bodily pain, indeed, he had always despised, and had yielded\par
only to the strong and terrible energies of the soul. He was attended\par
by old Carlo and by Signor Bertolini, but Madame Montoni was not with\par
him.\par
\par
Emily trembled, as she approached and received his severe rebuke, for\par
not having obeyed his first summons; and perceived, also, that he\par
attributed her stay in the corridor to a motive, that had not even\par
occurred to her artless mind.\par
\par
“This is an instance of female caprice,” said he, “which I ought to\par
have foreseen. Count Morano, whose suit you obstinately rejected, so\par
long as it was countenanced by me, you favour, it seems, since you find\par
I have dismissed him.”\par
\par
Emily looked astonished. “I do not comprehend you, sir,” said she, “You\par
certainly do not mean to imply, that the design of the Count to visit\par
the double-chamber, was founded upon any approbation of mine.”\par
\par
“To that I reply nothing,” said Montoni; “but it must certainly be a\par
more than common interest, that made you plead so warmly in his cause,\par
and that could detain you thus long in his presence, contrary to my\par
express order—in the presence of a man, whom you have hitherto, on all\par
occasions, most scrupulously shunned!”\par
\par
“I fear, sir, it was a more than common interest, that detained me,”\par
said Emily calmly; “for of late I have been inclined to think, that of\par
compassion is an uncommon one. But how could I, could _you_, sir,\par
witness Count Morano’s deplorable condition, and not wish to relieve\par
it?”\par
\par
“You add hypocrisy to caprice,” said Montoni, frowning, “and an attempt\par
at satire, to both; but, before you undertake to regulate the morals of\par
other persons, you should learn and practise the virtues, which are\par
indispensable to a woman—sincerity, uniformity of conduct and\par
obedience.”\par
\par
Emily, who had always endeavoured to regulate her conduct by the nicest\par
laws, and whose mind was finely sensible, not only of what is just in\par
morals, but of whatever is beautiful in the female character, was\par
shocked by these words; yet, in the next moment, her heart swelled with\par
the consciousness of having deserved praise, instead of censure, and\par
she was proudly silent. Montoni, acquainted with the delicacy of her\par
mind, knew how keenly she would feel his rebuke; but he was a stranger\par
to the luxury of conscious worth, and, therefore, did not foresee the\par
energy of that sentiment, which now repelled his satire. Turning to a\par
servant who had lately entered the room, he asked whether Morano had\par
quitted the castle. The man answered, that his servants were then\par
removing him, on a couch, to a neighbouring cottage. Montoni seemed\par
somewhat appeased, on hearing this; and, when Ludovico appeared, a few\par
moments after, and said, that Morano was gone, he told Emily she might\par
retire to her apartment.\par
\par
She withdrew willingly from his presence; but the thought of passing\par
the remainder of the night in a chamber, which the door from the\par
staircase made liable to the intrusion of any person, now alarmed her\par
more than ever, and she determined to call at Madame Montoni’s room,\par
and request, that Annette might be permitted to be with her.\par
\par
On reaching the great gallery, she heard voices seemingly in dispute,\par
and, her spirits now apt to take alarm, she paused, but soon\par
distinguished some words of Cavigni and Verezzi, and went towards them,\par
in the hope of conciliating their difference. They were alone.\par
Verezzi’s face was still flushed with rage; and, as the first object of\par
it was now removed from him, he appeared willing to transfer his\par
resentment to Cavigni, who seemed to be expostulating, rather than\par
disputing, with him.\par
\par
Verezzi was protesting, that he would instantly inform Montoni of the\par
insult, which Morano had thrown out against him, and above all, that,\par
wherein he had accused him of murder.\par
\par
“There is no answering,” said Cavigni, “for the words of a man in a\par
passion; little serious regard ought to be paid to them. If you persist\par
in your resolution, the consequences may be fatal to both. We have now\par
more serious interests to pursue, than those of a petty revenge.”\par
\par
Emily joined her entreaties to Cavigni’s arguments, and they, at\par
length, prevailed so far, as that Verezzi consented to retire, without\par
seeing Montoni.\par
\par
On calling at her aunt’s apartment, she found it fastened. In a few\par
minutes, however, it was opened by Madame Montoni herself.\par
\par
It may be remembered, that it was by a door leading into the bedroom\par
from a back passage, that Emily had secretly entered a few hours\par
preceding. She now conjectured, by the calmness of Madame Montoni’s\par
air, that she was not apprised of the accident, which had befallen her\par
husband, and was beginning to inform her of it, in the tenderest manner\par
she could, when her aunt interrupted her, by saying, she was acquainted\par
with the whole affair.\par
\par
Emily knew indeed, that she had little reason to love Montoni, but\par
could scarcely have believed her capable of such perfect apathy, as she\par
now discovered towards him; having obtained permission, however, for\par
Annette to sleep in her chamber, she went thither immediately.\par
\par
A track of blood appeared along the corridor, leading to it; and on the\par
spot, where the Count and Montoni had fought, the whole floor was\par
stained. Emily shuddered, and leaned on Annette, as she passed. When\par
she reached her apartment, she instantly determined, since the door of\par
the staircase had been left open, and that Annette was now with her, to\par
explore whither it led,—a circumstance now materially connected with\par
her own safety. Annette accordingly, half curious and half afraid,\par
proposed to descend the stairs; but, on approaching the door, they\par
perceived, that it was already fastened without, and their care was\par
then directed to the securing it on the inside also, by placing against\par
it as much of the heavy furniture of the room, as they could lift.\par
Emily then retired to bed, and Annette continued on a chair by the\par
hearth, where some feeble embers remained.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER VII\par
\par
Of aery tongues, that syllable men’s names\par
On sands and shores and desert wildernesses.\par
                    MILTON\par
\par
\par
It is now necessary to mention some circumstances, which could not be\par
related amidst the events of Emily’s hasty departure from Venice, or\par
together with those, which so rapidly succeeded to her arrival in the\par
castle.\par
\par
On the morning of her journey, Count Morano had gone at the appointed\par
hour to the mansion of Montoni, to demand his bride. When he reached\par
it, he was somewhat surprised by the silence and solitary air of the\par
portico, where Montoni’s lacqueys usually loitered; but surprise was\par
soon changed to astonishment, and astonishment to the rage of\par
disappointment, when the door was opened by an old woman, who told his\par
servants, that her master and his family had left Venice, early in the\par
morning, for _Terra-firma_. Scarcely believing what his servants told,\par
he left his gondola, and rushed into the hall to enquire further. The\par
old woman, who was the only person left in care of the mansion,\par
persisted in her story, which the silent and deserted apartments soon\par
convinced him was no fiction. He then seized her with a menacing air,\par
as if he meant to wreak all his vengeance upon her, at the same time\par
asking her twenty questions in a breath, and all these with a\par
gesticulation so furious, that she was deprived of the power of\par
answering them; then suddenly letting her go, he stamped about the\par
hall, like a madman, cursing Montoni and his own folly.\par
\par
When the good woman was at liberty, and had somewhat recovered from her\par
fright, she told him all she knew of the affair, which was, indeed,\par
very little, but enough to enable Morano to discover, that Montoni was\par
gone to his castle on the Apennine. Thither he followed, as soon as his\par
servants could complete the necessary preparation for the journey,\par
accompanied by a friend, and attended by a number of his people,\par
determined to obtain Emily, or a full revenge on Montoni. When his mind\par
had recovered from the first effervescence of rage, and his thoughts\par
became less obscured, his conscience hinted to him certain\par
circumstances, which, in some measure, explained the conduct of\par
Montoni: but how the latter could have been led to suspect an\par
intention, which, he had believed, was known only to himself, he could\par
not even guess. On this occasion, however, he had been partly betrayed\par
by that sympathetic intelligence, which may be said to exist between\par
bad minds, and which teaches one man to judge what another will do in\par
the same circumstances. Thus it was with Montoni, who had now received\par
indisputable proof of a truth, which he had some time suspected—that\par
Morano’s circumstances, instead of being affluent, as he had been\par
bidden to believe, were greatly involved. Montoni had been interested\par
in his suit, by motives entirely selfish, those of avarice and pride;\par
the last of which would have been gratified by an alliance with a\par
Venetian nobleman, the former by Emily’s estate in Gascony, which he\par
had stipulated, as the price of his favour, should be delivered up to\par
him from the day of her marriage. In the meantime, he had been led to\par
suspect the consequence of the Count’s boundless extravagance; but it\par
was not till the evening, preceding the intended nuptials, that he\par
obtained certain information of his distressed circumstances. He did\par
not hesitate then to infer, that Morano designed to defraud him of\par
Emily’s estate; and in this supposition he was confirmed, and with\par
apparent reason, by the subsequent conduct of the Count, who, after\par
having appointed to meet him on that night, for the purpose of signing\par
the instrument, which was to secure to him his reward, failed in his\par
engagement. Such a circumstance, indeed, in a man of Morano’s gay and\par
thoughtless character, and at a time when his mind was engaged by the\par
bustle of preparation for his nuptials, might have been attributed to a\par
cause less decisive, than design; but Montoni did not hesitate an\par
instant to interpret it his own way, and, after vainly waiting the\par
Count’s arrival, for several hours, he gave orders for his people to be\par
in readiness to set off at a moment’s notice. By hastening to Udolpho\par
he intended to remove Emily from the reach of Morano, as well as to\par
break off the affair, without submitting himself to useless\par
altercation: and, if the Count meant what he called honourably, he\par
would doubtless follow Emily, and sign the writings in question. If\par
this was done, so little consideration had Montoni for her welfare,\par
that he would not have scrupled to sacrifice her to a man of ruined\par
fortune, since by that means he could enrich himself; and he forbore to\par
mention to her the motive of his sudden journey, lest the hope it might\par
revive should render her more intractable, when submission would be\par
required.\par
\par
With these considerations, he had left Venice; and, with others totally\par
different, Morano had, soon after, pursued his steps across the rugged\par
Apennines. When his arrival was announced at the castle, Montoni did\par
not believe, that he would have presumed to show himself, unless he had\par
meant to fulfil his engagement, and he, therefore, readily admitted\par
him; but the enraged countenance and expressions of Morano, as he\par
entered the apartment, instantly undeceived him; and, when Montoni had\par
explained, in part, the motives of his abrupt departure from Venice,\par
the Count still persisted in demanding Emily, and reproaching Montoni,\par
without even naming the former stipulation.\par
\par
Montoni, at length, weary of the dispute, deferred the settling of it\par
till the morrow, and Morano retired with some hope, suggested by\par
Montoni’s apparent indecision. When, however, in the silence of his own\par
apartment, he began to consider the past conversation, the character of\par
Montoni, and some former instances of his duplicity, the hope, which he\par
had admitted, vanished, and he determined not to neglect the present\par
possibility of obtaining Emily by other means. To his confidential\par
valet he told his design of carrying away Emily, and sent him back to\par
Montoni’s servants to find out one among them, who might enable him to\par
execute it. The choice of this person he entrusted to the fellow’s own\par
discernment, and not imprudently; for he discovered a man, whom Montoni\par
had, on some former occasion, treated harshly, and who was now ready to\par
betray him. This man conducted Cesario round the castle, through a\par
private passage, to the staircase, that led to Emily’s chamber; then\par
showed him a short way out of the building, and afterwards procured him\par
the keys, that would secure his retreat. The man was well rewarded for\par
his trouble; how the Count was rewarded for his treachery, had already\par
appeared.\par
\par
Meanwhile, old Carlo had overheard two of Morano’s servants, who had\par
been ordered to be in waiting with the carriage, beyond the castle\par
walls, expressing their surprise at their master’s sudden, and secret\par
departure, for the valet had entrusted them with no more of Morano’s\par
designs, than it was necessary for them to execute. They, however,\par
indulged themselves in surmises, and in expressing them to each other;\par
and from these Carlo had drawn a just conclusion. But, before he\par
ventured to disclose his apprehensions to Montoni, he endeavoured to\par
obtain further confirmation of them, and, for this purpose, placed\par
himself, with one of his fellow-servants, at the door of Emily’s\par
apartment, that opened upon the corridor. He did not watch long in\par
vain, though the growling of the dog had once nearly betrayed him. When\par
he was convinced, that Morano was in the room, and had listened long\par
enough to his conversation, to understand his scheme, he immediately\par
alarmed Montoni, and thus rescued Emily from the designs of the Count.\par
\par
Montoni, on the following morning, appeared as usual, except that he\par
wore his wounded arm in a sling; he went out upon the ramparts;\par
overlooked the men employed in repairing them; gave orders for\par
additional workmen, and then came into the castle to give audience to\par
several persons, who were just arrived, and who were shown into a\par
private apartment, where he communicated with them, for near an hour.\par
Carlo was then summoned, and ordered to conduct the strangers to a part\par
of the castle, which, in former times, had been occupied by the upper\par
servants of the family, and to provide them with every necessary\par
refreshment.—When he had done this, he was bidden to return to his\par
master.\par
\par
Meanwhile, the Count remained in a cottage in the skirts of the woods\par
below, suffering under bodily and mental pain, and meditating deep\par
revenge against Montoni. His servant, whom he had dispatched for a\par
surgeon to the nearest town, which was, however, at a considerable\par
distance, did not return till the following day, when, his wounds being\par
examined and dressed, the practitioner refused to deliver any positive\par
opinion, concerning the degree of danger attending them; but giving his\par
patient a composing draught and ordering him to be quiet, remained at\par
the cottage to watch the event.\par
\par
Emily, for the remainder of the late eventful night, had been suffered\par
to sleep, undisturbed; and, when her mind recovered from the confusion\par
of slumber, and she remembered, that she was now released from the\par
addresses of Count Morano, her spirits were suddenly relieved from a\par
part of the terrible anxiety, that had long oppressed them; that which\par
remained, arose chiefly from a recollection of Morano’s assertions,\par
concerning the schemes of Montoni. He had said, that plans of the\par
latter, concerning Emily, were insearchable, yet that he knew them to\par
be terrible. At the time he uttered this, she almost believed it to be\par
designed for the purpose of prevailing with her to throw herself into\par
his protection, and she still thought it might be chiefly so accounted\par
for; but his assertions had left an impression on her mind, which a\par
consideration of the character and former conduct of Montoni did not\par
contribute to efface. She, however, checked her propensity to\par
anticipate evil; and, determined to enjoy this respite from actual\par
misfortune, tried to dismiss thought, took her instruments for drawing,\par
and placed herself at a window, to select into a landscape some\par
features of the scenery without.\par
\par
As she was thus employed, she saw, walking on the rampart below, the\par
men, who had so lately arrived at the castle. The sight of strangers\par
surprised her, but still more, of strangers such as these. There was a\par
singularity in their dress, and a certain fierceness in their air, that\par
fixed all her attention. She withdrew from the casement, while they\par
passed, but soon returned to observe them further. Their figures seemed\par
so well suited to the wildness of the surrounding objects, that, as\par
they stood surveying the castle, she sketched them for banditti, amid\par
the mountain-view of her picture, when she had finished which, she was\par
surprised to observe the spirit of her group. But she had copied from\par
nature.\par
\par
Carlo, when he had placed refreshment before these men in the apartment\par
assigned to them, returned, as he was ordered, to Montoni, who was\par
anxious to discover by what servant the keys of the castle had been\par
delivered to Morano, on the preceding night. But this man, though he\par
was too faithful to his master quietly to see him injured, would not\par
betray a fellow-servant even to justice; he, therefore, pretended to be\par
ignorant who it was, that had conspired with Count Morano, and related,\par
as before, that he had only overheard some of the strangers describing\par
the plot.\par
\par
Montoni’s suspicions naturally fell upon the porter, whom he ordered\par
now to attend. Carlo hesitated, and then with slow steps went to seek\par
him.\par
\par
Barnardine, the porter, denied the accusation with a countenance so\par
steady and undaunted, that Montoni could scarcely believe him guilty,\par
though he knew not how to think him innocent. At length, the man was\par
dismissed from his presence, and, though the real offender, escaped\par
detection.\par
\par
Montoni then went to his wife’s apartment, whither Emily followed soon\par
after, but, finding them in high dispute, was instantly leaving the\par
room, when her aunt called her back, and desired her to stay.—“You\par
shall be a witness,” said she, “of my opposition. Now, sir, repeat the\par
command, I have so often refused to obey.”\par
\par
Montoni turned, with a stern countenance, to Emily, and bade her quit\par
the apartment, while his wife persisted in desiring, that she would\par
stay. Emily was eager to escape from this scene of contention, and\par
anxious, also, to serve her aunt; but she despaired of conciliating\par
Montoni, in whose eyes the rising tempest of his soul flashed terribly.\par
\par
“Leave the room,” said he, in a voice of thunder. Emily obeyed, and,\par
walking down to the rampart, which the strangers had now left,\par
continued to meditate on the unhappy marriage of her father’s sister,\par
and on her own desolate situation, occasioned by the ridiculous\par
imprudence of her, whom she had always wished to respect and love.\par
Madame Montoni’s conduct had, indeed, rendered it impossible for Emily\par
to do either; but her gentle heart was touched by her distress, and, in\par
the pity thus awakened, she forgot the injurious treatment she had\par
received from her.\par
\par
As she sauntered on the rampart, Annette appeared at the hall door,\par
looked cautiously round, and then advanced to meet her.\par
\par
“Dear ma’amselle, I have been looking for you all over the castle,”\par
said she. “If you will step this way, I will show you a picture.”\par
\par
“A picture!” exclaimed Emily, and shuddered.\par
\par
“Yes, ma’am, a picture of the late lady of this place. Old Carlo just\par
now told me it was her, and I thought you would be curious to see it.\par
As to my lady, you know, ma’amselle, one cannot talk about such things\par
to her.”\par
\par
“And so,” said Emily smilingly, “as you must talk of them to somebody—”\par
\par
“Why, yes, ma’amselle; what can one do in such a place as this, if one\par
must not talk? If I was in a dungeon, if they would let me talk—it\par
would be some comfort; nay, I would talk, if it was only to the walls.\par
But come, ma’amselle, we lose time—let me show you the picture.”\par
\par
“Is it veiled?” said Emily, pausing.\par
\par
“Dear ma’amselle!” said Annette, fixing her eyes on Emily’s face, “what\par
makes you look so pale?—are you ill?”\par
\par
“No, Annette, I am well enough, but I have no desire to see this\par
picture; return into the hall.”\par
\par
“What! ma’am, not to see the lady of this castle?” said the girl; “the\par
lady, who disappeared to strangely? Well! now, I would have run to the\par
furthest mountain we can see, yonder, to have got a sight of such a\par
picture; and, to speak my mind, that strange story is all, that makes\par
me care about this old castle, though it makes me thrill all over, as\par
it were, whenever I think of it.”\par
\par
“Yes, Annette, you love the wonderful; but do you know, that, unless\par
you guard against this inclination, it will lead you into all the\par
misery of superstition?”\par
\par
Annette might have smiled in her turn, at this sage observation of\par
Emily, who could tremble with ideal terrors, as much as herself, and\par
listen almost as eagerly to the recital of a mysterious story. Annette\par
urged her request.\par
\par
“Are you sure it is a picture?” said Emily, “Have you seen it?—Is it\par
veiled?”\par
\par
“Holy Maria! ma’amselle, yes, no, yes. I am sure it is a picture—I have\par
seen it, and it is not veiled!”\par
\par
The tone and look of surprise, with which this was uttered, recalled\par
Emily’s prudence; who concealed her emotion under a smile, and bade\par
Annette lead her to the picture. It was in an obscure chamber,\par
adjoining that part of the castle, allotted to the servants. Several\par
other portraits hung on the walls, covered, like this, with dust and\par
cobweb.\par
\par
“That is it, ma’amselle,” said Annette, in a low voice, and pointing.\par
Emily advanced, and surveyed the picture. It represented a lady in the\par
flower of youth and beauty; her features were handsome and noble, full\par
of strong expression, but had little of the captivating sweetness, that\par
Emily had looked for, and still less of the pensive mildness she loved.\par
It was a countenance, which spoke the language of passion, rather than\par
that of sentiment; a haughty impatience of misfortune—not the placid\par
melancholy of a spirit injured, yet resigned.\par
\par
“How many years have passed, since this lady disappeared, Annette?”\par
said Emily.\par
\par
“Twenty years, ma’amselle, or thereabout, as they tell me; I know it is\par
a long while ago.” Emily continued to gaze upon the portrait.\par
\par
“I think,” resumed Annette, “the Signor would do well to hang it in a\par
better place, than this old chamber. Now, in my mind, he ought to place\par
the picture of a lady, who gave him all these riches, in the handsomest\par
room in the castle. But he may have good reasons for what he does: and\par
some people do say that he has lost his riches, as well as his\par
gratitude. But hush, ma’am, not a word!” added Annette, laying her\par
finger on her lips. Emily was too much absorbed in thought, to hear\par
what she said.\par
\par
“’Tis a handsome lady, I am sure,” continued Annette: “the Signor need\par
not be ashamed to put her in the great apartment, where the veiled\par
picture hangs.” Emily turned round. “But for that matter, she would be\par
as little seen there, as here, for the door is always locked, I find.”\par
\par
“Let us leave this chamber,” said Emily: “and let me caution you again,\par
Annette; be guarded in your conversation, and never tell, that you know\par
anything of that picture.”\par
\par
“Holy Mother!” exclaimed Annette, “it is no secret; why all the\par
servants have seen it already!”\par
\par
Emily started. “How is this?” said she—“Have seen it! When?—how?”\par
\par
“Dear, ma’amselle, there is nothing surprising in that; we had all a\par
little more _curiousness_ than you had.”\par
\par
“I thought you told me, the door was kept locked?” said Emily.\par
\par
“If that was the case, ma’amselle,” replied Annette, looking about her,\par
“how could we get here?”\par
\par
“Oh, you mean _this_ picture,” said Emily, with returning calmness.\par
“Well, Annette, here is nothing more to engage my attention; we will\par
go.”\par
\par
Emily, as she passed to her own apartment, saw Montoni go down to the\par
hall, and she turned into her aunt’s dressing-room, whom she found\par
weeping and alone, grief and resentment struggling on her countenance.\par
Pride had hitherto restrained complaint. Judging of Emily’s disposition\par
from her own, and from a consciousness of what her treatment of her\par
deserved, she had believed, that her griefs would be cause of triumph\par
to her niece, rather than of sympathy; that she would despise, not pity\par
her. But she knew not the tenderness and benevolence of Emily’s heart,\par
that had always taught her to forget her own injuries in the\par
misfortunes of her enemy. The sufferings of others, whoever they might\par
be, called forth her ready compassion, which dissipated at once every\par
obscuring cloud to goodness, that passion or prejudice might have\par
raised in her mind.\par
\par
Madame Montoni’s sufferings, at length, rose above her pride, and, when\par
Emily had before entered the room, she would have told them all, had\par
not her husband prevented her; now that she was no longer restrained by\par
his presence, she poured forth all her complaints to her niece.\par
\par
“O Emily!” she exclaimed, “I am the most wretched of women—I am indeed\par
cruelly treated! Who, with my prospects of happiness, could have\par
foreseen such a wretched fate as this?—who could have thought, when I\par
married such a man as the Signor, I should ever have to bewail my lot?\par
But there is no judging what is for the best—there is no knowing what\par
is for our good! The most flattering prospects often change—the best\par
judgments may be deceived—who could have foreseen, when I married the\par
Signor, that I should ever repent my _generosity?_”\par
\par
Emily thought she might have foreseen it, but this was not a thought of\par
triumph. She placed herself in a chair near her aunt, took her hand,\par
and, with one of those looks of soft compassion, which might\par
characterise the countenance of a guardian angel, spoke to her in the\par
tenderest accents. But these did not sooth Madame Montoni, whom\par
impatience to talk made unwilling to listen. She wanted to complain,\par
not to be consoled; and it was by exclamations of complaint only, that\par
Emily learned the particular circumstances of her affliction.\par
\par
“Ungrateful man!” said Madame Montoni, “he has deceived me in every\par
respect; and now he has taken me from my country and friends, to shut\par
me up in this old castle; and, here he thinks he can compel me to do\par
whatever he designs! But he shall find himself mistaken, he shall find\par
that no threats can alter—But who would have believed! who would have\par
supposed, that a man of his family and apparent wealth had absolutely\par
no fortune?—no, scarcely a sequin of his own! I did all for the best; I\par
thought he was a man of consequence, of great property, or I am sure I\par
would never have married him,—ungrateful, artful man!” She paused to\par
take breath.\par
\par
“Dear Madam, be composed,” said Emily: “the Signor may not be so rich\par
as you had reason to expect, but surely he cannot be very poor, since\par
this castle and the mansion at Venice are his. May I ask what are the\par
circumstances, that particularly affect you?”\par
\par
“What are the circumstances!” exclaimed Madame Montoni with resentment:\par
“why is it not sufficient, that he had long ago ruined his own fortune\par
by play, and that he has since lost what I brought him—and that now he\par
would compel me to sign away my settlement (it was well I had the chief\par
of my property settled on myself!) that he may lose this also, or throw\par
it away in wild schemes, which nobody can understand but himself? And,\par
and—is not all this sufficient?”\par
\par
“It is, indeed,” said Emily, “but you must recollect, dear madam, that\par
I knew nothing of all this.”\par
\par
“Well, and is it not sufficient,” rejoined her aunt, “that he is also\par
absolutely ruined, that he is sunk deeply in debt, and that neither\par
this castle, nor the mansion at Venice, is his own, if all his debts,\par
honourable and dishonourable, were paid!”\par
\par
“I am shocked by what you tell me, madam,” said Emily.\par
\par
“And is it not enough,” interrupted Madame Montoni, “that he has\par
treated me with neglect, with cruelty, because I refused to relinquish\par
my settlements, and, instead of being frightened by his menaces,\par
resolutely defied him, and upbraided him with his shameful conduct? But\par
I bore all meekly,—you know, niece, I never uttered a word of\par
complaint, till now; no! That such a disposition as mine should be so\par
imposed upon! That I, whose only faults are too much kindness, too much\par
generosity, should be chained for life to such a vile, deceitful, cruel\par
monster!”\par
\par
Want of breath compelled Madame Montoni to stop. If anything could have\par
made Emily smile in these moments, it would have been this speech of\par
her aunt, delivered in a voice very little below a scream, and with a\par
vehemence of gesticulation and of countenance, that turned the whole\par
into burlesque. Emily saw, that her misfortunes did not admit of real\par
consolation, and, contemning the commonplace terms of superficial\par
comfort, she was silent; while Madame Montoni, jealous of her own\par
consequence, mistook this for the silence of indifference, or of\par
contempt, and reproached her with want of duty and feeling.\par
\par
“O! I suspected what all this boasted sensibility would prove to be!”\par
rejoined she; “I thought it would not teach you to feel either duty, or\par
affection, for your relations, who have treated you like their own\par
daughter!”\par
\par
“Pardon me, madam,” said Emily, mildly, “it is not natural to me to\par
boast, and if it was, I am sure I would not boast of sensibility—a\par
quality, perhaps, more to be feared, than desired.”\par
\par
“Well, well, niece, I will not dispute with you. But, as I said,\par
Montoni threatens me with violence, if I any longer refuse to sign away\par
my settlements, and this was the subject of our contest, when you came\par
into the room before. Now, I am determined no power on earth shall make\par
me do this. Neither will I bear all this tamely. He shall hear his true\par
character from me; I will tell him all he deserves, in spite of his\par
threats and cruel treatment.”\par
\par
Emily seized a pause of Madame Montoni’s voice, to speak. “Dear madam,”\par
said she, “but will not this serve to irritate the Signor\par
unnecessarily? will it not provoke the harsh treatment you dread?”\par
\par
“I do not care,” replied Madame Montoni, “it does not signify: I will\par
not submit to such usage. You would have me give up my settlements,\par
too, I suppose!”\par
\par
“No, madam, I do not exactly mean that.”\par
\par
“What is it you do mean then?”\par
\par
“You spoke of reproaching the Signor,”—said Emily, with hesitation.\par
“Why, does he not deserve reproaches?” said her aunt.\par
\par
“Certainly he does; but will it be prudent in you, madam, to make\par
them?”\par
\par
“Prudent!” exclaimed Madame Montoni. “Is this a time to talk of\par
prudence, when one is threatened with all sorts of violence?”\par
\par
“It is to avoid that violence, that prudence is necessary.” said Emily.\par
\par
“Of prudence!” continued Madame Montoni, without attending to her, “of\par
prudence towards a man, who does not scruple to break all the common\par
ties of humanity in his conduct to me! And is it for me to consider\par
prudence in my behaviour towards him! I am not so mean.”\par
\par
“It is for your own sake, not for the Signor’s, madam,” said Emily\par
modestly, “that you should consult prudence. Your reproaches, however\par
just, cannot punish him, but they may provoke him to further violence\par
against you.”\par
\par
“What! would you have me submit, then, to whatever he commands—would\par
you have me kneel down at his feet, and thank him for his cruelties?\par
Would you have me give up my settlements?”\par
\par
“How much you mistake me, madam!” said Emily, “I am unequal to advise\par
you on a point so important as the last: but you will pardon me for\par
saying, that, if you consult your own peace, you will try to conciliate\par
Signor Montoni, rather than to irritate him by reproaches.”\par
\par
“Conciliate indeed! I tell you, niece, it is utterly impossible; I\par
disdain to attempt it.”\par
\par
Emily was shocked to observe the perverted understanding and obstinate\par
temper of Madame Montoni; but, not less grieved for her sufferings, she\par
looked round for some alleviating circumstance to offer her. “Your\par
situation is, perhaps, not so desperate, dear madam,” said Emily, “as\par
you may imagine. The Signor may represent his affairs to be worse than\par
they are, for the purpose of pleading a stronger necessity for his\par
possession of your settlement. Besides, so long as you keep this, you\par
may look forward to it as a resource, at least, that will afford you a\par
competence, should the Signor’s future conduct compel you to sue for\par
separation.”\par
\par
Madame Montoni impatiently interrupted her. “Unfeeling, cruel girl!”\par
said she, “and so you would persuade me, that I have no reason to\par
complain; that the Signor is in very flourishing circumstances, that my\par
future prospects promise nothing but comfort, and that my griefs are as\par
fanciful and romantic as your own! Is it the way to console me, to\par
endeavour to persuade me out of my senses and my feelings, because you\par
happen to have no feelings yourself? I thought I was opening my heart\par
to a person, who could sympathise in my distress, but I find, that your\par
people of sensibility can feel for nobody but themselves! You may\par
retire to your chamber.”\par
\par
Emily, without replying, immediately left the room, with a mingled\par
emotion of pity and contempt, and hastened to her own, where she\par
yielded to the mournful reflections, which a knowledge of her aunt’s\par
situation had occasioned. The conversation of the Italian with\par
Valancourt, in France, again occurred to her. His hints, respecting the\par
broken fortunes of Montoni, were now completely justified; those, also,\par
concerning his character, appeared not less so, though the particular\par
circumstances, connected with his fame, to which the stranger had\par
alluded, yet remained to be explained. Notwithstanding, that her own\par
observations and the words of Count Morano had convinced her, that\par
Montoni’s situation was not what it formerly appeared to be, the\par
intelligence she had just received from her aunt on this point, struck\par
her with all the force of astonishment, which was not weakened, when\par
she considered the present style of Montoni’s living, the number of\par
servants he maintained, and the new expences he was incurring, by\par
repairing and fortifying his castle. Her anxiety for her aunt and for\par
herself increased with reflection. Several assertions of Morano, which,\par
on the preceding night, she had believed were prompted either by\par
interest, or by resentment, now returned to her mind with the strength\par
of truth. She could not doubt, that Montoni had formerly agreed to give\par
her to the Count, for a pecuniary reward;—his character, and his\par
distressed circumstances justified the belief; these, also, seemed to\par
confirm Morano’s assertion, that he now designed to dispose of her,\par
more advantageously for himself, to a richer suitor.\par
\par
Amidst the reproaches, which Morano had thrown out against Montoni, he\par
had said—he would not quit the castle _he dared to call his_, nor\par
willingly leave _another_ murder on his conscience—hints, which might\par
have no other origin than the passion of the moment: but Emily was now\par
inclined to account for them more seriously, and she shuddered to\par
think, that she was in the hands of a man, to whom it was even possible\par
they could apply. At length, considering, that reflection could neither\par
release her from her melancholy situation, nor enable her to bear it\par
with greater fortitude, she tried to divert her anxiety, and took down\par
from her little library a volume of her favourite Ariosto; but his wild\par
imagery and rich invention could not long enchant her attention; his\par
spells did not reach her heart, and over her sleeping fancy they\par
played, without awakening it.\par
\par
She now put aside the book, and took her lute, for it was seldom that\par
her sufferings refused to yield to the magic of sweet sounds; when they\par
did so, she was oppressed by sorrow, that came from excess of\par
tenderness and regret; and there were times, when music had increased\par
such sorrow to a degree, that was scarcely endurable; when, if it had\par
not suddenly ceased, she might have lost her reason. Such was the time,\par
when she mourned for her father, and heard the midnight strains, that\par
floated by her window near the convent in Languedoc, on the night that\par
followed his death.\par
\par
She continued to play, till Annette brought dinner into her chamber, at\par
which Emily was surprised, and enquired whose order she obeyed. “My\par
lady’s, ma’amselle,” replied Annette: “the Signor ordered her dinner to\par
be carried to her own apartment, and so she has sent you yours. There\par
have been sad doings between them, worse than ever, I think.”\par
\par
Emily, not appearing to notice what she said, sat down to the little\par
table, that was spread for her. But Annette was not to be silenced thus\par
easily. While she waited, she told of the arrival of the men, whom\par
Emily had observed on the ramparts, and expressed much surprise at\par
their strange appearance, as well as at the manner, in which they had\par
been attended by Montoni’s order. “Do they dine with the Signor, then?”\par
said Emily.\par
\par
“No, ma’amselle, they dined long ago, in an apartment at the north end\par
of the castle, but I know not when they are to go, for the Signor told\par
old Carlo to see them provided with everything necessary. They have\par
been walking all about the castle, and asking questions of the workmen\par
on the ramparts. I never saw such strange-looking men in my life; I am\par
frightened whenever I see them.”\par
\par
Emily enquired, if she had heard of Count Morano, and whether he was\par
likely to recover: but Annette only knew, that he was lodged in a\par
cottage in the wood below, and that everybody said he must die. Emily’s\par
countenance discovered her emotion.\par
\par
“Dear ma’amselle,” said Annette, “to see how young ladies will disguise\par
themselves, when they are in love! I thought you hated the Count, or I\par
am sure I would not have told you; and I am sure you have cause enough\par
to hate him.”\par
\par
“I hope I hate nobody,” replied Emily, trying to smile; “but certainly\par
I do not love Count Morano. I should be shocked to hear of any person\par
dying by violent means.”\par
\par
“Yes, ma’amselle, but it is his own fault.”\par
\par
Emily looked displeased; and Annette, mistaking the cause of her\par
displeasure, immediately began to excuse the Count, in her way. “To be\par
sure, it was very ungenteel behaviour,” said she, “to break into a\par
lady’s room, and then, when he found his discoursing was not agreeable\par
to her, to refuse to go; and then, when the gentleman of the castle\par
comes to desire him to walk about his business—to turn round, and draw\par
his sword, and swear he’ll run him through the body! To be sure it was\par
very ungenteel behaviour, but then he was disguised in love, and so did\par
not know what he was about.”\par
\par
“Enough of this,” said Emily, who now smiled without an effort; and\par
Annette returned to a mention of the disagreement between Montoni, and\par
her lady. “It is nothing new,” said she: “we saw and heard enough of\par
this at Venice, though I never told you of it, ma’amselle.”\par
\par
“Well, Annette, it was very prudent of you not to mention it then: be\par
as prudent now; the subject is an unpleasant one.”\par
\par
“Ah dear, ma’amselle!—to see now how considerate you can be about some\par
folks, who care so little about you! I cannot bear to see you so\par
deceived, and I must tell you. But it is all for your own good, and not\par
to spite my lady, though, to speak truth, I have little reason to love\par
her; but—”\par
\par
“You are not speaking thus of my aunt, I hope, Annette?” said Emily,\par
gravely.\par
\par
“Yes, ma’amselle, but I am, though; and if you knew as much as I do,\par
you would not look so angry. I have often, and often, heard the Signor\par
and her talking over your marriage with the Count, and she always\par
advised him never to give up to your foolish whims, as she was pleased\par
to call them, but to be resolute, and compel you to be obedient,\par
whether you would, or no. And I am sure, my heart has ached a thousand\par
times, and I have thought, when she was so unhappy herself, she might\par
have felt a little for other people, and—”\par
\par
“I thank you for your pity, Annette,” said Emily, interrupting her:\par
“but my aunt was unhappy then, and that disturbed her temper perhaps,\par
or I think—I am sure—You may take away, Annette, I have done.”\par
\par
“Dear ma’amselle, you have eat nothing at all! Do try, and take a\par
little bit more. Disturbed her temper truly! why, her temper is always\par
disturbed, I think. And at Thoulouse I have heard my lady talking of\par
you and Mons. Valancourt to Madame Merveille and Madame Vaison, often\par
and often, in a very ill-natured way, as I thought, telling them what a\par
deal of trouble she had to keep you in order, and what a fatigue and\par
distress it was to her, and that she believed you would run away with\par
Mons. Valancourt, if she was not to watch you closely; and that you\par
connived at his coming about the house at night, and—”\par
\par
“Good God!” exclaimed Emily, blushing deeply, “it is surely impossible\par
my aunt could thus have represented me!”\par
\par
“Indeed, ma’am, I say nothing more than the truth, and not all of that.\par
But I thought, myself, she might have found something better to\par
discourse about, than the faults of her own niece, even if you had been\par
in fault, ma’amselle; but I did not believe a word of what she said.\par
But my lady does not care what she says against anybody, for that\par
matter.”\par
\par
“However that may be, Annette,” interrupted Emily, recovering her\par
composure, “it does not become you to speak of the faults of my aunt to\par
me. I know you have meant well, but—say no more.—I have quite dined.”\par
\par
Annette blushed, looked down, and then began slowly to clear the table.\par
\par
“Is this, then, the reward of my ingenuousness?” said Emily, when she\par
was alone; “the treatment I am to receive from a relation—an aunt—who\par
ought to have been the guardian, not the slanderer of my\par
reputation,—who, as a woman, ought to have respected the delicacy of\par
female honour, and, as a relation, should have protected mine! But, to\par
utter falsehoods on so nice a subject—to repay the openness, and, I may\par
say with honest pride, the propriety of my conduct, with\par
slanders—required a depravity of heart, such as I could scarcely have\par
believed existed, such as I weep to find in a relation. O! what a\par
contrast does her character present to that of my beloved father; while\par
envy and low cunning form the chief traits of hers, his was\par
distinguished by benevolence and philosophic wisdom! But now, let me\par
only remember, if possible, that she is unfortunate.”\par
\par
Emily threw her veil over her, and went down to walk upon the ramparts,\par
the only walk, indeed, which was open to her, though she often wished,\par
that she might be permitted to ramble among the woods below, and still\par
more, that she might sometimes explore the sublime scenes of the\par
surrounding country. But, as Montoni would not suffer her to pass the\par
gates of the castle, she tried to be contented with the romantic views\par
she beheld from the walls. The peasants, who had been employed on the\par
fortifications, had left their work, and the ramparts were silent and\par
solitary. Their lonely appearance, together with the gloom of a\par
lowering sky, assisted the musings of her mind, and threw over it a\par
kind of melancholy tranquillity, such as she often loved to indulge.\par
She turned to observe a fine effect of the sun, as his rays, suddenly\par
streaming from behind a heavy cloud, lighted up the west towers of the\par
castle, while the rest of the edifice was in deep shade, except, that,\par
through a lofty gothic arch, adjoining the tower, which led to another\par
terrace, the beams darted in full splendour, and showed the three\par
strangers she had observed in the morning. Perceiving them, she\par
started, and a momentary fear came over her, as she looked up the long\par
rampart, and saw no other persons. While she hesitated, they\par
approached. The gate at the end of the terrace, whither they were\par
advancing, she knew, was always locked, and she could not depart by the\par
opposite extremity, without meeting them; but, before she passed them,\par
she hastily drew a thin veil over her face, which did, indeed, but ill\par
conceal her beauty. They looked earnestly at her, and spoke to each\par
other in bad Italian, of which she caught only a few words; but the\par
fierceness of their countenances, now that she was near enough to\par
discriminate them, struck her yet more than the wild singularity of\par
their air and dress had formerly done. It was the countenance and\par
figure of him, who walked between the other two, that chiefly seized\par
her attention, which expressed a sullen haughtiness and a kind of dark\par
watchful villany, that gave a thrill of horror to her heart. All this\par
was so legibly written on his features, as to be seen by a single\par
glance, for she passed the group swiftly, and her timid eyes scarcely\par
rested on them a moment. Having reached the terrace, she stopped, and\par
perceived the strangers standing in the shadow of one of the turrets,\par
gazing after her, and seemingly, by their action, in earnest\par
conversation. She immediately left the rampart, and retired to her\par
apartment.\par
\par
In the evening, Montoni sat late, carousing with his guests in the\par
cedar chamber. His recent triumph over Count Morano, or, perhaps, some\par
other circumstance, contributed to elevate his spirits to an unusual\par
height. He filled the goblet often, and gave a loose to merriment and\par
talk. The gaiety of Cavigni, on the contrary, was somewhat clouded by\par
anxiety. He kept a watchful eye upon Verezzi, whom, with the utmost\par
difficulty, he had hitherto restrained from exasperating Montoni\par
further against Morano, by a mention of his late taunting words.\par
\par
One of the company exultingly recurred to the event of the preceding\par
evening. Verezzi’s eyes sparkled. The mention of Morano led to that of\par
Emily, of whom they were all profuse in the praise, except Montoni, who\par
sat silent, and then interrupted the subject.\par
\par
When the servants had withdrawn, Montoni and his friends entered into\par
close conversation, which was sometimes checked by the irascible temper\par
of Verezzi, but in which Montoni displayed his conscious superiority,\par
by that decisive look and manner, which always accompanied the vigour\par
of his thought, and to which most of his companions submitted, as to a\par
power, that they had no right to question, though of each other’s\par
self-importance they were jealously scrupulous. Amidst this\par
conversation, one of them imprudently introduced again the name of\par
Morano; and Verezzi, now more heated by wine, disregarded the\par
expressive looks of Cavigni, and gave some dark hints of what had\par
passed on the preceding night. These, however, Montoni did not appear\par
to understand, for he continued silent in his chair, without\par
discovering any emotion, while, the choler of Verezzi increasing with\par
the apparent insensibility of Montoni, he at length told the suggestion\par
of Morano, that this castle did not lawfully belong to him, and that he\par
would not willingly leave another murder on his conscience.\par
\par
“Am I to be insulted at my own table, and by my own friends?” said\par
Montoni, with a countenance pale in anger. “Why are the words of that\par
madman repeated to me?” Verezzi, who had expected to hear Montoni’s\par
indignation poured forth against Morano, and answered by thanks to\par
himself, looked with astonishment at Cavigni, who enjoyed his\par
confusion. “Can you be weak enough to credit the assertions of a\par
madman?” rejoined Montoni, “or, what is the same thing, a man possessed\par
by the spirit of vengeance? But he has succeeded too well; you believe\par
what he said.”\par
\par
“Signor,” said Verezzi, “we believe only what we know.”—“How!”\par
interrupted Montoni, sternly: “produce your proof.”\par
\par
“We believe only what we know,” repeated Verezzi, “and we know nothing\par
of what Morano asserts.” Montoni seemed to recover himself. “I am\par
hasty, my friends,” said he, “with respect to my honour; no man shall\par
question it with impunity—you did not mean to question it. These\par
foolish words are not worth your remembrance, or my resentment.\par
Verezzi, here is to your first exploit.”\par
\par
“Success to your first exploit,” re-echoed the whole company.\par
\par
“Noble Signor,” replied Verezzi, glad to find he had escaped Montoni’s\par
resentment, “with my good will, you shall build your ramparts of gold.”\par
\par
“Pass the goblet,” cried Montoni. “We will drink to Signora St.\par
Aubert,” said Cavigni. “By your leave we will first drink to the lady\par
of the castle.” said Bertolini.—Montoni was silent. “To the lady of the\par
castle,” said his guests. He bowed his head.\par
\par
“It much surprises me, Signor,” said Bertolini, “that you have so long\par
neglected this castle; it is a noble edifice.”\par
\par
“It suits our purpose,” replied Montoni, “and _is_ a noble edifice. You\par
know not, it seems, by what mischance it came to me.”\par
\par
“It was a lucky mischance, be it what it may, Signor,” replied\par
Bertolini, smiling. “I would, that one so lucky had befallen me.”\par
\par
Montoni looked gravely at him. “If you will attend to what I say,” he\par
resumed, “you shall hear the story.”\par
\par
The countenances of Bertolini and Verezzi expressed something more than\par
curiosity; Cavigni, who seemed to feel none, had probably heard the\par
relation before.\par
\par
“It is now near twenty years,” said Montoni, “since this castle came\par
into my possession. I inherit it by the female line. The lady, my\par
predecessor, was only distantly related to me; I am the last of her\par
family. She was beautiful and rich; I wooed her; but her heart was\par
fixed upon another, and she rejected me. It is probable, however, that\par
she was herself rejected of the person, whoever he might be, on whom\par
she bestowed her favour, for a deep and settled melancholy took\par
possession of her; and I have reason to believe she put a period to her\par
own life. I was not at the castle at the time; but, as there are some\par
singular and mysterious circumstances attending that event, I shall\par
repeat them.”\par
\par
“Repeat them!” said a voice.\par
\par
Montoni was silent; the guests looked at each other, to know who spoke;\par
but they perceived, that each was making the same enquiry. Montoni, at\par
length, recovered himself. “We are overheard,” said he: “we will finish\par
this subject another time. Pass the goblet.”\par
\par
The cavaliers looked round the wide chamber.\par
\par
“Here is no person, but ourselves,” said Verezzi: “pray, Signor,\par
proceed.”\par
\par
“Did you hear anything?” said Montoni.\par
\par
“We did,” said Bertolini.\par
\par
“It could be only fancy,” said Verezzi, looking round again. “We see no\par
person besides ourselves; and the sound I thought I heard seemed within\par
the room. Pray, Signor, go on.”\par
\par
Montoni paused a moment, and then proceeded in a lowered voice, while\par
the cavaliers drew nearer to attend.\par
\par
“Ye are to know, Signors, that the Lady Laurentini had for some months\par
shown symptoms of a dejected mind, nay, of a disturbed imagination. Her\par
mood was very unequal; sometimes she was sunk in calm melancholy, and,\par
at others, as I have been told, she betrayed all the symptoms of\par
frantic madness. It was one night in the month of October, after she\par
had recovered from one of those fits of excess, and had sunk again into\par
her usual melancholy, that she retired alone to her chamber, and\par
forbade all interruption. It was the chamber at the end of the\par
corridor, Signors, where we had the affray, last night. From that hour,\par
she was seen no more.”\par
\par
“How! seen no more!” said Bertolini, “was not her body found in the\par
chamber?”\par
\par
“Were her remains never found?” cried the rest of the company all\par
together.\par
\par
“Never!” replied Montoni.\par
\par
“What reasons were there to suppose she destroyed herself, then?” said\par
Bertolini.—“Aye, what reasons?” said Verezzi.—“How happened it, that\par
her remains were never found? Although she killed herself, she could\par
not bury herself.” Montoni looked indignantly at Verezzi, who began to\par
apologise.\par
\par
“Your pardon, Signor,” said he: “I did not consider, that the lady was\par
your relative, when I spoke of her so lightly.”\par
\par
Montoni accepted the apology.\par
\par
“But the Signor will oblige us with the reasons, which urged him to\par
believe, that the lady committed suicide.”\par
\par
“Those I will explain hereafter,” said Montoni: “at present let me\par
relate a most extraordinary circumstance. This conversation goes no\par
further, Signors. Listen, then, to what I am going to say.”\par
\par
“Listen!” said a voice.\par
\par
They were all again silent, and the countenance of Montoni changed.\par
“This is no illusion of the fancy,” said Cavigni, at length breaking\par
the profound silence.—“No,” said Bertolini; “I heard it myself, now.\par
Yet here is no person in the room but ourselves!”\par
\par
“This is very extraordinary,” said Montoni, suddenly rising. “This is\par
not to be borne; here is some deception, some trick. I will know what\par
it means.”\par
\par
All the company rose from their chairs in confusion.\par
\par
“It is very odd!” said Bertolini. “Here is really no stranger in the\par
room. If it is a trick, Signor, you will do well to punish the author\par
of it severely.”\par
\par
“A trick! what else can it be?” said Cavigni, affecting a laugh.\par
\par
The servants were now summoned, and the chamber was searched, but no\par
person was found. The surprise and consternation of the company\par
increased. Montoni was discomposed. “We will leave this room,” said he,\par
“and the subject of our conversation also; it is too solemn.” His\par
guests were equally ready to quit the apartment; but the subject had\par
roused their curiosity, and they entreated Montoni to withdraw to\par
another chamber, and finish it; no entreaties could, however, prevail\par
with him. Notwithstanding his efforts to appear at ease, he was visibly\par
and greatly disordered.\par
\par
“Why, Signor, you are not superstitious,” cried Verezzi, jeeringly;\par
“you, who have so often laughed at the credulity of others!”\par
\par
“I am not superstitious,” replied Montoni, regarding him with stern\par
displeasure, “though I know how to despise the common-place sentences,\par
which are frequently uttered against superstition. I will enquire\par
further into this affair.” He then left the room; and his guests,\par
separating for the night, retired to their respective apartments.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER VIII\par
\par
He wears the rose of youth upon his cheek.\par
                    SHAKESPEARE\par
\par
\par
We now return to Valancourt, who, it may be remembered, remained at\par
Thoulouse, some time after the departure of Emily, restless and\par
miserable. Each morrow that approached, he designed should carry him\par
from thence; yet tomorrow and tomorrow came, and still saw him\par
lingering in the scene of his former happiness. He could not\par
immediately tear himself from the spot, where he had been accustomed to\par
converse with Emily, or from the objects they had viewed together,\par
which appeared to him memorials of her affection, as well as a kind of\par
surety for its faithfulness; and, next to the pain of bidding her\par
adieu, was that of leaving the scenes which so powerfully awakened her\par
image. Sometimes he had bribed a servant, who had been left in the care\par
of Madame Montoni’s château, to permit him to visit the gardens, and\par
there he would wander, for hours together, rapt in a melancholy, not\par
unpleasing. The terrace, and the pavilion at the end of it, where he\par
had taken leave of Emily, on the eve of her departure from Thoulouse,\par
were his most favourite haunts. There, as he walked, or leaned from the\par
window of the building, he would endeavour to recollect all she had\par
said, on that night; to catch the tones of her voice, as they faintly\par
vibrated on his memory, and to remember the exact expression of her\par
countenance, which sometimes came suddenly to his fancy, like a vision;\par
that beautiful countenance, which awakened, as by instantaneous magic,\par
all the tenderness of his heart, and seemed to tell with irresistible\par
eloquence—that he had lost her for ever! At these moments, his hurried\par
steps would have discovered to a spectator the despair of his heart.\par
The character of Montoni, such as he had received from hints, and such\par
as his fears represented it, would rise to his view, together with all\par
the dangers it seemed to threaten to Emily and to his love. He blamed\par
himself, that he had not urged these more forcibly to her, while it\par
might have been in his power to detain her, and that he had suffered an\par
absurd and criminal delicacy, as he termed it, to conquer so soon the\par
reasonable arguments he had opposed to this journey. Any evil, that\par
might have attended their marriage, seemed so inferior to those, which\par
now threatened their love, or even to the sufferings, that absence\par
occasioned, that he wondered how he could have ceased to urge his suit,\par
till he had convinced her of its propriety; and he would certainly now\par
have followed her to Italy, if he could have been spared from his\par
regiment for so long a journey. His regiment, indeed, soon reminded\par
him, that he had other duties to attend, than those of love.\par
\par
A short time after his arrival at his brother’s house, he was summoned\par
to join his brother officers, and he accompanied a battalion to Paris;\par
where a scene of novelty and gaiety opened upon him, such as, till\par
then, he had only a faint idea of. But gaiety disgusted, and company\par
fatigued, his sick mind; and he became an object of unceasing raillery\par
to his companions, from whom, whenever he could steal an opportunity,\par
he escaped, to think of Emily. The scenes around him, however, and the\par
company with whom he was obliged to mingle, engaged his attention,\par
though they failed to amuse his fancy, and thus gradually weakened the\par
habit of yielding to lamentation, till it appeared less a duty to his\par
love to indulge it. Among his brother-officers were many, who added to\par
the ordinary character of a French soldier’s gaiety some of those\par
fascinating qualities, which too frequently throw a veil over folly,\par
and sometimes even soften the features of vice into smiles. To these\par
men the reserved and thoughtful manners of Valancourt were a kind of\par
tacit censure on their own, for which they rallied him when present,\par
and plotted against him when absent; they gloried in the thought of\par
reducing him to their own level, and, considering it to be a spirited\par
frolic, determined to accomplish it.\par
\par
Valancourt was a stranger to the gradual progress of scheme and\par
intrigue, against which he could not be on his guard. He had not been\par
accustomed to receive ridicule, and he could ill endure its sting; he\par
resented it, and this only drew upon him a louder laugh. To escape from\par
such scenes, he fled into solitude, and there the image of Emily met\par
him, and revived the pangs of love and despair. He then sought to renew\par
those tasteful studies, which had been the delight of his early years;\par
but his mind had lost the tranquillity, which is necessary for their\par
enjoyment. To forget himself and the grief and anxiety, which the idea\par
of her recalled, he would quit his solitude, and again mingle in the\par
crowd—glad of a temporary relief, and rejoicing to snatch amusement for\par
the moment.\par
\par
Thus passed weeks after weeks, time gradually softening his sorrow, and\par
habit strengthening his desire of amusement, till the scenes around him\par
seemed to awaken into a new character, and Valancourt, to have fallen\par
among them from the clouds.\par
\par
His figure and address made him a welcome visitor, wherever he had been\par
introduced, and he soon frequented the most gay and fashionable circles\par
of Paris. Among these, was the assembly of the Countess Lacleur, a\par
woman of eminent beauty and captivating manners. She had passed the\par
spring of youth, but her wit prolonged the triumph of its reign, and\par
they mutually assisted the fame of each other; for those, who were\par
charmed by her loveliness, spoke with enthusiasm of her talents; and\par
others, who admired her playful imagination, declared, that her\par
personal graces were unrivalled. But her imagination was merely\par
playful, and her wit, if such it could be called, was brilliant, rather\par
than just; it dazzled, and its fallacy escaped the detection of the\par
moment; for the accents, in which she pronounced it, and the smile,\par
that accompanied them, were a spell upon the judgment of the auditors.\par
Her _petits soupers_ were the most tasteful of any in Paris, and were\par
frequented by many of the second class of literati. She was fond of\par
music, was herself a scientific performer, and had frequently concerts\par
at her house. Valancourt, who passionately loved music, and who\par
sometimes assisted at these concerts, admired her execution, but\par
remembered with a sigh the eloquent simplicity of Emily’s songs and the\par
natural expression of her manner, which waited not to be approved by\par
the judgment, but found their way at once to the heart.\par
\par
Madame _La Comtesse_ had often deep play at her house, which she\par
affected to restrain, but secretly encouraged; and it was well known\par
among her friends, that the splendour of her establishment was chiefly\par
supplied from the profits of her tables. But her _petits soupers_ were\par
the most charming imaginable! Here were all the delicacies of the four\par
quarters of the world, all the wit and the lighter efforts of genius,\par
all the graces of conversation—the smiles of beauty, and the charm of\par
music; and Valancourt passed his pleasantest, as well as most dangerous\par
hours in these parties.\par
\par
His brother, who remained with his family in Gascony, had contented\par
himself with giving him letters of introduction to such of his\par
relations, residing at Paris, as the latter was not already known to.\par
All these were persons of some distinction; and, as neither the person,\par
mind, nor manners of Valancourt the younger threatened to disgrace\par
their alliance, they received him with as much kindness as their\par
nature, hardened by uninterrupted prosperity, would admit of; but their\par
attentions did not extend to acts of real friendship; for they were too\par
much occupied by their own pursuits, to feel any interest in his; and\par
thus he was set down in the midst of Paris, in the pride of youth, with\par
an open, unsuspicious temper and ardent affections, without one friend,\par
to warn him of the dangers, to which he was exposed. Emily, who, had\par
she been present, would have saved him from these evils by awakening\par
his heart, and engaging him in worthy pursuits, now only increased his\par
danger;—it was to lose the grief, which the remembrance of her\par
occasioned, that he first sought amusement; and for this end he pursued\par
it, till habit made it an object of abstract interest.\par
\par
There was also a Marchioness Champfort, a young widow, at whose\par
assemblies he passed much of his time. She was handsome, still more\par
artful, gay and fond of intrigue. The society, which she drew round\par
her, was less elegant and more vicious, than that of the Countess\par
Lacleur: but, as she had address enough to throw a veil, though but a\par
slight one, over the worst part of her character, she was still visited\par
by many persons of what is called distinction. Valancourt was\par
introduced to her parties by two of his brother officers, whose late\par
ridicule he had now forgiven so far, that he could sometimes join in\par
the laugh, which a mention of his former manners would renew.\par
\par
The gaiety of the most splendid court in Europe, the magnificence of\par
the palaces, entertainments, and equipages, that surrounded him—all\par
conspired to dazzle his imagination, and reanimate his spirits, and the\par
example and maxims of his military associates to delude his mind.\par
Emily’s image, indeed, still lived there; but it was no longer the\par
friend, the monitor, that saved him from himself, and to which he\par
retired to weep the sweet, yet melancholy, tears of tenderness. When he\par
had recourse to it, it assumed a countenance of mild reproach, that\par
wrung his soul, and called forth tears of unmixed misery; his only\par
escape from which was to forget the object of it, and he endeavoured,\par
therefore, to think of Emily as seldom as he could.\par
\par
Thus dangerously circumstanced was Valancourt, at the time, when Emily\par
was suffering at Venice, from the persecuting addresses of Count\par
Morano, and the unjust authority of Montoni; at which period we leave\par
him.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER IX\par
\par
The image of a wicked, heinous fault\par
Lives in his eye; that close aspect of his\par
Does show the mood of a much-troubled breast.\par
                    KING JOHN\par
\par
\par
Leaving the gay scenes of Paris, we return to those of the gloomy\par
Apennine, where Emily’s thoughts were still faithful to Valancourt.\par
Looking to him as to her only hope, she recollected, with jealous\par
exactness, every assurance and every proof she had witnessed of his\par
affection; read again and again the letters she had received from him;\par
weighed, with intense anxiety, the force of every word, that spoke of\par
his attachment; and dried her tears, as she trusted in his truth.\par
\par
Montoni, meanwhile, had made strict enquiry concerning the strange\par
circumstance of his alarm, without obtaining information; and was, at\par
length, obliged to account for it by the reasonable supposition, that\par
it was a mischievous trick played off by one of his domestics. His\par
disagreements with Madame Montoni, on the subject of her settlements,\par
were now more frequent than ever; he even confined her entirely to her\par
own apartment, and did not scruple to threaten her with much greater\par
severity, should she persevere in a refusal.\par
\par
Reason, had she consulted it, would now have perplexed her in the\par
choice of a conduct to be adopted. It would have pointed out the danger\par
of irritating by further opposition a man, such as Montoni had proved\par
himself to be, and to whose power she had so entirely committed\par
herself; and it would also have told her, of what extreme importance to\par
her future comfort it was, to reserve for herself those possessions,\par
which would enable her to live independently of Montoni, should she\par
ever escape from his immediate control. But she was directed by a more\par
decisive guide than reason—the spirit of revenge, which urged her to\par
oppose violence to violence, and obstinacy to obstinacy.\par
\par
Wholly confined to the solitude of her apartment, she was now reduced\par
to solicit the society she had lately rejected; for Emily was the only\par
person, except Annette, with whom she was permitted to converse.\par
\par
Generously anxious for her peace, Emily, therefore, tried to persuade,\par
when she could not convince, and sought by every gentle means to induce\par
her to forbear that asperity of reply, which so greatly irritated\par
Montoni. The pride of her aunt did sometimes soften to the soothing\par
voice of Emily, and there even were moments, when she regarded her\par
affectionate attentions with goodwill.\par
\par
The scenes of terrible contention, to which Emily was frequently\par
compelled to be witness, exhausted her spirits more than any\par
circumstances, that had occurred since her departure from Thoulouse.\par
The gentleness and goodness of her parents, together with the scenes of\par
her early happiness, often stole on her mind, like the visions of a\par
higher world; while the characters and circumstances, now passing\par
beneath her eye, excited both terror and surprise. She could scarcely\par
have imagined, that passions so fierce and so various, as those which\par
Montoni exhibited, could have been concentrated in one individual; yet\par
what more surprised her, was, that, on great occasions, he could bend\par
these passions, wild as they were, to the cause of his interest, and\par
generally could disguise in his countenance their operation on his\par
mind; but she had seen him too often, when he had thought it\par
unnecessary to conceal his nature, to be deceived on such occasions.\par
\par
Her present life appeared like the dream of a distempered imagination,\par
or like one of those frightful fictions, in which the wild genius of\par
the poets sometimes delighted. Reflection brought only regret, and\par
anticipation terror. How often did she wish to “steal the lark’s wing,\par
and mount the swiftest gale,” that Languedoc and repose might once more\par
be hers!\par
\par
Of Count Morano’s health she made frequent enquiry; but Annette heard\par
only vague reports of his danger, and that his surgeon had said he\par
would never leave the cottage alive; while Emily could not but be\par
shocked to think, that she, however innocently, might be the means of\par
his death; and Annette, who did not fail to observe her emotion,\par
interpreted it in her own way.\par
\par
But a circumstance soon occurred, which entirely withdrew Annette’s\par
attention from this subject, and awakened the surprise and curiosity so\par
natural to her. Coming one day to Emily’s apartment, with a countenance\par
full of importance, “What can all this mean, ma’amselle?” said she.\par
“Would I was once safe in Languedoc again, they should never catch me\par
going on my travels any more! I must think it a fine thing, truly, to\par
come abroad, and see foreign parts! I little thought I was coming to be\par
catched up in an old castle, among such dreary mountains, with the\par
chance of being murdered, or, what is as good, having my throat cut!”\par
\par
“What can all this mean, indeed, Annette?” said Emily, in astonishment.\par
\par
“Aye, ma’amselle, you may look surprised; but you won’t believe it,\par
perhaps, till they have murdered you, too. You would not believe about\par
the ghost I told you of, though I showed you the very place, where it\par
used to appear!—You will believe nothing, ma’amselle.”\par
\par
“Not till you speak more reasonably, Annette; for Heaven’s sake,\par
explain your meaning. You spoke of murder!”\par
\par
“Aye, ma’amselle, they are coming to murder us all, perhaps; but what\par
signifies explaining?—you will not believe.”\par
\par
Emily again desired her to relate what she had seen, or heard.\par
\par
“O, I have seen enough, ma’am, and heard too much, as Ludovico can\par
prove. Poor soul! they will murder him, too! I little thought, when he\par
sung those sweet verses under my lattice, at Venice!”—Emily looked\par
impatient and displeased. “Well, ma’amselle, as I was saying, these\par
preparations about the castle, and these strange-looking people, that\par
are calling here every day, and the Signor’s cruel usage of my lady,\par
and his odd goings-on—all these, as I told Ludovico, can bode no good.\par
And he bid me hold my tongue. So, says I, the Signor’s strangely\par
altered, Ludovico, in this gloomy castle, to what he was in France;\par
there, all so gay! Nobody so gallant to my lady, then; and he could\par
smile, too, upon a poor servant, sometimes, and jeer her, too,\par
good-naturedly enough. I remember once, when he said to me, as I was\par
going out of my lady’s dressing-room—Annette, says he—”\par
\par
“Never mind what the Signor said,” interrupted Emily; “but tell me, at\par
once, the circumstance, which has thus alarmed you.”\par
\par
“Aye, ma’amselle,” rejoined Annette, “that is just what Ludovico said:\par
says he, Never mind what the Signor says to you. So I told him what I\par
thought about the Signor. He is so strangely altered, said I: for now\par
he is so haughty, and so commanding, and so sharp with my lady; and, if\par
he meets one, he’ll scarcely look at one, unless it be to frown. So\par
much the better, says Ludovico, so much the better. And to tell you the\par
truth, ma’amselle, I thought this was a very ill-natured speech of\par
Ludovico: but I went on. And then, says I, he is always knitting his\par
brows; and if one speaks to him, he does not hear; and then he sits up\par
counselling so, of a night, with the other Signors—there they are, till\par
long past midnight, discoursing together! Aye, but says Ludovico, you\par
don’t know what they are counselling about. No, said I, but I can\par
guess—it is about my young lady. Upon that, Ludovico burst out\par
a-laughing, quite loud; so he put me in a huff, for I did not like that\par
either I or you, ma’amselle, should be laughed at; and I turned away\par
quick, but he stopped me. ‘Don’t be affronted, Annette,’ said he, ‘but\par
I cannot help laughing;’ and with that he laughed again. ‘What!’ says\par
he, ‘do you think the Signors sit up, night after night, only to\par
counsel about thy young lady! No, no, there is something more in the\par
wind than that. And these repairs about the castle, and these\par
preparations about the ramparts—they are not making about young\par
ladies.’ Why, surely, said I, the Signor, my master, is not going to\par
make war? ‘Make war!’ said Ludovico, ‘what, upon the mountains and the\par
woods? for here is no living soul to make war upon that I see.’\par
\par
‘What are these preparations for, then?’ said I; why surely nobody is\par
coming to take away my master’s castle! ‘Then there are so many\par
ill-looking fellows coming to the castle every day,’ says Ludovico,\par
without answering my question, ‘and the Signor sees them all, and talks\par
with them all, and they all stay in the neighbourhood! By holy St.\par
Marco! some of them are the most cut-throat-looking dogs I ever set my\par
eyes upon.’\par
\par
“I asked Ludovico again, if he thought they were coming to take away my\par
master’s castle; and he said, No, he did not think they were, but he\par
did not know for certain. ‘Then yesterday,’ said he, but you must not\par
tell this, ma’amselle, ‘yesterday, a party of these men came, and left\par
all their horses in the castle stables, where, it seems, they are to\par
stay, for the Signor ordered them all to be entertained with the best\par
provender in the manger; but the men are, most of them, in the\par
neighbouring cottages.’\par
\par
“So, ma’amselle, I came to tell you all this, for I never heard\par
anything so strange in my life. But what can these ill-looking men be\par
come about, if it is not to murder us? And the Signor knows this, or\par
why should he be so civil to them? And why should he fortify the\par
castle, and counsel so much with the other Signors, and be so\par
thoughtful?”\par
\par
“Is this all you have to tell, Annette?” said Emily. “Have you heard\par
nothing else, that alarms you?”\par
\par
“Nothing else, ma’amselle!” said Annette; “why, is not this enough?”\par
“Quite enough for my patience, Annette, but not quite enough to\par
convince me we are all to be murdered, though I acknowledge here is\par
sufficient food for curiosity.” She forbore to speak her apprehensions,\par
because she would not encourage Annette’s wild terrors; but the present\par
circumstances of the castle both surprised, and alarmed her. Annette,\par
having told her tale, left the chamber, on the wing for new wonders.\par
\par
In the evening, Emily had passed some melancholy hours with Madame\par
Montoni, and was retiring to rest, when she was alarmed by a strange\par
and loud knocking at her chamber door, and then a heavy weight fell\par
against it, that almost burst it open. She called to know who was\par
there, and receiving no answer, repeated the call; but a chilling\par
silence followed. It occurred to her—for, at this moment, she could not\par
reason on the probability of circumstances—that some one of the\par
strangers, lately arrived at the castle, had discovered her apartment,\par
and was come with such intent, as their looks rendered too possible—to\par
rob, perhaps to murder, her. The moment she admitted this possibility,\par
terror supplied the place of conviction, and a kind of instinctive\par
remembrance of her remote situation from the family heightened it to a\par
degree, that almost overcame her senses. She looked at the door, which\par
led to the staircase, expecting to see it open, and listening, in\par
fearful silence, for a return of the noise, till she began to think it\par
had proceeded from this door, and a wish of escaping through the\par
opposite one rushed upon her mind. She went to the gallery door, and\par
then, fearing to open it, lest some person might be silently lurking\par
for her without, she stopped, but with her eyes fixed in expectation\par
upon the opposite door of the staircase. As thus she stood, she heard a\par
faint breathing near her, and became convinced, that some person was on\par
the other side of the door, which was already locked. She sought for\par
other fastening, but there was none.\par
\par
While she yet listened, the breathing was distinctly heard, and her\par
terror was not soothed, when, looking round her wide and lonely\par
chamber, she again considered her remote situation. As she stood\par
hesitating whether to call for assistance, the continuance of the\par
stillness surprised her; and her spirits would have revived, had she\par
not continued to hear the faint breathing, that convinced her, the\par
person, whoever it was, had not quitted the door.\par
\par
At length, worn out with anxiety, she determined to call loudly for\par
assistance from her casement, and was advancing to it, when, whether\par
the terror of her mind gave her ideal sounds, or that real ones did\par
come, she thought footsteps were ascending the private staircase; and,\par
expecting to see its door unclose, she forgot all other cause of alarm,\par
and retreated towards the corridor. Here she endeavoured to make her\par
escape, but, on opening the door, was very near falling over a person,\par
who lay on the floor without. She screamed, and would have passed, but\par
her trembling frame refused to support her; and the moment, in which\par
she leaned against the wall of the gallery, allowed her leisure to\par
observe the figure before her, and to recognise the features of\par
Annette. Fear instantly yielded to surprise. She spoke in vain to the\par
poor girl, who remained senseless on the floor, and then, losing all\par
consciousness of her own weakness, hurried to her assistance.\par
\par
When Annette recovered, she was helped by Emily into the chamber, but\par
was still unable to speak, and looked round her, as if her eyes\par
followed some person in the room. Emily tried to sooth her disturbed\par
spirits, and forbore, at present, to ask her any questions; but the\par
faculty of speech was never long withheld from Annette, and she\par
explained, in broken sentences, and in her tedious way, the occasion of\par
her disorder. She affirmed, and with a solemnity of conviction, that\par
almost staggered the incredulity of Emily, that she had seen an\par
apparition, as she was passing to her bedroom, through the corridor.\par
\par
“I had heard strange stories of that chamber before,” said Annette:\par
“but as it was so near yours, ma’amselle, I would not tell them to you,\par
because they would frighten you. The servants had told me, often and\par
often, that it was haunted, and that was the reason why it was shut up:\par
nay, for that matter, why the whole string of these rooms, here, are\par
shut up. I quaked whenever I went by, and I must say, I did sometimes\par
think I heard odd noises within it. But, as I said, as I was passing\par
along the corridor, and not thinking a word about the matter, or even\par
of the strange voice that the Signors heard the other night, all of a\par
sudden comes a great light, and, looking behind me, there was a tall\par
figure, (I saw it as plainly, ma’amselle, as I see you at this moment),\par
a tall figure gliding along (Oh! I cannot describe how!) into the room,\par
that is always shut up, and nobody has the key of it but the Signor,\par
and the door shut directly.”\par
\par
“Then it doubtless was the Signor,” said Emily.\par
\par
“O no, ma’amselle, it could not be him, for I left him busy\par
a-quarrelling in my lady’s dressing-room!”\par
\par
“You bring me strange tales, Annette,” said Emily: “it was but this\par
morning, that you would have terrified me with the apprehension of\par
murder; and now you would persuade me, you have seen a ghost! These\par
wonderful stories come too quickly.”\par
\par
“Nay, ma’amselle, I will say no more, only, if I had not been\par
frightened, I should not have fainted dead away so. I ran as fast as I\par
could, to get to your door; but, what was worst of all, I could not\par
call out; then I thought something must be strangely the matter with\par
me, and directly I dropt down.”\par
\par
“Was it the chamber where the black veil hangs?” said Emily. “O! no,\par
ma’amselle, it was one nearer to this. What shall I do, to get to my\par
room? I would not go out into the corridor again, for the whole world!”\par
Emily, whose spirits had been severely shocked, and who, therefore, did\par
not like the thought of passing the night alone, told her she might\par
sleep where she was. “O, no, ma’amselle,” replied Annette, “I would not\par
sleep in the room now for a thousand sequins!”\par
\par
Wearied and disappointed, Emily first ridiculed, though she shared, her\par
fears, and then tried to sooth them; but neither attempt succeeded, and\par
the girl persisted in believing and affirming, that what she had seen\par
was nothing human. It was not till some time after Emily had recovered\par
her composure, that she recollected the steps she had heard on the\par
staircase—a remembrance, however, which made her insist that Annette\par
should pass the night with her, and, with much difficulty, she, at\par
length, prevailed, assisted by that part of the girl’s fear, which\par
concerned the corridor.\par
\par
Early on the following morning, as Emily crossed the hall to the\par
ramparts, she heard a noisy bustle in the courtyard, and the clatter of\par
horses’ hoofs. Such unusual sounds excited her curiosity; and, instead\par
of going to the ramparts, she went to an upper casement, from whence\par
she saw, in the court below, a large party of horsemen, dressed in a\par
singular, but uniform, habit, and completely, though variously, armed.\par
They wore a kind of short jacket, composed of black and scarlet, and\par
several of them had a cloak, of plain black, which, covering the person\par
entirely, hung down to the stirrups. As one of these cloaks glanced\par
aside, she saw, beneath, daggers, apparently of different sizes, tucked\par
into the horseman’s belt. She further observed, that these were\par
carried, in the same manner, by many of the horsemen without cloaks,\par
most of whom bore also pikes, or javelins. On their heads, were the\par
small Italian caps, some of which were distinguished by black feathers.\par
Whether these caps gave a fierce air to the countenance, or that the\par
countenances they surmounted had naturally such an appearance, Emily\par
thought she had never, till then, seen an assemblage of faces so savage\par
and terrific. While she gazed, she almost fancied herself surrounded by\par
banditti; and a vague thought glanced athwart her fancy—that Montoni\par
was the captain of the group before her, and that this castle was to be\par
the place of rendezvous. The strange and horrible supposition was but\par
momentary, though her reason could supply none more probable, and\par
though she discovered, among the band, the strangers she had formerly\par
noticed with so much alarm, who were now distinguished by the black\par
plume.\par
\par
While she continued gazing, Cavigni, Verezzi, and Bertolini came forth\par
from the hall, habited like the rest, except that they wore hats, with\par
a mixed plume of black and scarlet, and that their arms differed from\par
those of the rest of the party. As they mounted their horses, Emily was\par
struck with the exulting joy, expressed on the visage of Verezzi, while\par
Cavigni was gay, yet with a shade of thought on his countenance; and,\par
as he managed his horse with dexterity, his graceful and commanding\par
figure, which exhibited the majesty of a hero, had never appeared to\par
more advantage. Emily, as she observed him, thought he somewhat\par
resembled Valancourt, in the spirit and dignity of his person; but she\par
looked in vain for the noble, benevolent countenance—the soul’s\par
intelligence, which overspread the features of the latter.\par
\par
As she was hoping, she scarcely knew why, that Montoni would accompany\par
the party, he appeared at the hall door, but un-accoutred. Having\par
carefully observed the horsemen, conversed awhile with the cavaliers,\par
and bidden them farewell, the band wheeled round the court, and, led by\par
Verezzi, issued forth under the portcullis; Montoni following to the\par
portal, and gazing after them for some time. Emily then retired from\par
the casement, and, now certain of being unmolested, went to walk on the\par
ramparts, from whence she soon after saw the party winding among the\par
mountains to the west, appearing and disappearing between the woods,\par
till distance confused their figures, consolidated their numbers, and\par
only a dingy mass appeared moving along the heights.\par
\par
Emily observed, that no workmen were on the ramparts, and that the\par
repairs of the fortifications seemed to be completed. While she\par
sauntered thoughtfully on, she heard distant footsteps, and, raising\par
her eyes, saw several men lurking under the castle walls, who were\par
evidently not workmen, but looked as if they would have accorded well\par
with the party which was gone. Wondering where Annette had hid herself\par
so long, who might have explained some of the late circumstances, and\par
then considering that Madame Montoni was probably risen, she went to\par
her dressing-room, where she mentioned what had occurred; but Madame\par
Montoni either would not, or could not, give any explanation of the\par
event. The Signor’s reserve to his wife, on this subject, was probably\par
nothing more than usual; yet, to Emily, it gave an air of mystery to\par
the whole affair, that seemed to hint there was danger, if not villany,\par
in his schemes.\par
\par
Annette presently came, and, as usual, was full of alarm; to her lady’s\par
eager enquiries of what she had heard among the servants, she replied:\par
\par
“Ah, madam! nobody knows what it is all about, but old Carlo; he knows\par
well enough, I dare say, but he is as close as his master. Some say the\par
Signor is going out to frighten the enemy, as they call it: but where\par
is the enemy? Then others say, he is going to take away somebody’s\par
castle: but I am sure he has room enough in his own, without taking\par
other people’s; and I am sure I should like it a great deal better, if\par
there were more people to fill it.”\par
\par
“Ah! you will soon have your wish, I fear,” replied Madame Montoni.\par
\par
“No, madam, but such ill-looking fellows are not worth having. I mean\par
such gallant, smart, merry fellows as Ludovico, who is always telling\par
droll stories, to make one laugh. It was but yesterday, he told me such\par
a _humoursome_ tale! I can’t help laughing at it now.—Says he—”\par
\par
“Well, we can dispense with the story,” said her lady.\par
\par
“Ah!” continued Annette, “he sees a great way further than other\par
people! Now he sees into all the Signor’s meaning, without knowing a\par
word about the matter!”\par
\par
“How is that?” said Madame Montoni.\par
\par
“Why he says—but he made me promise not to tell, and I would not\par
disoblige him for the world.”\par
\par
“What is it he made you promise not to tell?” said her lady, sternly.\par
“I insist upon knowing immediately—what is it he made you promise?”\par
\par
“O madam,” cried Annette, “I would not tell for the universe!”\par
\par
“I insist upon your telling this instant,” said Madame Montoni.\par
\par
“O dear madam! I would not tell for a hundred sequins! You would not\par
have me forswear myself madam!” exclaimed Annette.\par
\par
“I will not wait another moment,” said Madame Montoni. Annette was\par
silent.\par
\par
“The Signor shall be informed of this directly,” rejoined her mistress;\par
“he will make you discover all.”\par
\par
“It is Ludovico, who has discovered,” said Annette: “but for mercy’s\par
sake, madam, don’t tell the Signor, and you shall know all directly.”\par
Madame Montoni said, that she would not.\par
\par
“Well then, madam, Ludovico says, that the Signor, my master,\par
is—is—that is, he only thinks so, and anybody, you know, madam, is free\par
to think—that the Signor, my master, is—is—”\par
\par
“Is what?” said her lady, impatiently.\par
\par
“That the Signor, my master, is going to be—a great robber—that is—he\par
is going to rob on his own account;—to be, (but I am sure I don’t\par
understand what he means) to be a—captain of—robbers.”\par
\par
“Art thou in thy senses, Annette?” said Madame Montoni; “or is this a\par
trick to deceive me? Tell me, this instant, what Ludovico _did_ say to\par
thee;—no equivocation;—this instant.”\par
\par
“Nay, madam,” cried Annette, “if this is all I am to get for having\par
told the secret—” Her mistress thus continued to insist, and Annette to\par
protest, till Montoni, himself, appeared, who bade the latter leave the\par
room, and she withdrew, trembling for the fate of her story. Emily also\par
was retiring, but her aunt desired she would stay; and Montoni had so\par
often made her a witness of their contention, that he no longer had\par
scruples on that account.\par
\par
“I insist upon knowing this instant, Signor, what all this means:” said\par
his wife—“what are all these armed men, whom they tell me of, gone out\par
about?” Montoni answered her only with a look of scorn; and Emily\par
whispered something to her. “It does not signify,” said her aunt: “I\par
will know; and I will know, too, what the castle has been fortified\par
for.”\par
\par
“Come, come,” said Montoni, “other business brought me here. I must be\par
trifled with no longer. I have immediate occasion for what I\par
demand—those estates must be given up, without further contention; or I\par
may find a way—”\par
\par
“They never shall be given up,” interrupted Madame Montoni: “they never\par
shall enable you to carry on your wild schemes;—but what are these? I\par
will know. Do you expect the castle to be attacked? Do you expect\par
enemies? Am I to be shut up here, to be killed in a siege?”\par
\par
“Sign the writings,” said Montoni, “and you shall know more.”\par
\par
“What enemy can be coming?” continued his wife. “Have you entered into\par
the service of the state? Am I to be blocked up here to die?”\par
\par
“That may possibly happen,” said Montoni, “unless you yield to my\par
demand: for, come what may, you shall not quit the castle till then.”\par
Madame Montoni burst into loud lamentation, which she as suddenly\par
checked, considering, that her husband’s assertions might be only\par
artifices, employed to extort her consent. She hinted this suspicion,\par
and, in the next moment, told him also, that his designs were not so\par
honourable as to serve the state, and that she believed he had only\par
commenced a captain of banditti, to join the enemies of Venice, in\par
plundering and laying waste the surrounding country.\par
\par
Montoni looked at her for a moment with a steady and stern countenance;\par
while Emily trembled, and his wife, for once, thought she had said too\par
much. “You shall be removed, this night,” said he, “to the east turret:\par
there, perhaps, you may understand the danger of offending a man, who\par
has an unlimited power over you.”\par
\par
Emily now fell at his feet, and, with tears of terror, supplicated for\par
her aunt, who sat, trembling with fear, and indignation; now ready to\par
pour forth execrations, and now to join the intercessions of Emily.\par
Montoni, however, soon interrupted these entreaties with a horrible\par
oath; and, as he burst from Emily, leaving his cloak, in her hand, she\par
fell to the floor, with a force, that occasioned her a severe blow on\par
the forehead. But he quitted the room, without attempting to raise her,\par
whose attention was called from herself, by a deep groan from Madame\par
Montoni, who continued otherwise unmoved in her chair, and had not\par
fainted. Emily, hastening to her assistance, saw her eyes rolling, and\par
her features convulsed.\par
\par
Having spoken to her, without receiving an answer, she brought water,\par
and supported her head, while she held it to her lips; but the\par
increasing convulsions soon compelled Emily to call for assistance. On\par
her way through the hall, in search of Annette, she met Montoni, whom\par
she told what had happened, and conjured to return and comfort her\par
aunt; but he turned silently away, with a look of indifference, and\par
went out upon the ramparts. At length she found old Carlo and Annette,\par
and they hastened to the dressing-room, where Madame Montoni had fallen\par
on the floor, and was lying in strong convulsions. Having lifted her\par
into the adjoining room, and laid her on the bed, the force of her\par
disorder still made all their strength necessary to hold her, while\par
Annette trembled and sobbed, and old Carlo looked silently and\par
piteously on, as his feeble hands grasped those of his mistress, till,\par
turning his eyes upon Emily, he exclaimed, “Good God! Signora, what is\par
the matter?”\par
\par
Emily looked calmly at him, and saw his enquiring eyes fixed on her:\par
and Annette, looking up, screamed loudly; for Emily’s face was stained\par
with blood, which continued to fall slowly from her forehead: but her\par
attention had been so entirely occupied by the scene before her, that\par
she had felt no pain from the wound. She now held a handkerchief to her\par
face, and, notwithstanding her faintness, continued to watch Madame\par
Montoni, the violence of whose convulsions was abating, till at length\par
they ceased, and left her in a kind of stupor.\par
\par
“My aunt must remain quiet,” said Emily. “Go, good Carlo; if we should\par
want your assistance, I will send for you. In the mean time, if you\par
have an opportunity, speak kindly of your mistress to your master.”\par
\par
“Alas!” said Carlo, “I have seen too much! I have little influence with\par
the Signor. But do, dear young lady, take some care of yourself; that\par
is an ugly wound, and you look sadly.”\par
\par
“Thank you, my friend, for your consideration,” said Emily, smiling\par
kindly: “the wound is trifling, it came by a fall.”\par
\par
Carlo shook his head, and left the room; and Emily, with Annette,\par
continued to watch by her aunt. “Did my lady tell the Signor what\par
Ludovico said, ma’amselle?” asked Annette in a whisper; but Emily\par
quieted her fears on the subject.\par
\par
“I thought what this quarrelling would come to,” continued Annette: “I\par
suppose the Signor has been beating my lady.”\par
\par
“No, no, Annette, you are totally mistaken, nothing extraordinary has\par
happened.”\par
\par
“Why, extraordinary things happen here so often, ma’amselle, that there\par
is nothing in them. Here is another legion of those ill-looking\par
fellows, come to the castle, this morning.”\par
\par
“Hush! Annette, you will disturb my aunt; we will talk of that by and\par
bye.”\par
\par
They continued watching silently, till Madame Montoni uttered a low\par
sigh, when Emily took her hand, and spoke soothingly to her; but the\par
former gazed with unconscious eyes, and it was long before she knew her\par
niece. Her first words then enquired for Montoni; to which Emily\par
replied by an entreaty, that she would compose her spirits, and consent\par
to be kept quiet, adding, that, if she wished any message to be\par
conveyed to him, she would herself deliver it. “No,” said her aunt\par
faintly, “no—I have nothing new to tell him. Does he persist in saying\par
I shall be removed from my chamber?”\par
\par
Emily replied that he had not spoken on the subject since Madame\par
Montoni heard him; and then she tried to divert her attention to some\par
other topic; but her aunt seemed to be inattentive to what she said,\par
and lost in secret thoughts. Emily, having brought her some\par
refreshment, now left her to the care of Annette, and went in search of\par
Montoni, whom she found on a remote part of the rampart, conversing\par
among a group of the men described by Annette. They stood round him\par
with fierce, yet subjugated, looks, while he, speaking earnestly, and\par
pointing to the walls, did not perceive Emily, who remained at some\par
distance, waiting till he should be at leisure, and observing\par
involuntarily the appearance of one man, more savage than his fellows,\par
who stood resting on his pike, and looking, over the shoulders of a\par
comrade, at Montoni, to whom he listened with uncommon earnestness.\par
This man was apparently of low condition; yet his looks appeared not to\par
acknowledge the superiority of Montoni, as did those of his companions;\par
and sometimes they even assumed an air of authority, which the decisive\par
manner of the Signor could not repress. Some few words of Montoni then\par
passed in the wind; and, as the men were separating, she heard him say,\par
“This evening, then, begin the watch at sunset.”\par
\par
“At sunset, Signor,” replied one or two of them, and walked away; while\par
Emily approached Montoni, who appeared desirous of avoiding her: but,\par
though she observed this, she had courage to proceed. She endeavoured\par
to intercede once more for her aunt, represented to him her sufferings,\par
and urged the danger of exposing her to a cold apartment in her present\par
state. “She suffers by her own folly,” said Montoni, “and is not to be\par
pitied;—she knows how she may avoid these sufferings in future—if she\par
is removed to the turret, it will be her own fault. Let her be\par
obedient, and sign the writings you heard of, and I will think no more\par
of it.”\par
\par
When Emily ventured still to plead, he sternly silenced and rebuked her\par
for interfering in his domestic affairs, but, at length, dismissed her\par
with this concession—That he would not remove Madame Montoni, on the\par
ensuing night, but allow her till the next to consider, whether she\par
would resign her settlements, or be imprisoned in the east turret of\par
the castle, “where she shall find,” he added, “a punishment she may not\par
expect.”\par
\par
Emily then hastened to inform her aunt of this short respite and of the\par
alternative, that awaited her, to which the latter made no reply, but\par
appeared thoughtful, while Emily, in consideration of her extreme\par
languor, wished to sooth her mind by leading it to less interesting\par
topics: and, though these efforts were unsuccessful, and Madame Montoni\par
became peevish, her resolution, on the contended point, seemed somewhat\par
to relax, and Emily recommended, as her only means of safety, that she\par
should submit to Montoni’s demand. “You know not what you advise,” said\par
her aunt. “Do you understand, that these estates will descend to you at\par
my death, if I persist in a refusal?”\par
\par
“I was ignorant of that circumstance, madam,” replied Emily, “but the\par
knowledge of it cannot withhold me from advising you to adopt the\par
conduct, which not only your peace, but, I fear, your safety requires,\par
and I entreat, that you will not suffer a consideration comparatively\par
so trifling, to make you hesitate a moment in resigning them.”\par
\par
“Are you sincere, niece?” “Is it possible you can doubt it, madam?” Her\par
aunt appeared to be affected. “You are not unworthy of these estates,\par
niece,” said she: “I would wish to keep them for your sake—you show a\par
virtue I did not expect.”\par
\par
“How have I deserved this reproof, madam?” said Emily sorrowfully.\par
\par
“Reproof!” replied Madame Montoni: “I meant to praise your virtue.”\par
\par
“Alas! here is no exertion of virtue,” rejoined Emily, “for here is no\par
temptation to be overcome.”\par
\par
“Yet Monsieur Valancourt—” said her aunt. “O, madam!” interrupted\par
Emily, anticipating what she would have said, “do not let me glance on\par
that subject: do not let my mind be stained with a wish so shockingly\par
self-interested.” She immediately changed the topic, and continued with\par
Madame Montoni, till she withdrew to her apartment for the night.\par
\par
At that hour, the castle was perfectly still, and every inhabitant of\par
it, except herself, seemed to have retired to rest. As she passed along\par
the wide and lonely galleries, dusky and silent, she felt forlorn and\par
apprehensive of—she scarcely knew what; but when, entering the\par
corridor, she recollected the incident of the preceding night, a dread\par
seized her, lest a subject of alarm, similar to that, which had\par
befallen Annette, should occur to her, and which, whether real, or\par
ideal, would, she felt, have an almost equal effect upon her weakened\par
spirits. The chamber, to which Annette had alluded, she did not exactly\par
know, but understood it to be one of those she must pass in the way to\par
her own; and, sending a fearful look forward into the gloom, she\par
stepped lightly and cautiously along, till, coming to a door, from\par
whence issued a low sound, she hesitated and paused; and, during the\par
delay of that moment, her fears so much increased, that she had no\par
power to move from the spot. Believing, that she heard a human voice\par
within, she was somewhat revived; but, in the next moment, the door was\par
opened, and a person, whom she conceived to be Montoni, appeared, who\par
instantly started back, and closed it, though not before she had seen,\par
by the light that burned in the chamber, another person, sitting in a\par
melancholy attitude by the fire. Her terror vanished, but her\par
astonishment only began, which was now roused by the mysterious secrecy\par
of Montoni’s manner, and by the discovery of a person, whom he thus\par
visited at midnight, in an apartment, which had long been shut up, and\par
of which such extraordinary reports were circulated.\par
\par
While she thus continued hesitating, strongly prompted to watch\par
Montoni’s motions, yet fearing to irritate him by appearing to notice\par
them, the door was again opened cautiously, and as instantly closed as\par
before. She then stepped softly to her chamber, which was the next but\par
one to this, but, having put down her lamp, returned to an obscure\par
corner of the corridor, to observe the proceedings of this half-seen\par
person, and to ascertain, whether it was indeed Montoni.\par
\par
Having waited in silent expectation for a few minutes, with her eyes\par
fixed on the door, it was again opened, and the same person appeared,\par
whom she now knew to be Montoni. He looked cautiously round, without\par
perceiving her, then, stepping forward, closed the door, and left the\par
corridor. Soon after, Emily heard the door fastened on the inside, and\par
she withdrew to her chamber, wondering at what she had witnessed.\par
\par
It was now twelve o’clock. As she closed her casement, she heard\par
footsteps on the terrace below, and saw imperfectly, through the gloom,\par
several persons advancing, who passed under the casement. She then\par
heard the clink of arms, and, in the next moment, the watch-word; when,\par
recollecting the command she had overheard from Montoni, and the hour\par
of the night, she understood, that these men were, for the first time,\par
relieving guard in the castle. Having listened till all was again\par
still, she retired to sleep.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER X\par
\par
And shall no lay of death\par
With pleasing murmur sooth\par
Her parted soul?\par
Shall no tear wet her grave?\par
                    SAYERS\par
\par
\par
On the following morning, Emily went early to the apartment of Madame\par
Montoni, who had slept well, and was much recovered. Her spirits had\par
also returned with her health, and her resolution to oppose Montoni’s\par
demands revived, though it yet struggled with her fears, which Emily,\par
who trembled for the consequence of further opposition, endeavoured to\par
confirm.\par
\par
Her aunt, as has been already shown, had a disposition, which delighted\par
in contradiction, and which taught her, when unpleasant circumstances\par
were offered to her understanding, not to enquire into their truth, but\par
to seek for arguments, by which she might make them appear false. Long\par
habit had so entirely confirmed this natural propensity, that she was\par
not conscious of possessing it. Emily’s remonstrances and\par
representations, therefore, roused her pride, instead of alarming, or\par
convincing her judgment, and she still relied upon the discovery of\par
some means, by which she might yet avoid submitting to the demand of\par
her husband. Considering, that, if she could once escape from his\par
castle, she might defy his power, and, obtaining a decisive separation,\par
live in comfort on the estates, that yet remained for her, she\par
mentioned this to her niece, who accorded with her in the wish, but\par
differed from her, as to the probability of its completion. She\par
represented the impossibility of passing the gates, secured and guarded\par
as they were, and the extreme danger of committing her design to the\par
discretion of a servant, who might either purposely betray, or\par
accidentally disclose it.—Montoni’s vengeance would also disdain\par
restraint, if her intention was detected: and, though Emily wished, as\par
fervently as she could do, to regain her freedom, and return to France,\par
she consulted only Madame Montoni’s safety, and persevered in advising\par
her to relinquish her settlement, without braving further outrage.\par
\par
The struggle of contrary emotions, however, continued to rage in her\par
aunt’s bosom, and she still brooded over the chance of effecting an\par
escape. While she thus sat, Montoni entered the room, and, without\par
noticing his wife’s indisposition, said, that he came to remind her of\par
the impolicy of trifling with him, and that he gave her only till the\par
evening to determine, whether she would consent to his demand, or\par
compel him, by a refusal, to remove her to the east turret. He added,\par
that a party of cavaliers would dine with him, that day, and that he\par
expected that she would sit at the head of the table, where Emily,\par
also, must be present. Madame Montoni was now on the point of uttering\par
an absolute refusal, but, suddenly considering, that her liberty,\par
during this entertainment, though circumscribed, might favour her\par
further plans, she acquiesced, with seeming reluctance, and Montoni,\par
soon after, left the apartment. His command struck Emily with surprise\par
and apprehension, who shrank from the thought of being exposed to the\par
gaze of strangers, such as her fancy represented these to be, and the\par
words of Count Morano, now again recollected, did not sooth her fears.\par
\par
When she withdrew to prepare for dinner, she dressed herself with even\par
more simplicity than usual, that she might escape observation—a policy,\par
which did not avail her, for, as she repassed to her aunt’s apartment,\par
she was met by Montoni, who censured what he called her prudish\par
appearance, and insisted, that she should wear the most splendid dress\par
she had, even that, which had been prepared for her intended nuptials\par
with Count Morano, and which, it now appeared, her aunt had carefully\par
brought with her from Venice. This was made, not in the Venetian, but,\par
in the Neapolitan fashion, so as to set off the shape and figure, to\par
the utmost advantage. In it, her beautiful chestnut tresses were\par
negligently bound up in pearls, and suffered to fall back again on her\par
neck. The simplicity of a better taste, than Madame Montoni’s, was\par
conspicuous in this dress, splendid as it was, and Emily’s unaffected\par
beauty never had appeared more captivatingly. She had now only to hope,\par
that Montoni’s order was prompted, not by any extraordinary design, but\par
by an ostentation of displaying his family, richly attired, to the eyes\par
of strangers; yet nothing less than his absolute command could have\par
prevailed with her to wear a dress, that had been designed for such an\par
offensive purpose, much less to have worn it on this occasion. As she\par
descended to dinner, the emotion of her mind threw a faint blush over\par
her countenance, and heightened its interesting expression; for\par
timidity had made her linger in her apartment, till the utmost moment,\par
and, when she entered the hall, in which a kind of state dinner was\par
spread, Montoni and his guests were already seated at the table. She\par
was then going to place herself by her aunt; but Montoni waved his\par
hand, and two of the cavaliers rose, and seated her between them.\par
\par
The eldest of these was a tall man, with strong Italian features, an\par
aquiline nose, and dark penetrating eyes, that flashed with fire, when\par
his mind was agitated, and, even in its state of rest, retained\par
somewhat of the wildness of the passions. His visage was long and\par
narrow, and his complexion of a sickly yellow.\par
\par
The other, who appeared to be about forty, had features of a different\par
cast, yet Italian, and his look was slow, subtle and penetrating; his\par
eyes, of a dark grey, were small, and hollow; his complexion was a\par
sun-burnt brown, and the contour of his face, though inclined to oval,\par
was irregular and ill-formed.\par
\par
Eight other guests sat round the table, who were all dressed in a\par
uniform, and had all an expression, more or less, of wild fierceness,\par
of subtle design, or of licentious passions. As Emily timidly surveyed\par
them, she remembered the scene of the preceding morning, and again\par
almost fancied herself surrounded by banditti; then, looking back to\par
the tranquillity of her early life, she felt scarcely less\par
astonishment, than grief, at her present situation. The scene, in which\par
they sat, assisted the illusion; it was an ancient hall, gloomy from\par
the style of its architecture, from its great extent, and because\par
almost the only light it received was from one large gothic window, and\par
from a pair of folding doors, which, being open, admitted likewise a\par
view of the west rampart, with the wild mountains of the Apennine\par
beyond.\par
\par
The middle compartment of this hall rose into a vaulted roof, enriched\par
with fretwork, and supported, on three sides, by pillars of marble;\par
beyond these, long colonnades retired in gloomy grandeur, till their\par
extent was lost in twilight. The lightest footsteps of the servants, as\par
they advanced through these, were returned in whispering echoes, and\par
their figures, seen at a distance imperfectly through the dusk,\par
frequently awakened Emily’s imagination. She looked alternately at\par
Montoni, at his guests and on the surrounding scene; and then,\par
remembering her dear native province, her pleasant home and the\par
simplicity and goodness of the friends, whom she had lost, grief and\par
surprise again occupied her mind.\par
\par
When her thoughts could return from these considerations, she fancied\par
she observed an air of authority towards his guests, such as she had\par
never before seen him assume, though he had always been distinguished\par
by a haughty carriage; there was something also in the manners of the\par
strangers, that seemed perfectly, though not servilely, to acknowledge\par
his superiority.\par
\par
During dinner, the conversation was chiefly on war and politics. They\par
talked with energy of the state of Venice, its dangers, the character\par
of the reigning Doge and of the chief senators; and then spoke of the\par
state of Rome. When the repast was over, they rose, and, each filling\par
his goblet with wine from the gilded ewer, that stood beside him, drank\par
“Success to our exploits!” Montoni was lifting his goblet to his lips\par
to drink this toast, when suddenly the wine hissed, rose to the brim,\par
and, as he held the glass from him, it burst into a thousand pieces.\par
\par
To him, who constantly used that sort of Venice glass, which had the\par
quality of breaking, upon receiving poisoned liquor, a suspicion, that\par
some of his guests had endeavoured to betray him, instantly occurred,\par
and he ordered all the gates to be closed, drew his sword, and, looking\par
round on them, who stood in silent amazement, exclaimed, “Here is a\par
traitor among us; let those, that are innocent, assist in discovering\par
the guilty.”\par
\par
Indignation flashed from the eyes of the cavaliers, who all drew their\par
swords; and Madame Montoni, terrified at what might ensue, was\par
hastening from the hall, when her husband commanded her to stay; but\par
his further words could not now be distinguished, for the voice of\par
every person rose together. His order, that all the servants should\par
appear, was at length obeyed, and they declared their ignorance of any\par
deceit—a protestation which could not be believed; for it was evident,\par
that, as Montoni’s liquor, and his only, had been poisoned, a\par
deliberate design had been formed against his life, which could not\par
have been carried so far towards its accomplishment, without the\par
connivance of the servant, who had the care of the wine ewers.\par
\par
This man, with another, whose face betrayed either the consciousness of\par
guilt, or the fear of punishment, Montoni ordered to be chained\par
instantly, and confined in a strong room, which had formerly been used\par
as a prison. Thither, likewise, he would have sent all his guests, had\par
he not foreseen the consequence of so bold and unjustifiable a\par
proceeding. As to those, therefore, he contented himself with swearing,\par
that no man should pass the gates, till this extraordinary affair had\par
been investigated, and then sternly bade his wife retire to her\par
apartment, whither he suffered Emily to attend her.\par
\par
In about half an hour, he followed to the dressing-room; and Emily\par
observed, with horror, his dark countenance and quivering lip, and\par
heard him denounce vengeance on her aunt.\par
\par
“It will avail you nothing,” said he to his wife, “to deny the fact; I\par
have proof of your guilt. Your only chance of mercy rests on a full\par
confession;—there is nothing to hope from sullenness, or falsehood;\par
your accomplice has confessed all.”\par
\par
Emily’s fainting spirits were roused by astonishment, as she heard her\par
aunt accused of a crime so atrocious, and she could not, for a moment,\par
admit the possibility of her guilt. Meanwhile Madame Montoni’s\par
agitation did not permit her to reply; alternately her complexion\par
varied from livid paleness to a crimson flush; and she trembled,—but,\par
whether with fear, or with indignation, it were difficult to decide.\par
\par
“Spare your words,” said Montoni, seeing her about to speak, “your\par
countenance makes full confession of your crime.—You shall be instantly\par
removed to the east turret.”\par
\par
“This accusation,” said Madame Montoni, speaking with difficulty, “is\par
used only as an excuse for your cruelty; I disdain to reply to it. You\par
do not believe me guilty.”\par
\par
“Signor!” said Emily solemnly, “this dreadful charge, I would answer\par
with my life, is false. Nay, Signor,” she added, observing the severity\par
of his countenance, “this is no moment for restraint, on my part; I do\par
not scruple to tell you, that you are deceived—most wickedly deceived,\par
by the suggestion of some person, who aims at the ruin of my aunt:—it\par
is impossible, that you could yourself have imagined a crime so\par
hideous.”\par
\par
Montoni, his lips trembling more than before, replied only, “If you\par
value your own safety,” addressing Emily, “you will be silent. I shall\par
know how to interpret your remonstrances, should you persevere in\par
them.”\par
\par
Emily raised her eyes calmly to heaven. “Here is, indeed, then, nothing\par
to hope!” said she.\par
\par
“Peace!” cried Montoni, “or you shall find there is something to fear.”\par
\par
He turned to his wife, who had now recovered her spirits, and who\par
vehemently and wildly remonstrated upon this mysterious suspicion: but\par
Montoni’s rage heightened with her indignation, and Emily, dreading the\par
event of it, threw herself between them, and clasped his knees in\par
silence, looking up in his face with an expression, that might have\par
softened the heart of a fiend. Whether his was hardened by a conviction\par
of Madame Montoni’s guilt, or that a bare suspicion of it made him\par
eager to exercise vengeance, he was totally and alike insensible to the\par
distress of his wife, and to the pleading looks of Emily, whom he made\par
no attempt to raise, but was vehemently menacing both, when he was\par
called out of the room by some person at the door. As he shut the door,\par
Emily heard him turn the lock and take out the key; so that Madame\par
Montoni and herself were now prisoners; and she saw that his designs\par
became more and more terrible. Her endeavours to explain his motives\par
for this circumstance were almost as ineffectual as those to sooth the\par
distress of her aunt, whose innocence she could not doubt; but she, at\par
length, accounted for Montoni’s readiness to suspect his wife by his\par
own consciousness of cruelty towards her, and for the sudden violence\par
of his present conduct against both, before even his suspicions could\par
be completely formed, by his general eagerness to effect suddenly\par
whatever he was led to desire and his carelessness of justice, or\par
humanity, in accomplishing it.\par
\par
Madame Montoni, after some time, again looked round, in search of a\par
possibility of escape from the castle, and conversed with Emily on the\par
subject, who was now willing to encounter any hazard, though she\par
forbore to encourage a hope in her aunt, which she herself did not\par
admit. How strongly the edifice was secured, and how vigilantly\par
guarded, she knew too well; and trembled to commit their safety to the\par
caprice of the servant, whose assistance they must solicit. Old Carlo\par
was compassionate, but he seemed to be too much in his master’s\par
interest to be trusted by them; Annette could of herself do little, and\par
Emily knew Ludovico only from her report. At present, however, these\par
considerations were useless, Madame Montoni and her niece being shut up\par
from all intercourse, even with the persons, whom there might be these\par
reasons to reject.\par
\par
In the hall, confusion and tumult still reigned. Emily, as she listened\par
anxiously to the murmur, that sounded along the gallery, sometimes\par
fancied she heard the clashing of swords, and, when she considered the\par
nature of the provocation, given by Montoni, and his impetuosity, it\par
appeared probable, that nothing less than arms would terminate the\par
contention. Madame Montoni, having exhausted all her expressions of\par
indignation, and Emily, hers of comfort, they remained silent, in that\par
kind of breathless stillness, which, in nature, often succeeds to the\par
uproar of conflicting elements; a stillness, like the morning, that\par
dawns upon the ruins of an earthquake.\par
\par
An uncertain kind of terror pervaded Emily’s mind; the circumstances of\par
the past hour still came dimly and confusedly to her memory; and her\par
thoughts were various and rapid, though without tumult.\par
\par
From this state of waking visions she was recalled by a knocking at the\par
chamber-door, and, enquiring who was there, heard the whispering voice\par
of Annette.\par
\par
“Dear madam, let me come in, I have a great deal to say,” said the poor\par
girl.\par
\par
“The door is locked,” answered the lady.\par
\par
“Yes, ma’am, but do pray open it.”\par
\par
“The Signor has the key,” said Madame Montoni.\par
\par
“O blessed Virgin! what will become of us?” exclaimed Annette.\par
\par
“Assist us to escape,” said her mistress. “Where is Ludovico?”\par
\par
“Below in the hall, ma’am, amongst them all, fighting with the best of\par
them!”\par
\par
“Fighting! Who are fighting?” cried Madame Montoni.\par
\par
“Why the Signor, ma’am, and all the Signors, and a great many more.”\par
\par
“Is any person much hurt?” said Emily, in a tremulous voice. “Hurt!\par
Yes, ma’amselle,—there they lie bleeding, and the swords are clashing,\par
and—O holy saints! Do let me in, ma’am, they are coming this way—I\par
shall be murdered!”\par
\par
“Fly!” cried Emily, “fly! we cannot open the door.”\par
\par
Annette repeated, that they were coming, and in the same moment fled.\par
\par
“Be calm, madam,” said Emily, turning to her aunt, “I entreat you to be\par
calm, I am not frightened—not frightened in the least, do not you be\par
alarmed.”\par
\par
“You can scarcely support yourself,” replied her aunt; “Merciful God!\par
what is it they mean to do with us?”\par
\par
“They come, perhaps, to liberate us,” said Emily, “Signor Montoni\par
perhaps is—is conquered.”\par
\par
The belief of his death gave her spirits a sudden shock, and she grew\par
faint as she saw him in imagination, expiring at her feet.\par
\par
“They are coming!” cried Madame Montoni—“I hear their steps—they are at\par
the door!”\par
\par
Emily turned her languid eyes to the door, but terror deprived her of\par
utterance. The key sounded in the lock; the door opened, and Montoni\par
appeared, followed by three ruffian-like men. “Execute your orders,”\par
said he, turning to them, and pointing to his wife, who shrieked, but\par
was immediately carried from the room; while Emily sunk, senseless, on\par
a couch, by which she had endeavoured to support herself. When she\par
recovered, she was alone, and recollected only, that Madame Montoni had\par
been there, together with some unconnected particulars of the preceding\par
transaction, which were, however, sufficient to renew all her terror.\par
She looked wildly round the apartment, as if in search of some means of\par
intelligence, concerning her aunt, while neither her own danger, nor an\par
idea of escaping from the room, immediately occurred.\par
\par
When her recollection was more complete, she raised herself and went,\par
but with only a faint hope, to examine whether the door was unfastened.\par
It was so, and she then stepped timidly out into the gallery, but\par
paused there, uncertain which way she should proceed. Her first wish\par
was to gather some information, as to her aunt, and she, at length,\par
turned her steps to go to the lesser hall, where Annette and the other\par
servants usually waited.\par
\par
Everywhere, as she passed, she heard, from a distance, the uproar of\par
contention, and the figures and faces, which she met, hurrying along\par
the passages, struck her mind with dismay. Emily might now have\par
appeared, like an angel of light, encompassed by fiends. At length, she\par
reached the lesser hall, which was silent and deserted, but, panting\par
for breath, she sat down to recover herself. The total stillness of\par
this place was as awful as the tumult, from which she had escaped: but\par
she had now time to recall her scattered thoughts, to remember her\par
personal danger, and to consider of some means of safety. She\par
perceived, that it was useless to seek Madame Montoni, through the wide\par
extent and intricacies of the castle, now, too, when every avenue\par
seemed to be beset by ruffians; in this hall she could not resolve to\par
stay, for she knew not how soon it might become their place of\par
rendezvous; and, though she wished to go to her chamber, she dreaded\par
again to encounter them on the way.\par
\par
Thus she sat, trembling and hesitating, when a distant murmur broke on\par
the silence, and grew louder and louder, till she distinguished voices\par
and steps approaching. She then rose to go, but the sounds came along\par
the only passage, by which she could depart, and she was compelled to\par
await in the hall, the arrival of the persons, whose steps she heard.\par
As these advanced, she distinguished groans, and then saw a man borne\par
slowly along by four others. Her spirits faltered at the sight, and she\par
leaned against the wall for support. The bearers, meanwhile, entered\par
the hall, and, being too busily occupied to detain, or even notice\par
Emily, she attempted to leave it, but her strength failed, and she\par
again sat down on the bench. A damp chillness came over her; her sight\par
became confused; she knew not what had passed, or where she was, yet\par
the groans of the wounded person still vibrated on her heart. In a few\par
moments, the tide of life seemed again to flow; she began to breathe\par
more freely, and her senses revived. She had not fainted, nor had ever\par
totally lost her consciousness, but had contrived to support herself on\par
the bench; still without courage to turn her eyes upon the unfortunate\par
object, which remained near her, and about whom the men were yet too\par
much engaged to attend to her.\par
\par
When her strength returned, she rose, and was suffered to leave the\par
hall, though her anxiety, having produced some vain enquiries,\par
concerning Madame Montoni, had thus made a discovery of herself.\par
Towards her chamber she now hastened, as fast as her steps would bear\par
her, for she still perceived, upon her passage, the sounds of confusion\par
at a distance, and she endeavoured, by taking her way through some\par
obscure rooms, to avoid encountering the persons, whose looks had\par
terrified her before, as well as those parts of the castle, where the\par
tumult might still rage.\par
\par
At length, she reached her chamber, and, having secured the door of the\par
corridor, felt herself, for a moment, in safety. A profound stillness\par
reigned in this remote apartment, which not even the faint murmur of\par
the most distant sounds now reached. She sat down, near one of the\par
casements, and, as she gazed on the mountain-view beyond, the deep\par
repose of its beauty struck her with all the force of contrast, and she\par
could scarcely believe herself so near a scene of savage discord. The\par
contending elements seemed to have retired from their natural spheres,\par
and to have collected themselves into the minds of men, for there alone\par
the tempest now reigned.\par
\par
Emily tried to tranquillize her spirits, but anxiety made her\par
constantly listen for some sound, and often look out upon the ramparts,\par
where all, however, was lonely and still. As a sense of her own\par
immediate danger had decreased, her apprehension concerning Madame\par
Montoni heightened, who, she remembered, had been fiercely threatened\par
with confinement in the east turret, and it was possible, that her\par
husband had satisfied his present vengeance with this punishment. She,\par
therefore, determined, when night should return, and the inhabitants of\par
the castle should be asleep, to explore the way to the turret, which,\par
as the direction it stood in was mentioned, appeared not very difficult\par
to be done. She knew, indeed, that although her aunt might be there,\par
she could afford her no effectual assistance, but it might give her\par
some comfort even to know, that she was discovered, and to hear the\par
sound of her niece’s voice; for herself, any certainty, concerning\par
Madame Montoni’s fate, appeared more tolerable, than this exhausting\par
suspense.\par
\par
Meanwhile, Annette did not appear, and Emily was surprised, and\par
somewhat alarmed for her, whom, in the confusion of the late scene,\par
various accidents might have befallen, and it was improbable, that she\par
would have failed to come to her apartment, unless something\par
unfortunate had happened.\par
\par
Thus the hours passed in solitude, in silence, and in anxious\par
conjecturing. Being not once disturbed by a message, or a sound, it\par
appeared, that Montoni had wholly forgotten her, and it gave her some\par
comfort to find, that she could be so unnoticed. She endeavoured to\par
withdraw her thoughts from the anxiety, that preyed upon them, but they\par
refused control; she could neither read, nor draw, and the tones of her\par
lute were so utterly discordant with the present state of her feelings,\par
that she could not endure them for a moment.\par
\par
The sun, at length, set behind the western mountains; his fiery beams\par
faded from the clouds, and then a dun melancholy purple drew over them,\par
and gradually involved the features of the country below. Soon after,\par
the sentinels passed on the rampart to commence the watch.\par
\par
Twilight had now spread its gloom over every object; the dismal\par
obscurity of her chamber recalled fearful thoughts, but she remembered,\par
that to procure a light she must pass through a great extent of the\par
castle, and, above all, through the halls, where she had already\par
experienced so much horror. Darkness, indeed, in the present state of\par
her spirits, made silence and solitude terrible to her; it would also\par
prevent the possibility of her finding her way to the turret, and\par
condemn her to remain in suspense, concerning the fate of her aunt; yet\par
she dared not to venture forth for a lamp.\par
\par
Continuing at the casement, that she might catch the last lingering\par
gleam of evening, a thousand vague images of fear floated on her fancy.\par
“What if some of these ruffians,” said she, “should find out the\par
private staircase, and in the darkness of night steal into my chamber!”\par
Then, recollecting the mysterious inhabitant of the neighbouring\par
apartment, her terror changed its object. “He is not a prisoner,” said\par
she, “though he remains in one chamber, for Montoni did not fasten the\par
door, when he left it; the unknown person himself did this; it is\par
certain, therefore, he can come out when he pleases.”\par
\par
She paused, for, notwithstanding the terrors of darkness, she\par
considered it to be very improbable, whoever he was, that he could have\par
any interest in intruding upon her retirement; and again the subject of\par
her emotion changed, when, remembering her nearness to the chamber,\par
where the veil had formerly disclosed a dreadful spectacle, she doubted\par
whether some passage might not communicate between it and the insecure\par
door of the staircase.\par
\par
It was now entirely dark, and she left the casement. As she sat with\par
her eyes fixed on the hearth, she thought she perceived there a spark\par
of light; it twinkled and disappeared, and then again was visible. At\par
length, with much care, she fanned the embers of a wood fire, that had\par
been lighted in the morning, into flame, and, having communicated it to\par
a lamp, which always stood in her room, felt a satisfaction not to be\par
conceived, without a review of her situation. Her first care was to\par
guard the door of the staircase, for which purpose she placed against\par
it all the furniture she could move, and she was thus employed, for\par
some time, at the end of which she had another instance how much more\par
oppressive misfortune is to the idle, than to the busy; for, having\par
then leisure to think over all the circumstances of her present\par
afflictions, she imagined a thousand evils for futurity, and these real\par
and ideal subjects of distress alike wounded her mind.\par
\par
Thus heavily moved the hours till midnight, when she counted the sullen\par
notes of the great clock, as they rolled along the rampart, unmingled\par
with any sound, except the distant foot-fall of a sentinel, who came to\par
relieve guard. She now thought she might venture towards the turret,\par
and, having gently opened the chamber door to examine the corridor, and\par
to listen if any person was stirring in the castle, found all around in\par
perfect stillness. Yet no sooner had she left the room, than she\par
perceived a light flash on the walls of the corridor, and, without\par
waiting to see by whom it was carried, she shrunk back, and closed her\par
door. No one approaching, she conjectured, that it was Montoni going to\par
pay his midnight visit to her unknown neighbour, and she determined to\par
wait, till he should have retired to his own apartment.\par
\par
When the chimes had tolled another half hour, she once more opened the\par
door, and, perceiving that no person was in the corridor, hastily\par
crossed into a passage, that led along the south side of the castle\par
towards the staircase, whence she believed she could easily find her\par
way to the turret. Often pausing on her way, listening apprehensively\par
to the murmurs of the wind, and looking fearfully onward into the gloom\par
of the long passages, she, at length, reached the staircase; but there\par
her perplexity began. Two passages appeared, of which she knew not how\par
to prefer one, and was compelled, at last, to decide by chance, rather\par
than by circumstances. That she entered, opened first into a wide\par
gallery, along which she passed lightly and swiftly; for the lonely\par
aspect of the place awed her, and she started at the echo of her own\par
steps.\par
\par
On a sudden, she thought she heard a voice, and, not distinguishing\par
from whence it came, feared equally to proceed, or to return. For some\par
moments, she stood in an attitude of listening expectation, shrinking\par
almost from herself and scarcely daring to look round her. The voice\par
came again, but, though it was now near her, terror did not allow her\par
to judge exactly whence it proceeded. She thought, however, that it was\par
the voice of complaint, and her belief was soon confirmed by a low\par
moaning sound, that seemed to proceed from one of the chambers, opening\par
into the gallery. It instantly occurred to her, that Madame Montoni\par
might be there confined, and she advanced to the door to speak, but was\par
checked by considering, that she was, perhaps, going to commit herself\par
to a stranger, who might discover her to Montoni; for, though this\par
person, whoever it was, seemed to be in affliction, it did not follow,\par
that he was a prisoner.\par
\par
While these thoughts passed over her mind, and left her still in\par
hesitation, the voice spoke again, and, calling “Ludovico,” she then\par
perceived it to be that of Annette; on which, no longer hesitating, she\par
went in joy to answer her.\par
\par
“Ludovico!” cried Annette, sobbing—“Ludovico!”\par
\par
“It is not Ludovico, it is I—Mademoiselle Emily.”\par
\par
Annette ceased sobbing, and was silent.\par
\par
“If you can open the door, let me in,” said Emily, “here is no person\par
to hurt you.”\par
\par
“Ludovico!—O, Ludovico!” cried Annette.\par
\par
Emily now lost her patience, and her fear of being overheard\par
increasing, she was even nearly about to leave the door, when she\par
considered, that Annette might, possibly, know something of the\par
situation of Madame Montoni, or direct her to the turret. At length,\par
she obtained a reply, though little satisfactory, to her questions, for\par
Annette knew nothing of Madame Montoni, and only conjured Emily to tell\par
her what was become of Ludovico. Of him she had no information to give,\par
and she again asked who had shut Annette up.\par
\par
“Ludovico,” said the poor girl, “Ludovico shut me up. When I ran away\par
from the dressing-room door today, I went I scarcely knew where, for\par
safety; and, in this gallery, here, I met Ludovico, who hurried me into\par
this chamber, and locked me up to keep me out of harm, as he said. But\par
he was in such a hurry himself, he hardly spoke ten words, but he told\par
me he would come, and let me out, when all was quiet, and he took away\par
the key with him. Now all these hours are passed, and I have neither\par
seen, nor heard a word of him; they have murdered him—I know they\par
have!”\par
\par
Emily suddenly remembered the wounded person, whom she had seen borne\par
into the servants’ hall, and she scarcely doubted, that he was\par
Ludovico, but she concealed the circumstance from Annette, and\par
endeavoured to comfort her. Then, impatient to learn something of her\par
aunt, she again enquired the way to the turret.\par
\par
“O! you are not going, ma’amselle,” said Annette, “for Heaven’s sake,\par
do not go, and leave me here by myself.”\par
\par
“Nay, Annette, you do not think I can wait in the gallery all night,”\par
replied Emily. “Direct me to the turret; in the morning I will\par
endeavour to release you.”\par
\par
“O holy Mary!” exclaimed Annette, “am I to stay here by myself all\par
night! I shall be frightened out of my senses, and I shall die of\par
hunger; I have had nothing to eat since dinner!”\par
\par
Emily could scarcely forbear smiling at the heterogeneous distresses of\par
Annette, though she sincerely pitied them, and said what she could to\par
sooth her. At length, she obtained something like a direction to the\par
east turret, and quitted the door, from whence, after many intricacies\par
and perplexities, she reached the steep and winding stairs of the\par
turret, at the foot of which she stopped to rest, and to reanimate her\par
courage with a sense of her duty. As she surveyed this dismal place,\par
she perceived a door on the opposite side of the staircase, and,\par
anxious to know whether it would lead her to Madame Montoni, she tried\par
to undraw the bolts, which fastened it. A fresher air came to her face,\par
as she unclosed the door, which opened upon the east rampart, and the\par
sudden current had nearly extinguished her light, which she now removed\par
to a distance; and again, looking out upon the obscure terrace, she\par
perceived only the faint outline of the walls and of some towers,\par
while, above, heavy clouds, borne along the wind, seemed to mingle with\par
the stars, and wrap the night in thicker darkness. As she gazed, now\par
willing to defer the moment of certainty, from which she expected only\par
confirmation of evil, a distant footstep reminded her, that she might\par
be observed by the men on watch, and, hastily closing the door, she\par
took her lamp, and passed up the staircase. Trembling came upon her, as\par
she ascended through the gloom. To her melancholy fancy this seemed to\par
be a place of death, and the chilling silence, that reigned, confirmed\par
its character. Her spirits faltered. “Perhaps,” said she, “I am come\par
hither only to learn a dreadful truth, or to witness some horrible\par
spectacle; I feel that my senses would not survive such an addition of\par
horror.”\par
\par
The image of her aunt murdered—murdered, perhaps, by the hand of\par
Montoni, rose to her mind; she trembled, gasped for breath—repented\par
that she had dared to venture hither, and checked her steps. But, after\par
she had paused a few minutes, the consciousness of her duty returned,\par
and she went on. Still all was silent. At length a track of blood, upon\par
a stair, caught her eye; and instantly she perceived, that the wall and\par
several other steps were stained. She paused, again struggled to\par
support herself, and the lamp almost fell from her trembling hand.\par
Still no sound was heard, no living being seemed to inhabit the turret;\par
a thousand times she wished herself again in her chamber; dreaded to\par
enquire farther—dreaded to encounter some horrible spectacle, and yet\par
could not resolve, now that she was so near the termination of her\par
efforts, to desist from them. Having again collected courage to\par
proceed, after ascending about half way up the turret, she came to\par
another door, but here again she stopped in hesitation; listened for\par
sounds within, and then, summoning all her resolution, unclosed it, and\par
entered a chamber, which, as her lamp shot its feeble rays through the\par
darkness, seemed to exhibit only dew-stained and deserted walls. As she\par
stood examining it, in fearful expectation of discovering the remains\par
of her unfortunate aunt, she perceived something lying in an obscure\par
corner of the room, and, struck with a horrible conviction, she became,\par
for an instant, motionless and nearly insensible. Then, with a kind of\par
desperate resolution, she hurried towards the object that excited her\par
terror, when, perceiving the clothes of some person, on the floor, she\par
caught hold of them, and found in her grasp the old uniform of a\par
soldier, beneath which appeared a heap of pikes and other arms.\par
Scarcely daring to trust her sight, she continued, for some moments, to\par
gaze on the object of her late alarm, and then left the chamber, so\par
much comforted and occupied by the conviction, that her aunt was not\par
there, that she was going to descend the turret, without enquiring\par
farther; when, on turning to do so, she observed upon some steps on the\par
second flight an appearance of blood, and remembering, that there was\par
yet another chamber to be explored, she again followed the windings of\par
the ascent. Still, as she ascended, the track of blood glared upon the\par
stairs.\par
\par
It led her to the door of a landing-place, that terminated them, but\par
she was unable to follow it farther. Now that she was so near the\par
sought-for certainty, she dreaded to know it, even more than before,\par
and had not fortitude sufficient to speak, or to attempt opening the\par
door.\par
\par
Having listened, in vain, for some sound, that might confirm, or\par
destroy her fears, she, at length, laid her hand on the lock, and,\par
finding it fastened, called on Madame Montoni; but only a chilling\par
silence ensued.\par
\par
“She is dead!” she cried,—“murdered!—her blood is on the stairs!”\par
\par
Emily grew very faint; could support herself no longer, and had\par
scarcely presence of mind to set down the lamp, and place herself on a\par
step.\par
\par
When her recollection returned, she spoke again at the door, and again\par
attempted to open it, and, having lingered for some time, without\par
receiving any answer, or hearing a sound, she descended the turret,\par
and, with all the swiftness her feebleness would permit, sought her own\par
apartment.\par
\par
As she turned into the corridor, the door of a chamber opened, from\par
whence Montoni came forth; but Emily, more terrified than ever to\par
behold him, shrunk back into the passage soon enough to escape being\par
noticed, and heard him close the door, which she had perceived was the\par
same she formerly observed. Having here listened to his departing\par
steps, till their faint sound was lost in distance, she ventured to her\par
apartment, and, securing it once again, retired to her bed, leaving the\par
lamp burning on the hearth. But sleep was fled from her harassed mind,\par
to which images of horror alone occurred. She endeavoured to think it\par
possible, that Madame Montoni had not been taken to the turret; but,\par
when she recollected the former menaces of her husband and the terrible\par
spirit of vengeance, which he had displayed on a late occasion; when\par
she remembered his general character, the looks of the men, who had\par
forced Madame Montoni from her apartment, and the written traces on the\par
stairs of the turret—she could not doubt, that her aunt had been\par
carried thither, and could scarcely hope, that she had not been carried\par
to be murdered.\par
\par
The grey of morning had long dawned through her casements, before Emily\par
closed her eyes in sleep; when wearied nature, at length, yielded her a\par
respite from suffering.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XI\par
\par
Who rears the bloody hand?\par
                    SAYERS\par
\par
\par
Emily remained in her chamber, on the following morning, without\par
receiving any notice from Montoni, or seeing a human being, except the\par
armed men, who sometimes passed on the terrace below. Having tasted no\par
food since the dinner of the preceding day, extreme faintness made her\par
feel the necessity of quitting the asylum of her apartment to obtain\par
refreshment, and she was also very anxious to procure liberty for\par
Annette. Willing, however, to defer venturing forth, as long as\par
possible, and considering, whether she should apply to Montoni, or to\par
the compassion of some other person, her excessive anxiety concerning\par
her aunt, at length, overcame her abhorrence of his presence, and she\par
determined to go to him, and to entreat, that he would suffer her to\par
see Madame Montoni.\par
\par
Meanwhile, it was too certain, from the absence of Annette, that some\par
accident had befallen Ludovico, and that she was still in confinement;\par
Emily, therefore, resolved also to visit the chamber, where she had\par
spoken to her, on the preceding night, and, if the poor girl was yet\par
there, to inform Montoni of her situation.\par
\par
It was near noon, before she ventured from her apartment, and went\par
first to the south gallery, whither she passed without meeting a single\par
person, or hearing a sound, except, now and then, the echo of a distant\par
footstep.\par
\par
It was unnecessary to call Annette, whose lamentations were audible\par
upon the first approach to the gallery, and who, bewailing her own and\par
Ludovico’s fate, told Emily, that she should certainly be starved to\par
death, if she was not let out immediately. Emily replied, that she was\par
going to beg her release of Montoni; but the terrors of hunger now\par
yielded to those of the Signor, and, when Emily left her, she was\par
loudly entreating, that her place of refuge might be concealed from\par
him.\par
\par
As Emily drew near the great hall, the sounds she heard and the people\par
she met in the passages renewed her alarm. The latter, however, were\par
peaceable, and did not interrupt her, though they looked earnestly at\par
her, as she passed, and sometimes spoke. On crossing the hall towards\par
the cedar room, where Montoni usually sat, she perceived, on the\par
pavement, fragments of swords, some tattered garments stained with\par
blood, and almost expected to have seen among them a dead body; but\par
from such a spectacle she was, at present, spared. As she approached\par
the room, the sound of several voices issued from within, and a dread\par
of appearing before many strangers, as well as of irritating Montoni by\par
such an intrusion, made her pause and falter from her purpose. She\par
looked up through the long arcades of the hall, in search of a servant,\par
who might bear a message, but no one appeared, and the urgency of what\par
she had to request made her still linger near the door. The voices\par
within were not in contention, though she distinguished those of\par
several of the guests of the preceding day; but still her resolution\par
failed, whenever she would have tapped at the door, and she had\par
determined to walk in the hall, till some person should appear, who\par
might call Montoni from the room, when, as she turned from the door, it\par
was suddenly opened by himself. Emily trembled, and was confused, while\par
he almost started with surprise, and all the terrors of his countenance\par
unfolded themselves. She forgot all she would have said, and neither\par
enquired for her aunt, nor entreated for Annette, but stood silent and\par
embarrassed.\par
\par
After closing the door he reproved her for a meanness, of which she had\par
not been guilty, and sternly questioned her what she had overheard; an\par
accusation, which revived her recollection so far, that she assured him\par
she had not come thither with an intention to listen to his\par
conversation, but to entreat his compassion for her aunt, and for\par
Annette. Montoni seemed to doubt this assertion, for he regarded her\par
with a scrutinizing look; and the doubt evidently arose from no\par
trifling interest. Emily then further explained herself, and concluded\par
with entreating him to inform her, where her aunt was placed, and to\par
permit, that she might visit her; but he looked upon her only with a\par
malignant smile, which instantaneously confirmed her worst fears for\par
her aunt, and, at that moment, she had not courage to renew her\par
entreaties.\par
\par
“For Annette,” said he,—“if you go to Carlo, he will release the girl;\par
the foolish fellow, who shut her up, died yesterday.” Emily\par
shuddered.—“But my aunt, Signor”—said she, “O tell me of my aunt!”\par
\par
“She is taken care of,” replied Montoni hastily, “I have no time to\par
answer idle questions.”\par
\par
He would have passed on, but Emily, in a voice of agony, that could not\par
be wholly resisted, conjured him to tell her, where Madame Montoni was;\par
while he paused, and she anxiously watched his countenance, a trumpet\par
sounded, and, in the next moment, she heard the heavy gates of the\par
portal open, and then the clattering of horses’ hoofs in the court,\par
with the confusion of many voices. She stood for a moment hesitating\par
whether she should follow Montoni, who, at the sound of the trumpet,\par
had passed through the hall, and, turning her eyes whence it came, she\par
saw through the door, that opened beyond a long perspective of arches\par
into the courts, a party of horsemen, whom she judged, as well as the\par
distance and her embarrassment would allow, to be the same she had seen\par
depart, a few days before. But she staid not to scrutinize, for, when\par
the trumpet sounded again, the chevaliers rushed out of the cedar room,\par
and men came running into the hall from every quarter of the castle.\par
Emily once more hurried for shelter to her own apartment. Thither she\par
was still pursued by images of horror. She reconsidered Montoni’s\par
manner and words, when he had spoken of his wife, and they served only\par
to confirm her most terrible suspicions. Tears refused any longer to\par
relieve her distress, and she had sat for a considerable time absorbed\par
in thought, when a knocking at the chamber door aroused her, on opening\par
which she found old Carlo.\par
\par
“Dear young lady,” said he, “I have been so flurried, I never once\par
thought of you till just now. I have brought you some fruit and wine,\par
and I am sure you must stand in need of them by this time.”\par
\par
“Thank you, Carlo,” said Emily, “this is very good of you. Did the\par
Signor remind you of me?”\par
\par
“No, Signora,” replied Carlo, “his _Excellenza_ has business enough on\par
his hands.” Emily then renewed her enquiries, concerning Madame\par
Montoni, but Carlo had been employed at the other end of the castle,\par
during the time, that she was removed, and he had heard nothing since,\par
concerning her.\par
\par
While he spoke, Emily looked steadily at him, for she scarcely knew\par
whether he was really ignorant, or concealed his knowledge of the truth\par
from a fear of offending his master. To several questions, concerning\par
the contentions of yesterday, he gave very limited answers; but told,\par
that the disputes were now amicably settled, and that the Signor\par
believed himself to have been mistaken in his suspicions of his guests.\par
“The fighting was about that, Signora,” said Carlo; “but I trust I\par
shall never see such another day in this castle, though strange things\par
are about to be done.”\par
\par
On her enquiring his meaning, “Ah, Signora!” added he, “it is not for\par
me to betray secrets, or tell all I think, but time will tell.”\par
\par
She then desired him to release Annette, and, having described the\par
chamber in which the poor girl was confined, he promised to obey her\par
immediately, and was departing, when she remembered to ask who were the\par
persons just arrived. Her late conjecture was right; it was Verezzi,\par
with his party.\par
\par
Her spirits were somewhat soothed by this short conversation with\par
Carlo; for, in her present circumstances, it afforded some comfort to\par
hear the accents of compassion, and to meet the look of sympathy.\par
\par
An hour passed before Annette appeared, who then came weeping and\par
sobbing. “O Ludovico—Ludovico!” cried she.\par
\par
“My poor Annette!” said Emily, and made her sit down.\par
\par
“Who could have foreseen this, ma’amselle? O miserable, wretched,\par
day—that ever I should live to see it!” and she continued to moan and\par
lament, till Emily thought it necessary to check her excess of grief.\par
“We are continually losing dear friends by death,” said she, with a\par
sigh, that came from her heart. “We must submit to the will of\par
Heaven—our tears, alas! cannot recall the dead!”\par
\par
Annette took the handkerchief from her face.\par
\par
“You will meet Ludovico in a better world, I hope,” added Emily.\par
\par
“Yes—yes,—ma’amselle,” sobbed Annette, “but I hope I shall meet him\par
again in this—though he is so wounded!”\par
\par
“Wounded!” exclaimed Emily, “does he live?”\par
\par
“Yes, ma’am, but—but he has a terrible wound, and could not come to let\par
me out. They thought him dead, at first, and he has not been rightly\par
himself, till within this hour.”\par
\par
“Well, Annette, I rejoice to hear he lives.”\par
\par
“Lives! Holy Saints! why he will not die, surely!”\par
\par
Emily said she hoped not, but this expression of hope Annette thought\par
implied fear, and her own increased in proportion, as Emily endeavoured\par
to encourage her. To enquiries, concerning Madame Montoni, she could\par
give no satisfactory answers.\par
\par
“I quite forgot to ask among the servants, ma’amselle,” said she, “for\par
I could think of nobody but poor Ludovico.”\par
\par
Annette’s grief was now somewhat assuaged, and Emily sent her to make\par
enquiries, concerning her lady, of whom, however, she could obtain no\par
intelligence, some of the people she spoke with being really ignorant\par
of her fate, and others having probably received orders to conceal it.\par
\par
This day passed with Emily in continued grief and anxiety for her aunt;\par
but she was unmolested by any notice from Montoni; and, now that\par
Annette was liberated, she obtained food, without exposing herself to\par
danger, or impertinence.\par
\par
Two following days passed in the same manner, unmarked by any\par
occurrence, during which she obtained no information of Madame Montoni.\par
On the evening of the second, having dismissed Annette, and retired to\par
bed, her mind became haunted by the most dismal images, such as her\par
long anxiety, concerning her aunt, suggested; and, unable to forget\par
herself, for a moment, or to vanquish the phantoms, that tormented her,\par
she rose from her bed, and went to one of the casements of her chamber,\par
to breathe a freer air.\par
\par
All without was silent and dark, unless that could be called light,\par
which was only the faint glimmer of the stars, showing imperfectly the\par
outline of the mountains, the western towers of the castle and the\par
ramparts below, where a solitary sentinel was pacing. What an image of\par
repose did this scene present! The fierce and terrible passions, too,\par
which so often agitated the inhabitants of this edifice, seemed now\par
hushed in sleep;—those mysterious workings, that rouse the elements of\par
man’s nature into tempest—were calm. Emily’s heart was not so; but her\par
sufferings, though deep, partook of the gentle character of her mind.\par
Hers was a silent anguish, weeping, yet enduring; not the wild energy\par
of passion, inflaming imagination, bearing down the barriers of reason\par
and living in a world of its own.\par
\par
The air refreshed her, and she continued at the casement, looking on\par
the shadowy scene, over which the planets burned with a clear light,\par
amid the deep blue æther, as they silently moved in their destined\par
course. She remembered how often she had gazed on them with her dear\par
father, how often he had pointed out their way in the heavens, and\par
explained their laws; and these reflections led to others, which, in an\par
almost equal degree, awakened her grief and astonishment.\par
\par
They brought a retrospect of all the strange and mournful events, which\par
had occurred since she lived in peace with her parents. And to Emily,\par
who had been so tenderly educated, so tenderly loved, who once knew\par
only goodness and happiness—to her, the late events and her present\par
situation—in a foreign land—in a remote castle—surrounded by vice and\par
violence—seemed more like the visions of a distempered imagination,\par
than the circumstances of truth. She wept to think of what her parents\par
would have suffered, could they have foreseen the events of her future\par
life.\par
\par
While she raised her streaming eyes to heaven, she observed the same\par
planet, which she had seen in Languedoc, on the night, preceding her\par
father’s death, rise above the eastern towers of the castle, while she\par
remembered the conversation, which has passed, concerning the probable\par
state of departed souls; remembered, also, the solemn music she had\par
heard, and to which the tenderness of her spirits had, in spite of her\par
reason, given a superstitious meaning. At these recollections she wept\par
again, and continued musing, when suddenly the notes of sweet music\par
passed on the air. A superstitious dread stole over her; she stood\par
listening, for some moments, in trembling expectation, and then\par
endeavoured to recollect her thoughts, and to reason herself into\par
composure; but human reason cannot establish her laws on subjects, lost\par
in the obscurity of imagination, any more than the eye can ascertain\par
the form of objects, that only glimmer through the dimness of night.\par
\par
Her surprise, on hearing such soothing and delicious sounds, was, at\par
least, justifiable; for it was long—very long, since she had listened\par
to anything like melody. The fierce trumpet and the shrill fife were\par
the only instruments she had heard, since her arrival at Udolpho.\par
\par
When her mind was somewhat more composed, she tried to ascertain from\par
what quarter the sounds proceeded, and thought they came from below;\par
but whether from a room of the castle, or from the terrace, she could\par
not with certainty judge. Fear and surprise now yielded to the\par
enchantment of a strain, that floated on the silent night, with the\par
most soft and melancholy sweetness. Suddenly, it seemed removed to a\par
distance, trembled faintly, and then entirely ceased.\par
\par
She continued to listen, sunk in that pleasing repose, which soft music\par
leaves on the mind—but it came no more. Upon this strange circumstance\par
her thoughts were long engaged, for strange it certainly was to hear\par
music at midnight, when every inhabitant of the castle had long since\par
retired to rest, and in a place, where nothing like harmony had been\par
heard before, probably, for many years. Long suffering had made her\par
spirits peculiarly sensible to terror, and liable to be affected by the\par
illusions of superstition.—It now seemed to her, as if her dead father\par
had spoken to her in that strain, to inspire her with comfort and\par
confidence, on the subject, which had then occupied her mind. Yet\par
reason told her, that this was a wild conjecture, and she was inclined\par
to dismiss it; but, with the inconsistency so natural, when imagination\par
guides the thoughts, she then wavered towards a belief as wild. She\par
remembered the singular event, connected with the castle, which had\par
given it into the possession of its present owner; and, when she\par
considered the mysterious manner, in which its late possessor had\par
disappeared, and that she had never since been heard of, her mind was\par
impressed with a high degree of solemn awe; so that, though there\par
appeared no clue to connect that event with the late music, she was\par
inclined fancifully to think they had some relation to each other. At\par
this conjecture, a sudden chillness ran through her frame; she looked\par
fearfully upon the duskiness of her chamber, and the dead silence, that\par
prevailed there, heightened to her fancy its gloomy aspect.\par
\par
At length, she left the casement, but her steps faltered, as she\par
approached the bed, and she stopped and looked round. The single lamp,\par
that burned in her spacious chamber, was expiring; for a moment, she\par
shrunk from the darkness beyond; and then, ashamed of the weakness,\par
which, however, she could not wholly conquer, went forward to the bed,\par
where her mind did not soon know the soothings of sleep. She still\par
mused on the late occurrence, and looked with anxiety to the next\par
night, when, at the same hour, she determined to watch whether the\par
music returned. “If those sounds were human,” said she, “I shall\par
probably hear them again.”\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XII\par
\par
Then, oh, you blessed ministers above,\par
Keep me in patience; and, in ripen’d time,\par
Unfold the evil which is here wrapt up\par
In countenance.\par
                    SHAKESPEARE\par
\par
\par
Annette came almost breathless to Emily’s apartment in the morning. “O\par
ma’amselle!” said she, in broken sentences, “what news I have to tell!\par
I have found out who the prisoner is—but he was no prisoner,\par
neither;—he that was shut up in the chamber I told you of. I must think\par
him a ghost, forsooth!”\par
\par
“Who was the prisoner?” enquired Emily, while her thoughts glanced back\par
to the circumstance of the preceding night.\par
\par
“You mistake, ma’am,” said Annette; “he was not a prisoner, after all.”\par
\par
“Who is the person, then?”\par
\par
“Holy Saints!” rejoined Annette; “How I was surprised! I met him just\par
now, on the rampart below, there. I never was so surprised in my life!\par
Ah! ma’amselle! this is a strange place! I should never have done\par
wondering, if I was to live here a hundred years. But, as I was saying,\par
I met him just now on the rampart, and I was thinking of nobody less\par
than of him.”\par
\par
“This trifling is insupportable,” said Emily; “pr’ythee, Annette, do\par
not torture my patience any longer.”\par
\par
“Nay, ma’amselle, guess—guess who it was; it was somebody you know very\par
well.”\par
\par
“I cannot guess,” said Emily impatiently.\par
\par
“Nay, ma’amselle, I’ll tell you something to guess by—A tall Signor,\par
with a longish face, who walks so stately, and used to wear such a high\par
feather in his hat; and used often to look down upon the ground, when\par
people spoke to him; and to look at people from under his eyebrows, as\par
it were, all so dark and frowning. You have seen him, often and often,\par
at Venice, ma’am. Then he was so intimate with the Signor, too. And,\par
now I think of it, I wonder what he could be afraid of in this lonely\par
old castle, that he should shut himself up for. But he is come abroad\par
now, for I met him on the rampart just this minute. I trembled when I\par
saw him, for I always was afraid of him, somehow; but I determined I\par
would not let him see it; so I went up to him, and made him a low\par
curtesy, ‘You are welcome to the castle, Signor Orsino,’ said I.”\par
\par
“O, it was Signor Orsino, then!” said Emily.\par
\par
“Yes, ma’amselle, Signor Orsino, himself, who caused that Venetian\par
gentleman to be killed, and has been popping about from place to place,\par
ever since, as I hear.”\par
\par
“Good God!” exclaimed Emily, recovering from the shock of this\par
intelligence; “and is _he_ come to Udolpho! He does well to endeavour\par
to conceal himself.”\par
\par
“Yes, ma’amselle, but if that was all, this desolate place would\par
conceal him, without his shutting himself up in one room. Who would\par
think of coming to look for him here? I am sure I should as soon think\par
of going to look for anybody in the other world.”\par
\par
“There is some truth in that,” said Emily, who would now have concluded\par
it was Orsino’s music, which she had heard, on the preceding night, had\par
she not known, that he had neither taste, nor skill in the art. But,\par
though she was unwilling to add to the number of Annette’s surprises,\par
by mentioning the subject of her own, she enquired, whether any person\par
in the castle played on a musical instrument?\par
\par
“O yes, ma’amselle! there is Benedetto plays the great drum to\par
admiration; and then, there is Launcelot the trumpeter; nay, for that\par
matter, Ludovico himself can play on the trumpet;—but he is ill now. I\par
remember once—”\par
\par
Emily interrupted her; “Have you heard no other music since you came to\par
the castle—none last night?”\par
\par
“Why, did _you_ hear any last night, ma’amselle?”\par
\par
Emily evaded this question, by repeating her own.\par
\par
“Why, no, ma’am,” replied Annette; “I never heard any music here, I\par
must say, but the drums and the trumpet; and, as for last night, I did\par
nothing but dream I saw my late lady’s ghost.”\par
\par
“Your _late_ lady’s,” said Emily in a tremulous voice; “you have heard\par
more, then. Tell me—tell me all, Annette, I entreat; tell me the worst\par
at once.”\par
\par
“Nay, ma’amselle, you know the worst already.”\par
\par
“I know nothing,” said Emily.\par
\par
“Yes, you do, ma’amselle; you know, that nobody knows anything about\par
her; and it is plain, therefore, she is gone, the way of the first lady\par
of the castle—nobody ever knew anything about her.”\par
\par
Emily leaned her head upon her hand, and was, for some time, silent;\par
then, telling Annette she wished to be alone, the latter left the room.\par
\par
The remark of Annette had revived Emily’s terrible suspicion,\par
concerning the fate of Madame Montoni; and she resolved to make another\par
effort to obtain certainty on this subject, by applying to Montoni once\par
more.\par
\par
When Annette returned, a few hours after, she told Emily, that the\par
porter of the castle wished very much to speak with her, for that he\par
had something of importance to say; her spirits had, however, of late\par
been so subject to alarm, that any new circumstance excited it; and\par
this message from the porter, when her first surprise was over, made\par
her look round for some lurking danger, the more suspiciously, perhaps,\par
because she had frequently remarked the unpleasant air and countenance\par
of this man. She now hesitated, whether to speak with him, doubting\par
even, that this request was only a pretext to draw her into some\par
danger; but a little reflection showed her the improbability of this,\par
and she blushed at her weak fears.\par
\par
“I will speak to him, Annette,” said she; “desire him to come to the\par
corridor immediately.”\par
\par
Annette departed, and soon after returned.\par
\par
“Barnardine, ma’amselle,” said she, “dare not come to the corridor,\par
lest he should be discovered, it is so far from his post; and he dare\par
not even leave the gates for a moment now; but, if you will come to him\par
at the portal, through some roundabout passages he told me of, without\par
crossing the courts, he has that to tell, which will surprise you. But\par
you must not come through the courts, lest the Signor should see you.”\par
\par
Emily, neither approving these “roundabout passage,” nor the other part\par
of the request, now positively refused to go. “Tell him,” said she, “if\par
he has anything of consequence to impart, I will hear him in the\par
corridor, whenever he has an opportunity of coming thither.”\par
\par
Annette went to deliver this message, and was absent a considerable\par
time. When she returned, “It won’t do, ma’amselle,” said she.\par
“Barnardine has been considering all this time what can be done, for it\par
is as much as his place is worth to leave his post now. But, if you\par
will come to the east rampart in the dusk of the evening, he can,\par
perhaps, steal away, and tell you all he has to say.”\par
\par
Emily was surprised and alarmed, at the secrecy which this man seemed\par
to think so necessary, and hesitated whether to meet him, till,\par
considering, that he might mean to warn her of some serious danger, she\par
resolved to go.\par
\par
“Soon after sunset,” said she, “I will be at the end of the east\par
rampart. But then the watch will be set,” she added, recollecting\par
herself, “and how can Barnardine pass unobserved?”\par
\par
“That is just what I said to him, ma’am, and he answered me, that he\par
had the key of the gate, at the end of the rampart, that leads towards\par
the courts, and could let himself through that way; and as for the\par
sentinels, there were none at this end of the terrace, because the\par
place is guarded enough by the high walls of the castle, and the east\par
turret; and he said those at the other end were too far off to see him,\par
if it was pretty duskyish.”\par
\par
“Well,” said Emily, “I must hear what he has to tell; and, therefore,\par
desire you will go with me to the terrace, this evening.”\par
\par
“He desired it might be pretty duskyish, ma’amselle,” repeated Annette,\par
“because of the watch.”\par
\par
Emily paused, and then said she would be on the terrace, an hour after\par
sunset;—“and tell Barnardine,” she added, “to be punctual to the time;\par
for that I, also, may be observed by Signor Montoni. Where is the\par
Signor? I would speak with him.”\par
\par
“He is in the cedar chamber, ma’am, counselling with the other Signors.\par
He is going to give them a sort of treat today, to make up for what\par
passed at the last, I suppose; the people are all very busy in the\par
kitchen.”\par
\par
Emily now enquired, if Montoni expected any new guests? and Annette\par
believed that he did not. “Poor Ludovico!” added she, “he would be as\par
merry as the best of them, if he was well; but he may recover yet.\par
Count Morano was wounded as bad, as he, and he is got well again, and\par
is gone back to Venice.”\par
\par
“Is he so?” said Emily, “when did you hear this?”\par
\par
“I heard it last night, ma’amselle, but I forgot to tell it.”\par
\par
Emily asked some further questions, and then, desiring Annette would\par
observe and inform her, when Montoni was alone, the girl went to\par
deliver her message to Barnardine.\par
\par
Montoni was, however, so much engaged, during the whole day, that Emily\par
had no opportunity of seeking a release from her terrible suspense,\par
concerning her aunt. Annette was employed in watching his steps, and in\par
attending upon Ludovico, whom she, assisted by Caterina, nursed with\par
the utmost care; and Emily was, of course, left much alone. Her\par
thoughts dwelt often on the message of the porter, and were employed in\par
conjecturing the subject, that occasioned it, which she sometimes\par
imagined concerned the fate of Madame Montoni; at others, that it\par
related to some personal danger, which threatened herself. The cautious\par
secrecy which Barnardine observed in his conduct, inclined her to\par
believe the latter.\par
\par
As the hour of appointment drew near, her impatience increased. At\par
length, the sun set; she heard the passing steps of the sentinels going\par
to their posts; and waited only for Annette to accompany her to the\par
terrace, who, soon after, came, and they descended together. When Emily\par
expressed apprehensions of meeting Montoni, or some of his guests, “O,\par
there is no fear of that, ma’amselle,” said Annette, “they are all set\par
in to feasting yet, and that Barnardine knows.”\par
\par
They reached the first terrace, where the sentinels demanded who\par
passed; and Emily, having answered, walked on to the east rampart, at\par
the entrance of which they were again stopped; and, having again\par
replied, were permitted to proceed. But Emily did not like to expose\par
herself to the discretion of these men, at such an hour; and, impatient\par
to withdraw from the situation, she stepped hastily on in search of\par
Barnardine. He was not yet come. She leaned pensively on the wall of\par
the rampart, and waited for him. The gloom of twilight sat deep on the\par
surrounding objects, blending in soft confusion the valley, the\par
mountains, and the woods, whose tall heads, stirred by the evening\par
breeze, gave the only sounds, that stole on silence, except a faint,\par
faint chorus of distant voices, that arose from within the castle.\par
\par
“What voices are those?” said Emily, as she fearfully listened.\par
\par
“It is only the Signor and his guests, carousing,” replied Annette.\par
\par
“Good God!” thought Emily, “can this man’s heart be so gay, when he has\par
made another being so wretched; if, indeed, my aunt is yet suffered to\par
feel her wretchedness? O! whatever are my own sufferings, may my heart\par
never, never be hardened against those of others!”\par
\par
She looked up, with a sensation of horror, to the east turret, near\par
which she then stood; a light glimmered through the grates of the lower\par
chamber, but those of the upper one were dark. Presently, she perceived\par
a person moving with a lamp across the lower room; but this\par
circumstance revived no hope, concerning Madame Montoni, whom she had\par
vainly sought in that apartment, which had appeared to contain only\par
soldiers’ accoutrements. Emily, however, determined to attempt the\par
outer door of the turret, as soon as Barnardine should withdraw; and,\par
if it was unfastened, to make another effort to discover her aunt.\par
\par
The moments passed, but still Barnardine did not appear; and Emily,\par
becoming uneasy, hesitated whether to wait any longer. She would have\par
sent Annette to the portal to hasten him, but feared to be left alone,\par
for it was now almost dark, and a melancholy streak of red, that still\par
lingered in the west, was the only vestige of departed day. The strong\par
interest, however, which Barnardine’s message had awakened, overcame\par
other apprehensions, and still detained her.\par
\par
While she was conjecturing with Annette what could thus occasion his\par
absence, they heard a key turn in the lock of the gate near them, and\par
presently saw a man advancing. It was Barnardine, of whom Emily hastily\par
enquired what he had to communicate, and desired, that he would tell\par
her quickly, “for I am chilled with this evening air,” said she.\par
\par
“You must dismiss your maid, lady,” said the man in a voice, the deep\par
tone of which shocked her, “what I have to tell is to you only.”\par
\par
Emily, after some hesitation, desired Annette to withdraw to a little\par
distance. “Now, my friend, what would you say?”\par
\par
He was silent a moment, as if considering, and then said,—\par
\par
“That which would cost me my place, at least, if it came to the\par
Signor’s ears. You must promise, lady, that nothing shall ever make you\par
tell a syllable of the matter; I have been trusted in this affair, and,\par
if it was known, that I betrayed my trust, my life, perhaps, might\par
answer it. But I was concerned for you, lady, and I resolved to tell\par
you.” He paused.—\par
\par
Emily thanked him, assured him that he might repose on her discretion,\par
and entreated him to dispatch.\par
\par
“Annette told us in the hall how unhappy you were about Signora\par
Montoni, and how much you wished to know what was become of her.”\par
\par
“Most true,” said Emily eagerly, “and you can inform me. I conjure you\par
tell me the worst, without hesitation.” She rested her trembling arm\par
upon the wall.\par
\par
“I can tell you,” said Barnardine, and paused.—\par
\par
Emily had no power to enforce her entreaties.\par
\par
“I _can_ tell you,” resumed Barnardine,—“but—”\par
\par
“But what?” exclaimed Emily, recovering her resolution.\par
\par
“Here I am, ma’amselle,” said Annette, who, having heard the eager\par
tone, in which Emily pronounced these words, came running towards her.\par
\par
“Retire!” said Barnardine, sternly; “you are not wanted;” and, as Emily\par
said nothing, Annette obeyed.\par
\par
“I _can_ tell you,” repeated the porter,—“but I know not how—you were\par
afflicted before.—”\par
\par
“I am prepared for the worst, my friend,” said Emily, in a firm and\par
solemn voice. “I can support any certainty better than this suspense.”\par
\par
“Well, Signora, if that is the case, you shall hear.—You know, I\par
suppose, that the Signor and his lady used sometimes to disagree. It is\par
none of my concerns to enquire what it was about, but I believe you\par
know it was so.”\par
\par
“Well,” said Emily, “proceed.”\par
\par
“The Signor, it seems, had lately been very wrath against her. I saw\par
all, and heard all,—a great deal more than people thought for; but it\par
was none of my business, so I said nothing. A few days ago, the Signor\par
sent for me. ‘Barnardine,’ says he, ‘you are—an honest man, I think I\par
can trust you.’ I assured his _Excellenza_ that he could. ‘Then,’ says\par
he, as near as I can remember, ‘I have an affair in hand, which I want\par
you to assist me in.’—Then he told me what I was to do; but that I\par
shall say nothing about—it concerned only the Signora.”\par
\par
“O Heavens!” exclaimed Emily—“what have you done?”\par
\par
Barnardine hesitated, and was silent.\par
\par
“What fiend could tempt him, or you, to such an act!” cried Emily,\par
chilled with horror, and scarcely able to support her fainting spirits.\par
\par
“It was a fiend,” said Barnardine in a gloomy tone of voice. They were\par
now both silent;—Emily had not courage to enquire further, and\par
Barnardine seemed to shrink from telling more. At length he said, “It\par
is of no use to think of the past; the Signor was cruel enough, but he\par
would be obeyed. What signified my refusing? He would have found\par
others, who had no scruples.”\par
\par
“You have murdered her, then!” said Emily, in a hollow and inward\par
voice—“I am talking with a murderer!” Barnardine stood silent; while\par
Emily turned from him, and attempted to leave the place.\par
\par
“Stay, lady!” said he, “You deserve to think so still—since you can\par
believe me capable of such a deed.”\par
\par
“If you are innocent, tell me quickly,” said Emily, in faint accents,\par
“for I feel I shall not be able to hear you long.”\par
\par
“I will tell you no more,” said he, and walked away. Emily had just\par
strength enough to bid him stay, and then to call Annette, on whose arm\par
she leaned, and they walked slowly up the rampart, till they heard\par
steps behind them. It was Barnardine again.\par
\par
“Send away the girl,” said he, “and I will tell you more.”\par
\par
“She must not go,” said Emily; “what you have to say, she may hear.”\par
\par
“May she so, lady?” said he. “You shall know no more, then;” and he was\par
going, though slowly, when Emily’s anxiety, overcoming the resentment\par
and fear, which the man’s behaviour had roused, she desired him to\par
stay, and bade Annette retire.\par
\par
“The Signora is alive,” said he, “for me. She is my prisoner, though;\par
his _Excellenza_ has shut her up in the chamber over the great gates of\par
the court, and I have the charge of her. I was going to have told you,\par
you might see her—but now—”\par
\par
Emily, relieved from an unutterable load of anguish by this speech, had\par
now only to ask Barnardine’s forgiveness, and to conjure, that he would\par
let her visit her aunt.\par
\par
He complied with less reluctance, than she expected, and told her,\par
that, if she would repair, on the following night, when the Signor was\par
retired to rest, to the postern-gate of the castle, she should,\par
perhaps, see Madame Montoni.\par
\par
Amid all the thankfulness, which Emily felt for this concession, she\par
thought she observed a malicious triumph in his manner, when he\par
pronounced the last words; but, in the next moment, she dismissed the\par
thought, and, having again thanked him, commended her aunt to his pity,\par
and assured him, that she would herself reward him, and would be\par
punctual to her appointment, she bade him good night, and retired,\par
unobserved, to her chamber. It was a considerable time, before the\par
tumult of joy, which Barnardine’s unexpected intelligence had\par
occasioned, allowed Emily to think with clearness, or to be conscious\par
of the real dangers, that still surrounded Madame Montoni and herself.\par
When this agitation subsided, she perceived, that her aunt was yet the\par
prisoner of a man, to whose vengeance, or avarice, she might fall a\par
sacrifice; and, when she further considered the savage aspect of the\par
person, who was appointed to guard Madame Montoni, her doom appeared to\par
be already sealed, for the countenance of Barnardine seemed to bear the\par
stamp of a murderer; and, when she had looked upon it, she felt\par
inclined to believe, that there was no deed, however black, which he\par
might not be prevailed upon to execute. These reflections brought to\par
her remembrance the tone of voice, in which he had promised to grant\par
her request to see his prisoner; and she mused upon it long in\par
uneasiness and doubt. Sometimes, she even hesitated, whether to trust\par
herself with him at the lonely hour he had appointed; and once, and\par
only once, it struck her, that Madame Montoni might be already\par
murdered, and that this ruffian was appointed to decoy herself to some\par
secret place, where her life also was to be sacrificed to the avarice\par
of Montoni, who then would claim securely the contested estates in\par
Languedoc. The consideration of the enormity of such guilt did, at\par
length, relieve her from the belief of its probability, but not from\par
all the doubts and fears, which a recollection of Barnardine’s manner\par
had occasioned. From these subjects, her thoughts, at length, passed to\par
others; and, as the evening advanced, she remembered, with somewhat\par
more than surprise, the music she had heard, on the preceding night,\par
and now awaited its return, with more than curiosity.\par
\par
She distinguished, till a late hour, the distant carousals of Montoni\par
and his companions—the loud contest, the dissolute laugh and the choral\par
song, that made the halls re-echo. At length, she heard the heavy gates\par
of the castle shut for the night, and those sounds instantly sunk into\par
a silence, which was disturbed only by the whispering steps of persons,\par
passing through the galleries to their remote rooms. Emily now judging\par
it to be about the time, when she had heard the music, on the preceding\par
night, dismissed Annette, and gently opened the casement to watch for\par
its return. The planet she had so particularly noticed, at the\par
recurrence of the music, was not yet risen; but, with superstitious\par
weakness, she kept her eyes fixed on that part of the hemisphere, where\par
it would rise, almost expecting, that, when it appeared, the sounds\par
would return. At length, it came, serenely bright, over the eastern\par
towers of the castle. Her heart trembled, when she perceived it, and\par
she had scarcely courage to remain at the casement, lest the returning\par
music should confirm her terror, and subdue the little strength she yet\par
retained. The clock soon after struck one, and, knowing this to be\par
about the time, when the sounds had occurred, she sat down in a chair,\par
near the casement, and endeavoured to compose her spirits; but the\par
anxiety of expectation yet disturbed them. Everything, however,\par
remained still; she heard only the solitary step of a sentinel, and the\par
lulling murmur of the woods below, and she again leaned from the\par
casement, and again looked, as if for intelligence, to the planet,\par
which was now risen high above the towers.\par
\par
Emily continued to listen, but no music came. “Those were surely no\par
mortal sounds!” said she, recollecting their entrancing melody. “No\par
inhabitant of this castle could utter such; and, where is the feeling,\par
that could modulate such exquisite expression? We all know, that it has\par
been affirmed celestial sounds have sometimes been heard on earth.\par
Father Pierre and Father Antoine declared, that they had sometimes\par
heard them in the stillness of night, when they alone were waking to\par
offer their orisons to heaven. Nay, my dear father himself, once said,\par
that, soon after my mother’s death, as he lay watchful in grief, sounds\par
of uncommon sweetness called him from his bed; and, on opening his\par
window, he heard lofty music pass along the midnight air. It soothed\par
him, he said; he looked up with confidence to heaven, and resigned her\par
to his God.”\par
\par
Emily paused to weep at this recollection. “Perhaps,” resumed she,\par
“perhaps, those strains I heard were sent to comfort,—to encourage me!\par
Never shall I forget those I heard, at this hour, in Languedoc!\par
Perhaps, my father watches over me, at this moment!” She wept again in\par
tenderness. Thus passed the hour in watchfulness and solemn thought;\par
but no sounds returned; and, after remaining at the casement, till the\par
light tint of dawn began to edge the mountain-tops and steal upon the\par
night-shade, she concluded, that they would not return, and retired\par
reluctantly to repose.\par
\par
\par
\par
VOLUME 3\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER I\par
\par
I will advise you where to plant yourselves;\par
Acquaint you with the perfect spy o’ the time,\par
The moment on ’t; for ’t must be done tonight.\par
                    MACBETH\par
\par
\par
Emily was somewhat surprised, on the following day, to find that\par
Annette had heard of Madame Montoni’s confinement in the chamber over\par
the portal, as well as of her purposed visit there, on the approaching\par
night. That the circumstance, which Barnardine had so solemnly enjoined\par
her to conceal, he had himself told to so indiscreet a hearer as\par
Annette, appeared very improbable, though he had now charged her with a\par
message, concerning the intended interview. He requested, that Emily\par
would meet him, unattended, on the terrace, at a little after midnight,\par
when he himself would lead her to the place he had promised; a\par
proposal, from which she immediately shrunk, for a thousand vague fears\par
darted athwart her mind, such as had tormented her on the preceding\par
night, and which she neither knew how to trust, nor to dismiss. It\par
frequently occurred to her, that Barnardine might have deceived her,\par
concerning Madame Montoni, whose murderer, perhaps, he really was; and\par
that he had deceived her by order of Montoni, the more easily to draw\par
her into some of the desperate designs of the latter. The terrible\par
suspicion, that Madame Montoni no longer lived, thus came, accompanied\par
by one not less dreadful for herself. Unless the crime, by which the\par
aunt had suffered, was instigated merely by resentment, unconnected\par
with profit, a motive, upon which Montoni did not appear very likely to\par
act, its object must be unattained, till the niece was also dead, to\par
whom Montoni knew that his wife’s estates must descend. Emily\par
remembered the words, which had informed her, that the contested\par
estates in France would devolve to her, if Madame Montoni died, without\par
consigning them to her husband, and the former obstinate perseverance\par
of her aunt made it too probable, that she had, to the last, withheld\par
them. At this instant, recollecting Barnardine’s manner, on the\par
preceding night, she now believed, what she had then fancied, that it\par
expressed malignant triumph. She shuddered at the recollection, which\par
confirmed her fears, and determined not to meet him on the terrace.\par
Soon after, she was inclined to consider these suspicions as the\par
extravagant exaggerations of a timid and harassed mind, and could not\par
believe Montoni liable to such preposterous depravity as that of\par
destroying, from one motive, his wife and her niece. She blamed herself\par
for suffering her romantic imagination to carry her so far beyond the\par
bounds of probability, and determined to endeavour to check its rapid\par
flights, lest they should sometimes extend into madness. Still,\par
however, she shrunk from the thought of meeting Barnardine, on the\par
terrace, at midnight; and still the wish to be relieved from this\par
terrible suspense, concerning her aunt, to see her, and to sooth her\par
sufferings, made her hesitate what to do.\par
\par
“Yet how is it possible, Annette, I can pass to the terrace at that\par
hour?” said she, recollecting herself, “the sentinels will stop me, and\par
Signor Montoni will hear of the affair.”\par
\par
“O ma’amselle! that is well thought of,” replied Annette. “That is what\par
Barnardine told me about. He gave me this key, and bade me say it\par
unlocks the door at the end of the vaulted gallery, that opens near the\par
end of the east rampart, so that you need not pass any of the men on\par
watch. He bade me say, too, that his reason for requesting you to come\par
to the terrace was, because he could take you to the place you want to\par
go to, without opening the great doors of the hall, which grate so\par
heavily.”\par
\par
Emily’s spirits were somewhat calmed by this explanation, which seemed\par
to be honestly given to Annette. “But why did he desire I would come\par
alone, Annette?” said she.\par
\par
“Why that was what I asked him myself, ma’amselle. Says I, ‘Why is my\par
young lady to come alone?—Surely I may come with her!—What harm can _I_\par
do?’ But he said ‘No—no—I tell you not,’ in his gruff way. Nay, says I,\par
I have been trusted in as great affairs as this, I warrant, and it’s a\par
hard matter if I can’t keep a secret now. Still he would say nothing\par
but—‘No—no—no.’ Well, says I, if you will only trust me, I will tell\par
you a great secret, that was told me a month ago, and I have never\par
opened my lips about it yet—so you need not be afraid of telling me.\par
But all would not do. Then, ma’amselle, I went so far as to offer him a\par
beautiful new sequin, that Ludovico gave me for a keepsake, and I would\par
not have parted with it for all St. Marco’s Place; but even that would\par
not do! Now what can be the reason of this? But I know, you know,\par
ma’am, who you are going to see.”\par
\par
“Pray did Barnardine tell you this?”\par
\par
“He! No, ma’amselle, that he did not.”\par
\par
Emily enquired who did, but Annette showed, that she _could_ keep a\par
secret.\par
\par
During the remainder of the day, Emily’s mind was agitated with doubts\par
and fears and contrary determinations, on the subject of meeting this\par
Barnardine on the rampart, and submitting herself to his guidance, she\par
scarcely knew whither. Pity for her aunt and anxiety for herself\par
alternately swayed her determination, and night came, before she had\par
decided upon her conduct. She heard the castle clock strike\par
eleven—twelve—and yet her mind wavered. The time, however, was now\par
come, when she could hesitate no longer: and then the interest she felt\par
for her aunt overcame other considerations, and, bidding Annette follow\par
her to the outer door of the vaulted gallery, and there await her\par
return, she descended from her chamber. The castle was perfectly still,\par
and the great hall, where so lately she had witnessed a scene of\par
dreadful contention, now returned only the whispering footsteps of the\par
two solitary figures gliding fearfully between the pillars, and gleamed\par
only to the feeble lamp they carried. Emily, deceived by the long\par
shadows of the pillars and by the catching lights between, often\par
stopped, imagining she saw some person, moving in the distant obscurity\par
of the perspective; and, as she passed these pillars, she feared to\par
turn her eyes toward them, almost expecting to see a figure start out\par
from behind their broad shaft. She reached, however, the vaulted\par
gallery, without interruption, but unclosed its outer door with a\par
trembling hand, and, charging Annette not to quit it and to keep it a\par
little open, that she might be heard if she called, she delivered to\par
her the lamp, which she did not dare to take herself because of the men\par
on watch, and, alone, stepped out upon the dark terrace. Everything was\par
so still, that she feared, lest her own light steps should be heard by\par
the distant sentinels, and she walked cautiously towards the spot,\par
where she had before met Barnardine, listening for a sound, and looking\par
onward through the gloom in search of him. At length, she was startled\par
by a deep voice, that spoke near her, and she paused, uncertain whether\par
it was his, till it spoke again, and she then recognised the hollow\par
tones of Barnardine, who had been punctual to the moment, and was at\par
the appointed place, resting on the rampart wall. After chiding her for\par
not coming sooner, and saying that he had been waiting nearly half an\par
hour, he desired Emily, who made no reply, to follow him to the door\par
through which he had entered the terrace.\par
\par
While he unlocked it, she looked back to that she had left, and,\par
observing the rays of the lamp stream through a small opening, was\par
certain, that Annette was still there. But her remote situation could\par
little befriend Emily, after she had quitted the terrace; and, when\par
Barnardine unclosed the gate, the dismal aspect of the passage beyond,\par
shown by a torch burning on the pavement, made her shrink from\par
following him alone, and she refused to go, unless Annette might\par
accompany her. This, however, Barnardine absolutely refused to permit,\par
mingling at the same time with his refusal such artful circumstances to\par
heighten the pity and curiosity of Emily towards her aunt, that she, at\par
length, consented to follow him alone to the portal.\par
\par
He then took up the torch, and led her along the passage, at the\par
extremity of which he unlocked another door, whence they descended, a\par
few steps, into a chapel, which, as Barnardine held up the torch to\par
light her, Emily observed to be in ruins, and she immediately\par
recollected a former conversation of Annette, concerning it, with very\par
unpleasant emotions. She looked fearfully on the almost roofless walls,\par
green with damps, and on the gothic points of the windows, where the\par
ivy and the briony had long supplied the place of glass, and ran\par
mantling among the broken capitals of some columns, that had once\par
supported the roof. Barnardine stumbled over the broken pavement, and\par
his voice, as he uttered a sudden oath, was returned in hollow echoes,\par
that made it more terrific. Emily’s heart sunk; but she still followed\par
him, and he turned out of what had been the principal aisle of the\par
chapel. “Down these steps, lady,” said Barnardine, as he descended a\par
flight, which appeared to lead into the vaults; but Emily paused on the\par
top, and demanded, in a tremulous tone, whither he was conducting her.\par
\par
“To the portal,” said Barnardine.\par
\par
“Cannot we go through the chapel to the portal?” said Emily.\par
\par
“No, Signora, that leads to the inner court, which I don’t choose to\par
unlock. This way, and we shall reach the outer court presently.”\par
\par
Emily still hesitated; fearing not only to go on, but, since she had\par
gone thus far, to irritate Barnardine by refusing to go further.\par
\par
“Come, lady,” said the man, who had nearly reached the bottom of the\par
flight, “make a little haste; I cannot wait here all night.”\par
\par
“Whither do these steps lead?” said Emily, yet pausing.\par
\par
“To the portal,” repeated Barnardine, in an angry tone, “I will wait no\par
longer.” As he said this, he moved on with the light, and Emily,\par
fearing to provoke him by further delay, reluctantly followed. From the\par
steps, they proceeded through a passage, adjoining the vaults, the\par
walls of which were dropping with unwholesome dews, and the vapours,\par
that crept along the ground, made the torch burn so dimly, that Emily\par
expected every moment to see it extinguished, and Barnardine could\par
scarcely find his way. As they advanced, these vapours thickened, and\par
Barnardine, believing the torch was expiring, stopped for a moment to\par
trim it. As he then rested against a pair of iron gates, that opened\par
from the passage, Emily saw, by uncertain flashes of light, the vaults\par
beyond, and, near her, heaps of earth, that seemed to surround an open\par
grave. Such an object, in such a scene, would, at any time, have\par
disturbed her; but now she was shocked by an instantaneous\par
presentiment, that this was the grave of her unfortunate aunt, and that\par
the treacherous Barnardine was leading herself to destruction. The\par
obscure and terrible place, to which he had conducted her, seemed to\par
justify the thought; it was a place suited for murder, a receptacle for\par
the dead, where a deed of horror might be committed, and no vestige\par
appear to proclaim it. Emily was so overwhelmed with terror, that, for\par
a moment, she was unable to determine what conduct to pursue. She then\par
considered that it would be vain to attempt an escape from Barnardine\par
by flight, since the length and the intricacy of the way she had passed\par
would soon enable him to overtake her, who was unacquainted with the\par
turnings, and whose feebleness would not suffer her to run long with\par
swiftness. She feared equally to irritate him by a disclosure of her\par
suspicions, which a refusal to accompany him further certainly would\par
do; and, since she was already as much in his power as it was possible\par
she could be, if she proceeded, she, at length, determined to suppress,\par
as far as she could, the appearance of apprehension, and to follow\par
silently whither he designed to lead her. Pale with horror and anxiety,\par
she now waited till Barnardine had trimmed the torch, and, as her sight\par
glanced again upon the grave, she could not forbear enquiring, for whom\par
it was prepared. He took his eyes from the torch, and fixed them upon\par
her face without speaking. She faintly repeated the question, but the\par
man, shaking the torch, passed on; and she followed, trembling, to a\par
second flight of steps, having ascended which, a door delivered them\par
into the first court of the castle. As they crossed it, the light\par
showed the high black walls around them, fringed with long grass and\par
dank weeds, that found a scanty soil among the mouldering stones; the\par
heavy buttresses, with, here and there, between them, a narrow grate,\par
that admitted a freer circulation of air to the court, the massy iron\par
gates, that led to the castle, whose clustering turrets appeared above,\par
and, opposite, the huge towers and arch of the portal itself. In this\par
scene the large, uncouth person of Barnardine, bearing the torch,\par
formed a characteristic figure. This Barnardine was wrapt in a long\par
dark cloak, which scarcely allowed the kind of half-boots, or sandals,\par
that were laced upon his legs, to appear, and showed only the point of\par
a broad sword, which he usually wore, slung in a belt across his\par
shoulders. On his head was a heavy flat velvet cap, somewhat resembling\par
a turban, in which was a short feather; the visage beneath it showed\par
strong features, and a countenance furrowed with the lines of cunning\par
and darkened by habitual discontent.\par
\par
The view of the court, however, reanimated Emily, who, as she crossed\par
silently towards the portal, began to hope, that her own fears, and not\par
the treachery of Barnardine, had deceived her. She looked anxiously up\par
at the first casement, that appeared above the lofty arch of the\par
portcullis; but it was dark, and she enquired, whether it belonged to\par
the chamber where Madame Montoni was confined. Emily spoke low, and\par
Barnardine, perhaps, did not hear her question, for he returned no\par
answer; and they, soon after, entered the postern door of the gateway,\par
which brought them to the foot of a narrow staircase that wound up one\par
of the towers.\par
\par
“Up this staircase the Signora lies,” said Barnardine.\par
\par
“Lies!” repeated Emily faintly, as she began to ascend.\par
\par
“She lies in the upper chamber,” said Barnardine.\par
\par
As they passed up, the wind, which poured through the narrow cavities\par
in the wall, made the torch flare, and it threw a stronger gleam upon\par
the grim and sallow countenance of Barnardine, and discovered more\par
fully the desolation of the place—the rough stone walls, the spiral\par
stairs, black with age, and a suit of ancient armour, with an iron\par
visor, that hung upon the walls, and appeared a trophy of some former\par
victory.\par
\par
Having reached a landing-place, “You may wait here, lady,” said he,\par
applying a key to the door of a chamber, “while I go up, and tell the\par
Signora you are coming.”\par
\par
“That ceremony is unnecessary,” replied Emily, “my aunt will rejoice to\par
see me.”\par
\par
“I am not so sure of that,” said Barnardine, pointing to the room he\par
had opened: “Come in here, lady, while I step up.”\par
\par
Emily, surprised and somewhat shocked, did not dare to oppose him\par
further, but, as he was turning away with the torch, desired he would\par
not leave her in darkness. He looked around, and, observing a tripod\par
lamp, that stood on the stairs, lighted and gave it to Emily, who\par
stepped forward into a large old chamber, and he closed the door. As\par
she listened anxiously to his departing steps, she thought he\par
descended, instead of ascending, the stairs; but the gusts of wind,\par
that whistled round the portal, would not allow her to hear distinctly\par
any other sound. Still, however, she listened, and, perceiving no step\par
in the room above, where he had affirmed Madame Montoni to be, her\par
anxiety increased, though she considered, that the thickness of the\par
floor in this strong building might prevent any sound reaching her from\par
the upper chamber. The next moment, in a pause of the wind, she\par
distinguished Barnardine’s step descending to the court, and then\par
thought she heard his voice; but, the rising gust again overcoming\par
other sounds, Emily, to be certain on this point, moved softly to the\par
door, which, on attempting to open it, she discovered was fastened. All\par
the horrid apprehensions, that had lately assailed her, returned at\par
this instant with redoubled force, and no longer appeared like the\par
exaggerations of a timid spirit, but seemed to have been sent to warn\par
her of her fate. She now did not doubt, that Madame Montoni had been\par
murdered, perhaps in this very chamber; or that she herself was brought\par
hither for the same purpose. The countenance, the manners and the\par
recollected words of Barnardine, when he had spoken of her aunt,\par
confirmed her worst fears. For some moments, she was incapable of\par
considering of any means, by which she might attempt an escape. Still\par
she listened, but heard footsteps neither on the stairs, nor in the\par
room above; she thought, however, that she again distinguished\par
Barnardine’s voice below, and went to a grated window, that opened upon\par
the court, to enquire further. Here, she plainly heard his hoarse\par
accents, mingling with the blast, that swept by, but they were lost\par
again so quickly, that their meaning could not be interpreted; and then\par
the light of a torch, which seemed to issue from the portal below,\par
flashed across the court, and the long shadow of a man, who was under\par
the arch-way, appeared upon the pavement. Emily, from the hugeness of\par
this sudden portrait, concluded it to be that of Barnardine; but other\par
deep tones, which passed in the wind, soon convinced her he was not\par
alone, and that his companion was not a person very liable to pity.\par
\par
When her spirits had overcome the first shock of her situation, she\par
held up the lamp to examine, if the chamber afforded a possibility of\par
an escape. It was a spacious room, whose walls, wainscoted with rough\par
oak, showed no casement but the grated one, which Emily had left, and\par
no other door than that, by which she had entered. The feeble rays of\par
the lamp, however, did not allow her to see at once its full extent;\par
she perceived no furniture, except, indeed, an iron chair, fastened in\par
the centre of the chamber, immediately over which, depending on a chain\par
from the ceiling, hung an iron ring. Having gazed upon these, for some\par
time, with wonder and horror, she next observed iron bars below, made\par
for the purpose of confining the feet, and on the arms of the chair\par
were rings of the same metal. As she continued to survey them, she\par
concluded, that they were instruments of torture, and it struck her,\par
that some poor wretch had once been fastened in this chair, and had\par
there been starved to death. She was chilled by the thought; but, what\par
was her agony, when, in the next moment, it occurred to her, that her\par
aunt might have been one of these victims, and that she herself might\par
be the next! An acute pain seized her head, she was scarcely able to\par
hold the lamp, and, looking round for support, was seating herself,\par
unconsciously, in the iron chair itself; but suddenly perceiving where\par
she was, she started from it in horror, and sprung towards a remote end\par
of the room. Here again she looked round for a seat to sustain her, and\par
perceived only a dark curtain, which, descending from the ceiling to\par
the floor, was drawn along the whole side of the chamber. Ill as she\par
was, the appearance of this curtain struck her, and she paused to gaze\par
upon it, in wonder and apprehension.\par
\par
It seemed to conceal a recess of the chamber; she wished, yet dreaded,\par
to lift it, and to discover what it veiled: twice she was withheld by a\par
recollection of the terrible spectacle her daring hand had formerly\par
unveiled in an apartment of the castle, till, suddenly conjecturing,\par
that it concealed the body of her murdered aunt, she seized it, in a\par
fit of desperation, and drew it aside. Beyond, appeared a corpse,\par
stretched on a kind of low couch, which was crimsoned with human blood,\par
as was the floor beneath. The features, deformed by death, were ghastly\par
and horrible, and more than one livid wound appeared in the face.\par
Emily, bending over the body, gazed, for a moment, with an eager,\par
frenzied eye; but, in the next, the lamp dropped from her hand, and she\par
fell senseless at the foot of the couch.\par
\par
When her senses returned, she found herself surrounded by men, among\par
whom was Barnardine, who were lifting her from the floor, and then bore\par
her along the chamber. She was sensible of what passed, but the extreme\par
languor of her spirits did not permit her to speak, or move, or even to\par
feel any distinct fear. They carried her down the staircase, by which\par
she had ascended; when, having reached the arch-way, they stopped, and\par
one of the men, taking the torch from Barnardine, opened a small door,\par
that was cut in the great gate, and, as he stepped out upon the road,\par
the light he bore showed several men on horseback, in waiting. Whether\par
it was the freshness of the air, that revived Emily, or that the\par
objects she now saw roused the spirit of alarm, she suddenly spoke, and\par
made an ineffectual effort to disengage herself from the grasp of the\par
ruffians, who held her.\par
\par
Barnardine, meanwhile, called loudly for the torch, while distant\par
voices answered, and several persons approached, and, in the same\par
instant, a light flashed upon the court of the castle. Again he\par
vociferated for the torch, and the men hurried Emily through the gate.\par
At a short distance, under the shelter of the castle walls, she\par
perceived the fellow, who had taken the light from the porter, holding\par
it to a man, busily employed in altering the saddle of a horse, round\par
which were several horsemen, looking on, whose harsh features received\par
the full glare of the torch; while the broken ground beneath them, the\par
opposite walls, with the tufted shrubs, that overhung their summits,\par
and an embattled watch-tower above, were reddened with the gleam,\par
which, fading gradually away, left the remoter ramparts and the woods\par
below to the obscurity of night.\par
\par
“What do you waste time for, there?” said Barnardine with an oath, as\par
he approached the horsemen. “Dispatch—dispatch!”\par
\par
“The saddle will be ready in a minute,” replied the man who was\par
buckling it, at whom Barnardine now swore again, for his negligence,\par
and Emily, calling feebly for help, was hurried towards the horses,\par
while the ruffians disputed on which to place her, the one designed for\par
her not being ready. At this moment a cluster of lights issued from the\par
great gates, and she immediately heard the shrill voice of Annette\par
above those of several other persons, who advanced. In the same moment,\par
she distinguished Montoni and Cavigni, followed by a number of\par
ruffian-faced fellows, to whom she no longer looked with terror, but\par
with hope, for, at this instant, she did not tremble at the thought of\par
any dangers, that might await her within the castle, whence so lately,\par
and so anxiously she had wished to escape. Those, which threatened her\par
from without, had engrossed all her apprehensions.\par
\par
A short contest ensued between the parties, in which that of Montoni,\par
however, were presently victors, and the horsemen, perceiving that\par
numbers were against them, and being, perhaps, not very warmly\par
interested in the affair they had undertaken, galloped off, while\par
Barnardine had run far enough to be lost in the darkness, and Emily was\par
led back into the castle. As she repassed the courts, the remembrance\par
of what she had seen in the portal-chamber came, with all its horror,\par
to her mind; and when, soon after, she heard the gate close, that shut\par
her once more within the castle walls, she shuddered for herself, and,\par
almost forgetting the danger she had escaped, could scarcely think,\par
that anything less precious than liberty and peace was to be found\par
beyond them.\par
\par
Montoni ordered Emily to await him in the cedar parlour, whither he\par
soon followed, and then sternly questioned her on this mysterious\par
affair. Though she now viewed him with horror, as the murderer of her\par
aunt, and scarcely knew what she said in reply to his impatient\par
enquiries, her answers and her manner convinced him, that she had not\par
taken a voluntary part in the late scheme, and he dismissed her upon\par
the appearance of his servants, whom he had ordered to attend, that he\par
might enquire further into the affair, and discover those, who had been\par
accomplices in it.\par
\par
Emily had been some time in her apartment, before the tumult of her\par
mind allowed her to remember several of the past circumstances. Then,\par
again, the dead form, which the curtain in the portal-chamber had\par
disclosed, came to her fancy, and she uttered a groan, which terrified\par
Annette the more, as Emily forbore to satisfy her curiosity, on the\par
subject of it, for she feared to trust her with so fatal a secret, lest\par
her indiscretion should call down the immediate vengeance of Montoni on\par
herself.\par
\par
Thus compelled to bear within her own mind the whole horror of the\par
secret, that oppressed it, her reason seemed to totter under the\par
intolerable weight. She often fixed a wild and vacant look on Annette,\par
and, when she spoke, either did not hear her, or answered from the\par
purpose. Long fits of abstraction succeeded; Annette spoke repeatedly,\par
but her voice seemed not to make any impression on the sense of the\par
long agitated Emily, who sat fixed and silent, except that, now and\par
then, she heaved a heavy sigh, but without tears.\par
\par
Terrified at her condition, Annette, at length, left the room, to\par
inform Montoni of it, who had just dismissed his servants, without\par
having made any discoveries on the subject of his enquiry. The wild\par
description, which this girl now gave of Emily, induced him to follow\par
her immediately to the chamber.\par
\par
At the sound of his voice, Emily turned her eyes, and a gleam of\par
recollection seemed to shoot athwart her mind, for she immediately rose\par
from her seat, and moved slowly to a remote part of the room. He spoke\par
to her in accents somewhat softened from their usual harshness, but she\par
regarded him with a kind of half curious, half terrified look, and\par
answered only “yes,” to whatever he said. Her mind still seemed to\par
retain no other impression, than that of fear.\par
\par
Of this disorder Annette could give no explanation, and Montoni, having\par
attempted, for some time, to persuade Emily to talk, retired, after\par
ordering Annette to remain with her, during the night, and to inform\par
him, in the morning, of her condition.\par
\par
When he was gone, Emily again came forward, and asked who it was, that\par
had been there to disturb her. Annette said it was the Signor—Signor\par
Montoni. Emily repeated the name after her, several times, as if she\par
did not recollect it, and then suddenly groaned, and relapsed into\par
abstraction.\par
\par
With some difficulty, Annette led her to the bed, which Emily examined\par
with an eager, frenzied eye, before she lay down, and then, pointing,\par
turned with shuddering emotion, to Annette, who, now more terrified,\par
went towards the door, that she might bring one of the female servants\par
to pass the night with them; but Emily, observing her going, called her\par
by name, and then in the naturally soft and plaintive tone of her\par
voice, begged, that she, too, would not forsake her.—“For since my\par
father died,” added she, sighing, “everybody forsakes me.”\par
\par
“Your father, ma’amselle!” said Annette, “he was dead before you knew\par
me.”\par
\par
“He was, indeed!” rejoined Emily, and her tears began to flow. She now\par
wept silently and long, after which, becoming quite calm, she at length\par
sunk to sleep, Annette having had discretion enough not to interrupt\par
her tears. This girl, as affectionate as she was simple, lost in these\par
moments all her former fears of remaining in the chamber, and watched\par
alone by Emily, during the whole night.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER II\par
\par
unfold\par
What worlds, or what vast regions, hold\par
Th’ immortal mind, that hath forsook\par
Her mansion in this fleshly nook!\par
                    IL PENSEROSO\par
\par
\par
Emily’s mind was refreshed by sleep. On waking in the morning, she\par
looked with surprise on Annette, who sat sleeping in a chair beside the\par
bed, and then endeavoured to recollect herself; but the circumstances\par
of the preceding night were swept from her memory, which seemed to\par
retain no trace of what had passed, and she was still gazing with\par
surprise on Annette, when the latter awoke.\par
\par
“O dear ma’amselle! do you know me?” cried she.\par
\par
“Know you! Certainly,” replied Emily, “you are Annette; but why are you\par
sitting by me thus?”\par
\par
“O you have been very ill, ma’amselle,—very ill indeed! and I am sure I\par
thought—”\par
\par
“This is very strange!” said Emily, still trying to recollect the\par
past.—“But I think I do remember, that my fancy has been haunted by\par
frightful dreams. Good God!” she added, suddenly starting—“surely it\par
was nothing more than a dream!”\par
\par
She fixed a terrified look upon Annette, who, intending to quiet her,\par
said “Yes, ma’amselle, it was more than a dream, but it is all over\par
now.”\par
\par
“She _is_ murdered, then!” said Emily in an inward voice, and\par
shuddering instantaneously. Annette screamed; for, being ignorant of\par
the circumstance to which Emily referred, she attributed her manner to\par
a disordered fancy; but, when she had explained to what her own speech\par
alluded, Emily, recollecting the attempt that had been made to carry\par
her off, asked if the contriver of it had been discovered. Annette\par
replied, that he had not, though he might easily be guessed at; and\par
then told Emily she might thank her for her deliverance, who,\par
endeavouring to command the emotion, which the remembrance of her aunt\par
had occasioned, appeared calmly to listen to Annette, though, in truth,\par
she heard scarcely a word that was said.\par
\par
“And so, ma’amselle,” continued the latter, “I was determined to be\par
even with Barnardine for refusing to tell me the secret, by finding it\par
out myself; so I watched you, on the terrace, and, as soon as he had\par
opened the door at the end, I stole out from the castle, to try to\par
follow you; for, says I, I am sure no good can be planned, or why all\par
this secrecy? So, sure enough, he had not bolted the door after him,\par
and, when I opened it, I saw, by the glimmer of the torch, at the other\par
end of the passage, which way you were going. I followed the light, at\par
a distance, till you came to the vaults of the chapel, and there I was\par
afraid to go further, for I had heard strange things about these\par
vaults. But then, again, I was afraid to go back, all in darkness, by\par
myself; so by the time Barnardine had trimmed the light, I had resolved\par
to follow you, and I did so, till you came to the great court, and\par
there I was afraid he would see me; so I stopped at the door again, and\par
watched you across to the gates, and, when you were gone up the stairs,\par
I whipt after. There, as I stood under the gateway, I heard horses’\par
feet without, and several men talking; and I heard them swearing at\par
Barnardine for not bringing you out, and just then, he had like to have\par
caught me, for he came down the stairs again, and I had hardly time to\par
get out of his way. But I had heard enough of his secret now, and I\par
determined to be even with him, and to save you, too, ma’amselle, for I\par
guessed it to be some new scheme of Count Morano, though he was gone\par
away. I ran into the castle, but I had hard work to find my way through\par
the passage under the chapel, and what is very strange, I quite forgot\par
to look for the ghosts they had told me about, though I would not go\par
into that place again by myself for all the world! Luckily the Signor\par
and Signor Cavigni were up, so we had soon a train at our heels,\par
sufficient to frighten that Barnardine and his rogues, all together.”\par
\par
Annette ceased to speak, but Emily still appeared to listen. At length\par
she said, suddenly, “I think I will go to him myself;—where is he?”\par
\par
Annette asked who was meant.\par
\par
“Signor Montoni,” replied Emily. “I would speak with him;” and Annette,\par
now remembering the order he had given, on the preceding night,\par
respecting her young lady, rose, and said she would seek him herself.\par
\par
This honest girl’s suspicions of Count Morano were perfectly just;\par
Emily, too, when she thought on the scheme, had attributed it to him;\par
and Montoni, who had not a doubt on this subject, also, began to\par
believe, that it was by the direction of Morano, that poison had\par
formerly been mingled with his wine.\par
\par
The professions of repentance, which Morano had made to Emily, under\par
the anguish of his wound, was sincere at the moment he offered them;\par
but he had mistaken the subject of his sorrow, for, while he thought he\par
was condemning the cruelty of his late design, he was lamenting only\par
the state of suffering, to which it had reduced him. As these\par
sufferings abated, his former views revived, till, his health being\par
re-established, he again found himself ready for enterprise and\par
difficulty. The porter of the castle, who had served him, on a former\par
occasion, willingly accepted a second bribe; and, having concerted the\par
means of drawing Emily to the gates, Morano publicly left the hamlet,\par
whither he had been carried after the affray, and withdrew with his\par
people to another at several miles distance. From thence, on a night\par
agreed upon by Barnardine, who had discovered from the thoughtless\par
prattle of Annette, the most probable means of decoying Emily, the\par
Count sent back his servants to the castle, while he awaited her\par
arrival at the hamlet, with an intention of carrying her immediately to\par
Venice. How this, his second scheme, was frustrated, has already\par
appeared; but the violent, and various passions with which this Italian\par
lover was now agitated, on his return to that city, can only be\par
imagined.\par
\par
Annette having made her report to Montoni of Emily’s health and of her\par
request to see him, he replied, that she might attend him in the cedar\par
room, in about an hour. It was on the subject, that pressed so heavily\par
on her mind, that Emily wished to speak to him, yet she did not\par
distinctly know what good purpose this could answer, and sometimes she\par
even recoiled in horror from the expectation of his presence. She\par
wished, also, to petition, though she scarcely dared to believe the\par
request would be granted, that he would permit her, since her aunt was\par
no more, to return to her native country.\par
\par
As the moment of interview approached, her agitation increased so much,\par
that she almost resolved to excuse herself under what could scarcely be\par
called a pretence of illness; and, when she considered what could be\par
said, either concerning herself, or the fate of her aunt, she was\par
equally hopeless as to the event of her entreaty, and terrified as to\par
its effect upon the vengeful spirit of Montoni. Yet, to pretend\par
ignorance of her death, appeared, in some degree, to be sharing its\par
criminality, and, indeed, this event was the only ground, on which\par
Emily could rest her petition for leaving Udolpho.\par
\par
While her thoughts thus wavered, a message was brought, importing, that\par
Montoni could not see her, till the next day; and her spirits were then\par
relieved, for a moment, from an almost intolerable weight of\par
apprehension. Annette said, she fancied the Chevaliers were going out\par
to the wars again, for the courtyard was filled with horses, and she\par
heard, that the rest of the party, who went out before, were expected\par
at the castle. “And I heard one of the soldiers, too,” added she, “say\par
to his comrade, that he would warrant they’d bring home a rare deal of\par
booty.—So, thinks I, if the Signor can, with a safe conscience, send\par
his people out a-robbing—why it is no business of mine. I only wish I\par
was once safe out of this castle; and, if it had not been for poor\par
Ludovico’s sake, I would have let Count Morano’s people run away with\par
us both, for it would have been serving you a good turn, ma’amselle, as\par
well as myself.”\par
\par
Annette might have continued thus talking for hours for any\par
interruption she would have received from Emily, who was silent,\par
inattentive, absorbed in thought, and passed the whole of this day in a\par
kind of solemn tranquillity, such as is often the result of faculties\par
overstrained by suffering.\par
\par
When night returned, Emily recollected the mysterious strains of music,\par
that she had lately heard, in which she still felt some degree of\par
interest, and of which she hoped to hear again the soothing sweetness.\par
The influence of superstition now gained on the weakness of her\par
long-harassed mind; she looked, with enthusiastic expectation, to the\par
guardian spirit of her father, and, having dismissed Annette for the\par
night, determined to watch alone for their return. It was not yet,\par
however, near the time when she had heard the music on a former night,\par
and anxious to call off her thoughts from distressing subjects, she sat\par
down with one of the few books, that she had brought from France; but\par
her mind, refusing control, became restless and agitated, and she went\par
often to the casement to listen for a sound. Once, she thought she\par
heard a voice, but then, everything without the casement remaining\par
still, she concluded, that her fancy had deceived her.\par
\par
Thus passed the time, till twelve o’clock, soon after which the distant\par
sounds, that murmured through the castle, ceased, and sleep seemed to\par
reign over all. Emily then seated herself at the casement, where she\par
was soon recalled from the reverie, into which she sunk, by very\par
unusual sounds, not of music, but like the low mourning of some person\par
in distress. As she listened, her heart faltered in terror, and she\par
became convinced, that the former sound was more than imaginary. Still,\par
at intervals, she heard a kind of feeble lamentation, and sought to\par
discover whence it came. There were several rooms underneath, adjoining\par
the rampart, which had been long shut up, and, as the sound probably\par
rose from one of these, she leaned from the casement to observe,\par
whether any light was visible there. The chambers, as far as she could\par
perceive, were quite dark, but, at a little distance, on the rampart\par
below, she thought she saw something moving.\par
\par
The faint twilight, which the stars shed, did not enable her to\par
distinguish what it was; but she judged it to be a sentinel, on watch,\par
and she removed her light to a remote part of the chamber, that she\par
might escape notice, during her further observation.\par
\par
The same object still appeared. Presently, it advanced along the\par
rampart, towards her window, and she then distinguished something like\par
a human form, but the silence, with which it moved, convinced her it\par
was no sentinel. As it drew near, she hesitated whether to retire; a\par
thrilling curiosity inclined her to stay, but a dread of she scarcely\par
knew what warned her to withdraw.\par
\par
While she paused, the figure came opposite to her casement, and was\par
stationary. Everything remained quiet; she had not heard even a\par
foot-fall; and the solemnity of this silence, with the mysterious form\par
she saw, subdued her spirits, so that she was moving from the casement,\par
when, on a sudden, she observed the figure start away, and glide down\par
the rampart, after which it was soon lost in the obscurity of night.\par
Emily continued to gaze, for some time, on the way it had passed, and\par
then retired within her chamber, musing on this strange circumstance,\par
and scarcely doubting, that she had witnessed a supernatural\par
appearance.\par
\par
When her spirits recovered composure, she looked round for some other\par
explanation. Remembering what she had heard of the daring enterprises\par
of Montoni, it occurred to her, that she had just seen some unhappy\par
person, who, having been plundered by his banditti, was brought hither\par
a captive; and that the music she had formerly heard, came from him.\par
Yet, if they had plundered him, it still appeared improbable, that they\par
should have brought him to the castle, and it was also more consistent\par
with the manners of banditti to murder those they rob, than to make\par
them prisoners. But what, more than any other circumstance,\par
contradicted the supposition, that it was a prisoner, was that it\par
wandered on the terrace, without a guard: a consideration, which made\par
her dismiss immediately her first surmise.\par
\par
Afterwards, she was inclined to believe, that Count Morano had obtained\par
admittance into the castle; but she soon recollected the difficulties\par
and dangers, that must have opposed such an enterprise, and that, if he\par
had so far succeeded, to come alone and in silence to her casement at\par
midnight was not the conduct he would have adopted, particularly since\par
the private staircase, communicating with her apartment, was known to\par
him; neither would he have uttered the dismal sounds she had heard.\par
\par
Another suggestion represented, that this might be some person, who had\par
designs upon the castle; but the mournful sounds destroyed, also, that\par
probability. Thus, enquiry only perplexed her. Who, or what, it could\par
be that haunted this lonely hour, complaining in such doleful accents\par
and in such sweet music (for she was still inclined to believe, that\par
the former strains and the late appearance were connected), she had no\par
means of ascertaining; and imagination again assumed her empire, and\par
roused the mysteries of superstition.\par
\par
She determined, however, to watch on the following night, when her\par
doubts might, perhaps, be cleared up; and she almost resolved to\par
address the figure, if it should appear again.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER III\par
\par
Such are those thick and gloomy shadows damp,\par
Oft seen in charnel-vaults and sepulchres,\par
Lingering, and sitting, by a new-made grave.\par
                    MILTON\par
\par
\par
On the following day, Montoni sent a second excuse to Emily, who was\par
surprised at the circumstance. “This is very strange!” said she to\par
herself. “His conscience tells him the purport of my visit, and he\par
defers it, to avoid an explanation.” She now almost resolved to throw\par
herself in his way, but terror checked the intention, and this day\par
passed, as the preceding one, with Emily, except that a degree of awful\par
expectation, concerning the approaching night, now somewhat disturbed\par
the dreadful calmness that had pervaded her mind.\par
\par
Towards evening, the second part of the band, which had made the first\par
excursion among the mountains, returned to the castle, where, as they\par
entered the courts, Emily, in her remote chamber, heard their loud\par
shouts and strains of exultation, like the orgies of furies over some\par
horrid sacrifice. She even feared they were about to commit some\par
barbarous deed; a conjecture from which, however, Annette soon relieved\par
her, by telling, that the people were only exulting over the plunder\par
they had brought with them. This circumstance still further confirmed\par
her in the belief, that Montoni had really commenced to be a captain of\par
banditti, and meant to retrieve his broken fortunes by the plunder of\par
travellers! Indeed, when she considered all the circumstances of his\par
situation—in an armed, and almost inaccessible castle, retired far\par
among the recesses of wild and solitary mountains, along whose distant\par
skirts were scattered towns, and cities, whither wealthy travellers\par
were continually passing—this appeared to be the situation of all\par
others most suited for the success of schemes of rapine, and she\par
yielded to the strange thought, that Montoni was become a captain of\par
robbers. His character also, unprincipled, dauntless, cruel and\par
enterprising, seemed to fit him for the situation. Delighting in the\par
tumult and in the struggles of life, he was equally a stranger to pity\par
and to fear; his very courage was a sort of animal ferocity; not the\par
noble impulse of a principle, such as inspirits the mind against the\par
oppressor, in the cause of the oppressed; but a constitutional\par
hardiness of nerve, that cannot feel, and that, therefore, cannot fear.\par
\par
Emily’s supposition, however natural, was in part erroneous, for she\par
was a stranger to the state of this country and to the circumstances,\par
under which its frequent wars were partly conducted. The revenues of\par
the many states of Italy being, at that time, insufficient to the\par
support of standing armies, even during the short periods, which the\par
turbulent habits both of the governments and the people permitted to\par
pass in peace, an order of men arose not known in our age, and but\par
faintly described in the history of their own. Of the soldiers,\par
disbanded at the end of every war, few returned to the safe, but\par
unprofitable occupations, then usual in peace. Sometimes they passed\par
into other countries, and mingled with armies, which still kept the\par
field. Sometimes they formed themselves into bands of robbers, and\par
occupied remote fortresses, where their desperate character, the\par
weakness of the governments which they offended, and the certainty,\par
that they could be recalled to the armies, when their presence should\par
be again wanted, prevented them from being much pursued by the civil\par
power; and, sometimes, they attached themselves to the fortunes of a\par
popular chief, by whom they were led into the service of any state,\par
which could settle with him the price of their valour. From this latter\par
practice arose their name—_Condottieri_; a term formidable all over\par
Italy, for a period, which concluded in the earlier part of the\par
seventeenth century, but of which it is not so easy to ascertain the\par
commencement.\par
\par
Contests between the smaller states were then, for the most part,\par
affairs of enterprise alone, and the probabilities of success were\par
estimated, not from the skill, but from the personal courage of the\par
general, and the soldiers. The ability, which was necessary to the\par
conduct of tedious operations, was little valued. It was enough to know\par
how a party might be led towards their enemies, with the greatest\par
secrecy, or conducted from them in the compactest order. The officer\par
was to precipitate himself into a situation, where, but for his\par
example, the soldiers might not have ventured; and, as the opposed\par
parties knew little of each other’s strength, the event of the day was\par
frequently determined by the boldness of the first movements. In such\par
services the _Condottieri_ were eminent, and in these, where plunder\par
always followed success, their characters acquired a mixture of\par
intrepidity and profligacy, which awed even those whom they served.\par
\par
When they were not thus engaged, their chief had usually his own\par
fortress, in which, or in its neighbourhood, they enjoyed an irksome\par
rest; and, though their wants were, at one time, partly supplied from\par
the property of the inhabitants, the lavish distribution of their\par
plunder at others, prevented them from being obnoxious; and the\par
peasants of such districts gradually shared the character of their\par
warlike visitors. The neighbouring governments sometimes professed, but\par
seldom endeavoured, to suppress these military communities; both\par
because it was difficult to do so, and because a disguised protection\par
of them ensured, for the service of their wars, a body of men, who\par
could not otherwise be so cheaply maintained, or so perfectly\par
qualified. The commanders sometimes even relied so far upon this policy\par
of the several powers, as to frequent their capitals; and Montoni,\par
having met them in the gaming parties of Venice and Padua, conceived a\par
desire to emulate their characters, before his ruined fortunes tempted\par
him to adopt their practices. It was for the arrangement of his present\par
plan of life, that the midnight councils were held at his mansion in\par
Venice, and at which Orsino and some other members of the present\par
community then assisted with suggestions, which they had since executed\par
with the wreck of their fortunes.\par
\par
On the return of night, Emily resumed her station at the casement.\par
There was now a moon; and, as it rose over the tufted woods, its yellow\par
light served to show the lonely terrace and the surrounding objects,\par
more distinctly, than the twilight of the stars had done, and promised\par
Emily to assist her observations, should the mysterious form return. On\par
this subject, she again wavered in conjecture, and hesitated whether to\par
speak to the figure, to which a strong and almost irresistible interest\par
urged her; but terror, at intervals, made her reluctant to do so.\par
\par
“If this is a person who has designs upon the castle,” said she, “my\par
curiosity may prove fatal to me; yet the mysterious music, and the\par
lamentations I heard, must surely have proceeded from him: if so, he\par
cannot be an enemy.”\par
\par
She then thought of her unfortunate aunt, and, shuddering with grief\par
and horror, the suggestions of imagination seized her mind with all the\par
force of truth, and she believed, that the form she had seen was\par
supernatural. She trembled, breathed with difficulty, an icy coldness\par
touched her cheeks, and her fears for a while overcame her judgment.\par
Her resolution now forsook her, and she determined, if the figure\par
should appear, not to speak to it.\par
\par
Thus the time passed, as she sat at her casement, awed by expectation,\par
and by the gloom and stillness of midnight; for she saw obscurely in\par
the moonlight only the mountains and woods, a cluster of towers, that\par
formed the west angle of the castle, and the terrace below; and heard\par
no sound, except, now and then, the lonely watch-word, passed by the\par
sentinels on duty, and afterwards the steps of the men who came to\par
relieve guard, and whom she knew at a distance on the rampart by their\par
pikes, that glittered in the moonbeam, and then, by the few short\par
words, in which they hailed their fellows of the night. Emily retired\par
within her chamber, while they passed the casement. When she returned\par
to it, all was again quiet. It was now very late, she was wearied with\par
watching, and began to doubt the reality of what she had seen on the\par
preceding night; but she still lingered at the window, for her mind was\par
too perturbed to admit of sleep. The moon shone with a clear lustre,\par
that afforded her a complete view of the terrace; but she saw only a\par
solitary sentinel, pacing at one end of it; and, at length, tired with\par
expectation, she withdrew to seek rest.\par
\par
Such, however, was the impression, left on her mind by the music, and\par
the complaining she had formerly heard, as well as by the figure, which\par
she fancied she had seen, that she determined to repeat the watch, on\par
the following night.\par
\par
Montoni, on the next day, took no notice of Emily’s appointed visit,\par
but she, more anxious than before to see him, sent Annette to enquire,\par
at what hour he would admit her. He mentioned eleven o’clock, and Emily\par
was punctual to the moment; at which she called up all her fortitude to\par
support the shock of his presence and the dreadful recollections it\par
enforced. He was with several of his officers, in the cedar room; on\par
observing whom she paused; and her agitation increased, while he\par
continued to converse with them, apparently not observing her, till\par
some of his officers, turning round, saw Emily, and uttered an\par
exclamation. She was hastily retiring, when Montoni’s voice arrested\par
her, and, in a faultering accent, she said,—“I would speak with you,\par
Signor Montoni, if you are at leisure.”\par
\par
“These are my friends,” he replied, “whatever you would say, they may\par
hear.”\par
\par
Emily, without replying, turned from the rude gaze of the chevaliers,\par
and Montoni then followed her to the hall, whence he led her to a small\par
room, of which he shut the door with violence. As she looked on his\par
dark countenance, she again thought she saw the murderer of her aunt;\par
and her mind was so convulsed with horror, that she had not power to\par
recall thought enough to explain the purport of her visit; and to trust\par
herself with the mention of Madame Montoni was more than she dared.\par
\par
Montoni at length impatiently enquired what she had to say? “I have no\par
time for trifling,” he added, “my moments are important.”\par
\par
Emily then told him, that she wished to return to France, and came to\par
beg, that he would permit her to do so.—But when he looked surprised,\par
and enquired for the motive of the request, she hesitated, became paler\par
than before, trembled, and had nearly sunk at his feet. He observed her\par
emotion, with apparent indifference, and interrupted the silence by\par
telling her, he must be gone. Emily, however, recalled her spirits\par
sufficiently to enable her to repeat her request. And, when Montoni\par
absolutely refused it, her slumbering mind was roused.\par
\par
“I can no longer remain here with propriety, sir,” said she, “and I may\par
be allowed to ask, by what right you detain me.”\par
\par
“It is my will that you remain here,” said Montoni, laying his hand on\par
the door to go; “let that suffice you.”\par
\par
Emily, considering that she had no appeal from this will, forbore to\par
dispute his right, and made a feeble effort to persuade him to be just.\par
“While my aunt lived, sir,” said she, in a tremulous voice, “my\par
residence here was not improper; but now, that she is no more, I may\par
surely be permitted to depart. My stay cannot benefit you, sir, and\par
will only distress me.”\par
\par
“Who told you, that Madame Montoni was dead?” said Montoni, with an\par
inquisitive eye. Emily hesitated, for nobody had told her so, and she\par
did not dare to avow the having seen that spectacle in the\par
portal-chamber, which had compelled her to the belief.\par
\par
“Who told you so?” he repeated, more sternly.\par
\par
“Alas! I know it too well,” replied Emily: “spare me on this terrible\par
subject!”\par
\par
She sat down on a bench to support herself.\par
\par
“If you wish to see her,” said Montoni, “you may; she lies in the east\par
turret.”\par
\par
He now left the room, without awaiting her reply, and returned to the\par
cedar chamber, where such of the chevaliers as had not before seen\par
Emily, began to rally him, on the discovery they had made; but Montoni\par
did not appear disposed to bear this mirth, and they changed the\par
subject.\par
\par
Having talked with the subtle Orsino, on the plan of an excursion,\par
which he meditated for a future day, his friend advised, that they\par
should lie in wait for the enemy, which Verezzi impetuously opposed,\par
reproached Orsino with want of spirit, and swore, that, if Montoni\par
would let him lead on fifty men, he would conquer all that should\par
oppose him.\par
\par
Orsino smiled contemptuously; Montoni smiled too, but he also listened.\par
Verezzi then proceeded with vehement declamation and assertion, till he\par
was stopped by an argument of Orsino, which he knew not how to answer\par
better than by invective. His fierce spirit detested the cunning\par
caution of Orsino, whom he constantly opposed, and whose inveterate,\par
though silent, hatred he had long ago incurred. And Montoni was a calm\par
observer of both, whose different qualifications he knew, and how to\par
bend their opposite character to the perfection of his own designs. But\par
Verezzi, in the heat of opposition, now did not scruple to accuse\par
Orsino of cowardice, at which the countenance of the latter, while he\par
made no reply, was overspread with a livid paleness; and Montoni, who\par
watched his lurking eye, saw him put his hand hastily into his bosom.\par
But Verezzi, whose face, glowing with crimson, formed a striking\par
contrast to the complexion of Orsino, remarked not the action, and\par
continued boldly declaiming against cowards to Cavigni, who was slily\par
laughing at his vehemence, and at the silent mortification of Orsino,\par
when the latter, retiring a few steps behind, drew forth a stilletto to\par
stab his adversary in the back. Montoni arrested his half-extended arm,\par
and, with a significant look, made him return the poniard into his\par
bosom, unseen by all except himself; for most of the party were\par
disputing at a distant window, on the situation of a dell where they\par
meant to form an ambuscade.\par
\par
When Verezzi had turned round, the deadly hatred, expressed on the\par
features of his opponent, raising, for the first time, a suspicion of\par
his intention, he laid his hand on his sword, and then, seeming to\par
recollect himself, strode up to Montoni.\par
\par
“Signor,” said he, with a significant look at Orsino, “we are not a\par
band of assassins; if you have business for brave men, employ me on\par
this expedition: you shall have the last drop of my blood; if you have\par
only work for cowards—keep him,” pointing to Orsino, “and let me quit\par
Udolpho.”\par
\par
Orsino, still more incensed, again drew forth his stilletto, and rushed\par
towards Verezzi, who, at the same instant, advanced with his sword,\par
when Montoni and the rest of the party interfered and separated them.\par
\par
“This is the conduct of a boy,” said Montoni to Verezzi, “not of a man:\par
be more moderate in your speech.”\par
\par
“Moderation is the virtue of cowards,” retorted Verezzi; “they are\par
moderate in everything—but in fear.”\par
\par
“I accept your words,” said Montoni, turning upon him with a fierce and\par
haughty look, and drawing his sword out of the scabbard.\par
\par
“With all my heart,” cried Verezzi, “though I did not mean them for\par
you.”\par
\par
He directed a pass at Montoni; and, while they fought, the villain\par
Orsino made another attempt to stab Verezzi, and was again prevented.\par
\par
The combatants were, at length, separated; and, after a very long and\par
violent dispute, reconciled. Montoni then left the room with Orsino,\par
whom he detained in private consultation for a considerable time.\par
\par
Emily, meanwhile, stunned by the last words of Montoni, forgot, for the\par
moment, his declaration, that she should continue in the castle, while\par
she thought of her unfortunate aunt, who, he had said, was laid in the\par
east turret. In suffering the remains of his wife to lie thus long\par
unburied, there appeared a degree of brutality more shocking than she\par
had suspected even Montoni could practise.\par
\par
After a long struggle, she determined to accept his permission to visit\par
the turret, and to take a last look of her ill-fated aunt: with which\par
design she returned to her chamber, and, while she waited for Annette\par
to accompany her, endeavoured to acquire fortitude sufficient to\par
support her through the approaching scene; for, though she trembled to\par
encounter it, she knew that to remember the performance of this last\par
act of duty would hereafter afford her consoling satisfaction.\par
\par
Annette came, and Emily mentioned her purpose, from which the former\par
endeavoured to dissuade her, though without effect, and Annette was,\par
with much difficulty, prevailed upon to accompany her to the turret;\par
but no consideration could make her promise to enter the chamber of\par
death.\par
\par
They now left the corridor, and, having reached the foot of the\par
staircase, which Emily had formerly ascended, Annette declared she\par
would go no further, and Emily proceeded alone. When she saw the track\par
of blood, which she had before observed, her spirits fainted, and,\par
being compelled to rest on the stairs, she almost determined to proceed\par
no further. The pause of a few moments restored her resolution, and she\par
went on.\par
\par
As she drew near the landing-place, upon which the upper chamber\par
opened, she remembered, that the door was formerly fastened, and\par
apprehended, that it might still be so. In this expectation, however,\par
she was mistaken; for the door opened at once, into a dusky and silent\par
chamber, round which she fearfully looked, and then slowly advanced,\par
when a hollow voice spoke. Emily, who was unable to speak, or to move\par
from the spot, uttered no sound of terror. The voice spoke again; and,\par
then, thinking that it resembled that of Madame Montoni, Emily’s\par
spirits were instantly roused; she rushed towards a bed, that stood in\par
a remote part of the room, and drew aside the curtains. Within,\par
appeared a pale and emaciated face. She started back, then again\par
advanced, shuddered as she took up the skeleton hand, that lay\par
stretched upon the quilt; then let it drop, and then viewed the face\par
with a long, unsettled gaze. It was that of Madame Montoni, though so\par
changed by illness, that the resemblance of what it had been, could\par
scarcely be traced in what it now appeared. She was still alive, and,\par
raising her heavy eyes, she turned them on her niece.\par
\par
“Where have you been so long?” said she, in the same tone, “I thought\par
you had forsaken me.”\par
\par
“Do you indeed live,” said Emily, at length, “or is this but a terrible\par
apparition?” She received no answer, and again she snatched up the\par
hand. “This is substance,” she exclaimed, “but it is cold—cold as\par
marble!” She let it fall. “O, if you really live, speak!” said Emily,\par
in a voice of desperation, “that I may not lose my senses—say you know\par
me!”\par
\par
“I do live,” replied Madame Montoni, “but—I feel that I am about to\par
die.”\par
\par
Emily clasped the hand she held, more eagerly, and groaned. They were\par
both silent for some moments. Then Emily endeavoured to soothe her, and\par
enquired what had reduced her to this present deplorable state.\par
\par
Montoni, when he removed her to the turret under the improbable\par
suspicion of having attempted his life, had ordered the men employed on\par
the occasion, to observe a strict secrecy concerning her. To this he\par
was influenced by a double motive. He meant to debar her from the\par
comfort of Emily’s visits, and to secure an opportunity of privately\par
dispatching her, should any new circumstances occur to confirm the\par
present suggestions of his suspecting mind. His consciousness of the\par
hatred he deserved it was natural enough should at first lead him to\par
attribute to her the attempt that had been made upon his life; and,\par
though there was no other reason to believe that she was concerned in\par
that atrocious design, his suspicions remained; he continued to confine\par
her in the turret, under a strict guard; and, without pity or remorse,\par
had suffered her to lie, forlorn and neglected, under a raging fever,\par
till it had reduced her to the present state.\par
\par
The track of blood, which Emily had seen on the stairs, had flowed from\par
the unbound wound of one of the men employed to carry Madame Montoni,\par
and which he had received in the late affray. At night these men,\par
having contented themselves with securing the door of their prisoner’s\par
room, had retired from guard; and then it was, that Emily, at the time\par
of her first enquiry, had found the turret so silent and deserted.\par
\par
When she had attempted to open the door of the chamber, her aunt was\par
sleeping, and this occasioned the silence, which had contributed to\par
delude her into a belief, that she was no more; yet had her terror\par
permitted her to persevere longer in the call, she would probably have\par
awakened Madame Montoni, and have been spared much suffering. The\par
spectacle in the portal-chamber, which afterwards confirmed Emily’s\par
horrible suspicion, was the corpse of a man, who had fallen in the\par
affray, and the same which had been borne into the servants’ hall,\par
where she took refuge from the tumult. This man had lingered under his\par
wounds for some days; and, soon after his death, his body had been\par
removed on the couch, on which he died, for interment in the vault\par
beneath the chapel, through which Emily and Barnardine had passed to\par
the chamber.\par
\par
Emily, after asking Madame Montoni a thousand questions concerning\par
herself, left her, and sought Montoni; for the more solemn interest she\par
felt for her aunt, made her now regardless of the resentment her\par
remonstrances might draw upon herself, and of the improbability of his\par
granting what she meant to entreat.\par
\par
“Madame Montoni is now dying, sir,” said Emily, as soon as she saw\par
him—“Your resentment, surely will not pursue her to the last moment!\par
Suffer her to be removed from that forlorn room to her own apartment,\par
and to have necessary comforts administered.”\par
\par
“Of what service will that be, if she is dying?” said Montoni, with\par
apparent indifference.\par
\par
“The service, at leave, of saving you, sir, from a few of those pangs\par
of conscience you must suffer, when you shall be in the same\par
situation,” said Emily, with imprudent indignation, of which Montoni\par
soon made her sensible, by commanding her to quit his presence. Then,\par
forgetting her resentment, and impressed only by compassion for the\par
piteous state of her aunt, dying without succour, she submitted to\par
humble herself to Montoni, and to adopt every persuasive means, that\par
might induce him to relent towards his wife.\par
\par
For a considerable time he was proof against all she said, and all she\par
looked; but at length the divinity of pity, beaming in Emily’s eyes,\par
seemed to touch his heart. He turned away, ashamed of his better\par
feelings, half sullen and half relenting; but finally consented, that\par
his wife should be removed to her own apartment, and that Emily should\par
attend her. Dreading equally, that this relief might arrive too late,\par
and that Montoni might retract his concession, Emily scarcely staid to\par
thank him for it, but, assisted by Annette, she quickly prepared Madame\par
Montoni’s bed, and they carried her a cordial, that might enable her\par
feeble frame to sustain the fatigue of a removal.\par
\par
Madame was scarcely arrived in her own apartment, when an order was\par
given by her husband, that she should remain in the turret; but Emily,\par
thankful that she had made such dispatch, hastened to inform him of it,\par
as well as that a second removal would instantly prove fatal, and he\par
suffered his wife to continue where she was.\par
\par
During this day, Emily never left Madame Montoni, except to prepare\par
such little nourishing things as she judged necessary to sustain her,\par
and which Madame Montoni received with quiet acquiescence, though she\par
seemed sensible that they could not save her from approaching\par
dissolution, and scarcely appeared to wish for life. Emily meanwhile\par
watched over her with the most tender solicitude, no longer seeing her\par
imperious aunt in the poor object before her, but the sister of her\par
late beloved father, in a situation that called for all her compassion\par
and kindness. When night came, she determined to sit up with her aunt,\par
but this the latter positively forbade, commanding her to retire to\par
rest, and Annette alone to remain in her chamber. Rest was, indeed,\par
necessary to Emily, whose spirits and frame were equally wearied by the\par
occurrences and exertions of the day; but she would not leave Madame\par
Montoni, till after the turn of midnight, a period then thought so\par
critical by the physicians.\par
\par
Soon after twelve, having enjoined Annette to be wakeful, and to call\par
her, should any change appear for the worse, Emily sorrowfully bade\par
Madame Montoni good night, and withdrew to her chamber. Her spirits\par
were more than usually depressed by the piteous condition of her aunt,\par
whose recovery she scarcely dared to expect. To her own misfortunes she\par
saw no period, inclosed as she was, in a remote castle, beyond the\par
reach of any friends, had she possessed such, and beyond the pity even\par
of strangers; while she knew herself to be in the power of a man\par
capable of any action, which his interest, or his ambition, might\par
suggest.\par
\par
Occupied by melancholy reflections and by anticipations as sad, she did\par
not retire immediately to rest, but leaned thoughtfully on her open\par
casement. The scene before her of woods and mountains, reposing in the\par
moonlight, formed a regretted contrast with the state of her mind; but\par
the lonely murmur of these woods, and the view of this sleeping\par
landscape, gradually soothed her emotions and softened her to tears.\par
\par
She continued to weep, for some time, lost to everything, but to a\par
gentle sense of her misfortunes. When she, at length, took the\par
handkerchief from her eyes, she perceived, before her, on the terrace\par
below, the figure she had formerly observed, which stood fixed and\par
silent, immediately opposite to her casement. On perceiving it, she\par
started back, and terror for some time overcame curiosity;—at length,\par
she returned to the casement, and still the figure was before it, which\par
she now compelled herself to observe, but was utterly unable to speak,\par
as she had formerly intended. The moon shone with a clear light, and it\par
was, perhaps, the agitation of her mind, that prevented her\par
distinguishing, with any degree of accuracy, the form before her. It\par
was still stationary, and she began to doubt, whether it was really\par
animated.\par
\par
Her scattered thoughts were now so far returned as to remind her, that\par
her light exposed her to dangerous observation, and she was stepping\par
back to remove it, when she perceived the figure move, and then wave\par
what seemed to be its arm, as if to beckon her; and, while she gazed,\par
fixed in fear, it repeated the action. She now attempted to speak, but\par
the words died on her lips, and she went from the casement to remove\par
her light; as she was doing which, she heard, from without, a faint\par
groan. Listening, but not daring to return, she presently heard it\par
repeated.\par
\par
“Good God!—what can this mean!” said she.\par
\par
Again she listened, but the sound came no more; and, after a long\par
interval of silence, she recovered courage enough to go to the\par
casement, when she again saw the same appearance! It beckoned again,\par
and again uttered a low sound.\par
\par
“That groan was surely human!” said she. “I _will_ speak.” “Who is it,”\par
cried Emily in a faint voice, “that wanders at this late hour?”\par
\par
The figure raised its head but suddenly started away, and glided down\par
the terrace. She watched it, for a long while, passing swiftly in the\par
moonlight, but heard no footstep, till a sentinel from the other\par
extremity of the rampart walked slowly along. The man stopped under her\par
window, and, looking up, called her by name. She was retiring\par
precipitately, but, a second summons inducing her to reply, the soldier\par
then respectfully asked if she had seen anything pass. On her\par
answering, that she had; he said no more, but walked away down the\par
terrace, Emily following him with her eyes, till he was lost in the\par
distance. But, as he was on guard, she knew he could not go beyond the\par
rampart, and, therefore, resolved to await his return.\par
\par
Soon after, his voice was heard, at a distance, calling loudly; and\par
then a voice still more distant answered, and, in the next moment, the\par
watch-word was given, and passed along the terrace. As the soldiers\par
moved hastily under the casement, she called to enquire what had\par
happened, but they passed without regarding her.\par
\par
Emily’s thoughts returning to the figure she had seen, “It cannot be a\par
person, who has designs upon the castle,” said she; “such a one would\par
conduct himself very differently. He would not venture where sentinels\par
were on watch, nor fix himself opposite to a window, where he perceived\par
he must be observed; much less would he beckon, or utter a sound of\par
complaint. Yet it cannot be a prisoner, for how could he obtain the\par
opportunity to wander thus?”\par
\par
If she had been subject to vanity, she might have supposed this figure\par
to be some inhabitant of the castle, who wandered under her casement in\par
the hope of seeing her, and of being allowed to declare his admiration;\par
but this opinion never occurred to Emily, and, if it had, she would\par
have dismissed it as improbable, on considering, that, when the\par
opportunity of speaking had occurred, it had been suffered to pass in\par
silence; and that, even at the moment in which she had spoken, the form\par
had abruptly quitted the place.\par
\par
While she mused, two sentinels walked up the rampart in earnest\par
conversation, of which she caught a few words, and learned from these,\par
that one of their comrades had fallen down senseless. Soon after, three\par
other soldiers appeared slowly advancing from the bottom of the\par
terrace, but she heard only a low voice, that came at intervals. As\par
they drew near, she perceived this to be the voice of him, who walked\par
in the middle, apparently supported by his comrades; and she again\par
called to them, enquiring what had happened. At the sound of her voice,\par
they stopped, and looked up, while she repeated her question, and was\par
told, that Roberto, their fellow of the watch, had been seized with a\par
fit, and that his cry, as he fell, had caused a false alarm.\par
\par
“Is he subject to fits?” said Emily.\par
\par
“Yes, Signora,” replied Roberto; “but if I had not, what I saw was\par
enough to have frightened the Pope himself.”\par
\par
“What was it?” enquired Emily, trembling.\par
\par
“I cannot tell what it was, lady, or what I saw, or how it vanished,”\par
replied the soldier, who seemed to shudder at the recollection.\par
\par
“Was it the person, whom you followed down the rampart, that has\par
occasioned you this alarm?” said Emily, endeavouring to conceal her\par
own.\par
\par
“Person!” exclaimed the man,—“it was the devil, and this is not the\par
first time I have seen him!”\par
\par
“Nor will it be the last,” observed one of his comrades, laughing.\par
\par
“No, no, I warrant not,” said another.\par
\par
“Well,” rejoined Roberto, “you may be as merry now, as you please; you\par
were none so jocose the other night, Sebastian, when you were on watch\par
with Launcelot.”\par
\par
“Launcelot need not talk of that,” replied Sebastian, “let him remember\par
how he stood trembling, and unable to give the _word_, till the man was\par
gone. If the man had not come so silently upon us, I would have seized\par
him, and soon made him tell who he was.”\par
\par
“What man?” enquired Emily.\par
\par
“It was no man, lady,” said Launcelot, who stood by, “but the devil\par
himself, as my comrade says. What man, who does not live in the castle,\par
could get within the walls at midnight? Why, I might just as well\par
pretend to march to Venice, and get among all the Senators, when they\par
are counselling; and I warrant I should have more chance of getting out\par
again alive, than any fellow, that we should catch within the gates\par
after dark. So I think I have proved plainly enough, that this can be\par
nobody that lives out of the castle; and now I will prove, that it can\par
be nobody that lives in the castle—for, if he did—why should he be\par
afraid to be seen? So after this, I hope nobody will pretend to tell me\par
it was anybody. No, I say again, by holy Pope! it was the devil, and\par
Sebastian, there, knows this is not the first time we have seen him.”\par
\par
“When did you see the figure, then, before?” said Emily half smiling,\par
who, though she thought the conversation somewhat too much, felt an\par
interest, which would not permit her to conclude it.\par
\par
“About a week ago, lady,” said Sebastian, taking up the story.\par
\par
“And where?”\par
\par
“On the rampart, lady, higher up.”\par
\par
“Did you pursue it, that it fled?”\par
\par
“No, Signora. Launcelot and I were on watch together, and everything\par
was so still, you might have heard a mouse stir, when, suddenly,\par
Launcelot says—Sebastian! do you see nothing? I turned my head a little\par
to the left, as it might be—thus. No, says I. Hush! said\par
Launcelot,—look yonder—just by the last cannon on the rampart! I\par
looked, and then thought I did see something move; but there being no\par
light, but what the stars gave, I could not be certain. We stood quite\par
silent, to watch it, and presently saw something pass along the castle\par
wall just opposite to us!”\par
\par
“Why did you not seize it, then?” cried a soldier, who had scarcely\par
spoken till now.\par
\par
“Aye, why did you not seize it?” said Roberto.\par
\par
“You should have been there to have done that,” replied Sebastian. “You\par
would have been bold enough to have taken it by the throat, though it\par
had been the devil himself; we could not take such a liberty, perhaps,\par
because we are not so well acquainted with him, as you are. But, as I\par
was saying, it stole by us so quickly, that we had not time to get rid\par
of our surprise, before it was gone. Then, we knew it was in vain to\par
follow. We kept constant watch all that night, but we saw it no more.\par
Next morning, we told some of our comrades, who were on duty on other\par
parts of the ramparts, what we had seen; but they had seen nothing, and\par
laughed at us, and it was not till tonight, that the same figure walked\par
again.”\par
\par
“Where did you lose it, friend?” said Emily to Roberto.\par
\par
“When I left you, lady,” replied the man, “you might see me go down the\par
rampart, but it was not till I reached the east terrace, that I saw\par
anything. Then, the moon shining bright, I saw something like a shadow\par
flitting before me, as it were, at some distance. I stopped, when I\par
turned the corner of the east tower, where I had seen this figure not a\par
moment before,—but it was gone! As I stood, looking through the old\par
arch which leads to the east rampart, and where I am sure it had\par
passed, I heard, all of a sudden, such a sound!—it was not like a\par
groan, or a cry, or a shout, or anything I ever heard in my life. I\par
heard it only once, and that was enough for me; for I know nothing that\par
happened after, till I found my comrades, here, about me.”\par
\par
“Come,” said Sebastian, “let us go to our posts—the moon is setting.\par
Good night, lady!”\par
\par
“Aye, let us go,” rejoined Roberto. “Good night, lady.”\par
\par
“Good night; the holy mother guard you!” said Emily, as she closed her\par
casement and retired to reflect upon the strange circumstance that had\par
just occurred, connecting which with what had happened on former\par
nights, she endeavoured to derive from the whole something more\par
positive, than conjecture. But her imagination was inflamed, while her\par
judgment was not enlightened, and the terrors of superstition again\par
pervaded her mind.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER IV\par
\par
There is one within,\par
Besides the things that we have heard and seen,\par
Recounts most horrid sights, seen by the watch.\par
                    JULIUS CÆSAR\par
\par
\par
In the morning, Emily found Madame Montoni nearly in the same\par
condition, as on the preceding night; she had slept little, and that\par
little had not refreshed her; she smiled on her niece, and seemed\par
cheered by her presence, but spoke only a few words, and never named\par
Montoni, who, however, soon after, entered the room. His wife, when she\par
understood that he was there, appeared much agitated, but was entirely\par
silent, till Emily rose from a chair at the bedside, when she begged,\par
in a feeble voice, that she would not leave her.\par
\par
The visit of Montoni was not to sooth his wife, whom he knew to be\par
dying, or to console, or to ask her forgiveness, but to make a last\par
effort to procure that signature, which would transfer her estates in\par
Languedoc, after her death, to him rather than to Emily. This was a\par
scene, that exhibited, on his part, his usual inhumanity, and, on that\par
of Madame Montoni, a persevering spirit, contending with a feeble\par
frame; while Emily repeatedly declared to him her willingness to resign\par
all claim to those estates, rather than that the last hours of her aunt\par
should be disturbed by contention. Montoni, however, did not leave the\par
room, till his wife, exhausted by the obstinate dispute, had fainted,\par
and she lay so long insensible, that Emily began to fear that the spark\par
of life was extinguished. At length, she revived, and, looking feebly\par
up at her niece, whose tears were falling over her, made an effort to\par
speak, but her words were unintelligible, and Emily again apprehended\par
she was dying. Afterwards, however, she recovered her speech, and,\par
being somewhat restored by a cordial, conversed for a considerable\par
time, on the subject of her estates in France, with clearness and\par
precision. She directed her niece where to find some papers relative to\par
them, which she had hitherto concealed from the search of Montoni, and\par
earnestly charged her never to suffer these papers to escape her.\par
\par
Soon after this conversation, Madame Montoni sunk into a dose, and\par
continued slumbering, till evening, when she seemed better than she had\par
been since her removal from the turret. Emily never left her, for a\par
moment, till long after midnight, and even then would not have quitted\par
the room, had not her aunt entreated, that she would retire to rest.\par
She then obeyed, the more willingly, because her patient appeared\par
somewhat recruited by sleep; and, giving Annette the same injunction,\par
as on the preceding night, she withdrew to her own apartment. But her\par
spirits were wakeful and agitated, and, finding it impossible to sleep,\par
she determined to watch, once more, for the mysterious appearance, that\par
had so much interested and alarmed her.\par
\par
It was now the second watch of the night, and about the time when the\par
figure had before appeared. Emily heard the passing steps of the\par
sentinels, on the rampart, as they changed guard; and, when all was\par
again silent, she took her station at the casement, leaving her lamp in\par
a remote part of the chamber, that she might escape notice from\par
without. The moon gave a faint and uncertain light, for heavy vapours\par
surrounded it, and, often rolling over the disk, left the scene below\par
in total darkness. It was in one of these moments of obscurity, that\par
she observed a small and lambent flame, moving at some distance on the\par
terrace. While she gazed, it disappeared, and, the moon again emerging\par
from the lurid and heavy thunder clouds, she turned her attention to\par
the heavens, where the vivid lightnings darted from cloud to cloud, and\par
flashed silently on the woods below. She loved to catch, in the\par
momentary gleam, the gloomy landscape. Sometimes, a cloud opened its\par
light upon a distant mountain, and, while the sudden splendour\par
illumined all its recesses of rock and wood, the rest of the scene\par
remained in deep shadow; at others, partial features of the castle were\par
revealed by the glimpse—the ancient arch leading to the east rampart,\par
the turret above, or the fortifications beyond; and then, perhaps, the\par
whole edifice with all its towers, its dark massy walls and pointed\par
casements would appear, and vanish in an instant.\par
\par
Emily, looking again upon the rampart, perceived the flame she had seen\par
before; it moved onward; and, soon after, she thought she heard a\par
footstep. The light appeared and disappeared frequently, while, as she\par
watched, it glided under her casements, and, at the same instant, she\par
was certain, that a footstep passed, but the darkness did not permit\par
her to distinguish any object except the flame. It moved away, and\par
then, by a gleam of lightning, she perceived some person on the\par
terrace. All the anxieties of the preceding night returned. This person\par
advanced, and the playing flame alternately appeared and vanished.\par
Emily wished to speak, to end her doubts, whether this figure were\par
human or supernatural; but her courage failed as often as she attempted\par
utterance, till the light moved again under the casement, and she\par
faintly demanded, who passed.\par
\par
“A friend,” replied a voice.\par
\par
“What friend?” said Emily, somewhat encouraged “who are you, and what\par
is that light you carry?”\par
\par
“I am Anthonio, one of the Signor’s soldiers,” replied the voice.\par
\par
“And what is that tapering light you bear?” said Emily, “see how it\par
darts upwards,—and now it vanishes!”\par
\par
“This light, lady,” said the soldier, “has appeared tonight as you see\par
it, on the point of my lance, ever since I have been on watch; but what\par
it means I cannot tell.”\par
\par
“This is very strange!” said Emily.\par
\par
“My fellow-guard,” continued the man, “has the same flame on his arms;\par
he says he has sometimes seen it before. I never did; I am but lately\par
come to the castle, for I have not been long a soldier.”\par
\par
“How does your comrade account for it?” said Emily.\par
\par
“He says it is an omen, lady, and bodes no good.”\par
\par
“And what harm can it bode?” rejoined Emily.\par
\par
“He knows not so much as that, lady.”\par
\par
Whether Emily was alarmed by this omen, or not, she certainly was\par
relieved from much terror by discovering this man to be only a soldier\par
on duty, and it immediately occurred to her, that it might be he, who\par
had occasioned so much alarm on the preceding night. There were,\par
however, some circumstances, that still required explanation. As far as\par
she could judge by the faint moonlight, that had assisted her\par
observation, the figure she had seen did not resemble this man either\par
in shape or size; besides, she was certain it had carried no arms. The\par
silence of its steps, if steps it had, the moaning sounds, too, which\par
it had uttered, and its strange disappearance, were circumstances of\par
mysterious import, that did not apply, with probability, to a soldier\par
engaged in the duty of his guard.\par
\par
She now enquired of the sentinel, whether he had seen any person\par
besides his fellow watch, walking on the terrace, about midnight; and\par
then briefly related what she had herself observed.\par
\par
“I was not on guard that night, lady,” replied the man, “but I heard of\par
what happened. There are amongst us, who believe strange things.\par
Strange stories, too, have long been told of this castle, but it is no\par
business of mine to repeat them; and, for my part, I have no reason to\par
complain; our Chief does nobly by us.”\par
\par
“I commend your prudence,” said Emily. “Good night, and accept this\par
from me,” she added, throwing him a small piece of coin, and then\par
closing the casement to put an end to the discourse.\par
\par
When he was gone, she opened it again, listened with a gloomy pleasure\par
to the distant thunder, that began to murmur among the mountains, and\par
watched the arrowy lightnings, which broke over the remoter scene. The\par
pealing thunder rolled onward, and then, reverbed by the mountains,\par
other thunder seemed to answer from the opposite horizon; while the\par
accumulating clouds, entirely concealing the moon, assumed a red\par
sulphureous tinge, that foretold a violent storm.\par
\par
Emily remained at her casement, till the vivid lightning, that now,\par
every instant, revealed the wide horizon and the landscape below, made\par
it no longer safe to do so, and she went to her couch; but, unable to\par
compose her mind to sleep, still listened in silent awe to the\par
tremendous sounds, that seemed to shake the castle to its foundation.\par
\par
She had continued thus for a considerable time, when, amidst the uproar\par
of the storm, she thought she heard a voice, and, raising herself to\par
listen, saw the chamber door open, and Annette enter with a countenance\par
of wild affright.\par
\par
“She is dying, ma’amselle, my lady is dying!” said she.\par
\par
Emily started up, and ran to Madame Montoni’s room. When she entered,\par
her aunt appeared to have fainted, for she was quite still, and\par
insensible; and Emily with a strength of mind, that refused to yield to\par
grief, while any duty required her activity, applied every means that\par
seemed likely to restore her. But the last struggle was over—she was\par
gone for ever.\par
\par
When Emily perceived, that all her efforts were ineffectual, she\par
interrogated the terrified Annette, and learned, that Madame Montoni\par
had fallen into a doze soon after Emily’s departure, in which she had\par
continued, until a few minutes before her death.\par
\par
“I wondered, ma’amselle,” said Annette, “what was the reason my lady\par
did not seem frightened at the thunder, when I was so terrified, and I\par
went often to the bed to speak to her, but she appeared to be asleep;\par
till presently I heard a strange noise, and, on going to her, saw she\par
was dying.”\par
\par
Emily, at this recital, shed tears. She had no doubt but that the\par
violent change in the air, which the tempest produced, had effected\par
this fatal one, on the exhausted frame of Madame Montoni.\par
\par
After some deliberation, she determined that Montoni should not be\par
informed of this event till the morning, for she considered, that he\par
might, perhaps, utter some inhuman expressions, such as in the present\par
temper of her spirits she could not bear. With Annette alone,\par
therefore, whom she encouraged by her own example, she performed some\par
of the last solemn offices for the dead, and compelled herself to watch\par
during the night, by the body of her deceased aunt. During this solemn\par
period, rendered more awful by the tremendous storm that shook the air,\par
she frequently addressed herself to Heaven for support and protection,\par
and her pious prayers, we may believe, were accepted of the God, that\par
giveth comfort.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER V\par
\par
The midnight clock has toll’d; and hark, the bell\par
Of death beats slow! heard ye the note profound?\par
It pauses now; and now with rising knell\par
Flings to the hollow gale its sullen sound.\par
                    MASON\par
\par
\par
When Montoni was informed of the death of his wife, and considered that\par
she had died without giving him the signature so necessary to the\par
accomplishment of his wishes, no sense of decency restrained the\par
expression of his resentment. Emily anxiously avoided his presence, and\par
watched, during two days and two nights, with little intermission, by\par
the corpse of her late aunt. Her mind deeply impressed with the unhappy\par
fate of this object, she forgot all her faults, her unjust and\par
imperious conduct to herself; and, remembering only her sufferings,\par
thought of her only with tender compassion. Sometimes, however, she\par
could not avoid musing upon the strange infatuation that had proved so\par
fatal to her aunt, and had involved herself in a labyrinth of\par
misfortune, from which she saw no means of escaping,—the marriage with\par
Montoni. But, when she considered this circumstance, it was “more in\par
sorrow than in anger,”—more for the purpose of indulging lamentation,\par
than reproach.\par
\par
In her pious cares she was not disturbed by Montoni, who not only\par
avoided the chamber, where the remains of his wife were laid, but that\par
part of the castle adjoining to it, as if he had apprehended a\par
contagion in death. He seemed to have given no orders respecting the\par
funeral, and Emily began to fear he meant to offer a new insult to the\par
memory of Madame Montoni; but from this apprehension she was relieved,\par
when, on the evening of the second day, Annette informed her, that the\par
interment was to take place that night. She knew, that Montoni would\par
not attend; and it was so very grievous to her to think that the\par
remains of her unfortunate aunt would pass to the grave without one\par
relative, or friend to pay them the last decent rites, that she\par
determined to be deterred by no considerations for herself, from\par
observing this duty. She would otherwise have shrunk from the\par
circumstance of following them to the cold vault, to which they were to\par
be carried by men whose air and countenances seemed to stamp them for\par
murderers, at the midnight hour of silence and privacy, which Montoni\par
had chosen for committing, if possible, to oblivion the reliques of a\par
woman, whom his harsh conduct had, at least, contributed to destroy.\par
\par
Emily, shuddering with emotions of horror and grief, assisted by\par
Annette, prepared the corpse for interment; and, having wrapt it in\par
cerements, and covered it with a winding-sheet, they watched beside it,\par
till past midnight, when they heard the approaching footsteps of the\par
men, who were to lay it in its earthy bed. It was with difficulty that\par
Emily overcame her emotion, when, the door of the chamber being thrown\par
open, their gloomy countenances were seen by the glare of the torch\par
they carried, and two of them, without speaking, lifted the body on\par
their shoulders, while the third preceding them with the light,\par
descended through the castle towards the grave, which was in the lower\par
vault of the chapel within the castle walls.\par
\par
They had to cross two courts, towards the east wing of the castle,\par
which, adjoining the chapel, was, like it, in ruins: but the silence\par
and gloom of these courts had now little power over Emily’s mind,\par
occupied as it was, with more mournful ideas; and she scarcely heard\par
the low and dismal hooting of the night-birds, that roosted among the\par
ivied battlements of the ruin, or perceived the still flittings of the\par
bat, which frequently crossed her way. But, when, having entered the\par
chapel, and passed between the mouldering pillars of the aisles, the\par
bearers stopped at a flight of steps, that led down to a low arched\par
door, and, their comrade having descended to unlock it, she saw\par
imperfectly the gloomy abyss beyond;—saw the corpse of her aunt carried\par
down these steps, and the ruffian-like figure, that stood with a torch\par
at the bottom to receive it—all her fortitude was lost in emotions of\par
inexpressible grief and terror. She turned to lean upon Annette, who\par
was cold and trembling like herself, and she lingered so long on the\par
summit of the flight, that the gleam of the torch began to die away on\par
the pillars of the chapel, and the men were almost beyond her view.\par
Then, the gloom around her awakening other fears, and a sense of what\par
she considered to be her duty overcoming her reluctance, she descended\par
to the vaults, following the echo of footsteps and the faint ray, that\par
pierced the darkness, till the harsh grating of a distant door, that\par
was opened to receive the corpse, again appalled her.\par
\par
After the pause of a moment, she went on, and, as she entered the\par
vaults, saw between the arches, at some distance, the men lay down the\par
body near the edge of an open grave, where stood another of Montoni’s\par
men and a priest, whom she did not observe, till he began the burial\par
service; then, lifting her eyes from the ground, she saw the venerable\par
figure of the friar, and heard him in a low voice, equally solemn and\par
affecting, perform the service for the dead. At the moment, in which\par
they let down the body into the earth, the scene was such as only the\par
dark pencil of a Domenichino, perhaps, could have done justice to. The\par
fierce features and wild dress of the _condottieri_, bending with their\par
torches over the grave, into which the corpse was descending, were\par
contrasted by the venerable figure of the monk, wrapt in long black\par
garments, his cowl thrown back from his pale face, on which the light\par
gleaming strongly showed the lines of affliction softened by piety, and\par
the few grey locks, which time had spared on his temples: while, beside\par
him, stood the softer form of Emily, who leaned for support upon\par
Annette; her face half averted, and shaded by a thin veil, that fell\par
over her figure; and her mild and beautiful countenance fixed in grief\par
so solemn as admitted not of tears, while she thus saw committed\par
untimely to the earth her last relative and friend. The gleams, thrown\par
between the arches of the vaults, where, here and there, the broken\par
ground marked the spots in which other bodies had been recently\par
interred, and the general obscurity beyond were circumstances, that\par
alone would have led on the imagination of a spectator to scenes more\par
horrible than even that which was pictured at the grave of the\par
misguided and unfortunate Madame Montoni.\par
\par
When the service was over, the friar regarded Emily with attention and\par
surprise, and looked as if he wished to speak to her, but was\par
restrained by the presence of the _condottieri_, who, as they now led\par
the way to the courts, amused themselves with jokes upon his holy\par
order, which he endured in silence, demanding only to be conducted\par
safely to his convent, and to which Emily listened with concern and\par
even horror. When they reached the court, the monk gave her his\par
blessing, and, after a lingering look of pity, turned away to the\par
portal, whither one of the men carried a torch; while Annette, lighting\par
another, preceded Emily to her apartment. The appearance of the friar\par
and the expression of tender compassion, with which he had regarded\par
her, had interested Emily, who, though it was at her earnest\par
supplication, that Montoni had consented to allow a priest to perform\par
the last rites for his deceased wife, knew nothing concerning this\par
person, till Annette now informed her, that he belonged to a monastery,\par
situated among the mountains at a few miles distance. The Superior, who\par
regarded Montoni and his associates, not only with aversion, but with\par
terror, had probably feared to offend him by refusing his request, and\par
had, therefore, ordered a monk to officiate at the funeral, who, with\par
the meek spirit of a christian, had overcome his reluctance to enter\par
the walls of such a castle, by the wish of performing what he\par
considered to be his duty, and, as the chapel was built on consecrated\par
ground, had not objected to commit to it the remains of the late\par
unhappy Madame Montoni.\par
\par
Several days passed with Emily in total seclusion, and in a state of\par
mind partaking both of terror for herself, and grief for the departed.\par
She, at length, determined to make other efforts to persuade Montoni to\par
permit her return to France. Why he should wish to detain her, she\par
could scarcely dare to conjecture; but it was too certain that he did\par
so, and the absolute refusal he had formerly given to her departure\par
allowed her little hope, that he would now consent to it. But the\par
horror, which his presence inspired, made her defer, from day to day,\par
the mention of this subject; and at last she was awakened from her\par
inactivity only by a message from him, desiring her attendance at a\par
certain hour. She began to hope he meant to resign, now that her aunt\par
was no more, the authority he had usurped over her; till she\par
recollected, that the estates, which had occasioned so much contention,\par
were now hers, and she then feared Montoni was about to employ some\par
stratagem for obtaining them, and that he would detain her his\par
prisoner, till he succeeded. This thought, instead of overcoming her\par
with despondency, roused all the latent powers of her fortitude into\par
action; and the property, which she would willingly have resigned to\par
secure the peace of her aunt, she resolved, that no common sufferings\par
of her own should ever compel her to give to Montoni. For Valancourt’s\par
sake also she determined to preserve these estates, since they would\par
afford that competency, by which she hoped to secure the comfort of\par
their future lives. As she thought of this, she indulged the tenderness\par
of tears, and anticipated the delight of that moment, when, with\par
affectionate generosity, she might tell him they were his own. She saw\par
the smile, that lighted up his features—the affectionate regard, which\par
spoke at once his joy and thanks; and, at this instant, she believed\par
she could brave any suffering, which the evil spirit of Montoni might\par
be preparing for her. Remembering then, for the first time since her\par
aunt’s death, the papers relative to the estates in question, she\par
determined to search for them, as soon as her interview with Montoni\par
was over.\par
\par
With these resolutions she met him at the appointed time, and waited to\par
hear his intention before she renewed her request. With him were Orsino\par
and another officer, and both were standing near a table, covered with\par
papers, which he appeared to be examining.\par
\par
“I sent for you, Emily,” said Montoni, raising his head, “that you\par
might be a witness in some business, which I am transacting with my\par
friend Orsino. All that is required of you will be to sign your name to\par
this paper:” he then took one up, hurried unintelligibly over some\par
lines, and, laying it before her on the table, offered her a pen. She\par
took it, and was going to write—when the design of Montoni came upon\par
her mind like a flash of lightning; she trembled, let the pen fall, and\par
refused to sign what she had not read. Montoni affected to laugh at her\par
scruples, and, taking up the paper, again pretended to read; but Emily,\par
who still trembled on perceiving her danger, and was astonished, that\par
her own credulity had so nearly betrayed her, positively refused to\par
sign any paper whatever. Montoni, for some time, persevered in\par
affecting to ridicule this refusal; but, when he perceived by her\par
steady perseverance, that she understood his design, he changed his\par
manner, and bade her follow him to another room. There he told her,\par
that he had been willing to spare himself and her the trouble of\par
useless contest, in an affair, where his will was justice, and where\par
she should find it law; and had, therefore, endeavoured to persuade,\par
rather than to compel, her to the practice of her duty.\par
\par
“I, as the husband of the late Signora Montoni,” he added, “am the heir\par
of all she possessed; the estates, therefore, which she refused to me\par
in her life-time, can no longer be withheld, and, for your own sake, I\par
would undeceive you, respecting a foolish assertion she once made to\par
you in my hearing—that these estates would be yours, if she died\par
without resigning them to me. She knew at that moment, she had no power\par
to withhold them from me, after her decease; and I think you have more\par
sense, than to provoke my resentment by advancing an unjust claim. I am\par
not in the habit of flattering, and you will, therefore, receive, as\par
sincere, the praise I bestow, when I say, that you possess an\par
understanding superior to that of your sex; and that you have none of\par
those contemptible foibles, that frequently mark the female\par
character—such as avarice and the love of power, which latter makes\par
women delight to contradict and to tease, when they cannot conquer. If\par
I understand your disposition and your mind, you hold in sovereign\par
contempt these common failings of your sex.”\par
\par
Montoni paused; and Emily remained silent and expecting; for she knew\par
him too well, to believe he would condescend to such flattery, unless\par
he thought it would promote his own interest; and, though he had\par
forborne to name vanity among the foibles of women, it was evident,\par
that he considered it to be a predominant one, since he designed to\par
sacrifice to hers the character and understanding of her whole sex.\par
\par
“Judging as I do,” resumed Montoni, “I cannot believe you will oppose,\par
where you know you cannot conquer, or, indeed, that you would wish to\par
conquer, or be avaricious of any property, when you have not justice on\par
your side. I think it proper, however, to acquaint you with the\par
alternative. If you have a just opinion of the subject in question, you\par
shall be allowed a safe conveyance to France, within a short period;\par
but, if you are so unhappy as to be misled by the late assertion of the\par
Signora, you shall remain my prisoner, till you are convinced of your\par
error.”\par
\par
Emily calmly said,\par
\par
“I am not so ignorant, Signor, of the laws on this subject, as to be\par
misled by the assertion of any person. The law, in the present\par
instance, gives me the estates in question, and my own hand shall never\par
betray my right.”\par
\par
“I have been mistaken in my opinion of you, it appears,” rejoined\par
Montoni, sternly. “You speak boldly, and presumptuously, upon a\par
subject, which you do not understand. For once, I am willing to pardon\par
the conceit of ignorance; the weakness of your sex, too, from which, it\par
seems, you are not exempt, claims some allowance; but, if you persist\par
in this strain—you have everything to fear from my justice.”\par
\par
“From your justice, Signor,” rejoined Emily, “I have nothing to fear—I\par
have only to hope.”\par
\par
Montoni looked at her with vexation, and seemed considering what to\par
say. “I find that you are weak enough,” he resumed, “to credit the idle\par
assertion I alluded to! For your own sake I lament this; as to me, it\par
is of little consequence. Your credulity can punish only yourself; and\par
I must pity the weakness of mind, which leads you to so much suffering\par
as you are compelling me to prepare for you.”\par
\par
“You may find, perhaps, Signor,” said Emily, with mild dignity, “that\par
the strength of my mind is equal to the justice of my cause; and that I\par
can endure with fortitude, when it is in resistance of oppression.”\par
\par
“You speak like a heroine,” said Montoni, contemptuously; “we shall see\par
whether you can suffer like one.”\par
\par
Emily was silent, and he left the room.\par
\par
Recollecting, that it was for Valancourt’s sake she had thus resisted,\par
she now smiled complacently upon the threatened sufferings, and retired\par
to the spot, which her aunt had pointed out as the repository of the\par
papers, relative to the estates, where she found them as described;\par
and, since she knew of no better place of concealment, than this,\par
returned them, without examining their contents, being fearful of\par
discovery, while she should attempt a perusal.\par
\par
To her own solitary chamber she once more returned, and there thought\par
again of the late conversation with Montoni, and of the evil she might\par
expect from opposition to his will. But his power did not appear so\par
terrible to her imagination, as it was wont to do: a sacred pride was\par
in her heart, that taught it to swell against the pressure of\par
injustice, and almost to glory in the quiet sufferance of ills, in a\par
cause, which had also the interest of Valancourt for its object. For\par
the first time, she felt the full extent of her own superiority to\par
Montoni, and despised the authority, which, till now, she had only\par
feared.\par
\par
As she sat musing, a peal of laughter rose from the terrace, and, on\par
going to the casement, she saw, with inexpressible surprise, three\par
ladies, dressed in the gala habit of Venice, walking with several\par
gentlemen below. She gazed in an astonishment that made her remain at\par
the window, regardless of being observed, till the group passed under\par
it; and, one of the strangers looking up, she perceived the features of\par
Signora Livona, with whose manners she had been so much charmed, the\par
day after her arrival at Venice, and who had been there introduced at\par
the table of Montoni. This discovery occasioned her an emotion of\par
doubtful joy; for it was matter of joy and comfort to know, that a\par
person, of a mind so gentle, as that of Signora Livona seemed to be,\par
was near her; yet there was something so extraordinary in her being at\par
this castle, circumstanced as it now was, and evidently, by the gaiety\par
of her air, with her own consent, that a very painful surmise arose,\par
concerning her character. But the thought was so shocking to Emily,\par
whose affection the fascinating manners of the Signora had won, and\par
appeared so improbable, when she remembered these manners, that she\par
dismissed it almost instantly.\par
\par
On Annette’s appearance, however, she enquired, concerning these\par
strangers; and the former was as eager to tell, as Emily was to learn.\par
\par
“They are just come, ma’amselle,” said Annette, “with two Signors from\par
Venice, and I was glad to see such Christian faces once again.—But what\par
can they mean by coming here? They must surely be stark mad to come\par
freely to such a place as this! Yet they do come freely, for they seem\par
merry enough, I am sure.”\par
\par
“They were taken prisoners, perhaps?” said Emily.\par
\par
“Taken prisoners!” exclaimed Annette; “no, indeed, ma’amselle, not\par
they. I remember one of them very well at Venice: she came two or three\par
times, to the Signor’s you know, ma’amselle, and it was said, but I did\par
not believe a word of it—it was said, that the Signor liked her better\par
than he should do. Then why, says I, bring her to my lady? Very true,\par
said Ludovico; but he looked as if he knew more, too.”\par
\par
Emily desired Annette would endeavour to learn who these ladies were,\par
as well as all she could concerning them; and she then changed the\par
subject, and spoke of distant France.\par
\par
“Ah, ma’amselle! we shall never see it more!” said Annette, almost\par
weeping.—“I must come on my travels, forsooth!”\par
\par
Emily tried to sooth and to cheer her, with a hope, in which she\par
scarcely herself indulged.\par
\par
“How—how, ma’amselle, could you leave France, and leave Mons.\par
Valancourt, too?” said Annette, sobbing. “I—I—am sure, if Ludovico had\par
been in France, I would never have left it.”\par
\par
“Why do you lament quitting France, then?” said Emily, trying to smile,\par
“since, if you had remained there, you would not have found Ludovico.”\par
\par
“Ah, ma’amselle! I only wish I was out of this frightful castle,\par
serving you in France, and I would care about nothing else!”\par
\par
“Thank you, my good Annette, for your affectionate regard; the time\par
will come, I hope, when you may remember the expression of that wish\par
with pleasure.”\par
\par
Annette departed on her business, and Emily sought to lose the sense of\par
her own cares, in the visionary scenes of the poet; but she had again\par
to lament the irresistible force of circumstances over the taste and\par
powers of the mind; and that it requires a spirit at ease to be\par
sensible even to the abstract pleasures of pure intellect. The\par
enthusiasm of genius, with all its pictured scenes, now appeared cold,\par
and dim. As she mused upon the book before her, she involuntarily\par
exclaimed, “Are these, indeed, the passages, that have so often given\par
me exquisite delight? Where did the charm exist?—Was it in my mind, or\par
in the imagination of the poet? It lived in each,” said she, pausing.\par
“But the fire of the poet is vain, if the mind of his reader is not\par
tempered like his own, however it may be inferior to his in power.”\par
\par
Emily would have pursued this train of thinking, because it relieved\par
her from more painful reflection, but she found again, that thought\par
cannot always be controlled by will; and hers returned to the\par
consideration of her own situation.\par
\par
In the evening, not choosing to venture down to the ramparts, where she\par
would be exposed to the rude gaze of Montoni’s associates, she walked\par
for air in the gallery, adjoining her chamber; on reaching the further\par
end of which she heard distant sounds of merriment and laughter. It was\par
the wild uproar of riot, not the cheering gaiety of tempered mirth; and\par
seemed to come from that part of the castle, where Montoni usually was.\par
Such sounds, at this time, when her aunt had been so few days dead,\par
particularly shocked her, consistent as they were with the late conduct\par
of Montoni.\par
\par
As she listened, she thought she distinguished female voices mingling\par
with the laughter, and this confirmed her worst surmise, concerning the\par
character of Signora Livona and her companions. It was evident, that\par
they had not been brought hither by compulsion; and she beheld herself\par
in the remote wilds of the Apennine, surrounded by men, whom she\par
considered to be little less than ruffians, and their worst associates,\par
amid scenes of vice, from which her soul recoiled in horror. It was at\par
this moment, when the scenes of the present and the future opened to\par
her imagination, that the image of Valancourt failed in its influence,\par
and her resolution shook with dread. She thought she understood all the\par
horrors, which Montoni was preparing for her, and shrunk from an\par
encounter with such remorseless vengeance, as he could inflict. The\par
disputed estates she now almost determined to yield at once, whenever\par
he should again call upon her, that she might regain safety and\par
freedom; but then, the remembrance of Valancourt would steal to her\par
heart, and plunge her into the distractions of doubt.\par
\par
She continued walking in the gallery, till evening threw its melancholy\par
twilight through the painted casements, and deepened the gloom of the\par
oak wainscoting around her; while the distant perspective of the\par
corridor was so much obscured, as to be discernible only by the\par
glimmering window, that terminated it.\par
\par
Along the vaulted halls and passages below, peals of laughter echoed\par
faintly, at intervals, to this remote part of the castle, and seemed to\par
render the succeeding stillness more dreary. Emily, however, unwilling\par
to return to her more forlorn chamber, whither Annette was not yet\par
come, still paced the gallery. As she passed the door of the apartment,\par
where she had once dared to lift the veil, which discovered to her a\par
spectacle so horrible, that she had never after remembered it, but with\par
emotions of indescribable awe, this remembrance suddenly recurred. It\par
now brought with it reflections more terrible, than it had yet done,\par
which the late conduct of Montoni occasioned; and, hastening to quit\par
the gallery, while she had power to do so, she heard a sudden step\par
behind her.—It might be that of Annette; but, turning fearfully to\par
look, she saw, through the gloom, a tall figure following her, and all\par
the horrors of that chamber rushed upon her mind. In the next moment,\par
she found herself clasped in the arms of some person, and heard a deep\par
voice murmur in her ear.\par
\par
When she had power to speak, or to distinguish articulated sounds, she\par
demanded who detained her.\par
\par
“It is I,” replied the voice—“Why are you thus alarmed?”\par
\par
She looked on the face of the person who spoke, but the feeble light,\par
that gleamed through the high casement at the end of the gallery, did\par
not permit her to distinguish the features.\par
\par
“Whoever you are,” said Emily, in a trembling voice, “for heaven’s sake\par
let me go!”\par
\par
“My charming Emily,” said the man, “why will you shut yourself up in\par
this obscure place, when there is so much gaiety below? Return with me\par
to the cedar parlour, where you will be the fairest ornament of the\par
party;—you shall not repent the exchange.”\par
\par
Emily disdained to reply, and still endeavoured to liberate herself.\par
\par
“Promise, that you will come,” he continued, “and I will release you\par
immediately; but first give me a reward for so doing.”\par
\par
“Who are you?” demanded Emily, in a tone of mingled terror and\par
indignation, while she still struggled for liberty—“who are you, that\par
have the cruelty thus to insult me?”\par
\par
“Why call me cruel?” said the man, “I would remove you from this dreary\par
solitude to a merry party below. Do you not know me?”\par
\par
Emily now faintly remembered, that he was one of the officers who were\par
with Montoni when she attended him in the morning. “I thank you for the\par
kindness of your intention,” she replied, without appearing to\par
understand him, “but I wish for nothing so much as that you would leave\par
me.”\par
\par
“Charming Emily!” said he, “give up this foolish whim for solitude, and\par
come with me to the company, and eclipse the beauties who make part of\par
it; you, only, are worthy of my love.” He attempted to kiss her hand,\par
but the strong impulse of her indignation gave her power to liberate\par
herself, and she fled towards the chamber. She closed the door, before\par
he reached it, having secured which, she sunk in a chair, overcome by\par
terror and by the exertion she had made, while she heard his voice, and\par
his attempts to open the door, without having the power to raise\par
herself. At length, she perceived him depart, and had remained,\par
listening, for a considerable time, and was somewhat revived by not\par
hearing any sound, when suddenly she remembered the door of the private\par
staircase, and that he might enter that way, since it was fastened only\par
on the other side. She then employed herself in endeavouring to secure\par
it, in the manner she had formerly done. It appeared to her, that\par
Montoni had already commenced his scheme of vengeance, by withdrawing\par
from her his protection, and she repented of the rashness, that had\par
made her brave the power of such a man. To retain the estates seemed to\par
be now utterly impossible, and to preserve her life, perhaps her\par
honour, she resolved, if she should escape the horrors of this night,\par
to give up all claims to the estates, on the morrow, provided Montoni\par
would suffer her to depart from Udolpho.\par
\par
When she had come to this decision, her mind became more composed,\par
though she still anxiously listened, and often started at ideal sounds,\par
that appeared to issue from the staircase.\par
\par
Having sat in darkness for some hours, during all which time Annette\par
did not appear, she began to have serious apprehensions for her; but,\par
not daring to venture down into the castle, was compelled to remain in\par
uncertainty, as to the cause of this unusual absence.\par
\par
Emily often stole to the staircase door to listen if any step\par
approached, but still no sound alarmed her: determining, however, to\par
watch, during the night, she once more rested on her dark and desolate\par
couch, and bathed the pillow with innocent tears. She thought of her\par
deceased parents and then of the absent Valancourt, and frequently\par
called upon their names; for the profound stillness, that now reigned,\par
was propitious to the musing sorrow of her mind.\par
\par
While she thus remained, her ear suddenly caught the notes of distant\par
music, to which she listened attentively, and, soon perceiving this to\par
be the instrument she had formerly heard at midnight, she rose, and\par
stepped softly to the casement, to which the sounds appeared to come\par
from a lower room.\par
\par
In a few moments, their soft melody was accompanied by a voice so full\par
of pathos, that it evidently sang not of imaginary sorrows. Its sweet\par
and peculiar tones she thought she had somewhere heard before; yet, if\par
this was not fancy, it was, at most, a very faint recollection. It\par
stole over her mind, amidst the anguish of her present suffering, like\par
a celestial strain, soothing, and reassuring her;—“Pleasant as the gale\par
of spring, that sighs on the hunter’s ear, when he awakens from dreams\par
of joy, and has heard the music of the spirits of the hill.”*\par
\par
(*Note: Ossian. [A. R.])\par
\par
\par
But her emotion can scarcely be imagined, when she heard sung, with the\par
taste and simplicity of true feeling, one of the popular airs of her\par
native province, to which she had so often listened with delight, when\par
a child, and which she had so often heard her father repeat! To this\par
well-known song, never, till now, heard but in her native country, her\par
heart melted, while the memory of past times returned. The pleasant,\par
peaceful scenes of Gascony, the tenderness and goodness of her parents,\par
the taste and simplicity of her former life—all rose to her fancy, and\par
formed a picture, so sweet and glowing, so strikingly contrasted with\par
the scenes, the characters and the dangers, which now surrounded\par
her—that her mind could not bear to pause upon the retrospect, and\par
shrunk at the acuteness of its own sufferings.\par
\par
Her sighs were deep and convulsed; she could no longer listen to the\par
strain, that had so often charmed her to tranquillity, and she withdrew\par
from the casement to a remote part of the chamber. But she was not yet\par
beyond the reach of the music; she heard the measure change, and the\par
succeeding air called her again to the window, for she immediately\par
recollected it to be the same she had formerly heard in the\par
fishing-house in Gascony. Assisted, perhaps, by the mystery, which had\par
then accompanied this strain, it had made so deep an impression on her\par
memory, that she had never since entirely forgotten it; and the manner,\par
in which it was now sung, convinced her, however unaccountable the\par
circumstances appeared, that this was the same voice she had then\par
heard. Surprise soon yielded to other emotions; a thought darted, like\par
lightning, upon her mind, which discovered a train of hopes, that\par
revived all her spirits. Yet these hopes were so new, so unexpected, so\par
astonishing, that she did not dare to trust, though she could not\par
resolve to discourage them. She sat down by the casement, breathless,\par
and overcome with the alternate emotions of hope and fear; then rose\par
again, leaned from the window, that she might catch a nearer sound,\par
listened, now doubting and then believing, softly exclaimed the name of\par
Valancourt, and then sunk again into the chair. Yes, it was possible,\par
that Valancourt was near her, and she recollected circumstances, which\par
induced her to believe it was his voice she had just heard. She\par
remembered he had more than once said that the fishing-house, where she\par
had formerly listened to this voice and air, and where she had seen\par
pencilled sonnets, addressed to herself, had been his favourite haunt,\par
before he had been made known to her; there, too, she had herself\par
unexpectedly met him. It appeared, from these circumstances, more than\par
probable, that he was the musician, who had formerly charmed her\par
attention, and the author of the lines, which had expressed such tender\par
admiration;—who else, indeed, could it be? She was unable, at that\par
time, to form a conjecture, as to the writer, but, since her\par
acquaintance with Valancourt, whenever he had mentioned the\par
fishing-house to have been known to him, she had not scrupled to\par
believe that he was the author of the sonnets.\par
\par
As these considerations passed over her mind, joy, fear and tenderness\par
contended at her heart; she leaned again from the casement to catch the\par
sounds, which might confirm, or destroy her hope, though she did not\par
recollect to have ever heard him sing; but the voice, and the\par
instrument, now ceased.\par
\par
She considered for a moment whether she should venture to speak: then,\par
not choosing, lest it should be he, to mention his name, and yet too\par
much interested to neglect the opportunity of enquiring, she called\par
from the casement, “Is that song from Gascony?” Her anxious attention\par
was not cheered by any reply; everything remained silent. Her\par
impatience increasing with her fears, she repeated the question; but\par
still no sound was heard, except the sighings of the wind among the\par
battlements above; and she endeavoured to console herself with a\par
belief, that the stranger, whoever he was, had retired, before she had\par
spoken, beyond the reach of her voice, which, it appeared certain, had\par
Valancourt heard and recognised, he would instantly have replied to.\par
Presently, however, she considered, that a motive of prudence, and not\par
an accidental removal, might occasion his silence; but the surmise that\par
led to this reflection, suddenly changed her hope and joy to terror and\par
grief; for, if Valancourt were in the castle, it was too probable that\par
he was here a prisoner, taken with some of his countrymen, many of whom\par
were at that time engaged in the wars of Italy, or intercepted in some\par
attempt to reach her. Had he even recollected Emily’s voice, he would\par
have feared, in these circumstances, to reply to it, in the presence of\par
the men, who guarded his prison.\par
\par
What so lately she had eagerly hoped she now believed she\par
dreaded;—dreaded to know, that Valancourt was near her; and, while she\par
was anxious to be relieved from her apprehension for his safety, she\par
still was unconscious, that a hope of soon seeing him, struggled with\par
the fear.\par
\par
She remained listening at the casement, till the air began to freshen,\par
and one high mountain in the east to glimmer with the morning; when,\par
wearied with anxiety, she retired to her couch, where she found it\par
utterly impossible to sleep, for joy, tenderness, doubt and\par
apprehension, distracted her during the whole night. Now she rose from\par
the couch, and opened the casement to listen; then she would pace the\par
room with impatient steps, and, at length, return with despondence to\par
her pillow. Never did hours appear to move so heavily, as those of this\par
anxious night; after which she hoped that Annette might appear, and\par
conclude her present state of torturing suspense.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER VI\par
\par
Might we but hear\par
The folded flocks penn’d in their wattled cotes,\par
Or sound of pastoral reed with oaten stops,\par
Or whistle from the lodge, or village cock\par
Count the night watches to his feathery dames,\par
’Twould be some solace yet, some little cheering\par
In this close dungeon of innumerous boughs.\par
                    MILTON\par
\par
\par
In the morning Emily was relieved from her fears for Annette, who came\par
at an early hour.\par
\par
“Here were fine doings in the castle, last night, ma’amselle,” said\par
she, as soon as she entered the room,—“fine doings, indeed! Were you\par
not frightened, ma’amselle, at not seeing me?”\par
\par
“I was alarmed both on your account and on my own,” replied Emily—“What\par
detained you?”\par
\par
“Aye, I said so, I told him so; but it would not do. It was not my\par
fault, indeed, ma’amselle, for I could not get out. That rogue Ludovico\par
locked me up again.”\par
\par
“Locked you up!” said Emily, with displeasure, “Why do you permit\par
Ludovico to lock you up?”\par
\par
“Holy Saints!” exclaimed Annette, “how can I help it! If he will lock\par
the door, ma’amselle, and take away the key, how am I to get out,\par
unless I jump through the window? But that I should not mind so much,\par
if the casements here were not all so high; one can hardly scramble up\par
to them on the inside, and one should break one’s neck, I suppose,\par
going down on the outside. But you know, I dare say, ma’am, what a\par
hurly-burly the castle was in last night; you must have heard some of\par
the uproar.”\par
\par
“What, were they disputing, then?” said Emily.\par
\par
“No, ma’amselle, nor fighting, but almost as good, for I believe there\par
was not one of the Signors sober; and what is more, not one of those\par
fine ladies sober, either. I thought, when I saw them first, that all\par
those fine silks and fine veils,—why, ma’amselle, their veils were\par
worked with silver! and fine trimmings—boded no good—I guessed what\par
they were!”\par
\par
“Good God!” exclaimed Emily, “what will become of me!”\par
\par
“Aye, ma’am, Ludovico said much the same thing of me. ‘Good God!’ said\par
he, ‘Annette, what is to become of you, if you are to go running about\par
the castle among all these drunken Signors?’\par
\par
“‘O!’ says I, ‘for that matter, I only want to go to my young lady’s\par
chamber, and I have only to go, you know, along the vaulted passage and\par
across the great hall and up the marble staircase and along the north\par
gallery and through the west wing of the castle and I am in the\par
corridor in a minute.’ ‘Are you so?’ says he, ‘and what is to become of\par
you, if you meet any of those noble cavaliers in the way?’ ‘Well,’ says\par
I, ‘if you think there is danger, then, go with me, and guard me; I am\par
never afraid when you are by.’ ‘What!’ says he, ‘when I am scarcely\par
recovered of one wound, shall I put myself in the way of getting\par
another? for if any of the cavaliers meet you, they will fall\par
a-fighting with me directly. No, no,’ says he, ‘I will cut the way\par
shorter, than through the vaulted passage and up the marble staircase,\par
and along the north gallery and through the west wing of the castle,\par
for you shall stay here, Annette; you shall not go out of this room,\par
tonight.’ So, with that I says—”\par
\par
“Well, well,” said Emily, impatiently, and anxious to enquire on\par
another subject,—“so he locked you up?”\par
\par
“Yes, he did indeed, ma’amselle, notwithstanding all I could say to the\par
contrary; and Caterina and I and he staid there all night. And in a few\par
minutes after I was not so vexed, for there came Signor Verezzi roaring\par
along the passage, like a mad bull, and he mistook Ludovico’s hall, for\par
old Carlo’s; so he tried to burst open the door, and called out for\par
more wine, for that he had drunk all the flasks dry, and was dying of\par
thirst. So we were all as still as night, that he might suppose there\par
was nobody in the room; but the Signor was as cunning as the best of\par
us, and kept calling out at the door, ‘Come forth, my ancient hero!’\par
said he, ‘here is no enemy at the gate, that you need hide yourself:\par
come forth, my valorous Signor Steward!’ Just then old Carlo opened his\par
door, and he came with a flask in his hand; for, as soon as the Signor\par
saw him, he was as tame as could be, and followed him away as naturally\par
as a dog does a butcher with a piece of meat in his basket. All this I\par
saw through the key-hole. ‘Well, Annette,’ said Ludovico, jeeringly,\par
‘shall I let you out now?’ ‘O no,’ says I, ‘I would not—’”\par
\par
“I have some questions to ask you on another subject,” interrupted\par
Emily, quite wearied by this story. “Do you know whether there are any\par
prisoners in the castle, and whether they are confined at this end of\par
the edifice?”\par
\par
“I was not in the way, ma’amselle,” replied Annette, “when the first\par
party came in from the mountains, and the last party is not come back\par
yet, so I don’t know, whether there are any prisoners; but it is\par
expected back tonight, or tomorrow, and I shall know then, perhaps.”\par
\par
Emily enquired if she had ever heard the servants talk of prisoners.\par
\par
“Ah ma’amselle!” said Annette archly, “now I dare say you are thinking\par
of Monsieur Valancourt, and that he may have come among the armies,\par
which, they say, are come from our country, to fight against this\par
state, and that he has met with some of _our_ people, and is taken\par
captive. O Lord! how glad I should be, if it was so!”\par
\par
“Would you, indeed, be glad?” said Emily, in a tone of mournful\par
reproach.\par
\par
“To be sure I should, ma’am,” replied Annette, “and would not you be\par
glad too, to see Signor Valancourt? I don’t know any chevalier I like\par
better, I have a very great regard for the Signor, truly.”\par
\par
“Your regard for him cannot be doubted,” said Emily, “since you wish to\par
see him a prisoner.”\par
\par
“Why no, ma’amselle, not a prisoner either; but one must be glad to see\par
him, you know. And it was only the other night I dreamt—I dreamt I saw\par
him drive into the castle-yard all in a coach and six, and dressed out,\par
with a laced coat and a sword, like a lord as he is.”\par
\par
Emily could not forbear smiling at Annette’s ideas of Valancourt, and\par
repeated her enquiry, whether she had heard the servants talk of\par
prisoners.\par
\par
“No, ma’amselle,” replied she, “never; and lately they have done\par
nothing but talk of the apparition, that has been walking about of a\par
night on the ramparts, and that frightened the sentinels into fits. It\par
came among them like a flash of fire, they say, and they all fell down\par
in a row, till they came to themselves again; and then it was gone, and\par
nothing to be seen but the old castle walls; so they helped one another\par
up again as fast as they could. You would not believe, ma’amselle,\par
though I showed you the very cannon, where it used to appear.”\par
\par
“And are you, indeed, so simple, Annette,” said Emily, smiling at this\par
curious exaggeration of the circumstances she had witnessed, “as to\par
credit these stories?”\par
\par
“Credit them, ma’amselle! why all the world could not persuade me out\par
of them. Roberto and Sebastian and half a dozen more of them went into\par
fits! To be sure, there was no occasion for that; I said, myself, there\par
was no need of that, for, says I, when the enemy comes, what a pretty\par
figure they will cut, if they are to fall down in fits, all of a row!\par
The enemy won’t be so civil, perhaps, as to walk off, like the ghost,\par
and leave them to help one another up, but will fall to, cutting and\par
slashing, till he makes them all rise up dead men. No, no, says I,\par
there is reason in all things: though I might have fallen down in a fit\par
that was no rule for them, being, because it is no business of mine to\par
look gruff, and fight battles.”\par
\par
Emily endeavoured to correct the superstitious weakness of Annette,\par
though she could not entirely subdue her own; to which the latter only\par
replied, “Nay, ma’amselle, you will believe nothing; you are almost as\par
bad as the Signor himself, who was in a great passion when they told of\par
what had happened, and swore that the first man, who repeated such\par
nonsense, should be thrown into the dungeon under the east turret. This\par
was a hard punishment too, for only talking nonsense, as he called it,\par
but I dare say he had other reasons for calling it so, than you have,\par
ma’am.”\par
\par
Emily looked displeased, and made no reply. As she mused upon the\par
recollected appearance, which had lately so much alarmed her, and\par
considered the circumstances of the figure having stationed itself\par
opposite to her casement, she was for a moment inclined to believe it\par
was Valancourt, whom she had seen. Yet, if it was he, why did he not\par
speak to her, when he had the opportunity of doing so—and, if he was a\par
prisoner in the castle, and he could be here in no other character, how\par
could he obtain the means of walking abroad on the rampart? Thus she\par
was utterly unable to decide, whether the musician and the form she had\par
observed, were the same, or, if they were, whether this was Valancourt.\par
She, however, desired that Annette would endeavour to learn whether any\par
prisoners were in the castle, and also their names.\par
\par
“O dear, ma’amselle!” said Annette, “I forget to tell you what you bade\par
me ask about, the ladies, as they call themselves, who are lately come\par
to Udolpho. Why that Signora Livona, that the Signor brought to see my\par
late lady at Venice, is his mistress now, and was little better then, I\par
dare say. And Ludovico says (but pray be secret, ma’am) that his\par
_Excellenza_ introduced her only to impose upon the world, that had\par
begun to make free with her character. So when people saw my lady\par
notice her, they thought what they had heard must be scandal. The other\par
two are the mistresses of Signor Verezzi and Signor Bertolini; and\par
Signor Montoni invited them all to the castle; and so, yesterday, he\par
gave a great entertainment; and there they were, all drinking Tuscany\par
wine and all sorts, and laughing and singing, till they made the castle\par
ring again. But I thought they were dismal sounds, so soon after my\par
poor lady’s death too; and they brought to my mind what she would have\par
thought, if she had heard them—but she cannot hear them now, poor soul!\par
said I.”\par
\par
Emily turned away to conceal her emotion, and then desired Annette to\par
go, and make enquiry, concerning the prisoners, that might be in the\par
castle, but conjured her to do it with caution, and on no account to\par
mention her name, or that of Monsieur Valancourt.\par
\par
“Now I think of it, ma’amselle,” said Annette, “I do believe there are\par
prisoners, for I overheard one of the Signor’s men, yesterday, in the\par
servants hall, talking something about ransoms, and saying what a fine\par
thing it was for his _Excellenza_ to catch up men, and they were as\par
good booty as any other, because of the ransoms. And the other man was\par
grumbling, and saying it was fine enough for the Signor, but none so\par
fine for his soldiers, because, said he, we don’t go shares there.”\par
\par
This information heightened Emily’s impatience to know more, and\par
Annette immediately departed on her enquiry.\par
\par
The late resolution of Emily to resign her estates to Montoni, now gave\par
way to new considerations; the possibility, that Valancourt was near\par
her, revived her fortitude, and she determined to brave the threatened\par
vengeance, at least, till she could be assured whether he was really in\par
the castle. She was in this temper of mind, when she received a message\par
from Montoni, requiring her attendance in the cedar parlour, which she\par
obeyed with trembling, and, on her way thither, endeavoured to animate\par
her fortitude with the idea of Valancourt.\par
\par
Montoni was alone. “I sent for you,” said he, “to give you another\par
opportunity of retracting your late mistaken assertions concerning the\par
Languedoc estates. I will condescend to advise, where I may command.—If\par
you are really deluded by an opinion, that you have any right to these\par
estates, at least, do not persist in the error—an error, which you may\par
perceive, too late, has been fatal to you. Dare my resentment no\par
further, but sign the papers.”\par
\par
“If I have no right in these estates, sir,” said Emily, “of what\par
service can it be to you, that I should sign any papers, concerning\par
them? If the lands are yours by law, you certainly may possess them,\par
without my interference, or my consent.”\par
\par
“I will have no more argument,” said Montoni, with a look that made her\par
tremble. “What had I but trouble to expect, when I condescended to\par
reason with a baby! But I will be trifled with no longer: let the\par
recollection of your aunt’s sufferings, in consequence of her folly and\par
obstinacy, teach you a lesson.—Sign the papers.”\par
\par
Emily’s resolution was for a moment awed:—she shrunk at the\par
recollections he revived, and from the vengeance he threatened; but\par
then, the image of Valancourt, who so long had loved her, and who was\par
now, perhaps, so near her, came to her heart, and, together with the\par
strong feelings of indignation, with which she had always, from her\par
infancy, regarded an act of injustice, inspired her with a noble,\par
though imprudent, courage.\par
\par
“Sign the papers,” said Montoni, more impatiently than before.\par
\par
“Never, sir,” replied Emily; “that request would have proved to me the\par
injustice of your claim, had I even been ignorant of my right.”\par
\par
Montoni turned pale with anger, while his quivering lip and lurking eye\par
made her almost repent the boldness of her speech.\par
\par
“Then all my vengeance falls upon you,” he exclaimed, with a horrible\par
oath. “And think not it shall be delayed. Neither the estates in\par
Languedoc, nor Gascony, shall be yours; you have dared to question my\par
right,—now dare to question my power. I have a punishment which you\par
think not of; it is terrible! This night—this very night—”\par
\par
“This night!” repeated another voice.\par
\par
Montoni paused, and turned half round, but, seeming to recollect\par
himself, he proceeded in a lower tone.\par
\par
“You have lately seen one terrible example of obstinacy and folly; yet\par
this, it appears, has not been sufficient to deter you.—I could tell\par
you of others—I could make you tremble at the bare recital.”\par
\par
He was interrupted by a groan, which seemed to rise from underneath the\par
chamber they were in; and, as he threw a glance round it, impatience\par
and rage flashed from his eyes, yet something like a shade of fear\par
passed over his countenance. Emily sat down in a chair, near the door,\par
for the various emotions she had suffered, now almost overcame her; but\par
Montoni paused scarcely an instant, and, commanding his features,\par
resumed his discourse in a lower, yet sterner voice.\par
\par
“I say, I could give you other instances of my power and of my\par
character, which it seems you do not understand, or you would not defy\par
me.—I could tell you, that, when once my resolution is taken—but I am\par
talking to a baby. Let me, however, repeat, that terrible as are the\par
examples I could recite, the recital could not now benefit you; for,\par
though your repentance would put an immediate end to opposition, it\par
would not now appease my indignation.—I will have vengeance as well as\par
justice.”\par
\par
Another groan filled the pause which Montoni made.\par
\par
“Leave the room instantly!” said he, seeming not to notice this strange\par
occurrence. Without power to implore his pity, she rose to go, but\par
found that she could not support herself; awe and terror overcame her,\par
and she sunk again into the chair.\par
\par
“Quit my presence!” cried Montoni. “This affectation of fear ill\par
becomes the heroine who has just dared to brave my indignation.”\par
\par
“Did you hear nothing, Signor?” said Emily, trembling, and still unable\par
to leave the room.\par
\par
“I heard my own voice,” rejoined Montoni, sternly.\par
\par
“And nothing else?” said Emily, speaking with difficulty.—“There again!\par
Do you hear nothing now?”\par
\par
“Obey my order,” repeated Montoni. “And for these fool’s tricks—I will\par
soon discover by whom they are practised.”\par
\par
Emily again rose, and exerted herself to the utmost to leave the room,\par
while Montoni followed her; but, instead of calling aloud to his\par
servants to search the chamber, as he had formerly done on a similar\par
occurrence, passed to the ramparts.\par
\par
As, in her way to the corridor, she rested for a moment at an open\par
casement, Emily saw a party of Montoni’s troops winding down a distant\par
mountain, whom she noticed no further than as they brought to her mind\par
the wretched prisoners they were, perhaps, bringing to the castle. At\par
length, having reached her apartment, she threw herself upon the couch,\par
overcome with the new horrors of her situation. Her thoughts lost in\par
tumult and perplexity, she could neither repent of, nor approve, her\par
late conduct; she could only remember, that she was in the power of a\par
man, who had no principle of action—but his will; and the astonishment\par
and terrors of superstition, which had, for a moment, so strongly\par
assailed her, now yielded to those of reason.\par
\par
She was, at length, roused from the reverie which engaged her, by a\par
confusion of distant voices, and a clattering of hoofs, that seemed to\par
come, on the wind, from the courts. A sudden hope, that some good was\par
approaching, seized her mind, till she remembered the troops she had\par
observed from the casement, and concluded this to be the party, which\par
Annette had said were expected at Udolpho.\par
\par
Soon after, she heard voices faintly from the halls, and the noise of\par
horses’ feet sunk away in the wind; silence ensued. Emily listened\par
anxiously for Annette’s step in the corridor, but a pause of total\par
stillness continued, till again the castle seemed to be all tumult and\par
confusion. She heard the echoes of many footsteps, passing to and fro\par
in the halls and avenues below, and then busy tongues were loud on the\par
rampart. Having hurried to her casement, she perceived Montoni, with\par
some of his officers, leaning on the walls, and pointing from them;\par
while several soldiers were employed at the further end of the rampart\par
about some cannon; and she continued to observe them, careless of the\par
passing time.\par
\par
Annette at length appeared, but brought no intelligence of Valancourt,\par
“For, ma’amselle,” said she, “all the people pretend to know nothing\par
about any prisoners. But here is a fine piece of business! The rest of\par
the party are just arrived, ma’am; they came scampering in, as if they\par
would have broken their necks; one scarcely knew whether the man, or\par
his horse would get within the gates first. And they have brought\par
word—and such news! they have brought word, that a party of the enemy,\par
as they call them, are coming towards the castle; so we shall have all\par
the officers of justice, I suppose, besieging it! all those\par
terrible-looking fellows one used to see at Venice.”\par
\par
“Thank God!” exclaimed Emily, fervently, “there is yet a hope left for\par
me, then!”\par
\par
“What mean you, ma’amselle? Do you wish to fall into the hands of those\par
sad-looking men! Why I used to shudder as I passed them, and should\par
have guessed what they were, if Ludovico had not told me.”\par
\par
“We cannot be in worse hands than at present,” replied Emily,\par
unguardedly; “but what reason have you to suppose these are officers of\par
justice?”\par
\par
“Why _our_ people, ma’am, are all in such a fright, and a fuss; and I\par
don’t know anything but the fear of justice, that could make them so. I\par
used to think nothing on earth could fluster them, unless, indeed, it\par
was a ghost, or so; but now, some of them are for hiding down in the\par
vaults under the castle; but you must not tell the Signor this,\par
ma’amselle, and I overheard two of them talking—Holy Mother! what makes\par
you look so sad, ma’amselle? You don’t hear what I say!”\par
\par
“Yes, I do, Annette; pray proceed.”\par
\par
“Well, ma’amselle, all the castle is in such hurly-burly. Some of the\par
men are loading the cannon, and some are examining the great gates, and\par
the walls all round, and are hammering and patching up, just as if all\par
those repairs had never been made, that were so long about. But what is\par
to become of me and you, ma’amselle, and Ludovico? O! when I hear the\par
sound of the cannon, I shall die with fright. If I could but catch the\par
great gate open for one minute, I would be even with it for shutting me\par
within these walls so long!—it should never see me again.”\par
\par
Emily caught the latter words of Annette. “O! if you could find it\par
open, but for one moment!” she exclaimed, “my peace might yet be\par
saved!” The heavy groan she uttered, and the wildness of her look,\par
terrified Annette, still more than her words; who entreated Emily to\par
explain the meaning of them, to whom it suddenly occurred, that\par
Ludovico might be of some service, if there should be a possibility of\par
escape, and who repeated the substance of what had passed between\par
Montoni and herself, but conjured her to mention this to no person\par
except to Ludovico. “It may, perhaps, be in his power,” she added, “to\par
effect our escape. Go to him, Annette, tell him what I have to\par
apprehend, and what I have already suffered; but entreat him to be\par
secret, and to lose no time in attempting to release us. If he is\par
willing to undertake this he shall be amply rewarded. I cannot speak\par
with him myself, for we might be observed, and then effectual care\par
would be taken to prevent our flight. But be quick, Annette, and, above\par
all, be discreet—I will await your return in this apartment.”\par
\par
The girl, whose honest heart had been much affected by the recital, was\par
now as eager to obey, as Emily was to employ her, and she immediately\par
quitted the room.\par
\par
Emily’s surprise increased, as she reflected upon Annette’s\par
intelligence. “Alas!” said she, “what can the officers of justice do\par
against an armed castle? these cannot be such.” Upon further\par
consideration, however, she concluded, that, Montoni’s bands having\par
plundered the country round, the inhabitants had taken arms, and were\par
coming with the officers of police and a party of soldiers, to force\par
their way into the castle. “But they know not,” thought she, “its\par
strength, or the armed numbers within it. Alas! except from flight, I\par
have nothing to hope!”\par
\par
Montoni, though not precisely what Emily apprehended him to be—a\par
captain of banditti—had employed his troops in enterprises not less\par
daring, or less atrocious, than such a character would have undertaken.\par
They had not only pillaged, whenever opportunity offered, the helpless\par
traveller, but had attacked, and plundered the villas of several\par
persons, which, being situated among the solitary recesses of the\par
mountains, were totally unprepared for resistance. In these expeditions\par
the commanders of the party did not appear, and the men, partly\par
disguised, had sometimes been mistaken for common robbers, and, at\par
others, for bands of the foreign enemy, who, at that period, invaded\par
the country. But, though they had already pillaged several mansions,\par
and brought home considerable treasures, they had ventured to approach\par
only one castle, in the attack of which they were assisted by other\par
troops of their own order; from this, however, they were vigorously\par
repulsed, and pursued by some of the foreign enemy, who were in league\par
with the besieged. Montoni’s troops fled precipitately towards Udolpho,\par
but were so closely tracked over the mountains, that, when they reached\par
one of the heights in the neighbourhood of the castle, and looked back\par
upon the road, they perceived the enemy winding among the cliffs below,\par
and at not more than a league distant. Upon this discovery, they\par
hastened forward with increased speed, to prepare Montoni for the\par
enemy; and it was their arrival, which had thrown the castle into such\par
confusion and tumult.\par
\par
As Emily awaited anxiously some information from below, she now saw\par
from her casements a body of troops pour over the neighbouring heights;\par
and, though Annette had been gone a very short time, and had a\par
difficult and dangerous business to accomplish, her impatience for\par
intelligence became painful: she listened; opened her door; and often\par
went out upon the corridor to meet her.\par
\par
At length, she heard a footstep approach her chamber; and, on opening\par
the door, saw, not Annette, but old Carlo! New fears rushed upon her\par
mind. He said he came from the Signor, who had ordered him to inform\par
her, that she must be ready to depart from Udolpho immediately, for\par
that the castle was about to be besieged; and that mules were preparing\par
to convey her, with her guides, to a place of safety.\par
\par
“Of safety!” exclaimed Emily, thoughtlessly; “has, then, the Signor so\par
much consideration for me?”\par
\par
Carlo looked upon the ground, and made no reply. A thousand opposite\par
emotions agitated Emily, successively, as she listened to old Carlo;\par
those of joy, grief, distrust and apprehension, appeared, and vanished\par
from her mind, with the quickness of lightning. One moment, it seemed\par
impossible, that Montoni could take this measure merely for her\par
preservation; and so very strange was his sending her from the castle\par
at all, that she could attribute it only to the design of carrying into\par
execution the new scheme of vengeance, with which he had menaced her.\par
In the next instant, it appeared so desirable to quit the castle, under\par
any circumstances, that she could not but rejoice in the prospect,\par
believing that change must be for the better, till she remembered the\par
probability of Valancourt being detained in it, when sorrow and regret\par
usurped her mind, and she wished, much more fervently than she had yet\par
done, that it might not be his voice which she had heard.\par
\par
Carlo having reminded her, that she had no time to lose, for that the\par
enemy were within sight of the castle, Emily entreated him to inform\par
her whither she was to go; and, after some hesitation, he said he had\par
received no orders to tell; but, on her repeating the question,\par
replied, that he believed she was to be carried into Tuscany.\par
\par
“To Tuscany!” exclaimed Emily—“and why thither?”\par
\par
Carlo answered, that he knew nothing further, than that she was to be\par
lodged in a cottage on the borders of Tuscany, at the feet of the\par
Apennines—“Not a day’s journey distant,” said he.\par
\par
Emily now dismissed him; and, with trembling hands, prepared the small\par
package, that she meant to take with her; while she was employed about\par
which Annette returned.\par
\par
“O ma’amselle!” said she, “nothing can be done! Ludovico says the new\par
porter is more watchful even than Barnardine was, and we might as well\par
throw ourselves in the way of a dragon, as in his. Ludovico is almost\par
as broken-hearted as you are, ma’am, on my account, he says, and I am\par
sure I shall never live to hear the cannon fire twice!”\par
\par
She now began to weep, but revived upon hearing of what had just\par
occurred, and entreated Emily to take her with her.\par
\par
“That I will do most willingly,” replied Emily, “if Signor Montoni\par
permits it;” to which Annette made no reply, but ran out of the room,\par
and immediately sought Montoni, who was on the terrace, surrounded by\par
his officers, where she began her petition. He sharply bade her go into\par
the castle, and absolutely refused her request. Annette, however, not\par
only pleaded for herself, but for Ludovico; and Montoni had ordered\par
some of his men to take her from his presence, before she would retire.\par
\par
In an agony of disappointment, she returned to Emily, who foreboded\par
little good towards herself, from this refusal to Annette, and who,\par
soon after, received a summons to repair to the great court, where the\par
mules, with her guides, were in waiting. Emily here tried in vain to\par
sooth the weeping Annette, who persisted in saying, that she should\par
never see her dear young lady again; a fear, which her mistress\par
secretly thought too well justified, but which she endeavoured to\par
restrain, while, with apparent composure, she bade this affectionate\par
servant farewell. Annette, however, followed to the courts, which were\par
now thronged with people, busy in preparation for the enemy; and,\par
having seen her mount her mule and depart, with her attendants, through\par
the portal, turned into the castle and wept again.\par
\par
Emily, meanwhile, as she looked back upon the gloomy courts of the\par
castle, no longer silent as when she had first entered them, but\par
resounding with the noise of preparation for their defence, as well as\par
crowded with soldiers and workmen, hurrying to and fro; and, when she\par
passed once more under the huge portcullis, which had formerly struck\par
her with terror and dismay, and, looking round, saw no walls to confine\par
her steps—felt, in spite of anticipation, the sudden joy of a prisoner,\par
who unexpectedly finds himself at liberty. This emotion would not\par
suffer her now to look impartially on the dangers that awaited her\par
without; on mountains infested by hostile parties, who seized every\par
opportunity for plunder; and on a journey commenced under the guidance\par
of men, whose countenances certainly did not speak favourably of their\par
dispositions. In the present moments, she could only rejoice, that she\par
was liberated from those walls, which she had entered with such dismal\par
forebodings; and, remembering the superstitious presentiment, which had\par
then seized her, she could now smile at the impression it had made upon\par
her mind.\par
\par
As she gazed, with these emotions, upon the turrets of the castle,\par
rising high over the woods, among which she wound, the stranger, whom\par
she believed to be confined there, returned to her remembrance, and\par
anxiety and apprehension, lest he should be Valancourt, again passed\par
like a cloud upon her joy. She recollected every circumstance,\par
concerning this unknown person, since the night, when she had first\par
heard him play the song of her native province;—circumstances, which\par
she had so often recollected, and compared before, without extracting\par
from them anything like conviction, and which still only prompted her\par
to believe, that Valancourt was a prisoner at Udolpho. It was possible,\par
however, that the men, who were her conductors, might afford her\par
information, on this subject; but, fearing to question them\par
immediately, lest they should be unwilling to discover any circumstance\par
to her in the presence of each other, she watched for an opportunity of\par
speaking with them separately.\par
\par
Soon after, a trumpet echoed faintly from a distance; the guides\par
stopped, and looked toward the quarter whence it came, but the thick\par
woods, which surrounded them, excluding all view of the country beyond,\par
one of the men rode on to the point of an eminence, that afforded a\par
more extensive prospect, to observe how near the enemy, whose trumpet\par
he guessed this to be, were advanced; the other, meanwhile, remained\par
with Emily, and to him she put some questions, concerning the stranger\par
at Udolpho. Ugo, for this was his name, said, that there were several\par
prisoners in the castle, but he neither recollected their persons, nor\par
the precise time of their arrival, and could therefore give her no\par
information. There was a surliness in his manner, as he spoke, that\par
made it probable he would not have satisfied her enquiries, even if he\par
could have done so.\par
\par
Having asked him what prisoners had been taken, about the time, as\par
nearly as she could remember, when she had first heard the music, “All\par
that week,” said Ugo, “I was out with a party, upon the mountains, and\par
knew nothing of what was doing at the castle. We had enough upon our\par
hands, we had warm work of it.”\par
\par
Bertrand, the other man, being now returned, Emily enquired no further,\par
and, when he had related to his companion what he had seen, they\par
travelled on in deep silence; while Emily often caught, between the\par
opening woods, partial glimpses of the castle above—the west towers,\par
whose battlements were now crowded with archers, and the ramparts\par
below, where soldiers were seen hurrying along, or busy upon the walls,\par
preparing the cannon.\par
\par
Having emerged from the woods, they wound along the valley in an\par
opposite direction to that, from whence the enemy were approaching.\par
Emily now had a full view of Udolpho, with its grey walls, towers and\par
terraces, high over-topping the precipices and the dark woods, and\par
glittering partially with the arms of the _Condottieri_, as the sun’s\par
rays, streaming through an autumnal cloud, glanced upon a part of the\par
edifice, whose remaining features stood in darkened majesty. She\par
continued to gaze, through her tears, upon walls that, perhaps,\par
confined Valancourt, and which now, as the cloud floated away, were\par
lighted up with sudden splendour, and then, as suddenly were shrouded\par
in gloom; while the passing gleam fell on the wood-tops below, and\par
heightened the first tints of autumn, that had begun to steal upon the\par
foliage. The winding mountains, at length, shut Udolpho from her view,\par
and she turned, with mournful reluctance, to other objects. The\par
melancholy sighing of the wind among the pines, that waved high over\par
the steeps, and the distant thunder of a torrent assisted her musings,\par
and conspired with the wild scenery around, to diffuse over her mind\par
emotions solemn, yet not unpleasing, but which were soon interrupted by\par
the distant roar of cannon, echoing among the mountains. The sounds\par
rolled along the wind, and were repeated in faint and fainter\par
reverberation, till they sunk in sullen murmurs. This was a signal,\par
that the enemy had reached the castle, and fear for Valancourt again\par
tormented Emily. She turned her anxious eyes towards that part of the\par
country, where the edifice stood, but the intervening heights concealed\par
it from her view; still, however, she saw the tall head of a mountain,\par
which immediately fronted her late chamber, and on this she fixed her\par
gaze, as if it could have told her of all that was passing in the scene\par
it overlooked. The guides twice reminded her, that she was losing time\par
and that they had far to go, before she could turn from this\par
interesting object, and, even when she again moved onward, she often\par
sent a look back, till only its blue point, brightening in a gleam of\par
sunshine, appeared peeping over other mountains.\par
\par
The sound of the cannon affected Ugo, as the blast of the trumpet does\par
the war-horse; it called forth all the fire of his nature; he was\par
impatient to be in the midst of the fight, and uttered frequent\par
execrations against Montoni for having sent him to a distance. The\par
feelings of his comrade seemed to be very opposite, and adapted rather\par
to the cruelties, than to the dangers of war.\par
\par
Emily asked frequent questions, concerning the place of her\par
destination, but could only learn, that she was going to a cottage in\par
Tuscany; and, whenever she mentioned the subject, she fancied she\par
perceived, in the countenances of these men, an expression of malice\par
and cunning, that alarmed her.\par
\par
It was afternoon, when they had left the castle. During several hours,\par
they travelled through regions of profound solitude, where no bleat of\par
sheep, or bark of watch-dog, broke on silence, and they were now too\par
far off to hear even the faint thunder of the cannon. Towards evening,\par
they wound down precipices, black with forests of cypress, pine and\par
cedar, into a glen so savage and secluded, that, if Solitude ever had\par
local habitation, this might have been “her place of dearest\par
residence.” To Emily it appeared a spot exactly suited for the retreat\par
of banditti, and, in her imagination, she already saw them lurking\par
under the brow of some projecting rock, whence their shadows,\par
lengthened by the setting sun, stretched across the road, and warned\par
the traveller of his danger. She shuddered at the idea, and, looking at\par
her conductors, to observe whether they were armed, thought she saw in\par
them the banditti she dreaded!\par
\par
It was in this glen, that they proposed to alight, “For,” said Ugo,\par
“night will come on presently, and then the wolves will make it\par
dangerous to stop.” This was a new subject of alarm to Emily, but\par
inferior to what she suffered from the thought of being left in these\par
wilds, at midnight, with two such men as her present conductors. Dark\par
and dreadful hints of what might be Montoni’s purpose in sending her\par
hither, came to her mind. She endeavoured to dissuade the men from\par
stopping, and enquired, with anxiety, how far they had yet to go.\par
\par
“Many leagues yet,” replied Bertrand. “As for you, Signora, you may do\par
as you please about eating, but for us, we will make a hearty supper,\par
while we can. We shall have need of it, I warrant, before we finish our\par
journey. The sun’s going down apace; let us alight under that rock,\par
yonder.”\par
\par
His comrade assented, and, turning the mules out of the road, they\par
advanced towards a cliff, overhung with cedars, Emily following in\par
trembling silence. They lifted her from her mule, and, having seated\par
themselves on the grass, at the foot of the rocks, drew some homely\par
fare from a wallet, of which Emily tried to eat a little, the better to\par
disguise her apprehensions.\par
\par
The sun was now sunk behind the high mountains in the west, upon which\par
a purple haze began to spread, and the gloom of twilight to draw over\par
the surrounding objects. To the low and sullen murmur of the breeze,\par
passing among the woods, she no longer listened with any degree of\par
pleasure, for it conspired with the wildness of the scene and the\par
evening hour, to depress her spirits.\par
\par
Suspense had so much increased her anxiety, as to the prisoner at\par
Udolpho, that, finding it impracticable to speak alone with Bertrand,\par
on that subject, she renewed her questions in the presence of Ugo; but\par
he either was, or pretended to be entirely ignorant, concerning the\par
stranger. When he had dismissed the question, he talked with Ugo on\par
some subject, which led to the mention of Signor Orsino and of the\par
affair that had banished him from Venice; respecting which Emily had\par
ventured to ask a few questions. Ugo appeared to be well acquainted\par
with the circumstances of that tragical event, and related some minute\par
particulars, that both shocked and surprised her; for it appeared very\par
extraordinary how such particulars could be known to any, but to\par
persons, present when the assassination was committed.\par
\par
“He was of rank,” said Bertrand, “or the State would not have troubled\par
itself to enquire after his assassins. The Signor has been lucky\par
hitherto; this is not the first affair of the kind he has had upon his\par
hands; and to be sure, when a gentleman has no other way of getting\par
redress—why he must take this.”\par
\par
“Aye,” said Ugo, “and why is not this as good as another? This is the\par
way to have justice done at once, without more ado. If you go to law,\par
you must stay till the judges please, and may lose your cause, at last.\par
Why the best way, then, is to make sure of your right, while you can,\par
and execute justice yourself.”\par
\par
“Yes, yes,” rejoined Bertrand, “if you wait till justice is done\par
you—you may stay long enough. Why if I want a friend of mine properly\par
served, how am I to get my revenge? Ten to one they will tell me he is\par
in the right, and I am in the wrong. Or, if a fellow has got possession\par
of property, which I think ought to be mine, why I may wait, till I\par
starve, perhaps, before the law will give it me, and then, after all,\par
the judge may say—the estate is his. What is to be done then?—Why the\par
case is plain enough, I must take it at last.”\par
\par
Emily’s horror at this conversation was heightened by a suspicion, that\par
the latter part of it was pointed against herself, and that these men\par
had been commissioned by Montoni to execute a similar kind of\par
_justice_, in his cause.\par
\par
“But I was speaking of Signor Orsino,” resumed Bertrand, “he is one of\par
those, who love to do justice at once. I remember, about ten years ago,\par
the Signor had a quarrel with a cavaliero of Milan. The story was told\par
me then, and it is still fresh in my head. They quarrelled about a\par
lady, that the Signor liked, and she was perverse enough to prefer the\par
gentleman of Milan, and even carried her whim so far as to marry him.\par
This provoked the Signor, as well it might, for he had tried to talk\par
reason to her a long while, and used to send people to serenade her,\par
under her windows, of a night; and used to make verses about her, and\par
would swear she was the handsomest lady in Milan—But all would not\par
do—nothing would bring her to reason; and, as I said, she went so far\par
at last, as to marry this other cavaliero. This made the Signor wrath,\par
with a vengeance; he resolved to be even with her though, and he\par
watched his opportunity, and did not wait long, for, soon after the\par
marriage, they set out for Padua, nothing doubting, I warrant, of what\par
was preparing for them. The cavaliero thought, to be sure, he was to be\par
called to no account, but was to go off triumphant; but he was soon\par
made to know another sort of story.”\par
\par
“What then, the lady had promised to have Signor Orsino?” said Ugo.\par
\par
“Promised! No,” replied Bertrand, “she had not wit enough even to tell\par
him she liked him, as I heard, but the contrary, for she used to say,\par
from the first, she never meant to have him. And this was what provoked\par
the Signor, so, and with good reason, for, who likes to be told that he\par
is disagreeable? and this was saying as good. It was enough to tell him\par
this; she need not have gone, and married another.”\par
\par
“What, she married, then, on purpose to plague the Signor?” said Ugo.\par
\par
“I don’t know as for that,” replied Bertrand, “they said, indeed, that\par
she had had a regard for the other gentleman a great while; but that is\par
nothing to the purpose, she should not have married him, and then the\par
Signor would not have been so much provoked. She might have expected\par
what was to follow; it was not to be supposed he would bear her ill\par
usage tamely, and she might thank herself for what happened. But, as I\par
said, they set out for Padua, she and her husband, and the road lay\par
over some barren mountains like these. This suited the Signor’s purpose\par
well. He watched the time of their departure, and sent his men after\par
them, with directions what to do. They kept their distance, till they\par
saw their opportunity, and this did not happen, till the second day’s\par
journey, when, the gentleman having sent his servants forward to the\par
next town, maybe to have horses in readiness, the Signor’s men\par
quickened their pace, and overtook the carriage, in a hollow, between\par
two mountains, where the woods prevented the servants from seeing what\par
passed, though they were then not far off. When we came up, we fired\par
our tromboni, but missed.”\par
\par
Emily turned pale, at these words, and then hoped she had mistaken\par
them; while Bertrand proceeded:\par
\par
“The gentleman fired again, but he was soon made to alight, and it was\par
as he turned to call his people, that he was struck. It was the most\par
dexterous feat you ever saw—he was struck in the back with three\par
stillettos at once. He fell, and was dispatched in a minute; but the\par
lady escaped, for the servants had heard the firing, and came up before\par
she could be taken care of. ‘Bertrand,’ said the Signor, when his men\par
returned—”\par
\par
“Bertrand!” exclaimed Emily, pale with horror, on whom not a syllable\par
of this narrative had been lost.\par
\par
“Bertrand, did I say?” rejoined the man, with some confusion—“No,\par
Giovanni. But I have forgot where I was;—‘Bertrand,’ said the Signor—\par
\par
“Bertrand, again!” said Emily, in a faltering voice, “Why do you repeat\par
that name?”\par
\par
Bertrand swore. “What signifies it,” he proceeded, “what the man was\par
called—Bertrand, or Giovanni—or Roberto? it’s all one for that. You\par
have put me out twice with that—question. Bertrand, or Giovanni—or what\par
you will—‘Bertrand,’ said the Signor, ‘if your comrades had done their\par
duty, as well as you, I should not have lost the lady. Go, my honest\par
fellow, and be happy with this.’ He gave him a purse of gold—and little\par
enough too, considering the service he had done him.”\par
\par
“Aye, aye,” said Ugo, “little enough—little enough.”\par
\par
Emily now breathed with difficulty, and could scarcely support herself.\par
When first she saw these men, their appearance and their connection\par
with Montoni had been sufficient to impress her with distrust; but now,\par
when one of them had betrayed himself to be a murderer, and she saw\par
herself, at the approach of night, under his guidance, among wild and\par
solitary mountains, and going she scarcely knew whither, the most\par
agonizing terror seized her, which was the less supportable from the\par
necessity she found herself under of concealing all symptoms of it from\par
her companions. Reflecting on the character and the menaces of Montoni,\par
it appeared not improbable, that he had delivered her to them, for the\par
purpose of having her murdered, and of thus securing to himself,\par
without further opposition, or delay, the estates, for which he had so\par
long and so desperately contended. Yet, if this was his design, there\par
appeared no necessity for sending her to such a distance from the\par
castle; for, if any dread of discovery had made him unwilling to\par
perpetrate the deed there, a much nearer place might have sufficed for\par
the purpose of concealment. These considerations, however, did not\par
immediately occur to Emily, with whom so many circumstances conspired\par
to rouse terror, that she had no power to oppose it, or to enquire\par
coolly into its grounds; and, if she had done so, still there were many\par
appearances which would too well have justified her most terrible\par
apprehensions. She did not now dare to speak to her conductors, at the\par
sound of whose voices she trembled; and when, now and then, she stole a\par
glance at them, their countenances, seen imperfectly through the gloom\par
of evening, served to confirm her fears.\par
\par
The sun had now been set some time; heavy clouds, whose lower skirts\par
were tinged with sulphureous crimson, lingered in the west, and threw a\par
reddish tint upon the pine forests, which sent forth a solemn sound, as\par
the breeze rolled over them. The hollow moan struck upon Emily’s heart,\par
and served to render more gloomy and terrific every object around\par
her,—the mountains, shaded in twilight—the gleaming torrent, hoarsely\par
roaring—the black forests, and the deep glen, broken into rocky\par
recesses, high overshadowed by cypress and sycamore and winding into\par
long obscurity. To this glen, Emily, as she sent forth her anxious eye,\par
thought there was no end; no hamlet, or even cottage, was seen, and\par
still no distant bark of watch-dog, or even faint, far-off halloo came\par
on the wind. In a tremulous voice, she now ventured to remind the\par
guides, that it was growing late, and to ask again how far they had to\par
go: but they were too much occupied by their own discourse to attend to\par
her question, which she forbore to repeat, lest it should provoke a\par
surly answer. Having, however, soon after, finished their supper, the\par
men collected the fragments into their wallet, and proceeded along this\par
winding glen, in gloomy silence; while Emily again mused upon her own\par
situation, and concerning the motives of Montoni for involving her in\par
it. That it was for some evil purpose towards herself, she could not\par
doubt; and it seemed, that, if he did not intend to destroy her, with a\par
view of immediately seizing her estates, he meant to reserve her a\par
while in concealment, for some more terrible design, for one that might\par
equally gratify his avarice and still more his deep revenge. At this\par
moment, remembering Signor Brochio and his behaviour in the corridor, a\par
few preceding nights, the latter supposition, horrible as it was,\par
strengthened in her belief. Yet, why remove her from the castle, where\par
deeds of darkness had, she feared, been often executed with\par
secrecy?—from chambers, perhaps\par
\par
With many a foul, and midnight murder stain’d.\par
\par
\par
The dread of what she might be going to encounter was now so excessive,\par
that it sometimes threatened her senses; and, often as she went, she\par
thought of her late father and of all he would have suffered, could he\par
have foreseen the strange and dreadful events of her future life; and\par
how anxiously he would have avoided that fatal confidence, which\par
committed his daughter to the care of a woman so weak as was Madame\par
Montoni. So romantic and improbable, indeed, did her present situation\par
appear to Emily herself, particularly when she compared it with the\par
repose and beauty of her early days, that there were moments, when she\par
could almost have believed herself the victim of frightful visions,\par
glaring upon a disordered fancy.\par
\par
Restrained by the presence of her guides from expressing her terrors,\par
their acuteness was, at length, lost in gloomy despair. The dreadful\par
view of what might await her hereafter rendered her almost indifferent\par
to the surrounding dangers. She now looked, with little emotion, on the\par
wild dingles, and the gloomy road and mountains, whose outlines were\par
only distinguishable through the dusk;—objects, which but lately had\par
affected her spirits so much, as to awaken horrid views of the future,\par
and to tinge these with their own gloom.\par
\par
It was now so nearly dark, that the travellers, who proceeded only by\par
the slowest pace, could scarcely discern their way. The clouds, which\par
seemed charged with thunder, passed slowly along the heavens, showing,\par
at intervals, the trembling stars; while the groves of cypress and\par
sycamore, that overhung the rocks, waved high in the breeze, as it\par
swept over the glen, and then rushed among the distant woods. Emily\par
shivered as it passed.\par
\par
“Where is the torch?” said Ugo, “it grows dark.”\par
\par
“Not so dark yet,” replied Bertrand, “but we may find our way, and ’tis\par
best not light the torch, before we can help, for it may betray us, if\par
any straggling party of the enemy is abroad.”\par
\par
Ugo muttered something, which Emily did not understand, and they\par
proceeded in darkness, while she almost wished, that the enemy might\par
discover them; for from change there was something to hope, since she\par
could scarcely imagine any situation more dreadful than her present\par
one.\par
\par
As they moved slowly along, her attention was surprised by a thin\par
tapering flame, that appeared, by fits, at the point of the pike, which\par
Bertrand carried, resembling what she had observed on the lance of the\par
sentinel, the night Madame Montoni died, and which he had said was an\par
omen. The event immediately following it appeared to justify the\par
assertion, and a superstitious impression had remained on Emily’s mind,\par
which the present appearance confirmed. She thought it was an omen of\par
her own fate, and watched it successively vanish and return, in gloomy\par
silence, which was at length interrupted by Bertrand.\par
\par
“Let us light the torch,” said he, “and get under shelter of the\par
woods;—a storm is coming on—look at my lance.”\par
\par
He held it forth, with the flame tapering at its point.*\par
\par
(*Note: See the Abbé Berthelon on Electricity. [A. R.])\par
\par
\par
“Aye,” said Ugo, “you are not one of those, that believe in omens: we\par
have left cowards at the castle, who would turn pale at such a sight. I\par
have often seen it before a thunder-storm, it is an omen of that, and\par
one is coming now, sure enough. The clouds flash fast already.”\par
\par
Emily was relieved by this conversation from some of the terrors of\par
superstition, but those of reason increased, as, waiting while Ugo\par
searched for a flint, to strike fire, she watched the pale lightning\par
gleam over the woods they were about to enter, and illumine the harsh\par
countenances of her companions. Ugo could not find a flint, and\par
Bertrand became impatient, for the thunder sounded hollowly at a\par
distance, and the lightning was more frequent. Sometimes, it revealed\par
the nearer recesses of the woods, or, displaying some opening in their\par
summits, illumined the ground beneath with partial splendour, the thick\par
foliage of the trees preserving the surrounding scene in deep shadow.\par
\par
At length, Ugo found a flint, and the torch was lighted. The men then\par
dismounted, and, having assisted Emily, led the mules towards the\par
woods, that skirted the glen, on the left, over broken ground,\par
frequently interrupted with brush-wood and wild plants, which she was\par
often obliged to make a circuit to avoid.\par
\par
She could not approach these woods, without experiencing keener sense\par
of her danger. Their deep silence, except when the wind swept among\par
their branches, and impenetrable glooms shown partially by the sudden\par
flash, and then, by the red glare of the torch, which served only to\par
make “darkness visible,” were circumstances, that contributed to renew\par
all her most terrible apprehensions; she thought, too, that, at this\par
moment, the countenances of her conductors displayed more than their\par
usual fierceness, mingled with a kind of lurking exultation, which they\par
seemed endeavouring to disguise. To her affrighted fancy it occurred,\par
that they were leading her into these woods to complete the will of\par
Montoni by her murder. The horrid suggestion called a groan from her\par
heart, which surprised her companions, who turned round quickly towards\par
her, and she demanded why they led her thither, beseeching them to\par
continue their way along the open glen, which she represented to be\par
less dangerous than the woods, in a thunder-storm.\par
\par
“No, no,” said Bertrand, “we know best where the danger lies. See how\par
the clouds open over our heads. Besides, we can glide under cover of\par
the woods with less hazard of being seen, should any of the enemy be\par
wandering this way. By holy St. Peter and all the rest of them, I’ve as\par
stout a heart as the best, as many a poor devil could tell, if he were\par
alive again—but what can we do against numbers?”\par
\par
“What are you whining about?” said Ugo, contemptuously, “who fears\par
numbers! Let them come, though they were as many as the Signor’s castle\par
could hold; I would show the knaves what fighting is. For you—I would\par
lay you quietly in a dry ditch, where you might peep out, and see me\par
put the rogues to flight.—Who talks of fear!”\par
\par
Bertrand replied, with a horrible oath, that he did not like such\par
jesting, and a violent altercation ensued, which was, at length,\par
silenced by the thunder, whose deep volley was heard afar, rolling\par
onward till it burst over their heads in sounds, that seemed to shake\par
the earth to its centre. The ruffians paused, and looked upon each\par
other. Between the boles of the trees, the blue lightning flashed and\par
quivered along the ground, while, as Emily looked under the boughs, the\par
mountains beyond, frequently appeared to be clothed in livid flame. At\par
this moment, perhaps, she felt less fear of the storm, than did either\par
of her companions, for other terrors occupied her mind.\par
\par
The men now rested under an enormous chesnut-tree, and fixed their\par
pikes in the ground, at some distance, on the iron points of which\par
Emily repeatedly observed the lightning play, and then glide down them\par
into the earth.\par
\par
“I would we were well in the Signor’s castle!” said Bertrand, “I know\par
not why he should send us on this business. Hark! how it rattles above,\par
there! I could almost find in my heart to turn priest, and pray. Ugo,\par
hast got a rosary?”\par
\par
“No,” replied Ugo, “I leave it to cowards like thee, to carry\par
rosaries—I carry a sword.”\par
\par
“And much good may it do thee in fighting against the storm!” said\par
Bertrand.\par
\par
Another peal, which was reverberated in tremendous echoes among the\par
mountains, silenced them for a moment. As it rolled away, Ugo proposed\par
going on. “We are only losing time here,” said he, “for the thick\par
boughs of the woods will shelter us as well as this chesnut-tree.”\par
\par
They again led the mules forward, between the boles of the trees, and\par
over pathless grass, that concealed their high knotted roots. The\par
rising wind was now heard contending with the thunder, as it rushed\par
furiously among the branches above, and brightened the red flame of the\par
torch, which threw a stronger light forward among the woods, and showed\par
their gloomy recesses to be suitable resorts for the wolves, of which\par
Ugo had formerly spoken.\par
\par
At length, the strength of the wind seemed to drive the storm before\par
it, for the thunder rolled away into distance, and was only faintly\par
heard. After travelling through the woods for nearly an hour, during\par
which the elements seemed to have returned to repose, the travellers,\par
gradually ascending from the glen, found themselves upon the open brow\par
of a mountain, with a wide valley, extending in misty moonlight, at\par
their feet, and above, the blue sky, trembling through the few thin\par
clouds, that lingered after the storm, and were sinking slowly to the\par
verge of the horizon.\par
\par
Emily’s spirits, now that she had quitted the woods, began to revive;\par
for she considered, that, if these men had received an order to destroy\par
her, they would probably have executed their barbarous purpose in the\par
solitary wild, from whence they had just emerged, where the deed would\par
have been shrouded from every human eye. Reassured by this reflection,\par
and by the quiet demeanour of her guides, Emily, as they proceeded\par
silently, in a kind of sheep track, that wound along the skirts of the\par
woods, which ascended on the right, could not survey the sleeping\par
beauty of the vale, to which they were declining, without a momentary\par
sensation of pleasure. It seemed varied with woods, pastures, and\par
sloping grounds, and was screened to the north and the east by an\par
amphitheatre of the Apennines, whose outline on the horizon was here\par
broken into varied and elegant forms; to the west and the south, the\par
landscape extended indistinctly into the lowlands of Tuscany.\par
\par
“There is the sea yonder,” said Bertrand, as if he had known that Emily\par
was examining the twilight view, “yonder in the west, though we cannot\par
see it.”\par
\par
Emily already perceived a change in the climate, from that of the wild\par
and mountainous tract she had left; and, as she continued descending,\par
the air became perfumed by the breath of a thousand nameless flowers\par
among the grass, called forth by the late rain. So soothingly beautiful\par
was the scene around her, and so strikingly contrasted to the gloomy\par
grandeur of those, to which she had long been confined, and to the\par
manners of the people, who moved among them, that she could almost have\par
fancied herself again at La Vallée, and, wondering why Montoni had sent\par
her hither, could scarcely believe, that he had selected so enchanting\par
a spot for any cruel design. It was, however, probably not the spot,\par
but the persons, who happened to inhabit it, and to whose care he could\par
safely commit the execution of his plans, whatever they might be, that\par
had determined his choice.\par
\par
She now ventured again to enquire, whether they were near the place of\par
their destination, and was answered by Ugo, that they had not far to\par
go. “Only to the wood of chesnuts in the valley yonder,” said he,\par
“there, by the brook, that sparkles with the moon; I wish I was once at\par
rest there, with a flask of good wine, and a slice of Tuscany bacon.”\par
\par
Emily’s spirits revived, when she heard, that the journey was so nearly\par
concluded, and saw the wood of chesnuts in an open part of the vale, on\par
the margin of the stream.\par
\par
In a short time they reached the entrance of the wood, and perceived,\par
between the twinkling leaves, a light, streaming from a distant cottage\par
window. They proceeded along the edge of the brook to where the trees,\par
crowding over it, excluded the moonbeams, but a long line of light,\par
from the cottage above, was seen on its dark tremulous surface.\par
Bertrand now stepped on first, and Emily heard him knock, and call\par
loudly at the door. As she reached it, the small upper casement, where\par
the light appeared, was unclosed by a man, who, having enquired what\par
they wanted, immediately descended, let them into a neat rustic cot,\par
and called up his wife to set refreshments before the travellers. As\par
this man conversed, rather apart, with Bertrand, Emily anxiously\par
surveyed him. He was a tall, but not robust, peasant, of a sallow\par
complexion, and had a shrewd and cunning eye; his countenance was not\par
of a character to win the ready confidence of youth, and there was\par
nothing in his manner, that might conciliate a stranger.\par
\par
\par
Ugo called impatiently for supper, and in a tone as if he knew his\par
authority here to be unquestionable. “I expected you an hour ago,” said\par
the peasant, “for I have had Signor Montoni’s letter these three hours,\par
and I and my wife had given you up, and gone to bed. How did you fare\par
in the storm?”\par
\par
“Ill enough,” replied Ugo, “ill enough and we are like to fare ill\par
enough here, too, unless you will make more haste. Get us more wine,\par
and let us see what you have to eat.”\par
\par
The peasant placed before them all that his cottage afforded—ham, wine,\par
figs, and grapes of such size and flavour, as Emily had seldom tasted.\par
\par
After taking refreshment, she was shown by the peasant’s wife to her\par
little bed-chamber, where she asked some questions concerning Montoni,\par
to which the woman, whose name was Dorina, gave reserved answers,\par
pretending ignorance of his _Excellenza_’s intention in sending Emily\par
hither, but acknowledging that her husband had been apprized of the\par
circumstance. Perceiving that she could obtain no intelligence\par
concerning her destination, Emily dismissed Dorina, and retired to\par
repose; but all the busy scenes of her past and the anticipated ones of\par
the future came to her anxious mind, and conspired with the sense of\par
her new situation to banish sleep.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER VII\par
\par
Was nought around but images of rest,\par
Sleep-soothing groves and quiet lawns between,\par
And flowery beds that slumbrous influence kept,\par
From poppies breath’d, and banks of pleasant green,\par
Where never yet was creeping creature seen.\par
Meantime unnumbered glittering streamlets play’d,\par
And hurled everywhere their water’s sheen,\par
That, as they bicker’d through the sunny glade,\par
Though restless still themselves, a lulling murmur made.\par
                    THOMSON\par
\par
\par
When Emily, in the morning, opened her casement, she was surprised to\par
observe the beauties, that surrounded it. The cottage was nearly\par
embowered in the woods, which were chiefly of chesnut intermixed with\par
some cypress, larch and sycamore. Beneath the dark and spreading\par
branches appeared to the north and to the east the woody Apennines,\par
rising in majestic amphitheatre, not black with pines, as she had been\par
accustomed to see them, but their loftiest summits crowned with ancient\par
forests of chesnut, oak, and oriental plane, now animated with the rich\par
tints of autumn, and which swept downward to the valley\par
uninterruptedly, except where some bold rocky promontory looked out\par
from among the foliage, and caught the passing gleam. Vineyards\par
stretched along the feet of the mountains, where the elegant villas of\par
the Tuscan nobility frequently adorned the scene, and overlooked slopes\par
clothed with groves of olive, mulberry, orange and lemon. The plain, to\par
which these declined, was coloured with the riches of cultivation,\par
whose mingled hues were mellowed into harmony by an Italian sun. Vines,\par
their purple clusters blushing between the russet foliage, hung in\par
luxuriant festoons from the branches of standard fig and cherry trees,\par
while pastures of verdure, such as Emily had seldom seen in Italy,\par
enriched the banks of a stream that, after descending from the\par
mountains, wound along the landscape, which it reflected, to a bay of\par
the sea. There, far in the west, the waters, fading into the sky,\par
assumed a tint of the faintest purple, and the line of separation\par
between them was, now and then, discernible only by the progress of a\par
sail, brightened with the sunbeam, along the horizon.\par
\par
The cottage, which was shaded by the woods from the intenser rays of\par
the sun, and was open only to his evening light, was covered entirely\par
with vines, fig-trees and jessamine, whose flowers surpassed in size\par
and fragrance any that Emily had seen. These and ripening clusters of\par
grapes hung round her little casement. The turf, that grew under the\par
woods, was inlaid with a variety of wild flowers and perfumed herbs,\par
and, on the opposite margin of the stream, whose current diffused\par
freshness beneath the shades, rose a grove of lemon and orange trees.\par
This, though nearly opposite to Emily’s window, did not interrupt her\par
prospect, but rather heightened, by its dark verdure, the effect of the\par
perspective; and to her this spot was a bower of sweets, whose charms\par
communicated imperceptibly to her mind somewhat of their own serenity.\par
\par
She was soon summoned to breakfast, by the peasant’s daughter, a girl\par
about seventeen, of a pleasant countenance, which, Emily was glad to\par
observe, seemed animated with the pure affections of nature, though the\par
others, that surrounded her, expressed, more or less, the worst\par
qualities—cruelty, ferocity, cunning and duplicity; of the latter style\par
of countenance, especially, were those of the peasant and his wife.\par
Maddelina spoke little, but what she said was in a soft voice, and with\par
an air of modesty and complacency, that interested Emily, who\par
breakfasted at a separate table with Dorina, while Ugo and Bertrand\par
were taking a repast of Tuscany bacon and wine with their host, near\par
the cottage door; when they had finished which, Ugo, rising hastily,\par
enquired for his mule, and Emily learned that he was to return to\par
Udolpho, while Bertrand remained at the cottage; a circumstance, which,\par
though it did not surprise, distressed her.\par
\par
When Ugo was departed, Emily proposed to walk in the neighbouring\par
woods; but, on being told, that she must not quit the cottage, without\par
having Bertrand for her attendant, she withdrew to her own room. There,\par
as her eyes settled on the towering Apennines, she recollected the\par
terrific scenery they had exhibited and the horrors she had suffered,\par
on the preceding night, particularly at the moment when Bertrand had\par
betrayed himself to be an assassin; and these remembrances awakened a\par
train of images, which, since they abstracted her from a consideration\par
of her own situation, she pursued for some time, and then arranged in\par
the following lines; pleased to have discovered any innocent means, by\par
which she could beguile an hour of misfortune.\par
\par
THE PILGRIM*\par
\par
Slow o’er the Apennine, with bleeding feet,\par
A patient Pilgrim wound his lonely way,\par
To deck the Lady of Loretto’s seat\par
With all the little wealth his zeal could pay.\par
From mountain-tops cold died the evening ray,\par
And, stretch’d in twilight, slept the vale below;\par
And now the last, last purple streaks of day\par
Along the melancholy West fade slow.\par
High o’er his head, the restless pines complain,\par
As on their summit rolls the breeze of night;\par
Beneath, the hoarse stream chides the rocks in vain:\par
The Pilgrim pauses on the dizzy height.\par
Then to the vale his cautious step he press’d,\par
For there a hermit’s cross was dimly seen,\par
Cresting the rock, and there his limbs might rest,\par
Cheer’d in the good man’s cave, by faggot’s sheen,\par
On leafy beds, nor guile his sleep molest.\par
Unhappy Luke! he trusts a treacherous clue!\par
Behind the cliff the lurking robber stood;\par
No friendly moon his giant shadow threw\par
Athwart the road, to save the Pilgrim’s blood;\par
On as he went a vesper-hymn he sang,\par
The hymn, that nightly sooth’d him to repose.\par
Fierce on his harmless prey the ruffian sprang!\par
The Pilgrim bleeds to death, his eye-lids close.\par
Yet his meek spirit knew no vengeful care,\par
But, dying, for his murd’rer breath’d—a sainted pray’r!\par
\par
\par
(*Note: This poem and that entitled _The Traveller_ in vol. ii, have\par
already appeared in a periodical publication. [A. R.])\par
\par
\par
Preferring the solitude of her room to the company of the persons below\par
stairs, Emily dined above, and Maddelina was suffered to attend her,\par
from whose simple conversation she learned, that the peasant and his\par
wife were old inhabitants of this cottage, which had been purchased for\par
them by Montoni, in reward of some service, rendered him, many years\par
before, by Marco, to whom Carlo, the steward at the castle, was nearly\par
related. “So many years ago, Signora,” added Maddelina, “that I know\par
nothing about it; but my father did the Signor a great good, for my\par
mother has often said to him, this cottage was the least he ought to\par
have had.”\par
\par
To the mention of this circumstance Emily listened with a painful\par
interest, since it appeared to give a frightful colour to the character\par
of Marco, whose service, thus rewarded by Montoni, she could scarcely\par
doubt have been criminal; and, if so, had too much reason to believe,\par
that she had been committed into his hands for some desperate purpose.\par
“Did you ever hear how many years it is,” said Emily, who was\par
considering of Signora Laurentini’s disappearance from Udolpho, “since\par
your father performed the services you spoke of?”\par
\par
“It was a little before he came to live at the cottage, Signora,”\par
replied Maddelina, “and that is about eighteen years ago.”\par
\par
This was near the period, when Signora Laurentini had been said to\par
disappear, and it occurred to Emily, that Marco had assisted in that\par
mysterious affair, and, perhaps, had been employed in a murder! This\par
horrible suggestion fixed her in such profound reverie, that Maddelina\par
quitted the room, unperceived by her, and she remained unconscious of\par
all around her, for a considerable time. Tears, at length, came to her\par
relief, after indulging which, her spirits becoming calmer, she ceased\par
to tremble at a view of evils, that might never arrive; and had\par
sufficient resolution to endeavour to withdraw her thoughts from the\par
contemplation of her own interests. Remembering the few books, which\par
even in the hurry of her departure from Udolpho she had put into her\par
little package, she sat down with one of them at her pleasant casement,\par
whence her eyes often wandered from the page to the landscape, whose\par
beauty gradually soothed her mind into gentle melancholy.\par
\par
Here, she remained alone, till evening, and saw the sun descend the\par
western sky, throw all his pomp of light and shadow upon the mountains,\par
and gleam upon the distant ocean and the stealing sails, as he sunk\par
amidst the waves. Then, at the musing hour of twilight, her softened\par
thoughts returned to Valancourt; she again recollected every\par
circumstance, connected with the midnight music, and all that might\par
assist her conjecture, concerning his imprisonment at the castle, and,\par
becoming confirmed in the supposition, that it was his voice she had\par
heard there, she looked back to that gloomy abode with emotions of\par
grief and momentary regret.\par
\par
Refreshed by the cool and fragrant air, and her spirits soothed to a\par
state of gentle melancholy by the still murmur of the brook below and\par
of the woods around, she lingered at her casement long after the sun\par
had set, watching the valley sinking into obscurity, till only the\par
grand outline of the surrounding mountains, shadowed upon the horizon,\par
remained visible. But a clear moonlight, that succeeded, gave to the\par
landscape, what time gives to the scenes of past life, when it softens\par
all their harsher features, and throws over the whole the mellowing\par
shade of distant contemplation. The scenes of La Vallée, in the early\par
morn of her life, when she was protected and beloved by parents equally\par
loved, appeared in Emily’s memory tenderly beautiful, like the prospect\par
before her, and awakened mournful comparisons. Unwilling to encounter\par
the coarse behaviour of the peasant’s wife, she remained supperless in\par
her room, while she wept again over her forlorn and perilous situation,\par
a review of which entirely overcame the small remains of her fortitude,\par
and, reducing her to temporary despondence, she wished to be released\par
from the heavy load of life, that had so long oppressed her, and prayed\par
to Heaven to take her, in its mercy, to her parents.\par
\par
Wearied with weeping, she, at length, lay down on her mattress, and\par
sunk to sleep, but was soon awakened by a knocking at her chamber door,\par
and, starting up in terror, she heard a voice calling her. The image of\par
Bertrand, with a stilletto in his hand, appeared to her alarmed fancy,\par
and she neither opened the door nor answered, but listened in profound\par
silence, till, the voice repeating her name in the same low tone, she\par
demanded who called. “It is I, Signora,” replied the voice, which she\par
now distinguished to be Maddelina’s, “pray open the door. Don’t be\par
frightened, it is I.”\par
\par
“And what brings you here so late, Maddelina?” said Emily, as she let\par
her in.\par
\par
“Hush! signora, for heaven’s sake hush!—if we are overheard I shall\par
never be forgiven. My father and mother and Bertrand are all gone to\par
bed,” continued Maddelina, as she gently shut the door, and crept\par
forward, “and I have brought you some supper, for you had none, you\par
know, Signora, below stairs. Here are some grapes and figs and half a\par
cup of wine.” Emily thanked her, but expressed apprehension lest this\par
kindness should draw upon her the resentment of Dorina, when she\par
perceived the fruit was gone. “Take it back, therefore, Maddelina,”\par
added Emily, “I shall suffer much less from the want of it, than I\par
should do, if this act of good-nature was to subject you to your\par
mother’s displeasure.”\par
\par
“O Signora! there is no danger of that,” replied Maddelina, “my mother\par
cannot miss the fruit, for I saved it from my own supper. You will make\par
me very unhappy, if you refuse to take it, Signora.” Emily was so much\par
affected by this instance of the good girl’s generosity, that she\par
remained for some time unable to reply, and Maddelina watched her in\par
silence, till, mistaking the cause of her emotion, she said, “Do not\par
weep so, Signora! My mother, to be sure, is a little cross, sometimes,\par
but then it is soon over,—so don’t take it so much to heart. She often\par
scolds me, too, but then I have learned to bear it, and, when she has\par
done, if I can but steal out into the woods, and play upon my sticcado,\par
I forget it all directly.”\par
\par
Emily, smiling through her tears, told Maddelina, that she was a good\par
girl, and then accepted her offering. She wished anxiously to know,\par
whether Bertrand and Dorina had spoken of Montoni, or of his designs,\par
concerning herself, in the presence of Maddelina, but disdained to\par
tempt the innocent girl to a conduct so mean, as that of betraying the\par
private conversations of her parents. When she was departing, Emily\par
requested, that she would come to her room as often as she dared,\par
without offending her mother, and Maddelina, after promising that she\par
would do so, stole softly back again to her own chamber.\par
\par
Thus several days passed, during which Emily remained in her own room,\par
Maddelina attending her only at her repast, whose gentle countenance\par
and manners soothed her more than any circumstance she had known for\par
many months. Of her pleasant embowered chamber she now became fond, and\par
began to experience in it those feelings of security, which we\par
naturally attach to home. In this interval also, her mind, having been\par
undisturbed by any new circumstance of disgust, or alarm, recovered its\par
tone sufficiently to permit her the enjoyment of her books, among which\par
she found some unfinished sketches of landscapes, several blank sheets\par
of paper, with her drawing instruments, and she was thus enabled to\par
amuse herself with selecting some of the lovely features of the\par
prospect, that her window commanded, and combining them in scenes, to\par
which her tasteful fancy gave a last grace. In these little sketches\par
she generally placed interesting groups, characteristic of the scenery\par
they animated, and often contrived to tell, with perspicuity, some\par
simple and affecting story, when, as a tear fell over the pictured\par
griefs, which her imagination drew, she would forget, for a moment, her\par
real sufferings. Thus innocently she beguiled the heavy hours of\par
misfortune, and, with meek patience, awaited the events of futurity.\par
\par
A beautiful evening, that had succeeded to a sultry day, at length\par
induced Emily to walk, though she knew that Bertrand must attend her,\par
and, with Maddelina for her companion, she left the cottage, followed\par
by Bertrand, who allowed her to choose her own way. The hour was cool\par
and silent, and she could not look upon the country around her without\par
delight. How lovely, too, appeared the brilliant blue that coloured all\par
the upper region of the air, and, thence fading downward, was lost in\par
the saffron glow of the horizon! Nor less so were the varied shades and\par
warm colouring of the Apennines, as the evening sun threw his slanting\par
rays athwart their broken surface. Emily followed the course of the\par
stream, under the shades, that overhung its grassy margin. On the\par
opposite banks, the pastures were animated with herds of cattle of a\par
beautiful cream-colour; and, beyond, were groves of lemon and orange,\par
with fruit glowing on the branches, frequent almost as the leaves,\par
which partly concealed it. She pursued her way towards the sea, which\par
reflected the warm glow of sunset, while the cliffs, that rose over its\par
edge, were tinted with the last rays. The valley was terminated on the\par
right by a lofty promontory, whose summit, impending over the waves,\par
was crowned with a ruined tower, now serving for the purpose of a\par
beacon, whose shattered battlements and the extended wings of some\par
sea-fowl, that circled near it, were still illumined by the upward\par
beams of the sun, though his disk was now sunk beneath the horizon;\par
while the lower part of the ruin, the cliff on which it stood and the\par
waves at its foot, were shaded with the first tints of twilight.\par
\par
Having reached this headland, Emily gazed with solemn pleasure on the\par
cliffs, that extended on either hand along the sequestered shores, some\par
crowned with groves of pine, and others exhibiting only barren\par
precipices of greyish marble, except where the crags were tufted with\par
myrtle and other aromatic shrubs. The sea slept in a perfect calm; its\par
waves, dying in murmurs on the shores, flowed with the gentlest\par
undulation, while its clear surface reflected in softened beauty the\par
vermeil tints of the west. Emily, as she looked upon the ocean, thought\par
of France and of past times, and she wished, Oh! how ardently, and\par
vainly—wished! that its waves would bear her to her distant native\par
home!\par
\par
“Ah! that vessel,” said she, “that vessel, which glides along so\par
stately, with its tall sails reflected in the water is, perhaps, bound\par
for France! Happy—happy bark!” She continued to gaze upon it, with warm\par
emotion, till the grey of twilight obscured the distance, and veiled it\par
from her view. The melancholy sound of the waves at her feet assisted\par
the tenderness, that occasioned her tears, and this was the only sound,\par
that broke upon the hour, till, having followed the windings of the\par
beach, for some time, a chorus of voices passed her on the air. She\par
paused a moment, wishing to hear more, yet fearing to be seen, and, for\par
the first time, looked back to Bertrand, as her protector, who was\par
following, at a short distance, in company with some other person.\par
Reassured by this circumstance, she advanced towards the sounds, which\par
seemed to arise from behind a high promontory, that projected athwart\par
the beach. There was now a sudden pause in the music, and then one\par
female voice was heard to sing in a kind of chant. Emily quickened her\par
steps, and, winding round the rock, saw, within the sweeping bay,\par
beyond, which was hung with woods from the borders of the beach to the\par
very summit of the cliffs, two groups of peasants, one seated beneath\par
the shades, and the other standing on the edge of the sea, round the\par
girl, who was singing, and who held in her hand a chaplet of flowers,\par
which she seemed about to drop into the waves.\par
\par
Emily, listening with surprise and attention, distinguished the\par
following invocation delivered in the pure and elegant tongue of\par
Tuscany, and accompanied by a few pastoral instruments.\par
\par
TO A SEA-NYMPH\par
\par
O nymph! who loves to float on the green wave,\par
When Neptune sleeps beneath the moonlight hour,\par
Lull’d by the music’s melancholy pow’r,\par
O nymph, arise from out thy pearly cave!\par
\par
For Hesper beams amid the twilight shade,\par
And soon shall Cynthia tremble o’er the tide,\par
Gleam on these cliffs, that bound the ocean’s pride,\par
And lonely silence all the air pervade.\par
\par
Then, let thy tender voice at distance swell,\par
And steal along this solitary shore,\par
Sink on the breeze, till dying—heard no more—\par
Thou wak’st the sudden magic of thy shell.\par
\par
While the long coast in echo sweet replies,\par
Thy soothing strains the pensive heart beguile,\par
And bid the visions of the future smile,\par
O nymph! from out thy pearly cave—arise!\par
\par
(Chorus)—_Arise!_\par
(Semi-chorus)—_Arise!_\par
\par
\par
The last words being repeated by the surrounding group, the garland of\par
flowers was thrown into the waves, and the chorus, sinking gradually\par
into a chant, died away in silence.\par
\par
“What can this mean, Maddelina?” said Emily, awakening from the\par
pleasing trance, into which the music had lulled her. “This is the eve\par
of a festival, Signora,” replied Maddelina; “and the peasants then\par
amuse themselves with all kinds of sports.”\par
\par
“But they talked of a sea-nymph,” said Emily: “how came these good\par
people to think of a sea-nymph?”\par
\par
“O, Signora,” rejoined Maddelina, mistaking the reason of Emily’s\par
surprise, “nobody _believes_ in such things, but our old songs tell of\par
them, and, when we are at our sports, we sometimes sing to them, and\par
throw garlands into the sea.”\par
\par
Emily had been early taught to venerate Florence as the seat of\par
literature and of the fine arts; but, that its taste for classic story\par
should descend to the peasants of the country, occasioned her both\par
surprise and admiration. The Arcadian air of the girls next attracted\par
her attention. Their dress was a very short full petticoat of light\par
green, with a boddice of white silk; the sleeves loose, and tied up at\par
the shoulders with ribbons and bunches of flowers. Their hair, falling\par
in ringlets on their necks, was also ornamented with flowers, and with\par
a small straw hat, which, set rather backward and on one side of the\par
head, gave an expression of gaiety and smartness to the whole figure.\par
When the song had concluded, several of these girls approached Emily,\par
and, inviting her to sit down among them, offered her, and Maddelina,\par
whom they knew, grapes and figs.\par
\par
Emily accepted their courtesy, much pleased with the gentleness and\par
grace of their manners, which appeared to be perfectly natural to them;\par
and when Bertrand, soon after, approached, and was hastily drawing her\par
away, a peasant, holding up a flask, invited him to drink; a\par
temptation, which Bertrand was seldom very valiant in resisting.\par
\par
“Let the young lady join in the dance, my friend,” said the peasant,\par
“while we empty this flask. They are going to begin directly. Strike\par
up! my lads, strike up your tambourines and merry flutes!”\par
\par
They sounded gaily; and the younger peasants formed themselves into a\par
circle, which Emily would readily have joined, had her spirits been in\par
unison with their mirth. Maddelina, however, tripped it lightly, and\par
Emily, as she looked on the happy group, lost the sense of her\par
misfortunes in that of a benevolent pleasure. But the pensive\par
melancholy of her mind returned, as she sat rather apart from the\par
company, listening to the mellow music, which the breeze softened as it\par
bore it away, and watching the moon, stealing its tremulous light over\par
the waves and on the woody summits of the cliffs, that wound along\par
these Tuscan shores.\par
\par
Meanwhile, Bertrand was so well pleased with his first flask, that he\par
very willingly commenced the attack on a second, and it was late before\par
Emily, not without some apprehension, returned to the cottage.\par
\par
After this evening, she frequently walked with Maddelina, but was never\par
unattended by Bertrand; and her mind became by degrees as tranquil as\par
the circumstances of her situation would permit. The quiet, in which\par
she was suffered to live, encouraged her to hope, that she was not sent\par
hither with an evil design; and, had it not appeared probable, that\par
Valancourt was at this time an inhabitant of Udolpho, she would have\par
wished to remain at the cottage, till an opportunity should offer of\par
returning to her native country. But, concerning Montoni’s motive for\par
sending her into Tuscany, she was more than ever perplexed, nor could\par
she believe that any consideration for her safety had influenced him on\par
this occasion.\par
\par
She had been some time at the cottage, before she recollected, that, in\par
the hurry of leaving Udolpho, she had forgotten the papers committed to\par
her by her late aunt, relative to the Languedoc estates; but, though\par
this remembrance occasioned her much uneasiness, she had some hope,\par
that, in the obscure place, where they were deposited, they would\par
escape the detection of Montoni.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER VIII\par
\par
My tongue hath but a heavier tale to say.\par
I play the torturer, by small and small,\par
To lengthen out the worst that must be spoken.\par
                    RICHARD II\par
\par
\par
We now return, for a moment, to Venice, where Count Morano was\par
suffering under an accumulation of misfortunes. Soon after his arrival\par
in that city, he had been arrested by order of the Senate, and, without\par
knowing of what he was suspected, was conveyed to a place of\par
confinement, whither the most strenuous enquiries of his friends had\par
been unable to trace him. Who the enemy was, that had occasioned him\par
this calamity, he had not been able to guess, unless, indeed, it was\par
Montoni, on whom his suspicions rested, and not only with much apparent\par
probability, but with justice.\par
\par
In the affair of the poisoned cup, Montoni had suspected Morano; but,\par
being unable to obtain the degree of proof, which was necessary to\par
convict him of a guilty intention, he had recourse to means of other\par
revenge, than he could hope to obtain by prosecution. He employed a\par
person, in whom he believed he might confide, to drop a letter of\par
accusation into the _Denunzie secrete_, or lions’ mouths, which are\par
fixed in a gallery of the Doge’s palace, as receptacles for anonymous\par
information, concerning persons, who may be disaffected towards the\par
state. As, on these occasions, the accuser is not confronted with the\par
accused, a man may falsely impeach his enemy, and accomplish an unjust\par
revenge, without fear of punishment, or detection. That Montoni should\par
have recourse to these diabolical means of ruining a person, whom he\par
suspected of having attempted his life, is not in the least surprising.\par
In the letter, which he had employed as the instrument of his revenge,\par
he accused Morano of designs against the state, which he attempted to\par
prove, with all the plausible simplicity of which he was master; and\par
the Senate, with whom a suspicion was, at that time, almost equal to a\par
proof, arrested the Count, in consequence of this accusation; and,\par
without even hinting to him his crime, threw him into one of those\par
secret prisons, which were the terror of the Venetians, and in which\par
persons often languished, and sometimes died, without being discovered\par
by their friends.\par
\par
Morano had incurred the personal resentment of many members of the\par
state; his habits of life had rendered him obnoxious to some; and his\par
ambition, and the bold rivalship, which he discovered, on several\par
public occasions,—to others; and it was not to be expected, that mercy\par
would soften the rigour of a law, which was to be dispensed from the\par
hands of his enemies.\par
\par
Montoni, meantime, was beset by dangers of another kind. His castle was\par
besieged by troops, who seemed willing to dare everything, and to\par
suffer patiently any hardships in pursuit of victory. The strength of\par
the fortress, however, withstood their attack, and this, with the\par
vigorous defence of the garrison and the scarcity of provision on these\par
wild mountains, soon compelled the assailants to raise the siege.\par
\par
When Udolpho was once more left to the quiet possession of Montoni, he\par
dispatched Ugo into Tuscany for Emily, whom he had sent from\par
considerations of her personal safety, to a place of greater security,\par
than a castle, which was, at that time, liable to be overrun by his\par
enemies. Tranquillity being once more restored to Udolpho, he was\par
impatient to secure her again under his roof, and had commissioned Ugo\par
to assist Bertrand in guarding her back to the castle. Thus compelled\par
to return, Emily bade the kind Maddelina farewell, with regret, and,\par
after about a fortnight’s stay in Tuscany, where she had experienced an\par
interval of quiet, which was absolutely necessary to sustain her\par
long-harassed spirits, began once more to ascend the Apennines, from\par
whose heights she gave a long and sorrowful look to the beautiful\par
country, that extended at their feet, and to the distant Mediterranean,\par
whose waves she had so often wished would bear her back to France. The\par
distress she felt, on her return towards the place of her former\par
sufferings, was, however, softened by a conjecture, that Valancourt was\par
there, and she found some degree of comfort in the thought of being\par
near him, notwithstanding the consideration, that he was probably a\par
prisoner.\par
\par
It was noon when she had left the cottage, and the evening was closed,\par
long before she came within the neighbourhood of Udolpho. There was a\par
moon, but it shone only at intervals, for the night was cloudy, and,\par
lighted by the torch, which Ugo carried, the travellers paced silently\par
along, Emily musing on her situation, and Bertrand and Ugo anticipating\par
the comforts of a flask of wine and a good fire, for they had perceived\par
for some time the difference between the warm climate of the lowlands\par
of Tuscany and the nipping air of these upper regions. Emily was, at\par
length, roused from her reverie by the far-off sound of the castle\par
clock, to which she listened not without some degree of awe, as it\par
rolled away on the breeze. Another and another note succeeded, and died\par
in sullen murmur among the mountains:—to her mournful imagination it\par
seemed a knell measuring out some fateful period for her.\par
\par
“Aye, there is the old clock,” said Bertrand, “there he is still; the\par
cannon have not silenced him!”\par
\par
“No,” answered Ugo, “he crowed as loud as the best of them in the midst\par
of it all. There he was roaring out in the hottest fire I have seen\par
this many a day! I said that some of them would have a hit at the old\par
fellow, but he escaped, and the tower too.”\par
\par
The road winding round the base of a mountain, they now came within\par
view of the castle, which was shown in the perspective of the valley by\par
a gleam of moonshine, and then vanished in shade; while even a\par
transient view of it had awakened the poignancy of Emily’s feelings.\par
Its massy and gloomy walls gave her terrible ideas of imprisonment and\par
suffering: yet, as she advanced, some degree of hope mingled with her\par
terror; for, though this was certainly the residence of Montoni, it was\par
possibly, also, that of Valancourt, and she could not approach a place,\par
where he might be, without experiencing somewhat of the joy of hope.\par
\par
They continued to wind along the valley, and, soon after, she saw again\par
the old walls and moonlit towers, rising over the woods: the strong\par
rays enabled her, also, to perceive the ravages, which the siege had\par
made,—with the broken walls, and shattered battlements, for they were\par
now at the foot of the steep, on which Udolpho stood. Massy fragments\par
had rolled down among the woods, through which the travellers now began\par
to ascend, and there mingled with the loose earth, and pieces of rock\par
they had brought with them. The woods, too, had suffered much from the\par
batteries above, for here the enemy had endeavoured to screen\par
themselves from the fire of the ramparts. Many noble trees were\par
levelled with the ground, and others, to a wide extent, were entirely\par
stripped of their upper branches. “We had better dismount,” said Ugo,\par
“and lead the mules up the hill, or we shall get into some of the\par
holes, which the balls have left. Here are plenty of them. Give me the\par
torch,” continued Ugo, after they had dismounted, “and take care you\par
don’t stumble over anything, that lies in your way, for the ground is\par
not yet cleared of the enemy.”\par
\par
“How!” exclaimed Emily, “are any of the enemy here, then?”\par
\par
“Nay, I don’t know for that, now,” he replied, “but when I came away I\par
saw one or two of them lying under the trees.”\par
\par
As they proceeded, the torch threw a gloomy light upon the ground, and\par
far among the recesses of the woods, and Emily feared to look forward,\par
lest some object of horror should meet her eye. The path was often\par
strewn with broken heads of arrows, and with shattered remains of\par
armour, such as at that period was mingled with the lighter dress of\par
the soldiers. “Bring the light hither,” said Bertrand, “I have stumbled\par
over something, that rattles loud enough.” Ugo holding up the torch,\par
they perceived a steel breastplate on the ground, which Bertrand\par
raised, and they saw, that it was pierced through, and that the lining\par
was entirely covered with blood; but upon Emily’s earnest entreaties\par
that they would proceed, Bertrand, uttering some joke upon the\par
unfortunate person, to whom it had belonged, threw it hard upon the\par
ground, and they passed on.\par
\par
At every step she took, Emily feared to see some vestige of death.\par
Coming soon after to an opening in the woods, Bertrand stopped to\par
survey the ground, which was encumbered with massy trunks and branches\par
of the trees, that had so lately adorned it, and seemed to have been a\par
spot particularly fatal to the besiegers; for it was evident from the\par
destruction of the trees, that here the hottest fire of the garrison\par
had been directed. As Ugo held again forth the torch, steel glittered\par
between the fallen trees; the ground beneath was covered with broken\par
arms, and with the torn vestments of soldiers, whose mangled forms\par
Emily almost expected to see; and she again entreated her companions to\par
proceed, who were, however, too intent in their examination, to regard\par
her, and she turned her eyes from this desolated scene to the castle\par
above, where she observed lights gliding along the ramparts. Presently,\par
the castle clock struck twelve, and then a trumpet sounded, of which\par
Emily enquired the occasion.\par
\par
“O! they are only changing watch,” replied Ugo. “I do not remember this\par
trumpet,” said Emily, “it is a new custom.” “It is only an old one\par
revived, lady; we always use it in time of war. We have sounded it, at\par
midnight, ever since the place was besieged.”\par
\par
“Hark!” said Emily, as the trumpet sounded again; and, in the next\par
moment, she heard a faint clash of arms, and then the watchword passed\par
along the terrace above, and was answered from a distant part of the\par
castle; after which all was again still. She complained of cold, and\par
begged to go on. “Presently, lady,” said Bertrand, turning over some\par
broken arms with the pike he usually carried. “What have we here?”\par
\par
“Hark!” cried Emily, “what noise was that?”\par
\par
“What noise was it?” said Ugo, starting up and listening.\par
\par
“Hush!” repeated Emily. “It surely came from the ramparts above:” and,\par
on looking up, they perceived a light moving along the walls, while, in\par
the next instant, the breeze swelling, the voice sounded louder than\par
before.\par
\par
“Who goes yonder?” cried a sentinel of the castle. “Speak or it will be\par
worse for you.” Bertrand uttered a shout of joy. “Hah! my brave\par
comrade, is it you?” said he, and he blew a shrill whistle, which\par
signal was answered by another from the soldier on watch; and the\par
party, then passing forward, soon after emerged from the woods upon the\par
broken road, that led immediately to the castle gates, and Emily saw,\par
with renewed terror, the whole of that stupendous structure. “Alas!”\par
said she to herself, “I am going again into my prison!”\par
\par
“Here has been warm work, by St. Marco!” cried Bertrand, waving a torch\par
over the ground; “the balls have torn up the earth here with a\par
vengeance.”\par
\par
“Aye,” replied Ugo, “they were fired from that redoubt, yonder, and\par
rare execution they did. The enemy made a furious attack upon the great\par
gates; but they might have guessed they could never carry it there;\par
for, besides the cannon from the walls, our archers, on the two round\par
towers, showered down upon them at such a rate, that, by holy Peter!\par
there was no standing it. I never saw a better sight in my life; I\par
laughed, till my sides aked, to see how the knaves scampered. Bertrand,\par
my good fellow, thou shouldst have been among them; I warrant thou\par
wouldst have won the race!”\par
\par
“Hah! you are at your old tricks again,” said Bertrand in a surly tone.\par
“It is well for thee thou art so near the castle; thou knowest I have\par
killed my man before now.” Ugo replied only by a laugh, and then gave\par
some further account of the siege, to which as Emily listened, she was\par
struck by the strong contrast of the present scene with that which had\par
so lately been acted here.\par
\par
The mingled uproar of cannon, drums, and trumpets, the groans of the\par
conquered, and the shouts of the conquerors were now sunk into a\par
silence so profound, that it seemed as if death had triumphed alike\par
over the vanquished and the victor. The shattered condition of one of\par
the towers of the great gates by no means confirmed the _valiant_\par
account just given by Ugo of the scampering party, who, it was evident,\par
had not only made a stand, but had done much mischief before they took\par
to flight; for this tower appeared, as far as Emily could judge by the\par
dim moonlight that fell upon it, to be laid open, and the battlements\par
were nearly demolished. While she gazed, a light glimmered through one\par
of the lower loop-holes, and disappeared; but, in the next moment, she\par
perceived through the broken wall, a soldier, with a lamp, ascending\par
the narrow staircase, that wound within the tower, and, remembering\par
that it was the same she had passed up, on the night, when Barnardine\par
had deluded her with a promise of seeing Madame Montoni, fancy gave her\par
somewhat of the terror she had then suffered. She was now very near the\par
gates, over which the soldier having opened the door of the\par
portal-chamber, the lamp he carried gave her a dusky view of that\par
terrible apartment, and she almost sunk under the recollected horrors\par
of the moment, when she had drawn aside the curtain, and discovered the\par
object it was meant to conceal.\par
\par
“Perhaps,” said she to herself, “it is now used for a similar purpose;\par
perhaps, that soldier goes, at this dead hour, to watch over the corpse\par
of his friend!” The little remains of her fortitude now gave way to the\par
united force of remembered and anticipated horrors, for the melancholy\par
fate of Madame Montoni appeared to foretell her own. She considered,\par
that, though the Languedoc estates, if she relinquished them, would\par
satisfy Montoni’s avarice, they might not appease his vengeance, which\par
was seldom pacified but by a terrible sacrifice; and she even thought,\par
that, were she to resign them, the fear of justice might urge him\par
either to detain her a prisoner, or to take away her life.\par
\par
They were now arrived at the gates, where Bertrand, observing the light\par
glimmer through a small casement of the portal-chamber, called aloud;\par
and the soldier, looking out, demanded who was there. “Here, I have\par
brought you a prisoner,” said Ugo, “open the gate, and let us in.”\par
\par
“Tell me first who it is, that demands entrance,” replied the soldier.\par
“What! my old comrade,” cried Ugo, “don’t you know me? not know Ugo? I\par
have brought home a prisoner here, bound hand and foot—a fellow, who\par
has been drinking Tuscany wine, while we here have been fighting.”\par
\par
“You will not rest till you meet with your match,” said Bertrand\par
sullenly. “Hah! my comrade, is it you?” said the soldier—“I’ll be with\par
you directly.”\par
\par
Emily presently heard his steps descending the stairs within, and then\par
the heavy chain fall, and the bolts undraw of a small postern door,\par
which he opened to admit the party. He held the lamp low, to show the\par
step of the gate, and she found herself once more beneath the gloomy\par
arch, and heard the door close, that seemed to shut her from the world\par
for ever. In the next moment, she was in the first court of the castle,\par
where she surveyed the spacious and solitary area, with a kind of calm\par
despair; while the dead hour of the night, the gothic gloom of the\par
surrounding buildings, and the hollow and imperfect echoes, which they\par
returned, as Ugo and the soldier conversed together, assisted to\par
increase the melancholy forebodings of her heart. Passing on to the\par
second court, a distant sound broke feebly on the silence, and\par
gradually swelling louder, as they advanced, Emily distinguished voices\par
of revelry and laughter, but they were to her far other than sounds of\par
joy. “Why, you have got some Tuscany wine among you, _here_,” said\par
Bertrand, “if one may judge by the uproar that is going forward. Ugo\par
has taken a larger share of that than of fighting, I’ll be sworn. Who\par
is carousing at this late hour?”\par
\par
“His _Excellenza_ and the Signors,” replied the soldier: “it is a sign\par
you are a stranger at the castle, or you would not need to ask the\par
question. They are brave spirits, that do without sleep—they generally\par
pass the night in good cheer; would that we, who keep the watch, had a\par
little of it! It is cold work, pacing the ramparts so many hours of the\par
night, if one has no good liquor to warm one’s heart.”\par
\par
“Courage, my lad, courage ought to warm your heart,” said Ugo.\par
“Courage!” replied the soldier sharply, with a menacing air, which Ugo\par
perceiving, prevented his saying more, by returning to the subject of\par
the carousal. “This is a new custom,” said he; “when I left the castle,\par
the Signors used to sit up counselling.”\par
\par
“Aye, and for that matter, carousing too,” replied the soldier, “but,\par
since the siege, they have done nothing but make merry: and if I was\par
they, I would settle accounts with myself, for all my hard fighting,\par
the same way.”\par
\par
They had now crossed the second court, and reached the hall door, when\par
the soldier, bidding them good night, hastened back to his post; and,\par
while they waited for admittance, Emily considered how she might avoid\par
seeing Montoni, and retire unnoticed to her former apartment, for she\par
shrunk from the thought of encountering either him, or any of his\par
party, at this hour. The uproar within the castle was now so loud,\par
that, though Ugo knocked repeatedly at the hall door, he was not heard\par
by any of the servants, a circumstance, which increased Emily’s alarm,\par
while it allowed her time to deliberate on the means of retiring\par
unobserved; for, though she might, perhaps, pass up the great staircase\par
unseen, it was impossible she could find the way to her chamber,\par
without a light, the difficulty of procuring which, and the danger of\par
wandering about the castle, without one, immediately struck her.\par
Bertrand had only a torch, and she knew, that the servants never\par
brought a taper to the door, for the hall was sufficiently lighted by\par
the large tripod lamp, which hung in the vaulted roof; and, while she\par
should wait till Annette could bring a taper, Montoni, or some of his\par
companions, might discover her.\par
\par
The door was now opened by Carlo; and Emily, having requested him to\par
send Annette immediately with a light to the great gallery, where she\par
determined to await her, passed on with hasty steps towards the\par
staircase; while Bertrand and Ugo, with the torch, followed old Carlo\par
to the servants’ hall, impatient for supper and the warm blaze of a\par
wood fire. Emily, lighted only by the feeble rays, which the lamp above\par
threw between the arches of this extensive hall, endeavoured to find\par
her way to the staircase, now hid in obscurity; while the shouts of\par
merriment, that burst from a remote apartment, served, by heightening\par
her terror, to increase her perplexity, and she expected, every\par
instant, to see the door of that room open, and Montoni and his\par
companions issue forth. Having, at length, reached the staircase, and\par
found her way to the top, she seated herself on the last stair, to\par
await the arrival of Annette; for the profound darkness of the gallery\par
deterred her from proceeding farther, and, while she listened for her\par
footstep, she heard only distant sounds of revelry, which rose in\par
sullen echoes from among the arcades below. Once she thought she heard\par
a low sound from the dark gallery behind her; and, turning her eyes,\par
fancied she saw something luminous move in it; and, since she could\par
not, at this moment, subdue the weakness that caused her fears, she\par
quitted her seat, and crept softly down a few stairs lower.\par
\par
Annette not yet appearing, Emily now concluded, that she was gone to\par
bed, and that nobody chose to call her up; and the prospect, that\par
presented itself, of passing the night in darkness, in this place, or\par
in some other equally forlorn (for she knew it would be impracticable\par
to find her way through the intricacies of the galleries to her\par
chamber), drew tears of mingled terror and despondency from her eyes.\par
\par
While thus she sat, she fancied she heard again an odd sound from the\par
gallery, and she listened, scarcely daring to breathe, but the\par
increasing voices below overcame every other sound. Soon after, she\par
heard Montoni and his companions burst into the hall, who spoke, as if\par
they were much intoxicated, and seemed to be advancing towards the\par
staircase. She now remembered, that they must come this way to their\par
chambers, and, forgetting all the terrors of the gallery, hurried\par
towards it with an intention of secreting herself in some of the\par
passages, that opened beyond, and of endeavouring, when the Signors\par
were retired, to find her way to her own room, or to that of Annette,\par
which was in a remote part of the castle.\par
\par
With extended arms, she crept along the gallery, still hearing the\par
voices of persons below, who seemed to stop in conversation at the foot\par
of the staircase, and then pausing for a moment to listen, half fearful\par
of going further into the darkness of the gallery, where she still\par
imagined, from the noise she had heard, that some person was lurking,\par
“They are already informed of my arrival,” said she, “and Montoni is\par
coming himself to seek me! In the present state of his mind, his\par
purpose must be desperate.” Then, recollecting the scene, that had\par
passed in the corridor, on the night preceding her departure from the\par
castle, “O Valancourt!” said she, “I must then resign you for ever. To\par
brave any longer the injustice of Montoni, would not be fortitude, but\par
rashness.” Still the voices below did not draw nearer, but they became\par
louder, and she distinguished those of Verezzi and Bertolini above the\par
rest, while the few words she caught made her listen more anxiously for\par
others. The conversation seemed to concern herself; and, having\par
ventured to step a few paces nearer to the staircase, she discovered,\par
that they were disputing about her, each seeming to claim some former\par
promise of Montoni, who appeared, at first, inclined to appease and to\par
persuade them to return to their wine, but afterwards to be weary of\par
the dispute, and, saying that he left them to settle it as they could,\par
was returning with the rest of the party to the apartment he had just\par
quitted. Verezzi then stopped him. “Where is she? Signor,” said he, in\par
a voice of impatience: “tell us where she is.” “I have already told you\par
that I do not know,” replied Montoni, who seemed to be somewhat\par
overcome with wine; “but she is most probably gone to her apartment.”\par
Verezzi and Bertolini now desisted from their enquiries, and sprang to\par
the staircase together, while Emily, who, during this discourse, had\par
trembled so excessively, that she had with difficulty supported\par
herself, seemed inspired with new strength, the moment she heard the\par
sound of their steps, and ran along the gallery, dark as it was, with\par
the fleetness of a fawn. But, long before she reached its extremity,\par
the light, which Verezzi carried, flashed upon the walls; both\par
appeared, and, instantly perceiving Emily, pursued her. At this moment,\par
Bertolini, whose steps, though swift, were not steady, and whose\par
impatience overcame what little caution he had hitherto used, stumbled,\par
and fell at his length. The lamp fell with him, and was presently\par
expiring on the floor; but Verezzi, regardless of saving it, seized the\par
advantage this accident gave him over his rival, and followed Emily, to\par
whom, however, the light had shown one of the passages that branched\par
from the gallery, and she instantly turned into it. Verezzi could just\par
discern the way she had taken, and this he pursued; but the sound of\par
her steps soon sunk in distance, while he, less acquainted with the\par
passage, was obliged to proceed through the dark, with caution, lest he\par
should fall down a flight of steps, such as in this extensive old\par
castle frequently terminated an avenue. This passage at length brought\par
Emily to the corridor, into which her own chamber opened, and, not\par
hearing any footstep, she paused to take breath, and consider what was\par
the safest design to be adopted. She had followed this passage, merely\par
because it was the first that appeared, and now that she had reached\par
the end of it, was as perplexed as before. Whither to go, or how\par
further to find her way in the dark, she knew not; she was aware only\par
that she must not seek her apartment, for there she would certainly be\par
sought, and her danger increased every instant, while she remained near\par
it. Her spirits and her breath, however, were so much exhausted, that\par
she was compelled to rest, for a few minutes, at the end of the\par
passage, and still she heard no steps approaching. As thus she stood,\par
light glimmered under an opposite door of the gallery, and, from its\par
situation, she knew, that it was the door of that mysterious chamber,\par
where she had made a discovery so shocking, that she never remembered\par
it but with the utmost horror. That there should be light in this\par
chamber, and at this hour, excited her strong surprise, and she felt a\par
momentary terror concerning it, which did not permit her to look again,\par
for her spirits were now in such a state of weakness, that she almost\par
expected to see the door slowly open, and some horrible object appear\par
at it. Still she listened for a step along the passage, and looked up\par
it, where, not a ray of light appearing, she concluded, that Verezzi\par
had gone back for the lamp; and, believing that he would shortly be\par
there, she again considered which way she should go, or rather which\par
way she could find in the dark.\par
\par
A faint ray still glimmered under the opposite door, but so great, and,\par
perhaps, so just was her horror of that chamber, that she would not\par
again have tempted its secrets, though she had been certain of\par
obtaining the light so important to her safety. She was still breathing\par
with difficulty, and resting at the end of the passage, when she heard\par
a rustling sound, and then a low voice, so very near her, that it\par
seemed close to her ear; but she had presence of mind to check her\par
emotions, and to remain quite still; in the next moment, she perceived\par
it to be the voice of Verezzi, who did not appear to know, that she was\par
there, but to have spoken to himself. “The air is fresher here,” said\par
he: “this should be the corridor.” Perhaps, he was one of those heroes,\par
whose courage can defy an enemy better than darkness, and he tried to\par
rally his spirits with the sound of his own voice. However this might\par
be, he turned to the right, and proceeded, with the same stealing\par
steps, towards Emily’s apartment, apparently forgetting, that, in\par
darkness, she could easily elude his search, even in her chamber; and,\par
like an intoxicated person, he followed pertinaciously the one idea,\par
that had possessed his imagination.\par
\par
The moment she heard his steps steal away, she left her station and\par
moved softly to the other end of the corridor, determined to trust\par
again to chance, and to quit it by the first avenue she could find;\par
but, before she could effect this, light broke upon the walls of the\par
gallery, and, looking back, she saw Verezzi crossing it towards her\par
chamber. She now glided into a passage, that opened on the left,\par
without, as she thought, being perceived; but, in the next instant,\par
another light, glimmering at the further end of this passage, threw her\par
into new terror. While she stopped and hesitated which way to go, the\par
pause allowed her to perceive, that it was Annette, who advanced, and\par
she hurried to meet her: but her imprudence again alarmed Emily, on\par
perceiving whom, she burst into a scream of joy, and it was some\par
minutes, before she could be prevailed with to be silent, or to release\par
her mistress from the ardent clasp, in which she held her. When, at\par
length, Emily made Annette comprehend her danger, they hurried towards\par
Annette’s room, which was in a distant part of the castle. No\par
apprehensions, however, could yet silence the latter. “Oh dear\par
ma’amselle,” said she, as they passed along, “what a terrified time\par
have I had of it! Oh! I thought I should have died a hundred times! I\par
never thought I should live to see you again! and I never was so glad\par
to see anybody in my whole life, as I am to see you now.” “Hark!” cried\par
Emily, “we are pursued; that was the echo of steps!” “No, ma’amselle,”\par
said Annette, “it was only the echo of a door shutting; sound runs\par
along these vaulted passages so, that one is continually deceived by\par
it; if one does but speak, or cough, it makes a noise as loud as a\par
cannon.” “Then there is the greater necessity for us to be silent,”\par
said Emily: “pr’ythee say no more, till we reach your chamber.” Here,\par
at length, they arrived, without interruption, and, Annette having\par
fastened the door, Emily sat down on her little bed, to recover breath\par
and composure. To her enquiry, whether Valancourt was among the\par
prisoners in the castle, Annette replied, that she had not been able to\par
hear, but that she knew there were several persons confined. She then\par
proceeded, in her tedious way, to give an account of the siege, or\par
rather a detail of her terrors and various sufferings, during the\par
attack. “But,” added she, “when I heard the shouts of victory from the\par
ramparts, I thought we were all taken, and gave myself up for lost,\par
instead of which, _we_ had driven the enemy away. I went then to the\par
north gallery, and saw a great many of them scampering away among the\par
mountains; but the rampart walls were all in ruins, as one may say, and\par
there was a dismal sight to see down among the woods below, where the\par
poor fellows were lying in heaps, but were carried off presently by\par
their comrades. While the siege was going on, the Signor was here, and\par
there, and everywhere, at the same time, as Ludovico told me, for he\par
would not let me see anything hardly, and locked me up, as he has often\par
done before, in a room in the middle of the castle, and used to bring\par
me food, and come and talk with me as often as he could; and I must\par
say, if it had not been for Ludovico, I should have died outright.”\par
\par
“Well, Annette,” said Emily, “and how have affairs gone on, since the\par
siege?”\par
\par
“O! sad hurly-burly doings, ma’amselle,” replied Annette; “the Signors\par
have done nothing but sit and drink and game, ever since. They sit up,\par
all night, and play among themselves, for all those riches and fine\par
things, they brought in, some time since, when they used to go out\par
a-robbing, or as good, for days together; and then they have dreadful\par
quarrels about who loses, and who wins. That fierce Signor Verezzi is\par
always losing, as they tell me, and Signor Orsino wins from him, and\par
this makes him very wroth, and they have had several hard set-to’s\par
about it. Then, all those fine ladies are at the castle still; and I\par
declare I am frighted, whenever I meet any of them in the passages—”\par
\par
“Surely, Annette,” said Emily starting, “I heard a noise: listen.”\par
After a long pause, “No, ma’amselle,” said Annette, “it was only the\par
wind in the gallery; I often hear it, when it shakes the old doors, at\par
the other end. But won’t you go to bed, ma’amselle? you surely will not\par
sit up starving, all night.” Emily now laid herself down on the\par
mattress, and desired Annette to leave the lamp burning on the hearth;\par
having done which, the latter placed herself beside Emily, who,\par
however, was not suffered to sleep, for she again thought she heard a\par
noise from the passage; and Annette was again trying to convince her,\par
that it was only the wind, when footsteps were distinctly heard near\par
the door. Annette was now starting from the bed, but Emily prevailed\par
with her to remain there, and listened with her in a state of terrible\par
expectation. The steps still loitered at the door, when presently an\par
attempt was made on the lock, and, in the next instant, a voice called.\par
“For heaven’s sake, Annette, do not answer,” said Emily softly, “remain\par
quite still; but I fear we must extinguish the lamp, or its glare will\par
betray us.” “Holy Virgin!” exclaimed Annette, forgetting her\par
discretion, “I would not be in darkness now for the whole world.” While\par
she spoke, the voice became louder than before, and repeated Annette’s\par
name; “Blessed Virgin!” cried she suddenly, “it is only Ludovico.” She\par
rose to open the door, but Emily prevented her, till they should be\par
more certain, that it was he alone; with whom Annette, at length,\par
talked for some time, and learned, that he was come to enquire after\par
herself, whom he had let out of her room to go to Emily, and that he\par
was now returned to lock her in again. Emily, fearful of being\par
overheard, if they conversed any longer through the door, consented\par
that it should be opened, and a young man appeared, whose open\par
countenance confirmed the favourable opinion of him, which his care of\par
Annette had already prompted her to form. She entreated his protection,\par
should Verezzi make this requisite; and Ludovico offered to pass the\par
night in an old chamber, adjoining, that opened from the gallery, and,\par
on the first alarm, to come to their defence.\par
\par
Emily was much soothed by this proposal; and Ludovico, having lighted\par
his lamp, went to his station, while she, once more, endeavoured to\par
repose on her mattress. But a variety of interests pressed upon her\par
attention, and prevented sleep. She thought much on what Annette had\par
told her of the dissolute manners of Montoni and his associates, and\par
more of his present conduct towards herself, and of the danger, from\par
which she had just escaped. From the view of her present situation she\par
shrunk, as from a new picture of terror. She saw herself in a castle,\par
inhabited by vice and violence, seated beyond the reach of law or\par
justice, and in the power of a man, whose perseverance was equal to\par
every occasion, and in whom passions, of which revenge was not the\par
weakest, entirely supplied the place of principles. She was compelled,\par
once more, to acknowledge, that it would be folly, and not fortitude,\par
any longer to dare his power; and, resigning all hopes of future\par
happiness with Valancourt, she determined, that, on the following\par
morning, she would compromise with Montoni, and give up her estates, on\par
condition, that he would permit her immediate return to France. Such\par
considerations kept her waking for many hours; but, the night passed,\par
without further alarm from Verezzi.\par
\par
On the next morning, Emily had a long conversation with Ludovico, in\par
which she heard circumstances concerning the castle, and received hints\par
of the designs of Montoni, that considerably increased her alarms. On\par
expressing her surprise, that Ludovico, who seemed to be so sensible of\par
the evils of his situation, should continue in it, he informed her,\par
that it was not his intention to do so, and she then ventured to ask\par
him, if he would assist her to escape from the castle. Ludovico assured\par
her of his readiness to attempt this, but strongly represented the\par
difficulty of the enterprise, and the certain destruction which must\par
ensue, should Montoni overtake them, before they had passed the\par
mountains; he, however, promised to be watchful of every circumstance,\par
that might contribute to the success of the attempt, and to think upon\par
some plan of departure.\par
\par
Emily now confided to him the name of Valancourt, and begged he would\par
enquire for such a person among the prisoners in the castle; for the\par
faint hope, which this conversation awakened, made her now recede from\par
her resolution of an immediate compromise with Montoni. She determined,\par
if possible, to delay this, till she heard further from Ludovico, and,\par
if his designs were found to be impracticable, to resign the estates at\par
once. Her thoughts were on this subject, when Montoni, who was now\par
recovered from the intoxication of the preceding night, sent for her,\par
and she immediately obeyed the summons. He was alone. “I find,” said\par
he, “that you were not in your chamber, last night; where were you?”\par
Emily related to him some circumstances of her alarm, and entreated his\par
protection from a repetition of them. “You know the terms of my\par
protection,” said he; “if you really value this, you will secure it.”\par
His open declaration, that he would only conditionally protect her,\par
while she remained a prisoner in the castle, showed Emily the necessity\par
of an immediate compliance with his terms; but she first demanded,\par
whether he would permit her immediately to depart, if she gave up her\par
claim to the contested estates. In a very solemn manner he then assured\par
her, that he would, and immediately laid before her a paper, which was\par
to transfer the right of those estates to himself.\par
\par
She was, for a considerable time, unable to sign it, and her heart was\par
torn with contending interests, for she was about to resign the\par
happiness of all her future years—the hope, which had sustained her in\par
so many hours of adversity.\par
\par
After hearing from Montoni a recapitulation of the conditions of her\par
compliance, and a remonstrance, that his time was valuable, she put her\par
hand to the paper; when she had done which, she fell back in her chair,\par
but soon recovered, and desired, that he would give orders for her\par
departure, and that he would allow Annette to accompany her. Montoni\par
smiled. “It was necessary to deceive you,” said he,—“there was no other\par
way of making you act reasonably; you shall go, but it must not be at\par
present. I must first secure these estates by possession: when that is\par
done, you may return to France if you will.”\par
\par
The deliberate villany, with which he violated the solemn engagement he\par
had just entered into, shocked Emily as much, as the certainty, that\par
she had made a fruitless sacrifice, and must still remain his prisoner.\par
She had no words to express what she felt, and knew, that it would have\par
been useless, if she had. As she looked piteously at Montoni, he turned\par
away, and at the same time desired she would withdraw to her apartment;\par
but, unable to leave the room, she sat down in a chair near the door,\par
and sighed heavily. She had neither words nor tears.\par
\par
“Why will you indulge this childish grief?” said he. “Endeavour to\par
strengthen your mind, to bear patiently what cannot now be avoided; you\par
have no real evil to lament; be patient, and you will be sent back to\par
France. At present retire to your apartment.”\par
\par
“I dare not go, sir,” said she, “where I shall be liable to the\par
intrusion of Signor Verezzi.” “Have I not promised to protect you?”\par
said Montoni. “You have promised, sir,”—replied Emily, after some\par
hesitation. “And is not my promise sufficient?” added he sternly. “You\par
will recollect your former promise, Signor,” said Emily, trembling,\par
“and may determine for me, whether I ought to rely upon this.” “Will\par
you provoke me to declare to you, that I will not protect you then?”\par
said Montoni, in a tone of haughty displeasure. “If that will satisfy\par
you, I will do it immediately. Withdraw to your chamber, before I\par
retract my promise; you have nothing to fear there.” Emily left the\par
room, and moved slowly into the hall, where the fear of meeting\par
Verezzi, or Bertolini, made her quicken her steps, though she could\par
scarcely support herself; and soon after she reached once more her own\par
apartment. Having looked fearfully round her, to examine if any person\par
was there, and having searched every part of it, she fastened the door,\par
and sat down by one of the casements. Here, while she looked out for\par
some hope to support her fainting spirits, which had been so long\par
harassed and oppressed, that, if she had not now struggled much against\par
misfortune, they would have left her, perhaps, for ever, she\par
endeavoured to believe, that Montoni did really intend to permit her\par
return to France as soon as he had secured her property, and that he\par
would, in the mean time, protect her from insult; but her chief hope\par
rested with Ludovico, who, she doubted not, would be zealous in her\par
cause, though he seemed almost to despair of success in it. One\par
circumstance, however, she had to rejoice in. Her prudence, or rather\par
her fears, had saved her from mentioning the name of Valancourt to\par
Montoni, which she was several times on the point of doing, before she\par
signed the paper, and of stipulating for his release, if he should be\par
really a prisoner in the castle. Had she done this, Montoni’s jealous\par
fears would now probably have loaded Valancourt with new severities,\par
and have suggested the advantage of holding him a captive for life.\par
\par
Thus passed the melancholy day, as she had before passed many in this\par
same chamber. When night drew on, she would have withdrawn herself to\par
Annette’s bed, had not a particular interest inclined her to remain in\par
this chamber, in spite of her fears; for, when the castle should be\par
still, and the customary hour arrived, she determined to watch for the\par
music, which she had formerly heard. Though its sounds might not enable\par
her positively to determine, whether Valancourt was there, they would\par
perhaps strengthen her opinion that he was, and impart the comfort, so\par
necessary to her present support. But, on the other hand, if all should\par
be silent! She hardly dared to suffer her thoughts to glance that way,\par
but waited, with impatient expectation, the approaching hour.\par
\par
The night was stormy; the battlements of the castle appeared to rock in\par
the wind, and, at intervals, long groans seemed to pass on the air,\par
such as those, which often deceive the melancholy mind, in tempests,\par
and amidst scenes of desolation. Emily heard, as formerly, the\par
sentinels pass along the terrace to their posts, and, looking out from\par
her casement, observed, that the watch was doubled; a precaution, which\par
appeared necessary enough, when she threw her eyes on the walls, and\par
saw their shattered condition. The well-known sounds of the soldiers’\par
march, and of their distant voices, which passed her in the wind, and\par
were lost again, recalled to her memory the melancholy sensation she\par
had suffered, when she formerly heard the same sounds; and occasioned\par
almost involuntary comparisons between her present, and her late\par
situation. But this was no subject for congratulations, and she wisely\par
checked the course of her thoughts, while, as the hour was not yet\par
come, in which she had been accustomed to hear the music, she closed\par
the casement, and endeavoured to await it in patience. The door of the\par
staircase she tried to secure, as usual, with some of the furniture of\par
the room; but this expedient her fears now represented to her to be\par
very inadequate to the power and perseverance of Verezzi; and she often\par
looked at a large and heavy chest, that stood in the chamber, with\par
wishes that she and Annette had strength enough to move it. While she\par
blamed the long stay of this girl, who was still with Ludovico and some\par
other of the servants, she trimmed her wood fire, to make the room\par
appear less desolate, and sat down beside it with a book, which her\par
eyes perused, while her thoughts wandered to Valancourt, and her own\par
misfortunes. As she sat thus, she thought, in a pause of the wind, she\par
distinguished music, and went to the casement to listen, but the loud\par
swell of the gust overcame every other sound. When the wind sunk again,\par
she heard distinctly, in the deep pause that succeeded, the sweet\par
strings of a lute; but again the rising tempest bore away the notes,\par
and again was succeeded by a solemn pause. Emily, trembling with hope\par
and fear, opened her casement to listen, and to try whether her own\par
voice could be heard by the musician; for to endure any longer this\par
state of torturing suspense concerning Valancourt, seemed to be utterly\par
impossible. There was a kind of breathless stillness in the chambers,\par
that permitted her to distinguish from below the tender notes of the\par
very lute she had formerly heard, and with it, a plaintive voice, made\par
sweeter by the low rustling sound, that now began to creep along the\par
wood-tops, till it was lost in the rising wind. Their tall heads then\par
began to wave, while, through a forest of pine, on the left, the wind,\par
groaning heavily, rolled onward over the woods below, bending them\par
almost to their roots; and, as the long-resounding gale swept away,\par
other woods, on the right, seemed to answer the “loud lament;” then,\par
others, further still, softened it into a murmur, that died into\par
silence. Emily listened, with mingled awe and expectation, hope and\par
fear; and again the melting sweetness of the lute was heard, and the\par
same solemn-breathing voice. Convinced that these came from an\par
apartment underneath, she leaned far out of her window, that she might\par
discover whether any light was there; but the casements below, as well\par
as those above, were sunk so deep in the thick walls of the castle,\par
that she could not see them, or even the faint ray, that probably\par
glimmered through their bars. She then ventured to call; but the wind\par
bore her voice to the other end of the terrace, and then the music was\par
heard as before, in the pause of the gust. Suddenly, she thought she\par
heard a noise in her chamber, and she drew herself within the casement;\par
but, in a moment after, distinguishing Annette’s voice at the door, she\par
concluded it was her she had heard before, and she let her in. “Move\par
softly, Annette, to the casement,” said she, “and listen with me; the\par
music is returned.” They were silent till, the measure changing,\par
Annette exclaimed, “Holy Virgin! I know that song well; it is a French\par
song, one of the favourite songs of my dear country.” This was the\par
ballad Emily had heard on a former night, though not the one she had\par
first listened to from the fishing-house in Gascony. “O! it is a\par
Frenchman, that sings,” said Annette: “it must be Monsieur Valancourt.”\par
“Hark! Annette, do not speak so loud,” said Emily, “we may be\par
overheard.” “What! by the Chevalier?” said Annette. “No,” replied Emily\par
mournfully, “but by somebody, who may report us to the Signor. What\par
reason have you to think it is Monsieur Valancourt, who sings? But\par
hark! now the voice swells louder! Do you recollect those tones? I fear\par
to trust my own judgment.” “I never happened to hear the Chevalier\par
sing, Mademoiselle,” replied Annette, who, as Emily was disappointed to\par
perceive, had no stronger reason for concluding this to be Valancourt,\par
than that the musician must be a Frenchman. Soon after, she heard the\par
song of the fishing-house, and distinguished her own name, which was\par
repeated so distinctly, that Annette had heard it also. She trembled,\par
sunk into a chair by the window, and Annette called aloud, “Monsieur\par
Valancourt! Monsieur Valancourt!” while Emily endeavoured to check her,\par
but she repeated the call more loudly than before, and the lute and the\par
voice suddenly stopped. Emily listened, for some time, in a state of\par
intolerable suspense; but, no answer being returned, “It does not\par
signify, Mademoiselle,” said Annette; “it is the Chevalier, and I will\par
speak to him.” “No, Annette,” said Emily, “I think I will speak myself;\par
if it is he, he will know my voice, and speak again.” “Who is it,” said\par
she, “that sings at this late hour?”\par
\par
A long silence ensued, and, having repeated the question, she perceived\par
some faint accents, mingling in the blast, that swept by; but the\par
sounds were so distant, and passed so suddenly, that she could scarcely\par
hear them, much less distinguish the words they uttered, or recognise\par
the voice. After another pause, Emily called again; and again they\par
heard a voice, but as faintly as before; and they perceived, that there\par
were other circumstances, besides the strength, and direction of the\par
wind, to content with; for the great depth, at which the casements were\par
fixed in the castle walls, contributed, still more than the distance,\par
to prevent articulated sounds from being understood, though general\par
ones were easily heard. Emily, however, ventured to believe, from the\par
circumstance of her voice alone having been answered, that the stranger\par
was Valancourt, as well as that he knew her, and she gave herself up to\par
speechless joy. Annette, however, was not speechless. She renewed her\par
calls, but received no answer; and Emily, fearing, that a further\par
attempt, which certainly was, as present, highly dangerous, might\par
expose them to the guards of the castle, while it could not perhaps\par
terminate her suspense, insisted on Annette’s dropping the enquiry for\par
this night; though she determined herself to question Ludovico, on the\par
subject, in the morning, more urgently than she had yet done. She was\par
now enabled to say, that the stranger, whom she had formerly heard, was\par
still in the castle, and to direct Ludovico to that part of it, in\par
which he was confined.\par
\par
Emily, attended by Annette, continued at the casement, for some time,\par
but all remained still; they heard neither lute nor voice again, and\par
Emily was now as much oppressed by anxious joy, as she lately was by a\par
sense of her misfortunes. With hasty steps she paced the room, now half\par
calling on Valancourt’s name, then suddenly stopping, and now going to\par
the casement and listening, where, however, she heard nothing but the\par
solemn waving of the woods. Sometimes her impatience to speak to\par
Ludovico prompted her to send Annette to call him; but a sense of the\par
impropriety of this at midnight restrained her. Annette, meanwhile, as\par
impatient as her mistress, went as often to the casement to listen, and\par
returned almost as much disappointed. She, at length, mentioned Signor\par
Verezzi, and her fear, lest he should enter the chamber by the\par
staircase, door. “But the night is now almost past, Mademoiselle,” said\par
she, recollecting herself; “there is the morning light, beginning to\par
peep over those mountains yonder in the east.”\par
\par
Emily had forgotten, till this moment, that such a person existed as\par
Verezzi, and all the danger that had appeared to threaten her; but the\par
mention of his name renewed her alarm, and she remembered the old\par
chest, that she had wished to place against the door, which she now,\par
with Annette, attempted to move, but it was so heavy, that they could\par
not lift it from the floor. “What is in this great old chest,\par
Mademoiselle,” said Annette, “that makes it so weighty?” Emily having\par
replied, “that she found it in the chamber, when she first came to the\par
castle, and had never examined it.”—“Then I will, ma’amselle,” said\par
Annette, and she tried to lift the lid; but this was held by a lock,\par
for which she had no key, and which, indeed, appeared, from its\par
peculiar construction, to open with a spring. The morning now glimmered\par
through the casements, and the wind had sunk into a calm. Emily looked\par
out upon the dusky woods, and on the twilight mountains, just stealing\par
in the eye, and saw the whole scene, after the storm, lying in profound\par
stillness, the woods motionless, and the clouds above, through which\par
the dawn trembled, scarcely appearing to move along the heavens. One\par
soldier was pacing the terrace beneath, with measured steps; and two,\par
more distant, were sunk asleep on the walls, wearied with the night’s\par
watch. Having inhaled, for a while, the pure spirit of the air, and of\par
vegetation, which the late rains had called forth; and having listened,\par
once more, for a note of music, she now closed the casement, and\par
retired to rest.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER IX\par
\par
Thus on the chill Lapponian’s dreary land,\par
For many a long month lost in snow profound,\par
When Sol from Cancer sends the seasons bland,\par
And in their northern cave the storms hath bound;\par
From silent mountains, straight, with startling sound,\par
Torrents are hurl’d, green hills emerge, and lo,\par
The trees with foliage, cliffs with flowers are crown’d;\par
Pure rills through vales of verdure warbling go;\par
And wonder, love, and joy, the peasant’s heart o’erflow.\par
                    BEATTIE\par
\par
\par
Several of her succeeding days passed in suspense, for Ludovico could\par
only learn from the soldiers, that there was a prisoner in the\par
apartment, described to him by Emily, and that he was a Frenchman, whom\par
they had taken in one of their skirmishes, with a party of his\par
countrymen. During this interval, Emily escaped the persecutions of\par
Bertolini, and Verezzi, by confining herself to her apartment; except\par
that sometimes, in an evening, she ventured to walk in the adjoining\par
corridor. Montoni appeared to respect his last promise, though he had\par
prophaned his first; for to his protection only could she attribute her\par
present repose; and in this she was now so secure, that she did not\par
wish to leave the castle, till she could obtain some certainty\par
concerning Valancourt; for which she waited, indeed, without any\par
sacrifice of her own comfort, since no circumstance had occurred to\par
make her escape probable.\par
\par
On the fourth day, Ludovico informed her, that he had hopes of being\par
admitted to the presence of the prisoner; it being the turn of a\par
soldier, with whom he had been for some time familiar, to attend him on\par
the following night. He was not deceived in his hope; for, under\par
pretence of carrying in a pitcher of water, he entered the prison,\par
though, his prudence having prevented him from telling the sentinel the\par
real motive of his visit, he was obliged to make his conference with\par
the prisoner a very short one.\par
\par
Emily awaited the result in her own apartment, Ludovico having promised\par
to accompany Annette to the corridor, in the evening; where, after\par
several hours impatiently counted, he arrived. Emily, having then\par
uttered the name of Valancourt, could articulate no more, but hesitated\par
in trembling expectation. “The Chevalier would not entrust me with his\par
name, Signora,” replied Ludovico; “but, when I just mentioned yours, he\par
seemed overwhelmed with joy, though he was not so much surprised as I\par
expected.” “Does he then remember me?” she exclaimed.\par
\par
“O! it is Mons. Valancourt,” said Annette, and looked impatiently at\par
Ludovico, who understood her look, and replied to Emily: “Yes, lady,\par
the Chevalier does, indeed, remember you, and, I am sure, has a very\par
great regard for you, and I made bold to say you had for him. He then\par
enquired how you came to know he was in the castle, and whether you\par
ordered me to speak to him. The first question I could not answer, but\par
the second I did; and then he went off into his ecstasies again. I was\par
afraid his joy would have betrayed him to the sentinel at the door.”\par
\par
“But how does he look, Ludovico?” interrupted Emily: “is he not\par
melancholy and ill with this long confinement?”—“Why, as to melancholy,\par
I saw no symptom of that, lady, while I was with him, for he seemed in\par
the finest spirits I ever saw anybody in, in all my life. His\par
countenance was all joy, and, if one may judge from that, he was very\par
well; but I did not ask him.” “Did he send me no message?” said Emily.\par
“O yes, Signora, and something besides,” replied Ludovico, who searched\par
his pockets. “Surely, I have not lost it,” added he. “The Chevalier\par
said, he would have written, madam, if he had had pen and ink, and was\par
going to have sent a very long message, when the sentinel entered the\par
room, but not before he had give me this.” Ludovico then drew forth a\par
miniature from his bosom, which Emily received with a trembling hand,\par
and perceived to be a portrait of herself—the very picture, which her\par
mother had lost so strangely in the fishing-house at La Vallée.\par
\par
Tears of mingled joy and tenderness flowed to her eyes, while Ludovico\par
proceeded—“‘Tell your lady,’ said the Chevalier, as he gave me the\par
picture, ‘that this has been my companion, and only solace in all my\par
misfortunes. Tell her, that I have worn it next my heart, and that I\par
sent it her as the pledge of an affection, which can never die; that I\par
would not part with it, but to her, for the wealth of worlds, and that\par
I now part with it, only in the hope of soon receiving it from her\par
hands. Tell her—’ Just then, Signora, the sentinel came in, and the\par
Chevalier said no more; but he had before asked me to contrive an\par
interview for him with you; and when I told him, how little hope I had\par
of prevailing with the guard to assist me, he said, that was not,\par
perhaps, of so much consequence as I imagined, and bade me contrive to\par
bring back your answer, and he would inform me of more than he chose to\par
do then. So this, I think, lady, is the whole of what passed.”\par
\par
“How, Ludovico, shall I reward you for your zeal?” said Emily: “but,\par
indeed, I do not now possess the means. When can you see the Chevalier\par
again?” “That is uncertain, Signora,” replied he. “It depends upon who\par
stands guard next: there are not more than one or two among them, from\par
whom I would dare to ask admittance to the prison-chamber.”\par
\par
“I need not bid you remember, Ludovico,” resumed Emily, “how very much\par
interested I am in your seeing the Chevalier soon; and, when you do so,\par
tell him, that I have received the picture, and, with the sentiments he\par
wished. Tell him I have suffered much, and still suffer—” She paused.\par
“But shall I tell him you will see him, lady?” said Ludovico. “Most\par
certainly I will,” replied Emily. “But when, Signora, and where?” “That\par
must depend upon circumstances,” returned Emily. “The place, and the\par
hour, must be regulated by his opportunities.”\par
\par
“As to the place, mademoiselle,” said Annette, “there is no other place\par
in the castle, besides this corridor, where _we_ can see him in safety,\par
you know; and, as for the hour,—it must be when all the Signors are\par
asleep, if that ever happens!” “You may mention these circumstances to\par
the Chevalier, Ludovico,” said she, checking the flippancy of Annette,\par
“and leave them to his judgment and opportunity. Tell him, my heart is\par
unchanged. But, above all, let him see you again as soon as possible;\par
and, Ludovico, I think it is needless to tell you I shall very\par
anxiously look for you.” Having then wished her good night, Ludovico\par
descended the staircase, and Emily retired to rest, but not to sleep,\par
for joy now rendered her as wakeful, as she had ever been from grief.\par
Montoni and his castle had all vanished from her mind, like the\par
frightful vision of a necromancer, and she wandered, once more, in\par
fairy scenes of unfading happiness:\par
\par
As when, beneath the beam\par
Of summer moons, the distant woods among,\par
Or by some flood, all silver’d with the gleam,\par
The soft embodied Fays thro’ airy portals stream.\par
\par
\par
A week elapsed, before Ludovico again visited the prison; for the\par
sentinels, during that period, were men, in whom he could not confide,\par
and he feared to awaken curiosity, by asking to see their prisoner. In\par
this interval, he communicated to Emily terrific reports of what was\par
passing in the castle; of riots, quarrels, and of carousals more\par
alarming than either; while from some circumstances, which he\par
mentioned, she not only doubted, whether Montoni meant ever to release\par
her, but greatly feared, that he had designs, concerning her,—such as\par
she had formerly dreaded. Her name was frequently mentioned in the\par
conversations, which Bertolini and Verezzi held together, and, at those\par
times, they were frequently in contention. Montoni had lost large sums\par
to Verezzi, so that there was a dreadful possibility of his designing\par
her to be a substitute for the debt; but, as she was ignorant, that he\par
had formerly encouraged the hopes of Bertolini also, concerning\par
herself, after the latter had done him some signal service, she knew\par
not how to account for these contentions between Bertolini and Verezzi.\par
The cause of them, however, appeared to be of little consequence, for\par
she thought she saw destruction approaching in many forms, and her\par
entreaties to Ludovico to contrive an escape and to see the prisoner\par
again, were more urgent than ever.\par
\par
At length, he informed her, that he had again visited the Chevalier,\par
who had directed him to confide in the guard of the prison, from whom\par
he had already received some instances of kindness, and who had\par
promised to permit his going into the castle for half an hour, on the\par
ensuing night, when Montoni and his companions should be engaged at\par
their carousals. “This was kind, to be sure,” added Ludovico: “but\par
Sebastian knows he runs no risk in letting the Chevalier out, for, if\par
he can get beyond the bars and iron doors of the castle, he must be\par
cunning indeed. But the Chevalier desired me, Signora, to go to you\par
immediately, and to beg you would allow him to visit you, this night,\par
if it was only for a moment, for that he could no longer live under the\par
same roof, without seeing you; the hour, he said, he could not mention,\par
for it must depend on circumstances (just as you said, Signora); and\par
the place he desired you would appoint, as knowing which was best for\par
your own safety.”\par
\par
Emily was now so much agitated by the near prospect of meeting\par
Valancourt, that it was some time, before she could give any answer to\par
Ludovico, or consider of the place of meeting; when she did, she saw\par
none, that promised so much security, as the corridor, near her own\par
apartment, which she was checked from leaving, by the apprehension of\par
meeting any of Montoni’s guests, on their way to their rooms; and she\par
dismissed the scruples, which delicacy opposed, now that a serious\par
danger was to be avoided by encountering them. It was settled,\par
therefore, that the Chevalier should meet her in the corridor, at that\par
hour of the night, which Ludovico, who was to be upon the watch, should\par
judge safest: and Emily, as may be imagined, passed this interval in a\par
tumult of hope and joy, anxiety and impatience. Never, since her\par
residence in the castle, had she watched, with so much pleasure, the\par
sun set behind the mountains, and twilight shade, and darkness veil the\par
scene, as on this evening. She counted the notes of the great clock,\par
and listened to the steps of the sentinels, as they changed the watch,\par
only to rejoice, that another hour was gone. “O, Valancourt!” said she,\par
“after all I have suffered; after our long, long separation, when I\par
thought I should never—never see you more—we are still to meet again!\par
O! I have endured grief, and anxiety, and terror, and let me, then, not\par
sink beneath this joy!” These were moments, when it was impossible for\par
her to feel emotions of regret, or melancholy, for any ordinary\par
interests;—even the reflection, that she had resigned the estates,\par
which would have been a provision for herself and Valancourt for life,\par
threw only a light and transient shade upon her spirits. The idea of\par
Valancourt, and that she should see him so soon, alone occupied her\par
heart.\par
\par
At length the clock struck twelve; she opened the door to listen, if\par
any noise was in the castle, and heard only distant shouts of riot and\par
laughter, echoed feebly along the gallery. She guessed, that the Signor\par
and his guests were at the banquet. “They are now engaged for the\par
night,” said she; “and Valancourt will soon be here.” Having softly\par
closed the door, she paced the room with impatient steps, and often\par
went to the casement to listen for the lute; but all was silent, and,\par
her agitation every moment increasing, she was at length unable to\par
support herself, and sat down by the window. Annette, whom she\par
detained, was, in the meantime, as loquacious as usual; but Emily heard\par
scarcely anything she said, and having at length risen to the casement,\par
she distinguished the chords of the lute, struck with an expressive\par
hand, and then the voice, she had formerly listened to, accompanied it.\par
\par
Now rising love they fann’d, now pleasing dole\par
They breath’d in tender musings through the heart;\par
And now a graver, sacred strain they stole,\par
As when seraphic hands a hymn impart!\par
\par
\par
Emily wept in doubtful joy and tenderness; and, when the strain ceased,\par
she considered it as a signal, that Valancourt was about to leave the\par
prison. Soon after, she heard steps in the corridor;—they were the\par
light, quick steps of hope; she could scarcely support herself, as they\par
approached, but opening the door of the apartment, she advanced to meet\par
Valancourt, and, in the next moment, sunk in the arms of a stranger.\par
His voice—his countenance instantly convinced her, and she fainted\par
away.\par
\par
On reviving, she found herself supported by the stranger, who was\par
watching over her recovery, with a countenance of ineffable tenderness\par
and anxiety. She had no spirits for reply, or enquiry; she asked no\par
questions, but burst into tears, and disengaged herself from his arms;\par
when the expression of his countenance changed to surprise and\par
disappointment, and he turned to Ludovico, for an explanation; Annette\par
soon gave the information, which Ludovico could not. “O, sir!” said\par
she, in a voice, interrupted with sobs; “O, sir! you are not the other\par
Chevalier. We expected Monsieur Valancourt, but you are not he! O\par
Ludovico! how could you deceive us so? my poor lady will never recover\par
it—never!” The stranger, who now appeared much agitated, attempted to\par
speak, but his words faltered; and then striking his hand against his\par
forehead, as if in sudden despair, he walked abruptly to the other end\par
of the corridor.\par
\par
Suddenly, Annette dried her tears, and spoke to Ludovico. “But,\par
perhaps,” said she, “after all, the other Chevalier is not this:\par
perhaps the Chevalier Valancourt is still below.” Emily raised her\par
head. “No,” replied Ludovico, “Monsieur Valancourt never was below, if\par
this gentleman is not he.” “If you, sir,” said Ludovico, addressing the\par
stranger, “would but have had the goodness to trust me with your name,\par
this mistake had been avoided.” “Most true,” replied the stranger,\par
speaking in broken Italian, “but it was of the utmost consequence to\par
me, that my name should be concealed from Montoni. Madam,” added he\par
then, addressing Emily in French, “will you permit me to apologise for\par
the pain I have occasioned you, and to explain to you alone my name,\par
and the circumstance, which has led me into this error? I am of\par
France;—I am your countryman;—we are met in a foreign land.” Emily\par
tried to compose her spirits; yet she hesitated to grant his request.\par
At length, desiring, that Ludovico would wait on the staircase, and\par
detaining Annette, she told the stranger, that her woman understood\par
very little Italian, and begged he would communicate what he wished to\par
say, in that language.—Having withdrawn to a distant part of the\par
corridor, he said, with a long-drawn sigh, “You, madam, are no stranger\par
to me, though I am so unhappy as to be unknown to you.—My name is Du\par
Pont; I am of France, of Gascony, your native province, and have long\par
admired,—and, why should I affect to disguise it?—have long loved you.”\par
He paused, but, in the next moment, proceeded. “My family, madam, is\par
probably not unknown to you, for we lived within a few miles of La\par
Vallée, and I have, sometimes, had the happiness of meeting you, on\par
visits in the neighbourhood. I will not offend you by repeating how\par
much you interested me; how much I loved to wander in the scenes you\par
frequented; how often I visited your favourite fishing-house, and\par
lamented the circumstance, which, at that time, forbade me to reveal my\par
passion. I will not explain how I surrendered to temptation, and became\par
possessed of a treasure, which was to me inestimable; a treasure, which\par
I committed to your messenger, a few days ago, with expectations very\par
different from my present ones. I will say nothing of these\par
circumstances, for I know they will avail me little; let me only\par
supplicate from you forgiveness, and the picture, which I so unwarily\par
returned. Your generosity will pardon the theft, and restore the prize.\par
My crime has been my punishment; for the portrait I stole has\par
contributed to nourish a passion, which must still be my torment.”\par
\par
Emily now interrupted him. “I think, sir, I may leave it to your\par
integrity to determine, whether, after what has just appeared,\par
concerning Mons. Valancourt, I ought to return the picture. I think you\par
will acknowledge, that this would not be generosity; and you will allow\par
me to add, that it would be doing myself an injustice. I must consider\par
myself honoured by your good opinion, but”—and she hesitated,—“the\par
mistake of this evening makes it unnecessary for me to say more.”\par
\par
“It does, madam,—alas! it does!” said the stranger, who, after a long\par
pause, proceeded.—“But you will allow me to show my disinterestedness,\par
though not my love, and will accept the services I offer. Yet, alas!\par
what services can I offer? I am myself a prisoner, a sufferer, like\par
you. But, dear as liberty is to me, I would not seek it through half\par
the hazards I would encounter to deliver you from this recess of vice.\par
Accept the offered services of a friend; do not refuse me the reward of\par
having, at least, attempted to deserve your thanks.”\par
\par
“You deserve them already, sir,” said Emily; “the wish deserves my\par
warmest thanks. But you will excuse me for reminding you of the danger\par
you incur by prolonging this interview. It will be a great consolation\par
to me to remember, whether your friendly attempts to release me succeed\par
or not, that I have a countryman, who would so generously protect\par
me.”—Monsieur Du Pont took her hand, which she but feebly attempted to\par
withdraw, and pressed it respectfully to his lips. “Allow me to breathe\par
another fervent sigh for your happiness,” said he, “and to applaud\par
myself for an affection, which I cannot conquer.” As he said this,\par
Emily heard a noise from her apartment, and, turning round, saw the\par
door from the staircase open, and a man rush into her chamber. “I will\par
teach you to conquer it,” cried he, as he advanced into the corridor,\par
and drew a stiletto, which he aimed at Du Pont, who was unarmed, but\par
who, stepping back, avoided the blow, and then sprung upon Verezzi,\par
from whom he wrenched the stiletto. While they struggled in each\par
other’s grasp, Emily, followed by Annette, ran further into the\par
corridor, calling on Ludovico, who was, however, gone from the\par
staircase, and, as she advanced, terrified and uncertain what to do, a\par
distant noise, that seemed to arise from the hall, reminded her of the\par
danger she was incurring; and, sending Annette forward in search of\par
Ludovico, she returned to the spot where Du Pont and Verezzi were still\par
struggling for victory. It was her own cause which was to be decided\par
with that of the former, whose conduct, independently of this\par
circumstance, would, however, have interested her in his success, even\par
had she not disliked and dreaded Verezzi. She threw herself in a chair,\par
and supplicated them to desist from further violence, till, at length,\par
Du Pont forced Verezzi to the floor, where he lay stunned by the\par
violence of his fall; and she then entreated Du Pont to escape from the\par
room, before Montoni, or his party, should appear; but he still refused\par
to leave her unprotected; and, while Emily, now more terrified for him,\par
than for herself, enforced the entreaty, they heard steps ascending the\par
private staircase.\par
\par
“O you are lost!” cried she, “these are Montoni’s people.” Du Pont made\par
no reply, but supported Emily, while, with a steady, though eager,\par
countenance, he awaited their appearance, and, in the next moment,\par
Ludovico, alone, mounted the landing-place. Throwing a hasty glance\par
round the chamber, “Follow me,” said he, “as you value your lives; we\par
have not an instant to lose!”\par
\par
Emily enquired what had occurred, and whither they were to go?\par
\par
“I cannot stay to tell you now, Signora,” replied Ludovico: “fly! fly!”\par
\par
She immediately followed him, accompanied by Mons. Du Pont, down the\par
staircase, and along a vaulted passage, when suddenly she recollected\par
Annette, and enquired for her. “She awaits us further on, Signora,”\par
said Ludovico, almost breathless with haste; “the gates were open, a\par
moment since, to a party just come in from the mountains: they will be\par
shut, I fear, before we can reach them! Through this door, Signora,”\par
added Ludovico, holding down the lamp, “take care, here are two steps.”\par
\par
Emily followed, trembling still more, than before she had understood,\par
that her escape from the castle, depended upon the present moment;\par
while Du Pont supported her, and endeavoured, as they passed along, to\par
cheer her spirits.\par
\par
“Speak low, Signor,” said Ludovico, “these passages send echoes all\par
round the castle.”\par
\par
“Take care of the light,” cried Emily, “you go so fast, that the air\par
will extinguish it.”\par
\par
Ludovico now opened another door, where they found Annette, and the\par
party then descended a short flight of steps into a passage, which,\par
Ludovico said, led round the inner court of the castle, and opened into\par
the outer one. As they advanced, confused and tumultuous sounds, that\par
seemed to come from the inner court, alarmed Emily. “Nay, Signora,”\par
said Ludovico, “our only hope is in that tumult; while the Signor’s\par
people are busied about the men, who are just arrived, we may, perhaps,\par
pass unnoticed through the gates. But hush!” he added, as they\par
approached the small door, that opened into the outer court, “if you\par
will remain here a moment, I will go to see whether the gates are open,\par
and anybody is in the way. Pray extinguish the light, Signor, if you\par
hear me talking,” continued Ludovico, delivering the lamp to Du Pont,\par
“and remain quite still.”\par
\par
Saying this, he stepped out upon the court, and they closed the door,\par
listening anxiously to his departing steps. No voice, however, was\par
heard in the court, which he was crossing, though a confusion of many\par
voices yet issued from the inner one. “We shall soon be beyond the\par
walls,” said Du Pont softly to Emily, “support yourself a little\par
longer, Madam, and all will be well.”\par
\par
But soon they heard Ludovico speaking loud, and the voice also of some\par
other person, and Du Pont immediately extinguished the lamp. “Ah! it is\par
too late!” exclaimed Emily, “what is to become of us?” They listened\par
again, and then perceived, that Ludovico was talking with a sentinel,\par
whose voices were heard also by Emily’s favourite dog, that had\par
followed her from the chamber, and now barked loudly. “This dog will\par
betray us!” said Du Pont, “I will hold him.” “I fear he has already\par
betrayed us!” replied Emily. Du Pont, however, caught him up, and,\par
again listening to what was going on without, they heard Ludovico say,\par
“I’ll watch the gates the while.”\par
\par
“Stay a minute,” replied the sentinel, “and you need not have the\par
trouble, for the horses will be sent round to the outer stables, then\par
the gates will be shut, and I can leave my post.” “I don’t mind the\par
trouble, comrade,” said Ludovico, “you will do such another good turn\par
for me, some time. Go—go, and fetch the wine; the rogues, that are just\par
come in, will drink it all else.”\par
\par
The soldier hesitated, and then called aloud to the people in the\par
second court, to know why they did not send out the horses, that the\par
gates might be shut; but they were too much engaged, to attend to him,\par
even if they had heard his voice.\par
\par
“Aye—aye,” said Ludovico, “they know better than that; they are sharing\par
it all among them; if you wait till the horses come out, you must wait\par
till the wine is drunk. I have had my share already, but, since you do\par
not care about yours, I see no reason why I should not have that too.”\par
\par
“Hold, hold, not so fast,” cried the sentinel, “do watch then, for a\par
moment: I’ll be with you presently.”\par
\par
“Don’t hurry yourself,” said Ludovico, coolly, “I have kept guard\par
before now. But you may leave me your trombone,* that, if the castle\par
should be attacked, you know, I may be able to defend the pass, like a\par
hero.”\par
\par
(*Note: A kind of blunderbuss. [A. R.])\par
\par
\par
“There, my good fellow,” returned the soldier, “there, take it—it has\par
seen service, though it could do little in defending the castle. I’ll\par
tell you a good story, though, about this same trombone.”\par
\par
“You’ll tell it better when you have had the wine,” said Ludovico.\par
“There! they are coming out from the court already.”\par
\par
“I’ll have the wine, though,” said the sentinel, running off. “I won’t\par
keep you a minute.”\par
\par
“Take your time, I am in no haste,” replied Ludovico, who was already\par
hurrying across the court, when the soldier came back. “Whither so\par
fast, friend—whither so fast?” said the latter. “What! is this the way\par
you keep watch! I must stand to my post myself, I see.”\par
\par
“Aye, well,” replied Ludovico, “you have saved me the trouble of\par
following you further, for I wanted to tell you, if you have a mind to\par
drink the Tuscany wine, you must go to Sebastian, he is dealing it out;\par
the other that Federico has, is not worth having. But you are not\par
likely to have any, I see, for they are all coming out.”\par
\par
“By St. Peter! so they are,” said the soldier, and again ran off, while\par
Ludovico, once more at liberty, hastened to the door of the passage,\par
where Emily was sinking under the anxiety this long discourse had\par
occasioned; but, on his telling them the court was clear, they followed\par
him to the gates, without waiting another instant, yet not before he\par
had seized two horses, that had strayed from the second court, and were\par
picking a scanty meal among the grass, which grew between the pavement\par
of the first.\par
\par
They passed, without interruption, the dreadful gates, and took the\par
road that led down among the woods, Emily, Monsieur Du Pont and Annette\par
on foot, and Ludovico, who was mounted on one horse, leading the other.\par
Having reached them, they stopped, while Emily and Annette were placed\par
on horseback with their two protectors, when, Ludovico leading the way,\par
they set off as fast as the broken road, and the feeble light, which a\par
rising moon threw among the foliage, would permit.\par
\par
Emily was so much astonished by this sudden departure, that she\par
scarcely dared to believe herself awake; and she yet much doubted\par
whether this adventure would terminate in escape,—a doubt, which had\par
too much probability to justify it; for, before they quitted the woods,\par
they heard shouts in the wind, and, on emerging from them, saw lights\par
moving quickly near the castle above. Du Pont whipped his horse, and\par
with some difficulty compelled him to go faster.\par
\par
“Ah! poor beast,” said Ludovico, “he is weary enough;—he has been out\par
all day; but, Signor, we must fly for it, now; for yonder are lights\par
coming this way.”\par
\par
Having given his own horse a lash, they now both set off on a full\par
gallop; and, when they again looked back, the lights were so distant as\par
scarcely to be discerned, and the voices were sunk into silence. The\par
travellers then abated their pace, and, consulting whither they should\par
direct their course, it was determined they should descend into\par
Tuscany, and endeavour to reach the Mediterranean, where they could\par
readily embark for France. Thither Du Pont meant to attend Emily, if he\par
should learn, that the regiment he had accompanied into Italy, was\par
returned to his native country.\par
\par
They were now in the road, which Emily had travelled with Ugo and\par
Bertrand; but Ludovico, who was the only one of the party, acquainted\par
with the passes of these mountains, said, that, a little further on, a\par
by-road, branching from this, would lead them down into Tuscany with\par
very little difficulty; and that, at a few leagues distance, was a\par
small town, where necessaries could be procured for their journey.\par
\par
“But, I hope,” added he, “we shall meet with no straggling parties of\par
banditti; some of them are abroad, I know. However, I have got a good\par
trombone, which will be of some service, if we should encounter any of\par
those brave spirits. You have no arms, Signor?” “Yes,” replied Du Pont,\par
“I have the villain’s stilletto, who would have stabbed me—but let us\par
rejoice in our escape from Udolpho, nor torment ourselves with looking\par
out for dangers, that may never arrive.”\par
\par
The moon was now risen high over the woods, that hung upon the sides of\par
the narrow glen, through which they wandered, and afforded them light\par
sufficient to distinguish their way, and to avoid the loose and broken\par
stones, that frequently crossed it. They now travelled leisurely, and\par
in profound silence; for they had scarcely yet recovered from the\par
astonishment, into which this sudden escape had thrown them.—Emily’s\par
mind, especially, was sunk, after the various emotions it had suffered,\par
into a kind of musing stillness, which the reposing beauty of the\par
surrounding scene and the creeping murmur of the night-breeze among the\par
foliage above contributed to prolong. She thought of Valancourt and of\par
France, with hope, and she would have thought of them with joy, had not\par
the first events of this evening harassed her spirits too much, to\par
permit her now to feel so lively a sensation. Meanwhile, Emily was\par
alone the object of Du Pont’s melancholy consideration; yet, with the\par
despondency he suffered, as he mused on his recent disappointment, was\par
mingled a sweet pleasure, occasioned by her presence, though they did\par
not now exchange a single word. Annette thought of this wonderful\par
escape, of the bustle in which Montoni and his people must be, now that\par
their flight was discovered; of her native country, whither she hoped\par
she was returning, and of her marriage with Ludovico, to which there no\par
longer appeared any impediment, for poverty she did not consider such.\par
Ludovico, on his part, congratulated himself, on having rescued his\par
Annette and Signora Emily from the danger, that had surrounded them; on\par
his own liberation from people, whose manners he had long detested; on\par
the freedom he had given to Monsieur Du Pont; on his prospect of\par
happiness with the object of his affections, and not a little on the\par
address, with which he had deceived the sentinel, and conducted the\par
whole of this affair.\par
\par
Thus variously engaged in thought, the travellers passed on silently,\par
for above an hour, a question only being, now and then, asked by Du\par
Pont, concerning the road, or a remark uttered by Annette, respecting\par
objects, seen imperfectly in the twilight. At length, lights were\par
perceived twinkling on the side of a mountain, and Ludovico had no\par
doubt, that they proceeded from the town he had mentioned, while his\par
companions, satisfied by this assurance, sunk again into silence.\par
Annette was the first who interrupted this. “Holy Peter!” said she,\par
“What shall we do for money on our journey? for I know neither I, nor\par
my lady, have a single sequin; the Signor took care of that!”\par
\par
This remark produced a serious enquiry, which ended in as serious an\par
embarrassment, for Du Pont had been rifled of nearly all his money,\par
when he was taken prisoner; the remainder he had given to the sentinel,\par
who had enabled him occasionally to leave his prison-chamber; and\par
Ludovico, who had for some time found a difficulty, in procuring any\par
part of the wages due to him, had now scarcely cash sufficient to\par
procure necessary refreshment at the first town, in which they should\par
arrive.\par
\par
Their poverty was the more distressing, since it would detain them\par
among the mountains, where, even in a town, they could scarcely\par
consider themselves safe from Montoni. The travellers, however, had\par
only to proceed and dare the future; and they continued their way\par
through lonely wilds and dusky valleys, where the overhanging foliage\par
now admitted, and then excluded the moonlight; wilds so desolate, that\par
they appeared, on the first glance, as if no human being had ever trod\par
them before. Even the road, in which the party were, did but slightly\par
contradict this error, for the high grass and other luxuriant\par
vegetation, with which it was overgrown, told how very seldom the foot\par
of a traveller had passed it.\par
\par
At length, from a distance, was heard the faint tinkling of a\par
sheep-bell; and, soon after, the bleat of flocks, and the party then\par
knew, that they were near some human habitation, for the light, which\par
Ludovico had fancied to proceed from a town, had long been concealed by\par
intervening mountains. Cheered by this hope, they quickened their pace\par
along the narrow pass they were winding, and it opened upon one of\par
those pastoral valleys of the Apennines, which might be painted for a\par
scene of Arcadia, and whose beauty and simplicity are finely contrasted\par
by the grandeur of the snowtopped mountains above.\par
\par
The morning light, now glimmering in the horizon, showed faintly, at a\par
little distance, upon the brow of a hill, which seemed to peep from\par
“under the opening eye-lids of the morn,” the town they were in search\par
of, and which they soon after reached. It was not without some\par
difficulty, that they there found a house, which could afford shelter\par
for themselves and their horses; and Emily desired they might not rest\par
longer than was necessary for refreshment. Her appearance excited some\par
surprise, for she was without a hat, having had time only to throw on\par
her veil before she left the castle, a circumstance, that compelled her\par
to regret again the want of money, without which it was impossible to\par
procure this necessary article of dress.\par
\par
Ludovico, on examining his purse, found it even insufficient to supply\par
present refreshment, and Du Pont, at length, ventured to inform the\par
landlord, whose countenance was simple and honest, of their exact\par
situation, and requested, that he would assist them to pursue their\par
journey; a purpose, which he promised to comply with, as far as he was\par
able, when he learned that they were prisoners escaping from Montoni,\par
whom he had too much reason to hate. But, though he consented to lend\par
them fresh horses to carry them to the next town, he was too poor\par
himself to trust them with money, and they were again lamenting their\par
poverty, when Ludovico, who had been with his tired horses to the\par
hovel, which served for a stable, entered the room, half frantic with\par
joy, in which his auditors soon participated. On removing the saddle\par
from one of the horses, he had found beneath it a small bag,\par
containing, no doubt, the booty of one of the _Condottieri_, who had\par
returned from a plundering excursion, just before Ludovico left the\par
castle, and whose horse having strayed from the inner court, while his\par
master was engaged in drinking, had brought away the treasure, which\par
the ruffian had considered the reward of his exploit.\par
\par
On counting over this, Du Pont found, that it would be more than\par
sufficient to carry them all to France, where he now determined to\par
accompany Emily, whether he should obtain intelligence of his regiment,\par
or not; for, though he had as much confidence in the integrity of\par
Ludovico, as his small knowledge of him allowed, he could not endure\par
the thought of committing her to his care for the voyage; nor, perhaps,\par
had he resolution enough to deny himself the dangerous pleasure, which\par
he might derive from her presence.\par
\par
He now consulted them, concerning the sea-port, to which they should\par
direct their way, and Ludovico, better informed of the geography of the\par
country, said, that Leghorn was the nearest port of consequence, which\par
Du Pont knew also to be the most likely of any in Italy to assist their\par
plan, since from thence vessels of all nations were continually\par
departing. Thither, therefore, it was determined, that they should\par
proceed.\par
\par
Emily, having purchased a little straw hat, such as was worn by the\par
peasant girls of Tuscany, and some other little necessary equipments\par
for the journey, and the travellers, having exchanged their tired\par
horses for others better able to carry them, recommenced their joyous\par
way, as the sun was rising over the mountains, and, after travelling\par
through this romantic country, for several hours, began to descend into\par
the vale of Arno. And here Emily beheld all the charms of sylvan and\par
pastoral landscape united, adorned with the elegant villas of the\par
Florentine nobles, and diversified with the various riches of\par
cultivation. How vivid the shrubs that embowered the slopes, with the\par
woods, that stretched amphitheatrically along the mountains! and, above\par
all, how elegant the outline of these waving Apennines, now softening\par
from the wildness, which their interior regions exhibited! At a\par
distance, in the east, Emily discovered Florence, with its towers\par
rising on the brilliant horizon, and its luxuriant plain, spreading to\par
the feet of the Apennines, speckled with gardens and magnificent\par
villas, or coloured with groves of orange and lemon, with vines, corn,\par
and plantations of olives and mulberry; while, to the west, the vale\par
opened to the waters of the Mediterranean, so distant, that they were\par
known only by a bluish line, that appeared upon the horizon, and by the\par
light marine vapour, which just stained the æther above.\par
\par
With a full heart, Emily hailed the waves, that were to bear her back\par
to her native country, the remembrance of which, however, brought with\par
it a pang; for she had there no home to receive, no parents to welcome\par
her, but was going, like a forlorn pilgrim, to weep over the sad spot,\par
where he, who _was_ her father, lay interred. Nor were her spirits\par
cheered, when she considered how long it would probably be before she\par
should see Valancourt, who might be stationed with his regiment in a\par
distant part of France, and that, when they did meet, it would be only\par
to lament the successful villany of Montoni; yet, still she would have\par
felt inexpressible delight at the thought of being once more in the\par
same country with Valancourt, had it even been certain, that she could\par
not see him.\par
\par
The intense heat, for it was now noon, obliged the travellers to look\par
out for a shady recess, where they might rest, for a few hours, and the\par
neighbouring thickets, abounding with wild grapes, raspberries, and\par
figs, promised them grateful refreshment. Soon after, they turned from\par
the road into a grove, whose thick foliage entirely excluded the\par
sunbeams, and where a spring, gushing from the rock, gave coolness to\par
the air; and, having alighted and turned the horses to graze, Annette\par
and Ludovico ran to gather fruit from the surrounding thickets, of\par
which they soon returned with an abundance. The travellers, seated\par
under the shade of a pine and cypress grove and on turf, enriched with\par
such a profusion of fragrant flowers, as Emily had scarcely ever seen,\par
even among the Pyrenees, took their simple repast, and viewed, with new\par
delight, beneath the dark umbrage of gigantic pines, the glowing\par
landscape stretching to the sea.\par
\par
Emily and Du Pont gradually became thoughtful and silent; but Annette\par
was all joy and loquacity, and Ludovico was gay, without forgetting the\par
respectful distance, which was due to his companions. The repast being\par
over, Du Pont recommended Emily to endeavour to sleep, during these\par
sultry hours, and, desiring the servants would do the same, said he\par
would watch the while; but Ludovico wished to spare him this trouble;\par
and Emily and Annette, wearied with travelling, tried to repose, while\par
he stood guard with his trombone.\par
\par
When Emily, refreshed by slumber, awoke, she found the sentinel asleep\par
on his post and Du Pont awake, but lost in melancholy thought. As the\par
sun was yet too high to allow them to continue their journey, and as it\par
was necessary, that Ludovico, after the toils and trouble he had\par
suffered, should finish his sleep, Emily took this opportunity of\par
enquiring by what accident Du Pont became Montoni’s prisoner, and he,\par
pleased with the interest this enquiry expressed and with the excuse it\par
gave him for talking to her of himself, immediately answered her\par
curiosity.\par
\par
“I came into Italy, madam,” said Du Pont, “in the service of my\par
country. In an adventure among the mountains our party, engaging with\par
the bands of Montoni, was routed, and I, with a few of my comrades, was\par
taken prisoner. When they told me, whose captive I was, the name of\par
Montoni struck me, for I remembered, that Madame Cheron, your aunt, had\par
married an Italian of that name, and that you had accompanied them into\par
Italy. It was not, however, till some time after, that I became\par
convinced this was the same Montoni, or learned that you, madam, was\par
under the same roof with myself. I will not pain you by describing what\par
were my emotions upon this discovery, which I owed to a sentinel, whom\par
I had so far won to my interest, that he granted me many indulgences,\par
one of which was very important to me, and somewhat dangerous to\par
himself; but he persisted in refusing to convey any letter, or notice\par
of my situation to you, for he justly dreaded a discovery and the\par
consequent vengeance of Montoni. He however enabled me to see you more\par
than once. You are surprised, madam, and I will explain myself. My\par
health and spirits suffered extremely from want of air and exercise,\par
and, at length, I gained so far upon the pity, or the avarice of the\par
man, that he gave me the means of walking on the terrace.”\par
\par
Emily now listened, with very anxious attention, to the narrative of Du\par
Pont, who proceeded:\par
\par
“In granting this indulgence, he knew, that he had nothing to apprehend\par
from a chance of my escaping from a castle, which was vigilantly\par
guarded, and the nearest terrace of which rose over a perpendicular\par
rock; he showed me also,” continued Du Pont, “a door concealed in the\par
cedar wainscot of the apartment where I was confined, which he\par
instructed me how to open; and which, leading into a passage, formed\par
within the thickness of the wall, that extended far along the castle,\par
finally opened in an obscure corner of the eastern rampart. I have\par
since been informed, that there are many passages of the same kind\par
concealed within the prodigious walls of that edifice, and which were,\par
undoubtedly, contrived for the purpose of facilitating escapes in time\par
of war. Through this avenue, at the dead of night, I often stole to the\par
terrace, where I walked with the utmost caution, lest my steps should\par
betray me to the sentinels on duty in distant parts; for this end of\par
it, being guarded by high buildings, was not watched by soldiers. In\par
one of these midnight wanderings, I saw light in a casement that\par
overlooked the rampart, and which, I observed, was immediately over my\par
prison-chamber. It occurred to me, that you might be in that apartment,\par
and, with the hope of seeing you, I placed myself opposite to the\par
window.”\par
\par
Emily, remembering the figure that had formerly appeared on the\par
terrace, and which had occasioned her so much anxiety, exclaimed, “It\par
was you then, Monsieur Du Pont, who occasioned me much foolish terror;\par
my spirits were, at that time, so much weakened by long suffering, that\par
they took alarm at every hint.” Du Pont, after lamenting, that he had\par
occasioned her any apprehension, added, “As I rested on the wall,\par
opposite to your casement, the consideration of your melancholy\par
situation and of my own called from me involuntary sounds of\par
lamentation, which drew you, I fancy, to the casement; I saw there a\par
person, whom I believed to be you. O! I will say nothing of my emotion\par
at that moment; I wished to speak, but prudence restrained me, till the\par
distant foot-step of a sentinel compelled me suddenly to quit my\par
station.\par
\par
“It was some time, before I had another opportunity of walking, for I\par
could only leave my prison, when it happened to be the turn of one man\par
to guard me; meanwhile I became convinced from some circumstances\par
related by him, that your apartment was over mine, and, when again I\par
ventured forth, I returned to your casement, where again I saw you, but\par
without daring to speak. I waved my hand, and you suddenly disappeared;\par
then it was, that I forgot my prudence, and yielded to lamentation;\par
again you appeared—you spoke—I heard the well-known accent of your\par
voice! and, at that moment, my discretion would have forsaken me again,\par
had I not heard also the approaching steps of a soldier, when I\par
instantly quitted the place, though not before the man had seen me. He\par
followed down the terrace and gained so fast upon me, that I was\par
compelled to make use of a stratagem, ridiculous enough, to save\par
myself. I had heard of the superstition of many of these men, and I\par
uttered a strange noise, with a hope, that my pursuer would mistake it\par
for something supernatural, and desist from pursuit. Luckily for myself\par
I succeeded; the man, it seems, was subject to fits, and the terror he\par
suffered threw him into one, by which accident I secured my retreat. A\par
sense of the danger I had escaped, and the increased watchfulness,\par
which my appearance had occasioned among the sentinels, deterred me\par
ever after from walking on the terrace; but, in the stillness of night,\par
I frequently beguiled myself with an old lute, procured for me by a\par
soldier, which I sometimes accompanied with my voice, and sometimes, I\par
will acknowledge, with a hope of making myself heard by you; but it was\par
only a few evenings ago, that this hope was answered. I then thought I\par
heard a voice in the wind, calling me; yet, even then I feared to\par
reply, lest the sentinel at the prison door should hear me. Was I\par
right, madam, in this conjecture—was it you who spoke?”\par
\par
“Yes,” said Emily, with an involuntary sigh, “you were right indeed.”\par
\par
Du Pont, observing the painful emotions, which this question revived,\par
now changed the subject. “In one of my excursions through the passage,\par
which I have mentioned, I overheard a singular conversation,” said he.\par
\par
“In the passage!” said Emily, with surprise.\par
\par
“I heard it in the passage,” said Du Pont, “but it proceeded from an\par
apartment, adjoining the wall, within which the passage wound, and the\par
shell of the wall was there so thin, and was also somewhat decayed,\par
that I could distinctly hear every word, spoken on the other side. It\par
happened that Montoni and his companions were assembled in the room,\par
and Montoni began to relate the extraordinary history of the lady, his\par
predecessor, in the castle. He did, indeed, mention some very\par
surprising circumstances, and whether they were strictly true, his\par
conscience must decide; I fear it will determine against him. But you,\par
madam, have doubtless heard the report, which he designs should\par
circulate, on the subject of that lady’s mysterious fate.”\par
\par
“I have, sir,” replied Emily, “and I perceive, that you doubt it.”\par
\par
“I doubted it before the period I am speaking of,” rejoined Du\par
Pont;—“but some circumstances, mentioned by Montoni, greatly\par
contributed to my suspicions. The account I then heard, almost\par
convinced me, that he was a murderer. I trembled for you;—the more so\par
that I had heard the guests mention your name in a manner, that\par
threatened your repose; and, knowing, that the most impious men are\par
often the most superstitious, I determined to try whether I could not\par
awaken their consciences, and awe them from the commission of the crime\par
I dreaded. I listened closely to Montoni, and, in the most striking\par
passages of his story, I joined my voice, and repeated his last words,\par
in a disguised and hollow tone.”\par
\par
“But were you not afraid of being discovered?” said Emily.\par
\par
“I was not,” replied Du Pont; “for I knew, that, if Montoni had been\par
acquainted with the secret of this passage, he would not have confined\par
me in the apartment, to which it led. I knew also, from better\par
authority, that he was ignorant of it. The party, for some time,\par
appeared inattentive to my voice; but, at length, were so much alarmed,\par
that they quitted the apartment; and, having heard Montoni order his\par
servants to search it, I returned to my prison, which was very distant\par
from this part of the passage.” “I remember perfectly to have heard of\par
the conversation you mention,” said Emily; “it spread a general alarm\par
among Montoni’s people, and I will own I was weak enough to partake of\par
it.”\par
\par
Monsieur Du Pont and Emily thus continued to converse of Montoni, and\par
then of France, and of the plan of their voyage; when Emily told him,\par
that it was her intention to retire to a convent in Languedoc, where\par
she had been formerly treated with much kindness, and from thence to\par
write to her relation Monsieur Quesnel, and inform him of her conduct.\par
There, she designed to wait, till La Vallée should again be her own,\par
whither she hoped her income would some time permit her to return; for\par
Du Pont now taught her to expect, that the estate, of which Montoni had\par
attempted to defraud her, was not irrecoverably lost, and he again\par
congratulated her on her escape from Montoni, who, he had not a doubt,\par
meant to have detained her for life. The possibility of recovering her\par
aunt’s estates for Valancourt and herself lighted up a joy in Emily’s\par
heart, such as she had not known for many months; but she endeavoured\par
to conceal this from Monsieur Du Pont, lest it should lead him to a\par
painful remembrance of his rival.\par
\par
They continued to converse, till the sun was declining in the west,\par
when Du Pont awoke Ludovico, and they set forward on their journey.\par
Gradually descending the lower slopes of the valley, they reached the\par
Arno, and wound along its pastoral margin, for many miles, delighted\par
with the scenery around them, and with the remembrances, which its\par
classic waves revived. At a distance, they heard the gay song of the\par
peasants among the vineyards, and observed the setting sun tint the\par
waves with yellow lustre, and twilight draw a dusky purple over the\par
mountains, which, at length, deepened into night. Then the _lucciola_,\par
the fire-fly of Tuscany, was seen to flash its sudden sparks among the\par
foliage, while the _cicala_, with its shrill note, became more\par
clamorous than even during the noon-day heat, loving best the hour when\par
the English beetle, with less offensive sound,\par
\par
winds\par
His small but sullen horn,\par
As oft he rises ’midst the twilight path,\par
Against the pilgrim borne in heedless hum.\par
                    COLLINS\par
\par
\par
The travellers crossed the Arno by moonlight, at a ferry, and, learning\par
that Pisa was distant only a few miles down the river, they wished to\par
have proceeded thither in a boat, but, as none could be procured, they\par
set out on their wearied horses for that city. As they approached it,\par
the vale expanded into a plain, variegated with vineyards, corn, olives\par
and mulberry groves; but it was late, before they reached its gates,\par
where Emily was surprised to hear the busy sound of footsteps and the\par
tones of musical instruments, as well as to see the lively groups, that\par
filled the streets, and she almost fancied herself again at Venice; but\par
here was no moonlight sea—no gay gondolas, dashing the waves,—no\par
_Palladian_ palaces, to throw enchantment over the fancy and lead it\par
into the wilds of fairy story. The Arno rolled through the town, but no\par
music trembled from balconies over its waters; it gave only the busy\par
voices of sailors on board vessels just arrived from the Mediterranean;\par
the melancholy heaving of the anchor, and the shrill boatswain’s\par
whistle;—sounds, which, since that period, have there sunk almost into\par
silence. They then served to remind Du Pont, that it was probable he\par
might hear of a vessel, sailing soon to France from this port, and thus\par
be spared the trouble of going to Leghorn. As soon as Emily had reached\par
the inn, he went therefore to the quay, to make his enquiries; but,\par
after all the endeavours of himself and Ludovico, they could hear of no\par
bark, destined immediately for France, and the travellers returned to\par
their resting-place. Here also, Du Pont endeavoured to learn where his\par
regiment then lay, but could acquire no information concerning it. The\par
travellers retired early to rest, after the fatigues of this day; and,\par
on the following, rose early, and, without pausing to view the\par
celebrated antiquities of the place, or the wonders of its hanging\par
tower, pursued their journey in the cooler hours, through a charming\par
country, rich with wine, and corn and oil. The Apennines, no longer\par
awful, or even grand, here softened into the beauty of sylvan and\par
pastoral landscape; and Emily, as she descended them, looked down\par
delighted on Leghorn, and its spacious bay, filled with vessels, and\par
crowned with these beautiful hills.\par
\par
She was no less surprised and amused, on entering this town, to find it\par
crowded with persons in the dresses of all nations; a scene, which\par
reminded her of a Venetian masquerade, such as she had witnessed at the\par
time of the Carnival; but here was bustle without gaiety, and noise\par
instead of music, while elegance was to be looked for only in the\par
waving outlines of the surrounding hills.\par
\par
Monsieur Du Pont, immediately on their arrival, went down to the quay,\par
where he heard of several French vessels, and of one, that was to sail,\par
in a few days, for Marseilles, from whence another vessel could be\par
procured, without difficulty, to take them across the gulf of Lyons\par
towards Narbonne, on the coast not many leagues from which city he\par
understood the convent was seated, to which Emily wished to retire. He,\par
therefore, immediately engaged with the captain to take them to\par
Marseilles, and Emily was delighted to hear, that her passage to France\par
was secured. Her mind was now relieved from the terror of pursuit, and\par
the pleasing hope of soon seeing her native country—that country which\par
held Valancourt, restored to her spirits a degree of cheerfulness, such\par
as she had scarcely known, since the death of her father. At Leghorn\par
also, Du Pont heard of his regiment, and that it had embarked for\par
France; a circumstance, which gave him great satisfaction, for he could\par
now accompany Emily thither, without reproach to his conscience, or\par
apprehension of displeasure from his commander. During these days, he\par
scrupulously forbore to distress her by a mention of his passion, and\par
she was compelled to esteem and pity, though she could not love him. He\par
endeavoured to amuse her by showing the environs of the town, and they\par
often walked together on the sea-shore, and on the busy quays, where\par
Emily was frequently interested by the arrival and departure of\par
vessels, participating in the joy of meeting friends, and, sometimes,\par
shedding a sympathetic tear to the sorrow of those, that were\par
separating. It was after having witnessed a scene of the latter kind,\par
that she arranged the following stanzas:\par
\par
THE MARINER\par
\par
Soft came the breath of spring; smooth flow’d the tide;\par
And blue the heaven in its mirror smil’d;\par
The white sail trembled, swell’d, expanded wide,\par
The busy sailors at the anchor toil’d.\par
\par
With anxious friends, that shed the parting tear,\par
The deck was throng’d—how swift the moments fly!\par
The vessel heaves, the farewell signs appear;\par
Mute is each tongue, and eloquent each eye!\par
\par
The last dread moment comes!—The sailor youth\par
Hides the big drop, then smiles amid his pain,\par
Sooths his sad bride, and vows eternal truth,\par
“Farewell, my love—we shall—shall meet again!”\par
\par
Long on the stern, with waving hand, he stood;\par
The crowded shore sinks, lessening, from his view,\par
As gradual glides the bark along the flood;\par
His bride is seen no more—“Adieu!—adieu!”\par
\par
The breeze of Eve moans low, her smile is o’er,\par
Dim steals her twilight down the crimson’d west,\par
He climbs the top-most mast, to seek once more\par
The far-seen coast, where all his wishes rest.\par
\par
He views its dark line on the distant sky,\par
And Fancy leads him to his little home,\par
He sees his weeping love, he hears her sigh,\par
He sooths her griefs, and tells of joys to come.\par
\par
Eve yields to night, the breeze to wintry gales,\par
In one vast shade the seas and shores repose;\par
He turns his aching eyes,—his spirit fails,\par
The chill tear falls;—sad to the deck he goes!\par
\par
The storm of midnight swells, the sails are furl’d,\par
Deep sounds the lead, but finds no friendly shore,\par
Fast o’er the waves the wretched bark is hurl’d,\par
“O Ellen, Ellen! we must meet no more!”\par
\par
Lightnings, that show the vast and foamy deep,\par
The rending thunders, as they onward roll,\par
The loud, loud winds, that o’er the billows sweep—\par
Shake the firm nerve, appall the bravest soul!\par
\par
Ah! what avails the seamen’s toiling care!\par
The straining cordage bursts, the mast is riv’n;\par
The sounds of terror groan along the air,\par
Then sink afar;—the bark on rocks is driv’n!\par
\par
Fierce o’er the wreck the whelming waters pass’d,\par
The helpless crew sunk in the roaring main!\par
Henry’s faint accents trembled in the blast—\par
“Farewell, my love!—we ne’er shall meet again!”\par
\par
Oft, at the calm and silent evening hour,\par
When summer-breezes linger on the wave,\par
A melancholy voice is heard to pour\par
Its lonely sweetness o’er poor Henry’s grave!\par
\par
And oft, at midnight, airy strains are heard\par
Around the grove, where Ellen’s form is laid;\par
Nor is the dirge by village-maidens fear’d,\par
For lovers’ spirits guard the holy shade!\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER X\par
\par
Oh! the joy\par
Of young ideas painted on the mind\par
In the warm glowing colours fancy spreads\par
On objects not yet known, when all is new,\par
And all is lovely!\par
                    SACRED DRAMAS\par
\par
\par
We now return to Languedoc and to the mention of Count De Villefort,\par
the nobleman, who succeeded to an estate of the Marquis De Villeroi\par
situated near the monastery of St. Claire. It may be recollected, that\par
this château was uninhabited, when St. Aubert and his daughter were in\par
the neighbourhood, and that the former was much affected on discovering\par
himself to be so near Château-le-Blanc, a place, concerning which the\par
good old La Voisin afterwards dropped some hints, that had alarmed\par
Emily’s curiosity.\par
\par
It was in the year 1584, the beginning of that, in which St. Aubert\par
died, that Francis Beauveau, Count De Villefort, came into possession\par
of the mansion and extensive domain called Château-le-Blanc, situated\par
in the province of Languedoc, on the shore of the Mediterranean. This\par
estate, which, during some centuries, had belonged to his family, now\par
descended to him, on the decease of his relative, the Marquis De\par
Villeroi, who had been latterly a man of reserved manners and austere\par
character; circumstances, which, together with the duties of his\par
profession, that often called him into the field, had prevented any\par
degree of intimacy with his cousin, the Count De Villefort. For many\par
years, they had known little of each other, and the Count received the\par
first intelligence of his death, which happened in a distant part of\par
France, together with the instruments, that gave him possession of the\par
domain Château-le-Blanc; but it was not till the following year, that\par
he determined to visit that estate, when he designed to pass the autumn\par
there. The scenes of Château-le-Blanc often came to his remembrance,\par
heightened by the touches, which a warm imagination gives to the\par
recollection of early pleasures; for, many years before, in the\par
life-time of the Marchioness, and at that age when the mind is\par
particularly sensible to impressions of gaiety and delight, he had once\par
visited this spot, and, though he had passed a long intervening period\par
amidst the vexations and tumults of public affairs, which too\par
frequently corrode the heart, and vitiate the taste, the shades of\par
Languedoc and the grandeur of its distant scenery had never been\par
remembered by him with indifference.\par
\par
During many years, the château had been abandoned by the late Marquis,\par
and, being inhabited only by an old steward and his wife, had been\par
suffered to fall much into decay. To superintend the repairs, that\par
would be requisite to make it a comfortable residence, had been a\par
principal motive with the Count for passing the autumnal months in\par
Languedoc; and neither the remonstrances, nor the tears of the\par
Countess, for, on urgent occasions, she could weep, were powerful\par
enough to overcome his determination. She prepared, therefore, to obey\par
the command, which she could not conquer, and to resign the gay\par
assemblies of Paris,—where her beauty was generally unrivalled and won\par
the applause, to which her wit had but feeble claim—for the twilight\par
canopy of woods, the lonely grandeur of mountains and the solemnity of\par
gothic halls and of long, long galleries, which echoed only the\par
solitary step of a domestic, or the measured clink, that ascended from\par
the great clock—the ancient monitor of the hall below. From these\par
melancholy expectations she endeavoured to relieve her spirits by\par
recollecting all that she had ever heard, concerning the joyous vintage\par
of the plains of Languedoc; but there, alas! no airy forms would bound\par
to the gay melody of Parisian dances, and a view of the rustic\par
festivities of peasants could afford little pleasure to a heart, in\par
which even the feelings of ordinary benevolence had long since decayed\par
under the corruptions of luxury.\par
\par
The Count had a son and a daughter, the children of a former marriage,\par
who, he designed, should accompany him to the south of France; Henri,\par
who was in his twentieth year, was in the French service; and Blanche,\par
who was not yet eighteen, had been hitherto confined to the convent,\par
where she had been placed immediately on her father’s second marriage.\par
The present Countess, who had neither sufficient ability, nor\par
inclination, to superintend the education of her daughter-in-law, had\par
advised this step, and the dread of superior beauty had since urged her\par
to employ every art, that might prevail on the Count to prolong the\par
period of Blanche’s seclusion; it was, therefore, with extreme\par
mortification, that she now understood he would no longer submit on\par
this subject, yet it afforded her some consolation to consider, that,\par
though the Lady Blanche would emerge from her convent, the shades of\par
the country would, for some time, veil her beauty from the public eye.\par
\par
On the morning, which commenced the journey, the postillions stopped at\par
the convent, by the Count’s order, to take up Blanche, whose heart beat\par
with delight, at the prospect of novelty and freedom now before her. As\par
the time of her departure drew nigh, her impatience had increased, and\par
the last night, during which she counted every note of every hour, had\par
appeared the most tedious of any she had ever known. The morning light,\par
at length, dawned; the matin-bell rang; she heard the nuns descending\par
from their chambers, and she started from a sleepless pillow to welcome\par
the day, which was to emancipate her from the severities of a cloister,\par
and introduce her to a world, where pleasure was ever smiling, and\par
goodness ever blessed—where, in short, nothing but pleasure and\par
goodness reigned! When the bell of the great gate rang, and the sound\par
was followed by that of carriage wheels, she ran, with a palpitating\par
heart, to her lattice, and, perceiving her father’s carriage in the\par
court below, danced, with airy steps, along the gallery, where she was\par
met by a nun with a summons from the abbess. In the next moment, she\par
was in the parlour, and in the presence of the Countess who now\par
appeared to her as an angel, that was to lead her into happiness. But\par
the emotions of the Countess, on beholding her, were not in unison with\par
those of Blanche, who had never appeared so lovely as at this moment,\par
when her countenance, animated by the lightning smile of joy, glowed\par
with the beauty of happy innocence.\par
\par
After conversing for a few minutes with the abbess, the Countess rose\par
to go. This was the moment, which Blanche had anticipated with such\par
eager expectation, the summit from which she looked down upon the\par
fairy-land of happiness, and surveyed all its enchantment; was it a\par
moment, then, for tears of regret? Yet it was so. She turned, with an\par
altered and dejected countenance, to her young companions, who were\par
come to bid her farewell, and wept! Even my lady abbess, so stately and\par
so solemn, she saluted with a degree of sorrow, which, an hour before,\par
she would have believed it impossible to feel, and which may be\par
accounted for by considering how reluctantly we all part, even with\par
unpleasing objects, when the separation is consciously for ever. Again,\par
she kissed the poor nuns and then followed the Countess from that spot\par
with tears, which she expected to leave only with smiles.\par
\par
But the presence of her father and the variety of objects, on the road,\par
soon engaged her attention, and dissipated the shade, which tender\par
regret had thrown upon her spirits. Inattentive to a conversation,\par
which was passing between the Countess and a Mademoiselle Bearn, her\par
friend, Blanche sat, lost in pleasing reverie, as she watched the\par
clouds floating silently along the blue expanse, now veiling the sun\par
and stretching their shadows along the distant scene, and then\par
disclosing all his brightness. The journey continued to give Blanche\par
inexpressible delight, for new scenes of nature were every instant\par
opening to her view, and her fancy became stored with gay and beautiful\par
imagery.\par
\par
It was on the evening of the seventh day, that the travellers came\par
within view of Château-le-Blanc, the romantic beauty of whose situation\par
strongly impressed the imagination of Blanche, who observed, with\par
sublime astonishment, the Pyrenean mountains, which had been seen only\par
at a distance during the day, now rising within a few leagues, with\par
their wild cliffs and immense precipices, which the evening clouds,\par
floating round them, now disclosed, and again veiled. The setting rays,\par
that tinged their snowy summits with a roseate hue, touched their lower\par
points with various colouring, while the bluish tint, that pervaded\par
their shadowy recesses, gave the strength of contrast to the splendour\par
of light. The plains of Languedoc, blushing with the purple vine and\par
diversified with groves of mulberry, almond and olives, spread far to\par
the north and the east; to the south, appeared the Mediterranean, clear\par
as crystal, and blue as the heavens it reflected, bearing on its bosom\par
vessels, whose white sails caught the sunbeams, and gave animation to\par
the scene. On a high promontory, washed by the waters of the\par
Mediterranean, stood her father’s mansion, almost secluded from the eye\par
by woods of intermingled pine, oak and chesnut, which crowned the\par
eminence, and sloped towards the plains, on one side; while, on the\par
other, they extended to a considerable distance along the sea-shores.\par
\par
As Blanche drew nearer, the gothic features of this ancient mansion\par
successively appeared—first an embattled turret, rising above the\par
trees—then the broken arch of an immense gateway, retiring beyond them;\par
and she almost fancied herself approaching a castle, such as is often\par
celebrated in early story, where the knights look out from the\par
battlements on some champion below, who, clothed in black armour,\par
comes, with his companions, to rescue the fair lady of his love from\par
the oppression of his rival; a sort of legends, to which she had once\par
or twice obtained access in the library of her convent, that, like many\par
others, belonging to the monks, was stored with these reliques of\par
romantic fiction.\par
\par
The carriages stopped at a gate, which led into the domain of the\par
château, but which was now fastened; and the great bell, that had\par
formerly served to announce the arrival of strangers, having long since\par
fallen from its station, a servant climbed over a ruined part of the\par
adjoining wall, to give notice to those within of the arrival of their\par
lord.\par
\par
As Blanche leaned from the coach window, she resigned herself to the\par
sweet and gentle emotions, which the hour and the scenery awakened. The\par
sun had now left the earth, and twilight began to darken the mountains;\par
while the distant waters, reflecting the blush that still glowed in the\par
west, appeared like a line of light, skirting the horizon. The low\par
murmur of waves, breaking on the shore, came in the breeze, and, now\par
and then, the melancholy dashing of oars was feebly heard from a\par
distance. She was suffered to indulge her pensive mood, for the\par
thoughts of the rest of the party were silently engaged upon the\par
subjects of their several interests. Meanwhile, the Countess,\par
reflecting, with regret, upon the gay parties she had left at Paris,\par
surveyed, with disgust, what she thought the gloomy woods and solitary\par
wildness of the scene; and, shrinking from the prospect of being shut\par
up in an old castle, was prepared to meet every object with\par
displeasure. The feelings of Henri were somewhat similar to those of\par
the Countess; he gave a mournful sigh to the delights of the capital,\par
and to the remembrance of a lady, who, he believed, had engaged his\par
affections, and who had certainly fascinated his imagination; but the\par
surrounding country, and the mode of life, on which he was entering,\par
had, for him, at least, the charm of novelty, and his regret was\par
softened by the gay expectations of youth. The gates being at length\par
unbarred, the carriage moved slowly on, under spreading chesnuts, that\par
almost excluded the remains of day, following what had been formerly a\par
road, but which now, overgrown with luxuriant vegetation, could be\par
traced only by the boundary, formed by trees, on either side, and which\par
wound for near half a mile among the woods, before it reached the\par
château. This was the very avenue that St. Aubert and Emily had\par
formerly entered, on their first arrival in the neighbourhood, with the\par
hope of finding a house, that would receive them, for the night, and\par
had so abruptly quitted, on perceiving the wildness of the place, and a\par
figure, which the postillion had fancied was a robber.\par
\par
“What a dismal place is this!” exclaimed the Countess, as the carriage\par
penetrated the deeper recesses of the woods. “Surely, my lord, you do\par
not mean to pass all the autumn in this barbarous spot! One ought to\par
bring hither a cup of the waters of Lethe, that the remembrance of\par
pleasanter scenes may not heighten, at least, the natural dreariness of\par
these.”\par
\par
“I shall be governed by circumstances, madam,” said the Count, “this\par
barbarous spot was inhabited by my ancestors.”\par
\par
The carriage now stopped at the château, where, at the door of the\par
great hall, appeared the old steward and the Parisian servants, who had\par
been sent to prepare the château, waiting to receive their lord. Lady\par
Blanche now perceived, that the edifice was not built entirely in the\par
gothic style, but that it had additions of a more modern date; the\par
large and gloomy hall, however, into which she now entered, was\par
entirely gothic, and sumptuous tapestry, which it was now too dark to\par
distinguish, hung upon the walls, and depictured scenes from some of\par
the ancient Provençal romances. A vast gothic window, embroidered with\par
_clematis_ and eglantine, that ascended to the south, led the eye, now\par
that the casements were thrown open, through this verdant shade, over a\par
sloping lawn, to the tops of dark woods, that hung upon the brow of the\par
promontory. Beyond, appeared the waters of the Mediterranean,\par
stretching far to the south, and to the east, where they were lost in\par
the horizon; while, to the north-east, they were bounded by the\par
luxuriant shores of Languedoc and Provence, enriched with wood, and gay\par
with vines and sloping pastures; and, to the south-west, by the\par
majestic Pyrenees, now fading from the eye, beneath the gradual gloom.\par
\par
Blanche, as she crossed the hall, stopped a moment to observe this\par
lovely prospect, which the evening twilight obscured, yet did not\par
conceal. But she was quickly awakened from the complacent delight,\par
which this scene had diffused upon her mind, by the Countess, who,\par
discontented with every object around, and impatient for refreshment\par
and repose, hastened forward to a large parlour, whose cedar wainscot,\par
narrow, pointed casements, and dark ceiling of carved cypress wood,\par
gave it an aspect of peculiar gloom, which the dingy green velvet of\par
the chairs and couches, fringed with tarnished gold, had once been\par
designed to enliven.\par
\par
While the Countess enquired for refreshment, the Count, attended by his\par
son, went to look over some part of the château, and Lady Blanche\par
reluctantly remained to witness the discontent and ill-humour of her\par
step-mother.\par
\par
“How long have you lived in this desolate place?” said her ladyship, to\par
the old housekeeper, who came to pay her duty.\par
\par
“Above twenty years, your ladyship, on the next feast of St. Jerome.”\par
\par
“How happened it, that you have lived here so long, and almost alone,\par
too? I understood, that the château had been shut up for some years?”\par
\par
“Yes, madam, it was for many years after my late lord, the Count, went\par
to the wars; but it is above twenty years, since I and my husband came\par
into his service. The place is so large, and has of late been so\par
lonely, that we were lost in it, and, after some time, we went to live\par
in a cottage at the end of the woods, near some of the tenants, and\par
came to look after the château, every now and then. When my lord\par
returned to France from the wars, he took a dislike to the place, and\par
never came to live here again, and so he was satisfied with our\par
remaining at the cottage. Alas—alas! how the château is changed from\par
what it once was! What delight my late lady used to take in it! I well\par
remember when she came here a bride, and how fine it was. Now, it has\par
been neglected so long, and is gone into such decay! I shall never see\par
those days again!”\par
\par
The Countess appearing to be somewhat offended by the thoughtless\par
simplicity, with which the old woman regretted former times, Dorothée\par
added—“But the château will now be inhabited, and cheerful again; not\par
all the world could tempt me to live in it alone.”\par
\par
“Well, the experiment will not be made, I believe,” said the Countess,\par
displeased that her own silence had been unable to awe the loquacity of\par
this rustic old housekeeper, now spared from further attendance by the\par
entrance of the Count, who said he had been viewing part of the\par
château, and found, that it would require considerable repairs and some\par
alterations, before it would be perfectly comfortable, as a place of\par
residence. “I am sorry to hear it, my lord,” replied the Countess. “And\par
why sorry, madam?” “Because the place will ill repay your trouble; and\par
were it even a paradise, it would be insufferable at such a distance\par
from Paris.”\par
\par
The Count made no reply, but walked abruptly to a window. “There are\par
windows, my lord, but they neither admit entertainment, nor light; they\par
show only a scene of savage nature.”\par
\par
“I am at a loss, madam,” said the Count, “to conjecture what you mean\par
by savage nature. Do those plains, or those woods, or that fine expanse\par
of water, deserve the name?”\par
\par
“Those mountains certainly do, my lord,” rejoined the Countess,\par
pointing to the Pyrenees, “and this château, though not a work of rude\par
nature, is, to my taste, at least, one of savage art.” The Count\par
coloured highly. “This place, madam, was the work of my ancestors,”\par
said he, “and you must allow me to say, that your present conversation\par
discovers neither good taste, nor good manners.” Blanche, now shocked\par
at an altercation, which appeared to be increasing to a serious\par
disagreement, rose to leave the room, when her mother’s woman entered\par
it; and the Countess, immediately desiring to be shown to her own\par
apartment, withdrew, attended by Mademoiselle Bearn.\par
\par
Lady Blanche, it being not yet dark, took this opportunity of exploring\par
new scenes, and, leaving the parlour, she passed from the hall into a\par
wide gallery, whose walls were decorated by marble pilasters, which\par
supported an arched roof, composed of a rich mosaic work. Through a\par
distant window, that seemed to terminate the gallery, were seen the\par
purple clouds of evening and a landscape, whose features, thinly veiled\par
in twilight, no longer appeared distinctly, but, blended into one grand\par
mass, stretched to the horizon, coloured only with a tint of solemn\par
grey.\par
\par
The gallery terminated in a saloon, to which the window she had seen\par
through an open door, belonged; but the increasing dusk permitted her\par
only an imperfect view of this apartment, which seemed to be\par
magnificent and of modern architecture; though it had been either\par
suffered to fall into decay, or had never been properly finished. The\par
windows, which were numerous and large, descended low, and afforded a\par
very extensive, and what Blanche’s fancy represented to be, a very\par
lovely prospect; and she stood for some time, surveying the grey\par
obscurity and depicturing imaginary woods and mountains, valleys and\par
rivers, on this scene of night; her solemn sensations rather assisted,\par
than interrupted, by the distant bark of a watch-dog, and by the\par
breeze, as it trembled upon the light foliage of the shrubs. Now and\par
then, appeared for a moment, among the woods, a cottage light; and, at\par
length, was heard, afar off, the evening bell of a convent, dying on\par
the air. When she withdrew her thoughts from these subjects of fanciful\par
delight, the gloom and silence of the saloon somewhat awed her; and,\par
having sought the door of the gallery, and pursued, for a considerable\par
time, a dark passage, she came to a hall, but one totally different\par
from that she had formerly seen. By the twilight, admitted through an\par
open portico, she could just distinguish this apartment to be of very\par
light and airy architecture, and that it was paved with white marble,\par
pillars of which supported the roof, that rose into arches built in the\par
Moorish style. While Blanche stood on the steps of this portico, the\par
moon rose over the sea, and gradually disclosed, in partial light, the\par
beauties of the eminence, on which she stood, whence a lawn, now rude\par
and overgrown with high grass, sloped to the woods, that, almost\par
surrounding the château, extended in a grand sweep down the southern\par
sides of the promontory to the very margin of the ocean. Beyond the\par
woods, on the north-side, appeared a long tract of the plains of\par
Languedoc; and, to the east, the landscape she had before dimly seen,\par
with the towers of a monastery, illumined by the moon, rising over dark\par
groves.\par
\par
The soft and shadowy tint, that overspread the scene, the waves,\par
undulating in the moonlight, and their low and measured murmurs on the\par
beach, were circumstances, that united to elevate the unaccustomed mind\par
of Blanche to enthusiasm.\par
\par
“And have I lived in this glorious world so long,” said she, “and never\par
till now beheld such a prospect—never experienced these delights! Every\par
peasant girl, on my father’s domain, has viewed from her infancy the\par
face of nature; has ranged, at liberty, her romantic wilds, while I\par
have been shut in a cloister from the view of these beautiful\par
appearances, which were designed to enchant all eyes, and awaken all\par
hearts. How can the poor nuns and friars feel the full fervour of\par
devotion, if they never see the sun rise, or set? Never, till this\par
evening, did I know what true devotion is; for, never before did I see\par
the sun sink below the vast earth! Tomorrow, for the first time in my\par
life, I will see it rise. O, who would live in Paris, to look upon\par
black walls and dirty streets, when, in the country, they might gaze on\par
the blue heavens, and all the green earth!”\par
\par
This enthusiastic soliloquy was interrupted by a rustling noise in the\par
hall; and, while the loneliness of the place made her sensible to fear,\par
she thought she perceived something moving between the pillars. For a\par
moment, she continued silently observing it, till, ashamed of her\par
ridiculous apprehensions, she recollected courage enough to demand who\par
was there. “O my young lady, is it you?” said the old housekeeper, who\par
was come to shut the windows, “I am glad it is you.” The manner, in\par
which she spoke this, with a faint breath, rather surprised Blanche,\par
who said, “You seemed frightened, Dorothée, what is the matter?”\par
\par
“No, not frightened, ma’amselle,” replied Dorothée, hesitating and\par
trying to appear composed, “but I am old, and—a little matter startles\par
me.” The Lady Blanche smiled at the distinction. “I am glad, that my\par
lord the Count is come to live at the château, ma’amselle,” continued\par
Dorothée, “for it has been many a year deserted, and dreary enough;\par
now, the place will look a little as it used to do, when my poor lady\par
was alive.” Blanche enquired how long it was, since the Marchioness\par
died? “Alas! my lady,” replied Dorothée, “so long—that I have ceased to\par
count the years! The place, to my mind, has mourned ever since, and I\par
am sure my lord’s vassals have! But you have lost yourself,\par
ma’amselle,—shall I show you to the other side of the château?”\par
\par
Blanche enquired how long this part of the edifice had been built.\par
“Soon after my lord’s marriage, ma’am,” replied Dorothée. “The place\par
was large enough without this addition, for many rooms of the old\par
building were even then never made use of, and my lord had a princely\par
household too; but he thought the ancient mansion gloomy, and gloomy\par
enough it is!” Lady Blanche now desired to be shown to the inhabited\par
part of the château; and, as the passages were entirely dark, Dorothée\par
conducted her along the edge of the lawn to the opposite side of the\par
edifice, where, a door opening into the great hall, she was met by\par
Mademoiselle Bearn. “Where have you been so long?” said she, “I had\par
begun to think some wonderful adventure had befallen you, and that the\par
giant of this enchanted castle, or the ghost, which, no doubt, haunts\par
it, had conveyed you through a trap-door into some subterranean vault,\par
whence you were never to return.”\par
\par
“No,” replied Blanche, laughingly, “you seem to love adventures so\par
well, that I leave them for you to achieve.”\par
\par
“Well, I am willing to achieve them, provided I am allowed to describe\par
them.”\par
\par
“My dear Mademoiselle Bearn,” said Henri, as he met her at the door of\par
the parlour, “no ghost of these days would be so savage as to impose\par
silence on you. Our ghosts are more civilized than to condemn a lady to\par
a purgatory severer even, than their own, be it what it may.”\par
\par
Mademoiselle Bearn replied only by a laugh; and, the Count now entering\par
the room, supper was served, during which he spoke little, frequently\par
appeared to be abstracted from the company, and more than once\par
remarked, that the place was greatly altered, since he had last seen\par
it. “Many years have intervened since that period,” said he; “and,\par
though the grand features of the scenery admit of no change, they\par
impress me with sensations very different from those I formerly\par
experienced.”\par
\par
“Did these scenes, sir,” said Blanche, “ever appear more lovely, than\par
they do now? To me this seems hardly possible.” The Count, regarding\par
her with a melancholy smile, said, “They once were as delightful to me,\par
as they are now to you; the landscape is not changed, but time has\par
changed me; from my mind the illusion, which gave spirit to the\par
colouring of nature, is fading fast! If you live, my dear Blanche, to\par
revisit this spot, at the distance of many years, you will, perhaps,\par
remember and understand the feelings of your father.”\par
\par
Lady Blanche, affected by these words, remained silent; she looked\par
forward to the period, which the Count anticipated, and considering,\par
that he, who now spoke, would then probably be no more, her eyes, bent\par
to the ground, were filed with tears. She gave her hand to her father,\par
who, smiling affectionately, rose from his chair, and went to a window\par
to conceal his emotion.\par
\par
The fatigues of the day made the party separate at an early hour, when\par
Blanche retired through a long oak gallery to her chamber, whose\par
spacious and lofty walls, high antiquated casements, and, what was the\par
effect of these, its gloomy air, did not reconcile her to its remote\par
situation, in this ancient building. The furniture, also, was of\par
ancient date; the bed was of blue damask, trimmed with tarnished gold\par
lace, and its lofty tester rose in the form of a canopy, whence the\par
curtains descended, like those of such tents as are sometimes\par
represented in old pictures, and, indeed, much resembling those,\par
exhibited on the faded tapestry, with which the chamber was hung. To\par
Blanche, every object here was matter of curiosity; and, taking the\par
light from her woman to examine the tapestry, she perceived, that it\par
represented scenes from the wars of Troy, though the almost colourless\par
worsted now mocked the glowing actions they once had painted. She\par
laughed at the ludicrous absurdity she observed, till, recollecting,\par
that the hands, which had wove it, were, like the poet, whose thoughts\par
of fire they had attempted to express, long since mouldered into dust,\par
a train of melancholy ideas passed over her mind, and she almost wept.\par
\par
Having given her woman a strict injunction to awaken her, before\par
sunrise, she dismissed her; and then, to dissipate the gloom, which\par
reflection had cast upon her spirits, opened one of the high casements,\par
and was again cheered by the face of living nature. The shadowy earth,\par
the air, and ocean—all was still. Along the deep serene of the heavens,\par
a few light clouds floated slowly, through whose skirts the stars now\par
seemed to tremble, and now to emerge with purer splendour. Blanche’s\par
thoughts arose involuntarily to the Great Author of the sublime objects\par
she contemplated, and she breathed a prayer of finer devotion, than any\par
she had ever uttered beneath the vaulted roof of a cloister. At this\par
casement, she remained till the glooms of midnight were stretched over\par
the prospect. She then retired to her pillow, and, “with gay visions of\par
tomorrow,” to those sweet slumbers, which health and happy innocence\par
only know.\par
\par
Tomorrow, to fresh woods and pastures new.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XI\par
\par
What transport to retrace our early plays,\par
Our easy bliss, when each thing joy supplied\par
The woods, the mountains and the warbling maze\par
Of the wild brooks!\par
                    THOMSON\par
\par
\par
Blanche’s slumbers continued, till long after the hour, which she had\par
so impatiently anticipated, for her woman, fatigued with travelling,\par
did not call her, till breakfast was nearly ready. Her disappointment,\par
however, was instantly forgotten, when, on opening the casement, she\par
saw, on one hand, the wide sea sparkling in the morning rays, with its\par
stealing sails and glancing oars; and, on the other, the fresh woods,\par
the plains far-stretching and the blue mountains, all glowing with the\par
splendour of day.\par
\par
As she inspired the pure breeze, health spread a deeper blush upon her\par
countenance, and pleasure danced in her eyes.\par
\par
“Who could first invent convents!” said she, “and who could first\par
persuade people to go into them? and to make religion a pretence, too,\par
where all that should inspire it, is so carefully shut out! God is best\par
pleased with the homage of a grateful heart, and, when we view his\par
glories, we feel most grateful. I never felt so much devotion, during\par
the many dull years I was in the convent, as I have done in the few\par
hours, that I have been here, where I need only look on all around\par
me—to adore God in my inmost heart!”\par
\par
Saying this, she left the window, bounded along the gallery, and, in\par
the next moment, was in the breakfast room, where the Count was already\par
seated. The cheerfulness of a bright sunshine had dispersed the\par
melancholy glooms of his reflections, a pleasant smile was on his\par
countenance, and he spoke in an enlivening voice to Blanche, whose\par
heart echoed back the tones. Henri and, soon after, the Countess with\par
Mademoiselle Bearn appeared, and the whole party seemed to acknowledge\par
the influence of the scene; even the Countess was so much reanimated as\par
to receive the civilities of her husband with complacency, and but once\par
forgot her good-humour, which was when she asked whether they had any\par
neighbours, who were likely to make _this barbarous spot _ more\par
tolerable, and whether the Count believed it possible for her to exist\par
here, without some amusement?\par
\par
Soon after breakfast the party dispersed; the Count, ordering his\par
steward to attend him in the library, went to survey the condition of\par
his premises, and to visit some of his tenants; Henri hastened with\par
alacrity to the shore to examine a boat, that was to bear them on a\par
little voyage in the evening and to superintend the adjustment of a\par
silk awning; while the Countess, attended by Mademoiselle Bearn,\par
retired to an apartment on the modern side of the château, which was\par
fitted up with airy elegance; and, as the windows opened upon\par
balconies, that fronted the sea, she was there saved from a view of the\par
_horrid_ Pyrenees. Here, while she reclined on a sofa, and, casting her\par
languid eyes over the ocean, which appeared beyond the wood-tops,\par
indulged in the luxuries of _ennui_, her companion read aloud a\par
sentimental novel, on some fashionable system of philosophy, for the\par
Countess was herself somewhat of a _philosopher_, especially as to\par
_infidelity_, and among a certain circle her opinions were waited for\par
with impatience, and received as doctrines.\par
\par
The Lady Blanche, meanwhile, hastened to indulge, amidst the wild\par
wood-walks around the château, her new enthusiasm, where, as she\par
wandered under the shades, her gay spirits gradually yielded to pensive\par
complacency. Now, she moved with solemn steps, beneath the gloom of\par
thickly interwoven branches, where the fresh dew still hung upon every\par
flower, that peeped from among the grass; and now tripped sportively\par
along the path, on which the sunbeams darted and the checquered foliage\par
trembled—where the tender greens of the beech, the acacia and the\par
mountain-ash, mingling with the solemn tints of the cedar, the pine and\par
cypress, exhibited as fine a contrast of colouring, as the majestic oak\par
and oriental plane did of form, to the feathery lightness of the cork\par
tree and the waving grace of the poplar.\par
\par
Having reached a rustic seat, within a deep recess of the woods, she\par
rested awhile, and, as her eyes caught, through a distant opening, a\par
glimpse of the blue waters of the Mediterranean, with the white sail,\par
gliding on its bosom, or of the broad mountain, glowing beneath the\par
mid-day sun, her mind experienced somewhat of that exquisite delight,\par
which awakens the fancy, and leads to poetry. The hum of bees alone\par
broke the stillness around her, as, with other insects of various hues,\par
they sported gaily in the shade, or sipped sweets from the fresh\par
flowers: and, while Blanche watched a butter-fly, flitting from bud to\par
bud, she indulged herself in imagining the pleasures of its short day,\par
till she had composed the following stanzas.\par
\par
THE BUTTER-FLY TO HIS LOVE\par
\par
What bowery dell, with fragrant breath,\par
Courts thee to stay thy airy flight;\par
Nor seek again the purple heath,\par
So oft the scene of gay delight?\par
\par
Long I’ve watch’d i’ the lily’s bell,\par
Whose whiteness stole the morning’s beam;\par
No fluttering sounds thy coming tell,\par
No waving wings, at distance, gleam.\par
\par
But fountain fresh, nor breathing grove,\par
Nor sunny mead, nor blossom’d tree,\par
So sweet as lily’s cell shall prove,—\par
The bower of constant love and me.\par
\par
When April buds begin to blow,\par
The prim-rose, and the hare-bell blue,\par
That on the verdant moss bank grow,\par
With violet cups, that weep in dew;\par
\par
When wanton gales breathe through the shade,\par
And shake the blooms, and steal their sweets,\par
And swell the song of ev’ry glade,\par
I range the forest’s green retreats:\par
\par
There, through the tangled wood-walks play,\par
Where no rude urchin paces near,\par
Where sparely peeps the sultry day,\par
And light dews freshen all the air.\par
\par
High on a sunbeam oft I sport\par
O’er bower and fountain, vale and hill;\par
Oft ev’ry blushing flow’ret court,\par
That hangs its head o’er winding rill.\par
\par
But these I’ll leave to be thy guide,\par
And show thee, where the jasmine spreads\par
Her snowy leaf, where may-flow’rs hide,\par
And rose-buds rear their peeping heads.\par
\par
With me the mountain’s summit scale,\par
And taste the wild-thyme’s honied bloom,\par
Whose fragrance, floating on the gale,\par
Oft leads me to the cedar’s gloom.\par
\par
Yet, yet, no sound comes in the breeze!\par
What shade thus dares to tempt thy stay?\par
Once, me alone thou wish’d to please,\par
And with me only thou wouldst stray.\par
\par
But, while thy long delay I mourn,\par
And chide the sweet shades for their guile,\par
Thou may’st be true, and they forlorn,\par
And fairy favours court thy smile.\par
\par
The tiny queen of fairy-land,\par
Who knows thy speed, hath sent thee far,\par
To bring, or ere the night-watch stand,\par
Rich essence for her shadowy car:\par
\par
Perchance her acorn-cups to fill\par
With nectar from the Indian rose,\par
Or gather, near some haunted rill,\par
May-dews, that lull to sleep Love’s woes:\par
\par
Or, o’er the mountains, bade thee fly,\par
To tell her fairy love to speed,\par
When ev’ning steals upon the sky,\par
To dance along the twilight mead.\par
\par
But now I see thee sailing low,\par
Gay as the brightest flow’rs of spring,\par
Thy coat of blue and jet I know,\par
And well thy gold and purple wing.\par
\par
Borne on the gale, thou com’st to me;\par
O! welcome, welcome to my home!\par
In lily’s cell we’ll live in glee,\par
Together o’er the mountains roam!\par
\par
\par
When Lady Blanche returned to the château, instead of going to the\par
apartment of the Countess, she amused herself with wandering over that\par
part of the edifice, which she had not yet examined, of which the most\par
ancient first attracted her curiosity; for, though what she had seen of\par
the modern was gay and elegant, there was something in the former more\par
interesting to her imagination. Having passed up the great staircase,\par
and through the oak gallery, she entered upon a long suite of chambers,\par
whose walls were either hung with tapestry, or wainscoted with cedar,\par
the furniture of which looked almost as ancient as the rooms\par
themselves; the spacious fire-places, where no mark of social cheer\par
remained, presented an image of cold desolation; and the whole suite\par
had so much the air of neglect and desertion, that it seemed, as if the\par
venerable persons, whose portraits hung upon the walls, had been the\par
last to inhabit them.\par
\par
On leaving these rooms, she found herself in another gallery, one end\par
of which was terminated by a back staircase, and the other by a door,\par
that seemed to communicate with the north-side of the château, but\par
which being fastened, she descended the staircase, and, opening a door\par
in the wall, a few steps down, found herself in a small square room,\par
that formed part of the west turret of the castle. Three windows\par
presented each a separate and beautiful prospect; that to the north,\par
overlooking Languedoc; another to the west, the hills ascending towards\par
the Pyrenees, whose awful summits crowned the landscape; and a third,\par
fronting the south, gave the Mediterranean, and a part of the wild\par
shores of Rousillon, to the eye.\par
\par
Having left the turret, and descended the narrow staircase, she found\par
herself in a dusky passage, where she wandered, unable to find her way,\par
till impatience yielded to apprehension, and she called for assistance.\par
Presently steps approached, and light glimmered through a door at the\par
other extremity of the passage, which was opened with caution by some\par
person, who did not venture beyond it, and whom Blanche observed in\par
silence, till the door was closing, when she called aloud, and,\par
hastening towards it, perceived the old housekeeper. “Dear ma’amselle!\par
is it you?” said Dorothée, “How could you find your way hither?” Had\par
Blanche been less occupied by her own fears, she would probably have\par
observed the strong expressions of terror and surprise on Dorothée’s\par
countenance, who now led her through a long succession of passages and\par
rooms, that looked as if they had been uninhabited for a century, till\par
they reached that appropriated to the housekeeper, where Dorothée\par
entreated she would sit down and take refreshment. Blanche accepted the\par
sweet meats, offered to her, mentioned her discovery of the pleasant\par
turret, and her wish to appropriate it to her own use. Whether\par
Dorothée’s taste was not so sensible to the beauties of landscape as\par
her young lady’s, or that the constant view of lovely scenery had\par
deadened it, she forbore to praise the subject of Blanche’s enthusiasm,\par
which, however, her silence did not repress. To Lady Blanche’s enquiry\par
of whither the door she had found fastened at the end of the gallery\par
led, she replied, that it opened to a suite of rooms, which had not\par
been entered, during many years, “For,” added she, “my late lady died\par
in one of them, and I could never find in my heart to go into them\par
since.”\par
\par
Blanche, though she wished to see these chambers, forbore, on observing\par
that Dorothée’s eyes were filled with tears, to ask her to unlock them,\par
and, soon after, went to dress for dinner, at which the whole party met\par
in good spirits and good humour, except the Countess, whose vacant\par
mind, overcome by the languor of idleness, would neither suffer her to\par
be happy herself, nor to contribute to the happiness of others.\par
Mademoiselle Bearn, attempting to be witty, directed her badinage\par
against Henri, who answered, because he could not well avoid it, rather\par
than from any inclination to notice her, whose liveliness sometimes\par
amused, but whose conceit and insensibility often disgusted him.\par
\par
The cheerfulness, with which Blanche rejoined the party, vanished, on\par
her reaching the margin of the sea; she gazed with apprehension upon\par
the immense expanse of waters, which, at a distance, she had beheld\par
only with delight and astonishment, and it was by a strong effort, that\par
she so far overcame her fears as to follow her father into the boat.\par
\par
As she silently surveyed the vast horizon, bending round the distant\par
verge of the ocean, an emotion of sublimest rapture struggled to\par
overcome a sense of personal danger. A light breeze played on the\par
water, and on the silk awning of the boat, and waved the foliage of the\par
receding woods, that crowned the cliffs, for many miles, and which the\par
Count surveyed with the pride of conscious property, as well as with\par
the eye of taste.\par
\par
At some distance, among these woods, stood a pavilion, which had once\par
been the scene of social gaiety, and which its situation still made one\par
of romantic beauty. Thither, the Count had ordered coffee and other\par
refreshment to be carried, and thither the sailors now steered their\par
course, following the windings of the shore round many a woody\par
promontory and circling bay; while the pensive tones of horns and other\par
wind instruments, played by the attendants in a distant boat, echoed\par
among the rocks, and died along the waves. Blanche had now subdued her\par
fears; a delightful tranquillity stole over her mind, and held her in\par
silence; and she was too happy even to remember the convent, or her\par
former sorrows, as subjects of comparison with her present felicity.\par
\par
The Countess felt less unhappy than she had done, since the moment of\par
her leaving Paris; for her mind was now under some degree of restraint;\par
she feared to indulge its wayward humours, and even wished to recover\par
the Count’s good opinion. On his family, and on the surrounding scene,\par
he looked with tempered pleasure and benevolent satisfaction, while his\par
son exhibited the gay spirits of youth, anticipating new delights, and\par
regretless of those, that were passed.\par
\par
After near an hour’s rowing, the party landed, and ascended a little\par
path, overgrown with vegetation. At a little distance from the point of\par
the eminence, within the shadowy recess of the woods, appeared the\par
pavilion, which Blanche perceived, as she caught a glimpse of its\par
portico between the trees, to be built of variegated marble. As she\par
followed the Countess, she often turned her eyes with rapture towards\par
the ocean, seen beneath the dark foliage, far below, and from thence\par
upon the deep woods, whose silence and impenetrable gloom awakened\par
emotions more solemn, but scarcely less delightful.\par
\par
The pavilion had been prepared, as far as was possible, on a very short\par
notice, for the reception of its visitors; but the faded colours of its\par
painted walls and ceiling, and the decayed drapery of its once\par
magnificent furniture, declared how long it had been neglected, and\par
abandoned to the empire of the changing seasons. While the party\par
partook of a collation of fruit and coffee, the horns, placed in a\par
distant part of the woods, where an echo sweetened and prolonged their\par
melancholy tones, broke softly on the stillness of the scene. This spot\par
seemed to attract even the admiration of the Countess, or, perhaps, it\par
was merely the pleasure of planning furniture and decorations, that\par
made her dwell so long on the necessity of repairing and adorning it;\par
while the Count, never happier than when he saw her mind engaged by\par
natural and simple objects, acquiesced in all her designs, concerning\par
the pavilion. The paintings on the walls and coved ceiling were to be\par
renewed, the canopies and sofas were to be of light green damask;\par
marble statues of wood-nymphs, bearing on their heads baskets of living\par
flowers, were to adorn the recesses between the windows, which,\par
descending to the ground, were to admit to every part of the room, and\par
it was of octagonal form, the various landscape. One window opened upon\par
a romantic glade, where the eye roved among the woody recesses, and the\par
scene was bounded only by a lengthened pomp of groves; from another,\par
the woods receding disclosed the distant summits of the Pyrenees; a\par
third fronted an avenue, beyond which the grey towers of\par
Château-le-Blanc, and a picturesque part of its ruin were seen\par
partially among the foliage; while a fourth gave, between the trees, a\par
glimpse of the green pastures and villages, that diversify the banks of\par
the Aude. The Mediterranean, with the bold cliffs, that overlooked its\par
shores, were the grand objects of a fifth window, and the others gave,\par
in different points of view, the wild scenery of the woods.\par
\par
After wandering, for some time, in these, the party returned to the\par
shore and embarked; and, the beauty of the evening tempting them to\par
extend their excursion, they proceeded further up the bay. A dead calm\par
had succeeded the light breeze, that wafted them hither, and the men\par
took to their oars. Around, the waters were spread into one vast\par
expanse of polished mirror, reflecting the grey cliffs and feathery\par
woods, that over-hung its surface, the glow of the western horizon and\par
the dark clouds, that came slowly from the east. Blanche loved to see\par
the dipping oars imprint the water, and to watch the spreading circles\par
they left, which gave a tremulous motion to the reflected landscape,\par
without destroying the harmony of its features.\par
\par
Above the darkness of the woods, her eye now caught a cluster of high\par
towers, touched with the splendour of the setting rays; and, soon\par
after, the horns being then silent, she heard the faint swell of choral\par
voices from a distance.\par
\par
“What voices are those, upon the air?” said the Count, looking round,\par
and listening; but the strain had ceased. “It seemed to be a\par
vesper-hymn, which I have often heard in my convent,” said Blanche.\par
\par
“We are near the monastery, then,” observed the Count; and, the boat\par
soon after doubling a lofty head-land, the monastery of St. Claire\par
appeared, seated near the margin of the sea, where the cliffs, suddenly\par
sinking, formed a low shore within a small bay, almost encircled with\par
woods, among which partial features of the edifice were seen;—the great\par
gate and gothic window of the hall, the cloisters and the side of a\par
chapel more remote; while a venerable arch, which had once led to a\par
part of the fabric, now demolished, stood a majestic ruin detached from\par
the main building, beyond which appeared a grand perspective of the\par
woods. On the grey walls, the moss had fastened, and, round the pointed\par
windows of the chapel, the ivy and the briony hung in many a fantastic\par
wreath.\par
\par
All without was silent and forsaken; but, while Blanche gazed with\par
admiration on this venerable pile, whose effect was heightened by the\par
strong lights and shadows thrown athwart it by a cloudy sunset, a sound\par
of many voices, slowly chanting, arose from within. The Count bade his\par
men rest on their oars. The monks were singing the hymn of vespers, and\par
some female voices mingled with the strain, which rose by soft degrees,\par
till the high organ and the choral sounds swelled into full and solemn\par
harmony. The strain, soon after, dropped into sudden silence, and was\par
renewed in a low and still more solemn key, till, at length, the holy\par
chorus died away, and was heard no more.—Blanche sighed, tears trembled\par
in her eyes, and her thoughts seemed wafted with the sounds to heaven.\par
While a rapt stillness prevailed in the boat, a train of friars, and\par
then of nuns, veiled in white, issued from the cloisters, and passed,\par
under the shade of the woods, to the main body of the edifice.\par
\par
The Countess was the first of her party to awaken from this pause of\par
silence.\par
\par
“These dismal hymns and friars make one quite melancholy,” said she;\par
“twilight is coming on; pray let us return, or it will be dark before\par
we get home.”\par
\par
The count, looking up, now perceived, that the twilight of evening was\par
anticipated by an approaching storm. In the east a tempest was\par
collecting; a heavy gloom came on, opposing and contrasting the glowing\par
splendour of the setting sun. The clamorous sea-fowl skimmed in fleet\par
circles upon the surface of the sea, dipping their light pinions in the\par
wave, as they fled away in search of shelter. The boatmen pulled hard\par
at their oars; but the thunder, that now muttered at a distance, and\par
the heavy drops, that began to dimple the water, made the Count\par
determine to put back to the monastery for shelter, and the course of\par
the boat was immediately changed. As the clouds approached the west,\par
their lurid darkness changed to a deep ruddy glow, which, by\par
reflection, seemed to fire the tops of the woods and the shattered\par
towers of the monastery.\par
\par
The appearance of the heavens alarmed the Countess and Mademoiselle\par
Bearn, whose expressions of apprehension distressed the Count, and\par
perplexed his men; while Blanche continued silent, now agitated with\par
fear, and now with admiration, as she viewed the grandeur of the\par
clouds, and their effect on the scenery, and listened to the long, long\par
peals of thunder, that rolled through the air.\par
\par
The boat having reached the lawn before the monastery, the Count sent a\par
servant to announce his arrival, and to entreat shelter of the\par
Superior, who, soon after, appeared at the great gate, attended by\par
several monks, while the servant returned with a message, expressive at\par
once of hospitality and pride, but of pride disguised in submission.\par
The party immediately disembarked, and, having hastily crossed the\par
lawn—for the shower was now heavy—were received at the gate by the\par
Superior, who, as they entered, stretched forth his hands and gave his\par
blessing; and they passed into the great hall, where the lady abbess\par
waited, attended by several nuns, clothed, like herself, in black, and\par
veiled in white. The veil of the abbess was, however, thrown half back,\par
and discovered a countenance, whose chaste dignity was sweetened by the\par
smile of welcome, with which she addressed the Countess, whom she led,\par
with Blanche and Mademoiselle Bearn, into the convent parlour, while\par
the Count and Henri were conducted by the Superior to the refectory.\par
\par
The Countess, fatigued and discontented, received the politeness of the\par
abbess with careless haughtiness, and had followed her, with indolent\par
steps, to the parlour, over which the painted casements and wainscot of\par
larch-wood threw, at all times, a melancholy shade, and where the gloom\par
of evening now loured almost to darkness.\par
\par
While the lady abbess ordered refreshment, and conversed with the\par
Countess, Blanche withdrew to a window, the lower panes of which, being\par
without painting, allowed her to observe the progress of the storm over\par
the Mediterranean, whose dark waves, that had so lately slept, now came\par
boldly swelling, in long succession, to the shore, where they burst in\par
white foam, and threw up a high spray over the rocks. A red sulphureous\par
tint overspread the long line of clouds, that hung above the western\par
horizon, beneath whose dark skirts the sun looking out, illumined the\par
distant shores of Languedoc, as well as the tufted summits of the\par
nearer woods, and shed a partial gleam on the western waves. The rest\par
of the scene was in deep gloom, except where a sunbeam, darting between\par
the clouds, glanced on the white wings of the sea-fowl, that circled\par
high among them, or touched the swelling sail of a vessel, which was\par
seen labouring in the storm. Blanche, for some time, anxiously watched\par
the progress of the bark, as it threw the waves in foam around it, and,\par
as the lightnings flashed, looked to the opening heavens, with many a\par
sigh for the fate of the poor mariners.\par
\par
The sun, at length, set, and the heavy clouds, which had long impended,\par
dropped over the splendour of his course; the vessel, however, was yet\par
dimly seen, and Blanche continued to observe it, till the quick\par
succession of flashes, lighting up the gloom of the whole horizon,\par
warned her to retire from the window, and she joined the Abbess, who,\par
having exhausted all her topics of conversation with the Countess, had\par
now leisure to notice her.\par
\par
But their discourse was interrupted by tremendous peals of thunder; and\par
the bell of the monastery soon after ringing out, summoned the\par
inhabitants to prayer. As Blanche passed the window, she gave another\par
look to the ocean, where, by the momentary flash, that illumined the\par
vast body of the waters, she distinguished the vessel she had observed\par
before, amidst a sea of foam, breaking the billows, the mast now bowing\par
to the waves, and then rising high in air.\par
\par
She sighed fervently as she gazed, and then followed the Lady Abbess\par
and the Countess to the chapel. Meanwhile, some of the Count’s\par
servants, having gone by land to the château for carriages, returned\par
soon after vespers had concluded, when, the storm being somewhat\par
abated, the Count and his family returned home. Blanche was surprised\par
to discover how much the windings of the shore had deceived her,\par
concerning the distance of the château from the monastery, whose vesper\par
bell she had heard, on the preceding evening, from the windows of the\par
west saloon, and whose towers she would also have seen from thence, had\par
not twilight veiled them.\par
\par
On their arrival at the château, the Countess, affecting more fatigue,\par
than she really felt, withdrew to her apartment, and the Count, with\par
his daughter and Henri, went to the supper-room, where they had not\par
been long, when they heard, in a pause of the gust, a firing of guns,\par
which the Count understanding to be signals of distress from some\par
vessel in the storm, went to a window, that opened towards the\par
Mediterranean, to observe further; but the sea was now involved in\par
utter darkness, and the loud howlings of the tempest had again overcome\par
every other sound. Blanche, remembering the bark, which she had before\par
seen, now joined her father, with trembling anxiety. In a few moments,\par
the report of guns was again borne along the wind, and as suddenly\par
wafted away; a tremendous burst of thunder followed, and, in the flash,\par
that had preceded it, and which seemed to quiver over the whole surface\par
of the waters, a vessel was discovered, tossing amidst the white foam\par
of the waves at some distance from the shore. Impenetrable darkness\par
again involved the scene, but soon a second flash showed the bark, with\par
one sail unfurled, driving towards the coast. Blanche hung upon her\par
father’s arm, with looks full of the agony of united terror and pity,\par
which were unnecessary to awaken the heart of the Count, who gazed upon\par
the sea with a piteous expression, and, perceiving, that no boat could\par
live in the storm, forbore to send one; but he gave orders to his\par
people to carry torches out upon the cliffs, hoping they might prove a\par
kind of beacon to the vessel, or, at least, warn the crew of the rocks\par
they were approaching. While Henri went out to direct on what part of\par
the cliffs the lights should appear, Blanche remained with her father,\par
at the window, catching, every now and then, as the lightnings flashed,\par
a glimpse of the vessel; and she soon saw, with reviving hope, the\par
torches flaming on the blackness of night, and, as they waved over the\par
cliffs, casting a red gleam on the gasping billows. When the firing of\par
guns was repeated, the torches were tossed high in the air, as if\par
answering the signal, and the firing was then redoubled; but, though\par
the wind bore the sound away, she fancied, as the lightnings glanced,\par
that the vessel was much nearer the shore.\par
\par
The Count’s servants were now seen, running to and fro, on the rocks;\par
some venturing almost to the point of the crags, and bending over, held\par
out their torches fastened to long poles; while others, whose steps\par
could be traced only by the course of the lights, descended the steep\par
and dangerous path, that wound to the margin of the sea, and, with loud\par
halloos, hailed the mariners, whose shrill whistle, and then feeble\par
voices, were heard, at intervals, mingling with the storm. Sudden\par
shouts from the people on the rocks increased the anxiety of Blanche to\par
an almost intolerable degree: but her suspense, concerning the fate of\par
the mariners, was soon over, when Henri, running breathless into the\par
room, told that the vessel was anchored in the bay below, but in so\par
shattered a condition, that it was feared she would part before the\par
crew could disembark. The Count immediately gave orders for his own\par
boats to assist in bringing them to shore, and that such of these\par
unfortunate strangers as could not be accommodated in the adjacent\par
hamlet should be entertained at the château. Among the latter, were\par
Emily St. Aubert, Monsieur Du Pont, Ludovico and Annette, who, having\par
embarked at Leghorn and reached Marseilles, were from thence crossing\par
the Gulf of Lyons, when this storm overtook them. They were received by\par
the Count with his usual benignity, who, though Emily wished to have\par
proceeded immediately to the monastery of St. Claire, would not allow\par
her to leave the château, that night; and, indeed, the terror and\par
fatigue she had suffered would scarcely have permitted her to go\par
farther.\par
\par
In Monsieur Du Pont the Count discovered an old acquaintance, and much\par
joy and congratulation passed between them, after which Emily was\par
introduced by name to the Count’s family, whose hospitable benevolence\par
dissipated the little embarrassment, which her situation had occasioned\par
her, and the party were soon seated at the supper-table. The unaffected\par
kindness of Blanche and the lively joy she expressed on the escape of\par
the strangers, for whom her pity had been so much interested, gradually\par
revived Emily’s languid spirits; and Du Pont, relieved from his terrors\par
for her and for himself, felt the full contrast, between his late\par
situation on a dark and tremendous ocean, and his present one, in a\par
cheerful mansion, where he was surrounded with plenty, elegance and\par
smiles of welcome.\par
\par
Annette, meanwhile, in the servants’ hall, was telling of all the\par
dangers she had encountered, and congratulating herself so heartily\par
upon her own and Ludovico’s escape, and on her present comforts, that\par
she often made all that part of the château ring with merriment and\par
laughter. Ludovico’s spirits were as gay as her own, but he had\par
discretion enough to restrain them, and tried to check hers, though in\par
vain, till her laughter, at length, ascended to _my Lady’s_ chamber,\par
who sent to enquire what occasioned so much uproar in the château, and\par
to command silence.\par
\par
Emily withdrew early to seek the repose she so much required, but her\par
pillow was long a sleepless one. On this her return to her native\par
country, many interesting remembrances were awakened; all the events\par
and sufferings she had experienced, since she quitted it, came in long\par
succession to her fancy, and were chased only by the image of\par
Valancourt, with whom to believe herself once more in the same land,\par
after they had been so long, and so distantly separated, gave her\par
emotions of indescribable joy, but which afterwards yielded to anxiety\par
and apprehension, when she considered the long period, that had\par
elapsed, since any letter had passed between them, and how much might\par
have happened in this interval to affect her future peace. But the\par
thought that Valancourt might be now no more, or, if living, might have\par
forgotten her, was so very terrible to her heart, that she would\par
scarcely suffer herself to pause upon the possibility. She determined\par
to inform him, on the following day, of her arrival in France, which it\par
was scarcely possible he could know but by a letter from herself, and,\par
after soothing her spirits with the hope of soon hearing, that he was\par
well, and unchanged in his affections, she, at length, sunk to repose.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XII\par
\par
Oft woo’d the gleam of Cynthia, silver-bright,\par
In cloisters dim, far from the haunts of Folly,\par
With Freedom by my side, and soft-ey’d Melancholy.\par
                    GRAY\par
\par
\par
The Lady Blanche was so much interested for Emily, that, upon hearing\par
she was going to reside in the neighbouring convent, she requested the\par
Count would invite her to lengthen her stay at the château. “And you\par
know, my dear sir,” added Blanche, “how delighted I shall be with such\par
a companion; for, at present, I have no friend to walk, or to read\par
with, since Mademoiselle Bearn is my mamma’s friend only.”\par
\par
The Count smiled at the youthful simplicity, with which his daughter\par
yielded to first impressions; and, though he chose to warn her of their\par
danger, he silently applauded the benevolence, that could thus readily\par
expand in confidence to a stranger. He had observed Emily, with\par
attention, on the preceding evening, and was as much pleased with her,\par
as it was possible he could be with any person, on so short an\par
acquaintance. The mention, made of her by Mons. Du Pont, had also given\par
him a favourable impression of Emily; but, extremely cautious as to\par
those, whom he introduced to the intimacy of his daughter, he\par
determined, on hearing that the former was no stranger at the convent\par
of St. Claire, to visit the abbess, and, if her account corresponded\par
with his wish, to invite Emily to pass some time at the château. On\par
this subject, he was influenced by a consideration of the Lady\par
Blanche’s welfare, still more than by either a wish to oblige her, or\par
to befriend the orphan Emily, for whom, however, he felt considerably\par
interested.\par
\par
On the following morning, Emily was too much fatigued to appear; but\par
Mons. Du Pont was at the breakfast-table, when the Count entered the\par
room, who pressed him, as his former acquaintance, and the son of a\par
very old friend, to prolong his stay at the château; an invitation,\par
which Du Pont willingly accepted, since it would allow him to be near\par
Emily; and, though he was not conscious of encouraging a hope, that she\par
would ever return his affection, he had not fortitude enough to\par
attempt, at present, to overcome it.\par
\par
Emily, when she was somewhat recovered, wandered with her new friend\par
over the grounds belonging to the château, as much delighted with the\par
surrounding views, as Blanche, in the benevolence of her heart, had\par
wished; from thence she perceived, beyond the woods, the towers of the\par
monastery, and remarked, that it was to this convent she designed to\par
go.\par
\par
“Ah!” said Blanche with surprise, “I am but just released from a\par
convent, and would you go into one? If you could know what pleasure I\par
feel in wandering here, at liberty,—and in seeing the sky and the\par
fields, and the woods all round me, I think you would not.” Emily,\par
smiling at the warmth, with which the Lady Blanche spoke, observed,\par
that she did not mean to confine herself to a convent for life.\par
\par
“No, you may not intend it now,” said Blanche; “but you do not know to\par
what the nuns may persuade you to consent: I know how kind they will\par
appear, and how happy, for I have seen too much of their art.”\par
\par
When they returned to the château, Lady Blanche conducted Emily to her\par
favourite turret, and from thence they rambled through the ancient\par
chambers, which Blanche had visited before. Emily was amused by\par
observing the structure of these apartments, and the fashion of their\par
old but still magnificent furniture, and by comparing them with those\par
of the castle of Udolpho, which were yet more antique and grotesque.\par
She was also interested by Dorothée the housekeeper, who attended them,\par
whose appearance was almost as antique as the objects around her, and\par
who seemed no less interested by Emily, on whom she frequently gazed\par
with so much deep attention, as scarcely to hear what was said to her.\par
\par
While Emily looked from one of the casements, she perceived, with\par
surprise, some objects, that were familiar to her memory;—the fields\par
and woods, with the gleaming brook, which she had passed with La\par
Voisin, one evening, soon after the death of Monsieur St. Aubert, in\par
her way from the monastery to her cottage; and she now knew this to be\par
the château, which he had then avoided, and concerning which he had\par
dropped some remarkable hints.\par
\par
Shocked by this discovery, yet scarcely knowing why, she mused for some\par
time in silence, and remembered the emotion, which her father had\par
betrayed on finding himself so near this mansion, and some other\par
circumstances of his conduct, that now greatly interested her. The\par
music, too, which she had formerly heard, and, respecting which La\par
Voisin had given such an odd account, occurred to her, and, desirous of\par
knowing more concerning it, she asked Dorothée whether it returned at\par
midnight, as usual, and whether the musician had yet been discovered.\par
\par
“Yes, ma’amselle,” replied Dorothée, “that music is still heard, but\par
the musician has never been found out, nor ever will, I believe; though\par
there are some people, who can guess.”\par
\par
“Indeed!” said Emily, “then why do they not pursue the enquiry?”\par
\par
“Ah, young lady! enquiry enough has been made—but who can pursue a\par
spirit?”\par
\par
Emily smiled, and, remembering how lately she had suffered herself to\par
be led away by superstition, determined now to resist its contagion;\par
yet, in spite of her efforts, she felt awe mingle with her curiosity,\par
on this subject; and Blanche, who had hitherto listened in silence, now\par
enquired what this music was, and how long it had been heard.\par
\par
“Ever since the death of my lady, madam,” replied Dorothée.\par
\par
“Why, the place is not haunted, surely?” said Blanche, between jesting\par
and seriousness.\par
\par
“I have heard that music almost ever since my dear lady died,”\par
continued Dorothée, “and never before then. But that is nothing to some\par
things I could tell of.”\par
\par
“Do, pray, tell them, then,” said Lady Blanche, now more in earnest\par
than in jest. “I am much interested, for I have heard sister Henriette,\par
and sister Sophie, in the convent, tell of such strange appearances,\par
which they themselves had witnessed!”\par
\par
“You never heard, my lady, I suppose, what made us leave the château,\par
and go and live in a cottage,” said Dorothée. “Never!” replied Blanche\par
with impatience.\par
\par
“Nor the reason, that my lord, the Marquis”—Dorothée checked herself,\par
hesitated, and then endeavoured to change the topic; but the curiosity\par
of Blanche was too much awakened to suffer the subject thus easily to\par
escape her, and she pressed the old housekeeper to proceed with her\par
account, upon whom, however, no entreaties could prevail; and it was\par
evident, that she was alarmed for the imprudence, into which she had\par
already betrayed herself.\par
\par
“I perceive,” said Emily, smiling, “that all old mansions are haunted;\par
I am lately come from a place of wonders; but unluckily, since I left\par
it, I have heard almost all of them explained.”\par
\par
Blanche was silent; Dorothée looked grave, and sighed; and Emily felt\par
herself still inclined to believe more of the wonderful, than she chose\par
to acknowledge. Just then, she remembered the spectacle she had\par
witnessed in a chamber of Udolpho, and, by an odd kind of coincidence,\par
the alarming words, that had accidentally met her eye in the MS.\par
papers, which she had destroyed, in obedience to the command of her\par
father; and she shuddered at the meaning they seemed to impart, almost\par
as much as at the horrible appearance, disclosed by the black veil.\par
\par
The Lady Blanche, meanwhile, unable to prevail with Dorothée to explain\par
the subject of her late hints, had desired, on reaching the door, that\par
terminated the gallery, and which she found fastened on the preceding\par
day, to see the suite of rooms beyond. “Dear young lady,” said the\par
housekeeper, “I have told you my reason for not opening them; I have\par
never seen them, since my dear lady died; and it would go hard with me\par
to see them now. Pray, madam, do not ask me again.”\par
\par
“Certainly I will not,” replied Blanche, “if that is really your\par
objection.”\par
\par
“Alas! it is,” said the old woman: “we all loved her well, and I shall\par
always grieve for her. Time runs round! it is now many years, since she\par
died; but I remember everything, that happened then, as if it was but\par
yesterday. Many things, that have passed of late years, are gone quite\par
from my memory, while those so long ago, I can see as if in a glass.”\par
She paused, but afterwards, as they walked up the gallery, added to\par
Emily, “this young lady sometimes brings the late Marchioness to my\par
mind; I can remember, when she looked just as blooming, and very like\par
her, when she smiles. Poor lady! how gay she was, when she first came\par
to the château!”\par
\par
“And was she not gay, afterwards?” said Blanche.\par
\par
Dorothée shook her head; and Emily observed her, with eyes strongly\par
expressive of the interest she now felt. “Let us sit down in this\par
window,” said the Lady Blanche, on reaching the opposite end of the\par
gallery: “and pray, Dorothée, if it is not painful to you, tell us\par
something more about the Marchioness. I should like to look into the\par
glass you spoke of just now, and see a few of the circumstances, which\par
you say often pass over it.”\par
\par
“No, my lady,” replied Dorothée; “if you knew as much as I do, you\par
would not, for you would find there a dismal train of them; I often\par
wish I could shut them out, but they will rise to my mind. I see my\par
dear lady on her death-bed,—her very look,—and remember all she said—it\par
was a terrible scene!”\par
\par
“Why was it so terrible?” said Emily with emotion.\par
\par
“Ah, dear young lady! is not death always terrible?” replied Dorothée.\par
\par
To some further enquiries of Blanche Dorothée was silent; and Emily,\par
observing the tears in her eyes, forbore to urge the subject, and\par
endeavoured to withdraw the attention of her young friend to some\par
object in the gardens, where the Count, with the Countess and Monsieur\par
Du Pont, appearing, they went down to join them.\par
\par
When he perceived Emily, he advanced to meet her, and presented her to\par
the Countess, in a manner so benign, that it recalled most powerfully\par
to her mind the idea of her late father, and she felt more gratitude to\par
him, than embarrassment towards the Countess, who, however, received\par
her with one of those fascinating smiles, which her caprice sometimes\par
allowed her to assume, and which was now the result of a conversation\par
the Count had held with her, concerning Emily. Whatever this might be,\par
or whatever had passed in his conversation with the lady abbess, whom\par
he had just visited, esteem and kindness were strongly apparent in his\par
manner, when he addressed Emily, who experienced that sweet emotion,\par
which arises from the consciousness of possessing the approbation of\par
the good; for to the Count’s worth she had been inclined to yield her\par
confidence almost from the first moment, in which she had seen him.\par
\par
Before she could finish her acknowledgments for the hospitality she had\par
received, and mention of her design of going immediately to the\par
convent, she was interrupted by an invitation to lengthen her stay at\par
the château, which was pressed by the Count and the Countess, with an\par
appearance of such friendly sincerity, that, though she much wished to\par
see her old friends at the monastery, and to sigh, once more, over her\par
father’s grave, she consented to remain a few days at the château.\par
\par
To the abbess, however, she immediately wrote, mentioning her arrival\par
in Languedoc and her wish to be received into the convent, as a\par
boarder; she also sent letters to Monsieur Quesnel and to Valancourt,\par
whom she merely informed of her arrival in France; and, as she knew not\par
where the latter might be stationed, she directed her letter to his\par
brother’s seat in Gascony.\par
\par
In the evening, Lady Blanche and Mons. Du Pont walked with Emily to the\par
cottage of La Voisin, which she had now a melancholy pleasure in\par
approaching, for time had softened her grief for the loss of St.\par
Aubert, though it could not annihilate it, and she felt a soothing\par
sadness in indulging the recollections, which this scene recalled. La\par
Voisin was still living, and seemed to enjoy, as much as formerly, the\par
tranquil evening of a blameless life. He was sitting at the door of his\par
cottage, watching some of his grandchildren, playing on the grass\par
before him, and, now and then, with a laugh, or a commendation,\par
encouraging their sports. He immediately recollected Emily, whom he was\par
much pleased to see, and she was as rejoiced to hear, that he had not\par
lost one of his family, since her departure.\par
\par
“Yes, ma’amselle,” said the old man, “we all live merrily together\par
still, thank God! and I believe there is not a happier family to be\par
found in Languedoc, than ours.”\par
\par
Emily did not trust herself in the chamber, where St. Aubert died; and,\par
after half an hour’s conversation with La Voisin and his family, she\par
left the cottage.\par
\par
During these the first days of her stay at Château-le-Blanc, she was\par
often affected, by observing the deep, but silent melancholy, which, at\par
times, stole over Du Pont; and Emily, pitying the self-delusion, which\par
disarmed him of the will to depart, determined to withdraw herself as\par
soon as the respect she owed the Count and Countess De Villefort would\par
permit. The dejection of his friend soon alarmed the anxiety of the\par
Count, to whom Du Pont, at length, confided the secret of his hopeless\par
affection, which, however, the former could only commiserate, though he\par
secretly determined to befriend his suit, if an opportunity of doing so\par
should ever occur. Considering the dangerous situation of Du Pont, he\par
but feebly opposed his intention of leaving Château-le-Blanc, on the\par
following day, but drew from him a promise of a longer visit, when he\par
could return with safety to his peace. Emily herself, though she could\par
not encourage his affection, esteemed him both for the many virtues he\par
possessed, and for the services she had received from him; and it was\par
not without tender emotions of gratitude and pity, that she now saw him\par
depart for his family seat in Gascony; while he took leave of her with\par
a countenance so expressive of love and grief, as to interest the Count\par
more warmly in his cause than before.\par
\par
In a few days, Emily also left the château, but not before the Count\par
and Countess had received her promise to repeat her visit very soon;\par
and she was welcomed by the abbess, with the same maternal kindness she\par
had formerly experienced, and by the nuns, with much expression of\par
regard. The well-known scenes of the convent occasioned her many\par
melancholy recollections, but with these were mingled others, that\par
inspired gratitude for having escaped the various dangers, that had\par
pursued her, since she quitted it, and for the good, which she yet\par
possessed; and, though she once more wept over her father’s grave, with\par
tears of tender affection, her grief was softened from its former\par
acuteness.\par
\par
Some time after her return to the monastery, she received a letter from\par
her uncle, Mons. Quesnel, in answer to information that she had arrived\par
in France, and to her enquiries, concerning such of her affairs as he\par
had undertaken to conduct during her absence, especially as to the\par
period for which La Vallée had been let, whither it was her wish to\par
return, if it should appear, that her income would permit her to do so.\par
The reply of Mons. Quesnel was cold and formal, as she expected,\par
expressing neither concern for the evils she suffered, nor pleasure,\par
that she was now removed from them; nor did he allow the opportunity to\par
pass, of reproving her for her rejection of Count Morano, whom he\par
affected still to believe a man of honour and fortune; nor of\par
vehemently declaiming against Montoni, to whom he had always, till now,\par
felt himself to be inferior. On Emily’s pecuniary concerns, he was not\par
very explicit; he informed her, however, that the term, for which La\par
Vallée had been engaged, was nearly expired; but, without inviting her\par
to his own house, added, that her circumstances would by no means allow\par
her to reside there, and earnestly advised her to remain, for the\par
present, in the convent of St. Claire.\par
\par
To her enquiries respecting poor old Theresa, her late father’s\par
servant, he gave no answer. In the postscript to his letter, Monsieur\par
Quesnel mentioned M. Motteville, in whose hands the late St. Aubert had\par
placed the chief of his personal property, as being likely to arrange\par
his affairs nearly to the satisfaction of his creditors, and that Emily\par
would recover much more of her fortune, than she had formerly reason to\par
expect. The letter also inclosed to Emily an order upon a merchant at\par
Narbonne, for a small sum of money.\par
\par
The tranquillity of the monastery, and the liberty she was suffered to\par
enjoy, in wandering among the woods and shores of this delightful\par
province, gradually restored her spirits to their natural tone, except\par
that anxiety would sometimes intrude, concerning Valancourt, as the\par
time approached, when it was possible that she might receive an answer\par
to her letter.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XIII\par
\par
As when a wave, that from a cloud impends,\par
And, swell’d with tempests, on the ship descends,\par
White are the decks with foam; the winds aloud,\par
Howl o’er the masts, and sing through ev’ry shroud:\par
Pale, trembling, tir’d, the sailors freeze with fears,\par
And instant death on ev’ry wave appears.\par
                    POPE’S HOMER\par
\par
\par
The Lady Blanche, meanwhile, who was left much alone, became impatient\par
for the company of her new friend, whom she wished to observe sharing\par
in the delight she received from the beautiful scenery around. She had\par
now no person, to whom she could express her admiration and communicate\par
her pleasures, no eye, that sparkled to her smile, or countenance, that\par
reflected her happiness; and she became spiritless and pensive. The\par
Count, observing her dissatisfaction, readily yielded to her\par
entreaties, and reminded Emily of her promised visit; but the silence\par
of Valancourt, which was now prolonged far beyond the period, when a\par
letter might have arrived from Estuvière, oppressed Emily with severe\par
anxiety, and, rendering her averse to society, she would willingly have\par
deferred her acceptance of this invitation, till her spirits should be\par
relieved. The Count and his family, however, pressed to see her; and,\par
as the circumstances, that prompted her wish for solitude, could not be\par
explained, there was an appearance of caprice in her refusal, which she\par
could not persevere in, without offending the friends, whose esteem she\par
valued. At length, therefore, she returned upon a second visit to\par
Château-le-Blanc. Here the friendly manner of Count De Villefort\par
encouraged Emily to mention to him her situation, respecting the\par
estates of her late aunt, and to consult him on the means of recovering\par
them. He had little doubt, that the law would decide in her favour,\par
and, advising her to apply to it, offered first to write to an advocate\par
at Avignon, on whose opinion he thought he could rely. His kindness was\par
gratefully accepted by Emily, who, soothed by the courtesy she daily\par
experienced, would have been once more happy, could she have been\par
assured of Valancourt’s welfare and unaltered affection. She had now\par
been above a week at the château, without receiving intelligence of\par
him, and, though she knew, that, if he was absent from his brother’s\par
residence, it was scarcely probable her letter had yet reached him, she\par
could not forbear to admit doubts and fears, that destroyed her peace.\par
Again she would consider of all, that might have happened in the long\par
period, since her first seclusion at Udolpho, and her mind was\par
sometimes so overwhelmed with an apprehension, that Valancourt was no\par
more, or that he lived no longer for her, that the company even of\par
Blanche became intolerably oppressive, and she would sit alone in her\par
apartment for hours together, when the engagements of the family\par
allowed her to do so, without incivility.\par
\par
In one of these solitary hours, she unlocked a little box, which\par
contained some letters of Valancourt, with some drawings she had\par
sketched, during her stay in Tuscany, the latter of which were no\par
longer interesting to her; but, in the letters, she now, with\par
melancholy indulgence, meant to retrace the tenderness, that had so\par
often soothed her, and rendered her, for a moment, insensible of the\par
distance, which separated her from the writer. But their effect was now\par
changed; the affection they expressed appealed so forcibly to her\par
heart, when she considered that it had, perhaps, yielded to the powers\par
of time and absence, and even the view of the hand-writing recalled so\par
many painful recollections, that she found herself unable to go through\par
the first she had opened, and sat musing, with her cheek resting on her\par
arm, and tears stealing from her eyes, when old Dorothée entered the\par
room to inform her, that dinner would be ready, an hour before the\par
usual time. Emily started on perceiving her, and hastily put up the\par
papers, but not before Dorothée had observed both her agitation and her\par
tears.\par
\par
“Ah, ma’amselle!” said she, “you, who are so young,—have you reason for\par
sorrow?”\par
\par
Emily tried to smile, but was unable to speak.\par
\par
“Alas! dear young lady, when you come to my age, you will not weep at\par
trifles; and surely you have nothing serious, to grieve you.”\par
\par
“No, Dorothée, nothing of any consequence,” replied Emily. Dorothée,\par
now stooping to pick up something, that had dropped from among the\par
papers, suddenly exclaimed, “Holy Mary! what is it I see?” and then,\par
trembling, sat down in a chair, that stood by the table.\par
\par
“What is it you do see?” said Emily, alarmed by her manner, and looking\par
round the room.\par
\par
“It is herself,” said Dorothée, “her very self! just as she looked a\par
little before she died!”\par
\par
Emily, still more alarmed, began now to fear, that Dorothée was seized\par
with sudden frenzy, but entreated her to explain herself.\par
\par
“That picture!” said she, “where did you find it, lady? it is my\par
blessed mistress herself!”\par
\par
She laid on the table the miniature, which Emily had long ago found\par
among the papers her father had enjoined her to destroy, and over which\par
she had once seen him shed such tender and affecting tears; and,\par
recollecting all the various circumstances of his conduct, that had\par
long perplexed her, her emotions increased to an excess, which deprived\par
her of all power to ask the questions she trembled to have answered,\par
and she could only enquire, whether Dorothée was certain the picture\par
resembled the late marchioness.\par
\par
“O, ma’amselle!” said she, “how came it to strike me so, the instant I\par
saw it, if it was not my lady’s likeness? Ah!” added she, taking up the\par
miniature, “these are her own blue eyes—looking so sweet and so mild;\par
and there is her very look, such as I have often seen it, when she had\par
sat thinking for a long while, and then, the tears would often steal\par
down her cheeks—but she never would complain! It was that look so meek,\par
as it were, and resigned, that used to break my heart and make me love\par
her so!”\par
\par
“Dorothée!” said Emily solemnly, “I am interested in the cause of that\par
grief, more so, perhaps, than you may imagine; and I entreat, that you\par
will no longer refuse to indulge my curiosity;—it is not a common one.”\par
\par
As Emily said this, she remembered the papers, with which the picture\par
had been found, and had scarcely a doubt, that they had concerned the\par
Marchioness de Villeroi; but with this supposition came a scruple,\par
whether she ought to enquire further on a subject, which might prove to\par
be the same, that her father had so carefully endeavoured to conceal.\par
Her curiosity, concerning the Marchioness, powerful as it was, it is\par
probable she would now have resisted, as she had formerly done, on\par
unwarily observing the few terrible words in the papers, which had\par
never since been erased from her memory, had she been certain that the\par
history of that lady was the subject of those papers, or, that such\par
simple particulars only as it was probable Dorothée could relate were\par
included in her father’s command. What was known to her could be no\par
secret to many other persons; and, since it appeared very unlikely,\par
that St. Aubert should attempt to conceal what Emily might learn by\par
ordinary means, she at length concluded, that, if the papers had\par
related to the story of the Marchioness, it was not those circumstances\par
of it, which Dorothée could disclose, that he had thought sufficiently\par
important to wish to have concealed. She, therefore, no longer\par
hesitated to make the enquiries, that might lead to the gratification\par
of her curiosity.\par
\par
“Ah, ma’amselle!” said Dorothée, “it is a sad story, and cannot be told\par
now: but what am I saying? I never will tell it. Many years have\par
passed, since it happened; and I never loved to talk of the Marchioness\par
to anybody, but my husband. He lived in the family, at that time, as\par
well as myself, and he knew many particulars from me, which nobody else\par
did; for I was about the person of my lady in her last illness, and saw\par
and heard as much, or more than my lord himself. Sweet saint! how\par
patient she was! When she died, I thought I could have died with her!”\par
\par
“Dorothée,” said Emily, interrupting her, “what you shall tell, you may\par
depend upon it, shall never be disclosed by me. I have, I repeat it,\par
particular reasons for wishing to be informed on this subject, and am\par
willing to bind myself, in the most solemn manner, never to mention\par
what you shall wish me to conceal.”\par
\par
Dorothée seemed surprised at the earnestness of Emily’s manner, and,\par
after regarding her for some moments, in silence, said, “Young lady!\par
that look of yours pleads for you—it is so like my dear mistress’s,\par
that I can almost fancy I see her before me; if you were her daughter,\par
you could not remind me of her more. But dinner will be ready—had you\par
not better go down?”\par
\par
“You will first promise to grant my request,” said Emily.\par
\par
“And ought not you first to tell me, ma’amselle, how this picture fell\par
into your hands, and the reasons you say you have for curiosity about\par
my lady?”\par
\par
“Why, no, Dorothée,” replied Emily, recollecting herself, “I have also\par
particular reasons for observing silence, on these subjects, at least,\par
till I know further; and, remember, I do not promise ever to speak upon\par
them; therefore, do not let me induce you to satisfy my curiosity, from\par
an expectation, that I shall gratify yours. What I may judge proper to\par
conceal, does not concern myself alone, or I should have less scruple\par
in revealing it: let a confidence in my honour alone persuade you to\par
disclose what I request.”\par
\par
“Well, lady!” replied Dorothée, after a long pause, during which her\par
eyes were fixed upon Emily, “you seem so much interested,—and this\par
picture and that face of yours make me think you have some reason to be\par
so,—that I will trust you—and tell some things, that I never told\par
before to anybody, but my husband, though there are people, who have\par
suspected as much. I will tell you the particulars of my lady’s death,\par
too, and some of my own suspicions; but you must first promise me by\par
all the saints—”\par
\par
Emily, interrupting her, solemnly promised never to reveal what should\par
be confided to her, without Dorothée’s consent.\par
\par
“But there is the horn, ma’amselle, sounding for dinner,” said\par
Dorothée; “I must be gone.”\par
\par
“When shall I see you again?” enquired Emily.\par
\par
Dorothée mused, and then replied, “Why, madam, it may make people\par
curious, if it is known I am so much in your apartment, and that I\par
should be sorry for; so I will come when I am least likely to be\par
observed. I have little leisure in the day, and I shall have a good\par
deal to say; so, if you please, ma’am, I will come, when the family are\par
all in bed.”\par
\par
“That will suit me very well,” replied Emily: “Remember, then,\par
tonight—”\par
\par
“Aye, that is well remembered,” said Dorothée, “I fear I cannot come\par
tonight, madam, for there will be the dance of the vintage, and it will\par
be late, before the servants go to rest; for, when they once set in to\par
dance, they will keep it up, in the cool of the air, till morning; at\par
least, it used to be so in my time.”\par
\par
“Ah! is it the dance of the vintage?” said Emily, with a deep sigh,\par
remembering, that it was on the evening of this festival, in the\par
preceding year, that St. Aubert and herself had arrived in the\par
neighbourhood of Château-le-Blanc. She paused a moment, overcome by the\par
sudden recollection, and then, recovering herself, added—“But this\par
dance is in the open woods; you, therefore, will not be wanted, and can\par
easily come to me.”\par
\par
Dorothée replied, that she had been accustomed to be present at the\par
dance of the vintage, and she did not wish to be absent now; “but if I\par
can get away, madam, I will,” said she.\par
\par
Emily then hastened to the dining-room, where the Count conducted\par
himself with the courtesy, which is inseparable from true dignity, and\par
of which the Countess frequently practised little, though her manner to\par
Emily was an exception to her usual habit. But, if she retained few of\par
the ornamental virtues, she cherished other qualities, which she seemed\par
to consider invaluable. She had dismissed the grace of modesty, but\par
then she knew perfectly well how to manage the stare of assurance; her\par
manners had little of the tempered sweetness, which is necessary to\par
render the female character interesting, but she could occasionally\par
throw into them an affectation of spirits, which seemed to triumph over\par
every person, who approached her. In the country, however, she\par
generally affected an elegant languor, that persuaded her almost to\par
faint, when her favourite read to her a story of fictitious sorrow; but\par
her countenance suffered no change, when living objects of distress\par
solicited her charity, and her heart beat with no transport to the\par
thought of giving them instant relief;—she was a stranger to the\par
highest luxury, of which, perhaps, the human mind can be sensible, for\par
her benevolence had never yet called smiles upon the face of misery.\par
\par
In the evening, the Count, with all his family, except the Countess and\par
Mademoiselle Bearn, went to the woods to witness the festivity of the\par
peasants. The scene was in a glade, where the trees, opening, formed a\par
circle round the turf they highly overshadowed; between their branches,\par
vines, loaded with ripe clusters, were hung in gay festoons; and,\par
beneath, were tables, with fruit, wine, cheese and other rural\par
fare,—and seats for the Count and his family. At a little distance,\par
were benches for the elder peasants, few of whom, however, could\par
forbear to join the jocund dance, which began soon after sunset, when\par
several of sixty tripped it with almost as much glee and airy\par
lightness, as those of sixteen.\par
\par
The musicians, who sat carelessly on the grass, at the foot of a tree,\par
seemed inspired by the sound of their own instruments, which were\par
chiefly flutes and a kind of long guitar. Behind, stood a boy,\par
flourishing a tamborine, and dancing a solo, except that, as he\par
sometimes gaily tossed the instrument, he tripped among the other\par
dancers, when his antic gestures called forth a broader laugh, and\par
heightened the rustic spirit of the scene.\par
\par
The Count was highly delighted with the happiness he witnessed, to\par
which his bounty had largely contributed, and the Lady Blanche joined\par
the dance with a young gentleman of her father’s party. Du Pont\par
requested Emily’s hand, but her spirits were too much depressed, to\par
permit her to engage in the present festivity, which called to her\par
remembrance that of the preceding year, when St. Aubert was living, and\par
of the melancholy scenes, which had immediately followed it.\par
\par
Overcome by these recollections, she, at length, left the spot, and\par
walked slowly into the woods, where the softened music, floating at a\par
distance, soothed her melancholy mind. The moon threw a mellow light\par
among the foliage; the air was balmy and cool, and Emily, lost in\par
thought, strolled on, without observing whither, till she perceived the\par
sounds sinking afar off, and an awful stillness round her, except that,\par
sometimes, the nightingale beguiled the silence with\par
\par
Liquid notes, that close the eye of day.\par
\par
\par
At length, she found herself near the avenue, which, on the night of\par
her father’s arrival, Michael had attempted to pass in search of a\par
house, which was still nearly as wild and desolate as it had then\par
appeared; for the Count had been so much engaged in directing other\par
improvements, that he had neglected to give orders, concerning this\par
extensive approach, and the road was yet broken, and the trees\par
overloaded with their own luxuriance.\par
\par
As she stood surveying it, and remembering the emotions, which she had\par
formerly suffered there, she suddenly recollected the figure, that had\par
been seen stealing among the trees, and which had returned no answer to\par
Michael’s repeated calls; and she experienced somewhat of the fear,\par
that had then assailed her, for it did not appear improbable, that\par
these deep woods were occasionally the haunt of banditti. She,\par
therefore, turned back, and was hastily pursuing her way to the\par
dancers, when she heard steps approaching from the avenue; and, being\par
still beyond the call of the peasants on the green, for she could\par
neither hear their voices, nor their music, she quickened her pace; but\par
the persons following gained fast upon her, and, at length,\par
distinguishing the voice of Henri, she walked leisurely, till he came\par
up. He expressed some surprise at meeting her so far from the company;\par
and, on her saying, that the pleasant moonlight had beguiled her to\par
walk farther than she intended, an exclamation burst from the lips of\par
his companion, and she thought she heard Valancourt speak! It was,\par
indeed, he! and the meeting was such as may be imagined, between\par
persons so affectionate, and so long separated as they had been.\par
\par
In the joy of these moments, Emily forgot all her past sufferings, and\par
Valancourt seemed to have forgotten, that any person but Emily existed;\par
while Henri was a silent and astonished spectator of the scene.\par
\par
Valancourt asked a thousand questions, concerning herself and Montoni,\par
which there was now no time to answer; but she learned, that her letter\par
had been forwarded to him, at Paris, which he had previously quitted,\par
and was returning to Gascony, whither the letter also returned, which,\par
at length, informed him of Emily’s arrival, and on the receipt of which\par
he had immediately set out for Languedoc. On reaching the monastery,\par
whence she had dated her letter, he found, to his extreme\par
disappointment, that the gates were already closed for the night; and\par
believing, that he should not see Emily, till the morrow, he was\par
returning to his little inn, with the intention of writing to her, when\par
he was overtaken by Henri, with whom he had been intimate at Paris, and\par
was led to her, whom he was secretly lamenting that he should not see,\par
till the following day.\par
\par
Emily, with Valancourt and Henri, now returned to the green, where the\par
latter presented Valancourt to the Count, who, she fancied, received\par
him with less than his usual benignity, though it appeared, that they\par
were not strangers to each other. He was invited, however, to partake\par
of the diversions of the evening; and, when he had paid his respects to\par
the Count, and while the dancers continued their festivity, he seated\par
himself by Emily, and conversed, without restraint. The lights, which\par
were hung among the trees, under which they sat, allowed her a more\par
perfect view of the countenance she had so frequently in absence\par
endeavoured to recollect, and she perceived, with some regret, that it\par
was not the same as when last she saw it. There was all its wonted\par
intelligence and fire; but it had lost much of the simplicity, and\par
somewhat of the open benevolence, that used to characterise it. Still,\par
however, it was an interesting countenance; but Emily thought she\par
perceived, at intervals, anxiety contract, and melancholy fix the\par
features of Valancourt; sometimes, too, he fell into a momentary\par
musing, and then appeared anxious to dissipate thought; while, at\par
others, as he fixed his eyes on Emily, a kind of sudden distraction\par
seemed to cross his mind. In her he perceived the same goodness and\par
beautiful simplicity, that had charmed him, on their first\par
acquaintance. The bloom of her countenance was somewhat faded, but all\par
its sweetness remained, and it was rendered more interesting, than\par
ever, by the faint expression of melancholy, that sometimes mingled\par
with her smile.\par
\par
At his request, she related the most important circumstances, that had\par
occurred to her, since she left France, and emotions of pity and\par
indignation alternately prevailed in his mind, when he heard how much\par
she had suffered from the villany of Montoni. More than once, when she\par
was speaking of his conduct, of which the guilt was rather softened,\par
than exaggerated, by her representation, he started from his seat, and\par
walked away, apparently overcome as much by self-accusation as by\par
resentment. Her sufferings alone were mentioned in the few words, which\par
he could address to her, and he listened not to the account, which she\par
was careful to give as distinctly as possible, of the present loss of\par
Madame Montoni’s estates, and of the little reason there was to expect\par
their restoration. At length, Valancourt remained lost in thought, and\par
then some secret cause seemed to overcome him with anguish. Again he\par
abruptly left her. When he returned, she perceived that he had been\par
weeping, and tenderly begged, that he would compose himself. “My\par
sufferings are all passed now,” said she, “for I have escaped from the\par
tyranny of Montoni, and I see you well—let me also see you happy.”\par
\par
Valancourt was more agitated than before. “I am unworthy of you,\par
Emily,” said he, “I am unworthy of you;”—words, by his manner of\par
uttering which Emily was then more shocked than by their import. She\par
fixed on him a mournful and enquiring eye. “Do not look thus on me,”\par
said he, turning away and pressing her hand; “I cannot bear those\par
looks.”\par
\par
“I would ask,” said Emily, in a gentle, but agitated voice, “the\par
meaning of your words; but I perceive, that the question would distress\par
you now. Let us talk on other subjects. Tomorrow, perhaps, you may be\par
more composed. Observe those moonlight woods, and the towers, which\par
appear obscurely in the perspective. You used to be a great admirer of\par
landscape, and I have heard you say, that the faculty of deriving\par
consolation, under misfortune, from the sublime prospects, which\par
neither oppression, nor poverty withhold from us, was the peculiar\par
blessing of the innocent.” Valancourt was deeply affected. “Yes,”\par
replied he, “I had once a taste for innocent and elegant delights—I had\par
once an uncorrupted heart.” Then, checking himself, he added, “Do you\par
remember our journey together in the Pyrenees?”\par
\par
“Can I forget it?” said Emily.—“Would that I could!” he replied;—“that\par
was the happiest period of my life. I then loved, with enthusiasm,\par
whatever was truly great, or good.” It was some time before Emily could\par
repress her tears, and try to command her emotions. “If you wish to\par
forget that journey,” said she, “it must certainly be my wish to forget\par
it also.” She paused, and then added, “You make me very uneasy; but\par
this is not the time for further enquiry;—yet, how can I bear to\par
believe, even for a moment, that you are less worthy of my esteem than\par
formerly? I have still sufficient confidence in your candour, to\par
believe, that, when I shall ask for an explanation, you will give it\par
me.”—“Yes,” said Valancourt, “yes, Emily: I have not yet lost my\par
candour: if I had, I could better have disguised my emotions, on\par
learning what were your sufferings—your virtues, while I—I—but I will\par
say no more. I did not mean to have said even so much—I have been\par
surprised into the self-accusation. Tell me, Emily, that you will not\par
forget that journey—will not wish to forget it, and I will be calm. I\par
would not lose the remembrance of it for the whole earth.”\par
\par
“How contradictory is this!” said Emily;—“but we may be overheard. My\par
recollection of it shall depend upon yours; I will endeavour to forget,\par
or to recollect it, as you may do. Let us join the Count.”—“Tell me\par
first,” said Valancourt, “that you forgive the uneasiness I have\par
occasioned you, this evening, and that you will still love me.”—“I\par
sincerely forgive you,” replied Emily. “You best know whether I shall\par
continue to love you, for you know whether you deserve my esteem. At\par
present, I will believe that you do. It is unnecessary to say,” added\par
she, observing his dejection, “how much pain it would give me to\par
believe otherwise.—The young lady, who approaches, is the Count’s\par
daughter.”\par
\par
Valancourt and Emily now joined the Lady Blanche; and the party, soon\par
after, sat down with the Count, his son, and the Chevalier Du Pont, at\par
a banquet, spread under a gay awning, beneath the trees. At the table\par
also were seated several of the most venerable of the Count’s tenants,\par
and it was a festive repast to all but Valancourt and Emily. When the\par
Count retired to the château, he did not invite Valancourt to accompany\par
him, who, therefore, took leave of Emily, and retired to his solitary\par
inn for the night: meanwhile, she soon withdrew to her own apartment,\par
where she mused, with deep anxiety and concern, on his behaviour, and\par
on the Count’s reception of him. Her attention was thus so wholly\par
engaged, that she forgot Dorothée and her appointment, till morning was\par
far advanced, when, knowing that the good old woman would not come, she\par
retired, for a few hours, to repose.\par
\par
On the following day, when the Count had accidentally joined Emily in\par
one of the walks, they talked of the festival of the preceding evening,\par
and this led him to a mention of Valancourt. “That is a young man of\par
talents,” said he; “you were formerly acquainted with him, I perceive.”\par
Emily said, that she was. “He was introduced to me, at Paris,” said the\par
Count, “and I was much pleased with him, on our first acquaintance.” He\par
paused, and Emily trembled, between the desire of hearing more and the\par
fear of showing the Count, that she felt an interest on the subject.\par
“May I ask,” said he, at length, “how long you have known Monsieur\par
Valancourt?”—“Will you allow me to ask your reason for the question,\par
sir?” said she; “and I will answer it immediately.”—“Certainly,” said\par
the Count, “that is but just. I will tell you my reason. I cannot but\par
perceive, that Monsieur Valancourt admires you; in that, however, there\par
is nothing extraordinary; every person, who sees you, must do the same.\par
I am above using common-place compliments; I speak with sincerity. What\par
I fear, is, that he is a favoured admirer.”—“Why do you fear it, sir?”\par
said Emily, endeavouring to conceal her emotion.—“Because,” replied the\par
Count, “I think him not worthy of your favour.” Emily, greatly\par
agitated, entreated further explanation. “I will give it,” said he, “if\par
you will believe, that nothing but a strong interest in your welfare\par
could induce me to hazard that assertion.”—“I must believe so, sir,”\par
replied Emily.\par
\par
“But let us rest under these trees,” said the Count, observing the\par
paleness of her countenance; “here is a seat—you are fatigued.” They\par
sat down, and the Count proceeded. “Many young ladies, circumstanced as\par
you are, would think my conduct, on this occasion, and on so short an\par
acquaintance, impertinent, instead of friendly; from what I have\par
observed of your temper and understanding, I do not fear such a return\par
from you. Our acquaintance has been short, but long enough to make me\par
esteem you, and feel a lively interest in your happiness. You deserve\par
to be very happy, and I trust that you will be so.” Emily sighed\par
softly, and bowed her thanks. The Count paused again. “I am\par
unpleasantly circumstanced,” said he; “but an opportunity of rendering\par
you important service shall overcome inferior considerations. Will you\par
inform me of the manner of your first acquaintance with the Chevalier\par
Valancourt, if the subject is not too painful?”\par
\par
Emily briefly related the accident of their meeting in the presence of\par
her father, and then so earnestly entreated the Count not to hesitate\par
in declaring what he knew, that he perceived the violent emotion,\par
against which she was contending, and, regarding her with a look of\par
tender compassion, considered how he might communicate his information\par
with least pain to his anxious auditor.\par
\par
“The Chevalier and my son,” said he, “were introduced to each other, at\par
the table of a brother officer, at whose house I also met him, and\par
invited him to my own, whenever he should be disengaged. I did not then\par
know, that he had formed an acquaintance with a set of men, a disgrace\par
to their species, who live by plunder and pass their lives in continual\par
debauchery. I knew several of the Chevalier’s family, resident at\par
Paris, and considered them as sufficient pledges for his introduction\par
to my own. But you are ill; I will leave the subject.”—“No, sir,” said\par
Emily, “I beg you will proceed: I am only distressed.”—“_Only!_” said\par
the Count, with emphasis; “however, I will proceed. I soon learned,\par
that these, his associates, had drawn him into a course of dissipation,\par
from which he appeared to have neither the power, nor the inclination,\par
to extricate himself. He lost large sums at the gaming-table; he became\par
infatuated with play; and was ruined. I spoke tenderly of this to his\par
friends, who assured me, that they had remonstrated with him, till they\par
were weary. I afterwards learned, that, in consideration of his talents\par
for play, which were generally successful, when unopposed by the tricks\par
of villany,—that in consideration of these, the party had initiated him\par
into the secrets of their trade, and allotted him a share of their\par
profits.” “Impossible!” said Emily suddenly; “but—pardon me, sir, I\par
scarcely know what I say; allow for the distress of my mind. I must,\par
indeed, I must believe, that you have not been truly informed. The\par
Chevalier had, doubtless, enemies, who misrepresented him.”—“I should\par
be most happy to believe so,” replied the Count, “but I cannot. Nothing\par
short of conviction, and a regard for your happiness, could have urged\par
me to repeat these unpleasant reports.”\par
\par
Emily was silent. She recollected Valancourt’s sayings, on the\par
preceding evening, which discovered the pangs of self-reproach, and\par
seemed to confirm all that the Count had related. Yet she had not\par
fortitude enough to dare conviction. Her heart was overwhelmed with\par
anguish at the mere suspicion of his guilt, and she could not endure a\par
belief of it. After a silence, the Count said, “I perceive, and can\par
allow for, your want of conviction. It is necessary I should give some\par
proof of what I have asserted; but this I cannot do, without subjecting\par
one, who is very dear to me, to danger.”—“What is the danger you\par
apprehend, sir?” said Emily; “if I can prevent it, you may safely\par
confide in my honour.”—“On your honour I am certain I can rely,” said\par
the Count; “but can I trust your fortitude? Do you think you can resist\par
the solicitation of a favoured admirer, when he pleads, in affliction,\par
for the name of one, who has robbed him of a blessing?”—“I shall not be\par
exposed to such a temptation, sir,” said Emily, with modest pride, “for\par
I cannot favour one, whom I must no longer esteem. I, however, readily\par
give my word.” Tears, in the mean time, contradicted her first\par
assertion; and she felt, that time and effort only could eradicate an\par
affection, which had been formed on virtuous esteem, and cherished by\par
habit and difficulty.\par
\par
“I will trust you then,” said the Count, “for conviction is necessary\par
to your peace, and cannot, I perceive, be obtained, without this\par
confidence. My son has too often been an eye-witness of the Chevalier’s\par
ill conduct; he was very near being drawn in by it; he was, indeed,\par
drawn in to the commission of many follies, but I rescued him from\par
guilt and destruction. Judge then, Mademoiselle St. Aubert, whether a\par
father, who had nearly lost his only son by the example of the\par
Chevalier, has not, from conviction, reason to warn those, whom he\par
esteems, against trusting their happiness in such hands. I have myself\par
seen the Chevalier engaged in deep play with men, whom I almost\par
shuddered to look upon. If you still doubt, I will refer you to my\par
son.”\par
\par
“I must not doubt what you have yourself witnessed,” replied Emily,\par
sinking with grief, “or what you assert. But the Chevalier has,\par
perhaps, been drawn only into a transient folly, which he may never\par
repeat. If you had known the justness of his former principles, you\par
would allow for my present incredulity.”\par
\par
“Alas!” observed the Count, “it is difficult to believe that, which\par
will make us wretched. But I will not sooth you by flattering and false\par
hopes. We all know how fascinating the vice of gaming is, and how\par
difficult it is, also, to conquer habits; the Chevalier might, perhaps,\par
reform for a while, but he would soon relapse into dissipation—for I\par
fear, not only the bonds of habit would be powerful, but that his\par
morals are corrupted. And—why should I conceal from you, that play is\par
not his only vice? he appears to have a taste for every vicious\par
pleasure.”\par
\par
The Count hesitated and paused; while Emily endeavoured to support\par
herself, as, with increasing perturbation, she expected what he might\par
further say. A long pause of silence ensued, during which he was\par
visibly agitated; at length, he said, “It would be a cruel delicacy,\par
that could prevail with me to be silent—and I will inform you, that the\par
Chevalier’s extravagance has brought him twice into the prisons of\par
Paris, from whence he was last extricated, as I was told upon\par
authority, which I cannot doubt, by a well-known Parisian Countess,\par
with whom he continued to reside, when I left Paris.”\par
\par
He paused again; and, looking at Emily, perceived her countenance\par
change, and that she was falling from the seat; he caught her, but she\par
had fainted, and he called loudly for assistance. They were, however,\par
beyond the hearing of his servants at the château, and he feared to\par
leave her while he went thither for assistance, yet knew not how\par
otherwise to obtain it; till a fountain at no great distance caught his\par
eye, and he endeavoured to support Emily against the tree, under which\par
she had been sitting, while he went thither for water. But again he was\par
perplexed, for he had nothing near him, in which water could be\par
brought; but while, with increased anxiety, he watched her, he thought\par
he perceived in her countenance symptoms of returning life.\par
\par
It was long, however, before she revived, and then she found herself\par
supported—not by the Count, but by Valancourt, who was observing her\par
with looks of earnest apprehension, and who now spoke to her in a tone,\par
tremulous with his anxiety. At the sound of his well-known voice, she\par
raised her eyes, but presently closed them, and a faintness again came\par
over her.\par
\par
The Count, with a look somewhat stern, waved him to withdraw; but he\par
only sighed heavily, and called on the name of Emily, as he again held\par
the water, that had been brought, to her lips. On the Count’s repeating\par
his action, and accompanying it with words, Valancourt answered him\par
with a look of deep resentment, and refused to leave the place, till\par
she should revive, or to resign her for a moment to the care of any\par
person. In the next instant, his conscience seemed to inform him of\par
what had been the subject of the Count’s conversation with Emily, and\par
indignation flashed in his eyes; but it was quickly repressed, and\par
succeeded by an expression of serious anguish, that induced the Count\par
to regard him with more pity than resentment, and the view of which so\par
much affected Emily, when she again revived, that she yielded to the\par
weakness of tears. But she soon restrained them, and, exerting her\par
resolution to appear recovered, she rose, thanked the Count and Henri,\par
with whom Valancourt had entered the garden, for their care, and moved\par
towards the château, without noticing Valancourt, who, heart-struck by\par
her manner, exclaimed in a low voice—“Good God! how have I deserved\par
this?—what has been said, to occasion this change?”\par
\par
Emily, without replying, but with increased emotion, quickened her\par
steps. “What has thus disordered you, Emily?” said he, as he still\par
walked by her side: “give me a few moments’ conversation, I entreat\par
you;—I am very miserable!”\par
\par
Though this was spoken in a low voice, it was overheard by the Count,\par
who immediately replied, that Mademoiselle St. Aubert was then too much\par
indisposed, to attend to any conversation, but that he would venture to\par
promise she would see Monsieur Valancourt on the morrow, if she was\par
better.\par
\par
Valancourt’s cheek was crimsoned: he looked haughtily at the Count, and\par
then at Emily, with successive expressions of surprise, grief and\par
supplication, which she could neither misunderstand, nor resist, and\par
she said languidly—“I shall be better tomorrow, and if you wish to\par
accept the Count’s permission, I will see you then.”\par
\par
“See me!” exclaimed Valancourt, as he threw a glance of mingled pride\par
and resentment upon the Count; and then, seeming to recollect himself,\par
he added—“But I will come, madam; I will accept the Count’s\par
_permission_.”\par
\par
When they reached the door of the château, he lingered a moment, for\par
his resentment was now fled; and then, with a look so expressive of\par
tenderness and grief, that Emily’s heart was not proof against it, he\par
bade her good morning, and, bowing slightly to the Count, disappeared.\par
\par
Emily withdrew to her own apartment, under such oppression of heart as\par
she had seldom known, when she endeavoured to recollect all that the\par
Count had told, to examine the probability of the circumstances he\par
himself believed, and to consider of her future conduct towards\par
Valancourt. But, when she attempted to think, her mind refused control,\par
and she could only feel that she was miserable. One moment, she sunk\par
under the conviction, that Valancourt was no longer the same, whom she\par
had so tenderly loved, the idea of whom had hitherto supported her\par
under affliction, and cheered her with the hope of happier days,—but a\par
fallen, a worthless character, whom she must teach herself to\par
despise—if she could not forget. Then, unable to endure this terrible\par
supposition, she rejected it, and disdained to believe him capable of\par
conduct, such as the Count had described, to whom she believed he had\par
been misrepresented by some artful enemy; and there were moments, when\par
she even ventured to doubt the integrity of the Count himself, and to\par
suspect, that he was influenced by some selfish motive, to break her\par
connection with Valancourt. But this was the error of an instant, only;\par
the Count’s character, which she had heard spoken of by Du Pont and\par
many other persons, and had herself observed, enabled her to judge, and\par
forbade the supposition; had her confidence, indeed, been less, there\par
appeared to be no temptation to betray him into conduct so treacherous,\par
and so cruel. Nor did reflection suffer her to preserve the hope, that\par
Valancourt had been mis-represented to the Count, who had said, that he\par
spoke chiefly from his own observation, and from his son’s experience.\par
She must part from Valancourt, therefore, for ever—for what of either\par
happiness or tranquillity could she expect with a man, whose tastes\par
were degenerated into low inclinations, and to whom vice was become\par
habitual? whom she must no longer esteem, though the remembrance of\par
what he once was, and the long habit of loving him, would render it\par
very difficult for her to despise him. “O Valancourt!” she would\par
exclaim, “having been separated so long—do we meet, only to be\par
miserable—only to part for ever?”\par
\par
Amidst all the tumult of her mind, she remembered pertinaciously the\par
seeming candour and simplicity of his conduct, on the preceding night;\par
and, had she dared to trust her own heart, it would have led her to\par
hope much from this. Still she could not resolve to dismiss him for\par
ever, without obtaining further proof of his ill conduct; yet she saw\par
no probability of procuring it, if, indeed, proof more positive was\par
possible. Something, however, it was necessary to decide upon, and she\par
almost determined to be guided in her opinion solely by the manner,\par
with which Valancourt should receive her hints concerning his late\par
conduct.\par
\par
Thus passed the hours till dinner-time, when Emily, struggling against\par
the pressure of her grief, dried her tears, and joined the family at\par
table, where the Count preserved towards her the most delicate\par
attention; but the Countess and Mademoiselle Bearn, having looked, for\par
a moment, with surprise, on her dejected countenance, began, as usual,\par
to talk of trifles, while the eyes of Lady Blanche asked much of her\par
friend, who could only reply by a mournful smile.\par
\par
Emily withdrew as soon after dinner as possible, and was followed by\par
the Lady Blanche, whose anxious enquiries, however, she found herself\par
quite unequal to answer, and whom she entreated to spare her on the\par
subject of her distress. To converse on any topic, was now, indeed, so\par
extremely painful to her, that she soon gave up the attempt, and\par
Blanche left her, with pity of the sorrow, which she perceived she had\par
no power to assuage.\par
\par
Emily secretly determined to go to her convent in a day or two; for\par
company, especially that of the Countess and Mademoiselle Bearn, was\par
intolerable to her, in the present state of her spirits; and, in the\par
retirement of the convent, as well as the kindness of the abbess, she\par
hoped to recover the command of her mind, and to teach it resignation\par
to the event, which, she too plainly perceived, was approaching.\par
\par
To have lost Valancourt by death, or to have seen him married to a\par
rival, would, she thought, have given her less anguish, than a\par
conviction of his unworthiness, which must terminate in misery to\par
himself, and which robbed her even of the solitary image her heart so\par
long had cherished. These painful reflections were interrupted, for a\par
moment, by a note from Valancourt, written in evident distraction of\par
mind, entreating, that she would permit him to see her on the\par
approaching evening, instead of the following morning; a request, which\par
occasioned her so much agitation, that she was unable to answer it. She\par
wished to see him, and to terminate her present state of suspense, yet\par
shrunk from the interview, and, incapable of deciding for herself, she,\par
at length, sent to beg a few moments’ conversation with the Count in\par
his library, where she delivered to him the note, and requested his\par
advice. After reading it, he said, that, if she believed herself well\par
enough to support the interview, his opinion was, that, for the relief\par
of both parties, it ought to take place, that evening.\par
\par
“His affection for you is, undoubtedly, a very sincere one,” added the\par
Count; “and he appears so much distressed, and you, my amiable friend,\par
are so ill at ease—that the sooner the affair is decided, the better.”\par
\par
Emily replied, therefore, to Valancourt, that she would see him, and\par
then exerted herself in endeavours to attain fortitude and composure,\par
to bear her through the approaching scene—a scene so afflictingly the\par
reverse of any, to which she had looked forward!\par
\par
\par
\par
VOLUME 4\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER I\par
\par
Is all the council that we two have shared,\par
the hours that we have spent,\par
When we have chid the hasty-footed time\par
For parting us—Oh! and is all forgot?\par
\par
And will you rend our ancient love asunder?\par
                    MIDSUMMER NIGHT’S DREAM\par
\par
\par
In the evening, when Emily was at length informed, that Count De\par
Villefort requested to see her, she guessed that Valancourt was below,\par
and, endeavouring to assume composure and to recollect all her spirits,\par
she rose and left the apartment; but on reaching the door of the\par
library, where she imagined him to be, her emotion returned with such\par
energy, that, fearing to trust herself in the room, she returned into\par
the hall, where she continued for a considerable time, unable to\par
command her agitated spirits.\par
\par
When she could recall them, she found in the library Valancourt, seated\par
with the Count, who both rose on her entrance; but she did not dare to\par
look at Valancourt, and the Count, having led her to a chair,\par
immediately withdrew.\par
\par
Emily remained with her eyes fixed on the floor, under such oppression\par
of heart, that she could not speak, and with difficulty breathed; while\par
Valancourt threw himself into a chair beside her, and, sighing heavily,\par
continued silent, when, had she raised her eyes, she would have\par
perceived the violent emotions, with which he was agitated.\par
\par
At length, in a tremulous voice, he said, “I have solicited to see you\par
this evening, that I might, at least, be spared the further torture of\par
suspense, which your altered manner had occasioned me, and which the\par
hints I have just received from the Count have in part explained. I\par
perceive I have enemies, Emily, who envied me my late happiness, and\par
who have been busy in searching out the means to destroy it: I\par
perceive, too, that time and absence have weakened the affection you\par
once felt for me, and that you can now easily be taught to forget me.”\par
\par
His last words faltered, and Emily, less able to speak than before,\par
continued silent.\par
\par
“O what a meeting is this!” exclaimed Valancourt, starting from his\par
seat, and pacing the room with hurried steps, “what a meeting is this,\par
after our long—long separation!” Again he sat down, and, after the\par
struggle of a moment, he added in a firm but despairing tone, “This is\par
too much—I cannot bear it! Emily, will you not speak to me?”\par
\par
He covered his face with his hand, as if to conceal his emotion, and\par
took Emily’s, which she did not withdraw. Her tears could no longer be\par
restrained; and, when he raised his eyes and perceived that she was\par
weeping, all his tenderness returned, and a gleam of hope appeared to\par
cross his mind, for he exclaimed, “O! you do pity me, then, you do love\par
me! Yes, you are still my own Emily—let me believe those tears, that\par
tell me so!”\par
\par
Emily now made an effort to recover her firmness, and, hastily drying\par
them, “Yes,” said she, “I do pity you—I weep for you—but, ought I to\par
think of you with affection? You may remember, that yester-evening I\par
said, I had still sufficient confidence in your candour to believe,\par
that, when I should request an explanation of your words, you would\par
give it. This explanation is now unnecessary, I understand them too\par
well; but prove, at least, that your candour is deserving of the\par
confidence I give it, when I ask you, whether you are conscious of\par
being the same estimable Valancourt—whom I once loved.”\par
\par
“Once loved!” cried he,—“the same—the same!” He paused in extreme\par
emotion, and then added, in a voice at once solemn, and dejected,—“No—I\par
am not the same!—I am lost—I am no longer worthy of you!”\par
\par
He again concealed his face. Emily was too much affected by this honest\par
confession to reply immediately, and, while she struggled to overcome\par
the pleadings of her heart, and to act with the decisive firmness,\par
which was necessary for her future peace, she perceived all the danger\par
of trusting long to her resolution, in the presence of Valancourt, and\par
was anxious to conclude an interview, that tortured them both; yet,\par
when she considered, that this was probably their last meeting, her\par
fortitude sunk at once, and she experienced only emotions of tenderness\par
and of despondency.\par
\par
Valancourt, meanwhile, lost in emotions of remorse and grief, which he\par
had neither the power, nor the will to express, sat insensible almost\par
of the presence of Emily, his features still concealed, and his breast\par
agitated by convulsive sighs.\par
\par
“Spare me the necessity,” said Emily, recollecting her fortitude,\par
“spare me the necessity of mentioning those circumstances of your\par
conduct, which oblige me to break our connection for ever.—We must\par
part, I now see you for the last time.”\par
\par
“Impossible!” cried Valancourt, roused from his deep silence, “You\par
cannot mean what you say!—you cannot mean to throw me from you for\par
ever!”\par
\par
“We must part,” repeated Emily, with emphasis,—“and that for ever! Your\par
own conduct has made this necessary.”\par
\par
“This is the Count’s determination,” said he haughtily, “not yours, and\par
I shall enquire by what authority he interferes between us.” He now\par
rose, and walked about the room in great emotion.\par
\par
“Let me save you from this error,” said Emily, not less agitated—“it is\par
my determination, and, if you reflect a moment on your late conduct,\par
you will perceive, that my future peace requires it.”\par
\par
“Your future peace requires, that we should part—part for ever!” said\par
Valancourt, “How little did I ever expect to hear you say so!”\par
\par
“And how little did I expect, that it would be necessary for me to say\par
so!” rejoined Emily, while her voice softened into tenderness, and her\par
tears flowed again.—“That you—you, Valancourt, would ever fall from my\par
esteem!”\par
\par
He was silent a moment, as if overwhelmed by the consciousness of no\par
longer deserving this esteem, as well as the certainty of having lost\par
it, and then, with impassioned grief, lamented the criminality of his\par
late conduct and the misery to which it had reduced him, till, overcome\par
by a recollection of the past and a conviction of the future, he burst\par
into tears, and uttered only deep and broken sighs.\par
\par
The remorse he had expressed, and the distress he suffered could not be\par
witnessed by Emily with indifference, and, had she not called to her\par
recollection all the circumstances, of which Count De Villefort had\par
informed her, and all he had said of the danger of confiding in\par
repentance, formed under the influence of passion, she might perhaps\par
have trusted to the assurances of her heart, and have forgotten his\par
misconduct in the tenderness, which that repentance excited.\par
\par
Valancourt, returning to the chair beside her, at length, said, in a\par
calm voice, “’Tis true, I am fallen—fallen from my own esteem! but\par
could you, Emily, so soon, so suddenly resign, if you had not before\par
ceased to love me, or, if your conduct was not governed by the designs,\par
I will say, the selfish designs of another person! Would you not\par
otherwise be willing to hope for my reformation—and could you bear, by\par
estranging me from you, to abandon me to misery—to myself!”—Emily wept\par
aloud.—“No, Emily—no—you would not do this, if you still loved me. You\par
would find your own happiness in saving mine.”\par
\par
“There are too many probabilities against that hope,” said Emily, “to\par
justify me in trusting the comfort of my whole life to it. May I not\par
also ask, whether you could wish me to do this, if you really loved\par
me?”\par
\par
“Really loved you!” exclaimed Valancourt—“is it possible you can doubt\par
my love! Yet it is reasonable, that you should do so, since you see,\par
that I am less ready to suffer the horror of parting with you, than\par
that of involving you in my ruin. Yes, Emily—I am ruined—irreparably\par
ruined—I am involved in debts, which I can never discharge!”\par
Valancourt’s look, which was wild, as he spoke this, soon settled into\par
an expression of gloomy despair; and Emily, while she was compelled to\par
admire his sincerity, saw, with unutterable anguish, new reasons for\par
fear in the suddenness of his feelings and the extent of the misery, in\par
which they might involve him. After some minutes, she seemed to contend\par
against her grief and to struggle for fortitude to conclude the\par
interview. “I will not prolong these moments,” said she, “by a\par
conversation, which can answer no good purpose. Valancourt, farewell!”\par
\par
“You are not going?” said he, wildly interrupting her—“You will not\par
leave me thus—you will not abandon me even before my mind has suggested\par
any possibility of compromise between the last indulgence of my despair\par
and the endurance of my loss!” Emily was terrified by the sternness of\par
his look, and said, in a soothing voice, “You have yourself\par
acknowledged, that it is necessary we should part;—if you wish, that I\par
should believe you love me, you will repeat the\par
acknowledgment.”—“Never—never,” cried he—“I was distracted when I made\par
it. O! Emily—this is too much;—though you are not deceived as to my\par
faults, you must be deluded into this exasperation against them. The\par
Count is the barrier between us; but he shall not long remain so.”\par
\par
“You are, indeed, distracted,” said Emily, “the Count is not your\par
enemy; on the contrary, he is my friend, and that might, in some\par
degree, induce you to consider him as yours.”—“Your friend!” said\par
Valancourt, hastily, “how long has he been your friend, that he can so\par
easily make you forget your lover? Was it he, who recommended to your\par
favour the Monsieur Du Pont, who, you say, accompanied you from Italy,\par
and who, I say, has stolen your affections? But I have no right to\par
question you;—you are your own mistress. Du Pont, perhaps, may not long\par
triumph over my fallen fortunes!” Emily, more frightened than before by\par
the frantic looks of Valancourt, said, in a tone scarcely audible, “For\par
heaven’s sake be reasonable—be composed. Monsieur Du Pont is not your\par
rival, nor is the Count his advocate. You have no rival; nor, except\par
yourself, an enemy. My heart is wrung with anguish, which must increase\par
while your frantic behaviour shows me, more than ever, that you are no\par
longer the Valancourt I have been accustomed to love.”\par
\par
He made no reply, but sat with his arms rested on the table and his\par
face concealed by his hands; while Emily stood, silent and trembling,\par
wretched for herself and dreading to leave him in this state of mind.\par
\par
“O excess of misery!” he suddenly exclaimed, “that I can never lament\par
my sufferings, without accusing myself, nor remember you, without\par
recollecting the folly and the vice, by which I have lost you! Why was\par
I forced to Paris, and why did I yield to allurements, which were to\par
make me despicable for ever! O! why cannot I look back, without\par
interruption, to those days of innocence and peace, the days of our\par
early love!”—The recollection seemed to melt his heart, and the frenzy\par
of despair yielded to tears. After a long pause, turning towards her\par
and taking her hand, he said, in a softened voice, “Emily, can you bear\par
that we should part—can you resolve to give up a heart, that loves you\par
like mine—a heart, which, though it has erred—widely erred, is not\par
irretrievable from error, as, you well know, it never can be\par
retrievable from love?” Emily made no reply, but with her tears. “Can\par
you,” continued he, “can you forget all our former days of happiness\par
and confidence—when I had not a thought, that I might wish to conceal\par
from you—when I had no taste—no pleasures, in which you did not\par
participate?”\par
\par
“O do not lead me to the remembrance of those days,” said Emily,\par
“unless you can teach me to forget the present; I do not mean to\par
reproach you; if I did, I should be spared these tears; but why will\par
you render your present sufferings more conspicuous, by contrasting\par
them with your former virtues?”\par
\par
“Those virtues,” said Valancourt, “might, perhaps, again be mine, if\par
your affection, which nurtured them, was unchanged;—but I fear, indeed,\par
I see, that you can no longer love me; else the happy hours, which we\par
have passed together, would plead for me, and you could not look back\par
upon them unmoved. Yet, why should I torture myself with the\par
remembrance—why do I linger here? Am I not ruined—would it not be\par
madness to involve you in my misfortunes, even if your heart was still\par
my own? I will not distress you further. Yet, before I go,” added he,\par
in a solemn voice, “let me repeat, that, whatever may be my\par
destiny—whatever I may be doomed to suffer, I must always love you—most\par
fondly love you! I am going, Emily, I am going to leave you—to leave\par
you, for ever!” As he spoke the last words, his voice trembled, and he\par
threw himself again into the chair, from which he had risen. Emily was\par
utterly unable to leave the room, or to say farewell. All impression of\par
his criminal conduct and almost of his follies was obliterated from her\par
mind, and she was sensible only of pity and grief.\par
\par
“My fortitude is gone,” said Valancourt at length; “I can no longer\par
even struggle to recall it. I cannot now leave you—I cannot bid you an\par
eternal farewell; say, at least, that you will see me once again.”\par
Emily’s heart was somewhat relieved by the request, and she endeavoured\par
to believe, that she ought not to refuse it. Yet she was embarrassed by\par
recollecting, that she was a visitor in the house of the Count, who\par
could not be pleased by the return of Valancourt. Other considerations,\par
however, soon overcame this, and she granted his request, on the\par
condition, that he would neither think of the Count, as his enemy, nor\par
Du Pont as his rival. He then left her, with a heart, so much lightened\par
by this short respite, that he almost lost every former sense of\par
misfortune.\par
\par
Emily withdrew to her own room, that she might compose her spirits and\par
remove the traces of her tears, which would encourage the censorious\par
remarks of the Countess and her favourite, as well as excite the\par
curiosity of the rest of the family. She found it, however, impossible\par
to tranquillize her mind, from which she could not expel the\par
remembrance of the late scene with Valancourt, or the consciousness,\par
that she was to see him again, on the morrow. This meeting now appeared\par
more terrible to her than the last, for the ingenuous confession he had\par
made of his ill conduct and his embarrassed circumstances, with the\par
strength and tenderness of affection, which this confession discovered,\par
had deeply impressed her, and, in spite of all she had heard and\par
believed to his disadvantage, her esteem began to return. It frequently\par
appeared to her impossible, that he could have been guilty of the\par
depravities, reported of him, which, if not inconsistent with his\par
warmth and impetuosity, were entirely so with his candour and\par
sensibility. Whatever was the criminality, which had given rise to the\par
reports, she could not now believe them to be wholly true, nor that his\par
heart was finally closed against the charms of virtue. The deep\par
consciousness, which he felt as well as expressed of his errors, seemed\par
to justify the opinion; and, as she understood not the instability of\par
youthful dispositions, when opposed by habit, and that professions\par
frequently deceive those, who make, as well as those, who hear them,\par
she might have yielded to the flattering persuasions of her own heart\par
and the pleadings of Valancourt, had she not been guided by the\par
superior prudence of the Count. He represented to her, in a clear\par
light, the danger of her present situation, that of listening to\par
promises of amendment, made under the influence of strong passion, and\par
the slight hope, which could attach to a connection, whose chance of\par
happiness rested upon the retrieval of ruined circumstances and the\par
reform of corrupted habits. On these accounts, he lamented, that Emily\par
had consented to a second interview, for he saw how much it would shake\par
her resolution and increase the difficulty of her conquest.\par
\par
Her mind was now so entirely occupied by nearer interests, that she\par
forgot the old housekeeper and the promised history, which so lately\par
had excited her curiosity, but which Dorothée was probably not very\par
anxious to disclose, for night came; the hours passed; and she did not\par
appear in Emily’s chamber. With the latter it was a sleepless and\par
dismal night; the more she suffered her memory to dwell on the late\par
scenes with Valancourt, the more her resolution declined, and she was\par
obliged to recollect all the arguments, which the Count had made use of\par
to strengthen it, and all the precepts, which she had received from her\par
deceased father, on the subject of self-command, to enable her to act,\par
with prudence and dignity, on this the most severe occasion of her\par
life. There were moments, when all her fortitude forsook her, and when,\par
remembering the confidence of former times, she thought it impossible,\par
that she could renounce Valancourt. His reformation then appeared\par
certain; the arguments of Count De Villefort were forgotten; she\par
readily believed all she wished, and was willing to encounter any evil,\par
rather than that of an immediate separation.\par
\par
Thus passed the night in ineffectual struggles between affection and\par
reason, and she rose, in the morning, with a mind, weakened and\par
irresolute, and a frame, trembling with illness.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER II\par
\par
Come, weep with me;—past hope, past cure, past help!\par
                    ROMEO AND JULIET\par
\par
\par
Valancourt, meanwhile, suffered the tortures of remorse and despair.\par
The sight of Emily had renewed all the ardour, with which he first\par
loved her, and which had suffered a temporary abatement from absence\par
and the passing scenes of busy life. When, on the receipt of her\par
letter, he set out for Languedoc, he then knew, that his own folly had\par
involved him in ruin, and it was no part of his design to conceal this\par
from her. But he lamented only the delay which his ill-conduct must\par
give to their marriage, and did not foresee, that the information could\par
induce her to break their connection for ever. While the prospect of\par
this separation overwhelmed his mind, before stung with self-reproach,\par
he awaited their second interview, in a state little short of\par
distraction, yet was still inclined to hope, that his pleadings might\par
prevail upon her not to exact it. In the morning, he sent to know at\par
what hour she would see him; and his note arrived, when she was with\par
the Count, who had sought an opportunity of again conversing with her\par
of Valancourt; for he perceived the extreme distress of her mind, and\par
feared, more than ever, that her fortitude would desert her. Emily\par
having dismissed the messenger, the Count returned to the subject of\par
their late conversation, urging his fear of Valancourt’s entreaties,\par
and again pointing out to her the lengthened misery, that must ensue,\par
if she should refuse to encounter some present uneasiness. His repeated\par
arguments could, indeed, alone have protected her from the affection\par
she still felt for Valancourt, and she resolved to be governed by them.\par
\par
The hour of interview, at length, arrived. Emily went to it, at least,\par
with composure of manner, but Valancourt was so much agitated, that he\par
could not speak, for several minutes, and his first words were\par
alternately those of lamentation, entreaty, and self-reproach.\par
Afterward, he said, “Emily, I have loved you—I do love you, better than\par
my life; but I am ruined by my own conduct. Yet I would seek to\par
entangle you in a connection, that must be miserable for you, rather\par
than subject myself to the punishment, which is my due, the loss of\par
you. I am a wretch, but I will be a villain no longer.—I will not\par
endeavour to shake your resolution by the pleadings of a selfish\par
passion. I resign you, Emily, and will endeavour to find consolation in\par
considering, that, though I am miserable, you, at least, may be happy.\par
The merit of the sacrifice is, indeed, not my own, for I should never\par
have attained strength of mind to surrender you, if your prudence had\par
not demanded it.”\par
\par
He paused a moment, while Emily attempted to conceal the tears, which\par
came to her eyes. She would have said, “You speak now, as you were wont\par
to do,” but she checked herself.—“Forgive me, Emily,” said he, “all the\par
sufferings I have occasioned you, and, sometimes, when you think of the\par
wretched Valancourt, remember, that his only consolation would be to\par
believe, that you are no longer unhappy by his folly.” The tears now\par
fell fast upon her cheek, and he was relapsing into the frenzy of\par
despair, when Emily endeavoured to recall her fortitude and to\par
terminate an interview, which only seemed to increase the distress of\par
both. Perceiving her tears and that she was rising to go, Valancourt\par
struggled, once more, to overcome his own feelings and to sooth hers.\par
“The remembrance of this sorrow,” said he, “shall in future be my\par
protection. O! never again will example, or temptation have power to\par
seduce me to evil, exalted as I shall be by the recollection of your\par
grief for me.”\par
\par
Emily was somewhat comforted by this assurance. “We are now parting for\par
ever,” said she; “but, if my happiness is dear to you, you will always\par
remember, that nothing can contribute to it more, than to believe, that\par
you have recovered your own esteem.” Valancourt took her hand;—his eyes\par
were covered with tears, and the farewell he would have spoken was lost\par
in sighs. After a few moments, Emily said, with difficulty and emotion,\par
“Farewell, Valancourt, may you be happy!” She repeated her “farewell,”\par
and attempted to withdraw her hand, but he still held it and bathed it\par
with his tears. “Why prolong these moments?” said Emily, in a voice\par
scarcely audible, “they are too painful to us both.” “This is too—too\par
much,” exclaimed Valancourt, resigning her hand and throwing himself\par
into a chair, where he covered his face with his hands and was\par
overcome, for some moments, by convulsive sighs. After a long pause,\par
during which Emily wept in silence, and Valancourt seemed struggling\par
with his grief, she again rose to take leave of him. Then, endeavouring\par
to recover his composure, “I am again afflicting you,” said he, “but\par
let the anguish I suffer plead for me.” He then added, in a solemn\par
voice, which frequently trembled with the agitation of his heart,\par
“Farewell, Emily, you will always be the only object of my tenderness.\par
Sometimes you will think of the unhappy Valancourt, and it will be with\par
pity, though it may not be with esteem. O! what is the whole world to\par
me, without you—without your esteem!” He checked himself—“I am falling\par
again into the error I have just lamented. I must not intrude longer\par
upon your patience, or I shall relapse into despair.”\par
\par
He once more bade Emily adieu, pressed her hand to his lips, looked at\par
her, for the last time, and hurried out of the room.\par
\par
Emily remained in the chair, where he had left her, oppressed with a\par
pain at her heart, which scarcely permitted her to breathe, and\par
listening to his departing steps, sinking fainter and fainter, as he\par
crossed the hall. She was, at length, roused by the voice of the\par
Countess in the garden, and, her attention being then awakened, the\par
first object, which struck her sight, was the vacant chair, where\par
Valancourt had sat. The tears, which had been, for some time, repressed\par
by the kind of astonishment, that followed his departure, now came to\par
her relief, and she was, at length, sufficiently composed to return to\par
her own room.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER III\par
\par
This is no mortal business, nor no sound\par
That the earth owes!\par
                    SHAKESPEARE\par
\par
\par
We now return to the mention of Montoni, whose rage and disappointment\par
were soon lost in nearer interests, than any, which the unhappy Emily\par
had awakened. His depredations having exceeded their usual limits, and\par
reached an extent, at which neither the timidity of the then commercial\par
senate of Venice, nor their hope of his occasional assistance would\par
permit them to connive, the same effort, it was resolved, should\par
complete the suppression of his power and the correction of his\par
outrages. While a corps of considerable strength was upon the point of\par
receiving orders to march for Udolpho, a young officer, prompted partly\par
by resentment, for some injury, received from Montoni, and partly by\par
the hope of distinction, solicited an interview with the Minister, who\par
directed the enterprise. To him he represented, that the situation of\par
Udolpho rendered it too strong to be taken by open force, except after\par
some tedious operations; that Montoni had lately shown how capable he\par
was of adding to its strength all the advantages, which could be\par
derived from the skill of a commander; that so considerable a body of\par
troops, as that allotted to the expedition, could not approach Udolpho\par
without his knowledge, and that it was not for the honour of the\par
republic to have a large part of its regular force employed, for such a\par
time as the siege of Udolpho would require, upon the attack of a\par
handful of banditti. The object of the expedition, he thought, might be\par
accomplished much more safely and speedily by mingling contrivance with\par
force. It was possible to meet Montoni and his party, without their\par
walls, and to attack them then; or, by approaching the fortress, with\par
the secrecy, consistent with the march of smaller bodies of troops, to\par
take advantage either of the treachery, or negligence of some of his\par
party, and to rush unexpectedly upon the whole even in the castle of\par
Udolpho.\par
\par
This advice was seriously attended to, and the officer, who gave it,\par
received the command of the troops, demanded for his purpose. His first\par
efforts were accordingly those of contrivance alone. In the\par
neighbourhood of Udolpho, he waited, till he had secured the assistance\par
of several of the _condottieri_, of whom he found none, that he\par
addressed, unwilling to punish their imperious master and to secure\par
their own pardon from the senate. He learned also the number of\par
Montoni’s troops, and that it had been much increased, since his late\par
successes. The conclusion of his plan was soon effected. Having\par
returned with his party, who received the watch-word and other\par
assistance from their friends within, Montoni and his officers were\par
surprised by one division, who had been directed to their apartment,\par
while the other maintained the slight combat, which preceded the\par
surrender of the whole garrison. Among the persons, seized with\par
Montoni, was Orsino, the assassin, who had joined him on his first\par
arrival at Udolpho, and whose concealment had been made known to the\par
senate by Count Morano, after the unsuccessful attempt of the latter to\par
carry off Emily. It was, indeed, partly for the purpose of capturing\par
this man, by whom one of the senate had been murdered, that the\par
expedition was undertaken, and its success was so acceptable to them,\par
that Morano was instantly released, notwithstanding the political\par
suspicions, which Montoni, by his secret accusation, had excited\par
against him. The celerity and ease, with which this whole transaction\par
was completed, prevented it from attracting curiosity, or even from\par
obtaining a place in any of the published records of that time; so that\par
Emily, who remained in Languedoc, was ignorant of the defeat and signal\par
humiliation of her late persecutor.\par
\par
Her mind was now occupied with sufferings, which no effort of reason\par
had yet been able to control. Count De Villefort, who sincerely\par
attempted whatever benevolence could suggest for softening them,\par
sometimes allowed her the solitude she wished for, sometimes led her\par
into friendly parties, and constantly protected her, as much as\par
possible, from the shrewd enquiries and critical conversation of the\par
Countess. He often invited her to make excursions, with him and his\par
daughter, during which he conversed entirely on questions, suitable to\par
her taste, without appearing to consult it, and thus endeavoured\par
gradually to withdraw her from the subject of her grief, and to awake\par
other interests in her mind. Emily, to whom he appeared as the\par
enlightened friend and protector of her youth, soon felt for him the\par
tender affection of a daughter, and her heart expanded to her young\par
friend Blanche, as to a sister, whose kindness and simplicity\par
compensated for the want of more brilliant qualities. It was long\par
before she could sufficiently abstract her mind from Valancourt to\par
listen to the story, promised by old Dorothée, concerning which her\par
curiosity had once been so deeply interested; but Dorothée, at length,\par
reminded her of it, and Emily desired, that she would come, that night,\par
to her chamber.\par
\par
Still her thoughts were employed by considerations, which weakened her\par
curiosity, and Dorothée’s tap at the door, soon after twelve, surprised\par
her almost as much as if it had not been appointed. “I am come, at\par
last, lady,” said she; “I wonder what it is makes my old limbs shake\par
so, tonight. I thought, once or twice, I should have dropped, as I was\par
a-coming.” Emily seated her in a chair, and desired, that she would\par
compose her spirits, before she entered upon the subject, that had\par
brought her thither. “Alas,” said Dorothée, “it is thinking of that, I\par
believe, which has disturbed me so. In my way hither too, I passed the\par
chamber, where my dear lady died, and everything was so still and\par
gloomy about me, that I almost fancied I saw her, as she appeared upon\par
her death-bed.”\par
\par
Emily now drew her chair near to Dorothée, who went on. “It is about\par
twenty years since my lady Marchioness came a bride to the château. O!\par
I well remember how she looked, when she came into the great hall,\par
where we servants were all assembled to welcome her, and how happy my\par
lord the Marquis seemed. Ah! who would have thought then!—But, as I was\par
saying, ma’amselle, I thought the Marchioness, with all her sweet\par
looks, did not look happy at heart, and so I told my husband, and he\par
said it was all fancy; so I said no more, but I made my remarks, for\par
all that. My lady Marchioness was then about your age, and, as I have\par
often thought, very like you. Well! my lord the Marquis kept open\par
house, for a long time, and gave such entertainments and there were\par
such gay doings as have never been in the château since. I was younger,\par
ma’amselle, then, than I am now, and was as gay at the best of them. I\par
remember I danced with Philip, the butler, in a pink gown, with yellow\par
ribbons, and a coif, not such as they wear now, but plaited high, with\par
ribbons all about it. It was very becoming truly;—my lord, the Marquis,\par
noticed me. Ah! he was a good-natured gentleman then—who would have\par
thought that he—”\par
\par
“But the Marchioness, Dorothée,” said Emily, “you were telling me of\par
her.”\par
\par
“O yes, my lady Marchioness, I thought she did not seem happy at heart,\par
and once, soon after the marriage, I caught her crying in her chamber;\par
but, when she saw me, she dried her eyes, and pretended to smile. I did\par
not dare then to ask what was the matter; but, the next time I saw her\par
crying, I did, and she seemed displeased;—so I said no more. I found\par
out, some time after, how it was. Her father, it seems, had commanded\par
her to marry my lord, the Marquis, for his money, and there was another\par
nobleman, or else a chevalier, that she liked better and that was very\par
fond of her, and she fretted for the loss of him, I fancy, but she\par
never told me so. My lady always tried to conceal her tears from the\par
Marquis, for I have often seen her, after she has been so sorrowful,\par
look so calm and sweet, when he came into the room! But my lord, all of\par
a sudden, grew gloomy and fretful, and very unkind sometimes to my\par
lady. This afflicted her very much, as I saw, for she never complained,\par
and she used to try so sweetly to oblige him and to bring him into a\par
good humour, that my heart has often ached to see it. But he used to be\par
stubborn, and give her harsh answers, and then, when she found it all\par
in vain, she would go to her own room, and cry so! I used to hear her\par
in the ante-room, poor dear lady! but I seldom ventured to go to her. I\par
used, sometimes, to think my lord was jealous. To be sure my lady was\par
greatly admired, but she was too good to deserve suspicion. Among the\par
many chevaliers, that visited at the château, there was one, that I\par
always thought seemed just suited for my lady; he was so courteous, yet\par
so spirited, and there was such a grace, as it were, in all he did, or\par
said. I always observed, that, whenever he had been there, the Marquis\par
was more gloomy and my lady more thoughtful, and it came into my head,\par
that this was the chevalier she ought to have married, but I never\par
could learn for certain.”\par
\par
“What was the chevalier’s name, Dorothée?” said Emily.\par
\par
“Why that I will not tell even to you, ma’amselle, for evil may come of\par
it. I once heard from a person, who is since dead, that the Marchioness\par
was not in law the wife of the Marquis, for that she had before been\par
privately married to the gentleman she was so much attached to, and was\par
afterwards afraid to own it to her father, who was a very stern man;\par
but this seems very unlikely, and I never gave much faith to it. As I\par
was saying, the Marquis was most out of humour, as I thought, when the\par
chevalier I spoke of had been at the château, and, at last, his ill\par
treatment of my lady made her quite miserable. He would see hardly any\par
visitors at the castle, and made her live almost by herself. I was her\par
constant attendant, and saw all she suffered, but still she never\par
complained.\par
\par
“After matters had gone on thus, for near a year, my lady was taken\par
ill, and I thought her long fretting had made her so,—but, alas! I fear\par
it was worse than that.”\par
\par
“Worse! Dorothée,” said Emily, “can that be possible?”\par
\par
“I fear it was so, madam, there were strange appearances. But I will\par
only tell what happened. My lord, the Marquis—”\par
\par
“Hush, Dorothée, what sounds were those?” said Emily.\par
\par
Dorothée changed countenance, and, while they both listened, they\par
heard, on the stillness of the night, music of uncommon sweetness.\par
\par
“I have surely heard that voice before!” said Emily, at length.\par
\par
“I have often heard it, and at this same hour,” said Dorothée,\par
solemnly, “and, if spirits ever bring music—that is surely the music of\par
one!”\par
\par
Emily, as the sounds drew nearer, knew them to be the same she had\par
formerly heard at the time of her father’s death, and, whether it was\par
the remembrance they now revived of that melancholy event, or that she\par
was struck with superstitious awe, it is certain she was so much\par
affected, that she had nearly fainted.\par
\par
“I think I once told you, madam,” said Dorothée, “that I first heard\par
this music, soon after my lady’s death! I well remember the night!”—\par
“Hark! it comes again!” said Emily, “let us open the window, and\par
listen.”\par
\par
They did so; but, soon, the sounds floated gradually away into\par
distance, and all was again still; they seemed to have sunk among the\par
woods, whose tufted tops were visible upon the clear horizon, while\par
every other feature of the scene was involved in the night-shade,\par
which, however, allowed the eye an indistinct view of some objects in\par
the garden below.\par
\par
As Emily leaned on the window, gazing with a kind of thrilling awe upon\par
the obscurity beneath, and then upon the cloudless arch above,\par
enlightened only by the stars, Dorothée, in a low voice, resumed her\par
narrative.\par
\par
“I was saying, ma’amselle, that I well remember when first I heard that\par
music. It was one night, soon after my lady’s death, that I had sat up\par
later than usual, and I don’t know how it was, but I had been thinking\par
a great deal about my poor mistress, and of the sad scene I had lately\par
witnessed. The château was quite still, and I was in the chamber at a\par
good distance from the rest of the servants, and this, with the\par
mournful things I had been thinking of, I suppose, made me low\par
spirited, for I felt very lonely and forlorn, as it were, and listened\par
often, wishing to hear a sound in the château, for you know,\par
ma’amselle, when one can hear people moving, one does not so much mind,\par
about one’s fears. But all the servants were gone to bed, and I sat,\par
thinking and thinking, till I was almost afraid to look round the room,\par
and my poor lady’s countenance often came to my mind, such as I had\par
seen her when she was dying, and, once or twice, I almost thought I saw\par
her before me,—when suddenly I heard such sweet music! It seemed just\par
at my window, and I shall never forget what I felt. I had not power to\par
move from my chair, but then, when I thought it was my dear lady’s\par
voice, the tears came to my eyes. I had often heard her sing, in her\par
life-time, and to be sure she had a very fine voice; it had made me cry\par
to hear her, many a time, when she has sat in her oriel, of an evening,\par
playing upon her lute such sad songs, and singing so. O! it went to\par
one’s heart! I have listened in the ante-chamber, for the hour\par
together, and she would sometimes sit playing, with the window open,\par
when it was summer time, till it was quite dark, and when I have gone\par
in, to shut it, she has hardly seemed to know what hour it was. But, as\par
I said, madam,” continued Dorothée, “when first I heard the music, that\par
came just now, I thought it was my late lady’s, and I have often\par
thought so again, when I have heard it, as I have done at intervals,\par
ever since. Sometimes, many months have gone by, but still it has\par
returned.”\par
\par
“It is extraordinary,” observed Emily, “that no person has yet\par
discovered the musician.”\par
\par
“Aye, ma’amselle, if it had been anything earthly it would have been\par
discovered long ago, but who could have courage to follow a spirit, and\par
if they had, what good could it do?—for spirits, _you know_, ma’am, can\par
take any shape, or no shape, and they will be here, one minute, and,\par
the next perhaps, in a quite different place!”\par
\par
“Pray resume your story of the Marchioness,” said Emily, “and acquaint\par
me with the manner of her death.”\par
\par
“I will, ma’am,” said Dorothée, “but shall we leave the window?”\par
\par
“This cool air refreshes me,” replied Emily, “and I love to hear it\par
creep along the woods, and to look upon this dusky landscape. You were\par
speaking of my lord, the Marquis, when the music interrupted us.”\par
\par
“Yes, madam, my lord, the Marquis, became more and more gloomy; and my\par
lady grew worse and worse, till, one night, she was taken very ill,\par
indeed. I was called up, and, when I came to her bedside, I was shocked\par
to see her countenance—it was so changed! She looked piteously up at\par
me, and desired I would call the Marquis again, for he was not yet\par
come, and tell him she had something particular to say to him. At last,\par
he came, and he did, to be sure, seem very sorry to see her, but he\par
said very little. My lady told him she felt herself to be dying, and\par
wished to speak with him alone, and then I left the room, but I shall\par
never forget his look as I went.”\par
\par
“When I returned, I ventured to remind my lord about sending for a\par
doctor, for I supposed he had forgot to do so, in his grief; but my\par
lady said it was then too late; but my lord, so far from thinking so,\par
seemed to think light of her disorder—till she was seized with such\par
terrible pains! O, I never shall forget her shriek! My lord then sent\par
off a man and horse for the doctor, and walked about the room and all\par
over the château in the greatest distress; and I staid by my dear lady,\par
and did what I could to ease her sufferings. She had intervals of ease,\par
and in one of these she sent for my lord again; when he came, I was\par
going, but she desired I would not leave her. O! I shall never forget\par
what a scene passed—I can hardly bear to think of it now! My lord was\par
almost distracted, for my lady behaved with so much goodness, and took\par
such pains to comfort him, that, if he ever had suffered a suspicion to\par
enter his head, he must now have been convinced he was wrong. And to be\par
sure he did seem to be overwhelmed with the thought of his treatment of\par
her, and this affected her so much, that she fainted away.\par
\par
“We then got my lord out of the room; he went into his library, and\par
threw himself on the floor, and there he staid, and would hear no\par
reason, that was talked to him. When my lady recovered, she enquired\par
for him, but, afterwards, said she could not bear to see his grief, and\par
desired we would let her die quietly. She died in my arms, ma’amselle,\par
and she went off as peacefully as a child, for all the violence of her\par
disorder was passed.”\par
\par
Dorothée paused and wept, and Emily wept with her; for she was much\par
affected by the goodness of the late Marchioness, and by the meek\par
patience with which she had suffered.\par
\par
“When the doctor came,” resumed Dorothée, “alas! he came too late; he\par
appeared greatly shocked to see her, for soon after her death a\par
frightful blackness spread all over her face. When he had sent the\par
attendants out of the room, he asked me several odd questions about the\par
Marchioness, particularly concerning the manner, in which she had been\par
seized, and he often shook his head at my answers, and seemed to mean\par
more, than he chose to say. But I understood him too well. However, I\par
kept my remarks to myself, and only told them to my husband, who bade\par
me hold my tongue. Some of the other servants, however, suspected what\par
I did, and strange reports were whispered about the neighbourhood, but\par
nobody dared to make any stir about them. When my lord heard that my\par
lady was dead, he shut himself up, and would see nobody but the doctor,\par
who used to be with him alone, sometimes for an hour together; and,\par
after that, the doctor never talked with me again about my lady. When\par
she was buried in the church of the convent, at a little distance\par
yonder, if the moon was up you might see the towers here, ma’amselle,\par
all my lord’s vassals followed the funeral, and there was not a dry eye\par
among them, for she had done a deal of good among the poor. My lord,\par
the Marquis, I never saw anybody so melancholy as he was afterwards,\par
and sometimes he would be in such fits of violence, that we almost\par
thought he had lost his senses. He did not stay long at the château,\par
but joined his regiment, and, soon after, all the servants, except my\par
husband and I, received notice to go, for my lord went to the wars. I\par
never saw him after, for he would not return to the château, though it\par
is such a fine place, and never finished those fine rooms he was\par
building on the west side of it, and it has, in a manner, been shut up\par
ever since, till my lord the Count came here.”\par
\par
“The death of the Marchioness appears extraordinary,” said Emily, who\par
was anxious to know more than she dared to ask.\par
\par
“Yes, madam,” replied Dorothée, “it was extraordinary; I have told you\par
all I saw, and you may easily guess what I think, I cannot say more,\par
because I would not spread reports, that might offend my lord the\par
Count.”\par
\par
“You are very right,” said Emily;—“where did the Marquis die?”—“In the\par
north of France, I believe, ma’amselle,” replied Dorothée. “I was very\par
glad, when I heard my lord the Count was coming, for this had been a\par
sad desolate place, these many years, and we heard such strange noises,\par
sometimes, after my lady’s death, that, as I told you before, my\par
husband and I left it for a neighbouring cottage. And now, lady, I have\par
told you all this sad history, and all my thoughts, and you have\par
promised, you know, never to give the least hint about it.”—“I have,”\par
said Emily, “and I will be faithful to my promise, Dorothée;—what you\par
have told has interested me more than you can imagine. I only wish I\par
could prevail upon you to tell the name of the chevalier, whom you\par
thought so deserving of the Marchioness.”\par
\par
Dorothée, however, steadily refused to do this, and then returned to\par
the notice of Emily’s likeness to the late Marchioness. “There is\par
another picture of her,” added she, “hanging in a room of the suite,\par
which was shut up. It was drawn, as I have heard, before she was\par
married, and is much more like you than the miniature.” When Emily\par
expressed a strong desire to see this, Dorothée replied, that she did\par
not like to open those rooms; but Emily reminded her, that the Count\par
had talked the other day of ordering them to be opened; of which\par
Dorothée seemed to consider much, and then she owned, that she should\par
feel less, if she went into them with Emily first, than otherwise, and\par
at length promised to show the picture.\par
\par
The night was too far advanced and Emily was too much affected by the\par
narrative of the scenes, which had passed in those apartments, to wish\par
to visit them at this hour, but she requested that Dorothée would\par
return on the following night, when they were not likely to be\par
observed, and conduct her thither. Besides her wish to examine the\par
portrait, she felt a thrilling curiosity to see the chamber, in which\par
the Marchioness had died, and which Dorothée had said remained, with\par
the bed and furniture, just as when the corpse was removed for\par
interment. The solemn emotions, which the expectation of viewing such a\par
scene had awakened, were in unison with the present tone of her mind,\par
depressed by severe disappointment. Cheerful objects rather added to,\par
than removed this depression; but, perhaps, she yielded too much to her\par
melancholy inclination, and imprudently lamented the misfortune, which\par
no virtue of her own could have taught her to avoid, though no effort\par
of reason could make her look unmoved upon the self-degradation of him,\par
whom she had once esteemed and loved.\par
\par
Dorothée promised to return, on the following night, with the keys of\par
the chambers, and then wished Emily good repose, and departed. Emily,\par
however, continued at the window, musing upon the melancholy fate of\par
the Marchioness and listening, in awful expectation, for a return of\par
the music. But the stillness of the night remained long unbroken,\par
except by the murmuring sounds of the woods, as they waved in the\par
breeze, and then by the distant bell of the convent, striking one. She\par
now withdrew from the window, and, as she sat at her bedside, indulging\par
melancholy reveries, which the loneliness of the hour assisted, the\par
stillness was suddenly interrupted not by music, but by very uncommon\par
sounds, that seemed to come either from the room adjoining her own, or\par
from one below. The terrible catastrophe that had been related to her,\par
together with the mysterious circumstances said to have since occurred\par
in the château, had so much shocked her spirits, that she now sunk for\par
a moment under the weakness of superstition. The sounds, however, did\par
not return, and she retired, to forget in sleep the disastrous story\par
she had heard.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER IV\par
\par
Now it is the time of night,\par
That, the graves all gaping wide,\par
Every one lets forth his sprite,\par
In the church-way path to glide.\par
                    SHAKESPEARE\par
\par
\par
On the next night, about the same hour as before, Dorothée came to\par
Emily’s chamber, with the keys of that suite of rooms, which had been\par
particularly appropriated to the late Marchioness. These extended along\par
the north side of the château, forming part of the old building; and,\par
as Emily’s room was in the south, they had to pass over a great extent\par
of the castle, and by the chambers of several of the family, whose\par
observations Dorothée was anxious to avoid, since it might excite\par
enquiry, and raise reports, such as would displease the Count. She,\par
therefore, requested, that Emily would wait half an hour, before they\par
ventured forth, that they might be certain all the servants were gone\par
to bed. It was nearly one, before the château was perfectly still, or\par
Dorothée thought it prudent to leave the chamber. In this interval, her\par
spirits seemed to be greatly affected by the remembrance of past\par
events, and by the prospect of entering again upon places, where these\par
had occurred, and in which she had not been for so many years. Emily\par
too was affected, but her feelings had more of solemnity, and less of\par
fear. From the silence, into which reflection and expectation had\par
thrown them, they, at length, roused themselves, and left the chamber.\par
Dorothée, at first, carried the lamp, but her hand trembled so much\par
with infirmity and alarm, that Emily took it from her, and offered her\par
arm, to support her feeble steps.\par
\par
They had to descend the great staircase, and, after passing over a wide\par
extent of the château, to ascend another, which led to the suite of\par
rooms they were in quest of. They stepped cautiously along the open\par
corridor, that ran round the great hall, and into which the chambers of\par
the Count, Countess, and the Lady Blanche, opened, and, from thence,\par
descending the chief staircase, they crossed the hall itself.\par
Proceeding through the servants hall, where the dying embers of a wood\par
fire still glimmered on the hearth, and the supper table was surrounded\par
by chairs, that obstructed their passage, they came to the foot of the\par
back staircase. Old Dorothée here paused, and looked around; “Let us\par
listen,” said she, “if anything is stirring; Ma’amselle, do you hear\par
any voice?” “None,” said Emily, “there certainly is no person up in the\par
château, besides ourselves.”—“No, ma’amselle,” said Dorothée, “but I\par
have never been here at this hour before, and, after what I know, my\par
fears are not wonderful.”—“What do you know?” said Emily.—“O,\par
ma’amselle, we have no time for talking now; let us go on. That door on\par
the left is the one we must open.”\par
\par
They proceeded, and, having reached the top of the staircase, Dorothée\par
applied the key to the lock. “Ah,” said she, as she endeavoured to turn\par
it, “so many years have passed since this was opened, that I fear it\par
will not move.” Emily was more successful, and they presently entered a\par
spacious and ancient chamber.\par
\par
“Alas!” exclaimed Dorothée, as she entered, “the last time I passed\par
through this door—I followed my poor lady’s corpse!”\par
\par
Emily, struck with the circumstance, and affected by the dusky and\par
solemn air of the apartment, remained silent, and they passed on\par
through a long suite of rooms, till they came to one more spacious than\par
the rest, and rich in the remains of faded magnificence.\par
\par
“Let us rest here awhile, madam,” said Dorothée faintly, “we are going\par
into the chamber, where my lady died! that door opens into it. Ah,\par
ma’amselle! why did you persuade me to come?”\par
\par
Emily drew one of the massy arm-chairs, with which the apartment was\par
furnished, and begged Dorothée would sit down, and try to compose her\par
spirits.\par
\par
“How the sight of this place brings all that passed formerly to my\par
mind!” said Dorothée; “it seems as if it was but yesterday since all\par
that sad affair happened!”\par
\par
“Hark! what noise is that?” said Emily.\par
\par
Dorothée, half starting from her chair, looked round the apartment, and\par
they listened—but, everything remaining still, the old woman spoke\par
again upon the subject of her sorrow. “This saloon, ma’amselle, was in\par
my lady’s time the finest apartment in the château, and it was fitted\par
up according to her own taste. All this grand furniture, but you can\par
now hardly see what it is for the dust, and our light is none of the\par
best—ah! how I have seen this room lighted up in my lady’s time!—all\par
this grand furniture came from Paris, and was made after the fashion of\par
some in the Louvre there, except those large glasses, and they came\par
from some outlandish place, and that rich tapestry. How the colours are\par
faded already!—since I saw it last!”\par
\par
“I understood, that was twenty years ago,” observed Emily.\par
\par
“Thereabout, madam,” said Dorothée, “and well remembered, but all the\par
time between then and now seems as nothing. That tapestry used to be\par
greatly admired at, it tells the stories out of some famous book, or\par
other, but I have forgot the name.”\par
\par
Emily now rose to examine the figures it exhibited, and discovered, by\par
verses in the Provençal tongue, wrought underneath each scene, that it\par
exhibited stories from some of the most celebrated ancient romances.\par
\par
Dorothée’s spirits being now more composed, she rose, and unlocked the\par
door that led into the late Marchioness’s apartment, and Emily passed\par
into a lofty chamber, hung round with dark arras, and so spacious, that\par
the lamp she held up did not show its extent; while Dorothée, when she\par
entered, had dropped into a chair, where, sighing deeply, she scarcely\par
trusted herself with the view of a scene so affecting to her. It was\par
some time before Emily perceived, through the dusk, the bed on which\par
the Marchioness was said to have died; when, advancing to the upper end\par
of the room, she discovered the high canopied tester of dark green\par
damask, with the curtains descending to the floor in the fashion of a\par
tent, half drawn, and remaining apparently, as they had been left\par
twenty years before; and over the whole bedding was thrown a\par
counterpane, or pall, of black velvet, that hung down to the floor.\par
Emily shuddered, as she held the lamp over it, and looked within the\par
dark curtains, where she almost expected to have seen a human face,\par
and, suddenly remembering the horror she had suffered upon discovering\par
the dying Madame Montoni in the turret-chamber of Udolpho, her spirits\par
fainted, and she was turning from the bed, when Dorothée, who had now\par
reached it, exclaimed, “Holy Virgin! methinks I see my lady stretched\par
upon that pall—as when last I saw her!”\par
\par
Emily, shocked by this exclamation, looked involuntarily again within\par
the curtains, but the blackness of the pall only appeared; while\par
Dorothée was compelled to support herself upon the side of the bed, and\par
presently tears brought her some relief.\par
\par
“Ah!” said she, after she had wept awhile, “it was here I sat on that\par
terrible night, and held my lady’s hand, and heard her last words, and\par
saw all her sufferings—_here_ she died in my arms!”\par
\par
“Do not indulge these painful recollections,” said Emily, “let us go.\par
Show me the picture you mentioned, if it will not too much affect you.”\par
\par
“It hangs in the oriel,” said Dorothée rising, and going towards a\par
small door near the bed’s head, which she opened, and Emily followed\par
with the light into the closet of the late Marchioness.\par
\par
“Alas! there she is, ma’amselle,” said Dorothée, pointing to a portrait\par
of a lady, “there is her very self! just as she looked when she came\par
first to the château. You see, madam, she was all blooming like you,\par
then—and so soon to be cut off!”\par
\par
While Dorothée spoke, Emily was attentively examining the picture,\par
which bore a strong resemblance to the miniature, though the expression\par
of the countenance in each was somewhat different; but still she\par
thought she perceived something of that pensive melancholy in the\par
portrait, which so strongly characterised the miniature.\par
\par
“Pray, ma’amselle, stand beside the picture, that I may look at you\par
together,” said Dorothée, who, when the request was complied with,\par
exclaimed again at the resemblance. Emily also, as she gazed upon it,\par
thought that she had somewhere seen a person very like it, though she\par
could not now recollect who this was.\par
\par
In this closet were many memorials of the departed Marchioness; a robe\par
and several articles of her dress were scattered upon the chairs, as if\par
they had just been thrown off. On the floor were a pair of black satin\par
slippers, and, on the dressing-table, a pair of gloves and a long black\par
veil, which, as Emily took it up to examine, she perceived was dropping\par
to pieces with age.\par
\par
“Ah!” said Dorothée, observing the veil, “my lady’s hand laid it there;\par
it has never been moved since!”\par
\par
Emily, shuddering, immediately laid it down again. “I well remember\par
seeing her take it off,” continued Dorothée, “it was on the night\par
before her death, when she had returned from a little walk I had\par
persuaded her to take in the gardens, and she seemed refreshed by it. I\par
told her how much better she looked, and I remember what a languid\par
smile she gave me; but, alas! she little thought, or I either, that she\par
was to die that night.”\par
\par
Dorothée wept again, and then, taking up the veil, threw it suddenly\par
over Emily, who shuddered to find it wrapped round her, descending even\par
to her feet, and, as she endeavoured to throw it off, Dorothée\par
entreated that she would keep it on for one moment. “I thought,” added\par
she, “how like you would look to my dear mistress in that veil;—may\par
your life, ma’amselle, be a happier one than hers!”\par
\par
Emily, having disengaged herself from the veil, laid it again on the\par
dressing-table, and surveyed the closet, where every object, on which\par
her eye fixed, seemed to speak of the Marchioness. In a large oriel\par
window of painted glass, stood a table, with a silver crucifix, and a\par
prayer-book open; and Emily remembered with emotion what Dorothée had\par
mentioned concerning her custom of playing on her lute in this window,\par
before she observed the lute itself, lying on a corner of the table, as\par
if it had been carelessly placed there by the hand, that had so often\par
awakened it.\par
\par
“This is a sad forlorn place!” said Dorothée, “for, when my dear lady\par
died, I had no heart to put it to rights, or the chamber either; and my\par
lord never came into the rooms after, so they remain just as they did\par
when my lady was removed for interment.”\par
\par
While Dorothée spoke, Emily was still looking on the lute, which was a\par
Spanish one, and remarkably large; and then, with a hesitating hand,\par
she took it up, and passed her fingers over the chords. They were out\par
of tune, but uttered a deep and full sound. Dorothée started at their\par
well-known tones, and, seeing the lute in Emily’s hand, said, “This is\par
the lute my lady Marchioness loved so! I remember when last she played\par
upon it—it was on the night that she died. I came as usual to undress\par
her, and, as I entered the bed-chamber, I heard the sound of music from\par
the oriel, and perceiving it was my lady’s, who was sitting there, I\par
stepped softly to the door, which stood a little open, to listen; for\par
the music—though it was mournful—was so sweet! There I saw her, with\par
the lute in her hand, looking upwards, and the tears fell upon her\par
cheeks, while she sung a vesper hymn, so soft, and so solemn! and her\par
voice trembled, as it were, and then she would stop for a moment, and\par
wipe away her tears, and go on again, lower than before. O! I had often\par
listened to my lady, but never heard anything so sweet as this; it made\par
me cry, almost, to hear it. She had been at prayers, I fancy, for there\par
was the book open on the table beside her—aye, and there it lies open\par
still! Pray, let us leave the oriel, ma’amselle,” added Dorothée, “this\par
is a heart-breaking place!”\par
\par
Having returned into the chamber, she desired to look once more upon\par
the bed, when, as they came opposite to the open door, leading into the\par
saloon, Emily, in the partial gleam, which the lamp threw into it,\par
thought she saw something glide along into the obscurer part of the\par
room. Her spirits had been much affected by the surrounding scene, or\par
it is probable this circumstance, whether real or imaginary, would not\par
have affected her in the degree it did; but she endeavoured to conceal\par
her emotion from Dorothée, who, however, observing her countenance\par
change, enquired if she was ill.\par
\par
“Let us go,” said Emily, faintly, “the air of these rooms is\par
unwholesome;” but, when she attempted to do so, considering that she\par
must pass through the apartment where the phantom of her terror had\par
appeared, this terror increased, and, too faint to support herself, she\par
sat down on the side of the bed.\par
\par
Dorothée, believing that she was only affected by a consideration of\par
the melancholy catastrophe, which had happened on this spot,\par
endeavoured to cheer her; and then, as they sat together on the bed,\par
she began to relate other particulars concerning it, and this without\par
reflecting, that it might increase Emily’s emotion, but because they\par
were particularly interesting to herself. “A little before my lady’s\par
death,” said she, “when the pains were gone off, she called me to her,\par
and stretching out her hand to me, I sat down just there—where the\par
curtain falls upon the bed. How well I remember her look at the\par
time—death was in it!—I can almost fancy I see her now.—There she lay,\par
ma’amselle—her face was upon the pillow there! This black counterpane\par
was not upon the bed then; it was laid on, after her death, and she was\par
laid out upon it.”\par
\par
Emily turned to look within the dusky curtains, as if she could have\par
seen the countenance of which Dorothée spoke. The edge of the white\par
pillow only appeared above the blackness of the pall, but, as her eyes\par
wandered over the pall itself, she fancied she saw it move. Without\par
speaking, she caught Dorothée’s arm, who, surprised by the action, and\par
by the look of terror that accompanied it, turned her eyes from Emily\par
to the bed, where, in the next moment she, too, saw the pall slowly\par
lifted, and fall again.\par
\par
Emily attempted to go, but Dorothée stood fixed and gazing upon the\par
bed; and, at length, said—“It is only the wind, that waves it,\par
ma’amselle; we have left all the doors open: see how the air waves the\par
lamp, too.—It is only the wind.”\par
\par
She had scarcely uttered these words, when the pall was more violently\par
agitated than before; but Emily, somewhat ashamed of her terrors,\par
stepped back to the bed, willing to be convinced that the wind only had\par
occasioned her alarm; when, as she gazed within the curtains, the pall\par
moved again, and, in the next moment, the apparition of a human\par
countenance rose above it.\par
\par
Screaming with terror, they both fled, and got out of the chamber as\par
fast as their trembling limbs would bear them, leaving open the doors\par
of all the rooms, through which they passed. When they reached the\par
staircase, Dorothée threw open a chamber door, where some of the female\par
servants slept, and sunk breathless on the bed; while Emily, deprived\par
of all presence of mind, made only a feeble attempt to conceal the\par
occasion of her terror from the astonished servants; and, though\par
Dorothée, when she could speak, endeavoured to laugh at her own fright,\par
and was joined by Emily, no remonstrances could prevail with the\par
servants, who had quickly taken the alarm, to pass even the remainder\par
of the night in a room so near to these terrific chambers.\par
\par
Dorothée having accompanied Emily to her own apartment, they then began\par
to talk over, with some degree of coolness, the strange circumstance,\par
that had just occurred; and Emily would almost have doubted her own\par
perceptions, had not those of Dorothée attested their truth. Having now\par
mentioned what she had observed in the outer chamber, she asked the\par
housekeeper, whether she was certain no door had been left unfastened,\par
by which a person might secretly have entered the apartments? Dorothée\par
replied, that she had constantly kept the keys of the several doors in\par
her own possession; that, when she had gone her rounds through the\par
castle, as she frequently did, to examine if all was safe, she had\par
tried these doors among the rest, and had always found them fastened.\par
It was, therefore, impossible, she added, that any person could have\par
got admittance into the apartments; and, if they could—it was very\par
improbable they should have chosen to sleep in a place so cold and\par
forlorn.\par
\par
Emily observed, that their visit to these chambers had, perhaps, been\par
watched, and that some person, for a frolic, had followed them into the\par
rooms, with a design to frighten them, and, while they were in the\par
oriel, had taken the opportunity of concealing himself in the bed.\par
\par
Dorothée allowed, that this was possible, till she recollected, that,\par
on entering the apartments, she had turned the key of the outer door,\par
and this, which had been done to prevent their visit being noticed by\par
any of the family, who might happen to be up, must effectually have\par
excluded every person, except themselves, from the chambers; and she\par
now persisted in affirming, that the ghastly countenance she had seen\par
was nothing human, but some dreadful apparition.\par
\par
Emily was very solemnly affected. Of whatever nature might be the\par
appearance she had witnessed, whether human or supernatural, the fate\par
of the deceased Marchioness was a truth not to be doubted; and this\par
unaccountable circumstance, occurring in the very scene of her\par
sufferings, affected Emily’s imagination with a superstitious awe, to\par
which, after having detected the fallacies at Udolpho, she might not\par
have yielded, had she been ignorant of the unhappy story, related by\par
the housekeeper. Her she now solemnly conjured to conceal the\par
occurrence of this night, and to make light of the terror she had\par
already betrayed, that the Count might not be distressed by reports,\par
which would certainly spread alarm and confusion among his family.\par
“Time,” she added, “may explain this mysterious affair; meanwhile let\par
us watch the event in silence.”\par
\par
Dorothée readily acquiesced; but she now recollected that she had left\par
all the doors of the north suite of rooms open, and, not having courage\par
to return alone to lock even the outer one, Emily, after some effort,\par
so far conquered her own fears, that she offered to accompany her to\par
the foot of the back staircase, and to wait there while Dorothée\par
ascended, whose resolution being reassured by this circumstance, she\par
consented to go, and they left Emily’s apartment together.\par
\par
No sound disturbed the stillness, as they passed along the halls and\par
galleries; but, on reaching the foot of the back staircase, Dorothée’s\par
resolution failed again; having, however, paused a moment to listen,\par
and no sound being heard above, she ascended, leaving Emily below, and,\par
scarcely suffering her eye to glance within the first chamber, she\par
fastened the door, which shut up the whole suite of apartments, and\par
returned to Emily.\par
\par
As they stepped along the passage, leading into the great hall, a sound\par
of lamentation was heard, which seemed to come from the hall itself,\par
and they stopped in new alarm to listen, when Emily presently\par
distinguished the voice of Annette, whom she found crossing the hall,\par
with another female servant, and so terrified by the report, which the\par
other maids had spread, that, believing she could be safe only where\par
her lady was, she was going for refuge to her apartment. Emily’s\par
endeavours to laugh, or to argue her out of these terrors, were equally\par
vain, and, in compassion to her distress, she consented that she should\par
remain in her room during the night.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER V\par
\par
Hail, mildly-pleasing Solitude!\par
Companion of the wise and good!\par
\par
Thine is the balmy breath of morn,\par
Just as the dew-bent rose is born.\par
\par
But chief when evening scenes decay\par
And the faint landscape swims away,\par
Thine is the doubtful, soft decline,\par
And that best hour of musing thine.\par
                    THOMSON\par
\par
\par
Emily’s injunctions to Annette to be silent on the subject of her\par
terror were ineffectual, and the occurrence of the preceding night\par
spread such alarm among the servants, who now all affirmed, that they\par
had frequently heard unaccountable noises in the château, that a report\par
soon reached the Count of the north side of the castle being haunted.\par
He treated this, at first, with ridicule, but, perceiving, that it was\par
productive of serious evil, in the confusion it occasioned among his\par
household, he forbade any person to repeat it, on pain of punishment.\par
\par
The arrival of a party of his friends soon withdrew his thoughts\par
entirely from this subject, and his servants had now little leisure to\par
brood over it, except, indeed, in the evenings after supper, when they\par
all assembled in their hall, and related stories of ghosts, till they\par
feared to look round the room; started, if the echo of a closing door\par
murmured along the passage, and refused to go singly to any part of the\par
castle.\par
\par
On these occasions Annette made a distinguished figure. When she told\par
not only of all the wonders she had witnessed, but of all that she had\par
imagined, in the castle of Udolpho, with the story of the strange\par
disappearance of Signora Laurentini, she made no trifling impression on\par
the mind of her attentive auditors. Her suspicions, concerning Montoni,\par
she would also have freely disclosed, had not Ludovico, who was now in\par
the service of the Count, prudently checked her loquacity, whenever it\par
pointed to that subject.\par
\par
Among the visitors at the château was the Baron de Saint Foix, an old\par
friend of the Count, and his son, the Chevalier St. Foix, a sensible\par
and amiable young man, who, having in the preceding year seen the Lady\par
Blanche, at Paris, had become her declared admirer. The friendship,\par
which the Count had long entertained for his father, and the equality\par
of their circumstances made him secretly approve of the connection;\par
but, thinking his daughter at this time too young to fix her choice for\par
life, and wishing to prove the sincerity and strength of the\par
Chevalier’s attachment, he then rejected his suit, though without\par
forbidding his future hope. This young man now came, with the Baron,\par
his father, to claim the reward of a steady affection, a claim, which\par
the Count admitted and which Blanche did not reject.\par
\par
While these visitors were at the château, it became a scene of gaiety\par
and splendour. The pavilion in the woods was fitted up and frequented,\par
in the fine evenings, as a supper-room, when the hour usually concluded\par
with a concert, at which the Count and Countess, who were scientific\par
performers, and the Chevaliers Henri and St. Foix, with the Lady\par
Blanche and Emily, whose voices and fine taste compensated for the want\par
of more skilful execution, usually assisted. Several of the Count’s\par
servants performed on horns and other instruments, some of which,\par
placed at a little distance among the woods, spoke, in sweet response,\par
to the harmony, that proceeded from the pavilion.\par
\par
At any other period, these parties would have been delightful to Emily;\par
but her spirits were now oppressed with a melancholy, which she\par
perceived that no kind of what is called amusement had power to\par
dissipate, and which the tender and, frequently, pathetic, melody of\par
these concerts sometimes increased to a very painful degree.\par
\par
She was particularly fond of walking in the woods, that hung on a\par
promontory, overlooking the sea. Their luxuriant shade was soothing to\par
her pensive mind, and, in the partial views, which they afforded of the\par
Mediterranean, with its winding shores and passing sails, tranquil\par
beauty was united with grandeur. The paths were rude and frequently\par
overgrown with vegetation, but their tasteful owner would suffer little\par
to be done to them, and scarcely a single branch to be lopped from the\par
venerable trees. On an eminence, in one of the most sequestered parts\par
of these woods, was a rustic seat, formed of the trunk of a decayed\par
oak, which had once been a noble tree, and of which many lofty branches\par
still flourishing united with beech and pines to over-canopy the spot.\par
Beneath their deep umbrage, the eye passed over the tops of other\par
woods, to the Mediterranean, and, to the left, through an opening, was\par
seen a ruined watch-tower, standing on a point of rock, near the sea,\par
and rising from among the tufted foliage.\par
\par
Hither Emily often came alone in the silence of evening, and, soothed\par
by the scenery and by the faint murmur, that rose from the waves, would\par
sit, till darkness obliged her to return to the château. Frequently,\par
also, she visited the watch-tower, which commanded the entire prospect,\par
and, when she leaned against its broken walls, and thought of\par
Valancourt, she not once imagined, what was so true, that this tower\par
had been almost as frequently his resort, as her own, since his\par
estrangement from the neighbouring château.\par
\par
One evening, she lingered here to a late hour. She had sat on the steps\par
of the building, watching, in tranquil melancholy, the gradual effect\par
of evening over the extensive prospect, till the grey waters of the\par
Mediterranean and the massy woods were almost the only features of the\par
scene, that remained visible; when, as she gazed alternately on these,\par
and on the mild blue of the heavens, where the first pale star of\par
evening appeared, she personified the hour in the following lines:—\par
\par
SONG OF THE EVENING HOUR\par
\par
Last of the Hours, that track the fading Day,\par
I move along the realms of twilight air,\par
And hear, remote, the choral song decay\par
Of sister-nymphs, who dance around his car.\par
\par
Then, as I follow through the azure void,\par
His partial splendour from my straining eye\par
Sinks in the depth of space; my only guide\par
His faint ray dawning on the farthest sky;\par
\par
Save that sweet, lingering strain of gayer Hours,\par
Whose close my voice prolongs in dying notes,\par
While mortals on the green earth own its pow’rs,\par
As downward on the evening gale it floats.\par
\par
When fades along the West the Sun’s last beam,\par
As, weary, to the nether world he goes,\par
And mountain-summits catch the purple gleam,\par
And slumbering ocean faint and fainter glows,\par
\par
Silent upon the globe’s broad shade I steal,\par
And o’er its dry turf shed the cooling dews,\par
And ev’ry fever’d herb and flow’ret heal,\par
And all their fragrance on the air diffuse.\par
\par
Where’er I move, a tranquil pleasure reigns;\par
O’er all the scene the dusky tints I send,\par
That forests wild and mountains, stretching plains\par
And peopled towns, in soft confusion blend.\par
\par
Wide o’er the world I waft the fresh’ning wind,\par
Low breathing through the woods and twilight vale,\par
In whispers soft, that woo the pensive mind\par
Of him, who loves my lonely steps to hail.\par
\par
His tender oaten reed I watch to hear,\par
Stealing its sweetness o’er some plaining rill,\par
Or soothing ocean’s wave, when storms are near,\par
Or swelling in the breeze from distant hill!\par
\par
I wake the fairy elves, who shun the light;\par
When, from their blossom’d beds, they slily peep,\par
And spy my pale star, leading on the night,—\par
Forth to their games and revelry they leap;\par
\par
Send all the prison’d sweets abroad in air,\par
That with them slumber’d in the flow’ret’s cell;\par
Then to the shores and moonlight brooks repair,\par
Till the high larks their matin-carol swell.\par
\par
The wood-nymphs hail my airs and temper’d shade,\par
With ditties soft and lightly sportive dance,\par
On river margin of some bow’ry glade,\par
And strew their fresh buds as my steps advance:\par
\par
But, swift I pass, and distant regions trace,\par
For moonbeams silver all the eastern cloud,\par
And Day’s last crimson vestige fades apace;\par
Down the steep west I fly from Midnight’s shroud.\par
\par
\par
The moon was now rising out of the sea. She watched its gradual\par
progress, the extending line of radiance it threw upon the waters, the\par
sparkling oars, the sail faintly silvered, and the wood-tops and the\par
battlements of the watch-tower, at whose foot she was sitting, just\par
tinted with the rays. Emily’s spirits were in harmony with this scene.\par
As she sat meditating, sounds stole by her on the air, which she\par
immediately knew to be the music and the voice she had formerly heard\par
at midnight, and the emotion of awe, which she felt, was not unmixed\par
with terror, when she considered her remote and lonely situation. The\par
sounds drew nearer. She would have risen to leave the place, but they\par
seemed to come from the way she must have taken towards the château,\par
and she awaited the event in trembling expectation. The sounds\par
continued to approach, for some time, and then ceased. Emily sat\par
listening, gazing and unable to move, when she saw a figure emerge from\par
the shade of the woods and pass along the bank, at some little distance\par
before her. It went swiftly, and her spirits were so overcome with awe,\par
that, though she saw, she did not much observe it.\par
\par
Having left the spot, with a resolution never again to visit it alone,\par
at so late an hour, she began to approach the château, when she heard\par
voices calling her from the part of the wood, which was nearest to it.\par
They were the shouts of the Count’s servants, who were sent to search\par
for her; and when she entered the supper-room, where he sat with Henri\par
and Blanche, he gently reproached her with a look, which she blushed to\par
have deserved.\par
\par
This little occurrence deeply impressed her mind, and, when she\par
withdrew to her own room, it recalled so forcibly the circumstances she\par
had witnessed, a few nights before, that she had scarcely courage to\par
remain alone. She watched to a late hour, when, no sound having renewed\par
her fears, she, at length, sunk to repose. But this was of short\par
continuance, for she was disturbed by a loud and unusual noise, that\par
seemed to come from the gallery, into which her chamber opened. Groans\par
were distinctly heard, and, immediately after, a dead weight fell\par
against the door, with a violence, that threatened to burst it open.\par
She called loudly to know who was there, but received no answer,\par
though, at intervals, she still thought she heard something like a low\par
moaning. Fear deprived her of the power to move. Soon after, she heard\par
footsteps in a remote part of the gallery, and, as they approached, she\par
called more loudly than before, till the steps paused at her door. She\par
then distinguished the voices of several of the servants, who seemed\par
too much engaged by some circumstance without, to attend to her calls;\par
but, Annette soon after entering the room for water, Emily understood,\par
that one of the maids had fainted, whom she immediately desired them to\par
bring into her room, where she assisted to restore her. When this girl\par
had recovered her speech, she affirmed, that, as she was passing up the\par
back staircase, in the way to her chamber, she had seen an apparition\par
on the second landing-place; she held the lamp low, she said, that she\par
might pick her way, several of the stairs being infirm and even\par
decayed, and it was upon raising her eyes, that she saw this\par
appearance. It stood for a moment in the corner of the landing-place,\par
which she was approaching, and then, gliding up the stairs, vanished at\par
the door of the apartment, that had been lately opened. She heard\par
afterwards a hollow sound.\par
\par
“Then the devil has got a key to that apartment,” said Dorothée, “for\par
it could be nobody but he; I locked the door myself!”\par
\par
The girl, springing down the stairs and passing up the great staircase,\par
had run, with a faint scream, till she reached the gallery, where she\par
fell, groaning, at Emily’s door.\par
\par
Gently chiding her for the alarm she had occasioned, Emily tried to\par
make her ashamed of her fears; but the girl persisted in saying, that\par
she had seen an apparition, till she went to her own room, whither she\par
was accompanied by all the servants present, except Dorothée, who, at\par
Emily’s request, remained with her during the night. Emily was\par
perplexed, and Dorothée was terrified, and mentioned many occurrences\par
of former times, which had long since confirmed her superstitions;\par
among these, according to her belief, she had once witnessed an\par
appearance, like that just described, and on the very same spot, and it\par
was the remembrance of it, that had made her pause, when she was going\par
to ascend the stairs with Emily, and which had increased her reluctance\par
to open the north apartments. Whatever might be Emily’s opinions, she\par
did not disclose them, but listened attentively to all that Dorothée\par
communicated, which occasioned her much thought and perplexity.\par
\par
From this night the terror of the servants increased to such an excess,\par
that several of them determined to leave the château, and requested\par
their discharge of the Count, who, if he had any faith in the subject\par
of their alarm, thought proper to dissemble it, and, anxious to avoid\par
the inconvenience that threatened him, employed ridicule and then\par
argument to convince them they had nothing to apprehend from\par
supernatural agency. But fear had rendered their minds inaccessible to\par
reason; and it was now, that Ludovico proved at once his courage and\par
his gratitude for the kindness he had received from the Count, by\par
offering to watch, during a night, in the suite of rooms, reputed to be\par
haunted. He feared, he said, no spirits, and, if anything of human form\par
appeared—he would prove that he dreaded that as little.\par
\par
The Count paused upon the offer, while the servants, who heard it,\par
looked upon one another in doubt and amazement, and Annette, terrified\par
for the safety of Ludovico, employed tears and entreaties to dissuade\par
him from his purpose.\par
\par
“You are a bold fellow,” said the Count, smiling, “Think well of what\par
you are going to encounter, before you finally determine upon it.\par
However, if you persevere in your resolution, I will accept your offer,\par
and your intrepidity shall not go unrewarded.”\par
\par
“I desire no reward, your _Excellenza_,” replied Ludovico, “but your\par
approbation. Your _Excellenza_ has been sufficiently good to me\par
already; but I wish to have arms, that I may be equal to my enemy, if\par
he should appear.”\par
\par
“Your sword cannot defend you against a ghost,” replied the Count,\par
throwing a glance of irony upon the other servants, “neither can bars,\par
nor bolts; for a spirit, you know, can glide through a keyhole as\par
easily as through a door.”\par
\par
“Give me a sword, my lord Count,” said Ludovico, “and I will lay all\par
the spirits, that shall attack me, in the red sea.”\par
\par
“Well,” said the Count, “you shall have a sword, and good cheer, too;\par
and your brave comrades here will, perhaps, have courage enough to\par
remain another night in the château, since your boldness will\par
certainly, for this night, at least, confine all the malice of the\par
spectre to yourself.”\par
\par
Curiosity now struggled with fear in the minds of several of his fellow\par
servants, and, at length, they resolved to await the event of\par
Ludovico’s rashness.\par
\par
Emily was surprised and concerned, when she heard of his intention, and\par
was frequently inclined to mention what she had witnessed in the north\par
apartments to the Count, for she could not entirely divest herself of\par
fears for Ludovico’s safety, though her reason represented these to be\par
absurd. The necessity, however, of concealing the secret, with which\par
Dorothée had entrusted her, and which must have been mentioned, with\par
the late occurrence, in excuse for her having so privately visited the\par
north apartments, kept her entirely silent on the subject of her\par
apprehension; and she tried only to sooth Annette, who held, that\par
Ludovico was certainly to be destroyed; and who was much less affected\par
by Emily’s consolatory efforts, than by the manner of old Dorothée, who\par
often, as she exclaimed Ludovico, sighed, and threw up her eyes to\par
heaven.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER VI\par
\par
Ye gods of quiet, and of sleep profound!\par
Whose soft dominion o’er this castle sways,\par
And all the widely-silent places round,\par
Forgive me, if my trembling pen displays\par
What never yet was sung in mortal lays.\par
                    THOMSON\par
\par
\par
The Count gave orders for the north apartments to be opened and\par
prepared for the reception of Ludovico; but Dorothée, remembering what\par
she had lately witnessed there, feared to obey, and, not one of the\par
other servants daring to venture thither, the rooms remained shut up\par
till the time when Ludovico was to retire thither for the night, an\par
hour, for which the whole household waited with impatience.\par
\par
After supper, Ludovico, by the order of the Count, attended him in his\par
closet, where they remained alone for near half an hour, and, on\par
leaving which, his Lord delivered to him a sword.\par
\par
“It has seen service in mortal quarrels,” said the Count, jocosely,\par
“you will use it honourably, no doubt, in a spiritual one. Tomorrow,\par
let me hear that there is not one ghost remaining in the château.”\par
\par
Ludovico received it with a respectful bow. “You shall be obeyed, my\par
Lord,” said he; “I will engage, that no spectre shall disturb the peace\par
of the château after this night.”\par
\par
They now returned to the supper-room, where the Count’s guests awaited\par
to accompany him and Ludovico to the door of the north apartments, and\par
Dorothée, being summoned for the keys, delivered them to Ludovico, who\par
then led the way, followed by most of the inhabitants of the château.\par
Having reached the back staircase, several of the servants shrunk back,\par
and refused to go further, but the rest followed him to the top of the\par
staircase, where a broad landing-place allowed them to flock round him,\par
while he applied the key to the door, during which they watched him\par
with as much eager curiosity as if he had been performing some magical\par
rite.\par
\par
Ludovico, unaccustomed to the lock, could not turn it, and Dorothée,\par
who had lingered far behind, was called forward, under whose hand the\par
door opened slowly, and, her eye glancing within the dusky chamber, she\par
uttered a sudden shriek, and retreated. At this signal of alarm, the\par
greater part of the crowd hurried down the stairs, and the Count, Henri\par
and Ludovico were left alone to pursue the enquiry, who instantly\par
rushed into the apartment, Ludovico with a drawn sword, which he had\par
just time to draw from the scabbard, the Count with the lamp in his\par
hand, and Henri carrying a basket, containing provisions for the\par
courageous adventurer.\par
\par
Having looked hastily round the first room, where nothing appeared to\par
justify alarm, they passed on to the second; and, here too all being\par
quiet, they proceeded to a third with a more tempered step. The Count\par
had now leisure to smile at the discomposure, into which he had been\par
surprised, and to ask Ludovico in which room he designed to pass the\par
night.\par
\par
“There are several chambers beyond these, your _Excellenza_,” said\par
Ludovico, pointing to a door, “and in one of them is a bed, they say. I\par
will pass the night there, and when I am weary of watching, I can lie\par
down.”\par
\par
“Good;” said the Count; “let us go on. You see these rooms show\par
nothing, but damp walls and decaying furniture. I have been so much\par
engaged since I came to the château, that I have not looked into them\par
till now. Remember, Ludovico, to tell the housekeeper, tomorrow, to\par
throw open these windows. The damask hangings are dropping to pieces, I\par
will have them taken down, and this antique furniture removed.”\par
\par
“Dear sir!” said Henri, “here is an arm-chair so massy with gilding,\par
that it resembles one of the state chairs at the Louvre, more then\par
anything else.”\par
\par
“Yes,” said the Count, stopping a moment to survey it, “there is a\par
history belonging to that chair, but I have not time to tell it.—Let us\par
pass on. This suite runs to a greater extent than I had imagined; it is\par
many years since I was in them. But where is the bedroom you speak of,\par
Ludovico?—these are only ante-chambers to the great drawing-room. I\par
remember them in their splendour!”\par
\par
“The bed, my Lord,” replied Ludovico, “they told me, was in a room that\par
opens beyond the saloon, and terminates the suite.”\par
\par
“O, here is the saloon,” said the Count, as they entered the spacious\par
apartment, in which Emily and Dorothée had rested. He here stood for a\par
moment, surveying the reliques of faded grandeur, which it\par
exhibited—the sumptuous tapestry—the long and low sophas of velvet,\par
with frames heavily carved and gilded—the floor inlaid with small\par
squares of fine marble, and covered in the centre with a piece of very\par
rich tapestry-work—the casements of painted glass, and the large\par
Venetian mirrors, of a size and quality, such as at that period France\par
could not make, which reflected, on every side, the spacious apartment.\par
These had formerly also reflected a gay and brilliant scene, for this\par
had been the state-room of the château, and here the Marchioness had\par
held the assemblies, that made part of the festivities of her nuptials.\par
If the wand of a magician could have recalled the vanished groups, many\par
of them vanished even from the earth, that once had passed over these\par
polished mirrors, what a varied and contrasted picture would they have\par
exhibited with the present! Now, instead of a blaze of lights, and a\par
splendid and busy crowd, they reflected only the rays of the one\par
glimmering lamp, which the Count held up, and which scarcely served to\par
show the three forlorn figures, that stood surveying the room, and the\par
spacious and dusky walls around them.\par
\par
“Ah!” said the Count to Henri, awaking from his deep reverie, “how the\par
scene is changed since last I saw it! I was a young man, then, and the\par
Marchioness was alive and in her bloom; many other persons were here,\par
too, who are now no more! There stood the orchestra; here we tripped in\par
many a sprightly maze—the walls echoing to the dance! Now, they resound\par
only one feeble voice—and even that will, ere long, be heard no more!\par
My son, remember, that I was once as young as yourself, and that you\par
must pass away like those, who have preceded you—like those, who, as\par
they sung and danced in this once gay apartment, forgot, that years are\par
made up of moments, and that every step they took carried them nearer\par
to their graves. But such reflections are useless, I had almost said\par
criminal, unless they teach us to prepare for eternity, since,\par
otherwise, they cloud our present happiness, without guiding us to a\par
future one. But enough of this; let us go on.”\par
\par
Ludovico now opened the door of the bedroom, and the Count, as he\par
entered, was struck with the funereal appearance which the dark arras\par
gave to it. He approached the bed with an emotion of solemnity, and,\par
perceiving it to be covered with the pall of black velvet, paused;\par
“What can this mean?” said he, as he gazed upon it.\par
\par
“I have heard, my Lord,” said Ludovico, as he stood at the feet,\par
looking within the canopied curtains, “that the Lady Marchioness de\par
Villeroi died in this chamber, and remained here till she was removed\par
to be buried; and this, perhaps, Signor, may account for the pall.”\par
\par
The Count made no reply, but stood for a few moments engaged in\par
thought, and evidently much affected. Then, turning to Ludovico, he\par
asked him with a serious air whether he thought his courage would\par
support him through the night? “If you doubt this,” added the Count,\par
“do not be ashamed to own it; I will release you from your engagement,\par
without exposing you to the triumphs of your fellow-servants.”\par
\par
Ludovico paused; pride, and something very like fear, seemed struggling\par
in his breast; pride, however, was victorious;—he blushed, and his\par
hesitation ceased.\par
\par
“No, my Lord,” said he, “I will go through with what I have begun; and\par
I am grateful for your consideration. On that hearth I will make a\par
fire, and, with the good cheer in this basket, I doubt not I shall do\par
well.”\par
\par
“Be it so,” said the Count; “but how will you beguile the tediousness\par
of the night, if you do not sleep?”\par
\par
“When I am weary, my Lord,” replied Ludovico, “I shall not fear to\par
sleep; in the meanwhile, I have a book that will entertain me.”\par
\par
“Well,” said the Count, “I hope nothing will disturb you; but if you\par
should be seriously alarmed in the night, come to my apartment. I have\par
too much confidence in your good sense and courage to believe you will\par
be alarmed on slight grounds; or suffer the gloom of this chamber, or\par
its remote situation, to overcome you with ideal terrors. Tomorrow, I\par
shall have to thank you for an important service; these rooms shall\par
then be thrown open, and my people will be convinced of their error.\par
Good night, Ludovico; let me see you early in the morning, and remember\par
what I lately said to you.”\par
\par
“I will, my Lord; good night to your _Excellenza_; let me attend you\par
with the light.”\par
\par
He lighted the Count and Henri through the chambers to the outer door.\par
On the landing-place stood a lamp, which one of the affrighted servants\par
had left, and Henri, as he took it up, again bade Ludovico good night,\par
who, having respectfully returned the wish, closed the door upon them,\par
and fastened it. Then, as he retired to the bed-chamber, he examined\par
the rooms through which he passed, with more minuteness than he had\par
done before, for he apprehended that some person might have concealed\par
himself in them, for the purpose of frightening him. No one, however,\par
but himself, was in these chambers, and leaving open the doors through\par
which he passed, he came again to the great drawing-room, whose\par
spaciousness and silent gloom somewhat awed him. For a moment he stood,\par
looking back through the long suite of rooms he had quitted, and as he\par
turned, perceiving a light and his own figure reflected in one of the\par
large mirrors, he started. Other objects too were seen obscurely on its\par
dark surface, but he paused not to examine them, and returned hastily\par
into the bedroom, as he surveyed which, he observed the door of the\par
oriel, and opened it. All within was still. On looking round, his eye\par
was arrested by the portrait of the deceased Marchioness, upon which he\par
gazed for a considerable time with great attention and some surprise;\par
and then, having examined the closet, he returned into the bedroom,\par
where he kindled a wood fire, the bright blaze of which revived his\par
spirits, which had begun to yield to the gloom and silence of the\par
place, for gusts of wind alone broke at intervals this silence. He now\par
drew a small table and a chair near the fire, took a bottle of wine and\par
some cold provision out of his basket, and regaled himself. When he had\par
finished his repast, he laid his sword upon the table, and, not feeling\par
disposed to sleep, drew from his pocket the book he had spoken of.—It\par
was a volume of old Provençal tales. Having stirred the fire into a\par
brighter blaze, he began to read, and his attention was soon wholly\par
occupied by the scenes which the page disclosed.\par
\par
The Count, meanwhile, had returned to the supper-room, whither those of\par
the party who had attended him to the north apartment had retreated,\par
upon hearing Dorothée’s scream, and who were now earnest in their\par
enquiries concerning those chambers. The Count rallied his guests on\par
their precipitate retreat, and on the superstitious inclination which\par
had occasioned it, and this led to the question, whether the spirit,\par
after it has quitted the body, is ever permitted to revisit the earth;\par
and if it is, whether it was possible for spirits to become visible to\par
the sense. The Baron was of opinion, that the first was probable, and\par
the last was possible, and he endeavoured to justify this opinion by\par
respectable authorities, both ancient and modern, which he quoted. The\par
Count, however, was decidedly against him, and a long conversation\par
ensued, in which the usual arguments on these subjects were on both\par
sides brought forward with skill, and discussed with candour, but\par
without converting either party to the opinion of his opponent. The\par
effect of their conversation on their auditors was various. Though the\par
Count had much the superiority of the Baron in point of argument, he\par
had considerably fewer adherents; for that love, so natural to the\par
human mind, of whatever is able to distend its faculties with wonder\par
and astonishment, attached the majority of the company to the side of\par
the Baron; and, though many of the Count’s propositions were\par
unanswerable, his opponents were inclined to believe this the\par
consequence of their own want of knowledge on so abstracted a subject,\par
rather than that arguments did not exist which were forcible enough to\par
conquer his.\par
\par
Blanche was pale with attention, till the ridicule in her father’s\par
glance called a blush upon her countenance, and she then endeavoured to\par
forget the superstitious tales she had been told in her convent.\par
Meanwhile, Emily had been listening with deep attention to the\par
discussion of what was to her a very interesting question, and,\par
remembering the appearance she had witnessed in the apartment of the\par
late Marchioness, she was frequently chilled with awe. Several times\par
she was on the point of mentioning what she had seen, but the fear of\par
giving pain to the Count, and the dread of his ridicule, restrained\par
her; and, awaiting in anxious expectation the event of Ludovico’s\par
intrepidity, she determined that her future silence should depend upon\par
it.\par
\par
When the party had separated for the night, and the Count retired to\par
his dressing-room, the remembrance of the desolate scenes he had lately\par
witnessed in his own mansion deeply affected him, but at length he was\par
aroused from his reverie and his silence. “What music is that I hear?”\par
said he suddenly to his valet, “Who plays at this late hour?”\par
\par
The man made no reply, and the Count continued to listen, and then\par
added, “That is no common musician; he touches the instrument with a\par
delicate hand; who is it, Pierre?”\par
\par
“My lord!” said the man, hesitatingly.\par
\par
“Who plays that instrument?” repeated the Count.\par
\par
“Does not your lordship know, then?” said the valet.\par
\par
“What mean you?” said the Count, somewhat sternly.\par
\par
“Nothing, my Lord, I meant nothing,” rejoined the man\par
submissively—“Only—that music—goes about the house at midnight often,\par
and I thought your lordship might have heard it before.”\par
\par
“Music goes about the house at midnight! Poor fellow!—does nobody dance\par
to the music, too?”\par
\par
“It is not in the château, I believe, my Lord; the sounds come from the\par
woods, they say, though they seem so near;—but then a spirit can do\par
anything!”\par
\par
“Ah, poor fellow!” said the Count, “I perceive you are as silly as the\par
rest of them; tomorrow you will be convinced of your ridiculous error.\par
But hark!—what voice is that?”\par
\par
“O my Lord! that is the voice we often hear with the music.”\par
\par
“Often!” said the Count, “How often, pray? It is a very fine one.”\par
\par
“Why, my Lord, I myself have not heard it more than two or three times,\par
but there are those who have lived here longer, that have heard it\par
often enough.”\par
\par
“What a swell was that!” exclaimed the Count, as he still listened,\par
“And now, what a dying cadence! This is surely something more than\par
mortal!”\par
\par
“That is what they say, my Lord,” said the valet; “they say it is\par
nothing mortal, that utters it; and if I might say my thoughts—”\par
\par
“Peace!” said the Count, and he listened till the strain died away.\par
\par
“This is strange!” said he, as he turned from the window, “Close the\par
casements, Pierre.”\par
\par
Pierre obeyed, and the Count soon after dismissed him, but did not so\par
soon lose the remembrance of the music, which long vibrated in his\par
fancy in tones of melting sweetness, while surprise and perplexity\par
engaged his thoughts.\par
\par
Ludovico, meanwhile, in his remote chamber, heard, now and then, the\par
faint echo of a closing door, as the family retired to rest, and then\par
the hall clock, at a great distance, strike twelve. “It is midnight,”\par
said he, and he looked suspiciously round the spacious chamber. The\par
fire on the hearth was now nearly expiring, for his attention having\par
been engaged by the book before him, he had forgotten everything\par
besides; but he soon added fresh wood, not because he was cold, though\par
the night was stormy, but because he was cheerless; and, having again\par
trimmed his lamp, he poured out a glass of wine, drew his chair nearer\par
to the crackling blaze, tried to be deaf to the wind, that howled\par
mournfully at the casements, endeavoured to abstract his mind from the\par
melancholy that was stealing upon him, and again took up his book. It\par
had been lent to him by Dorothée, who had formerly picked it up in an\par
obscure corner of the Marquis’s library, and who, having opened it and\par
perceived some of the marvels it related, had carefully preserved it\par
for her own entertainment, its condition giving her some excuse for\par
detaining it from its proper station. The damp corner into which it had\par
fallen had caused the cover to be disfigured and mouldy, and the leaves\par
to be so discoloured with spots, that it was not without difficulty the\par
letters could be traced. The fictions of the Provençal writers, whether\par
drawn from the Arabian legends, brought by the Saracens into Spain, or\par
recounting the chivalric exploits performed by the crusaders, whom the\par
Troubadors accompanied to the east, were generally splendid and always\par
marvellous, both in scenery and incident; and it is not wonderful, that\par
Dorothée and Ludovico should be fascinated by inventions, which had\par
captivated the careless imagination in every rank of society, in a\par
former age. Some of the tales, however, in the book now before\par
Ludovico, were of simple structure, and exhibited nothing of the\par
magnificent machinery and heroic manners, which usually characterised\par
the fables of the twelfth century, and of this description was the one\par
he now happened to open, which, in its original style, was of great\par
length, but which may be thus shortly related. The reader will perceive\par
that it is strongly tinctured with the superstition of the times.\par
\par
THE PROVENÇAL TALE\par
\par
\par
“There lived, in the province of Bretagne, a noble Baron, famous for\par
his magnificence and courtly hospitalities. His castle was graced with\par
ladies of exquisite beauty, and thronged with illustrious knights; for\par
the honour he paid to feats of chivalry invited the brave of distant\par
countries to enter his lists, and his court was more splendid than\par
those of many princes. Eight minstrels were retained in his service,\par
who used to sing to their harps romantic fictions, taken from the\par
Arabians, or adventures of chivalry, that befel knights during the\par
crusades, or the martial deeds of the Baron, their lord;—while he,\par
surrounded by his knights and ladies, banqueted in the great hall of\par
his castle, where the costly tapestry, that adorned the walls with\par
pictured exploits of his ancestors, the casements of painted glass,\par
enriched with armorial bearings, the gorgeous banners, that waved along\par
the roof, the sumptuous canopies, the profusion of gold and silver that\par
glittered on the sideboards, the numerous dishes, that covered the\par
tables, the number and gay liveries of the attendants, with the\par
chivalric and splendid attire of the guests, united to form a scene of\par
magnificence, such as we may not hope to see in these _degenerate\par
days_.\par
\par
“Of the Baron, the following adventure is related. One night, having\par
retired late from the banquet to his chamber, and dismissed his\par
attendants, he was surprised by the appearance of a stranger of a noble\par
air, but of a sorrowful and dejected countenance. Believing, that this\par
person had been secreted in the apartment, since it appeared impossible\par
he could have lately passed the ante-room, unobserved by the pages in\par
waiting, who would have prevented this intrusion on their lord, the\par
Baron, calling loudly for his people, drew his sword, which he had not\par
yet taken from his side, and stood upon his defence. The stranger\par
slowly advancing, told him, that there was nothing to fear; that he\par
came with no hostile design, but to communicate to him a terrible\par
secret, which it was necessary for him to know.\par
\par
“The Baron, appeased by the courteous manners of the stranger, after\par
surveying him, for some time, in silence, returned his sword into the\par
scabbard, and desired him to explain the means, by which he had\par
obtained access to the chamber, and the purpose of this extraordinary\par
visit.\par
\par
“Without answering either of these enquiries, the stranger said, that\par
he could not then explain himself, but that, if the Baron would follow\par
him to the edge of the forest, at a short distance from the castle\par
walls, he would there convince him, that he had something of importance\par
to disclose.\par
\par
“This proposal again alarmed the Baron, who could scarcely believe,\par
that the stranger meant to draw him to so solitary a spot, at this hour\par
of the night, without harbouring a design against his life, and he\par
refused to go, observing, at the same time, that, if the stranger’s\par
purpose was an honourable one, he would not persist in refusing to\par
reveal the occasion of his visit, in the apartment where they were.\par
\par
“While he spoke this, he viewed the stranger still more attentively\par
than before, but observed no change in his countenance, or any symptom,\par
that might intimate a consciousness of evil design. He was habited like\par
a knight, was of a tall and majestic stature, and of dignified and\par
courteous manners. Still, however, he refused to communicate the\par
subject of his errand in any place, but that he had mentioned, and, at\par
the same time, gave hints concerning the secret he would disclose, that\par
awakened a degree of solemn curiosity in the Baron, which, at length,\par
induced him to consent to follow the stranger on certain conditions.\par
\par
“‘Sir knight,’ said he, ‘I will attend you to the forest, and will take\par
with me only four of my people, who shall witness our conference.’\par
\par
“To this, however, the Knight objected.\par
\par
“‘What I would disclose,’ said he, with solemnity, ‘is to you alone.\par
There are only three living persons, to whom the circumstance is known;\par
it is of more consequence to you and your house, than I shall now\par
explain. In future years, you will look back to this night with\par
satisfaction or repentance, accordingly as you now determine. As you\par
would hereafter prosper—follow me; I pledge you the honour of a knight,\par
that no evil shall befall you;—if you are contented to dare\par
futurity—remain in your chamber, and I will depart as I came.’\par
\par
“‘Sir knight,’ replied the Baron, ‘how is it possible, that my future\par
peace can depend upon my present determination?’\par
\par
“‘That is not now to be told,’ said the stranger, ‘I have explained\par
myself to the utmost. It is late; if you follow me it must be\par
quickly;—you will do well to consider the alternative.’\par
\par
“The Baron mused, and, as he looked upon the knight, he perceived his\par
countenance assume a singular solemnity.”\par
\par
[Here Ludovico thought he heard a noise, and he threw a glance round\par
the chamber, and then held up the lamp to assist his observation; but,\par
not perceiving anything to confirm his alarm, he took up the book again\par
and pursued the story.]\par
\par
“The Baron paced his apartment, for some time, in silence, impressed by\par
the last words of the stranger, whose extraordinary request he feared\par
to grant, and feared, also, to refuse. At length, he said, ‘Sir knight,\par
you are utterly unknown to me; tell me yourself,—is it reasonable, that\par
I should trust myself alone with a stranger, at this hour, in a\par
solitary forest? Tell me, at least, who you are, and who assisted to\par
secrete you in this chamber.’\par
\par
“The knight frowned at these latter words, and was a moment silent;\par
then, with a countenance somewhat stern, he said,\par
\par
“‘I am an English knight; I am called Sir Bevys of Lancaster,—and my\par
deeds are not unknown at the Holy City, whence I was returning to my\par
native land, when I was benighted in the neighbouring forest.’\par
\par
“‘Your name is not unknown to fame,’ said the Baron, ‘I have heard of\par
it.’ (The Knight looked haughtily.) ‘But why, since my castle is known\par
to entertain all true knights, did not your herald announce you? Why\par
did you not appear at the banquet, where your presence would have been\par
welcomed, instead of hiding yourself in my castle, and stealing to my\par
chamber, at midnight?’\par
\par
“The stranger frowned, and turned away in silence; but the Baron\par
repeated the questions.\par
\par
“‘I come not,’ said the Knight, ‘to answer enquiries, but to reveal\par
facts. If you would know more, follow me, and again I pledge the honour\par
of a Knight that you shall return in safety.—Be quick in your\par
determination—I must be gone.’\par
\par
“After some further hesitation, the Baron determined to follow the\par
stranger, and to see the result of his extraordinary request; he,\par
therefore, again drew forth his sword, and, taking up a lamp, bade the\par
Knight lead on. The latter obeyed, and, opening the door of the\par
chamber, they passed into the ante-room, where the Baron, surprised to\par
find all his pages asleep, stopped, and, with hasty violence, was going\par
to reprimand them for their carelessness, when the Knight waved his\par
hand, and looked so expressively upon the Baron, that the latter\par
restrained his resentment, and passed on.\par
\par
“The Knight, having descended a staircase, opened a secret door, which\par
the Baron had believed was known only to himself, and, proceeding\par
through several narrow and winding passages, came, at length, to a\par
small gate, that opened beyond the walls of the castle. Meanwhile, the\par
Baron followed in silence and amazement, on perceiving that these\par
secret passages were so well known to a stranger, and felt inclined to\par
return from an adventure that appeared to partake of treachery, as well\par
as danger. Then, considering that he was armed, and observing the\par
courteous and noble air of his conductor, his courage returned, he\par
blushed, that it had failed him for a moment, and he resolved to trace\par
the mystery to its source.\par
\par
“He now found himself on the heathy platform, before the great gates of\par
his castle, where, on looking up, he perceived lights glimmering in the\par
different casements of the guests, who were retiring to sleep; and,\par
while he shivered in the blast, and looked on the dark and desolate\par
scene around him, he thought of the comforts of his warm chamber,\par
rendered cheerful by the blaze of wood, and felt, for a moment, the\par
full contrast of his present situation.”\par
\par
[Here Ludovico paused a moment, and, looking at his own fire, gave it a\par
brightening stir.]\par
\par
“The wind was strong, and the Baron watched his lamp with anxiety,\par
expecting every moment to see it extinguished; but, though the flame\par
wavered, it did not expire, and he still followed the stranger, who\par
often sighed as he went, but did not speak.\par
\par
“When they reached the borders of the forest, the Knight turned, and\par
raised his head, as if he meant to address the Baron, but then, closing\par
his lips in silence, he walked on.\par
\par
“As they entered beneath the dark and spreading boughs, the Baron,\par
affected by the solemnity of the scene, hesitated whether to proceed,\par
and demanded how much further they were to go. The Knight replied only\par
by a gesture, and the Baron, with hesitating steps and a suspicious\par
eye, followed through an obscure and intricate path, till, having\par
proceeded a considerable way, he again demanded whither they were\par
going, and refused to proceed unless he was informed.\par
\par
“As he said this, he looked at his own sword, and at the Knight\par
alternately, who shook his head, and whose dejected countenance\par
disarmed the Baron, for a moment, of suspicion.\par
\par
“‘A little further is the place, whither I would lead you,’ said the\par
stranger; ‘no evil shall befall you—I have sworn it on the honour of a\par
knight.’\par
\par
“The Baron, reassured, again followed in silence, and they soon arrived\par
at a deep recess of the forest, where the dark and lofty chesnuts\par
entirely excluded the sky, and which was so overgrown with underwood,\par
that they proceeded with difficulty. The Knight sighed deeply as he\par
passed, and sometimes paused; and having, at length, reached a spot,\par
where the trees crowded into a knot, he turned, and, with a terrific\par
look, pointing to the ground, the Baron saw there the body of a man,\par
stretched at its length, and weltering in blood; a ghastly wound was on\par
the forehead, and death appeared already to have contracted the\par
features.\par
\par
“The Baron, on perceiving the spectacle, started in horror, looked at\par
the Knight for explanation, and was then going to raise the body and\par
examine if there were yet any remains of life; but the stranger, waving\par
his hand, fixed upon him a look so earnest and mournful, as not only\par
much surprised him, but made him desist.\par
\par
“But, what were the Baron’s emotions, when, on holding the lamp near\par
the features of the corpse, he discovered the exact resemblance of the\par
stranger his conductor, to whom he now looked up in astonishment and\par
enquiry? As he gazed, he perceived the countenance of the Knight\par
change, and begin to fade, till his whole form gradually vanished from\par
his astonished sense! While the Baron stood, fixed to the spot, a voice\par
was heard to utter these words:—”\par
\par
[Ludovico started, and laid down the book, for he thought he heard a\par
voice in the chamber, and he looked toward the bed, where, however, he\par
saw only the dark curtains and the pall. He listened, scarcely daring\par
to draw his breath, but heard only the distant roaring of the sea in\par
the storm, and the blast, that rushed by the casements; when,\par
concluding, that he had been deceived by its sighings, he took up his\par
book to finish the story.]\par
\par
“While the Baron stood, fixed to the spot, a voice was heard to utter\par
these words:—*\par
\par
(*Note: This repetition seems to be intentional. Ludovico is picking up\par
the thread.)\par
\par
\par
“The body of Sir Bevys of Lancaster, a noble knight of England, lies\par
before you. He was, this night, waylaid and murdered, as he journeyed\par
from the Holy City towards his native land. Respect the honour of\par
knighthood and the law of humanity; inter the body in christian ground,\par
and cause his murderers to be punished. As ye observe, or neglect this,\par
shall peace and happiness, or war and misery, light upon you and your\par
house for ever!”\par
\par
“The Baron, when he recovered from the awe and astonishment, into which\par
this adventure had thrown him, returned to his castle, whither he\par
caused the body of Sir Bevys to be removed; and, on the following day,\par
it was interred, with the honours of knighthood, in the chapel of the\par
castle, attended by all the noble knights and ladies, who graced the\par
court of Baron de Brunne.”\par
\par
\par
\par
Ludovico, having finished this story, laid aside the book, for he felt\par
drowsy, and, after putting more wood on the fire and taking another\par
glass of wine, he reposed himself in the arm-chair on the hearth. In\par
his dream he still beheld the chamber where he really was, and, once or\par
twice, started from imperfect slumbers, imagining he saw a man’s face,\par
looking over the high back of his arm-chair. This idea had so strongly\par
impressed him, that, when he raised his eyes, he almost expected to\par
meet other eyes, fixed upon his own, and he quitted his seat and looked\par
behind the chair, before he felt perfectly convinced, that no person\par
was there.\par
\par
Thus closed the hour.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER VII\par
\par
Enjoy the honey-heavy dew of slumber;\par
Thou hast no figures, nor no fantasies,\par
Which busy care draws in the brains of men;\par
Therefore thou sleep’st so sound.\par
                    SHAKESPEARE\par
\par
\par
The Count, who had slept little during the night, rose early, and,\par
anxious to speak with Ludovico, went to the north apartment; but, the\par
outer door having been fastened, on the preceding night, he was obliged\par
to knock loudly for admittance. Neither the knocking, nor his voice was\par
heard; but, considering the distance of this door from the bedroom, and\par
that Ludovico, wearied with watching, had probably fallen into a deep\par
sleep, the Count was not surprised on receiving no answer, and, leaving\par
the door, he went down to walk in his grounds.\par
\par
It was a grey autumnal morning. The sun, rising over Provence, gave\par
only a feeble light, as his rays struggled through the vapours that\par
ascended from the sea, and floated heavily over the wood-tops, which\par
were now varied with many a mellow tint of autumn. The storm was\par
passed, but the waves were yet violently agitated, and their course was\par
traced by long lines of foam, while not a breeze fluttered in the sails\par
of the vessels, near the shore, that were weighing anchor to depart.\par
The still gloom of the hour was pleasing to the Count, and he pursued\par
his way through the woods, sunk in deep thought.\par
\par
Emily also rose at an early hour, and took her customary walk along the\par
brow of the promontory, that overhung the Mediterranean. Her mind was\par
now not occupied with the occurrences of the château, and Valancourt\par
was the subject of her mournful thoughts; whom she had not yet taught\par
herself to consider with indifference, though her judgment constantly\par
reproached her for the affection, that lingered in her heart, after her\par
esteem for him was departed. Remembrance frequently gave her his\par
parting look and the tones of his voice, when he had bade her a last\par
farewell; and, some accidental associations now recalling these\par
circumstances to her fancy, with peculiar energy, she shed bitter tears\par
to the recollection.\par
\par
Having reached the watch-tower, she seated herself on the broken steps,\par
and, in melancholy dejection, watched the waves, half hid in vapour, as\par
they came rolling towards the shore, and threw up their light spray\par
round the rocks below. Their hollow murmur and the obscuring mists,\par
that came in wreaths up the cliffs, gave a solemnity to the scene,\par
which was in harmony with the temper of her mind, and she sat, given up\par
to the remembrance of past times, till this became too painful, and she\par
abruptly quitted the place. On passing the little gate of the\par
watch-tower, she observed letters, engraved on the stone postern, which\par
she paused to examine, and, though they appeared to have been rudely\par
cut with a pen-knife, the characters were familiar to her; at length,\par
recognising the hand-writing of Valancourt, she read, with trembling\par
anxiety the following lines, entitled\par
\par
SHIPWRECK\par
\par
’Til solemn midnight!  On this lonely steep,\par
Beneath this watch-tow’r’s desolated wall,\par
Where mystic shapes the wonderer appall,\par
I rest; and view below the desert deep,\par
As through tempestuous clouds the moon’s cold light\par
Gleams on the wave.  Viewless, the winds of night\par
With loud mysterious force the billows sweep,\par
And sullen roar the surges, far below.\par
In the still pauses of the gust I hear\par
The voice of spirits, rising sweet and slow,\par
And oft among the clouds their forms appear.\par
But hark! what shriek of death comes in the gale,\par
And in the distant ray what glimmering sail\par
Bends to the storm?—Now sinks the note of fear!\par
Ah! wretched mariners!—no more shall day\par
Unclose his cheering eye to light ye on your way!\par
\par
\par
From these lines it appeared, that Valancourt had visited the tower;\par
that he had probably been here on the preceding night, for it was such\par
a one as they described, and that he had left the building very lately,\par
since it had not long been light, and without light it was impossible\par
these letters could have been cut. It was thus even probable, that he\par
might be yet in the gardens.\par
\par
As these reflections passed rapidly over the mind of Emily, they called\par
up a variety of contending emotions, that almost overcame her spirits;\par
but her first impulse was to avoid him, and, immediately leaving the\par
tower, she returned, with hasty steps, towards the château. As she\par
passed along, she remembered the music she had lately heard near the\par
tower, with the figure, which had appeared, and, in this moment of\par
agitation, she was inclined to believe, that she had then heard and\par
seen Valancourt; but other recollections soon convinced her of her\par
error. On turning into a thicker part of the woods, she perceived a\par
person, walking slowly in the gloom at some little distance, and, her\par
mind engaged by the idea of him, she started and paused, imagining this\par
to be Valancourt. The person advanced with quicker steps, and, before\par
she could recover recollection enough to avoid him, he spoke, and she\par
then knew the voice of the Count, who expressed some surprise, on\par
finding her walking at so early an hour, and made a feeble effort to\par
rally her on her love of solitude. But he soon perceived this to be\par
more a subject of concern than of light laughter, and, changing his\par
manner, affectionately expostulated with Emily, on thus indulging\par
unavailing regret; who, though she acknowledged the justness of all he\par
said, could not restrain her tears, while she did so, and he presently\par
quitted the topic. Expressing surprise at not having yet heard from his\par
friend, the Advocate at Avignon, in answer to the questions proposed to\par
him, respecting the estates of the late Madame Montoni, he, with\par
friendly zeal, endeavoured to cheer Emily with hopes of establishing\par
her claim to them; while she felt, that the estates could now\par
contribute little to the happiness of a life, in which Valancourt had\par
no longer an interest.\par
\par
When they returned to the château, Emily retired to her apartment, and\par
Count De Villefort to the door of the north chambers. This was still\par
fastened, but, being now determined to arouse Ludovico, he renewed his\par
calls more loudly than before, after which a total silence ensued, and\par
the Count, finding all his efforts to be heard ineffectual, at length\par
began to fear, that some accident had befallen Ludovico, whom terror of\par
an imaginary being might have deprived of his senses. He, therefore,\par
left the door with an intention of summoning his servants to force it\par
open, some of whom he now heard moving in the lower part of the\par
château.\par
\par
To the Count’s enquiries, whether they had seen or heard Ludovico, they\par
replied in affright, that not one of them had ventured on the north\par
side of the château, since the preceding night.\par
\par
“He sleeps soundly then,” said the Count, “and is at such a distance\par
from the outer door, which is fastened, that to gain admittance to the\par
chambers it will be necessary to force it. Bring an instrument, and\par
follow me.”\par
\par
The servants stood mute and dejected, and it was not till nearly all\par
the household were assembled, that the Count’s orders were obeyed. In\par
the mean time, Dorothée was telling of a door, that opened from a\par
gallery, leading from the great staircase into the last ante-room of\par
the saloon, and, this being much nearer to the bed-chamber, it appeared\par
probable, that Ludovico might be easily awakened by an attempt to open\par
it. Thither, therefore, the Count went, but his voice was as\par
ineffectual at this door as it had proved at the remoter one; and now,\par
seriously interested for Ludovico, he was himself going to strike upon\par
the door with the instrument, when he observed its singular beauty, and\par
withheld the blow. It appeared, on the first glance, to be of ebony, so\par
dark and close was its grain and so high its polish; but it proved to\par
be only of larch wood, of the growth of Provence, then famous for its\par
forests of larch. The beauty of its polished hue and of its delicate\par
carvings determined the Count to spare this door, and he returned to\par
that leading from the back staircase, which being, at length, forced,\par
he entered the first ante-room, followed by Henri and a few of the most\par
courageous of his servants, the rest awaiting the event of the enquiry\par
on the stairs and landing-place.\par
\par
All was silent in the chambers, through which the Count passed, and,\par
having reached the saloon, he called loudly upon Ludovico; after which,\par
still receiving no answer, he threw open the door of the bedroom, and\par
entered.\par
\par
The profound stillness within confirmed his apprehensions for Ludovico,\par
for not even the breathings of a person in sleep were heard; and his\par
uncertainty was not soon terminated, since the shutters being all\par
closed, the chamber was too dark for any object to be distinguished in\par
it.\par
\par
The Count bade a servant open them, who, as he crossed the room to do\par
so, stumbled over something, and fell to the floor, when his cry\par
occasioned such panic among the few of his fellows, who had ventured\par
thus far, that they instantly fled, and the Count and Henri were left\par
to finish the adventure.\par
\par
Henri then sprung across the room, and, opening a window-shutter, they\par
perceived, that the man had fallen over a chair near the hearth, in\par
which Ludovico had been sitting;—for he sat there no longer, nor could\par
anywhere be seen by the imperfect light, that was admitted into the\par
apartment. The Count, seriously alarmed, now opened other shutters,\par
that he might be enabled to examine further, and, Ludovico not yet\par
appearing, he stood for a moment, suspended in astonishment and\par
scarcely trusting his senses, till, his eyes glancing on the bed, he\par
advanced to examine whether he was there asleep. No person, however,\par
was in it, and he proceeded to the oriel, where everything remained as\par
on the preceding night, but Ludovico was nowhere to be found.\par
\par
The Count now checked his amazement, considering that Ludovico might\par
have left the chambers, during the night, overcome by the terrors,\par
which their lonely desolation and the recollected reports, concerning\par
them, had inspired. Yet, if this had been the fact, the man would\par
naturally have sought society, and his fellow servants had all declared\par
they had not seen him; the door of the outer room also had been found\par
fastened, with the key on the inside; it was impossible, therefore, for\par
him to have passed through that, and all the outer doors of this suite\par
were found, on examination, to be bolted and locked, with the keys also\par
within them. The Count, being then compelled to believe, that the lad\par
had escaped through the casements, next examined them, but such as\par
opened wide enough to admit the body of a man were found to be\par
carefully secured either by iron bars, or by shutters, and no vestige\par
appeared of any person having attempted to pass them; neither was it\par
probable, that Ludovico would have incurred the risk of breaking his\par
neck, by leaping from a window, when he might have walked safely\par
through a door.\par
\par
The Count’s amazement did not admit of words; but he returned once more\par
to examine the bedroom, where was no appearance of disorder, except\par
that occasioned by the late overthrow of the chair, near which had\par
stood a small table, and on this Ludovico’s sword, his lamp, the book\par
he had been reading, and the remnant of his flask of wine still\par
remained. At the foot of the table, too, was the basket with some\par
fragments of provision and wood.\par
\par
Henri and the servant now uttered their astonishment without reserve,\par
and, though the Count said little, there was a seriousness in his\par
manner, that expressed much. It appeared, that Ludovico must have\par
quitted these rooms by some concealed passage, for the Count could not\par
believe, that any supernatural means had occasioned this event, yet, if\par
there was any such passage, it seemed inexplicable why he should\par
retreat through it, and it was equally surprising, that not even the\par
smallest vestige should appear, by which his progress could be traced.\par
In the rooms everything remained as much in order as if he had just\par
walked out by the common way.\par
\par
The Count himself assisted in lifting the arras, with which the\par
bed-chamber, saloon and one of the ante-rooms were hung, that he might\par
discover if any door had been concealed behind it; but, after a\par
laborious search, none was found, and he, at length, quitted the\par
apartments, having secured the door of the last ante-chamber, the key\par
of which he took into his own possession. He then gave orders, that\par
strict search should be made for Ludovico not only in the château, but\par
in the neighbourhood, and, retiring with Henri to his closet, they\par
remained there in conversation for a considerable time, and whatever\par
was the subject of it, Henri from this hour lost much of his vivacity,\par
and his manners were particularly grave and reserved, whenever the\par
topic, which now agitated the Count’s family with wonder and alarm, was\par
introduced.\par
\par
On the disappearing of Ludovico, Baron St. Foix seemed strengthened in\par
all his former opinions concerning the probability of apparitions,\par
though it was difficult to discover what connection there could\par
possibly be between the two subjects, or to account for this effect\par
otherwise than by supposing, that the mystery attending Ludovico, by\par
exciting awe and curiosity, reduced the mind to a state of sensibility,\par
which rendered it more liable to the influence of superstition in\par
general. It is, however, certain, that from this period the Baron and\par
his adherents became more bigoted to their own systems than before,\par
while the terrors of the Count’s servants increased to an excess, that\par
occasioned many of them to quit the mansion immediately, and the rest\par
remained only till others could be procured to supply their places.\par
\par
The most strenuous search after Ludovico proved unsuccessful, and,\par
after several days of indefatigable enquiry, poor Annette gave herself\par
up to despair, and the other inhabitants of the château to amazement.\par
\par
Emily, whose mind had been deeply affected by the disastrous fate of\par
the late Marchioness and with the mysterious connection, which she\par
fancied had existed between her and St. Aubert, was particularly\par
impressed by the late extraordinary event, and much concerned for the\par
loss of Ludovico, whose integrity and faithful services claimed both\par
her esteem and gratitude. She was now very desirous to return to the\par
quiet retirement of her convent, but every hint of this was received\par
with real sorrow by the Lady Blanche, and affectionately set aside by\par
the Count, for whom she felt much of the respectful love and admiration\par
of a daughter, and to whom, by Dorothée’s consent, she, at length,\par
mentioned the appearance, which they had witnessed in the chamber of\par
the deceased Marchioness. At any other period, he would have smiled at\par
such a relation, and have believed, that its object had existed only in\par
the distempered fancy of the relater; but he now attended to Emily with\par
seriousness, and, when she concluded, requested of her a promise, that\par
this occurrence should rest in silence. “Whatever may be the cause and\par
the import of these extraordinary occurrences,” added the Count, “time\par
only can explain them. I shall keep a wary eye upon all that passes in\par
the château, and shall pursue every possible means of discovering the\par
fate of Ludovico. Meanwhile, we must be prudent and be silent. I will\par
myself watch in the north chambers, but of this we will say nothing,\par
till the night arrives, when I purpose doing so.”\par
\par
The Count then sent for Dorothée, and required of her also a promise of\par
silence, concerning what she had already, or might in future witness of\par
an extraordinary nature; and this ancient servant now related to him\par
the particulars of the Marchioness de Villeroi’s death, with some of\par
which he appeared to be already acquainted, while by others he was\par
evidently surprised and agitated. After listening to this narrative,\par
the Count retired to his closet, where he remained alone for several\par
hours; and, when he again appeared, the solemnity of his manner\par
surprised and alarmed Emily, but she gave no utterance to her thoughts.\par
\par
On the week following the disappearance of Ludovico, all the Count’s\par
guests took leave of him, except the Baron, his son Mons. St. Foix, and\par
Emily; the latter of whom was soon after embarrassed and distressed by\par
the arrival of another visitor, Mons. Du Pont, which made her determine\par
upon withdrawing to her convent immediately. The delight, that appeared\par
in his countenance, when he met her, told that he brought back the same\par
ardour of passion, which had formerly banished him from\par
Château-le-Blanc. He was received with reserve by Emily, and with\par
pleasure by the Count, who presented him to her with a smile, that\par
seemed intended to plead his cause, and who did not hope the less for\par
his friend, from the embarrassment she betrayed.\par
\par
But M. Du Pont, with truer sympathy, seemed to understand her manner,\par
and his countenance quickly lost its vivacity, and sunk into the\par
languor of despondency.\par
\par
On the following day, however, he sought an opportunity of declaring\par
the purport of his visit, and renewed his suit; a declaration, which\par
was received with real concern by Emily, who endeavoured to lessen the\par
pain she might inflict by a second rejection, with assurances of esteem\par
and friendship; yet she left him in a state of mind, that claimed and\par
excited her tenderest compassion; and, being more sensible than ever of\par
the impropriety of remaining longer at the château, she immediately\par
sought the Count, and communicated to him her intention of returning to\par
the convent.\par
\par
“My dear Emily,” said he, “I observe with extreme concern, the illusion\par
you are encouraging—an illusion common to young and sensible minds.\par
Your heart has received a severe shock; you believe you can never\par
entirely recover it, and you will encourage this belief, till the habit\par
of indulging sorrow will subdue the strength of your mind, and\par
discolour your future views with melancholy and regret. Let me\par
dissipate this illusion, and awaken you to a sense of your danger.”\par
\par
Emily smiled mournfully, “I know what you would say, my dear sir,” said\par
she, “and am prepared to answer you. I feel, that my heart can never\par
know a second affection; and that I must never hope even to recover its\par
tranquillity—if I suffer myself to enter into a second engagement.”\par
\par
“I know, that you feel all this,” replied the Count; “and I know, also,\par
that time will overcome these feelings, unless you cherish them in\par
solitude, and, pardon me, with romantic tenderness. Then, indeed, time\par
will only confirm habit. I am particularly empowered to speak on this\par
subject, and to sympathise in your sufferings,” added the Count, with\par
an air of solemnity, “for I have known what it is to love, and to\par
lament the object of my love. Yes,” continued he, while his eyes filled\par
with tears, “I have suffered!—but those times have passed away—long\par
passed! and I can now look back upon them without emotion.”\par
\par
“My dear sir,” said Emily, timidly, “what mean those tears?—they speak,\par
I fear, another language—they plead for me.”\par
\par
“They are weak tears, for they are useless ones,” replied the Count,\par
drying them, “I would have you superior to such weakness. These,\par
however, are only faint traces of a grief, which, if it had not been\par
opposed by long continued effort, might have led me to the verge of\par
madness! Judge, then, whether I have not cause to warn you of an\par
indulgence, which may produce so terrible an effect, and which must\par
certainly, if not opposed, overcloud the years, that otherwise might be\par
happy. M. Du Pont is a sensible and amiable man, who has long been\par
tenderly attached to you; his family and fortune are\par
unexceptionable;—after what I have said, it is unnecessary to add, that\par
I should rejoice in your felicity, and that I think M. Du Pont would\par
promote it. Do not weep, Emily,” continued the Count, taking her hand,\par
“there _is_ happiness reserved for you.”\par
\par
He was silent a moment; and then added, in a firmer voice, “I do not\par
wish, that you should make a violent effort to overcome your feelings;\par
all I, at present, ask, is, that you will check the thoughts, that\par
would lead you to a remembrance of the past; that you will suffer your\par
mind to be engaged by present objects; that you will allow yourself to\par
believe it possible you may yet be happy; and that you will sometimes\par
think with complacency of poor Du Pont, and not condemn him to the\par
state of despondency, from which, my dear Emily, I am endeavouring to\par
withdraw you.”\par
\par
“Ah! my dear sir,” said Emily, while her tears still fell, “do not\par
suffer the benevolence of your wishes to mislead Mons. Du Pont with an\par
expectation that I can ever accept his hand. If I understand my own\par
heart, this never can be; your instruction I can obey in almost every\par
other particular, than that of adopting a contrary belief.”\par
\par
“Leave me to understand your heart,” replied the Count, with a faint\par
smile. “If you pay me the compliment to be guided by my advice in other\par
instances, I will pardon your incredulity, respecting your future\par
conduct towards Mons. Du Pont. I will not even press you to remain\par
longer at the château than your own satisfaction will permit; but\par
though I forbear to oppose your present retirement, I shall urge the\par
claims of friendship for your future visits.”\par
\par
Tears of gratitude mingled with those of tender regret, while Emily\par
thanked the Count for the many instances of friendship she had received\par
from him; promised to be directed by his advice upon every subject but\par
one, and assured him of the pleasure, with which she should, at some\par
future period, accept the invitation of the Countess and himself—If\par
Mons. Du Pont was not at the château.\par
\par
The Count smiled at this condition. “Be it so,” said he, “meanwhile the\par
convent is so near the château, that my daughter and I shall often\par
visit you; and if, sometimes, we should dare to bring you another\par
visitor—will you forgive us?”\par
\par
Emily looked distressed, and remained silent.\par
\par
“Well,” rejoined the Count, “I will pursue this subject no further, and\par
must now entreat your forgiveness for having pressed it thus far. You\par
will, however, do me the justice to believe, that I have been urged\par
only by a sincere regard for your happiness, and that of my amiable\par
friend Mons. Du Pont.”\par
\par
Emily, when she left the Count, went to mention her intended departure\par
to the Countess, who opposed it with polite expressions of regret;\par
after which, she sent a note to acquaint the lady abbess, that she\par
should return to the convent; and thither she withdrew on the evening\par
of the following day. M. Du Pont, in extreme regret, saw her depart,\par
while the Count endeavoured to cheer him with a hope, that Emily would\par
sometimes regard him with a more favourable eye.\par
\par
She was pleased to find herself once more in the tranquil retirement of\par
the convent, where she experienced a renewal of all the maternal\par
kindness of the abbess, and of the sisterly attentions of the nuns. A\par
report of the late extraordinary occurrence at the château had already\par
reached them, and, after supper, on the evening of her arrival, it was\par
the subject of conversation in the convent parlour, where she was\par
requested to mention some particulars of that unaccountable event.\par
Emily was guarded in her conversation on this subject, and briefly\par
related a few circumstances concerning Ludovico, whose disappearance,\par
her auditors almost unanimously agreed, had been effected by\par
supernatural means.\par
\par
“A belief had so long prevailed,” said a nun, who was called sister\par
Frances, “that the château was haunted, that I was surprised, when I\par
heard the Count had the temerity to inhabit it. Its former possessor, I\par
fear, had some deed of conscience to atone for; let us hope, that the\par
virtues of its present owner will preserve him from the punishment due\par
to the errors of the last, if, indeed, he was a criminal.”\par
\par
“Of what crime, then, was he suspected?” said a Mademoiselle Feydeau, a\par
boarder at the convent.\par
\par
“Let us pray for his soul!” said a nun, who had till now sat in silent\par
attention. “If he was criminal, his punishment in this world was\par
sufficient.”\par
\par
There was a mixture of wildness and solemnity in her manner of\par
delivering this, which struck Emily exceedingly; but Mademoiselle\par
repeated her question, without noticing the solemn eagerness of the\par
nun.\par
\par
“I dare not presume to say what was his crime,” replied sister Frances;\par
“but I have heard many reports of an extraordinary nature, respecting\par
the late Marquis de Villeroi, and among others, that, soon after the\par
death of his lady, he quitted Château-le-Blanc, and never afterwards\par
returned to it. I was not here at the time, so I can only mention it\par
from report, and so many years have passed since the Marchioness died,\par
that few of our sisterhood, I believe, can do more.”\par
\par
“But I can,” said the nun, who had before spoke, and whom they called\par
sister Agnes.\par
\par
“You then,” said Mademoiselle Feydeau, “are possibly acquainted with\par
circumstances, that enable you to judge, whether he was criminal or\par
not, and what was the crime imputed to him.”\par
\par
“I am,” replied the nun; “but who shall dare to scrutinize my\par
thoughts—who shall dare to pluck out my opinion? God only is his judge,\par
and to that judge he is gone!”\par
\par
Emily looked with surprise at sister Frances, who returned her a\par
significant glance.\par
\par
“I only requested your opinion,” said Mademoiselle Feydeau, mildly; “if\par
the subject is displeasing to you, I will drop it.”\par
\par
“Displeasing!”—said the nun, with emphasis.—“We are idle talkers; we do\par
not weigh the meaning of the words we use; _displeasing_ is a poor\par
word. I will go pray.” As she said this she rose from her seat, and\par
with a profound sigh quitted the room.\par
\par
“What can be the meaning of this?” said Emily, when she was gone.\par
\par
“It is nothing extraordinary,” replied sister Frances, “she is often\par
thus; but she had no meaning in what she says. Her intellects are at\par
times deranged. Did you never see her thus before?”\par
\par
“Never,” said Emily. “I have, indeed, sometimes, thought, that there\par
was the melancholy of madness in her look, but never before perceived\par
it in her speech. Poor soul, I will pray for her!”\par
\par
“Your prayers then, my daughter, will unite with ours,” observed the\par
lady abbess, “she has need of them.”\par
\par
“Dear lady,” said Mademoiselle Feydeau, addressing the abbess, “what is\par
your opinion of the late Marquis? The strange circumstances, that have\par
occurred at the château, have so much awakened my curiosity, that I\par
shall be pardoned the question. What was his imputed crime, and what\par
the punishment, to which sister Agnes alluded?”\par
\par
“We must be cautious of advancing our opinion,” said the abbess, with\par
an air of reserve, mingled with solemnity, “we must be cautious of\par
advancing our opinion on so delicate a subject. I will not take upon me\par
to pronounce, that the late Marquis was criminal, or to say what was\par
the crime of which he was suspected; but, concerning the punishment our\par
daughter Agnes hinted, I know of none he suffered. She probably alluded\par
to the severe one, which an exasperated conscience can inflict. Beware,\par
my children, of incurring so terrible a punishment—it is the purgatory\par
of this life! The late Marchioness I knew well; she was a pattern to\par
such as live in the world; nay, our sacred order need not have blushed\par
to copy her virtues! Our holy convent received her mortal part; her\par
heavenly spirit, I doubt not, ascended to its sanctuary!”\par
\par
As the abbess spoke this, the last bell of vespers struck up, and she\par
rose. “Let us go, my children,” said she, “and intercede for the\par
wretched; let us go and confess our sins, and endeavour to purify our\par
souls for the heaven, to which _she_ is gone!”\par
\par
Emily was affected by the solemnity of this exhortation, and,\par
remembering her father, “The heaven, to which _he_, too, is gone!” said\par
she, faintly, as she suppressed her sighs, and followed the abbess and\par
the nuns to the chapel.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER VIII\par
\par
Be thou a spirit of health, or goblin damn’d,\par
Bring with thee airs from heaven, or blasts from hell,\par
Be thy intents wicked, or charitable,\par
\par
I will speak to thee.\par
                    HAMLET\par
\par
\par
Count de Villefort, at length, received a letter from the advocate at\par
Avignon, encouraging Emily to assert her claim to the estates of the\par
late Madame Montoni; and, about the same time, a messenger arrived from\par
Monsieur Quesnel with intelligence, that made an appeal to the law on\par
this subject unnecessary, since it appeared, that the only person, who\par
could have opposed her claim, was now no more. A friend of Monsieur\par
Quesnel, who resided at Venice, had sent him an account of the death of\par
Montoni who had been brought to trial with Orsino, as his supposed\par
accomplice in the murder of the Venetian nobleman. Orsino was found\par
guilty, condemned and executed upon the wheel, but, nothing being\par
discovered to criminate Montoni, and his colleagues, on this charge,\par
they were all released, except Montoni, who, being considered by the\par
senate as a very dangerous person, was, for other reasons, ordered\par
again into confinement, where, it was said, he had died in a doubtful\par
and mysterious manner, and not without suspicion of having been\par
poisoned. The authority, from which M. Quesnel had received this\par
information, would not allow him to doubt its truth, and he told Emily,\par
that she had now only to lay claim to the estates of her late aunt, to\par
secure them, and added, that he would himself assist in the necessary\par
forms of this business. The term, for which La Vallée had been let\par
being now also nearly expired, he acquainted her with the circumstance,\par
and advised her to take the road thither, through Thoulouse, where he\par
promised to meet her, and where it would be proper for her to take\par
possession of the estates of the late Madame Montoni; adding, that he\par
would spare her any difficulties, that might occur on that occasion\par
from the want of knowledge on the subject, and that he believed it\par
would be necessary for her to be at Thoulouse, in about three weeks\par
from the present time.\par
\par
An increase of fortune seemed to have awakened this sudden kindness in\par
M. Quesnel towards his niece, and it appeared, that he entertained more\par
respect for the rich heiress, than he had ever felt compassion for the\par
poor and unfriended orphan.\par
\par
The pleasure, with which she received this intelligence, was clouded\par
when she considered, that he, for whose sake she had once regretted the\par
want of fortune, was no longer worthy of sharing it with her; but,\par
remembering the friendly admonition of the Count, she checked this\par
melancholy reflection, and endeavoured to feel only gratitude for the\par
unexpected good, that now attended her; while it formed no\par
inconsiderable part of her satisfaction to know, that La Vallée, her\par
native home, which was endeared to her by it’s having been the\par
residence of her parents, would soon be restored to her possession.\par
There she meant to fix her future residence, for, though it could not\par
be compared with the château at Thoulouse, either for extent, or\par
magnificence, its pleasant scenes and the tender remembrances, that\par
haunted them, had claims upon her heart, which she was not inclined to\par
sacrifice to ostentation. She wrote immediately to thank M. Quesnel for\par
the active interest he took in her concerns, and to say, that she would\par
meet him at Thoulouse at the appointed time.\par
\par
When Count de Villefort, with Blanche, came to the convent to give\par
Emily the advice of the advocate, he was informed of the contents of M.\par
Quesnel’s letter, and gave her his sincere congratulations, on the\par
occasion; but she observed, that, when the first expression of\par
satisfaction had faded from his countenance, an unusual gravity\par
succeeded, and she scarcely hesitated to enquire its cause.\par
\par
“It has no new occasion,” replied the Count; “I am harassed and\par
perplexed by the confusion, into which my family is thrown by their\par
foolish superstition. Idle reports are floating round me, which I can\par
neither admit to be true, nor prove to be false; and I am, also, very\par
anxious about the poor fellow, Ludovico, concerning whom I have not\par
been able to obtain information. Every part of the château and every\par
part of the neighbourhood, too, has, I believe, been searched, and I\par
know not what further can be done, since I have already offered large\par
rewards for the discovery of him. The keys of the north apartment I\par
have not suffered to be out of my possession, since he disappeared, and\par
I mean to watch in those chambers, myself, this very night.”\par
\par
Emily, seriously alarmed for the Count, united her entreaties with\par
those of the Lady Blanche, to dissuade him from his purpose.\par
\par
“What should I fear?” said he. “I have no faith in supernatural\par
combats, and for human opposition I shall be prepared; nay, I will even\par
promise not to watch alone.”\par
\par
“But who, dear sir, will have courage enough to watch with you?” said\par
Emily.\par
\par
“My son,” replied the Count. “If I am not carried off in the night,”\par
added he, smiling, “you shall hear the result of my adventure,\par
tomorrow.”\par
\par
The Count and Lady Blanche, shortly afterwards, took leave of Emily,\par
and returned to the château, where he informed Henri of his intention,\par
who, not without some secret reluctance, consented to be the partner of\par
his watch; and, when the design was mentioned after supper, the\par
Countess was terrified, and the Baron, and M. Du Pont joined with her\par
in entreating, that he would not tempt his fate, as Ludovico had done.\par
“We know not,” added the Baron, “the nature, or the power of an evil\par
spirit; and that such a spirit haunts those chambers can now, I think,\par
scarcely be doubted. Beware, my lord, how you provoke its vengeance,\par
since it has already given us one terrible example of its malice. I\par
allow it may be probable, that the spirits of the dead are permitted to\par
return to the earth only on occasions of high import; but the present\par
import may be your destruction.”\par
\par
The Count could not forbear smiling; “Do you think then, Baron,” said\par
he, “that my destruction is of sufficient importance to draw back to\par
earth the soul of the departed? Alas! my good friend, there is no\par
occasion for such means to accomplish the destruction of any\par
individual. Wherever the mystery rests, I trust I shall, this night, be\par
able to detect it. You know I am not superstitious.”\par
\par
“I know that you are incredulous,” interrupted the Baron.\par
\par
“Well, call it what you will, I mean to say, that, though you know I am\par
free from superstition—if anything supernatural has appeared, I doubt\par
not it will appear to me, and if any strange event hangs over my house,\par
or if any extraordinary transaction has formerly been connected with\par
it, I shall probably be made acquainted with it. At all events I will\par
invite discovery; and, that I may be equal to a mortal attack, which in\par
good truth, my friend, is what I most expect, I shall take care to be\par
well armed.”\par
\par
The Count took leave of his family, for the night, with an assumed\par
gaiety, which but ill concealed the anxiety, that depressed his\par
spirits, and retired to the north apartments, accompanied by his son\par
and followed by the Baron, M. Du Pont and some of the domestics, who\par
all bade him good night at the outer door. In these chambers everything\par
appeared as when he had last been here; even in the bedroom no\par
alteration was visible, where he lighted his own fire, for none of the\par
domestics could be prevailed upon to venture thither. After carefully\par
examining the chamber and the oriel, the Count and Henri drew their\par
chairs upon the hearth, set a bottle of wine and a lamp before them,\par
laid their swords upon the table, and, stirring the wood into a blaze,\par
began to converse on indifferent topics. But Henri was often silent and\par
abstracted, and sometimes threw a glance of mingled awe and curiosity\par
round the gloomy apartment; while the Count gradually ceased to\par
converse, and sat either lost in thought, or reading a volume of\par
Tacitus, which he had brought to beguile the tediousness of the night.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER IX\par
\par
Give thy thoughts no tongue.\par
                    SHAKESPEARE\par
\par
\par
The Baron St. Foix, whom anxiety for his friend had kept awake, rose\par
early to enquire the event of the night, when, as he passed the Count’s\par
closet, hearing steps within, he knocked at the door, and it was opened\par
by his friend himself. Rejoicing to see him in safety, and curious to\par
learn the occurrences of the night, he had not immediately leisure to\par
observe the unusual gravity, that overspread the features of the Count,\par
whose reserved answers first occasioned him to notice it. The Count,\par
then smiling, endeavoured to treat the subject of his curiosity with\par
levity, but the Baron was serious, and pursued his enquiries so\par
closely, that the Count, at length, resuming his gravity, said, “Well,\par
my friend, press the subject no further, I entreat you; and let me\par
request also, that you will hereafter be silent upon anything you may\par
think extraordinary in my future conduct. I do not scruple to tell you,\par
that I am unhappy, and that the watch of the last night has not\par
assisted me to discover Ludovico; upon every occurrence of the night\par
you must excuse my reserve.”\par
\par
“But where is Henri?” said the Baron, with surprise and disappointment\par
at this denial.\par
\par
“He is well in his own apartment,” replied the Count. “You will not\par
question him on this topic, my friend, since you know my wish.”\par
\par
“Certainly not,” said the Baron, somewhat chagrined, “since it would be\par
displeasing to you; but methinks, my friend, you might rely on my\par
discretion, and drop this unusual reserve. However, you must allow me\par
to suspect, that you have seen reason to become a convert to my system,\par
and are no longer the incredulous knight you lately appeared to be.”\par
\par
“Let us talk no more upon this subject,” said the Count; “you may be\par
assured, that no ordinary circumstance has imposed this silence upon me\par
towards a friend, whom I have called so for near thirty years; and my\par
present reserve cannot make you question either my esteem, or the\par
sincerity of my friendship.”\par
\par
“I will not doubt either,” said the Baron, “though you must allow me to\par
express my surprise, at this silence.”\par
\par
“To me I will allow it,” replied the Count, “but I earnestly entreat\par
that you will forbear to notice it to my family, as well as everything\par
remarkable you may observe in my conduct towards them.”\par
\par
The Baron readily promised this, and, after conversing for some time on\par
general topics, they descended to the breakfast-room, where the Count\par
met his family with a cheerful countenance, and evaded their enquiries\par
by employing light ridicule, and assuming an air of uncommon gaiety,\par
while he assured them, that they need not apprehend any evil from the\par
north chambers, since Henri and himself had been permitted to return\par
from them in safety.\par
\par
Henri, however, was less successful in disguising his feelings. From\par
his countenance an expression of terror was not entirely faded; he was\par
often silent and thoughtful, and when he attempted to laugh at the\par
eager enquiries of Mademoiselle Bearn, it was evidently only an\par
attempt.\par
\par
In the evening, the Count called, as he had promised, at the convent,\par
and Emily was surprised to perceive a mixture of playful ridicule and\par
of reserve in his mention of the north apartment. Of what had occurred\par
there, however, he said nothing, and, when she ventured to remind him\par
of his promise to tell her the result of his enquiries, and to ask if\par
he had received any proof, that those chambers were haunted, his look\par
became solemn, for a moment, then, seeming to recollect himself, he\par
smiled, and said, “My dear Emily, do not suffer my lady abbess to\par
infect your good understanding with these fancies; she will teach you\par
to expect a ghost in every dark room. But believe me,” added he, with a\par
profound sigh, “the apparition of the dead comes not on light, or\par
sportive errands, to terrify, or to surprise the timid.” He paused, and\par
fell into a momentary thoughtfulness, and then added, “We will say no\par
more on this subject.”\par
\par
Soon after, he took leave, and, when Emily joined some of the nuns, she\par
was surprised to find them acquainted with a circumstance, which she\par
had carefully avoided to mention, and expressing their admiration of\par
his intrepidity in having dared to pass a night in the apartment,\par
whence Ludovico had disappeared; for she had not considered with what\par
rapidity a tale of wonder circulates. The nuns had acquired their\par
information from peasants, who brought fruit to the monastery, and\par
whose whole attention had been fixed, since the disappearance of\par
Ludovico, on what was passing in the castle.\par
\par
Emily listened in silence to the various opinions of the nuns,\par
concerning the conduct of the Count, most of whom condemned it as rash\par
and presumptuous, affirming, that it was provoking the vengeance of an\par
evil spirit, thus to intrude upon its haunts.\par
\par
Sister Frances contended, that the Count had acted with the bravery of\par
a virtuous mind. He knew himself guiltless of aught, that should\par
provoke a good spirit, and did not fear the spells of an evil one,\par
since he could claim the protection of a higher Power, of Him, who can\par
command the wicked, and will protect the innocent.\par
\par
“The guilty cannot claim that protection!” said sister Agnes, “let the\par
Count look to his conduct, that he do not forfeit his claim! Yet who is\par
he, that shall dare to call himself innocent!—all earthly innocence is\par
but comparative. Yet still how wide asunder are the extremes of guilt,\par
and to what a horrible depth may we fall! Oh!—”\par
\par
The nun, as she concluded, uttered a shuddering sigh, that startled\par
Emily, who, looking up, perceived the eyes of Agnes fixed on hers,\par
after which the sister rose, took her hand, gazed earnestly upon her\par
countenance, for some moments, in silence, and then said,\par
\par
“You are young—you are innocent! I mean you are yet innocent of any\par
great crime!—But you have passions in your heart,—scorpions; they sleep\par
now—beware how you awaken them!—they will sting you, even unto death!”\par
\par
Emily, affected by these words and by the solemnity, with which they\par
were delivered, could not suppress her tears.\par
\par
“Ah! is it so?” exclaimed Agnes, her countenance softening from its\par
sternness—“so young, and so unfortunate! We are sisters, then indeed.\par
Yet, there is no bond of kindness among the guilty,” she added, while\par
her eyes resumed their wild expression, “no gentleness,—no peace, no\par
hope! I knew them all once—my eyes could weep—but now they burn, for\par
now, my soul is fixed, and fearless!—I lament no more!”\par
\par
“Rather let us repent, and pray,” said another nun. “We are taught to\par
hope, that prayer and penitence will work our salvation. There is hope\par
for all who repent!”\par
\par
“Who repent and turn to the true faith,” observed sister Frances.\par
\par
“For all but me!” replied Agnes solemnly, who paused, and then abruptly\par
added, “My head burns, I believe I am not well. O! could I strike from\par
my memory all former scenes—the figures, that rise up, like furies, to\par
torment me!—I see them, when I sleep, and, when I am awake, they are\par
still before my eyes! I see them now—now!”\par
\par
She stood in a fixed attitude of horror, her straining eyes moving\par
slowly round the room, as if they followed something. One of the nuns\par
gently took her hand, to lead her from the parlour. Agnes became calm,\par
drew her other hand across her eyes, looked again, and, sighing deeply,\par
said, “They are gone—they are gone! I am feverish, I know not what I\par
say. I am thus, sometimes, but it will go off again, I shall soon be\par
better. Was not that the vesper-bell?”\par
\par
“No,” replied Frances, “the evening service is passed. Let Margaret\par
lead you to your cell.”\par
\par
“You are right,” replied sister Agnes, “I shall be better there. Good\par
night, my sisters, remember me in your orisons.”\par
\par
When they had withdrawn, Frances, observing Emily’s emotion, said, “Do\par
not be alarmed, our sister is often thus deranged, though I have not\par
lately seen her so frantic; her usual mood is melancholy. This fit has\par
been coming on, for several days; seclusion and the customary treatment\par
will restore her.”\par
\par
“But how rationally she conversed, at first!” observed Emily, “her\par
ideas followed each other in perfect order.”\par
\par
“Yes,” replied the nun, “this is nothing new; nay, I have sometimes\par
known her argue not only with method, but with acuteness, and then, in\par
a moment, start off into madness.”\par
\par
“Her conscience seems afflicted,” said Emily, “did you ever hear what\par
circumstance reduced her to this deplorable condition?”\par
\par
“I have,” replied the nun, who said no more till Emily repeated the\par
question, when she added in a low voice, and looking significantly\par
towards the other boarders, “I cannot tell you now, but, if you think\par
it worth your while, come to my cell, tonight, when our sisterhood are\par
at rest, and you shall hear more; but remember we rise to midnight\par
prayers, and come either before, or after midnight.”\par
\par
Emily promised to remember, and, the abbess soon after appearing, they\par
spoke no more of the unhappy nun.\par
\par
The Count meanwhile, on his return home, had found M. Du Pont in one of\par
those fits of despondency, which his attachment to Emily frequently\par
occasioned him, an attachment, that had subsisted too long to be easily\par
subdued, and which had already outlived the opposition of his friends.\par
M. Du Pont had first seen Emily in Gascony, during the lifetime of his\par
parent, who, on discovering his son’s partiality for Mademoiselle St.\par
Aubert, his inferior in point of fortune, forbade him to declare it to\par
her family, or to think of her more. During the life of his father, he\par
had observed the first command, but had found it impracticable to obey\par
the second, and had, sometimes, soothed his passion by visiting her\par
favourite haunts, among which was the fishing-house, where, once or\par
twice, he addressed her in verse, concealing his name, in obedience to\par
the promise he had given his father. There too he played the pathetic\par
air, to which she had listened with such surprise and admiration; and\par
there he found the miniature, that had since cherished a passion fatal\par
to his repose. During his expedition into Italy, his father died; but\par
he received his liberty at a moment, when he was the least enabled to\par
profit by it, since the object, that rendered it most valuable, was no\par
longer within the reach of his vows. By what accident he discovered\par
Emily, and assisted to release her from a terrible imprisonment, has\par
already appeared, and also the unavailing hope, with which he then\par
encouraged his love, and the fruitless efforts, that he had since made\par
to overcome it.\par
\par
The Count still endeavoured, with friendly zeal, to sooth him with a\par
belief, that patience, perseverance and prudence would finally obtain\par
for him happiness and Emily: “Time,” said he, “will wear away the\par
melancholy impression, which disappointment has left on her mind, and\par
she will be sensible of your merit. Your services have already awakened\par
her gratitude, and your sufferings her pity; and trust me, my friend,\par
in a heart so sensible as hers, gratitude and pity lead to love. When\par
her imagination is rescued from its present delusion, she will readily\par
accept the homage of a mind like yours.”\par
\par
Du Pont sighed, while he listened to these words; and, endeavouring to\par
hope what his friend believed, he willingly yielded to an invitation to\par
prolong his visit at the château, which we now leave for the monastery\par
of St. Claire.\par
\par
When the nuns had retired to rest, Emily stole to her appointment with\par
sister Frances, whom she found in her cell, engaged in prayer, before a\par
little table, where appeared the image she was addressing, and, above,\par
the dim lamp that gave light to the place. Turning her eyes, as the\par
door opened, she beckoned to Emily to come in, who, having done so,\par
seated herself in silence beside the nun’s little mattress of straw,\par
till her orisons should conclude. The latter soon rose from her knees,\par
and, taking down the lamp and placing it on the table, Emily perceived\par
there a human skull and bones, lying beside an hour-glass; but the nun,\par
without observing her emotion, sat down on the mattress by her, saying,\par
“Your curiosity, sister, has made you punctual, but you have nothing\par
remarkable to hear in the history of poor Agnes, of whom I avoided to\par
speak in the presence of my lay-sisters, only because I would not\par
publish her crime to them.”\par
\par
“I shall consider your confidence in me as a favour,” said Emily, “and\par
will not misuse it.”\par
\par
“Sister Agnes,” resumed the nun, “is of a noble family, as the dignity\par
of her air must already have informed you, but I will not dishonour\par
their name so much as to reveal it. Love was the occasion of her crime\par
and of her madness. She was beloved by a gentleman of inferior fortune,\par
and her father, as I have heard, bestowing her on a nobleman, whom she\par
disliked, an ill-governed passion proved her destruction.—Every\par
obligation of virtue and of duty was forgotten, and she prophaned her\par
marriage vows; but her guilt was soon detected, and she would have\par
fallen a sacrifice to the vengeance of her husband, had not her father\par
contrived to convey her from his power. By what means he did this, I\par
never could learn; but he secreted her in this convent, where he\par
afterwards prevailed with her to take the veil, while a report was\par
circulated in the world, that she was dead, and the father, to save his\par
daughter, assisted the rumour, and employed such means as induced her\par
husband to believe she had become a victim to his jealousy. You look\par
surprised,” added the nun, observing Emily’s countenance; “I allow the\par
story is uncommon, but not, I believe, without a parallel.”\par
\par
“Pray proceed,” said Emily, “I am interested.”\par
\par
“The story is already told,” resumed the nun, “I have only to mention,\par
that the long struggle, which Agnes suffered, between love, remorse and\par
a sense of the duties she had taken upon herself in becoming of our\par
order, at length unsettled her reason. At first, she was frantic and\par
melancholy by quick alternatives; then, she sunk into a deep and\par
settled melancholy, which still, however, has, at times, been\par
interrupted by fits of wildness, and, of late, these have again been\par
frequent.”\par
\par
Emily was affected by the history of the sister, some parts of whose\par
story brought to her remembrance that of the Marchioness de Villeroi,\par
who had also been compelled by her father to forsake the object of her\par
affections, for a nobleman of his choice; but, from what Dorothée had\par
related, there appeared no reason to suppose, that she had escaped the\par
vengeance of a jealous husband, or to doubt for a moment the innocence\par
of her conduct. But Emily, while she sighed over the misery of the nun,\par
could not forbear shedding a few tears to the misfortunes of the\par
Marchioness; and, when she returned to the mention of sister Agnes, she\par
asked Frances if she remembered her in her youth, and whether she was\par
then beautiful.\par
\par
“I was not here at the time, when she took the vows,” replied Frances,\par
“which is so long ago, that few of the present sisterhood, I believe,\par
were witnesses of the ceremony; nay, ever our lady mother did not then\par
preside over the convent: but I can remember, when sister Agnes was a\par
very beautiful woman. She retains that air of high rank, which always\par
distinguished her, but her beauty, you must perceive, is fled; I can\par
scarcely discover even a vestige of the loveliness, that once animated\par
her features.”\par
\par
“It is strange,” said Emily, “but there are moments, when her\par
countenance has appeared familiar to my memory! You will think me\par
fanciful, and I think myself so, for I certainly never saw sister\par
Agnes, before I came to this convent, and I must, therefore, have seen\par
some person, whom she strongly resembles, though of this I have no\par
recollection.”\par
\par
“You have been interested by the deep melancholy of her countenance,”\par
said Frances, “and its impression has probably deluded your\par
imagination; for I might as reasonably think I perceive a likeness\par
between you and Agnes, as you, that you have seen her anywhere but in\par
this convent, since this has been her place of refuge, for nearly as\par
many years as make your age.”\par
\par
“Indeed!” said Emily.\par
\par
“Yes,” rejoined Frances, “and why does that circumstance excite your\par
surprise?”\par
\par
Emily did not appear to notice this question, but remained thoughtful,\par
for a few moments, and then said, “It was about that same period that\par
the Marchioness de Villeroi expired.”\par
\par
“That is an odd remark,” said Frances.\par
\par
Emily, recalled from her reverie, smiled, and gave the conversation\par
another turn, but it soon came back to the subject of the unhappy nun,\par
and Emily remained in the cell of sister Frances, till the midnight\par
bell aroused her; when, apologising for having interrupted the sister’s\par
repose, till this late hour, they quitted the cell together. Emily\par
returned to her chamber, and the nun, bearing a glimmering taper, went\par
to her devotion in the chapel.\par
\par
Several days followed, during which Emily saw neither the Count, nor\par
any of his family; and, when, at length, he appeared, she remarked,\par
with concern, that his air was unusually disturbed.\par
\par
“My spirits are harassed,” said he, in answer to her anxious enquiries,\par
“and I mean to change my residence, for a little while, an experiment,\par
which, I hope, will restore my mind to its usual tranquillity. My\par
daughter and myself will accompany the Baron St. Foix to his château.\par
It lies in a valley of the Pyrenees, that opens towards Gascony, and I\par
have been thinking, Emily, that, when you set out for La Vallée, we may\par
go part of the way together; it would be a satisfaction to me to guard\par
you towards your home.”\par
\par
She thanked the Count for his friendly consideration, and lamented,\par
that the necessity for her going first to Thoulouse would render this\par
plan impracticable. “But, when you are at the Baron’s residence,” she\par
added, “you will be only a short journey from La Vallée, and I think,\par
sir, you will not leave the country without visiting me; it is\par
unnecessary to say with what pleasure I should receive you and the Lady\par
Blanche.”\par
\par
“I do not doubt it,” replied the Count, “and I will not deny myself and\par
Blanche the pleasure of visiting you, if your affairs should allow you\par
to be at La Vallée, about the time when we can meet you there.”\par
\par
When Emily said that she should hope to see the Countess also, she was\par
not sorry to learn that this lady was going, accompanied by\par
Mademoiselle Bearn, to pay a visit, for a few weeks, to a family in\par
lower Languedoc.\par
\par
The Count, after some further conversation on his intended journey and\par
on the arrangement of Emily’s, took leave; and many days did not\par
succeed this visit, before a second letter from M. Quesnel informed\par
her, that he was then at Thoulouse, that La Vallée was at liberty, and\par
that he wished her to set off for the former place, where he awaited\par
her arrival, with all possible dispatch, since his own affairs pressed\par
him to return to Gascony. Emily did not hesitate to obey him, and,\par
having taken an affecting leave of the Count’s family, in which M. Du\par
Pont was still included, and of her friends at the convent, she set out\par
for Thoulouse, attended by the unhappy Annette, and guarded by a steady\par
servant of the Count.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER X\par
\par
Lull’d in the countless chambers of the brain,\par
Our thoughts are link’d by many a hidden chain:\par
Awake but one, and lo! what myriads rise!\par
Each stamps its image as the other flies!\par
                    PLEASURES OF MEMORY\par
\par
\par
Emily pursued her journey, without any accident, along the plains of\par
Languedoc towards the north-west; and, on this her return to Thoulouse,\par
which she had last left with Madame Montoni, she thought much on the\par
melancholy fate of her aunt, who, but for her own imprudence, might now\par
have been living in happiness there! Montoni, too, often rose to her\par
fancy, such as she had seen him in his days of triumph, bold, spirited\par
and commanding; such also as she had since beheld him in his days of\par
vengeance; and now, only a few short months had passed—and he had no\par
longer the power, or the will to afflict;—he had become a clod of\par
earth, and his life was vanished like a shadow! Emily could have wept\par
at his fate, had she not remembered his crimes; for that of her\par
unfortunate aunt she did weep, and all sense of her errors was overcome\par
by the recollection of her misfortunes.\par
\par
Other thoughts and other emotions succeeded, as Emily drew near the\par
well-known scenes of her early love, and considered, that Valancourt\par
was lost to her and to himself, for ever. At length, she came to the\par
brow of the hill, whence, on her departure for Italy, she had given a\par
farewell look to this beloved landscape, amongst whose woods and fields\par
she had so often walked with Valancourt, and where he was then to\par
inhabit, when she would be far, far away! She saw, once more, that\par
chain of the Pyrenees, which overlooked La Vallée, rising, like faint\par
clouds, on the horizon. “There, too, is Gascony, extended at their\par
feet!” said she, “O my father,—my mother! And there, too, is the\par
Garonne!” she added, drying the tears, that obscured her sight,—“and\par
Thoulouse, and my aunt’s mansion—and the groves in her garden!—O my\par
friends! are ye all lost to me—must I never, never see ye more!” Tears\par
rushed again to her eyes, and she continued to weep, till an abrupt\par
turn in the road had nearly occasioned the carriage to overset, when,\par
looking up, she perceived another part of the well-known scene around\par
Thoulouse, and all the reflections and anticipations, which she had\par
suffered, at the moment, when she bade it last adieu, came with\par
recollected force to her heart. She remembered how anxiously she had\par
looked forward to the futurity, which was to decide her happiness\par
concerning Valancourt, and what depressing fears had assailed her; the\par
very words she had uttered, as she withdrew her last look from the\par
prospect, came to her memory. “Could I but be certain,” she had then\par
said, “that I should ever return, and that Valancourt would still live\par
for me—I should go in peace!”\par
\par
Now, that futurity, so anxiously anticipated, was arrived, she was\par
returned—but what a dreary blank appeared!—Valancourt no longer lived\par
for her! She had no longer even the melancholy satisfaction of\par
contemplating his image in her heart, for he was no longer the same\par
Valancourt she had cherished there—the solace of many a mournful hour,\par
the animating friend, that had enabled her to bear up against the\par
oppression of Montoni—the distant hope, that had beamed over her gloomy\par
prospect! On perceiving this beloved idea to be an illusion of her own\par
creation, Valancourt seemed to be annihilated, and her soul sickened at\par
the blank, that remained. His marriage with a rival, even his death,\par
she thought she could have endured with more fortitude, than this\par
discovery; for then, amidst all her grief, she could have looked in\par
secret upon the image of goodness, which her fancy had drawn of him,\par
and comfort would have mingled with her suffering!\par
\par
Drying her tears, she looked, once more, upon the landscape, which had\par
excited them, and perceived, that she was passing the very bank, where\par
she had taken leave of Valancourt, on the morning of her departure from\par
Thoulouse, and she now saw him, through her returning tears, such as he\par
had appeared, when she looked from the carriage to give him a last\par
adieu—saw him leaning mournfully against the high trees, and remembered\par
the fixed look of mingled tenderness and anguish, with which he had\par
then regarded her. This recollection was too much for her heart, and\par
she sunk back in the carriage, nor once looked up, till it stopped at\par
the gates of what was now her own mansion.\par
\par
These being opened, and by the servant, to whose care the château had\par
been entrusted, the carriage drove into the court, where, alighting,\par
she hastily passed through the great hall, now silent and solitary, to\par
a large oak parlour, the common sitting room of the late Madame\par
Montoni, where, instead of being received by M. Quesnel, she found a\par
letter from him, informing her that business of consequence had obliged\par
him to leave Thoulouse two days before. Emily was, upon the whole, not\par
sorry to be spared his presence, since his abrupt departure appeared to\par
indicate the same indifference, with which he had formerly regarded\par
her. This letter informed her, also, of the progress he had made in the\par
settlement of her affairs, and concluded with directions, concerning\par
the forms of some business, which remained for her to transact. But M.\par
Quesnel’s unkindness did not long occupy her thoughts, which returned\par
the remembrance of the persons she had been accustomed to see in this\par
mansion, and chiefly of the ill-guided and unfortunate Madame Montoni.\par
In the room, where she now sat, she had breakfasted with her on the\par
morning of their departure for Italy; and the view of it brought most\par
forcibly to her recollection all she had herself suffered, at that\par
time, and the many gay expectations, which her aunt had formed,\par
respecting the journey before her. While Emily’s mind was thus engaged,\par
her eyes wandered unconsciously to a large window, that looked upon the\par
garden, and here new memorials of the past spoke to her heart, for she\par
saw extended before her the very avenue, in which she had parted with\par
Valancourt, on the eve of her journey; and all the anxiety, the tender\par
interest he had shown, concerning her future happiness, his earnest\par
remonstrances against her committing herself to the power of Montoni,\par
and the truth of his affection, came afresh to her memory. At this\par
moment, it appeared almost impossible, that Valancourt could have\par
become unworthy of her regard, and she doubted all that she had lately\par
heard to his disadvantage, and even his own words, which had confirmed\par
Count De Villefort’s report of him. Overcome by the recollections,\par
which the view of this avenue occasioned, she turned abruptly from the\par
window, and sunk into a chair beside it, where she sat, given up to\par
grief, till the entrance of Annette, with coffee, aroused her.\par
\par
“Dear madam, how melancholy this place looks now,” said Annette, “to\par
what it used to do! It is dismal coming home, when there is nobody to\par
welcome one!”\par
\par
This was not the moment, in which Emily could bear the remark; her\par
tears fell again, and, as soon as she had taken the coffee, she retired\par
to her apartment, where she endeavoured to repose her fatigued spirits.\par
But busy memory would still supply her with the visions of former\par
times: she saw Valancourt interesting and benevolent, as he had been\par
wont to appear in the days of their early love, and, amidst the scenes,\par
where she had believed that they should sometimes pass their years\par
together!—but, at length, sleep closed these afflicting scenes from her\par
view.\par
\par
On the following morning, serious occupation recovered her from such\par
melancholy reflections; for, being desirous of quitting Thoulouse, and\par
of hastening on to La Vallée, she made some enquiries into the\par
condition of the estate, and immediately dispatched a part of the\par
necessary business concerning it, according to the directions of Mons.\par
Quesnel. It required a strong effort to abstract her thoughts from\par
other interests sufficiently to attend to this, but she was rewarded\par
for her exertions by again experiencing, that employment is the surest\par
antidote to sorrow.\par
\par
This day was devoted entirely to business; and, among other concerns,\par
she employed means to learn the situation of all her poor tenants, that\par
she might relieve their wants, or confirm their comforts.\par
\par
In the evening, her spirits were so much strengthened, that she thought\par
she could bear to visit the gardens, where she had so often walked with\par
Valancourt; and, knowing, that, if she delayed to do so, their scenes\par
would only affect her the more, whenever they should be viewed, she\par
took advantage of the present state of her mind, and entered them.\par
\par
Passing hastily the gate leading from the court into the gardens, she\par
hurried up the great avenue, scarcely permitting her memory to dwell\par
for a moment on the circumstance of her having here parted with\par
Valancourt, and soon quitted this for other walks less interesting to\par
her heart. These brought her, at length, to the flight of steps, that\par
led from the lower garden to the terrace, on seeing which, she became\par
agitated, and hesitated whether to ascend, but, her resolution\par
returning, she proceeded.\par
\par
“Ah!” said Emily, as she ascended, “these are the same high trees, that\par
used to wave over the terrace, and these the same flowery thickets—the\par
liburnum, the wild rose, and the cerinthe—which were wont to grow\par
beneath them! Ah! and there, too, on that bank, are the very plants,\par
which Valancourt so carefully reared!—O, when last I saw them!”—she\par
checked the thought, but could not restrain her tears, and, after\par
walking slowly on for a few moments, her agitation, upon the view of\par
this well-known scene, increased so much, that she was obliged to stop,\par
and lean upon the wall of the terrace. It was a mild, and beautiful\par
evening. The sun was setting over the extensive landscape, to which his\par
beams, sloping from beneath a dark cloud, that overhung the west, gave\par
rich and partial colouring, and touched the tufted summits of the\par
groves, that rose from the garden below, with a yellow gleam. Emily and\par
Valancourt had often admired together this scene, at the same hour; and\par
it was exactly on this spot, that, on the night preceding her departure\par
for Italy, she had listened to his remonstrances against the journey,\par
and to the pleadings of passionate affection. Some observations, which\par
she made on the landscape, brought this to her remembrance, and with it\par
all the minute particulars of that conversation;—the alarming doubts he\par
had expressed concerning Montoni, doubts, which had since been fatally\par
confirmed; the reasons and entreaties he had employed to prevail with\par
her to consent to an immediate marriage; the tenderness of his love,\par
the paroxysms of this grief, and the conviction that he had repeatedly\par
expressed, that they should never meet again in happiness! All these\par
circumstances rose afresh to her mind, and awakened the various\par
emotions she had then suffered. Her tenderness for Valancourt became as\par
powerful as in the moments, when she thought, that she was parting with\par
him and happiness together, and when the strength of her mind had\par
enabled her to triumph over present suffering, rather than to deserve\par
the reproach of her conscience by engaging in a clandestine\par
marriage.—“Alas!” said Emily, as these recollections came to her mind,\par
“and what have I gained by the fortitude I then practised?—am I happy\par
now?—He said, we should meet no more in happiness; but, O! he little\par
thought his own misconduct would separate us, and lead to the very evil\par
he then dreaded!”\par
\par
Her reflections increased her anguish, while she was compelled to\par
acknowledge, that the fortitude she had formerly exerted, if it had not\par
conducted her to happiness, had saved her from irretrievable\par
misfortune—from Valancourt himself! But in these moments she could not\par
congratulate herself on the prudence, that had saved her; she could\par
only lament, with bitterest anguish, the circumstances, which had\par
conspired to betray Valancourt into a course of life so different from\par
that, which the virtues, the tastes, and the pursuits of his early\par
years had promised; but she still loved him too well to believe, that\par
his heart was even now depraved, though his conduct had been criminal.\par
An observation, which had fallen from M. St. Aubert more than once, now\par
occurred to her. “This young man,” said he, speaking of Valancourt,\par
“has never been at Paris;” a remark, that had surprised her at the time\par
it was uttered, but which she now understood, and she exclaimed\par
sorrowfully, “O Valancourt! if such a friend as my father had been with\par
you at Paris—your noble, ingenuous nature would not have fallen!”\par
\par
The sun was now set, and, recalling her thoughts from their melancholy\par
subject, she continued her walk; for the pensive shade of twilight was\par
pleasing to her, and the nightingales from the surrounding groves began\par
to answer each other in the long-drawn, plaintive note, which always\par
touched her heart; while all the fragrance of the flowery thickets,\par
that bounded the terrace, was awakened by the cool evening air, which\par
floated so lightly among their leaves, that they scarcely trembled as\par
it passed.\par
\par
Emily came, at length, to the steps of the pavilion, that terminated\par
the terrace, and where her last interview with Valancourt, before her\par
departure from Thoulouse, had so unexpectedly taken place. The door was\par
now shut, and she trembled, while she hesitated whether to open it; but\par
her wish to see again a place, which had been the chief scene of her\par
former happiness, at length overcoming her reluctance to encounter the\par
painful regret it would renew, she entered. The room was obscured by a\par
melancholy shade; but through the open lattices, darkened by the\par
hanging foliage of the vines, appeared the dusky landscape, the Garonne\par
reflecting the evening light, and the west still glowing. A chair was\par
placed near one of the balconies, as if some person had been sitting\par
there, but the other furniture of the pavilion remained exactly as\par
usual, and Emily thought it looked as if it had not once been moved\par
since she set out for Italy. The silent and deserted air of the place\par
added solemnity to her emotions, for she heard only the low whisper of\par
the breeze, as it shook the leaves of the vines, and the very faint\par
murmur of the Garonne.\par
\par
She seated herself in a chair, near the lattice, and yielded to the\par
sadness of her heart, while she recollected the circumstances of her\par
parting interview with Valancourt, on this spot. It was here too, that\par
she had passed some of the happiest hours of her life with him, when\par
her aunt favoured the connection, for here she had often sat and\par
worked, while he conversed, or read; and she now well remembered with\par
what discriminating judgment, with what tempered energy, he used to\par
repeat some of the sublimest passages of their favourite authors; how\par
often he would pause to admire with her their excellence, and with what\par
tender delight he would listen to her remarks, and correct her taste.\par
\par
“And is it possible,” said Emily, as these recollections returned—“is\par
it possible, that a mind, so susceptible of whatever is grand and\par
beautiful, could stoop to low pursuits, and be subdued by frivolous\par
temptations?”\par
\par
She remembered how often she had seen the sudden tear start in his eye,\par
and had heard his voice tremble with emotion, while he related any\par
great or benevolent action, or repeated a sentiment of the same\par
character. “And such a mind,” said she, “such a heart, were to be\par
sacrificed to the habits of a great city!”\par
\par
These recollections becoming too painful to be endured, she abruptly\par
left the pavilion, and, anxious to escape from the memorials of her\par
departed happiness, returned towards the château. As she passed along\par
the terrace, she perceived a person, walking, with a slow step, and a\par
dejected air, under the trees, at some distance. The twilight, which\par
was now deep, would not allow her to distinguish who it was, and she\par
imagined it to be one of the servants, till, the sound of her steps\par
seeming to reach him, he turned half round, and she thought she saw\par
Valancourt!\par
\par
Whoever it was, he instantly struck among the thickets on the left, and\par
disappeared, while Emily, her eyes fixed on the place, whence he had\par
vanished, and her frame trembling so excessively, that she could\par
scarcely support herself, remained, for some moments, unable to quit\par
the spot, and scarcely conscious of existence. With her recollection,\par
her strength returned, and she hurried toward the house, where she did\par
not venture to enquire who had been in the gardens, lest she should\par
betray her emotion; and she sat down alone, endeavouring to recollect\par
the figure, air and features of the person she had just seen. Her view\par
of him, however, had been so transient, and the gloom had rendered it\par
so imperfect, that she could remember nothing with exactness; yet the\par
general appearance of his figure, and his abrupt departure, made her\par
still believe, that this person was Valancourt. Sometimes, indeed, she\par
thought, that her fancy, which had been occupied by the idea of him,\par
had suggested his image to her uncertain sight: but this conjecture was\par
fleeting. If it was himself whom she had seen, she wondered much, that\par
he should be at Thoulouse, and more, how he had gained admittance into\par
the garden; but as often as her impatience prompted her to enquire\par
whether any stranger had been admitted, she was restrained by an\par
unwillingness to betray her doubts; and the evening was passed in\par
anxious conjecture, and in efforts to dismiss the subject from her\par
thoughts. But, these endeavours were ineffectual, and a thousand\par
inconsistent emotions assailed her, whenever she fancied that\par
Valancourt might be near her; now, she dreaded it to be true, and now\par
she feared it to be false; and, while she constantly tried to persuade\par
herself, that she wished the person, whom she had seen, might not be\par
Valancourt, her heart as constantly contradicted her reason.\par
\par
The following day was occupied by the visits of several neighbouring\par
families, formerly intimate with Madame Montoni, who came to condole\par
with Emily on her death, to congratulate her upon the acquisition of\par
these estates, and to enquire about Montoni, and concerning the strange\par
reports they had heard of her own situation; all which was done with\par
the utmost decorum, and the visitors departed with as much composure as\par
they had arrived.\par
\par
Emily was wearied by these formalities, and disgusted by the\par
subservient manners of many persons, who had thought her scarcely\par
worthy of common attention, while she was believed to be a dependant on\par
Madame Montoni.\par
\par
“Surely,” said she, “there is some magic in wealth, which can thus make\par
persons pay their court to it, when it does not even benefit\par
themselves. How strange it is, that a fool or a knave, with riches,\par
should be treated with more respect by the world, than a good man, or a\par
wise man in poverty!”\par
\par
It was evening, before she was left alone, and she then wished to have\par
refreshed her spirits in the free air of her garden; but she feared to\par
go thither, lest she should meet again the person, whom she had seen on\par
the preceding night, and he should prove to be Valancourt. The suspense\par
and anxiety she suffered, on this subject, she found all her efforts\par
unable to control, and her secret wish to see Valancourt once more,\par
though unseen by him, powerfully prompted her to go, but prudence and a\par
delicate pride restrained her, and she determined to avoid the\par
possibility of throwing herself in his way, by forbearing to visit the\par
gardens, for several days.\par
\par
When, after near a week, she again ventured thither, she made Annette\par
her companion, and confined her walk to the lower grounds, but often\par
started as the leaves rustled in the breeze, imagining, that some\par
person was among the thickets; and, at the turn of every alley, she\par
looked forward with apprehensive expectation. She pursued her walk\par
thoughtfully and silently, for her agitation would not suffer her to\par
converse with Annette, to whom, however, thought and silence were so\par
intolerable, that she did not scruple at length to talk to her\par
mistress.\par
\par
“Dear madam,” said she, “why do you start so? one would think you knew\par
what has happened.”\par
\par
“What has happened?” said Emily, in a faltering voice, and trying to\par
command her emotion.\par
\par
“The night before last, you know, madam—”\par
\par
“I know nothing, Annette,” replied her lady in a more hurried voice.\par
\par
“The night before last, madam, there was a robber in the garden.”\par
\par
“A robber!” said Emily, in an eager, yet doubting tone.\par
\par
“I suppose he was a robber, madam. What else could he be?”\par
\par
“Where did you see him, Annette?” rejoined Emily, looking round her,\par
and turning back towards the château.\par
\par
“It was not I that saw him, madam, it was Jean the gardener. It was\par
twelve o’clock at night, and, as he was coming across the court to go\par
the back way into the house, what should he see—but somebody walking in\par
the avenue, that fronts the garden gate! So, with that, Jean guessed\par
how it was, and he went into the house for his gun.”\par
\par
“His gun!” exclaimed Emily.\par
\par
“Yes, madam, his gun; and then he came out into the court to watch him.\par
Presently, he sees him come slowly down the avenue, and lean over the\par
garden gate, and look up at the house for a long time; and I warrant he\par
examined it well, and settled what window he should break in at.”\par
\par
“But the gun,” said Emily—“the gun!”\par
\par
“Yes, madam, all in good time. Presently, Jean says, the robber opened\par
the gate, and was coming into the court, and then he thought proper to\par
ask him his business: so he called out again, and bade him say who he\par
was, and what he wanted. But the man would do neither; but turned upon\par
his heel, and passed into the garden again. Jean knew then well enough\par
how it was, and so he fired after him.”\par
\par
“Fired!” exclaimed Emily.\par
\par
“Yes, madam, fired off his gun; but, Holy Virgin! what makes you look\par
so pale, madam? The man was not killed,—I dare say; but if he was, his\par
comrades carried him off: for, when Jean went in the morning, to look\par
for the body, it was gone, and nothing to be seen but a track of blood\par
on the ground. Jean followed it, that he might find out where the man\par
got into the garden, but it was lost in the grass, and—”\par
\par
Annette was interrupted: for Emily’s spirits died away, and she would\par
have fallen to the ground, if the girl had not caught her, and\par
supported her to a bench, close to them.\par
\par
When, after a long absence, her senses returned, Emily desired to be\par
led to her apartment; and, though she trembled with anxiety to enquire\par
further on the subject of her alarm, she found herself too ill at\par
present, to dare the intelligence which it was possible she might\par
receive of Valancourt. Having dismissed Annette, that she might weep\par
and think at liberty, she endeavoured to recollect the exact air of the\par
person, whom she had seen on the terrace, and still her fancy gave her\par
the figure of Valancourt. She had, indeed, scarcely a doubt, that it\par
was he whom she had seen, and at whom the gardener had fired: for the\par
manner of the latter person, as described by Annette, was not that of a\par
robber; nor did it appear probable, that a robber would have come\par
alone, to break into a house so spacious as this.\par
\par
When Emily thought herself sufficiently recovered, to listen to what\par
Jean might have to relate, she sent for him; but he could inform her of\par
no circumstance, that might lead to a knowledge of the person, who had\par
been shot, or of the consequence of the wound; and, after severely\par
reprimanding him, for having fired with bullets, and ordering diligent\par
enquiry to be made in the neighbourhood for the discovery of the\par
wounded person, she dismissed him, and herself remained in the same\par
state of terrible suspense. All the tenderness she had ever felt for\par
Valancourt, was recalled by the sense of his danger; and the more she\par
considered the subject, the more her conviction strengthened, that it\par
was he, who had visited the gardens, for the purpose of soothing the\par
misery of disappointed affection, amidst the scenes of his former\par
happiness.\par
\par
“Dear madam,” said Annette, when she returned, “I never saw you so\par
affected before! I dare say the man is not killed.”\par
\par
Emily shuddered, and lamented bitterly the rashness of the gardener in\par
having fired.\par
\par
“I knew you would be angry enough about that, madam, or I should have\par
told you before; and he knew so too; for, says he, ‘Annette, say\par
nothing about this to my lady. She lies on the other side of the house,\par
so did not hear the gun, perhaps; but she would be angry with me, if\par
she knew, seeing there is blood. But then,’ says he, ‘how is one to\par
keep the garden clear, if one is afraid to fire at a robber, when one\par
sees him?’”\par
\par
“No more of this,” said Emily, “pray leave me.”\par
\par
Annette obeyed, and Emily returned to the agonizing considerations,\par
that had assailed her before, but which she, at length, endeavoured to\par
sooth by a new remark. If the stranger was Valancourt, it was certain\par
he had come alone, and it appeared, therefore, that he had been able to\par
quit the gardens, without assistance; a circumstance which did not seem\par
probable, had his wound been dangerous. With this consideration, she\par
endeavoured to support herself, during the enquiries, that were making\par
by her servants in the neighbourhood; but day after day came, and still\par
closed in uncertainty, concerning this affair: and Emily, suffering in\par
silence, at length, drooped, and sunk under the pressure of her\par
anxiety. She was attacked by a slow fever, and when she yielded to the\par
persuasion of Annette to send for medical advice, the physicians\par
prescribed little beside air, gentle exercise and amusement: but how\par
was this last to be obtained? She, however, endeavoured to abstract her\par
thoughts from the subject of her anxiety, by employing them in\par
promoting that happiness in others, which she had lost herself; and,\par
when the evening was fine, she usually took an airing, including in her\par
ride the cottages of some of her tenants, on whose condition she made\par
such observations, as often enabled her, unasked, to fulfil their\par
wishes.\par
\par
Her indisposition and the business she engaged in, relative to this\par
estate, had already protracted her stay at Thoulouse, beyond the period\par
she had formerly fixed for her departure to La Vallée; and now she was\par
unwilling to leave the only place, where it seemed possible, that\par
certainty could be obtained on the subject of her distress. But the\par
time was come, when her presence was necessary at La Vallée, a letter\par
from the Lady Blanche now informing her, that the Count and herself,\par
being then at the château of the Baron St. Foix, purposed to visit her\par
at La Vallée, on their way home, as soon as they should be informed of\par
her arrival there. Blanche added, that they made this visit, with the\par
hope of inducing her to return with them to Château-le-Blanc.\par
\par
Emily, having replied to the letter of her friend, and said that she\par
should be at La Vallée in a few days, made hasty preparations for the\par
journey; and, in thus leaving Thoulouse, endeavoured to support herself\par
with a belief, that, if any fatal accident had happened to Valancourt,\par
she must in this interval have heard of it.\par
\par
On the evening before her departure, she went to take leave of the\par
terrace and the pavilion. The day had been sultry, but a light shower,\par
that fell just before sunset, had cooled the air, and given that soft\par
verdure to the woods and pastures, which is so refreshing to the eye;\par
while the rain drops, still trembling on the shrubs, glittered in the\par
last yellow gleam, that lighted up the scene, and the air was filled\par
with fragrance, exhaled by the late shower, from herbs and flowers and\par
from the earth itself. But the lovely prospect, which Emily beheld from\par
the terrace, was no longer viewed by her with delight; she sighed\par
deeply as her eye wandered over it, and her spirits were in a state of\par
such dejection, that she could not think of her approaching return to\par
La Vallée, without tears, and seemed to mourn again the death of her\par
father, as if it had been an event of yesterday. Having reached the\par
pavilion, she seated herself at the open lattice, and, while her eyes\par
settled on the distant mountains, that overlooked Gascony, still\par
gleaming on the horizon, though the sun had now left the plains below,\par
“Alas!” said she, “I return to your long-lost scenes, but shall meet no\par
more the parents, that were wont to render them delightful!—no more\par
shall see the smile of welcome, or hear the well-known voice of\par
fondness:—all will now be cold and silent in what was once my happy\par
home.”\par
\par
Tears stole down her cheek, as the remembrance of what that home had\par
been, returned to her; but, after indulging her sorrow for some time,\par
she checked it, accusing herself of ingratitude in forgetting the\par
friends, that she possessed, while she lamented those that were\par
departed; and she, at length, left the pavilion and the terrace,\par
without having observed a shadow of Valancourt or of any other person.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XI\par
\par
Ah happy hills! ah pleasing shade!\par
Ah fields belov’d in vain!\par
Where once my careless childhood stray’d,\par
A stranger yet to pain!\par
I feel the gales, that from ye blow,\par
A momentary bliss bestow,\par
As waving fresh their gladsome wing,\par
My weary soul they seem to sooth.\par
                    GRAY\par
\par
\par
On the following morning, Emily left Thoulouse at an early hour, and\par
reached La Vallée about sunset. With the melancholy she experienced on\par
the review of a place which had been the residence of her parents, and\par
the scene of her earliest delight, was mingled, after the first shock\par
had subsided, a tender and undescribable pleasure. For time had so far\par
blunted the acuteness of her grief, that she now courted every scene,\par
that awakened the memory of her friends; in every room, where she had\par
been accustomed to see them, they almost seemed to live again; and she\par
felt that La Vallée was still her happiest home. One of the first\par
apartments she visited, was that, which had been her father’s library,\par
and here she seated herself in his arm-chair, and, while she\par
contemplated, with tempered resignation, the picture of past times,\par
which her memory gave, the tears she shed could scarcely be called\par
those of grief.\par
\par
Soon after her arrival, she was surprised by a visit from the venerable\par
M. Barreaux, who came impatiently to welcome the daughter of his late\par
respected neighbour, to her long-deserted home. Emily was comforted by\par
the presence of an old friend, and they passed an interesting hour in\par
conversing of former times, and in relating some of the circumstances,\par
that had occurred to each, since they parted.\par
\par
The evening was so far advanced, when M. Barreaux left Emily, that she\par
could not visit the garden that night; but, on the following morning,\par
she traced its long-regretted scenes with fond impatience; and, as she\par
walked beneath the groves, which her father had planted, and where she\par
had so often sauntered in affectionate conversation with him, his\par
countenance, his smile, even the accents of his voice, returned with\par
exactness to her fancy, and her heart melted to the tender\par
recollections.\par
\par
This, too, was his favourite season of the year, at which they had\par
often together admired the rich and variegated tints of these woods and\par
the magical effect of autumnal lights upon the mountains; and now, the\par
view of these circumstances made memory eloquent. As she wandered\par
pensively on, she fancied the following address\par
\par
TO AUTUMN\par
\par
Sweet Autumn! how thy melancholy grace\par
Steals on my heart, as through these shades I wind!\par
Sooth’d by thy breathing sigh, I fondly trace\par
Each lonely image of the pensive mind!\par
Lov’d scenes, lov’d friends—long lost! around me rise,\par
And wake the melting thought, the tender tear!\par
That tear, that thought, which more than mirth I prize—\par
Sweet as the gradual tint, that paints thy year!\par
Thy farewell smile, with fond regret, I view,\par
Thy beaming lights, soft gliding o’er the woods;\par
Thy distant landscape, touch’d with yellow hue\par
While falls the lengthen’d gleam; thy winding floods,\par
Now veil’d in shade, save where the skiff’s white sails\par
Swell to the breeze, and catch thy streaming ray.\par
But now, e’en now!—the partial vision fails,\par
And the wave smiles, as sweeps the cloud away!\par
Emblem of life!—Thus checquer’d is its plan,\par
Thus joy succeeds to grief—thus smiles the varied man!\par
\par
\par
One of Emily’s earliest enquiries, after her arrival at La Vallée, was\par
concerning Theresa, her father’s old servant, whom it may be remembered\par
that M. Quesnel had turned from the house when it was let, without any\par
provision. Understanding that she lived in a cottage at no great\par
distance, Emily walked thither, and, on approaching, was pleased to\par
see, that her habitation was pleasantly situated on a green slope,\par
sheltered by a tuft of oaks, and had an appearance of comfort and\par
extreme neatness. She found the old woman within, picking vine-stalks,\par
who, on perceiving her young mistress, was nearly overcome with joy.\par
\par
“Ah! my dear young lady!” said she, “I thought I should never see you\par
again in this world, when I heard you were gone to that outlandish\par
country. I have been hardly used, since you went; I little thought they\par
would have turned me out of my old master’s family in my old age!”\par
\par
Emily lamented the circumstance, and then assured her, that she would\par
make her latter days comfortable, and expressed satisfaction, on seeing\par
her in so pleasant a habitation.\par
\par
Theresa thanked her with tears, adding, “Yes, mademoiselle, it is a\par
very comfortable home, thanks to the kind friend, who took me out of my\par
distress, when you were too far off to help me, and placed me here! I\par
little thought!—but no more of that—”\par
\par
“And who was this kind friend?” said Emily: “whoever it was, I shall\par
consider him as mine also.”\par
\par
“Ah, mademoiselle! that friend forbade me to blazon the good deed—I\par
must not say, who it was. But how you are altered since I saw you last!\par
You look so pale now, and so thin, too; but then, there is my old\par
master’s smile! Yes, that will never leave you, any more than the\par
goodness, that used to make him smile. Alas-a-day! the poor lost a\par
friend indeed, when he died!”\par
\par
Emily was affected by this mention of her father, which Theresa\par
observing, changed the subject. “I heard, mademoiselle,” said she,\par
“that Madame Cheron married a foreign gentleman, after all, and took\par
you abroad; how does she do?”\par
\par
Emily now mentioned her death. “Alas!” said Theresa, “if she had not\par
been my master’s sister, I should never have loved her; she was always\par
so cross. But how does that dear young gentleman do, M. Valancourt? he\par
was a handsome youth, and a good one; is he well, mademoiselle?”\par
\par
Emily was much agitated.\par
\par
“A blessing on him!” continued Theresa. “Ah, my dear young lady, you\par
need not look so shy; I know all about it. Do you think I do not know,\par
that he loves you? Why, when you were away, mademoiselle, he used to\par
come to the château and walk about it, so disconsolate! He would go\par
into every room in the lower part of the house, and, sometimes, he\par
would sit himself down in a chair, with his arms across, and his eyes\par
on the floor, and there he would sit, and think, and think, for the\par
hour together. He used to be very fond of the south parlour, because I\par
told him it used to be yours; and there he would stay, looking at the\par
pictures, which I said you drew, and playing upon your lute, that hung\par
up by the window, and reading in your books, till sunset, and then he\par
must go back to his brother’s château. And then—”\par
\par
“It is enough, Theresa,” said Emily.—“How long have you lived in this\par
cottage—and how can I serve you? Will you remain here, or return and\par
live with me?”\par
\par
“Nay, mademoiselle,” said Theresa, “do not be so shy to your poor old\par
servant. I am sure it is no disgrace to like such a good young\par
gentleman.”\par
\par
A deep sigh escaped from Emily.\par
\par
“Ah! how he did love to talk of you! I loved him for that. Nay, for\par
that matter, he liked to hear me talk, for he did not say much himself.\par
But I soon found out what he came to the château about. Then, he would\par
go into the garden, and down to the terrace, and sit under that great\par
tree there, for the day together, with one of your books in his hand;\par
but he did not read much, I fancy; for one day I happened to go that\par
way, and I heard somebody talking. Who can be here? says I: I am sure I\par
let nobody into the garden, but the Chevalier. So I walked softly, to\par
see who it could be; and behold! it was the Chevalier himself, talking\par
to himself about you. And he repeated your name, and sighed so! and\par
said he had lost you for ever, for that you would never return for him.\par
I thought he was out in his reckoning there, but I said nothing, and\par
stole away.”\par
\par
“No more of this trifling,” said Emily, awakening from her reverie: “it\par
displeases me.”\par
\par
“But, when M. Quesnel let the château, I thought it would have broke\par
the Chevalier’s heart.”\par
\par
“Theresa,” said Emily seriously, “you must name the Chevalier no more!”\par
\par
“Not name him, mademoiselle!” cried Theresa: “what times are come up\par
now? Why, I love the Chevalier next to my old master and you,\par
mademoiselle.”\par
\par
“Perhaps your love was not well bestowed, then,” replied Emily, trying\par
to conceal her tears; “but, however that might be, we shall meet no\par
more.”\par
\par
“Meet no more!—not well bestowed!” exclaimed Theresa. “What do I hear?\par
No, mademoiselle, my love was well bestowed, for it was the Chevalier\par
Valancourt, who gave me this cottage, and has supported me in my old\par
age, ever since M. Quesnel turned me from my master’s house.”\par
\par
“The Chevalier Valancourt!” said Emily, trembling extremely.\par
\par
“Yes, mademoiselle, he himself, though he made me promise not to tell;\par
but how could one help, when one heard him ill spoken of? Ah! dear\par
young lady, you may well weep, if you have behaved unkindly to him, for\par
a more tender heart than his never young gentleman had. He found me out\par
in my distress, when you were too far off to help me; and M. Quesnel\par
refused to do so, and bade me go to service again—Alas! I was too old\par
for that!—The Chevalier found me, and bought me this cottage, and gave\par
me money to furnish it, and bade me seek out another poor woman to live\par
with me; and he ordered his brother’s steward to pay me, every quarter,\par
that which has supported me in comfort. Think then, mademoiselle,\par
whether I have not reason to speak well of the Chevalier. And there are\par
others, who could have afforded it better than he: and I am afraid he\par
has hurt himself by his generosity, for quarter day is gone by long\par
since, and no money for me! But do not weep so, mademoiselle: you are\par
not sorry surely to hear of the poor Chevalier’s goodness?”\par
\par
“Sorry!” said Emily, and wept the more. “But how long is it since you\par
have seen him?”\par
\par
“Not this many a day, mademoiselle.”\par
\par
“When did you hear of him?” enquired Emily, with increased emotion.\par
\par
“Alas! never since he went away so suddenly into Languedoc; and he was\par
but just come from Paris then, or I should have seen him, I am sure.\par
Quarter day is gone by long since, and, as I said, no money for me; and\par
I begin to fear some harm has happened to him: and if I was not so far\par
from Estuvière and so lame, I should have gone to enquire before this\par
time; and I have nobody to send so far.”\par
\par
Emily’s anxiety, as to the fate of Valancourt, was now scarcely\par
endurable, and, since propriety would not suffer her to send to the\par
château of his brother, she requested that Theresa would immediately\par
hire some person to go to his steward from herself, and, when he asked\par
for the quarterage due to her, to make enquiries concerning Valancourt.\par
But she first made Theresa promise never to mention her name in this\par
affair, or ever with that of the Chevalier Valancourt; and her former\par
faithfulness to M. St. Aubert induced Emily to confide in her\par
assurances. Theresa now joyfully undertook to procure a person for this\par
errand, and then Emily, after giving her a sum of money to supply her\par
with present comforts, returned, with spirits heavily oppressed, to her\par
home, lamenting, more than ever, that a heart, possessed of so much\par
benevolence as Valancourt’s, should have been contaminated by the vices\par
of the world, but affected by the delicate affection, which his\par
kindness to her old servant expressed for herself.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XII\par
\par
Light thickens, and the crow\par
Makes wing to the rooky wood:\par
Good things of day begin to droop, and drowse;\par
While night’s black agents to their preys do rouse.\par
                    MACBETH\par
\par
\par
Meanwhile Count De Villefort and Lady Blanche had passed a pleasant\par
fortnight at the château de St. Foix, with the Baron and Baroness,\par
during which they made frequent excursions among the mountains, and\par
were delighted with the romantic wildness of Pyrenean scenery. It was\par
with regret, that the Count bade adieu to his old friends, although\par
with the hope of being soon united with them in one family; for it was\par
settled that M. St. Foix, who now attended them into Gascony, should\par
receive the hand of the Lady Blanche, upon their arrival at\par
Château-le-Blanc. As the road, from the Baron’s residence to La Vallée,\par
was over some of the wildest tract of the Pyrenees, and where a\par
carriage-wheel had never passed, the Count hired mules for himself and\par
his family, as well as a couple of stout guides, who were well armed,\par
informed of all the passes of the mountains, and who boasted, too, that\par
they were acquainted with every brake and dingle in the way, could tell\par
the names of all the highest points of this chain of Alps, knew every\par
forest, that spread along their narrow valleys, the shallowest part of\par
every torrent they must cross, and the exact distance of every\par
goat-herd’s and hunter’s cabin they should have occasion to pass,—which\par
last article of learning required no very capacious memory, for even\par
such simple inhabitants were but thinly scattered over these wilds.\par
\par
The Count left the château de St. Foix, early in the morning, with an\par
intention of passing the night at a little inn upon the mountains,\par
about half way to La Vallée, of which his guides had informed him; and,\par
though this was frequented chiefly by Spanish muleteers, on their route\par
into France, and, of course, would afford only sorry accommodation, the\par
Count had no alternative, for it was the only place like an inn, on the\par
road.\par
\par
After a day of admiration and fatigue, the travellers found themselves,\par
about sunset, in a woody valley, overlooked, on every side, by abrupt\par
heights. They had proceeded for many leagues, without seeing a human\par
habitation, and had only heard, now and then, at a distance, the\par
melancholy tinkling of a sheep-bell; but now they caught the notes of\par
merry music, and presently saw, within a little green recess among the\par
rocks, a group of mountaineers, tripping through a dance. The Count,\par
who could not look upon the happiness, any more than on the misery of\par
others, with indifference, halted to enjoy this scene of simple\par
pleasure. The group before him consisted of French and Spanish\par
peasants, the inhabitants of a neighbouring hamlet, some of whom were\par
performing a sprightly dance, the women with castanets in their hands,\par
to the sounds of a lute and a tamborine, till, from the brisk melody of\par
France, the music softened into a slow movement, to which two female\par
peasants danced a Spanish Pavan.\par
\par
The Count, comparing this with the scenes of such gaiety as he had\par
witnessed at Paris, where false taste painted the features, and, while\par
it vainly tried to supply the glow of nature, concealed the charms of\par
animation—where affectation so often distorted the air, and vice\par
perverted the manners—sighed to think, that natural graces and innocent\par
pleasures flourished in the wilds of solitude, while they drooped\par
amidst the concourse of polished society. But the lengthening shadows\par
reminded the travellers, that they had no time to lose; and, leaving\par
this joyous group, they pursued their way towards the little inn, which\par
was to shelter them from the night.\par
\par
The rays of the setting sun now threw a yellow gleam upon the forests\par
of pine and chesnut, that swept down the lower region of the mountains,\par
and gave resplendent tints to the snowy points above. But soon, even\par
this light faded fast, and the scenery assumed a more tremendous\par
appearance, invested with the obscurity of twilight. Where the torrent\par
had been seen, it was now only heard; where the wild cliffs had\par
displayed every variety of form and attitude, a dark mass of mountains\par
now alone appeared; and the vale, which far, far below had opened its\par
dreadful chasm, the eye could no longer fathom. A melancholy gleam\par
still lingered on the summits of the highest Alps, overlooking the deep\par
repose of evening, and seeming to make the stillness of the hour more\par
awful.\par
\par
Blanche viewed the scene in silence, and listened with enthusiasm to\par
the murmur of the pines, that extended in dark lines along the\par
mountains, and to the faint voice of the izard, among the rocks, that\par
came at intervals on the air. But her enthusiasm sunk into\par
apprehension, when, as the shadows deepened, she looked upon the\par
doubtful precipice, that bordered the road, as well as on the various\par
fantastic forms of danger, that glimmered through the obscurity beyond\par
it; and she asked her father, how far they were from the inn, and\par
whether he did not consider the road to be dangerous at this late hour.\par
The Count repeated the first question to the guides, who returned a\par
doubtful answer, adding, that, when it was darker, it would be safest\par
to rest, till the moon rose. “It is scarcely safe to proceed now,” said\par
the Count; but the guides, assuring him that there was no danger, went\par
on. Blanche, revived by this assurance, again indulged a pensive\par
pleasure, as she watched the progress of twilight gradually spreading\par
its tints over the woods and mountains, and stealing from the eye every\par
minuter feature of the scene, till the grand outlines of nature alone\par
remained. Then fell the silent dews, and every wild flower, and\par
aromatic plant, that bloomed among the cliffs, breathed forth its\par
sweetness; then, too, when the mountain-bee had crept into its\par
blossomed bed, and the hum of every little insect, that had floated\par
gaily in the sunbeam, was hushed, the sound of many streams, not heard\par
till now, murmured at a distance.—The bats alone, of all the animals\par
inhabiting this region, seemed awake; and, while they flitted across\par
the silent path, which Blanche was pursuing, she remembered the\par
following lines, which Emily had given her:\par
\par
TO THE BAT\par
\par
From haunt of man, from day’s obtrusive glare,\par
Thou shroud’st thee in the ruin’s ivy’d tow’r.\par
Or in some shadowy glen’s romantic bow’r,\par
Where wizard forms their mystic charms prepare,\par
Where Horror lurks, and ever-boding Care!\par
But, at the sweet and silent ev’ning hour,\par
When clos’d in sleep is ev’ry languid flow’r,\par
Thou lov’st to sport upon the twilight air,\par
Mocking the eye, that would thy course pursue,\par
In many a wanton-round, elastic, gay,\par
Thou flit’st athwart the pensive wand’rer’s way,\par
As his lone footsteps print the mountain-dew.\par
From Indian isles thou com’st, with Summer’s car,\par
Twilight thy love—thy guide her beaming star!\par
\par
\par
To a warm imagination, the dubious forms, that float, half veiled in\par
darkness, afford a higher delight, than the most distinct scenery, that\par
the sun can show. While the fancy thus wanders over landscapes partly\par
of its own creation, a sweet complacency steals upon the mind, and\par
\par
Refines it all to subtlest feeling,\par
Bids the tear of rapture roll.\par
\par
\par
The distant note of a torrent, the weak trembling of the breeze among\par
the woods, or the far-off sound of a human voice, now lost and heard\par
again, are circumstances, which wonderfully heighten the enthusiastic\par
tone of the mind. The young St. Foix, who saw the presentations of a\par
fervid fancy, and felt whatever enthusiasm could suggest, sometimes\par
interrupted the silence, which the rest of the party seemed by mutual\par
consent to preserve, remarking and pointing out to Blanche the most\par
striking effect of the hour upon the scenery; while Blanche, whose\par
apprehensions were beguiled by the conversation of her lover, yielded\par
to the taste so congenial to his, and they conversed in a low\par
restrained voice, the effect of the pensive tranquillity, which\par
twilight and the scene inspired, rather than of any fear, that they\par
should be heard. But, while the heart was thus soothed to tenderness,\par
St. Foix gradually mingled, with his admiration of the country, a\par
mention of his affection; and he continued to speak, and Blanche to\par
listen, till the mountains, the woods, and the magical illusions of\par
twilight, were remembered no more.\par
\par
The shadows of evening soon shifted to the gloom of night, which was\par
somewhat anticipated by the vapours, that, gathering fast round the\par
mountains, rolled in dark wreaths along their sides; and the guides\par
proposed to rest, till the moon should rise, adding, that they thought\par
a storm was coming on. As they looked round for a spot, that might\par
afford some kind of shelter, an object was perceived obscurely through\par
the dusk, on a point of rock, a little way down the mountain, which\par
they imagined to be a hunter’s or a shepherd’s cabin, and the party,\par
with cautious steps, proceeded towards it. Their labour, however, was\par
not rewarded, or their apprehensions soothed; for, on reaching the\par
object of their search, they discovered a monumental cross, which\par
marked the spot to have been polluted by murder.\par
\par
The darkness would not permit them to read the inscription; but the\par
guides knew this to be a cross, raised to the memory of a Count de\par
Beliard, who had been murdered here by a horde of banditti, that had\par
infested this part of the Pyrenees, a few years before; and the\par
uncommon size of the monument seemed to justify the supposition, that\par
it was erected for a person of some distinction. Blanche shuddered, as\par
she listened to some horrid particulars of the Count’s fate, which one\par
of the guides related in a low, restrained tone, as if the sound of his\par
own voice frightened him; but, while they lingered at the cross,\par
attending to his narrative, a flash of lightning glanced upon the\par
rocks, thunder muttered at a distance, and the travellers, now alarmed,\par
quitted this scene of solitary horror, in search of shelter.\par
\par
Having regained their former track, the guides, as they passed on,\par
endeavoured to interest the Count by various stories of robbery, and\par
even of murder, which had been perpetrated in the very places they must\par
unavoidably pass, with accounts of their own dauntless courage and\par
wonderful escapes. The chief guide, or rather he, who was the most\par
completely armed, drawing forth one of the four pistols, that were\par
tucked into his belt, swore, that it had shot three robbers within the\par
year. He then brandished a clasp-knife of enormous length, and was\par
going to recount the wonderful execution it had done, when St. Foix,\par
perceiving, that Blanche was terrified, interrupted him. The Count,\par
meanwhile, secretly laughing at the terrible histories and extravagant\par
boastings of the man, resolved to humour him, and, telling Blanche in a\par
whisper, his design, began to recount some exploits of his own, which\par
infinitely exceeded any related by the guide.\par
\par
To these surprising circumstances he so artfully gave the colouring of\par
truth, that the courage of the guides was visibly affected by them, who\par
continued silent, long after the Count had ceased to speak. The\par
loquacity of the chief hero thus laid asleep, the vigilance of his eyes\par
and ears seemed more thoroughly awakened, for he listened, with much\par
appearance of anxiety, to the deep thunder, which murmured at\par
intervals, and often paused, as the breeze, that was now rising, rushed\par
among the pines. But, when he made a sudden halt before a tuft of cork\par
trees, that projected over the road, and drew forth a pistol, before he\par
would venture to brave the banditti which might lurk behind it, the\par
Count could no longer refrain from laughter.\par
\par
Having now, however, arrived at a level spot, somewhat sheltered from\par
the air, by overhanging cliffs and by a wood of larch, that rose over\par
the precipice on the left, and the guides being yet ignorant how far\par
they were from the inn, the travellers determined to rest, till the\par
moon should rise, or the storm disperse. Blanche, recalled to a sense\par
of the present moment, looked on the surrounding gloom, with terror;\par
but giving her hand to St. Foix, she alighted, and the whole party\par
entered a kind of cave, if such it could be called, which was only a\par
shallow cavity, formed by the curve of impending rocks. A light being\par
struck, a fire was kindled, whose blaze afforded some degree of\par
cheerfulness, and no small comfort, for, though the day had been hot,\par
the night air of this mountainous region was chilling; a fire was\par
partly necessary also to keep off the wolves, with which those wilds\par
were infested.\par
\par
Provisions being spread upon a projection of the rock, the Count and\par
his family partook of a supper, which, in a scene less rude, would\par
certainly have been thought less excellent. When the repast was\par
finished, St. Foix, impatient for the moon, sauntered along the\par
precipice, to a point, that fronted the east; but all was yet wrapt in\par
gloom, and the silence of night was broken only by the murmuring of\par
woods, that waved far below, or by distant thunder, and, now and then,\par
by the faint voices of the party he had quitted. He viewed, with\par
emotions of awful sublimity, the long volumes of sulphureous clouds,\par
that floated along the upper and middle regions of the air, and the\par
lightnings that flashed from them, sometimes silently, and, at others,\par
followed by sullen peals of thunder, which the mountains feebly\par
prolonged, while the whole horizon, and the abyss, on which he stood,\par
were discovered in the momentary light. Upon the succeeding darkness,\par
the fire, which had been kindled in the cave, threw a partial gleam,\par
illumining some points of the opposite rocks, and the summits of\par
pine-woods, that hung beetling on the cliffs below, while their\par
recesses seemed to frown in deeper shade.\par
\par
St. Foix stopped to observe the picture, which the party in the cave\par
presented, where the elegant form of Blanche was finely contrasted by\par
the majestic figure of the Count, who was seated by her on a rude\par
stone, and each was rendered more impressive by the grotesque habits\par
and strong features of the guides and other attendants, who were in the\par
back ground of the piece. The effect of the light, too, was\par
interesting; on the surrounding figures it threw a strong, though pale\par
gleam, and glittered on their bright arms; while upon the foliage of a\par
gigantic larch, that impended its shade over the cliff above, appeared\par
a red, dusky tint, deepening almost imperceptibly into the blackness of\par
night.\par
\par
While St. Foix contemplated the scene, the moon, broad and yellow, rose\par
over the eastern summits, from among embattled clouds, and showed dimly\par
the grandeur of the heavens, the mass of vapours, that rolled half way\par
down the precipice beneath, and the doubtful mountains.\par
\par
What dreadful pleasure! there to stand sublime,\par
Like shipwreck’d mariner on desert coast,\par
And view th’enormous waste of vapour, tost\par
In billows length’ning to th’horizon round!\par
                    THE MINSTREL\par
\par
\par
From this romantic reverie he was awakened by the voices of the guides,\par
repeating his name, which was reverbed from cliff to cliff, till a\par
hundred tongues seemed to call him; when he soon quieted the fears of\par
the Count and the Lady Blanche, by returning to the cave. As the storm,\par
however, seemed approaching, they did not quit their place of shelter;\par
and the Count, seated between his daughter and St. Foix, endeavoured to\par
divert the fears of the former, and conversed on subjects, relating to\par
the natural history of the scene, among which they wandered. He spoke\par
of the mineral and fossile substances, found in the depths of these\par
mountains,—the veins of marble and granite, with which they abounded,\par
the strata of shells, discovered near their summits, many thousand\par
fathom above the level of the sea, and at a vast distance from its\par
present shore;—of the tremendous chasms and caverns of the rocks, the\par
grotesque form of the mountains, and the various phenomena, that seem\par
to stamp upon the world the history of the deluge. From the natural\par
history he descended to the mention of events and circumstances,\par
connected with the civil story of the Pyrenees; named some of the most\par
remarkable fortresses, which France and Spain had erected in the passes\par
of these mountains; and gave a brief account of some celebrated sieges\par
and encounters in early times, when Ambition first frightened Solitude\par
from these her deep recesses, made her mountains, which before had\par
echoed only to the torrent’s roar, tremble with the clang of arms, and,\par
when man’s first footsteps in her sacred haunts had left the print of\par
blood!\par
\par
As Blanche sat, attentive to the narrative, that rendered the scenes\par
doubly interesting, and resigned to solemn emotion, while she\par
considered, that she was on the very ground, once polluted by these\par
events, her reverie was suddenly interrupted by a sound, that came in\par
the wind.—It was the distant bark of a watch-dog. The travellers\par
listened with eager hope, and, as the wind blew stronger, fancied, that\par
the sound came from no great distance; and, the guides having little\par
doubt, that it proceeded from the inn they were in search of, the Count\par
determined to pursue his way. The moon now afforded a stronger, though\par
still an uncertain light, as she moved among broken clouds; and the\par
travellers, led by the sound, recommenced their journey along the brow\par
of the precipice, preceded by a single torch, that now contended with\par
the moonlight; for the guides, believing they should reach the inn soon\par
after sunset, had neglected to provide more. In silent caution they\par
followed the sound, which was heard but at intervals, and which, after\par
some time entirely ceased. The guides endeavoured, however, to point\par
their course to the quarter, whence it had issued, but the deep roaring\par
of a torrent soon seized their attention, and presently they came to a\par
tremendous chasm of the mountain, which seemed to forbid all further\par
progress. Blanche alighted from her mule, as did the Count and St.\par
Foix, while the guides traversed the edge in search of a bridge, which,\par
however rude, might convey them to the opposite side, and they, at\par
length, confessed, what the Count had begun to suspect, that they had\par
been, for some time, doubtful of their way, and were now certain only,\par
that they had lost it.\par
\par
At a little distance, was discovered a rude and dangerous passage,\par
formed by an enormous pine, which, thrown across the chasm, united the\par
opposite precipices, and which had been felled probably by the hunter,\par
to facilitate his chace of the izard, or the wolf. The whole party, the\par
guides excepted, shuddered at the prospect of crossing this alpine\par
bridge, whose sides afforded no kind of defence, and from which to fall\par
was to die. The guides, however, prepared to lead over the mules, while\par
Blanche stood trembling on the brink, and listening to the roar of the\par
waters, which were seen descending from rocks above, overhung with\par
lofty pines, and thence precipitating themselves into the deep abyss,\par
where their white surges gleamed faintly in the moonlight. The poor\par
animals proceeded over this perilous bridge with instinctive caution,\par
neither frightened by the noise of the cataract, nor deceived by the\par
gloom, which the impending foliage threw athwart their way. It was now,\par
that the solitary torch, which had been hitherto of little service, was\par
found to be an inestimable treasure; and Blanche, terrified, shrinking,\par
but endeavouring to recollect all her firmness and presence of mind,\par
preceded by her lover and supported by her father, followed the red\par
gleam of the torch, in safety, to the opposite cliff.\par
\par
As they went on, the heights contracted, and formed a narrow pass, at\par
the bottom of which, the torrent they had just crossed, was heard to\par
thunder. But they were again cheered by the bark of a dog, keeping\par
watch, perhaps, over the flocks of the mountains, to protect them from\par
the nightly descent of the wolves. The sound was much nearer than\par
before, and, while they rejoiced in the hope of soon reaching a place\par
of repose, a light was seen to glimmer at a distance. It appeared at a\par
height considerably above the level of their path, and was lost and\par
seen again, as if the waving branches of trees sometimes excluded and\par
then admitted its rays. The guides hallooed with all their strength,\par
but the sound of no human voice was heard in return, and, at length, as\par
a more effectual means of making themselves known, they fired a pistol.\par
But, while they listened in anxious expectation, the noise of the\par
explosion was alone heard, echoing among the rocks, and it gradually\par
sunk into silence, which no friendly hint of man disturbed. The light,\par
however, that had been seen before, now became plainer, and, soon\par
after, voices were heard indistinctly on the wind; but, upon the guides\par
repeating the call, the voices suddenly ceased, and the light\par
disappeared.\par
\par
The Lady Blanche was now almost sinking beneath the pressure of\par
anxiety, fatigue and apprehension, and the united efforts of the Count\par
and St. Foix could scarcely support her spirits. As they continued to\par
advance, an object was perceived on a point of rock above, which, the\par
strong rays of the moon then falling on it, appeared to be a\par
watch-tower. The Count, from its situation and some other\par
circumstances, had little doubt, that it was such, and believing, that\par
the light had proceeded from thence, he endeavoured to reanimate his\par
daughter’s spirits by the near prospect of shelter and repose, which,\par
however rude the accommodation, a ruined watch-tower might afford.\par
\par
“Numerous watch-towers have been erected among the Pyrenees,” said the\par
Count, anxious only to call Blanche’s attention from the subject of her\par
fears; “and the method, by which they give intelligence of the approach\par
of the enemy, is, you know, by fires, kindled on the summits of these\par
edifices. Signals have thus, sometimes, been communicated from post to\par
post, along a frontier line of several hundred miles in length. Then,\par
as occasion may require, the lurking armies emerge from their\par
fortresses and the forests, and march forth, to defend, perhaps, the\par
entrance of some grand pass, where, planting themselves on the heights,\par
they assail their astonished enemies, who wind along the glen below,\par
with fragments of the shattered cliff, and pour death and defeat upon\par
them. The ancient forts, and watch-towers, overlooking the grand passes\par
of the Pyrenees, are carefully preserved; but some of those in inferior\par
stations have been suffered to fall into decay, and are now frequently\par
converted into the more peaceful habitation of the hunter, or the\par
shepherd, who, after a day of toil, retires hither, and, with his\par
faithful dogs, forgets, near a cheerful blaze, the labour of the chace,\par
or the anxiety of collecting his wandering flocks, while he is\par
sheltered from the nightly storm.”\par
\par
“But are they always thus peacefully inhabited?” said the Lady Blanche.\par
\par
“No,” replied the Count, “they are sometimes the asylum of French and\par
Spanish smugglers, who cross the mountains with contraband goods from\par
their respective countries, and the latter are particularly numerous,\par
against whom strong parties of the king’s troops are sometimes sent.\par
But the desperate resolution of these adventurers, who, knowing, that,\par
if they are taken, they must expiate the breach of the law by the most\par
cruel death, travel in large parties, well armed, often daunts the\par
courage of the soldiers. The smugglers, who seek only safety, never\par
engage, when they can possibly avoid it; the military, also, who know,\par
that in these encounters, danger is certain, and glory almost\par
unattainable, are equally reluctant to fight; an engagement, therefore,\par
very seldom happens, but, when it does, it never concludes till after\par
the most desperate and bloody conflict. You are inattentive, Blanche,”\par
added the Count: “I have wearied you with a dull subject; but see,\par
yonder, in the moonlight, is the edifice we have been in search of, and\par
we are fortunate to be so near it, before the storm bursts.”\par
\par
Blanche, looking up, perceived, that they were at the foot of the\par
cliff, on whose summit the building stood, but no light now issued from\par
it; the barking of the dog too had, for some time, ceased, and the\par
guides began to doubt, whether this was really the object of their\par
search. From the distance, at which they surveyed it, shown imperfectly\par
by a cloudy moon, it appeared to be of more extent than a single\par
watch-tower; but the difficulty was how to ascend the height, whose\par
abrupt declivities seemed to afford no kind of pathway.\par
\par
While the guides carried forward the torch to examine the cliff, the\par
Count, remaining with Blanche and St. Foix at its foot, under the\par
shadow of the woods, endeavoured again to beguile the time by\par
conversation, but again anxiety abstracted the mind of Blanche; and he\par
then consulted, apart with St. Foix, whether it would be advisable,\par
should a path be found, to venture to an edifice, which might possibly\par
harbour banditti. They considered, that their own party was not small,\par
and that several of them were well armed; and, after enumerating the\par
dangers, to be incurred by passing the night in the open wild, exposed,\par
perhaps, to the effects of a thunder-storm, there remained not a doubt,\par
that they ought to endeavour to obtain admittance to the edifice above,\par
at any hazard respecting the inhabitants it might harbour; but the\par
darkness, and the dead silence, that surrounded it, appeared to\par
contradict the probability of its being inhabited at all.\par
\par
A shout from the guides aroused their attention, after which, in a few\par
minutes, one of the Count’s servants returned with intelligence, that a\par
path was found, and they immediately hastened to join the guides, when\par
they all ascended a little winding way cut in the rock among thickets\par
of dwarf wood, and, after much toil and some danger, reached the\par
summit, where several ruined towers, surrounded by a massy wall, rose\par
to their view, partially illumined by the moonlight. The space around\par
the building was silent, and apparently forsaken, but the Count was\par
cautious; “Step softly,” said he, in a low voice, “while we reconnoitre\par
the edifice.”\par
\par
Having proceeded silently along for some paces, they stopped at a gate,\par
whose portals were terrible even in ruins, and, after a moment’s\par
hesitation, passed on to the court of entrance, but paused again at the\par
head of a terrace, which, branching from it, ran along the brow of a\par
precipice. Over this, rose the main body of the edifice, which was now\par
seen to be, not a watch-tower, but one of those ancient fortresses,\par
that, from age and neglect, had fallen to decay. Many parts of it,\par
however, appeared to be still entire; it was built of grey stone, in\par
the heavy Saxon-gothic style, with enormous round towers, buttresses of\par
proportionable strength, and the arch of the large gate, which seemed\par
to open into the hall of the fabric, was round, as was that of a window\par
above. The air of solemnity, which must so strongly have characterised\par
the pile even in the days of its early strength, was now considerably\par
heightened by its shattered battlements and half-demolished walls, and\par
by the huge masses of ruin, scattered in its wide area, now silent and\par
grass grown. In this court of entrance stood the gigantic remains of an\par
oak, that seemed to have flourished and decayed with the building,\par
which it still appeared frowningly to protect by the few remaining\par
branches, leafless and moss-grown, that crowned its trunk, and whose\par
wide extent told how enormous the tree had been in a former age. This\par
fortress was evidently once of great strength, and, from its situation\par
on a point of rock, impending over a deep glen, had been of great power\par
to annoy, as well as to resist; the Count, therefore, as he stood\par
surveying it, was somewhat surprised, that it had been suffered,\par
ancient as it was, to sink into ruins, and its present lonely and\par
deserted air excited in his breast emotions of melancholy awe. While he\par
indulged, for a moment, these emotions, he thought he heard a sound of\par
remote voices steal upon the stillness, from within the building, the\par
front of which he again surveyed with scrutinizing eyes, but yet no\par
light was visible. He now determined to walk round the fort, to that\par
remote part of it, whence he thought the voices had arisen, that he\par
might examine whether any light could be discerned there, before he\par
ventured to knock at the gate; for this purpose, he entered upon the\par
terrace, where the remains of cannon were yet apparent in the thick\par
walls, but he had not proceeded many paces, when his steps were\par
suddenly arrested by the loud barking of a dog within, and which he\par
fancied to be the same, whose voice had been the means of bringing the\par
travellers thither. It now appeared certain, that the place was\par
inhabited, and the Count returned to consult again with St. Foix,\par
whether he should try to obtain admittance, for its wild aspect had\par
somewhat shaken his former resolution; but, after a second\par
consultation, he submitted to the considerations, which before\par
determined him, and which were strengthened by the discovery of the\par
dog, that guarded the fort, as well as by the stillness that pervaded\par
it. He, therefore, ordered one of his servants to knock at the gate,\par
who was advancing to obey him, when a light appeared through the\par
loop-hole of one of the towers, and the Count called loudly, but,\par
receiving no answer, he went up to the gate himself, and struck upon it\par
with an iron-pointed pole, which had assisted him to climb the steep.\par
When the echoes had ceased, that this blow had awakened, the renewed\par
barking,—and there were now more than one dog,—was the only sound, that\par
was heard. The Count stepped back, a few paces, to observe whether the\par
light was in the tower, and, perceiving, that it was gone, he returned\par
to the portal, and had lifted the pole to strike again, when again he\par
fancied he heard the murmur of voices within, and paused to listen. He\par
was confirmed in the supposition, but they were too remote, to be heard\par
otherwise than in a murmur, and the Count now let the pole fall heavily\par
upon the gate; when almost immediately a profound silence followed. It\par
was apparent, that the people within had heard the sound, and their\par
caution in admitting strangers gave him a favourable opinion of them.\par
“They are either hunters or shepherds,” said he, “who, like ourselves,\par
have probably sought shelter from the night within these walls, and are\par
fearful of admitting strangers, lest they should prove robbers. I will\par
endeavour to remove their fears.” So saying, he called aloud, “We are\par
friends, who ask shelter from the night.” In a few moments, steps were\par
heard within, which approached, and a voice then enquired—“Who calls?”\par
“Friends,” repeated the Count; “open the gates, and you shall know\par
more.”—Strong bolts were now heard to be undrawn, and a man, armed with\par
a hunting spear, appeared. “What is it you want at this hour?” said he.\par
The Count beckoned his attendants, and then answered, that he wished to\par
enquire the way to the nearest cabin. “Are you so little acquainted\par
with these mountains,” said the man, “as not to know, that there is\par
none, within several leagues? I cannot show you the way; you must seek\par
it—there’s a moon.” Saying this, he was closing the gate, and the Count\par
was turning away, half disappointed and half afraid, when another voice\par
was heard from above, and, on looking up, he saw a light, and a man’s\par
face, at the grate of the portal. “Stay, friend, you have lost your\par
way?” said the voice. “You are hunters, I suppose, like ourselves: I\par
will be with you presently.” The voice ceased, and the light\par
disappeared. Blanche had been alarmed by the appearance of the man, who\par
had opened the gate, and she now entreated her father to quit the\par
place; but the Count had observed the hunter’s spear, which he carried;\par
and the words from the tower encouraged him to await the event. The\par
gate was soon opened, and several men in hunters’ habits, who had heard\par
above what had passed below, appeared, and, having listened some time\par
to the Count, told him he was welcome to rest there for the night. They\par
then pressed him, with much courtesy, to enter, and to partake of such\par
fare as they were about to sit down to. The Count, who had observed\par
them attentively while they spoke, was cautious, and somewhat\par
suspicious; but he was also weary, fearful of the approaching storm,\par
and of encountering alpine heights in the obscurity of night; being\par
likewise somewhat confident in the strength and number of his\par
attendants, he, after some further consideration, determined to accept\par
the invitation. With this resolution he called his servants, who,\par
advancing round the tower, behind which some of them had silently\par
listened to this conference, followed their Lord, the Lady Blanche, and\par
St. Foix into the fortress. The strangers led them on to a large and\par
rude hall, partially seen by a fire that blazed at its extremity, round\par
which four men, in the hunter’s dress, were seated, and on the hearth\par
were several dogs stretched in sleep. In the middle of the hall stood a\par
large table, and over the fire some part of an animal was boiling. As\par
the Count approached, the men arose, and the dogs, half raising\par
themselves, looked fiercely at the strangers, but, on hearing their\par
masters’ voices, kept their postures on the hearth.\par
\par
Blanche looked round this gloomy and spacious hall; then at the men,\par
and to her father, who, smiling cheerfully at her, addressed himself to\par
the hunters. “This is a hospitable hearth,” said he, “the blaze of a\par
fire is reviving after having wandered so long in these dreary wilds.\par
Your dogs are tired; what success have you had?” “Such as we usually\par
have,” replied one of the men, who had been seated in the hall, “we\par
kill our game with tolerable certainty.” “These are fellow hunters,”\par
said one of the men who had brought the Count hither, “that have lost\par
their way, and I have told them there is room enough in the fort for us\par
all.” “Very true, very true,” replied his companion, “What luck have\par
you had in the chace, brothers? We have killed two izards, and that,\par
you will say, is pretty well.” “You mistake, friend,” said the Count,\par
“we are not hunters, but travellers; but, if you will admit us to\par
hunters’ fare, we shall be well contented, and will repay your\par
kindness.” “Sit down then, brother,” said one of the men: “Jacques, lay\par
more fuel on the fire, the kid will soon be ready; bring a seat for the\par
lady too. Ma’amselle, will you taste our brandy? it is true Barcelona,\par
and as bright as ever flowed from a keg.” Blanche timidly smiled, and\par
was going to refuse, when her father prevented her, by taking, with a\par
good humoured air, the glass offered to his daughter; and Mons. St.\par
Foix, who was seated next her, pressed her hand, and gave her an\par
encouraging look, but her attention was engaged by a man, who sat\par
silently by the fire, observing St. Foix, with a steady and earnest\par
eye.\par
\par
“You lead a jolly life here,” said the Count. “The life of a hunter is\par
a pleasant and a healthy one; and the repose is sweet, which succeeds\par
to your labour.”\par
\par
“Yes,” replied one of his hosts, “our life is pleasant enough. We live\par
here only during the summer, and autumnal months; in winter, the place\par
is dreary, and the swoln torrents, that descend from the heights, put a\par
stop to the chace.”\par
\par
“’Tis a life of liberty and enjoyment,” said the Count: “I should like\par
to pass a month in your way very well.”\par
\par
“We find employment for our guns too,” said a man who stood behind the\par
Count: “here are plenty of birds, of delicious flavour, that feed upon\par
the wild thyme and herbs, that grow in the valleys. Now I think of it,\par
there is a brace of birds hung up in the stone gallery; go fetch them,\par
Jacques, we will have them dressed.”\par
\par
The Count now made enquiry, concerning the method of pursuing the chace\par
among the rocks and precipices of these romantic regions, and was\par
listening to a curious detail, when a horn was sounded at the gate.\par
Blanche looked timidly at her father, who continued to converse on the\par
subject of the chace, but whose countenance was somewhat expressive of\par
anxiety, and who often turned his eyes towards that part of the hall\par
nearest the gate. The horn sounded again, and a loud halloo succeeded.\par
“These are some of our companions, returned from their day’s labour,”\par
said a man, going lazily from his seat towards the gate; and in a few\par
minutes, two men appeared, each with a gun over his shoulder, and\par
pistols in his belt. “What cheer, my lads? what cheer?” said they, as\par
they approached. “What luck?” returned their companions: “have you\par
brought home your supper? You shall have none else.”\par
\par
“Hah! who the devil have you brought home?” said they in bad Spanish,\par
on perceiving the Count’s party, “are they from France, or Spain?—where\par
did you meet with them?”\par
\par
“They met with us, and a merry meeting too,” replied his companion\par
aloud in good French. “This chevalier, and his party, had lost their\par
way, and asked a night’s lodging in the fort.” The others made no\par
reply, but threw down a kind of knapsack, and drew forth several brace\par
of birds. The bag sounded heavily as it fell to the ground, and the\par
glitter of some bright metal within glanced on the eye of the Count,\par
who now surveyed, with a more enquiring look, the man, that held the\par
knapsack. He was a tall robust figure, of a hard countenance, and had\par
short black hair, curling in his neck. Instead of the hunter’s dress,\par
he wore a faded military uniform; sandals were laced on his broad legs,\par
and a kind of short trowsers hung from his waist. On his head he wore a\par
leathern cap, somewhat resembling in shape an ancient Roman helmet; but\par
the brows that scowled beneath it, would have characterised those of\par
the barbarians, who conquered Rome, rather than those of a Roman\par
soldier. The Count, at length, turned away his eyes, and remained\par
silent and thoughtful, till, again raising them, he perceived a figure\par
standing in an obscure part of the hall, fixed in attentive gaze on St.\par
Foix, who was conversing with Blanche, and did not observe this; but\par
the Count, soon after, saw the same man looking over the shoulder of\par
the soldier as attentively at himself. He withdrew his eye, when that\par
of the Count met it, who felt mistrust gathering fast upon his mind,\par
but feared to betray it in his countenance, and, forcing his features\par
to assume a smile, addressed Blanche on some indifferent subject. When\par
he again looked round, he perceived, that the soldier and his companion\par
were gone.\par
\par
The man, who was called Jacques, now returned from the stone gallery.\par
“A fire is lighted there,” said he, “and the birds are dressing; the\par
table too is spread there, for that place is warmer than this.”\par
\par
His companions approved of the removal, and invited their guests to\par
follow to the gallery, of whom Blanche appeared distressed, and\par
remained seated, and St. Foix looked at the Count, who said, he\par
preferred the comfortable blaze of the fire he was then near. The\par
hunters, however, commended the warmth of the other apartment, and\par
pressed his removal with such seeming courtesy, that the Count, half\par
doubting, and half fearful of betraying his doubts, consented to go.\par
The long and ruinous passages, through which they went, somewhat\par
daunted him, but the thunder, which now burst in loud peals above, made\par
it dangerous to quit this place of shelter, and he forbore to provoke\par
his conductors by showing that he distrusted them. The hunters led the\par
way, with a lamp; the Count and St. Foix, who wished to please their\par
hosts by some instances of familiarity, carried each a seat, and\par
Blanche followed, with faltering steps. As she passed on, part of her\par
dress caught on a nail in the wall, and, while she stopped, somewhat\par
too scrupulously, to disengage it, the Count, who was talking to St.\par
Foix, and neither of whom observed the circumstance, followed their\par
conductor round an abrupt angle of the passage, and Blanche was left\par
behind in darkness. The thunder prevented them from hearing her call\par
but, having disengaged her dress, she quickly followed, as she thought,\par
the way they had taken. A light, that glimmered at a distance,\par
confirmed this belief, and she proceeded towards an open door, whence\par
it issued, conjecturing the room beyond to be the stone gallery the men\par
had spoken of. Hearing voices as she advanced, she paused within a few\par
paces of the chamber, that she might be certain whether she was right,\par
and from thence, by the light of a lamp, that hung from the ceiling,\par
observed four men, seated round a table, over which they leaned in\par
apparent consultation. In one of them she distinguished the features of\par
him, whom she had observed, gazing at St. Foix, with such deep\par
attention; and who was now speaking in an earnest, though restrained\par
voice, till, one of his companions seeming to oppose him, they spoke\par
together in a loud and harsher tone. Blanche, alarmed by perceiving\par
that neither her father, nor St. Foix were there, and terrified at the\par
fierce countenances and manners of these men, was turning hastily from\par
the chamber, to pursue her search of the gallery, when she heard one of\par
the men say:\par
\par
“Let all dispute end here. Who talks of danger? Follow my advice, and\par
there will be none—secure _them_, and the rest are an easy prey.”\par
Blanche, struck with these words, paused a moment, to hear more. “There\par
is nothing to be got by the rest,” said one of his companions, “I am\par
never for blood when I can help it—dispatch the two others, and our\par
business is done; the rest may go.”\par
\par
“May they so?” exclaimed the first ruffian, with a tremendous\par
oath—“What! to tell how we have disposed of their masters, and to send\par
the king’s troops to drag us to the wheel! You were always a choice\par
adviser—I warrant we have not yet forgot St. Thomas’s eve last year.”\par
\par
Blanche’s heart now sunk with horror. Her first impulse was to retreat\par
from the door, but, when she would have gone, her trembling frame\par
refused to support her, and, having tottered a few paces, to a more\par
obscure part of the passage, she was compelled to listen to the\par
dreadful councils of those, who, she was no longer suffered to doubt,\par
were banditti. In the next moment, she heard the following words, “Why\par
you would not murder the whole _gang?_”\par
\par
“I warrant our lives are as good as theirs,” replied his comrade. “If\par
we don’t kill them, they will hang us: better they should die than we\par
be hanged.”\par
\par
“Better, better,” cried his comrades.\par
\par
“To commit murder, is a hopeful way of escaping the gallows!” said the\par
first ruffian—“many an honest fellow has run his head into the noose\par
that way, though.” There was a pause of some moments, during which they\par
appeared to be considering.\par
\par
“Confound those fellows,” exclaimed one of the robbers impatiently,\par
“they ought to have been here by this time; they will come back\par
presently with the old story, and no booty: if they were here, our\par
business would be plain and easy. I see we shall not be able to do the\par
business tonight, for our numbers are not equal to the enemy, and in\par
the morning they will be for marching off, and how can we detain them\par
without force?”\par
\par
“I have been thinking of a scheme, that will do,” said one of his\par
comrades: “if we can dispatch the two chevaliers silently, it will be\par
easy to master the rest.”\par
\par
“That’s a plausible scheme, in good faith,” said another with a smile\par
of scorn—“If I can eat my way through the prison wall, I shall be at\par
liberty!—How can we dispatch them _silently?_”\par
\par
“By poison,” replied his companions.\par
\par
“Well said! that will do,” said the second ruffian, “that will give a\par
lingering death too, and satisfy my revenge. These barons shall take\par
care how they again tempt our vengeance.”\par
\par
“I knew the son, the moment I saw him,” said the man, whom Blanche had\par
observed gazing on St. Foix, “though he does not know me; the father I\par
had almost forgotten.”\par
\par
“Well, you may say what you will,” said the third ruffian, “but I don’t\par
believe he is the Baron, and I am as likely to know as any of you, for\par
I was one of them, that attacked him, with our brave lads, that\par
suffered.”\par
\par
“And was not I another?” said the first ruffian, “I tell you he is the\par
Baron; but what does it signify whether he is or not?—shall we let all\par
this booty go out of our hands? It is not often we have such luck at\par
this. While we run the chance of the wheel for smuggling a few pounds\par
of tobacco, to cheat the king’s manufactory, and of breaking our necks\par
down the precipices in the chace of our food; and, now and then, rob a\par
brother smuggler, or a straggling pilgrim, of what scarcely repays us\par
the powder we fire at them, shall we let such a prize as this go? Why\par
they have enough about them to keep us for—”\par
\par
“I am not for that, I am not for that,” replied the third robber, “let\par
us make the most of them: only, if this is the Baron, I should like to\par
have a flash the more at him, for the sake of our brave comrades, that\par
he brought to the gallows.”\par
\par
“Aye, aye, flash as much as you will,” rejoined the first man, “but I\par
tell you the Baron is a taller man.”\par
\par
“Confound your quibbling,” said the second ruffian, “shall we let them\par
go or not? If we stay here much longer, they will take the hint, and\par
march off without our leave. Let them be who they will, they are rich,\par
or why all those servants? Did you see the ring, he, you call the\par
Baron, had on his finger?—it was a diamond; but he has not got it on\par
now: he saw me looking at it, I warrant, and took it off.”\par
\par
“Aye, and then there is the picture; did you see that? She has not\par
taken that off,” observed the first ruffian, “it hangs at her neck; if\par
it had not sparkled so, I should not have found it out, for it was\par
almost hid by her dress; those are diamonds too, and a rare many of\par
them there must be, to go round such a large picture.”\par
\par
“But how are we to manage this business?” said the second ruffian: “let\par
us talk of that, there is no fear of there being booty enough, but how\par
are we to secure it?”\par
\par
“Aye, aye,” said his comrades, “let us talk of that, and remember no\par
time is to be lost.”\par
\par
“I am still for poison,” observed the third, “but consider their\par
number; why there are nine or ten of them, and armed too; when I saw so\par
many at the gate, I was not for letting them in, you know, nor you\par
either.”\par
\par
“I thought they might be some of our enemies,” replied the second, “I\par
did not so much mind numbers.”\par
\par
“But you must mind them now,” rejoined his comrade, “or it will be\par
worse for you. We are not more than six, and how can we master ten by\par
open force? I tell you we must give some of them a dose, and the rest\par
may then be managed.”\par
\par
“I’ll tell you a better way,” rejoined the other impatiently, “draw\par
closer.”\par
\par
Blanche, who had listened to this conversation, in an agony, which it\par
would be impossible to describe, could no longer distinguish what was\par
said, for the ruffians now spoke in lowered voices; but the hope, that\par
she might save her friends from the plot, if she could find her way\par
quickly to them, suddenly reanimated her spirits, and lent her strength\par
enough to turn her steps in search of the gallery. Terror, however, and\par
darkness conspired against her, and, having moved a few yards, the\par
feeble light, that issued from the chamber, no longer even contended\par
with the gloom, and, her foot stumbling over a step that crossed the\par
passage, she fell to the ground.\par
\par
The noise startled the banditti, who became suddenly silent, and then\par
all rushed to the passage, to examine whether any person was there, who\par
might have overheard their councils. Blanche saw them approaching, and\par
perceived their fierce and eager looks: but, before she could raise\par
herself, they discovered and seized her, and, as they dragged her\par
towards the chamber they had quitted, her screams drew from them\par
horrible threatenings.\par
\par
Having reached the room, they began to consult what they should do with\par
her. “Let us first know what she had heard,” said the chief robber.\par
“How long have you been in the passage, lady, and what brought you\par
there?”\par
\par
“Let us first secure that picture,” said one of his comrades,\par
approaching the trembling Blanche. “Fair lady, by your leave that\par
picture is mine; come, surrender it, or I shall seize it.”\par
\par
Blanche, entreating their mercy, immediately gave up the miniature,\par
while another of the ruffians fiercely interrogated her, concerning\par
what she had overheard of their conversation, when, her confusion and\par
terror too plainly telling what her tongue feared to confess, the\par
ruffians looked expressively upon one another, and two of them withdrew\par
to a remote part of the room, as if to consult further.\par
\par
“These are diamonds, by St. Peter!” exclaimed the fellow, who had been\par
examining the miniature, “and here is a very pretty picture too,\par
“faith; as handsome a young chevalier, as you would wish to see by a\par
summer’s sun. Lady, this is your spouse, I warrant, for it is the\par
spark, that was in your company just now.”\par
\par
Blanche, sinking with terror, conjured him to have pity on her, and,\par
delivering him her purse, promised to say nothing of what had passed,\par
if he would suffer her to return to her friends.\par
\par
He smiled ironically, and was going to reply, when his attention was\par
called off by a distant noise; and, while he listened, he grasped the\par
arm of Blanche more firmly, as if he feared she would escape from him,\par
and she again shrieked for help.\par
\par
The approaching sounds called the ruffians from the other part of the\par
chamber. “We are betrayed,” said they; “but let us listen a moment,\par
perhaps it is only our comrades come in from the mountains, and if so,\par
our work is sure; listen!”\par
\par
A distant discharge of shot confirmed this supposition for a moment,\par
but, in the next, the former sounds drawing nearer, the clashing of\par
swords, mingled with the voices of loud contention and with heavy\par
groans, were distinguished in the avenue leading to the chamber. While\par
the ruffians prepared their arms, they heard themselves called by some\par
of their comrades afar off, and then a shrill horn was sounded without\par
the fortress, a signal, it appeared, they too well understood; for\par
three of them, leaving the Lady Blanche to the care of the fourth,\par
instantly rushed from the chamber.\par
\par
While Blanche, trembling, and nearly fainting, was supplicating for\par
release, she heard amid the tumult, that approached, the voice of St.\par
Foix, and she had scarcely renewed her shriek, when the door of the\par
room was thrown open, and he appeared, much disfigured with blood, and\par
pursued by several ruffians. Blanche neither saw, nor heard any more;\par
her head swam, her sight failed, and she became senseless in the arms\par
of the robber, who had detained her.\par
\par
When she recovered, she perceived, by the gloomy light that trembled\par
round her, that she was in the same chamber, but neither the Count, St.\par
Foix, nor any other person appeared, and she continued, for some time,\par
entirely still, and nearly in a state of stupefaction. But, the\par
dreadful images of the past returning, she endeavoured to raise\par
herself, that she might seek her friends, when a sullen groan, at a\par
little distance, reminded her of St. Foix, and of the condition, in\par
which she had seen him enter this room; then, starting from the floor,\par
by a sudden effort of horror, she advanced to the place whence the\par
sound had proceeded, where a body was lying stretched upon the\par
pavement, and where, by the glimmering light of a lamp, she discovered\par
the pale and disfigured countenance of St. Foix. Her horrors, at that\par
moment, may be easily imagined. He was speechless; his eyes were half\par
closed, and, on the hand, which she grasped in the agony of despair,\par
cold damps had settled. While she vainly repeated his name, and called\par
for assistance, steps approached, and a person entered the chamber,\par
who, she soon perceived, was not the Count, her father; but, what was\par
her astonishment, when, supplicating him to give his assistance to St.\par
Foix, she discovered Ludovico! He scarcely paused to recognise her, but\par
immediately bound up the wounds of the Chevalier, and, perceiving, that\par
he had fainted probably from loss of blood, ran for water; but he had\par
been absent only a few moments, when Blanche heard other steps\par
approaching, and, while she was almost frantic with apprehension of the\par
ruffians, the light of a torch flashed upon the walls, and then Count\par
De Villefort appeared, with an affrighted countenance, and breathless\par
with impatience, calling upon his daughter. At the sound of his voice,\par
she rose, and ran to his arms, while he, letting fall the bloody sword\par
he held, pressed her to his bosom in a transport of gratitude and joy,\par
and then hastily enquired for St. Foix, who now gave some signs of\par
life. Ludovico soon after returning with water and brandy, the former\par
was applied to his lips, and the latter to his temples and hands, and\par
Blanche, at length, saw him unclose his eyes, and then heard him\par
enquire for her; but the joy she felt, on this occasion, was\par
interrupted by new alarms, when Ludovico said it would be necessary to\par
remove Mons. St. Foix immediately, and added, “The banditti, that are\par
out, my Lord, were expected home, an hour ago, and they will certainly\par
find us, if we delay. That shrill horn, they know, is never sounded by\par
their comrades but on most desperate occasions, and it echoes among the\par
mountains for many leagues round. I have known them brought home by its\par
sound even from the Pied de Melicant. Is anybody standing watch at the\par
great gate, my Lord?”\par
\par
“Nobody,” replied the Count; “the rest of my people are now scattered\par
about, I scarcely know where. Go, Ludovico, collect them together, and\par
look out yourself, and listen if you hear the feet of mules.”\par
\par
Ludovico then hurried away, and the Count consulted as to the means of\par
removing St. Foix, who could not have borne the motion of a mule, even\par
if his strength would have supported him in the saddle.\par
\par
While the Count was telling, that the banditti, whom they had found in\par
the fort, were secured in the dungeon, Blanche observed that he was\par
himself wounded, and that his left arm was entirely useless; but he\par
smiled at her anxiety, assuring her the wound was trifling.\par
\par
The Count’s servants, except two who kept watch at the gate, now\par
appeared, and, soon after, Ludovico. “I think I hear mules coming along\par
the glen, my Lord,” said he, “but the roaring of the torrent below will\par
not let me be certain; however, I have brought what will serve the\par
Chevalier,” he added, showing a bear’s skin, fastened to a couple of\par
long poles, which had been adapted for the purpose of bringing home\par
such of the banditti as happened to be wounded in their encounters.\par
Ludovico spread it on the ground, and, placing the skins of several\par
goats upon it, made a kind of bed, into which the Chevalier, who was\par
however now much revived, was gently lifted; and, the poles being\par
raised upon the shoulders of the guides, whose footing among these\par
steeps could best be depended upon, he was borne along with an easy\par
motion. Some of the Count’s servants were also wounded—but not\par
materially, and, their wounds being bound up, they now followed to the\par
great gate. As they passed along the hall, a loud tumult was heard at\par
some distance, and Blanche was terrified. “It is only those villains in\par
the dungeon, my Lady,” said Ludovico. “They seem to be bursting it\par
open,” said the Count. “No, my Lord,” replied Ludovico, “it has an iron\par
door; we have nothing to fear from them; but let me go first, and look\par
out from the rampart.”\par
\par
They quickly followed him, and found their mules browsing before the\par
gates, where the party listened anxiously, but heard no sound, except\par
that of the torrent below and of the early breeze, sighing among the\par
branches of the old oak, that grew in the court; and they were now glad\par
to perceive the first tints of dawn over the mountain-tops. When they\par
had mounted their mules, Ludovico, undertaking to be their guide, led\par
them by an easier path, than that by which they had formerly ascended,\par
into the glen. “We must avoid that valley to the east, my Lord,” said\par
he, “or we may meet the banditti; they went out that way in the\par
morning.”\par
\par
The travellers, soon after, quitted this glen, and found themselves in\par
a narrow valley that stretched towards the north-west. The morning\par
light upon the mountains now strengthened fast, and gradually\par
discovered the green hillocks, that skirted the winding feet of the\par
cliffs, tufted with cork tree, and ever-green oak. The thunder-clouds\par
being dispersed, had left the sky perfectly serene, and Blanche was\par
revived by the fresh breeze, and by the view of verdure, which the late\par
rain had brightened. Soon after, the sun arose, when the dripping\par
rocks, with the shrubs that fringed their summits, and many a turfy\par
slope below, sparkled in his rays. A wreath of mist was seen, floating\par
along the extremity of the valley, but the gale bore it before the\par
travellers, and the sunbeams gradually drew it up towards the summit of\par
the mountains. They had proceeded about a league, when, St. Foix having\par
complained of extreme faintness, they stopped to give him refreshment,\par
and, that the men, who bore him, might rest. Ludovico had brought from\par
the fort some flasks of rich Spanish wine, which now proved a reviving\par
cordial not only to St. Foix but to the whole party, though to him it\par
gave only temporary relief, for it fed the fever, that burned in his\par
veins, and he could neither disguise in his countenance the anguish he\par
suffered, nor suppress the wish, that he was arrived at the inn, where\par
they had designed to pass the preceding night.\par
\par
While they thus reposed themselves under the shade of the dark green\par
pines, the Count desired Ludovico to explain shortly, by what means he\par
had disappeared from the north apartment, how he came into the hands of\par
the banditti, and how he had contributed so essentially to serve him\par
and his family, for to him he justly attributed their present\par
deliverance. Ludovico was going to obey him, when suddenly they heard\par
the echo of a pistol-shot, from the way they had passed, and they rose\par
in alarm, hastily to pursue their route.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XIII\par
\par
Ah why did Fate his steps decoy\par
In stormy paths to roam,\par
Remote from all congenial joy!\par
                    BEATTIE\par
\par
\par
Emily, meanwhile, was still suffering anxiety as to the fate of\par
Valancourt; but Theresa, having, at length, found a person, whom she\par
could entrust on her errand to the steward, informed her, that the\par
messenger would return on the following day; and Emily promised to be\par
at the cottage, Theresa being too lame to attend her.\par
\par
In the evening, therefore, Emily set out alone for the cottage, with a\par
melancholy foreboding, concerning Valancourt, while, perhaps, the gloom\par
of the hour might contribute to depress her spirits. It was a grey\par
autumnal evening towards the close of the season; heavy mists partially\par
obscured the mountains, and a chilling breeze, that sighed among the\par
beech woods, strewed her path with some of their last yellow leaves.\par
These, circling in the blast and foretelling the death of the year,\par
gave an image of desolation to her mind, and, in her fancy, seemed to\par
announce the death of Valancourt. Of this she had, indeed, more than\par
once so strong a presentiment, that she was on the point of returning\par
home, feeling herself unequal to an encounter with the certainty she\par
anticipated, but, contending with her emotions, she so far commanded\par
them, as to be able to proceed.\par
\par
While she walked mournfully on, gazing on the long volumes of vapour,\par
that poured upon the sky, and watching the swallows, tossed along the\par
wind, now disappearing among tempestuous clouds, and then emerging, for\par
a moment, in circles upon the calmer air, the afflictions and\par
vicissitudes of her late life seemed portrayed in these fleeting\par
images;—thus had she been tossed upon the stormy sea of misfortune for\par
the last year, with but short intervals of peace, if peace that could\par
be called, which was only the delay of evils. And now, when she had\par
escaped from so many dangers, was become independent of the will of\par
those, who had oppressed her, and found herself mistress of a large\par
fortune, now, when she might reasonably have expected happiness, she\par
perceived that she was as distant from it as ever. She would have\par
accused herself of weakness and ingratitude in thus suffering a sense\par
of the various blessings she possessed to be overcome by that of a\par
single misfortune, had this misfortune affected herself alone; but,\par
when she had wept for Valancourt even as living, tears of compassion\par
had mingled with those of regret, and while she lamented a human being\par
degraded to vice, and consequently to misery, reason and humanity\par
claimed these tears, and fortitude had not yet taught her to separate\par
them from those of love; in the present moments, however, it was not\par
the certainty of his guilt, but the apprehension of his death (of a\par
death also, to which she herself, however innocently, appeared to have\par
been in some degree instrumental) that oppressed her. This fear\par
increased, as the means of certainty concerning it approached; and,\par
when she came within view of Theresa’s cottage, she was so much\par
disordered, and her resolution failed her so entirely, that, unable to\par
proceed, she rested on a bank, beside her path; where, as she sat, the\par
wind that groaned sullenly among the lofty branches above, seemed to\par
her melancholy imagination to bear the sounds of distant lamentation,\par
and, in the pauses of the gust, she still fancied she heard the feeble\par
and far-off notes of distress. Attention convinced her, that this was\par
no more than fancy; but the increasing gloom, which seemed the sudden\par
close of day, soon warned her to depart, and, with faltering steps, she\par
again moved toward the cottage. Through the casement appeared the\par
cheerful blaze of a wood fire, and Theresa, who had observed Emily\par
approaching, was already at the door to receive her.\par
\par
“It is a cold evening, madam,” said she, “storms are coming on, and I\par
thought you would like a fire. Do take this chair by the hearth.”\par
\par
Emily, thanking her for this consideration, sat down, and then, looking\par
in her face, on which the wood fire threw a gleam, she was struck with\par
its expression, and, unable to speak, sunk back in her chair with a\par
countenance so full of woe, that Theresa instantly comprehended the\par
occasion of it, but she remained silent. “Ah!” said Emily, at length,\par
“it is unnecessary for me to ask the result of your enquiry, your\par
silence, and that look, sufficiently explain it;—he is dead!”\par
\par
“Alas! my dear young lady,” replied Theresa, while tears filled her\par
eyes, “this world is made up of trouble! the rich have their share as\par
well as the poor! But we must all endeavour to bear what Heaven\par
pleases.”\par
\par
“He is dead, then!”—interrupted Emily—“Valancourt is dead!”\par
\par
“A-well-a-day! I fear he is,” replied Theresa.\par
\par
“You fear!” said Emily, “do you only fear?”\par
\par
“Alas! yes, madam, I fear he is! neither the steward, nor any of the\par
Epourville family, have heard of him since he left Languedoc, and the\par
Count is in great affliction about him, for he says he was always\par
punctual in writing, but that now he has not received a line from him,\par
since he left Languedoc; he appointed to be at home, three weeks ago,\par
but he has neither come, nor written, and they fear some accident has\par
befallen him. Alas! that ever I should live to cry for his death! I am\par
old, and might have died without being missed, but he”—Emily was faint,\par
and asked for some water, and Theresa, alarmed by the voice, in which\par
she spoke, hastened to her assistance, and, while she held the water to\par
Emily’s lips, continued, “My dear young mistress, do not take it so to\par
heart; the Chevalier may be alive and well, for all this; let us hope\par
the best!”\par
\par
“O no! I cannot hope,” said Emily, “I am acquainted with circumstances,\par
that will not suffer me to hope. I am somewhat better now, and can hear\par
what you have to say. Tell me, I entreat, the particulars of what you\par
know.”\par
\par
“Stay, till you are a little better, mademoiselle, you look sadly!”\par
\par
“O no, Theresa, tell me all, while I have the power to hear it,” said\par
Emily, “tell me all, I conjure you!”\par
\par
“Well, madam, I will then; but the steward did not say much, for\par
Richard says he seemed shy of talking about Mons. Valancourt, and what\par
he gathered was from Gabriel, one of the servants, who said he had\par
heard it from my lord’s gentleman.”\par
\par
“What did he hear?” said Emily.\par
\par
“Why, madam, Richard has but a bad memory, and could not remember half\par
of it, and, if I had not asked him a great many questions, I should\par
have heard little indeed. But he says that Gabriel said, that he and\par
all the other servants were in great trouble about M. Valancourt, for\par
that he was such a kind young gentleman, they all loved him, as well as\par
if he had been their own brother—and now, to think what was become of\par
him! For he used to be so courteous to them all, and, if any of them\par
had been in fault, M. Valancourt was the first to persuade my lord to\par
forgive them. And then, if any poor family was in distress, M.\par
Valancourt was the first, too, to relieve them, though some folks, not\par
a great way off, could have afforded that much better than he. And\par
then, said Gabriel, he was so gentle to everybody, and, for all he had\par
such a noble look with him, he never would command, and call about him,\par
as some of your quality people do, and we never minded him the less for\par
that. Nay, says Gabriel, for that matter, we minded him the more, and\par
would all have run to obey him at a word, sooner than if some folks had\par
told us what to do at full length; aye, and were more afraid of\par
displeasing him, too, than of them, that used rough words to us.”\par
\par
Emily, who no longer considered it to be dangerous to listen to praise,\par
bestowed on Valancourt, did not attempt to interrupt Theresa, but sat,\par
attentive to her words, though almost overwhelmed with grief. “My\par
Lord,” continued Theresa, “frets about M. Valancourt sadly, and the\par
more, because, they say, he had been rather harsh against him lately.\par
Gabriel says he had it from my Lord’s valet, that M. Valancourt had\par
_comported_ himself wildly at Paris, and had spent a great deal of\par
money, more a great deal than my Lord liked, for he loves money better\par
than M. Valancourt, who had been led astray sadly. Nay, for that\par
matter, M. Valancourt had been put into prison at Paris, and my Lord,\par
says Gabriel, refused to take him out, and said he deserved to suffer;\par
and, when old Gregoire, the butler, heard of this, he actually bought a\par
walking-stick to take with him to Paris, to visit his young master; but\par
the next thing we hear is, that M. Valancourt is coming home. O, it was\par
a joyful day when he came; but he was sadly altered, and my Lord looked\par
very cool upon him, and he was very sad, indeed. And, soon after, he\par
went away again into Languedoc, and, since that time, we have never\par
seen him.”\par
\par
Theresa paused, and Emily, sighing deeply, remained with her eyes fixed\par
upon the floor, without speaking. After a long pause, she enquired what\par
further Theresa had heard. “Yet why should I ask?” she added; “what you\par
have already told is too much. O Valancourt! thou art gone—for ever\par
gone! and I—I have murdered thee!” These words, and the countenance of\par
despair which accompanied them, alarmed Theresa, who began to fear,\par
that the shock of the intelligence Emily had just received, had\par
affected her senses. “My dear young lady, be composed,” said she, “and\par
do not say such frightful words. You murder M. Valancourt,—dear heart!”\par
Emily replied only by a heavy sigh.\par
\par
“Dear lady, it breaks my heart to see you look so,” said Theresa, “do\par
not sit with your eyes upon the ground, and all so pale and melancholy;\par
it frightens me to see you.” Emily was still silent, and did not appear\par
to hear anything that was said to her. “Besides, mademoiselle,”\par
continued Theresa, “M. Valancourt may be alive and merry yet, for what\par
we know.”\par
\par
At the mention of his name, Emily raised her eyes, and fixed them, in a\par
wild gaze, upon Theresa, as if she was endeavouring to understand what\par
had been said. “Aye, my dear lady,” said Theresa, mistaking the meaning\par
of this considerate air, “M. Valancourt may be alive and merry yet.”\par
\par
On the repetition of these words, Emily comprehended their import, but,\par
instead of producing the effect intended, they seemed only to heighten\par
her distress. She rose hastily from her chair, paced the little room,\par
with quick steps, and, often sighing deeply, clasped her hands, and\par
shuddered.\par
\par
Meanwhile, Theresa, with simple, but honest affection, endeavoured to\par
comfort her; put more wood on the fire, stirred it up into a brighter\par
blaze, swept the hearth, set the chair, which Emily had left, in a\par
warmer situation, and then drew forth from a cupboard a flask of wine.\par
“It is a stormy night, madam,” said she, “and blows cold—do come nearer\par
the fire, and take a glass of this wine; it will comfort you, as it has\par
done me, often and often, for it is not such wine as one gets every\par
day; it is rich Languedoc, and the last of six flasks that M.\par
Valancourt sent me, the night before he left Gascony for Paris. They\par
have served me, ever since, as cordials, and I never drink it, but I\par
think of him, and what kind words he said to me when he gave them.\par
‘Theresa,’ says he, ‘you are not young now, and should have a glass of\par
good wine, now and then. I will send you a few flasks, and, when you\par
taste them, you will sometimes remember me your friend.’ Yes—those were\par
his very words—me your friend!” Emily still paced the room, without\par
seeming to hear what Theresa said, who continued speaking. “And I have\par
remembered him, often enough, poor young gentleman!—for he gave me this\par
roof for a shelter, and that, which has supported me. Ah! he is in\par
heaven, with my blessed master, if ever saint was!”\par
\par
Theresa’s voice faltered; she wept, and set down the flask, unable to\par
pour out the wine. Her grief seemed to recall Emily from her own, who\par
went towards her, but then stopped, and, having gazed on her, for a\par
moment, turned suddenly away, as if overwhelmed by the reflection, that\par
it was Valancourt, whom Theresa lamented.\par
\par
While she yet paced the room, the still, soft note of an oboe, or\par
flute, was heard mingling with the blast, the sweetness of which\par
affected Emily’s spirits; she paused a moment in attention; the tender\par
tones, as they swelled along the wind, till they were lost again in the\par
ruder gust, came with a plaintiveness, that touched her heart, and she\par
melted into tears.\par
\par
“Aye,” said Theresa, drying her eyes, “there is Richard, our\par
neighbour’s son, playing on the oboe; it is sad enough, to hear such\par
sweet music now.” Emily continued to weep, without replying. “He often\par
plays of an evening,” added Theresa, “and, sometimes, the young folks\par
dance to the sound of his oboe. But, dear young lady! do not cry so;\par
and pray take a glass of this wine,” continued she, pouring some into a\par
glass, and handing it to Emily, who reluctantly took it.\par
\par
“Taste it for M. Valancourt’s sake,” said Theresa, as Emily lifted the\par
glass to her lips, “for he gave it me, you know, madam.” Emily’s hand\par
trembled, and she spilt the wine as she withdrew it from her lips. “For\par
whose sake!—who gave the wine?” said she in a faltering voice. “M.\par
Valancourt, dear lady. I knew you would be pleased with it. It is the\par
last flask I have left.”\par
\par
Emily set the wine upon the table, and burst into tears, while Theresa,\par
disappointed and alarmed, tried to comfort her; but she only waved her\par
hand, entreated she might be left alone, and wept the more.\par
\par
A knock at the cottage door prevented Theresa from immediately obeying\par
her mistress, and she was going to open it, when Emily, checking her,\par
requested she would not admit any person; but, afterwards,\par
recollecting, that she had ordered her servant to attend her home, she\par
said it was only Philippe, and endeavoured to restrain her tears, while\par
Theresa opened the door.\par
\par
A voice, that spoke without, drew Emily’s attention. She listened,\par
turned her eyes to the door, when a person now appeared, and\par
immediately a bright gleam, that flashed from the fire,\par
discovered—Valancourt!\par
\par
Emily, on perceiving him, started from her chair, trembled, and,\par
sinking into it again, became insensible to all around her.\par
\par
A scream from Theresa now told, that she knew Valancourt, whom her\par
imperfect sight, and the duskiness of the place had prevented her from\par
immediately recollecting; but his attention was immediately called from\par
her to the person, whom he saw, falling from a chair near the fire;\par
and, hastening to her assistance,—he perceived, that he was supporting\par
Emily! The various emotions, that seized him upon thus unexpectedly\par
meeting with her, from whom he had believed he had parted for ever, and\par
on beholding her pale and lifeless in his arms—may, perhaps, be\par
imagined, though they could neither be then expressed, nor now\par
described, any more than Emily’s sensations, when, at length, she\par
unclosed her eyes, and, looking up, again saw Valancourt. The intense\par
anxiety, with which he regarded her, was instantly changed to an\par
expression of mingled joy and tenderness, as his eye met hers, and he\par
perceived, that she was reviving. But he could only exclaim, “Emily!”\par
as he silently watched her recovery, while she averted her eye, and\par
feebly attempted to withdraw her hand; but, in these the first moments,\par
which succeeded to the pangs his supposed death had occasioned her, she\par
forgot every fault, which had formerly claimed indignation, and\par
beholding Valancourt such as he had appeared, when he won her early\par
affection, she experienced emotions of only tenderness and joy. This,\par
alas! was but the sunshine of a few short moments; recollections rose,\par
like clouds, upon her mind, and, darkening the illusive image, that\par
possessed it, she again beheld Valancourt, degraded—Valancourt unworthy\par
of the esteem and tenderness she had once bestowed upon him; her\par
spirits faltered, and, withdrawing her hand, she turned from him to\par
conceal her grief, while he, yet more embarrassed and agitated,\par
remained silent.\par
\par
A sense of what she owed to herself restrained her tears, and taught\par
her soon to overcome, in some degree, the emotions of mingled joy and\par
sorrow, that contended at her heart, as she rose, and, having thanked\par
him for the assistance he had given her, bade Theresa good evening. As\par
she was leaving the cottage, Valancourt, who seemed suddenly awakened\par
as from a dream, entreated, in a voice, that pleaded powerfully for\par
compassion, a few moments attention. Emily’s heart, perhaps, pleaded as\par
powerfully, but she had resolution enough to resist both, together with\par
the clamorous entreaties of Theresa, that she would not venture home\par
alone in the dark, and had already opened the cottage door, when the\par
pelting storm compelled her to obey their requests.\par
\par
Silent and embarrassed, she returned to the fire, while Valancourt,\par
with increasing agitation, paced the room, as if he wished, yet feared,\par
to speak, and Theresa expressed without restraint her joy and wonder\par
upon seeing him.\par
\par
“Dear heart! sir,” said she, “I never was so surprised and overjoyed in\par
my life. We were in great tribulation before you came, for we thought\par
you were dead, and were talking, and lamenting about you, just when you\par
knocked at the door. My young mistress there was crying, fit to break\par
her heart—”\par
\par
Emily looked with much displeasure at Theresa, but, before she could\par
speak, Valancourt, unable to repress the emotion, which Theresa’s\par
imprudent discovery occasioned, exclaimed, “O my Emily! am I then still\par
dear to you! Did you, indeed, honour me with a thought—a tear? O\par
heavens! you weep—you weep now!”\par
\par
“Theresa, sir,” said Emily, with a reserved air, and trying to conquer\par
her tears, “has reason to remember you with gratitude, and she was\par
concerned, because she had not lately heard of you. Allow me to thank\par
you for the kindness you have shown her, and to say, that, since I am\par
now upon the spot, she must not be further indebted to you.”\par
\par
“Emily,” said Valancourt, no longer master of his emotions, “is it thus\par
you meet him, whom once you meant to honour with your hand—thus you\par
meet him, who has loved you—suffered for you?—Yet what do I say? Pardon\par
me, pardon me, mademoiselle St. Aubert, I know not what I utter. I have\par
no longer any claim upon your remembrance—I have forfeited every\par
pretension to your esteem, your love. Yes! let me not forget, that I\par
once possessed your affections, though to know that I have lost them,\par
is my severest affliction. Affliction—do I call it!—that is a term of\par
mildness.”\par
\par
“Dear heart!” said Theresa, preventing Emily from replying, “talk of\par
once having her affections! Why, my dear young lady loves you now,\par
better than she does anybody in the whole world, though she pretends to\par
deny it.”\par
\par
“This is insupportable!” said Emily; “Theresa, you know not what you\par
say. Sir, if you respect my tranquillity, you will spare me from the\par
continuance of this distress.”\par
\par
“I do respect your tranquillity too much, voluntarily to interrupt it,”\par
replied Valancourt, in whose bosom pride now contended with tenderness;\par
“and will not be a voluntary intruder. I would have entreated a few\par
moments attention—yet I know not for what purpose. You have ceased to\par
esteem me, and to recount to you my sufferings will degrade me more,\par
without exciting even your pity. Yet I have been, O Emily! I am indeed\par
very wretched!” added Valancourt, in a voice, that softened from\par
solemnity into grief.\par
\par
“What! is my dear young master going out in all this rain!” said\par
Theresa. “No, he shall not stir a step. Dear! dear! to see how\par
gentlefolks can afford to throw away their happiness! Now, if you were\par
poor people, there would be none of this. To talk of unworthiness, and\par
not caring about one another, when I know there are not such a\par
kind-hearted lady and gentleman in the whole province, nor any that\par
love one another half so well, if the truth was spoken!”\par
\par
Emily, in extreme vexation, now rose from her chair, “I must be gone,”\par
said she, “the storm is over.”\par
\par
“Stay, Emily, stay, mademoiselle St. Aubert!” said Valancourt,\par
summoning all his resolution, “I will no longer distress you by my\par
presence. Forgive me, that I did not sooner obey you, and, if you can,\par
sometimes, pity one, who, in losing you—has lost all hope of peace! May\par
you be happy, Emily, however wretched I remain, happy as my fondest\par
wish would have you!”\par
\par
His voice faltered with the last words, and his countenance changed,\par
while, with a look of ineffable tenderness and grief, he gazed upon her\par
for an instant, and then quitted the cottage.\par
\par
“Dear heart! dear heart!” cried Theresa, following him to the door,\par
“why, Monsieur Valancourt! how it rains! what a night is this to turn\par
him out in! Why it will give him his death; and it was but now you were\par
crying, mademoiselle, because he was dead. Well! young ladies do change\par
their mind in a minute, as one may say!”\par
\par
Emily made no reply, for she heard not what was said, while, lost in\par
sorrow and thought, she remained in her chair by the fire, with her\par
eyes fixed, and the image of Valancourt still before them.\par
\par
“M. Valancourt is sadly altered! madam,” said Theresa; “he looks so\par
thin to what he used to do, and so melancholy, and then he wears his\par
arm in a sling.”\par
\par
Emily raised her eyes at these words, for she had not observed this\par
last circumstance, and she now did not doubt, that Valancourt had\par
received the shot of her gardener at Thoulouse; with this conviction\par
her pity for him returning, she blamed herself for having occasioned\par
him to leave the cottage, during the storm.\par
\par
Soon after her servants arrived with the carriage, and Emily, having\par
censured Theresa for her thoughtless conversation to Valancourt, and\par
strictly charging her never to repeat any hints of the same kind to\par
him, withdrew to her home, thoughtful and disconsolate.\par
\par
Meanwhile, Valancourt had returned to a little inn of the village,\par
whither he had arrived only a few moments before his visit to Theresa’s\par
cottage, on the way from Thoulouse to the château of the Count de\par
Duvarney, where he had not been since he bade adieu to Emily at\par
Château-le-Blanc, in the neighbourhood of which he had lingered for a\par
considerable time, unable to summon resolution enough to quit a place,\par
that contained the object most dear to his heart. There were times,\par
indeed, when grief and despair urged him to appear again before Emily,\par
and, regardless of his ruined circumstances, to renew his suit. Pride,\par
however, and the tenderness of his affection, which could not long\par
endure the thought of involving her in his misfortunes, at length, so\par
far triumphed over passion, that he relinquished this desperate design,\par
and quitted Château-le-Blanc. But still his fancy wandered among the\par
scenes, which had witnessed his early love, and, on his way to Gascony,\par
he stopped at Thoulouse, where he remained when Emily arrived,\par
concealing, yet indulging his melancholy in the gardens, where he had\par
formerly passed with her so many happy hours; often recurring, with\par
vain regret, to the evening before her departure for Italy, when she\par
had so unexpectedly met him on the terrace, and endeavouring to recall\par
to his memory every word and look, which had then charmed him, the\par
arguments he had employed to dissuade her from the journey, and the\par
tenderness of their last farewell. In such melancholy recollections he\par
had been indulging, when Emily unexpectedly arrived to him on this very\par
terrace, the evening after her arrival at Thoulouse. His emotions, on\par
thus seeing her, can scarcely be imagined; but he so far overcame the\par
first promptings of love, that he forbore to discover himself, and\par
abruptly quitted the gardens. Still, however, the vision he had seen\par
haunted his mind; he became more wretched than before, and the only\par
solace of his sorrow was to return in the silence of the night; to\par
follow the paths which he believed her steps had pressed, during the\par
day; and, to watch round the habitation where she reposed. It was in\par
one of these mournful wanderings, that he had received by the fire of\par
the gardener, who mistook him for a robber, a wound in his arm, which\par
had detained him at Thoulouse till very lately, under the hands of a\par
surgeon. There, regardless of himself and careless of his friends,\par
whose late unkindness had urged him to believe, that they were\par
indifferent as to his fate, he remained, without informing them of his\par
situation; and now, being sufficiently recovered to bear travelling, he\par
had taken La Vallée in his way to Estuvière, the Count’s residence,\par
partly for the purpose of hearing of Emily, and of being again near\par
her, and partly for that of enquiring into the situation of poor old\par
Theresa, who, he had reason to suppose, had been deprived of her\par
stipend, small as it was, and which enquiry had brought him to her\par
cottage, when Emily happened to be there.\par
\par
This unexpected interview, which had at once shown him the tenderness\par
of her love and the strength of her resolution, renewed all the\par
acuteness of the despair, that had attended their former separation,\par
and which no effort of reason could teach him, in these moments, to\par
subdue. Her image, her look, the tones of her voice, all dwelt on his\par
fancy, as powerfully as they had late appeared to his senses, and\par
banished from his heart every emotion, except those of love and\par
despair.\par
\par
Before the evening concluded, he returned to Theresa’s cottage, that he\par
might hear her talk of Emily, and be in the place, where she had so\par
lately been. The joy, felt and expressed by that faithful servant, was\par
quickly changed to sorrow, when she observed, at one moment, his wild\par
and frenzied look, and, at another, the dark melancholy, that overhung\par
him.\par
\par
After he had listened, and for a considerable time, to all she had to\par
relate, concerning Emily, he gave Theresa nearly all the money he had\par
about him, though she repeatedly refused it, declaring, that her\par
mistress had amply supplied her wants; and then, drawing a ring of\par
value from his finger, he delivered it her with a solemn charge to\par
present it to Emily, of whom he entreated, as a last favour, that she\par
would preserve it for his sake, and sometimes, when she looked upon it,\par
remember the unhappy giver.\par
\par
Theresa wept, as she received the ring, but it was more from sympathy,\par
than from any presentiment of evil; and before she could reply,\par
Valancourt abruptly left the cottage. She followed him to the door,\par
calling upon his name and entreating him to return; but she received no\par
answer, and saw him no more.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XIV\par
\par
Call up him, that left half told\par
The story of Cambuscan bold.\par
                    MILTON\par
\par
\par
On the following morning, as Emily sat in the parlour adjoining the\par
library, reflecting on the scene of the preceding night, Annette rushed\par
wildly into the room, and, without speaking, sunk breathless into a\par
chair. It was some time before she could answer the anxious enquiries\par
of Emily, as to the occasion of her emotion, but, at length, she\par
exclaimed, “I have seen his ghost, madam, I have seen his ghost!”\par
\par
“Who do you mean?” said Emily, with extreme impatience.\par
\par
“It came in from the hall, madam,” continued Annette, “as I was\par
crossing to the parlour.”\par
\par
“Who are you speaking of?” repeated Emily, “Who came in from the hall?”\par
\par
“It was dressed just as I have seen him, often and often,” added\par
Annette. “Ah! who could have thought—”\par
\par
Emily’s patience was now exhausted, and she was reprimanding her for\par
such idle fancies, when a servant entered the room, and informed her,\par
that a stranger without begged leave to speak with her.\par
\par
It immediately occurred to Emily, that this stranger was Valancourt,\par
and she told the servant to inform him, that she was engaged, and could\par
not see any person.\par
\par
The servant, having delivered his message, returned with one from the\par
stranger, urging the first request, and saying, that he had something\par
of consequence to communicate; while Annette, who had hitherto sat\par
silent and amazed, now started up, and crying, “It is Ludovico!—it is\par
Ludovico!” ran out of the room. Emily bade the servant follow her, and,\par
if it really was Ludovico, to show him into the parlour.\par
\par
In a few minutes, Ludovico appeared, accompanied by Annette, who, as\par
joy rendered her forgetful of all rules of decorum towards her\par
mistress, would not suffer any person to be heard, for some time, but\par
herself. Emily expressed surprise and satisfaction, on seeing Ludovico\par
in safety, and the first emotions increased, when he delivered letters\par
from Count De Villefort and the Lady Blanche, informing her of their\par
late adventure, and of their present situation at an inn among the\par
Pyrenees, where they had been detained by the illness of Mons. St.\par
Foix, and the indisposition of Blanche, who added, that the Baron St.\par
Foix was just arrived to attend his son to his château, where he would\par
remain till the perfect recovery of his wounds, and then return to\par
Languedoc, but that her father and herself purposed to be at La Vallée,\par
on the following day. She added, that Emily’s presence would be\par
expected at the approaching nuptials, and begged she would be prepared\par
to proceed, in a few days to Château-le-Blanc. For an account of\par
Ludovico’s adventure, she referred her to himself; and Emily, though\par
much interested, concerning the means, by which he had disappeared from\par
the north apartments, had the forbearance to suspend the gratification\par
of her curiosity, till he had taken some refreshment, and had conversed\par
with Annette, whose joy, on seeing him in safety, could not have been\par
more extravagant, had he arisen from the grave.\par
\par
Meanwhile, Emily perused again the letters of her friends, whose\par
expressions of esteem and kindness were very necessary consolations to\par
her heart, awakened as it was by the late interview to emotions of\par
keener sorrow and regret.\par
\par
The invitation to Château-le-Blanc was pressed with so much kindness by\par
the Count and his daughter, who strengthened it by a message from the\par
Countess, and the occasion of it was so important to her friend, that\par
Emily could not refuse to accept it, nor, though she wished to remain\par
in the quiet shades of her native home, could she avoid perceiving the\par
impropriety of remaining there alone, since Valancourt was again in the\par
neighbourhood. Sometimes, too, she thought, that change of scenery and\par
the society of her friends might contribute, more than retirement, to\par
restore her to tranquillity.\par
\par
When Ludovico again appeared, she desired him to give a detail of his\par
adventure in the north apartments, and to tell by what means he became\par
a companion of the banditti, with whom the Count had found him.\par
\par
He immediately obeyed, while Annette, who had not yet had leisure to\par
ask him many questions, on the subject, prepared to listen, with a\par
countenance of extreme curiosity, venturing to remind her lady of her\par
incredulity, concerning spirits, in the castle of Udolpho, and of her\par
own sagacity in believing in them; while Emily, blushing at the\par
consciousness of her late credulity, observed, that, if Ludovico’s\par
adventure could justify Annette’s superstition, he had probably not\par
been here to relate it.\par
\par
Ludovico smiled at Annette, and bowed to Emily, and then began as\par
follows:\par
\par
“You may remember, madam, that, on the night, when I sat up in the\par
north chamber, my lord, the Count, and Mons. Henri accompanied me\par
thither, and that, while they remained there, nothing happened to\par
excite any alarm. When they were gone I made a fire in the bedroom,\par
and, not being inclined to sleep, I sat down on the hearth with a book\par
I had brought with me to divert my mind. I confess I did sometimes look\par
round the chamber, with something like apprehension—”\par
\par
“O very like it, I dare say,” interrupted Annette, “and I dare say too,\par
if the truth was known, you shook from head to foot.”\par
\par
“Not quite so bad as that,” replied Ludovico, smiling, “but several\par
times, as the wind whistled round the castle, and shook the old\par
casements, I did fancy I heard odd noises, and, once or twice, I got up\par
and looked about me; but nothing was to be seen, except the grim\par
figures in the tapestry, which seemed to frown upon me, as I looked at\par
them. I had sat thus for above an hour,” continued Ludovico, “when\par
again I thought I heard a noise, and glanced my eyes round the room, to\par
discover what it came from, but, not perceiving anything, I began to\par
read again, and, when I had finished the story I was upon, I felt\par
drowsy, and dropped asleep. But presently I was awakened by the noise I\par
had heard before, and it seemed to come from that part of the chamber,\par
where the bed stood; and then, whether it was the story I had been\par
reading that affected my spirits, or the strange reports, that had been\par
spread of these apartments, I don’t know, but, when I looked towards\par
the bed again, I fancied I saw a man’s face within the dusky curtains.”\par
\par
At the mention of this, Emily trembled, and looked anxiously,\par
remembering the spectacle she had herself witnessed there with\par
Dorothée.\par
\par
“I confess, madam, my heart did fail me, at that instant,” continued\par
Ludovico, “but a return of the noise drew my attention from the bed,\par
and I then distinctly heard a sound, like that of a key, turning in a\par
lock, but what surprised me more was, that I saw no door where the\par
sound seemed to come from. In the next moment, however, the arras near\par
the bed was slowly lifted, and a person appeared behind it, entering\par
from a small door in the wall. He stood for a moment as if half\par
retreating, with his head bending under the arras which concealed the\par
upper part of his face except his eyes scowling beneath the tapestry as\par
he held it; and then, while he raised it higher, I saw the face of\par
another man behind, looking over his shoulder. I know not how it was,\par
but, though my sword was upon the table before me, I had not the power\par
just then to seize it, but sat quite still, watching them, with my eyes\par
half shut as if I was asleep. I suppose they thought me so, and were\par
debating what they should do, for I heard them whisper, and they stood\par
in the same posture for the value of a minute, and then, I thought I\par
perceived other faces in the duskiness beyond the door, and heard\par
louder whispers.”\par
\par
“This door surprises me,” said Emily, “because I understood, that the\par
Count had caused the arras to be lifted, and the walls examined,\par
suspecting, that they might have concealed a passage through which you\par
had departed.”\par
\par
“It does not appear so extraordinary to me, madam,” replied Ludovico,\par
“that this door should escape notice, because it was formed in a narrow\par
compartment, which appeared to be part of the outward wall, and, if the\par
Count had not passed over it, he might have thought it was useless to\par
search for a door where it seemed as if no passage could communicate\par
with one; but the truth was, that the passage was formed within the\par
wall itself.—But, to return to the men, whom I saw obscurely beyond the\par
door, and who did not suffer me to remain long in suspense, concerning\par
their design. They all rushed into the room, and surrounded me, though\par
not before I had snatched up my sword to defend myself. But what could\par
one man do against four? They soon disarmed me, and, having fastened my\par
arms, and gagged my mouth, forced me through the private door, leaving\par
my sword upon the table, to assist, as they said, those who should come\par
in the morning to look for me, in fighting against the ghosts. They\par
then led me through many narrow passages, cut, as I fancied, in the\par
walls, for I had never seen them before, and down several flights of\par
steps, till we came to the vaults underneath the castle; and then\par
opening a stone door, which I should have taken for the wall itself, we\par
went through a long passage, and down other steps cut in the solid\par
rock, when another door delivered us into a cave. After turning and\par
twining about, for some time, we reached the mouth of it, and I found\par
myself on the sea-beach at the foot of the cliffs, with the château\par
above. A boat was in waiting, into which the ruffians got, forcing me\par
along with them, and we soon reached a small vessel, that was at\par
anchor, where other men appeared, when setting me aboard, two of the\par
fellows who had seized me, followed, and the other two rowed back to\par
the shore, while we set sail. I soon found out what all this meant, and\par
what was the business of these men at the château. We landed in\par
Rousillon, and, after lingering several days about the shore, some of\par
their comrades came down from the mountains, and carried me with them\par
to the fort, where I remained till my Lord so unexpectedly arrived, for\par
they had taken good care to prevent my running away, having blindfolded\par
me, during the journey, and, if they had not done this, I think I never\par
could have found my road to any town, through the wild country we\par
traversed. After I reached the fort I was watched like a prisoner, and\par
never suffered to go out, without two or three companions, and I became\par
so weary of life, that I often wished to get rid of it.”\par
\par
“Well, but they let you talk,” said Annette, “they did not gagg you\par
after they got you away from the château, so I don’t see what reason\par
there was to be so very weary of living; to say nothing about the\par
chance you had of seeing me again.”\par
\par
Ludovico smiled, and Emily also, who enquired what was the motive of\par
these men for carrying him off.\par
\par
“I soon found out, madam,” resumed Ludovico, “that they were pirates,\par
who had, during many years, secreted their spoil in the vaults of the\par
castle, which, being so near the sea, suited their purpose well. To\par
prevent detection they had tried to have it believed, that the château\par
was haunted, and, having discovered the private way to the north\par
apartments, which had been shut up ever since the death of the lady\par
marchioness, they easily succeeded. The housekeeper and her husband,\par
who were the only persons, that had inhabited the castle, for some\par
years, were so terrified by the strange noises they heard in the\par
nights, that they would live there no longer; a report soon went\par
abroad, that it was haunted, and the whole country believed this the\par
more readily, I suppose, because it had been said, that the lady\par
marchioness had died in a strange way, and because my lord never would\par
return to the place afterwards.”\par
\par
“But why,” said Emily, “were not these pirates contented with the\par
cave—why did they think it necessary to deposit their spoil in the\par
castle?”\par
\par
“The cave, madam,” replied Ludovico, “was open to anybody, and their\par
treasures would not long have remained undiscovered there, but in the\par
vaults they were secure so long as the report prevailed of their being\par
haunted. Thus then, it appears, that they brought at midnight, the\par
spoil they took on the seas, and kept it till they had opportunities of\par
disposing of it to advantage. The pirates were connected with Spanish\par
smugglers and banditti, who live among the wilds of the Pyrenees, and\par
carry on various kinds of traffic, such as nobody would think of; and\par
with this desperate horde of banditti I remained, till my lord arrived.\par
I shall never forget what I felt, when I first discovered him—I almost\par
gave him up for lost! but I knew, that, if I showed myself, the\par
banditti would discover who he was, and probably murder us all, to\par
prevent their secret in the château being detected. I, therefore, kept\par
out of my lord’s sight, but had a strict watch upon the ruffians, and\par
determined, if they offered him or his family violence, to discover\par
myself, and fight for our lives. Soon after, I overheard some of them\par
laying a most diabolical plan for the murder and plunder of the whole\par
party, when I contrived to speak to some of my lord’s attendants,\par
telling them what was going forward, and we consulted what was best to\par
be done; meanwhile my lord, alarmed at the absence of the Lady Blanche,\par
demanded her, and the ruffians having given some unsatisfactory answer,\par
my lord and Mons. St. Foix became furious, so then we thought it a good\par
time to discover the plot, and rushing into the chamber, I called out,\par
‘Treachery! my lord count, defend yourself!’ His lordship and the\par
chevalier drew their swords directly, and a hard battle we had, but we\par
conquered at last, as, madam, you are already informed of by my Lord\par
Count.”\par
\par
“This is an extraordinary adventure,” said Emily, “and much praise is\par
due, Ludovico, to your prudence and intrepidity. There are some\par
circumstances, however, concerning the north apartments, which still\par
perplex me; but, perhaps, you may be able to explain them. Did you ever\par
hear the banditti relate anything extraordinary of these rooms?”\par
\par
“No, madam,” replied Ludovico, “I never heard them speak about the\par
rooms, except to laugh at the credulity of the old housekeeper, who\par
once was very near catching one of the pirates; it was since the Count\par
arrived at the château, he said, and he laughed heartily as he related\par
the trick he had played off.”\par
\par
A blush overspread Emily’s cheek, and she impatiently desired Ludovico\par
to explain himself.\par
\par
“Why, my lady,” said he, “as this fellow was, one night in the bedroom,\par
he heard somebody approaching through the next apartment, and not\par
having time to lift up the arras, and unfasten the door, he hid himself\par
in the bed just by. There he lay for some time in as great a fright, I\par
suppose—”\par
\par
“As you were in,” interrupted Annette, “when you sat up so boldly to\par
watch by yourself.”\par
\par
“Aye,” said Ludovico, “in as great a fright as he ever made anybody\par
else suffer; and presently the housekeeper and some other person came\par
up to the bed, when he, thinking they were going to examine it,\par
bethought him, that his only chance of escaping detection, was by\par
terrifying them; so he lifted up the counterpane, but that did not do,\par
till he raised his face above it, and then they both set off, he said,\par
as if they had seen the devil, and he got out of the rooms\par
undiscovered.”\par
\par
Emily could not forbear smiling at this explanation of the deception,\par
which had given her so much superstitious terror, and was surprised,\par
that she could have suffered herself to be thus alarmed, till she\par
considered, that, when the mind has once begun to yield to the weakness\par
of superstition, trifles impress it with the force of conviction.\par
Still, however, she remembered with awe the mysterious music, which had\par
been heard, at midnight, near Château-le-Blanc, and she asked Ludovico\par
if he could give any explanation of it; but he could not.\par
\par
“I only know, madam,” he added, “that it did not belong to the pirates,\par
for I have heard them laugh about it, and say, they believed the devil\par
was in league with them there.”\par
\par
“Yes, I will answer for it he was,” said Annette, her countenance\par
brightening, “I was sure all along, that he or his spirits had\par
something to do with the north apartments, and now you see, madam, I am\par
right at last.”\par
\par
“It cannot be denied, that his spirits were very busy in that part of\par
the château,” replied Emily, smiling. “But I am surprised, Ludovico,\par
that these pirates should persevere in their schemes, after the arrival\par
of the Count; what could they expect but certain detection?”\par
\par
“I have reason to believe, madam,” replied Ludovico, “that it was their\par
intention to persevere no longer than was necessary for the removal of\par
the stores, which were deposited in the vaults; and it appeared, that\par
they had been employed in doing so from within a short period after the\par
Count’s arrival; but, as they had only a few hours in the night for\par
this business, and were carrying on other schemes at the same time, the\par
vaults were not above half emptied, when they took me away. They\par
gloried exceedingly in this opportunity of confirming the superstitious\par
reports, that had been spread of the north chambers, were careful to\par
leave everything there as they had found it, the better to promote the\par
deception, and frequently, in their jocose moods, would laugh at the\par
consternation, which they believed the inhabitants of the castle had\par
suffered upon my disappearing, and it was to prevent the possibility of\par
my betraying their secret, that they had removed me to such a distance.\par
From that period they considered the château as nearly their own; but I\par
found from the discourse of their comrades, that, though they were\par
cautious, at first, in showing their power there, they had once very\par
nearly betrayed themselves. Going, one night, as was their custom, to\par
the north chambers to repeat the noises, that had occasioned such alarm\par
among the servants, they heard, as they were about to unfasten the\par
secret door, voices in the bedroom. My lord has since told me, that\par
himself and M. Henri were then in the apartment, and they heard very\par
extraordinary sounds of lamentation, which it seems were made by these\par
fellows, with their usual design of spreading terror; and my lord has\par
owned, he then felt somewhat more, than surprise; but, as it was\par
necessary to the peace of his family, that no notice should be taken,\par
he was silent on the subject, and enjoined silence to his son.”\par
\par
Emily, recollecting the change, that had appeared in the spirits of the\par
Count, after the night, when he had watched in the north room, now\par
perceived the cause of it; and, having made some further enquiries upon\par
this strange affair, she dismissed Ludovico, and went to give orders\par
for the accommodation of her friends, on the following day.\par
\par
In the evening, Theresa, lame as she was, came to deliver the ring,\par
with which Valancourt had entrusted her, and, when she presented it,\par
Emily was much affected, for she remembered to have seen him wear it\par
often in happier days. She was, however, much displeased, that Theresa\par
had received it, and positively refused to accept it herself, though to\par
have done so would have afforded her a melancholy pleasure. Theresa\par
entreated, expostulated, and then described the distress of Valancourt,\par
when he had given the ring, and repeated the message, with which he had\par
commissioned her to deliver it; and Emily could not conceal the extreme\par
sorrow this recital occasioned her, but wept, and remained lost in\par
thought.\par
\par
“Alas! my dear young lady!” said Theresa, “why should all this be? I\par
have known you from your infancy, and it may well be supposed I love\par
you, as if you were my own, and wish as much to see you happy. M.\par
Valancourt, to be sure, I have not known so long, but then I have\par
reason to love him, as though he was my own son. I know how well you\par
love one another, or why all this weeping and wailing?” Emily waved her\par
hand for Theresa to be silent, who, disregarding the signal, continued,\par
“And how much you are alike in your tempers and ways, and, that, if you\par
were married, you would be the happiest couple in the whole\par
province—then what is there to prevent your marrying? Dear dear! to see\par
how some people fling away their happiness, and then cry and lament\par
about it, just as if it was not their own doing, and as if there was\par
more pleasure in wailing and weeping, than in being at peace. Learning,\par
to be sure, is a fine thing, but, if it teaches folks no better than\par
that, why I had rather be without it; if it would teach them to be\par
happier, I would say something to it, then it would be learning and\par
wisdom too.”\par
\par
Age and long services had given Theresa a privilege to talk, but Emily\par
now endeavoured to check her loquacity, and, though she felt the\par
justness of some of her remarks, did not choose to explain the\par
circumstances, that had determined her conduct towards Valancourt. She,\par
therefore, only told Theresa, that it would much displease her to hear\par
the subject renewed; that she had reasons for her conduct, which she\par
did not think it proper to mention, and that the ring must be returned,\par
with an assurance, that she could not accept it with propriety; and, at\par
the same time, she forbade Theresa to repeat any future message from\par
Valancourt, as she valued her esteem and kindness. Theresa was\par
afflicted, and made another attempt, though feeble, to interest her for\par
Valancourt, but the unusual displeasure, expressed in Emily’s\par
countenance, soon obliged her to desist, and she departed in wonder and\par
lamentation.\par
\par
To relieve her mind, in some degree, from the painful recollections,\par
that intruded upon it, Emily busied herself in preparations for the\par
journey into Languedoc, and, while Annette, who assisted her, spoke\par
with joy and affection of the safe return of Ludovico, she was\par
considering how she might best promote their happiness, and determined,\par
if it appeared, that his affection was as unchanged as that of the\par
simple and honest Annette, to give her a marriage portion, and settle\par
them on some part of her estate. These considerations led her to the\par
remembrance of her father’s paternal domain, which his affairs had\par
formerly compelled him to dispose of to M. Quesnel, and which she\par
frequently wished to regain, because St. Aubert had lamented, that the\par
chief lands of his ancestors had passed into another family, and\par
because they had been his birth-place and the haunt of his early years.\par
To the estate at Thoulouse she had no peculiar attachment, and it was\par
her wish to dispose of this, that she might purchase her paternal\par
domains, if M. Quesnel could be prevailed on to part with them, which,\par
as he talked much of living in Italy, did not appear very improbable.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XV\par
\par
Sweet is the breath of vernal shower,\par
The bees’ collected treasures sweet,\par
Sweet music’s melting fall, but sweeter yet\par
The still, small voice of gratitude.\par
                    GRAY\par
\par
\par
On the following day, the arrival of her friend revived the drooping\par
Emily, and La Vallée became once more the scene of social kindness and\par
of elegant hospitality. Illness and the terror she had suffered had\par
stolen from Blanche much of her sprightliness, but all her affectionate\par
simplicity remained, and, though she appeared less blooming, she was\par
not less engaging than before. The unfortunate adventure on the\par
Pyrenees had made the Count very anxious to reach home, and, after\par
little more than a week’s stay at La Vallée, Emily prepared to set out\par
with her friends for Languedoc, assigning the care of her house, during\par
her absence, to Theresa. On the evening, preceding her departure, this\par
old servant brought again the ring of Valancourt, and, with tears,\par
entreated her mistress to receive it, for that she had neither seen,\par
nor heard of M. Valancourt, since the night when he delivered it to\par
her. As she said this, her countenance expressed more alarm, than she\par
dared to utter; but Emily, checking her own propensity to fear,\par
considered, that he had probably returned to the residence of his\par
brother, and, again refusing to accept the ring, bade Theresa preserve\par
it, till she saw him, which, with extreme reluctance, she promised to\par
do.\par
\par
On the following day, Count De Villefort, with Emily and the Lady\par
Blanche, left La Vallée, and, on the ensuing evening, arrived at the\par
Château-le-Blanc, where the Countess, Henri, and M. Du Pont, whom Emily\par
was surprised to find there, received them with much joy and\par
congratulation. She was concerned to observe, that the Count still\par
encouraged the hopes of his friend, whose countenance declared, that\par
his affection had suffered no abatement from absence; and was much\par
distressed, when, on the second evening after her arrival, the Count,\par
having withdrawn her from the Lady Blanche, with whom she was walking,\par
renewed the subject of M. Du Pont’s hopes. The mildness, with which she\par
listened to his intercessions at first, deceiving him, as to her\par
sentiments, he began to believe, that, her affection for Valancourt\par
being overcome, she was, at length, disposed to think favourably of M.\par
Du Pont; and, when she afterwards convinced him of his mistake, he\par
ventured, in the earnestness of his wish to promote what he considered\par
to be the happiness of two persons, whom he so much esteemed, gently to\par
remonstrate with her, on thus suffering an ill-placed affection to\par
poison the happiness of her most valuable years.\par
\par
Observing her silence and the deep dejection of her countenance, he\par
concluded with saying, “I will not say more now, but I will still\par
believe, my dear Mademoiselle St. Aubert, that you will not always\par
reject a person, so truly estimable as my friend Du Pont.”\par
\par
He spared her the pain of replying, by leaving her; and she strolled\par
on, somewhat displeased with the Count for having persevered to plead\par
for a suit, which she had repeatedly rejected, and lost amidst the\par
melancholy recollections, which this topic had revived, till she had\par
insensibly reached the borders of the woods, that screened the\par
monastery of St. Clair, when, perceiving how far she had wandered, she\par
determined to extend her walk a little farther, and to enquire about\par
the abbess and some of her friends among the nuns.\par
\par
Though the evening was now drawing to a close, she accepted the\par
invitation of the friar, who opened the gate, and, anxious to meet some\par
of her old acquaintances, proceeded towards the convent parlour. As she\par
crossed the lawn, that sloped from the front of the monastery towards\par
the sea, she was struck with the picture of repose, exhibited by some\par
monks, sitting in the cloisters, which extended under the brow of the\par
woods, that crowned this eminence; where, as they meditated, at this\par
twilight hour, holy subjects, they sometimes suffered their attention\par
to be relieved by the scene before them, nor thought it profane to look\par
at nature, now that it had exchanged the brilliant colours of day for\par
the sober hue of evening. Before the cloisters, however, spread an\par
ancient chesnut, whose ample branches were designed to screen the full\par
magnificence of a scene, that might tempt the wish to worldly\par
pleasures; but still, beneath the dark and spreading foliage, gleamed a\par
wide extent of ocean, and many a passing sail; while, to the right and\par
left, thick woods were seen stretching along the winding shores. So\par
much as this had been admitted, perhaps, to give to the secluded votary\par
an image of the dangers and vicissitudes of life, and to console him,\par
now that he had renounced its pleasures, by the certainty of having\par
escaped its evils. As Emily walked pensively along, considering how\par
much suffering she might have escaped, had she become a votaress of the\par
order, and remained in this retirement from the time of her father’s\par
death, the vesper-bell struck up, and the monks retired slowly toward\par
the chapel, while she, pursuing her way, entered the great hall, where\par
an unusual silence seemed to reign. The parlour too, which opened from\par
it, she found vacant, but, as the evening bell was sounding, she\par
believed the nuns had withdrawn into the chapel, and sat down to rest,\par
for a moment, before she returned to the château, where, however, the\par
increasing gloom made her now anxious to be.\par
\par
Not many minutes had elapsed, before a nun, entering in haste, enquired\par
for the abbess, and was retiring, without recollecting Emily, when she\par
made herself known, and then learned, that a mass was going to be\par
performed for the soul of sister Agnes, who had been declining, for\par
some time, and who was now believed to be dying.\par
\par
Of her sufferings the sister gave a melancholy account, and of the\par
horrors, into which she had frequently started, but which had now\par
yielded to a dejection so gloomy, that neither the prayers, in which\par
she was joined by the sisterhood, nor the assurances of her confessor,\par
had power to recall her from it, or to cheer her mind even with a\par
momentary gleam of comfort.\par
\par
To this relation Emily listened with extreme concern, and, recollecting\par
the frenzied manners and the expressions of horror, which she had\par
herself witnessed of Agnes, together with the history, that sister\par
Frances had communicated, her compassion was heightened to a very\par
painful degree. As the evening was already far advanced, Emily did not\par
now desire to see her, or to join in the mass, and, after leaving many\par
kind remembrances with the nun, for her old friends, she quitted the\par
monastery, and returned over the cliffs towards the château, meditating\par
upon what she had just heard, till, at length she forced her mind upon\par
less interesting subjects.\par
\par
The wind was high, and as she drew near the château, she often paused\par
to listen to its awful sound, as it swept over the billows, that beat\par
below, or groaned along the surrounding woods; and, while she rested on\par
a cliff at a short distance from the château, and looked upon the wide\par
waters, seen dimly beneath the last shade of twilight, she thought of\par
the following address:\par
\par
TO THE WINDS\par
\par
Viewless, through heaven’s vast vault your course ye steer,\par
Unknown from whence ye come, or whither go!\par
Mysterious pow’rs! I hear ye murmur low,\par
Till swells your loud gust on my startled ear,\par
And, awful! seems to say—some God is near!\par
I love to list your midnight voices float\par
In the dread storm, that o’er the ocean rolls,\par
And, while their charm the angry wave controls,\par
Mix with its sullen roar, and sink remote.\par
Then, rising in the pause, a sweeter note,\par
The dirge of spirits, who your deeds bewail,\par
A sweeter note oft swells while sleeps the gale!\par
But soon, ye sightless pow’rs! your rest is o’er,\par
Solemn and slow, ye rise upon the air,\par
Speak in the shrouds, and bid the sea-boy fear,\par
And the faint-warbled dirge—is heard no more!\par
Oh! then I deprecate your awful reign!\par
The loud lament yet bear not on your breath!\par
Bear not the crash of bark far on the main,\par
Bear not the cry of men, who cry in vain,\par
The crew’s dread chorus sinking into death!\par
Oh! give not these, ye pow’rs!  I ask alone,\par
As rapt I climb these dark romantic steeps,\par
The elemental war, the billow’s moan;\par
I ask the still, sweet tear, that listening Fancy weeps!\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XVI\par
\par
Unnatural deeds\par
Do breed unnatural troubles:  infected minds\par
To their deaf pillows will discharge their secrets.\par
More needs she the divine, than the physician.\par
                    MACBETH\par
\par
\par
On the following evening, the view of the convent towers, rising among\par
the shadowy woods, reminded Emily of the nun, whose condition had so\par
much affected her; and, anxious to know how she was, as well as to see\par
some of her former friends, she and the Lady Blanche extended their\par
walk to the monastery. At the gate stood a carriage, which, from the\par
heat of the horses, appeared to have just arrived; but a more than\par
common stillness pervaded the court and the cloisters, through which\par
Emily and Blanche passed in their way to the great hall, where a nun,\par
who was crossing to the staircase, replied to the enquiries of the\par
former, that sister Agnes was still living, and sensible, but that it\par
was thought she could not survive the night. In the parlour, they found\par
several of the boarders, who rejoiced to see Emily, and told her many\par
little circumstances that had happened in the convent since her\par
departure, and which were interesting to her only because they related\par
to persons, whom she had regarded with affection. While they thus\par
conversed the abbess entered the room, and expressed much satisfaction\par
at seeing Emily, but her manner was unusually solemn, and her\par
countenance dejected. “Our house,” said she, after the first\par
salutations were over, “is truly a house of mourning—a daughter is now\par
paying the debt of nature.—You have heard, perhaps, that our daughter\par
Agnes is dying?”\par
\par
Emily expressed her sincere concern.\par
\par
“Her death presents to us a great and awful lesson,” continued the\par
abbess; “let us read it, and profit by it; let it teach us to prepare\par
ourselves for the change, that awaits us all! You are young, and have\par
it yet in your power to secure ‘the peace that passeth all\par
understanding’—the peace of conscience. Preserve it in your youth, that\par
it may comfort you in age; for vain, alas! and imperfect are the good\par
deeds of our latter years, if those of our early life have been evil!”\par
\par
Emily would have said, that good deeds, she hoped, were never vain; but\par
she considered that it was the abbess who spoke, and she remained\par
silent.\par
\par
“The latter days of Agnes,” resumed the abbess, “have been exemplary;\par
would they might atone for the errors of her former ones! Her\par
sufferings now, alas! are great; let us believe, that they will make\par
her peace hereafter! I have left her with her confessor, and a\par
gentleman, whom she has long been anxious to see, and who is just\par
arrived from Paris. They, I hope, will be able to administer the\par
repose, which her mind has hitherto wanted.”\par
\par
Emily fervently joined in the wish.\par
\par
“During her illness, she has sometimes named you,” resumed the abbess;\par
“perhaps, it would comfort her to see you; when her present visitors\par
have left her, we will go to her chamber, if the scene will not be too\par
melancholy for your spirits. But, indeed, to such scenes, however\par
painful, we ought to accustom ourselves, for they are salutary to the\par
soul, and prepare us for what we are ourselves to suffer.”\par
\par
Emily became grave and thoughtful; for this conversation brought to her\par
recollection the dying moments of her beloved father, and she wished\par
once more to weep over the spot, where his remains were buried. During\par
the silence, which followed the abbess’ speech, many minute\par
circumstances attending his last hours occurred to her—his emotion on\par
perceiving himself to be in the neighbourhood of Château-le-Blanc—his\par
request to be interred in a particular spot in the church of this\par
monastery—and the solemn charge he had delivered to her to destroy\par
certain papers, without examining them.—She recollected also the\par
mysterious and horrible words in those manuscripts, upon which her eye\par
had involuntarily glanced; and, though they now, and, indeed, whenever\par
she remembered them, revived an excess of painful curiosity, concerning\par
their full import, and the motives for her father’s command, it was\par
ever her chief consolation, that she had strictly obeyed him in this\par
particular.\par
\par
Little more was said by the abbess, who appeared too much affected by\par
the subject she had lately left, to be willing to converse, and her\par
companions had been for some time silent from the same cause, when this\par
general reverie was interrupted by the entrance of a stranger, Monsieur\par
Bonnac, who had just quitted the chamber of sister Agnes. He appeared\par
much disturbed, but Emily fancied, that his countenance had more the\par
expression of horror, than of grief. Having drawn the abbess to a\par
distant part of the room, he conversed with her for some time, during\par
which she seemed to listen with earnest attention, and he to speak with\par
caution, and a more than common degree of interest. When he had\par
concluded, he bowed silently to the rest of the company, and quitted\par
the room. The abbess, soon after, proposed going to the chamber of\par
sister Agnes, to which Emily consented, though not without some\par
reluctance, and Lady Blanche remained with the boarders below.\par
\par
At the door of the chamber they met the confessor, whom, as he lifted\par
up his head on their approach, Emily observed to be the same that had\par
attended her dying father; but he passed on, without noticing her, and\par
they entered the apartment, where, on a mattress, was laid sister\par
Agnes, with one nun watching in the chair beside her. Her countenance\par
was so much changed, that Emily would scarcely have recollected her,\par
had she not been prepared to do so: it was ghastly, and overspread with\par
gloomy horror; her dim and hollow eyes were fixed on a crucifix, which\par
she held upon her bosom; and she was so much engaged in thought, as not\par
to perceive the abbess and Emily, till they stood at the bedside. Then,\par
turning her heavy eyes, she fixed them, in wild horror, upon Emily,\par
and, screaming, exclaimed, “Ah! that vision comes upon me in my dying\par
hours!”\par
\par
Emily started back in terror, and looked for explanation to the abbess,\par
who made her a signal not to be alarmed, and calmly said to Agnes,\par
“Daughter, I have brought Mademoiselle St. Aubert to visit you: I\par
thought you would be glad to see her.”\par
\par
Agnes made no reply; but, still gazing wildly upon Emily, exclaimed,\par
“It is her very self! Oh! there is all that fascination in her look,\par
which proved my destruction! What would you have—what is it you came to\par
demand—Retribution?—It will soon be yours—it is yours already. How many\par
years have passed, since last I saw you! My crime is but as\par
yesterday.—Yet I am grown old beneath it; while you are still young and\par
blooming—blooming as when you forced me to commit that most abhorred\par
deed! O! could I once forget it!—yet what would that avail?—the deed is\par
done!”\par
\par
Emily, extremely shocked, would now have left the room; but the abbess,\par
taking her hand, tried to support her spirits, and begged she would\par
stay a few moments, when Agnes would probably be calm, whom now she\par
tried to sooth. But the latter seemed to disregard her, while she still\par
fixed her eyes on Emily, and added, “What are years of prayers and\par
repentance? they cannot wash out the foulness of murder!—Yes, murder!\par
Where is he—where is he?—Look there—look there!—see where he stalks\par
along the room! Why do you come to torment me now?” continued Agnes,\par
while her straining eyes were bent on air, “why was not I punished\par
before?—O! do not frown so sternly! Hah! there again! ’tis she herself!\par
Why do you look so piteously upon me—and smile, too? smile on me! What\par
groan was that?”\par
\par
Agnes sunk down, apparently lifeless, and Emily, unable to support\par
herself, leaned against the bed, while the abbess and the attendant nun\par
were applying the usual remedies to Agnes. “Peace,” said the abbess,\par
when Emily was going to speak, “the delirium is going off, she will\par
soon revive. When was she thus before, daughter?”\par
\par
“Not of many weeks, madam,” replied the nun, “but her spirits have been\par
much agitated by the arrival of the gentleman she wished so much to\par
see.”\par
\par
“Yes,” observed the abbess, “that has undoubtedly occasioned this\par
paroxysm of frenzy. When she is better, we will leave her to repose.”\par
\par
Emily very readily consented, but, though she could now give little\par
assistance, she was unwilling to quit the chamber, while any might be\par
necessary.\par
\par
When Agnes recovered her senses, she again fixed her eyes on Emily, but\par
their wild expression was gone, and a gloomy melancholy had succeeded.\par
It was some moments before she recovered sufficient spirits to speak;\par
she then said feebly—“The likeness is wonderful!—surely it must be\par
something more than fancy. Tell me, I conjure you,” she added,\par
addressing Emily, “though your name is St. Aubert, are you not the\par
daughter of the Marchioness?”\par
\par
“What Marchioness?” said Emily, in extreme surprise; for she had\par
imagined, from the calmness of Agnes’s manner, that her intellects were\par
restored. The abbess gave her a significant glance, but she repeated\par
the question.\par
\par
“What Marchioness?” exclaimed Agnes, “I know but of one—the Marchioness\par
de Villeroi.”\par
\par
Emily, remembering the emotion of her late father, upon the unexpected\par
mention of this lady, and his request to be laid near to the tomb of\par
the Villerois, now felt greatly interested, and she entreated Agnes to\par
explain the reason of her question. The abbess would now have withdrawn\par
Emily from the room, who being, however, detained by a strong interest,\par
repeated her entreaties.\par
\par
“Bring me that casket, sister,” said Agnes; “I will show her to you;\par
yet you need only look in that mirror, and you will behold her; you\par
surely are her daughter: such striking resemblance is never found but\par
among near relations.”\par
\par
The nun brought the casket, and Agnes, having directed her how to\par
unlock it, she took thence a miniature, in which Emily perceived the\par
exact resemblance of the picture, which she had found among her late\par
father’s papers. Agnes held out her hand to receive it; gazed upon it\par
earnestly for some moments in silence; and then, with a countenance of\par
deep despair, threw up her eyes to Heaven, and prayed inwardly. When\par
she had finished, she returned the miniature to Emily. “Keep it,” said\par
she, “I bequeath it to you, for I must believe it is your right. I have\par
frequently observed the resemblance between you; but never, till this\par
day, did it strike upon my conscience so powerfully! Stay, sister, do\par
not remove the casket—there is another picture I would show.”\par
\par
Emily trembled with expectation, and the abbess again would have\par
withdrawn her. “Agnes is still disordered,” said she, “you observe how\par
she wanders. In these moods she says anything, and does not scruple, as\par
you have witnessed, to accuse herself of the most horrible crimes.”\par
\par
Emily, however, thought she perceived something more than madness in\par
the inconsistencies of Agnes, whose mention of the Marchioness, and\par
production of her picture, had interested her so much, that she\par
determined to obtain further information, if possible, respecting the\par
subject of it.\par
\par
The nun returned with the casket, and, Agnes pointing out to her a\par
secret drawer, she took from it another miniature. “Here,” said Agnes,\par
as she offered it to Emily, “learn a lesson for your vanity, at least;\par
look well at this picture, and see if you can discover any resemblance\par
between what I was, and what I am.”\par
\par
Emily impatiently received the miniature, which her eyes had scarcely\par
glanced upon, before her trembling hands had nearly suffered it to\par
fall—it was the resemblance of the portrait of Signora Laurentini,\par
which she had formerly seen in the castle of Udolpho—the lady, who had\par
disappeared in so mysterious a manner, and whom Montoni had been\par
suspected of having caused to be murdered.\par
\par
In silent astonishment, Emily continued to gaze alternately upon the\par
picture and the dying nun, endeavouring to trace a resemblance between\par
them, which no longer existed.\par
\par
“Why do you look so sternly on me?” said Agnes, mistaking the nature of\par
Emily’s emotion.\par
\par
“I have seen this face before,” said Emily, at length; “was it really\par
your resemblance?”\par
\par
“You may well ask that question,” replied the nun,—“but it was once\par
esteemed a striking likeness of me. Look at me well, and see what guilt\par
has made me. I then was innocent; the evil passions of my nature slept.\par
Sister!” added she solemnly, and stretching forth her cold, damp hand\par
to Emily, who shuddered at its touch—“Sister! beware of the first\par
indulgence of the passions; beware of the first! Their course, if not\par
checked then, is rapid—their force is uncontrollable—they lead us we\par
know not whither—they lead us perhaps to the commission of crimes, for\par
which whole years of prayer and penitence cannot atone!—Such may be the\par
force of even a single passion, that it overcomes every other, and\par
sears up every other approach to the heart. Possessing us like a fiend,\par
it leads us on to the acts of a fiend, making us insensible to pity and\par
to conscience. And, when its purpose is accomplished, like a fiend, it\par
leaves us to the torture of those feelings, which its power had\par
suspended—not annihilated,—to the tortures of compassion, remorse, and\par
conscience. Then, we awaken as from a dream, and perceive a new world\par
around us—we gaze in astonishment, and horror—but the deed is\par
committed; not all the powers of heaven and earth united can undo\par
it—and the spectres of conscience will not fly! What are\par
riches—grandeur—health itself, to the luxury of a pure conscience, the\par
health of the soul;—and what the sufferings of poverty, disappointment,\par
despair—to the anguish of an afflicted one! O! how long is it since I\par
knew that luxury! I believed, that I had suffered the most agonizing\par
pangs of human nature, in love, jealousy, and despair—but these pangs\par
were ease, compared with the stings of conscience, which I have since\par
endured. I tasted too what was called the sweet of revenge—but it was\par
transient, it expired even with the object, that provoked it. Remember,\par
sister, that the passions are the seeds of vices as well as of virtues,\par
from which either may spring, accordingly as they are nurtured. Unhappy\par
they who have never been taught the art to govern them!”\par
\par
“Alas! unhappy!” said the abbess, “and ill-informed of our holy\par
religion!” Emily listened to Agnes, in silent awe, while she still\par
examined the miniature, and became confirmed in her opinion of its\par
strong resemblance to the portrait at Udolpho. “This face is familiar\par
to me,” said she, wishing to lead the nun to an explanation, yet\par
fearing to discover too abruptly her knowledge of Udolpho.\par
\par
“You are mistaken,” replied Agnes, “you certainly never saw that\par
picture before.”\par
\par
“No,” replied Emily, “but I have seen one extremely like it.”\par
“Impossible,” said Agnes, who may now be called the Lady Laurentini.\par
\par
“It was in the castle of Udolpho,” continued Emily, looking steadfastly\par
at her.\par
\par
“Of Udolpho!” exclaimed Laurentini, “of Udolpho in Italy!” “The same,”\par
replied Emily.\par
\par
“You know me then,” said Laurentini, “and you are the daughter of the\par
Marchioness.” Emily was somewhat surprised at this abrupt assertion. “I\par
am the daughter of the late Mons. St. Aubert,” said she; “and the lady\par
you name is an utter stranger to me.”\par
\par
“At least you believe so,” rejoined Laurentini.\par
\par
Emily asked what reasons there could be to believe otherwise.\par
\par
“The family likeness, that you bear her,” said the nun. “The\par
Marchioness, it is known, was attached to a gentleman of Gascony, at\par
the time when she accepted the hand of the Marquis, by the command of\par
her father. Ill-fated, unhappy woman!”\par
\par
Emily, remembering the extreme emotion which St. Aubert had betrayed on\par
the mention of the Marchioness, would now have suffered something more\par
than surprise, had her confidence in his integrity been less; as it\par
was, she could not, for a moment, believe what the words of Laurentini\par
insinuated; yet she still felt strongly interested, concerning them,\par
and begged, that she would explain them further.\par
\par
“Do not urge me on that subject,” said the nun, “it is to me a terrible\par
one! Would that I could blot it from my memory!” She sighed deeply,\par
and, after the pause of a moment, asked Emily, by what means she had\par
discovered her name?\par
\par
“By your portrait in the castle of Udolpho, to which this miniature\par
bears a striking resemblance,” replied Emily.\par
\par
“You have been at Udolpho then!” said the nun, with great emotion.\par
“Alas! what scenes does the mention of it revive in my fancy—scenes of\par
happiness—of suffering—and of horror!”\par
\par
At this moment, the terrible spectacle, which Emily had witnessed in a\par
chamber of that castle, occurred to her, and she shuddered, while she\par
looked upon the nun—and recollected her late words—that “years of\par
prayer and penitence could not wash out the foulness of murder.” She\par
was now compelled to attribute these to another cause, than that of\par
delirium. With a degree of horror, that almost deprived her of sense,\par
she now believed she looked upon a murderer; all the recollected\par
behaviour of Laurentini seemed to confirm the supposition, yet Emily\par
was still lost in a labyrinth of perplexities, and, not knowing how to\par
ask the questions, which might lead to truth, she could only hint them\par
in broken sentences.\par
\par
“Your sudden departure from Udolpho”—said she.\par
\par
Laurentini groaned.\par
\par
“The reports that followed it,” continued Emily—“The west chamber—the\par
mournful veil—the object it conceals!—when murders are committed—”\par
\par
The nun shrieked. “What! there again!” said she, endeavouring to raise\par
herself, while her starting eyes seemed to follow some object round the\par
room—“Come from the grave! What! Blood—blood too!—There was no\par
blood—thou canst not say it!—Nay, do not smile,—do not smile so\par
piteously!”\par
\par
Laurentini fell into convulsions, as she uttered the last words; and\par
Emily, unable any longer to endure the horror of the scene, hurried\par
from the room, and sent some nuns to the assistance of the abbess.\par
\par
The Lady Blanche, and the boarders, who were in the parlour, now\par
assembled round Emily, and, alarmed by her manner and affrighted\par
countenance, asked a hundred questions, which she avoided answering\par
further, than by saying, that she believed sister Agnes was dying. They\par
received this as a sufficient explanation of her terror, and had then\par
leisure to offer restoratives, which, at length, somewhat revived\par
Emily, whose mind was, however, so much shocked with the terrible\par
surmises, and perplexed with doubts by some words from the nun, that\par
she was unable to converse, and would have left the convent\par
immediately, had she not wished to know whether Laurentini would\par
survive the late attack. After waiting some time, she was informed,\par
that, the convulsions having ceased, Laurentini seemed to be reviving,\par
and Emily and Blanche were departing, when the abbess appeared, who,\par
drawing the former aside, said she had something of consequence to say\par
to her, but, as it was late, she would not detain her then, and\par
requested to see her on the following day.\par
\par
Emily promised to visit her, and, having taken leave, returned with the\par
Lady Blanche towards the château, on the way to which the deep gloom of\par
the woods made Blanche lament, that the evening was so far advanced;\par
for the surrounding stillness and obscurity rendered her sensible of\par
fear, though there was a servant to protect her; while Emily was too\par
much engaged by the horrors of the scene she had just witnessed, to be\par
affected by the solemnity of the shades, otherwise than as they served\par
to promote her gloomy reverie, from which, however, she was at length\par
recalled by the Lady Blanche, who pointed out, at some distance, in the\par
dusky path they were winding, two persons slowly advancing. It was\par
impossible to avoid them without striking into a still more secluded\par
part of the wood, whither the strangers might easily follow; but all\par
apprehension vanished, when Emily distinguished the voice of Mons. Du\par
Pont, and perceived, that his companion was the gentleman, whom she had\par
seen at the monastery, and who was now conversing with so much\par
earnestness as not immediately to perceive their approach. When Du Pont\par
joined the ladies, the stranger took leave, and they proceeded to the\par
château, where the Count, when he heard of Mons. Bonnac, claimed him\par
for an acquaintance, and, on learning the melancholy occasion of his\par
visit to Languedoc, and that he was lodged at a small inn in the\par
village, begged the favour of Mons. Du Pont to invite him to the\par
château.\par
\par
The latter was happy to do so, and the scruples of reserve, which made\par
M. Bonnac hesitate to accept the invitation, being at length overcome,\par
they went to the château, where the kindness of the Count and the\par
sprightliness of his son were exerted to dissipate the gloom, that\par
overhung the spirits of the stranger. M. Bonnac was an officer in the\par
French service, and appeared to be about fifty; his figure was tall and\par
commanding, his manners had received the last polish, and there was\par
something in his countenance uncommonly interesting; for over features,\par
which, in youth, must have been remarkably handsome, was spread a\par
melancholy, that seemed the effect of long misfortune, rather than of\par
constitution, or temper.\par
\par
The conversation he held, during supper, was evidently an effort of\par
politeness, and there were intervals in which, unable to struggle\par
against the feelings, that depressed him, he relapsed into silence and\par
abstraction, from which, however, the Count, sometimes, withdrew him in\par
a manner so delicate and benevolent, that Emily, while she observed\par
him, almost fancied she beheld her late father.\par
\par
The party separated, at an early hour, and then, in the solitude of her\par
apartment, the scenes, which Emily had lately witnessed, returned to\par
her fancy, with dreadful energy. That in the dying nun she should have\par
discovered Signora Laurentini, who, instead of having been murdered by\par
Montoni, was, as it now seemed, herself guilty of some dreadful crime,\par
excited both horror and surprise in a high degree; nor did the hints,\par
which she had dropped, respecting the marriage of the Marchioness de\par
Villeroi, and the enquiries she had made concerning Emily’s birth,\par
occasion her a less degree of interest, though it was of a different\par
nature.\par
\par
The history, which sister Frances had formerly related, and had said to\par
be that of Agnes, it now appeared, was erroneous; but for what purpose\par
it had been fabricated, unless the more effectually to conceal the true\par
story, Emily could not even guess. Above all, her interest was excited\par
as to the relation, which the story of the late Marchioness de Villeroi\par
bore to that of her father; for, that some kind of relation existed\par
between them, the grief of St. Aubert, upon hearing her named, his\par
request to be buried near her, and her picture, which had been found\par
among his papers, certainly proved. Sometimes it occurred to Emily,\par
that he might have been the lover, to whom it was said the Marchioness\par
was attached, when she was compelled to marry the Marquis de Villeroi;\par
but that he had afterwards cherished a passion for her, she could not\par
suffer herself to believe, for a moment. The papers, which he had so\par
solemnly enjoined her to destroy, she now fancied had related to this\par
connection, and she wished more earnestly than before to know the\par
reasons, that made him consider the injunction necessary, which, had\par
her faith in his principles been less, would have led to believe, that\par
there was a mystery in her birth dishonourable to her parents, which\par
those manuscripts might have revealed.\par
\par
Reflections, similar to these, engaged her mind, during the greater\par
part of the night, and when, at length, she fell into a slumber, it was\par
only to behold a vision of the dying nun, and to awaken in horrors,\par
like those she had witnessed.\par
\par
On the following morning, she was too much indisposed to attend her\par
appointment with the abbess, and, before the day concluded, she heard,\par
that sister Agnes was no more. Mons. Bonnac received this intelligence,\par
with concern; but Emily observed, that he did not appear so much\par
affected now, as on the preceding evening, immediately after quitting\par
the apartment of the nun, whose death was probably less terrible to\par
him, than the confession he had been then called upon to witness.\par
However this might be, he was perhaps consoled, in some degree, by a\par
knowledge of the legacy bequeathed him, since his family was large, and\par
the extravagance of some part of it had lately been the means of\par
involving him in great distress, and even in the horrors of a prison;\par
and it was the grief he had suffered from the wild career of a\par
favourite son, with the pecuniary anxieties and misfortunes consequent\par
upon it, that had given to his countenance the air of dejection, which\par
had so much interested Emily.\par
\par
To his friend Mons. Du Pont he recited some particulars of his late\par
sufferings, when it appeared, that he had been confined for several\par
months in one of the prisons of Paris, with little hope of release, and\par
without the comfort of seeing his wife, who had been absent in the\par
country, endeavouring, though in vain, to procure assistance from his\par
friends. When, at length, she had obtained an order for admittance, she\par
was so much shocked at the change, which long confinement and sorrow\par
had made in his appearance, that she was seized with fits, which, by\par
their long continuance, threatened her life.\par
\par
“Our situation affected those, who happened to witness it,” continued\par
Mons. Bonnac, “and one generous friend, who was in confinement at the\par
same time, afterwards employed the first moments of his liberty in\par
efforts to obtain mine. He succeeded; the heavy debt, that oppressed\par
me, was discharged; and, when I would have expressed my sense of the\par
obligation I had received, my benefactor was fled from my search. I\par
have reason to believe he was the victim of his own generosity, and\par
that he returned to the state of confinement, from which he had\par
released me; but every enquiry after him was unsuccessful. Amiable and\par
unfortunate Valancourt!”\par
\par
“Valancourt!” exclaimed Mons. Du Pont. “Of what family?”\par
\par
“The Valancourts, Counts Duvarney,” replied Mons. Bonnac.\par
\par
The emotion of Mons. Du Pont, when he discovered the generous\par
benefactor of his friend to be the rival of his love, can only be\par
imagined; but, having overcome his first surprise, he dissipated the\par
apprehensions of Mons. Bonnac by acquainting him, that Valancourt was\par
at liberty, and had lately been in Languedoc; after which his affection\par
for Emily prompted him to make some enquiries, respecting the conduct\par
of his rival, during his stay at Paris, of which M. Bonnac appeared to\par
be well informed. The answers he received were such as convinced him,\par
that Valancourt had been much misrepresented, and, painful as was the\par
sacrifice, he formed the just design of relinquishing his pursuit of\par
Emily to a lover, who, it now appeared, was not unworthy of the regard,\par
with which she honoured him.\par
\par
The conversation of Mons. Bonnac discovered, that Valancourt, some time\par
after his arrival at Paris, had been drawn into the snares, which\par
determined vice had spread for him, and that his hours had been chiefly\par
divided between the parties of the captivating Marchioness and those\par
gaming assemblies, to which the envy, or the avarice, of his brother\par
officers had spared no art to seduce him. In these parties he had lost\par
large sums, in efforts to recover small ones, and to such losses the\par
Count De Villefort and Mons. Henri had been frequent witnesses. His\par
resources were, at length, exhausted; and the Count, his brother,\par
exasperated by his conduct, refused to continue the supplies necessary\par
to his present mode of life, when Valancourt, in consequence of\par
accumulated debts, was thrown into confinement, where his brother\par
suffered him to remain, in the hope, that punishment might effect a\par
reform of conduct, which had not yet been confirmed by long habit.\par
\par
In the solitude of his prison, Valancourt had leisure for reflection,\par
and cause for repentance; here, too, the image of Emily, which, amidst\par
the dissipation of the city had been obscured, but never obliterated\par
from his heart, revived with all the charms of innocence and beauty, to\par
reproach him for having sacrificed his happiness and debased his\par
talents by pursuits, which his nobler faculties would formerly have\par
taught him to consider were as tasteless as they were degrading. But,\par
though his passions had been seduced, his heart was not depraved, nor\par
had habit riveted the chains, that hung heavily on his conscience; and,\par
as he retained that energy of will, which was necessary to burst them,\par
he, at length, emancipated himself from the bondage of vice, but not\par
till after much effort and severe suffering.\par
\par
Being released by his brother from the prison, where he had witnessed\par
the affecting meeting between Mons. Bonnac and his wife, with whom he\par
had been for some time acquainted, the first use of his liberty formed\par
a striking instance of his humanity and his rashness; for with nearly\par
all the money, just received from his brother, he went to a\par
gaming-house, and gave it as a last stake for the chance of restoring\par
his friend to freedom, and to his afflicted family. The event was\par
fortunate, and, while he had awaited the issue of this momentous stake,\par
he made a solemn vow never again to yield to the destructive and\par
fascinating vice of gaming.\par
\par
Having restored the venerable Mons. Bonnac to his rejoicing family, he\par
hurried from Paris to Estuvière; and, in the delight of having made the\par
wretched happy, forgot, for a while, his own misfortunes. Soon,\par
however, he remembered, that he had thrown away the fortune, without\par
which he could never hope to marry Emily; and life, unless passed with\par
her, now scarcely appeared supportable; for her goodness, refinement,\par
and simplicity of heart, rendered her beauty more enchanting, if\par
possible, to his fancy, than it had ever yet appeared. Experience had\par
taught him to understand the full value of the qualities, which he had\par
before admired, but which the contrasted characters he had seen in the\par
world made him now adore; and these reflections, increasing the pangs\par
of remorse and regret, occasioned the deep dejection, that had\par
accompanied him even into the presence of Emily, of whom he considered\par
himself no longer worthy. To the ignominy of having received pecuniary\par
obligations from the Marchioness Chamfort, or any other lady of\par
intrigue, as the Count De Villefort had been informed, or of having\par
been engaged in the depredating schemes of gamesters, Valancourt had\par
never submitted; and these were some of such scandals as often mingle\par
with truth, against the unfortunate. Count De Villefort had received\par
them from authority which he had no reason to doubt, and which the\par
imprudent conduct he had himself witnessed in Valancourt, had certainly\par
induced him the more readily to believe. Being such as Emily could not\par
name to the Chevalier, he had no opportunity of refuting them; and,\par
when he confessed himself to be unworthy of her esteem, he little\par
suspected, that he was confirming to her the most dreadful calumnies.\par
Thus the mistake had been mutual, and had remained so, when Mons.\par
Bonnac explained the conduct of his generous, but imprudent young\par
friend to Du Pont, who, with severe justice, determined not only to\par
undeceive the Count on this subject, but to resign all hope of Emily.\par
Such a sacrifice as his love rendered this, was deserving of a noble\par
reward, and Mons. Bonnac, if it had been possible for him to forget the\par
benevolent Valancourt, would have wished that Emily might accept the\par
just Du Pont.\par
\par
When the Count was informed of the error he had committed, he was\par
extremely shocked at the consequence of his credulity, and the account\par
which Mons. Bonnac gave of his friend’s situation, while at Paris,\par
convinced him, that Valancourt had been entrapped by the schemes of a\par
set of dissipated young men, with whom his profession had partly\par
obliged him to associate, rather than by an inclination to vice; and,\par
charmed by the humanity, and noble, though rash generosity, which his\par
conduct towards Mons. Bonnac exhibited, he forgave him the transient\par
errors, that had stained his youth, and restored him to the high degree\par
of esteem, with which he had regarded him, during their early\par
acquaintance. But, as the least reparation he could now make Valancourt\par
was to afford him an opportunity of explaining to Emily his former\par
conduct, he immediately wrote, to request his forgiveness of the\par
unintentional injury he had done him, and to invite him to\par
Château-le-Blanc. Motives of delicacy withheld the Count from informing\par
Emily of this letter, and of kindness from acquainting her with the\par
discovery respecting Valancourt, till his arrival should save her from\par
the possibility of anxiety, as to its event; and this precaution spared\par
her even severer inquietude, than the Count had foreseen, since he was\par
ignorant of the symptoms of despair, which Valancourt’s late conduct\par
had betrayed.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XVII\par
\par
But in these cases,\par
We still have judgment here; that we but teach\par
Bloody instructions, which, being taught, return\par
To plague the inventor:  thus even-handed justice\par
Commends the ingredients of our poison’d chalice\par
To our own lips.\par
                    MACBETH\par
\par
\par
Some circumstances of an extraordinary nature now withdrew Emily from\par
her own sorrows, and excited emotions, which partook of both surprise\par
and horror.\par
\par
A few days followed that, on which Signora Laurentini died, her will\par
was opened at the monastery, in the presence of the superiors and Mons.\par
Bonnac, when it was found, that one third of her personal property was\par
bequeathed to the nearest surviving relative of the late Marchioness de\par
Villeroi, and that Emily was the person.\par
\par
With the secret of Emily’s family the abbess had long been acquainted,\par
and it was in observance of the earnest request of St. Aubert, who was\par
known to the friar, that attended him on his death-bed, that his\par
daughter had remained in ignorance of her relationship to the\par
Marchioness. But some hints, which had fallen from Signora Laurentini,\par
during her last interview with Emily, and a confession of a very\par
extraordinary nature, given in her dying hours, had made the abbess\par
think it necessary to converse with her young friend, on the topic she\par
had not before ventured to introduce; and it was for this purpose, that\par
she had requested to see her on the morning that followed her interview\par
with the nun. Emily’s indisposition had then prevented the intended\par
conversation; but now, after the will had been examined, she received a\par
summons, which she immediately obeyed, and became informed of\par
circumstances, that powerfully affected her. As the narrative of the\par
abbess was, however, deficient in many particulars, of which the reader\par
may wish to be informed, and the history of the nun is materially\par
connected with the fate of the Marchioness de Villeroi, we shall omit\par
the conversation, that passed in the parlour of the convent, and mingle\par
with our relation a brief history of\par
\par
LAURENTINI DI UDOLPHO,\par
\par
\par
who was the only child of her parents, and heiress of the ancient house\par
of Udolpho, in the territory of Venice. It was the first misfortune of\par
her life, and that which led to all her succeeding misery, that the\par
friends, who ought to have restrained her strong passions, and mildly\par
instructed her in the art of governing them, nurtured them by early\par
indulgence. But they cherished their own failings in her; for their\par
conduct was not the result of rational kindness, and, when they either\par
indulged, or opposed the passions of their child, they gratified their\par
own. Thus they indulged her with weakness, and reprehended her with\par
violence; her spirit was exasperated by their vehemence, instead of\par
being corrected by their wisdom; and their oppositions became contest\par
for victory, in which the due tenderness of the parents, and the\par
affectionate duties of the child, were equally forgotten; but, as\par
returning fondness disarmed the parents’ resentment soonest, Laurentini\par
was suffered to believe that she had conquered, and her passions became\par
stronger by every effort, that had been employed to subdue them.\par
\par
The death of her father and mother in the same year left her to her own\par
discretion, under the dangerous circumstances attendant on youth and\par
beauty. She was fond of company, delighted with admiration, yet\par
disdainful of the opinion of the world, when it happened to contradict\par
her inclinations; had a gay and brilliant wit, and was mistress of all\par
the arts of fascination. Her conduct was such as might have been\par
expected, from the weakness of her principles and the strength of her\par
passions.\par
\par
Among her numerous admirers was the late Marquis de Villeroi, who, on\par
his tour through Italy, saw Laurentini at Venice, where she usually\par
resided, and became her passionate adorer. Equally captivated by the\par
figure and accomplishments of the Marquis, who was at that period one\par
of the most distinguished noblemen of the French court, she had the art\par
so effectually to conceal from him the dangerous traits of her\par
character and the blemishes of her late conduct, that he solicited her\par
hand in marriage.\par
\par
Before the nuptials were concluded, she retired to the castle of\par
Udolpho, whither the Marquis followed, and, where her conduct, relaxing\par
from the propriety, which she had lately assumed, discovered to him the\par
precipice, on which he stood. A minuter enquiry than he had before\par
thought it necessary to make, convinced him, that he had been deceived\par
in her character, and she, whom he had designed for his wife,\par
afterwards became his mistress.\par
\par
Having passed some weeks at Udolpho, he was called abruptly to France,\par
whither he returned with extreme reluctance, for his heart was still\par
fascinated by the arts of Laurentini, with whom, however, he had on\par
various pretences delayed his marriage; but, to reconcile her to this\par
separation, he now gave repeated promises of returning to conclude the\par
nuptials, as soon as the affair, which thus suddenly called him to\par
France, should permit.\par
\par
Soothed, in some degree, by these assurances, she suffered him to\par
depart; and, soon after, her relative, Montoni, arriving at Udolpho,\par
renewed the addresses, which she had before refused, and which she now\par
again rejected. Meanwhile, her thoughts were constantly with the\par
Marquis de Villeroi, for whom she suffered all the delirium of Italian\par
love, cherished by the solitude, to which she confined herself; for she\par
had now lost all taste for the pleasures of society and the gaiety of\par
amusement. Her only indulgences were to sigh and weep over a miniature\par
of the Marquis; to visit the scenes, that had witnessed their\par
happiness, to pour forth her heart to him in writing, and to count the\par
weeks, the days, which must intervene before the period that he had\par
mentioned as probable for his return. But this period passed without\par
bringing him; and week after week followed in heavy and almost\par
intolerable expectation. During this interval, Laurentini’s fancy,\par
occupied incessantly by one idea, became disordered; and, her whole\par
heart being devoted to one object, life became hateful to her, when she\par
believed that object lost.\par
\par
Several months passed, during which she heard nothing from the Marquis\par
de Villeroi, and her days were marked, at intervals, with the frenzy of\par
passion and the sullenness of despair. She secluded herself from all\par
visitors, and, sometimes, remained in her apartment, for weeks\par
together, refusing to speak to every person, except her favourite\par
female attendant, writing scraps of letters, reading, again and again,\par
those she had received from the Marquis, weeping over his picture, and\par
speaking to it, for many hours, upbraiding, reproaching and caressing\par
it alternately.\par
\par
At length, a report reached her, that the Marquis had married in\par
France, and, after suffering all the extremes of love, jealousy and\par
indignation, she formed the desperate resolution of going secretly to\par
that country, and, if the report proved true, of attempting a deep\par
revenge. To her favourite woman only she confided the plan of her\par
journey, and she engaged her to partake of it. Having collected her\par
jewels, which, descending to her from many branches of her family, were\par
of immense value, and all her cash, to a very large amount, they were\par
packed in a trunk, which was privately conveyed to a neighbouring town,\par
whither Laurentini, with this only servant, followed, and thence\par
proceeded secretly to Leghorn, where they embarked for France.\par
\par
When, on her arrival in Languedoc, she found, that the Marquis de\par
Villeroi had been married, for some months, her despair almost deprived\par
her of reason, and she alternately projected and abandoned the horrible\par
design of murdering the Marquis, his wife and herself. At length she\par
contrived to throw herself in his way, with an intention of reproaching\par
him, for his conduct, and of stabbing herself in his presence; but,\par
when she again saw him, who so long had been the constant object of her\par
thoughts and affections, resentment yielded to love; her resolution\par
failed; she trembled with the conflict of emotions, that assailed her\par
heart, and fainted away.\par
\par
The Marquis was not proof against her beauty and sensibility; all the\par
energy, with which he had first loved, returned, for his passion had\par
been resisted by prudence, rather than overcome by indifference; and,\par
since the honour of his family would not permit him to marry her, he\par
had endeavoured to subdue his love, and had so far succeeded, as to\par
select the then Marchioness for his wife, whom he loved at first with a\par
tempered and rational affection. But the mild virtues of that amiable\par
lady did not recompense him for her indifference, which appeared,\par
notwithstanding her efforts to conceal it; and he had, for some time,\par
suspected that her affections were engaged by another person, when\par
Laurentini arrived in Languedoc. This artful Italian soon perceived,\par
that she had regained her influence over him, and, soothed by the\par
discovery, she determined to live, and to employ all her enchantments\par
to win his consent to the diabolical deed, which she believed was\par
necessary to the security of her happiness. She conducted her scheme\par
with deep dissimulation and patient perseverance, and, having\par
completely estranged the affections of the Marquis from his wife, whose\par
gentle goodness and unimpassioned manners had ceased to please, when\par
contrasted with the captivations of the Italian, she proceeded to\par
awaken in his mind the jealousy of pride, for it was no longer that of\par
love, and even pointed out to him the person, to whom she affirmed the\par
Marchioness had sacrificed her honour; but Laurentini had first\par
extorted from him a solemn promise to forbear avenging himself upon his\par
rival. This was an important part of her plan, for she knew, that, if\par
his desire of vengeance was restrained towards one party, it would burn\par
more fiercely towards the other, and he might then, perhaps, be\par
prevailed on to assist in the horrible act, which would release him\par
from the only barrier, that withheld him from making her his wife.\par
\par
The innocent Marchioness, meanwhile, observed, with extreme grief, the\par
alteration in her husband’s manners. He became reserved and thoughtful\par
in her presence; his conduct was austere, and sometimes even rude; and\par
he left her, for many hours together, to weep for his unkindness, and\par
to form plans for the recovery of his affection. His conduct afflicted\par
her the more, because, in obedience to the command of her father, she\par
had accepted his hand, though her affections were engaged to another,\par
whose amiable disposition, she had reason to believe, would have\par
ensured her happiness. This circumstance Laurentini had discovered,\par
soon after her arrival in France, and had made ample use of it in\par
assisting her designs upon the Marquis, to whom she adduced such\par
seeming proof of his wife’s infidelity, that, in the frantic rage of\par
wounded honour, he consented to destroy his wife. A slow poison was\par
administered, and she fell a victim to the jealousy and subtlety of\par
Laurentini and to the guilty weakness of her husband.\par
\par
But the moment of Laurentini’s triumph, the moment, to which she had\par
looked forward for the completion of all her wishes, proved only the\par
commencement of a suffering, that never left her to her dying hour.\par
\par
The passion of revenge, which had in part stimulated her to the\par
commission of this atrocious deed, died, even at the moment when it was\par
gratified, and left her to the horrors of unavailing pity and remorse,\par
which would probably have empoisoned all the years she had promised\par
herself with the Marquis de Villeroi, had her expectations of an\par
alliance with him been realised. But he, too, had found the moment of\par
his revenge to be that of remorse, as to himself, and detestation, as\par
to the partner of his crime; the feeling, which he had mistaken for\par
conviction, was no more; and he stood astonished, and aghast, that no\par
proof remained of his wife’s infidelity, now that she had suffered the\par
punishment of guilt. Even when he was informed, that she was dying, he\par
had felt suddenly and unaccountably reassured of her innocence, nor was\par
the solemn assurance she made him in her last hour, capable of\par
affording him a stronger conviction of her blameless conduct.\par
\par
In the first horrors of remorse and despair, he felt inclined to\par
deliver up himself and the woman, who had plunged him into this abyss\par
of guilt, into the hands of justice; but, when the paroxysm of his\par
suffering was over, his intention changed. Laurentini, however, he saw\par
only once afterwards, and that was, to curse her as the instigator of\par
his crime, and to say, that he spared her life only on condition, that\par
she passed the rest of her days in prayer and penance. Overwhelmed with\par
disappointment, on receiving contempt and abhorrence from the man, for\par
whose sake she had not scrupled to stain her conscience with human\par
blood, and, touched with horror of the unavailing crime she had\par
committed, she renounced the world, and retired to the monastery of St.\par
Claire, a dreadful victim to unresisted passion.\par
\par
The Marquis, immediately after the death of his wife, quitted\par
Château-le-Blanc, to which he never returned, and endeavoured to lose\par
the sense of his crime amidst the tumult of war, or the dissipations of\par
a capital; but his efforts were vain; a deep dejection hung over him\par
ever after, for which his most intimate friend could not account, and\par
he, at length, died, with a degree of horror nearly equal to that,\par
which Laurentini had suffered. The physician, who had observed the\par
singular appearance of the unfortunate Marchioness, after death, had\par
been bribed to silence; and, as the surmises of a few of the servants\par
had proceeded no further than a whisper, the affair had never been\par
investigated. Whether this whisper ever reached the father of the\par
Marchioness, and, if it did, whether the difficulty of obtaining proof\par
deterred him from prosecuting the Marquis de Villeroi, is uncertain;\par
but her death was deeply lamented by some part of her family, and\par
particularly by her brother, M. St. Aubert; for that was the degree of\par
relationship, which had existed between Emily’s father and the\par
Marchioness; and there is no doubt, that he suspected the manner of her\par
death. Many letters passed between the Marquis and him, soon after the\par
decease of his beloved sister, the subject of which was not known, but\par
there is reason to believe, that they related to the cause of her\par
death; and these were the papers, together with some letters of the\par
Marchioness, who had confided to her brother the occasion of her\par
unhappiness, which St. Aubert had so solemnly enjoined his daughter to\par
destroy: and anxiety for her peace had probably made him forbid her to\par
enquire into the melancholy story, to which they alluded. Such, indeed,\par
had been his affliction, on the premature death of this his favourite\par
sister, whose unhappy marriage had from the first excited his tenderest\par
pity, that he never could hear her named, or mention her himself after\par
her death, except to Madame St. Aubert. From Emily, whose sensibility\par
he feared to awaken, he had so carefully concealed her history and\par
name, that she was ignorant, till now, that she ever had such a\par
relative as the Marchioness de Villeroi; and from this motive he had\par
enjoined silence to his only surviving sister, Madame Cheron, who had\par
scrupulously observed his request.\par
\par
It was over some of the last pathetic letters of the Marchioness, that\par
St. Aubert was weeping, when he was observed by Emily, on the eve of\par
her departure from La Vallée, and it was her picture, which he had so\par
tenderly caressed. Her disastrous death may account for the emotion he\par
had betrayed, on hearing her named by La Voisin, and for his request to\par
be interred near the monument of the Villerois, where her remains were\par
deposited, but not those of her husband, who was buried, where he died,\par
in the north of France.\par
\par
The confessor, who attended St. Aubert in his last moments, recollected\par
him to be the brother of the late Marchioness, when St. Aubert, from\par
tenderness to Emily, had conjured him to conceal the circumstance, and\par
to request that the abbess, to whose care he particularly recommended\par
her, would do the same; a request, which had been exactly observed.\par
\par
Laurentini, on her arrival in France, had carefully concealed her name\par
and family, and, the better to disguise her real history, had, on\par
entering the convent, caused the story to be circulated, which had\par
imposed on sister Frances, and it is probable, that the abbess, who did\par
not preside in the convent, at the time of her noviciation, was also\par
entirely ignorant of the truth. The deep remorse, that seized on the\par
mind of Laurentini, together with the sufferings of disappointed\par
passion, for she still loved the Marquis, again unsettled her\par
intellects, and, after the first paroxysms of despair were passed, a\par
heavy and silent melancholy had settled upon her spirits, which\par
suffered few interruptions from fits of frenzy, till the time of her\par
death. During many years, it had been her only amusement to walk in the\par
woods near the monastery, in the solitary hours of night, and to play\par
upon a favourite instrument, to which she sometimes joined the\par
delightful melody of her voice, in the most solemn and melancholy airs\par
of her native country, modulated by all the energetic feeling, that\par
dwelt in her heart. The physician, who had attended her, recommended it\par
to the superior to indulge her in this whim, as the only means of\par
soothing her distempered fancy; and she was suffered to walk in the\par
lonely hours of night, attended by the servant, who had accompanied her\par
from Italy; but, as the indulgence transgressed against the rules of\par
the convent, it was kept as secret as possible; and thus the mysterious\par
music of Laurentini had combined with other circumstances, to produce a\par
report, that not only the château, but its neighbourhood, was haunted.\par
\par
Soon after her entrance into this holy community, and before she had\par
shown any symptoms of insanity there, she made a will, in which, after\par
bequeathing a considerable legacy to the convent, she divided the\par
remainder of her personal property, which her jewels made very\par
valuable, between the wife of Mons. Bonnac, who was an Italian lady and\par
her relation, and the nearest surviving relative of the late\par
Marchioness de Villeroi. As Emily St. Aubert was not only the nearest,\par
but the sole relative, this legacy descended to her, and thus explained\par
to her the whole mystery of her father’s conduct.\par
\par
The resemblance between Emily and her unfortunate aunt had frequently\par
been observed by Laurentini, and had occasioned the singular behaviour,\par
which had formerly alarmed her; but it was in the nun’s dying hour,\par
when her conscience gave her perpetually the idea of the Marchioness,\par
that she became more sensible, than ever, of this likeness, and, in her\par
frenzy, deemed it no resemblance of the person she had injured, but the\par
original herself. The bold assertion, that had followed, on the\par
recovery of her senses, that Emily was the daughter of the Marchioness\par
de Villeroi, arose from a suspicion that she was so; for, knowing that\par
her rival, when she married the Marquis, was attached to another lover,\par
she had scarcely scrupled to believe, that her honour had been\par
sacrificed, like her own, to an unresisted passion.\par
\par
Of a crime, however, to which Emily had suspected, from her frenzied\par
confession of murder, that she had been instrumental in the castle of\par
Udolpho, Laurentini was innocent; and she had herself been deceived,\par
concerning the spectacle, that formerly occasioned her so much terror,\par
and had since compelled her, for a while, to attribute the horrors of\par
the nun to a consciousness of a murder, committed in that castle.\par
\par
It may be remembered, that, in a chamber of Udolpho, hung a black veil,\par
whose singular situation had excited Emily’s curiosity, and which\par
afterwards disclosed an object, that had overwhelmed her with horror;\par
for, on lifting it, there appeared, instead of the picture she had\par
expected, within a recess of the wall, a human figure of ghastly\par
paleness, stretched at its length, and dressed in the habiliments of\par
the grave. What added to the horror of the spectacle, was, that the\par
face appeared partly decayed and disfigured by worms, which were\par
visible on the features and hands. On such an object, it will be\par
readily believed, that no person could endure to look twice. Emily, it\par
may be recollected, had, after the first glance, let the veil drop, and\par
her terror had prevented her from ever after provoking a renewal of\par
such suffering, as she had then experienced. Had she dared to look\par
again, her delusion and her fears would have vanished together, and she\par
would have perceived, that the figure before her was not human, but\par
formed of wax. The history of it is somewhat extraordinary, though not\par
without example in the records of that fierce severity, which monkish\par
superstition has sometimes inflicted on mankind. A member of the house\par
of Udolpho, having committed some offence against the prerogative of\par
the church, had been condemned to the penance of contemplating, during\par
certain hours of the day, a waxen image, made to resemble a human body\par
in the state, to which it is reduced after death. This penance, serving\par
as a memento of the condition at which he must himself arrive, had been\par
designed to reprove the pride of the Marquis of Udolpho, which had\par
formerly so much exasperated that of the Romish church; and he had not\par
only superstitiously observed this penance himself, which, he had\par
believed, was to obtain a pardon for all his sins, but had made it a\par
condition in his will, that his descendants should preserve the image,\par
on pain of forfeiting to the church a certain part of his domain, that\par
they also might profit by the humiliating moral it conveyed. The\par
figure, therefore, had been suffered to retain its station in the wall\par
of the chamber, but his descendants excused themselves from observing\par
the penance, to which he had been enjoined.\par
\par
This image was so horribly natural, that it is not surprising Emily\par
should have mistaken it for the object it resembled, nor, since she had\par
heard such an extraordinary account, concerning the disappearing of the\par
late lady of the castle, and had such experience of the character of\par
Montoni, that she should have believed this to be the murdered body of\par
the lady Laurentini, and that he had been the contriver of her death.\par
\par
The situation, in which she had discovered it, occasioned her, at\par
first, much surprise and perplexity; but the vigilance, with which the\par
doors of the chamber, where it was deposited, were afterwards secured,\par
had compelled her to believe, that Montoni, not daring to confide the\par
secret of her death to any person, had suffered her remains to decay in\par
this obscure chamber. The ceremony of the veil, however, and the\par
circumstance of the doors having been left open, even for a moment, had\par
occasioned her much wonder and some doubts; but these were not\par
sufficient to overcome her suspicion of Montoni; and it was the dread\par
of his terrible vengeance, that had sealed her lips in silence,\par
concerning what she had seen in the west chamber.\par
\par
Emily, in discovering the Marchioness de Villeroi to have been the\par
sister of Mons. St. Aubert, was variously affected; but, amidst the\par
sorrow, which she suffered for her untimely death, she was released\par
from an anxious and painful conjecture, occasioned by the rash\par
assertion of Signora Laurentini, concerning her birth and the honour of\par
her parents. Her faith in St. Aubert’s principles would scarcely allow\par
her to suspect that he had acted dishonourably; and she felt such\par
reluctance to believe herself the daughter of any other, than her, whom\par
she had always considered and loved as a mother, that she would hardly\par
admit such a circumstance to be possible; yet the likeness, which it\par
had frequently been affirmed she bore to the late Marchioness, the\par
former behaviour of Dorothée the old housekeeper, the assertion of\par
Laurentini, and the mysterious attachment, which St. Aubert had\par
discovered, awakened doubts, as to his connection with the Marchioness,\par
which her reason could neither vanquish, nor confirm. From these,\par
however, she was now relieved, and all the circumstances of her\par
father’s conduct were fully explained: but her heart was oppressed by\par
the melancholy catastrophe of her amiable relative, and by the awful\par
lesson, which the history of the nun exhibited, the indulgence of whose\par
passions had been the means of leading her gradually to the commission\par
of a crime, from the prophecy of which in her early years she would\par
have recoiled in horror, and exclaimed—that it could not be!—a crime,\par
which whole years of repentance and of the severest penance had not\par
been able to obliterate from her conscience.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XVIII\par
\par
Then, fresh tears\par
Stood on her cheek, as doth the honey-dew\par
Upon a gather’d lily almost wither’d\par
                    SHAKESPEARE\par
\par
\par
After the late discoveries, Emily was distinguished at the château by\par
the Count and his family, as a relative of the house of Villeroi, and\par
received, if possible, more friendly attention, than had yet been shown\par
her.\par
\par
Count De Villefort’s surprise at the delay of an answer to his letter,\par
which had been directed to Valancourt, at Estuvière, was mingled with\par
satisfaction for the prudence, which had saved Emily from a share of\par
the anxiety he now suffered, though, when he saw her still drooping\par
under the effect of his former error, all his resolution was necessary\par
to restrain him from relating the truth, that would afford her a\par
momentary relief. The approaching nuptials of the Lady Blanche now\par
divided his attention with this subject of his anxiety, for the\par
inhabitants of the château were already busied in preparations for that\par
event, and the arrival of Mons. St. Foix was daily expected. In the\par
gaiety, which surrounded her, Emily vainly tried to participate, her\par
spirits being depressed by the late discoveries, and by the anxiety\par
concerning the fate of Valancourt, that had been occasioned by the\par
description of his manner, when he had delivered the ring. She seemed\par
to perceive in it the gloomy wildness of despair; and, when she\par
considered to what that despair might have urged him, her heart sunk\par
with terror and grief. The state of suspense, as to his safety, to\par
which she believed herself condemned, till she should return to La\par
Vallée, appeared insupportable, and, in such moments, she could not\par
even struggle to assume the composure, that had left her mind, but\par
would often abruptly quit the company she was with, and endeavour to\par
sooth her spirits in the deep solitudes of the woods, that overbrowed\par
the shore. Here, the faint roar of foaming waves, that beat below, and\par
the sullen murmur of the wind among the branches around, were\par
circumstances in unison with the temper of her mind; and she would sit\par
on a cliff, or on the broken steps of her favourite watch-tower,\par
observing the changing colours of the evening clouds, and the gloom of\par
twilight draw over the sea, till the white tops of billows, riding\par
towards the shore, could scarcely be discerned amidst the darkened\par
waters. The lines, engraved by Valancourt on this tower, she frequently\par
repeated with melancholy enthusiasm, and then would endeavour to check\par
the recollections and the grief they occasioned, and to turn her\par
thoughts to indifferent subjects.\par
\par
One evening, having wandered with her lute to this her favourite spot,\par
she entered the ruined tower, and ascended a winding staircase, that\par
led to a small chamber, which was less decayed than the rest of the\par
building, and whence she had often gazed, with admiration, on the wide\par
prospect of sea and land, that extended below. The sun was now setting\par
on that tract of the Pyrenees, which divided Languedoc from Rousillon,\par
and, placing herself opposite to a small grated window, which, like the\par
wood-tops beneath, and the waves lower still, gleamed with the red glow\par
of the west, she touched the chords of her lute in solemn symphony, and\par
then accompanied it with her voice, in one of the simple and affecting\par
airs, to which, in happier days, Valancourt had often listened in\par
rapture, and which she now adapted to the following lines.\par
\par
TO MELANCHOLY\par
\par
Spirit of love and sorrow—hail!\par
Thy solemn voice from far I hear,\par
Mingling with ev’ning’s dying gale:\par
Hail, with this sadly-pleasing tear!\par
\par
O! at this still, this lonely hour,\par
Thine own sweet hour of closing day,\par
Awake thy lute, whose charmful pow’r\par
Shall call up Fancy to obey:\par
\par
To paint the wild romantic dream,\par
That meets the poet’s musing eye,\par
As, on the bank of shadowy stream,\par
He breathes to her the fervid sigh.\par
\par
O lonely spirit! let thy song\par
Lead me through all thy sacred haunt;\par
The minister’s moonlight aisles along,\par
Where spectres raise the midnight chaunt.\par
\par
I hear their dirges faintly swell!\par
Then, sink at once in silence drear,\par
While, from the pillar’d cloister’s cell,\par
Dimly their gliding forms appear!\par
\par
Lead where the pine-woods wave on high,\par
Whose pathless sod is darkly seen,\par
As the cold moon, with trembling eye,\par
Darts her long beams the leaves between.\par
\par
Lead to the mountain’s dusky head,\par
Where, far below, in shade profound,\par
Wide forests, plains and hamlets spread,\par
And sad the chimes of vesper sound,\par
\par
Or guide me, where the dashing oar\par
Just breaks the stillness of the vale,\par
As slow it tracks the winding shore,\par
To meet the ocean’s distant sail:\par
\par
To pebbly banks, that Neptune laves,\par
With measur’d surges, loud and deep,\par
Where the dark cliff bends o’er the waves,\par
And wild the winds of autumn sweep.\par
\par
There pause at midnight’s spectred hour,\par
And list the long-resounding gale;\par
And catch the fleeting moonlight’s pow’r,\par
O’er foaming seas and distant sail.\par
\par
\par
The soft tranquillity of the scene below, where the evening breeze\par
scarcely curled the water, or swelled the passing sail, that caught the\par
last gleam of the sun, and where, now and then, a dipping oar was all\par
that disturbed the trembling radiance, conspired with the tender melody\par
of her lute to lull her mind into a state of gentle sadness, and she\par
sung the mournful songs of past times, till the remembrances they\par
awakened were too powerful for her heart, her tears fell upon the lute,\par
over which she drooped, and her voice trembled, and was unable to\par
proceed.\par
\par
Though the sun had now sunk behind the mountains, and even his\par
reflected light was fading from their highest points, Emily did not\par
leave the watch-tower, but continued to indulge her melancholy reverie,\par
till a footstep, at a little distance, startled her, and, on looking\par
through the grate, she observed a person walking below, whom, however,\par
soon perceiving to be Mons. Bonnac, she returned to the quiet\par
thoughtfulness his step had interrupted. After some time, she again\par
struck her lute, and sung her favourite air; but again a step disturbed\par
her, and, as she paused to listen, she heard it ascending the staircase\par
of the tower. The gloom of the hour, perhaps, made her sensible to some\par
degree of fear, which she might not otherwise have felt; for, only a\par
few minutes before, she had seen Mons. Bonnac pass. The steps were\par
quick and bounding, and, in the next moment, the door of the chamber\par
opened, and a person entered, whose features were veiled in the\par
obscurity of twilight; but his voice could not be concealed, for it was\par
the voice of Valancourt! At the sound, never heard by Emily, without\par
emotion, she started, in terror, astonishment and doubtful pleasure,\par
and had scarcely beheld him at her feet, when she sunk into a seat,\par
overcome by the various emotions, that contended at her heart, and\par
almost insensible to that voice, whose earnest and trembling calls\par
seemed as if endeavouring to save her. Valancourt, as he hung over\par
Emily, deplored his own rash impatience, in having thus surprised her:\par
for when he had arrived at the château, too anxious to await the return\par
of the Count, who, he understood, was in the grounds, he went himself\par
to seek him, when, as he passed the tower, he was struck by the sound\par
of Emily’s voice, and immediately ascended.\par
\par
It was a considerable time before she revived, but, when her\par
recollection returned, she repulsed his attentions, with an air of\par
reserve, and enquired, with as much displeasure as it was possible she\par
could feel in these first moments of his appearance, the occasion of\par
his visit.\par
\par
“Ah Emily!” said Valancourt, “that air, those words—alas! I have, then,\par
little to hope—when you ceased to esteem me, you ceased also to love\par
me!”\par
\par
“Most true, sir,” replied Emily, endeavouring to command her trembling\par
voice; “and if you had valued my esteem, you would not have given me\par
this new occasion for uneasiness.”\par
\par
Valancourt’s countenance changed suddenly from the anxieties of doubt\par
to an expression of surprise and dismay: he was silent a moment, and\par
then said, “I had been taught to hope for a very different reception!\par
Is it, then, true, Emily, that I have lost your regard for ever? am I\par
to believe, that, though your esteem for me may return—your affection\par
never can? Can the Count have meditated the cruelty, which now tortures\par
me with a second death?”\par
\par
The voice, in which he spoke this, alarmed Emily as much as his words\par
surprised her, and, with trembling impatience, she begged that he would\par
explain them.\par
\par
“Can any explanation be necessary?” said Valancourt, “do you not know\par
how cruelly my conduct has been misrepresented? that the actions of\par
which you once believed me guilty (and, O Emily! how could you so\par
degrade me in your opinion, even for a moment!) those actions—I hold in\par
as much contempt and abhorrence as yourself? Are you, indeed, ignorant,\par
that Count de Villefort has detected the slanders, that have robbed me\par
of all I hold dear on earth, and has invited me hither to justify to\par
you my former conduct? It is surely impossible you can be uninformed of\par
these circumstances, and I am again torturing myself with a false\par
hope!”\par
\par
The silence of Emily confirmed this supposition; for the deep twilight\par
would not allow Valancourt to distinguish the astonishment and doubting\par
joy, that fixed her features. For a moment, she continued unable to\par
speak; then a profound sigh seemed to give some relief to her spirits,\par
and she said,\par
\par
“Valancourt! I was, till this moment, ignorant of all the circumstances\par
you have mentioned; the emotion I now suffer may assure you of the\par
truth of this, and, that, though I had ceased to esteem, I had not\par
taught myself entirely to forget you.”\par
\par
“This moment,” said Valancourt, in a low voice, and leaning for support\par
against the window—“this moment brings with it a conviction that\par
overpowers me!—I am dear to you then—still dear to you, my Emily!”\par
\par
“Is it necessary that I should tell you so?” she replied, “is it\par
necessary, that I should say—these are the first moments of joy I have\par
known, since your departure, and that they repay me for all those of\par
pain I have suffered in the interval?”\par
\par
Valancourt sighed deeply, and was unable to reply; but, as he pressed\par
her hand to his lips, the tears, that fell over it, spoke a language,\par
which could not be mistaken, and to which words were inadequate.\par
\par
Emily, somewhat tranquillized, proposed returning to the château, and\par
then, for the first time, recollected that the Count had invited\par
Valancourt thither to explain his conduct, and that no explanation had\par
yet been given. But, while she acknowledged this, her heart would not\par
allow her to dwell, for a moment, on the possibility of his\par
unworthiness; his look, his voice, his manner, all spoke the noble\par
sincerity, which had formerly distinguished him; and she again\par
permitted herself to indulge the emotions of a joy, more surprising and\par
powerful, than she had ever before experienced.\par
\par
Neither Emily, nor Valancourt, were conscious how they reached the\par
château, whither they might have been transferred by the spell of a\par
fairy, for anything they could remember; and it was not, till they had\par
reached the great hall, that either of them recollected there were\par
other persons in the world besides themselves. The Count then came\par
forth with surprise, and with the joyfulness of pure benevolence, to\par
welcome Valancourt, and to entreat his forgiveness of the injustice he\par
had done him; soon after which, Mons. Bonnac joined this happy group,\par
in which he and Valancourt were mutually rejoiced to meet.\par
\par
When the first congratulations were over, and the general joy became\par
somewhat more tranquil, the Count withdrew with Valancourt to the\par
library, where a long conversation passed between them, in which the\par
latter so clearly justified himself of the criminal parts of the\par
conduct, imputed to him, and so candidly confessed and so feelingly\par
lamented the follies, which he had committed, that the Count was\par
confirmed in his belief of all he had hoped; and, while he perceived so\par
many noble virtues in Valancourt, and that experience had taught him to\par
detest the follies, which before he had only not admired, he did not\par
scruple to believe, that he would pass through life with the dignity of\par
a wise and good man, or to entrust to his care the future happiness of\par
Emily St. Aubert, for whom he felt the solicitude of a parent. Of this\par
he soon informed her, in a short conversation, when Valancourt had left\par
him. While Emily listened to a relation of the services, that\par
Valancourt had rendered Mons. Bonnac, her eyes overflowed with tears of\par
pleasure, and the further conversation of Count De Villefort perfectly\par
dissipated every doubt, as to the past and future conduct of him, to\par
whom she now restored, without fear, the esteem and affection, with\par
which she had formerly received him.\par
\par
When they returned to the supper-room, the Countess and Lady Blanche\par
met Valancourt with sincere congratulations; and Blanche, indeed, was\par
so much rejoiced to see Emily returned to happiness, as to forget, for\par
a while, that Mons. St. Foix was not yet arrived at the château, though\par
he had been expected for some hours; but her generous sympathy was,\par
soon after, rewarded by his appearance. He was now perfectly recovered\par
from the wounds, received, during his perilous adventure among the\par
Pyrenees, the mention of which served to heighten to the parties, who\par
had been involved in it, the sense of their present happiness. New\par
congratulations passed between them, and round the supper-table\par
appeared a group of faces, smiling with felicity, but with a felicity,\par
which had in each a different character. The smile of Blanche was frank\par
and gay, that of Emily tender and pensive; Valancourt’s was rapturous,\par
tender and gay alternately; Mons. St. Foix’s was joyous, and that of\par
the Count, as he looked on the surrounding party, expressed the\par
tempered complacency of benevolence; while the features of the\par
Countess, Henri, and Mons. Bonnac, discovered fainter traces of\par
animation. Poor Mons. Du Pont did not, by his presence, throw a shade\par
of regret over the company; for, when he had discovered, that\par
Valancourt was not unworthy of the esteem of Emily, he determined\par
seriously to endeavour at the conquest of his own hopeless affection,\par
and had immediately withdrawn from Château-le-Blanc—a conduct, which\par
Emily now understood, and rewarded with her admiration and pity.\par
\par
The Count and his guests continued together till a late hour, yielding\par
to the delights of social gaiety, and to the sweets of friendship. When\par
Annette heard of the arrival of Valancourt, Ludovico had some\par
difficulty to prevent her going into the supper-room, to express her\par
joy, for she declared, that she had never been so rejoiced at any\par
_accident_ as this, since she had found Ludovico himself.\par
\par
\par
\par
 CHAPTER XIX\par
\par
Now my task is smoothly done,\par
I can fly, or I can run\par
Quickly to the green earth’s end,\par
Where the bow’d welkin low doth bend,\par
And, from thence, can soar as soon\par
To the corners of the moon.\par
                    MILTON\par
\par
\par
The marriages of the Lady Blanche and Emily St. Aubert were celebrated,\par
on the same day, and with the ancient baronial magnificence, at\par
Château-le-Blanc. The feasts were held in the great hall of the castle,\par
which, on this occasion, was hung with superb new tapestry,\par
representing the exploits of Charlemagne and his twelve peers; here,\par
were seen the Saracens, with their horrible visors, advancing to\par
battle; and there, were displayed the wild solemnities of incantation,\par
and the necromantic feats, exhibited by the magician _Jarl_ before the\par
Emperor. The sumptuous banners of the family of Villeroi, which had\par
long slept in dust, were once more unfurled, to wave over the gothic\par
points of painted casements; and music echoed, in many a lingering\par
close, through every winding gallery and colonnade of that vast\par
edifice.\par
\par
As Annette looked down from the corridor upon the hall, whose arches\par
and windows were illuminated with brilliant festoons of lamps, and\par
gazed on the splendid dresses of the dancers, the costly liveries of\par
the attendants, the canopies of purple velvet and gold, and listened to\par
the gay strains that floated along the vaulted roof, she almost fancied\par
herself in an enchanted palace, and declared, that she had not met with\par
any place, which charmed her so much, since she read the fairy tales;\par
nay, that the fairies themselves, at their nightly revels in this old\par
hall, could display nothing finer; while old Dorothée, as she surveyed\par
the scene, sighed, and said, the castle looked as it was wont to do in\par
the time of her youth.\par
\par
After gracing the festivities of Château-le-Blanc, for some days,\par
Valancourt and Emily took leave of their kind friends, and returned to\par
La Vallée, where the faithful Theresa received them with unfeigned joy,\par
and the pleasant shades welcomed them with a thousand tender and\par
affecting remembrances; and, while they wandered together over the\par
scenes, so long inhabited by the late Mons. and Madame St. Aubert, and\par
Emily pointed out, with pensive affection, their favourite haunts, her\par
present happiness was heightened, by considering, that it would have\par
been worthy of their approbation, could they have witnessed it.\par
\par
Valancourt led her to the plane-tree on the terrace, where he had first\par
ventured to declare his love, and where now the remembrance of the\par
anxiety he had then suffered, and the retrospect of all the dangers and\par
misfortunes they had each encountered, since last they sat together\par
beneath its broad branches, exalted the sense of their present\par
felicity, which, on this spot, sacred to the memory of St. Aubert, they\par
solemnly vowed to deserve, as far as possible, by endeavouring to\par
imitate his benevolence,—by remembering, that superior attainments of\par
every sort bring with them duties of superior exertion,—and by\par
affording to their fellow-beings, together with that portion of\par
ordinary comforts, which prosperity always owes to misfortune, the\par
example of lives passed in happy thankfulness to God, and, therefore,\par
in careful tenderness to his creatures.\par
\par
Soon after their return to La Vallée, the brother of Valancourt came to\par
congratulate him on his marriage, and to pay his respects to Emily,\par
with whom he was so much pleased, as well as with the prospect of\par
rational happiness, which these nuptials offered to Valancourt, that he\par
immediately resigned to him a part of the rich domain, the whole of\par
which, as he had no family, would of course descend to his brother, on\par
his decease.\par
\par
The estates, at Thoulouse, were disposed of, and Emily purchased of\par
Mons. Quesnel the ancient domain of her late father, where, having\par
given Annette a marriage portion, she settled her as the housekeeper,\par
and Ludovico as the steward; but, since both Valancourt and herself\par
preferred the pleasant and long-loved shades of La Vallée to the\par
magnificence of Epourville, they continued to reside there, passing,\par
however, a few months in the year at the birth-place of St. Aubert, in\par
tender respect to his memory.\par
\par
The legacy, which had been bequeathed to Emily by Signora Laurentini,\par
she begged Valancourt would allow her to resign to Mons. Bonnac; and\par
Valancourt, when she made the request, felt all the value of the\par
compliment it conveyed. The castle of Udolpho, also, descended to the\par
wife of Mons. Bonnac, who was the nearest surviving relation of the\par
house of that name, and thus affluence restored his long-oppressed\par
spirits to peace, and his family to comfort.\par
\par
O! how joyful it is to tell of happiness, such as that of Valancourt\par
and Emily; to relate, that, after suffering under the oppression of the\par
vicious and the disdain of the weak, they were, at length, restored to\par
each other—to the beloved landscapes of their native country,—to the\par
securest felicity of this life, that of aspiring to moral and labouring\par
for intellectual improvement—to the pleasures of enlightened society,\par
and to the exercise of the benevolence, which had always animated their\par
hearts; while the bowers of La Vallée became, once more, the retreat of\par
goodness, wisdom and domestic blessedness!\par
\par
O! useful may it be to have shown, that, though the vicious can\par
sometimes pour affliction upon the good, their power is transient and\par
their punishment certain; and that innocence, though oppressed by\par
injustice, shall, supported by patience, finally triumph over\par
misfortune!\par
\par
And, if the weak hand, that has recorded this tale, has, by its scenes,\par
beguiled the mourner of one hour of sorrow, or, by its moral, taught\par
him to sustain it—the effort, however humble, has not been vain, nor is\par
the writer unrewarded.\par
\par
\par
